const COMMENTARY = {"1 Chronicles 12:16–18": {"title": "\"We are yours, O David\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David is not king yet. He is a fugitive in a stronghold, and armed men keep arriving — some from Saul's own tribe, which is exactly the problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you come to me in peace, to help me, then my heart will be knit to yours. But if you come to betray me…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts it to them straight, and leaves the judging to God rather than interrogating them or turning them away. A hunted man cannot afford either paranoia or naivety, and David does something in between: he names both possibilities out loud and waits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Spirit comes on Amasai, and what comes out is not a speech but a chant, three beats of *peace*:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "Peace, peace to you, and peace to all who help you, for your God is your help."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not offer terms or negotiate a position. He declares whose side they are on and blesses the man they are joining — and the blessing is grounded not in David's prospects but in God being his help."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David does with it.** He welcomed them and *made them leaders of his raiding bands* — command, straight away, to men who had been potential assassins ten minutes earlier. The chapter is a long list of people who came over to David before he had anything to give them, and the writer is keeping count on purpose."}]}, "1 Corinthians 1:26–31": {"title": "\"Look at your own calling\" — the argument from the congregation", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes his case by telling the Corinthians to look around the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evidence he points at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not many of you were wise by the reckoning of the flesh, not many were powerful, not many were nobly born.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not insulting them. He is presenting them as data. If the message spread by being intellectually fashionable or socially connected, this congregation would look different than it does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the precision: *not many* — not *none*. Corinth had some people of standing, and Paul does not overstate his case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four things God chose"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["what the world calls **foolish**", "what the world calls **weak**", "the **low and the despised**", "**the things that count for nothing at all**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last one escalates past the others — literally *the things that are not*, brought in to nullify *the things that are*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the stated purpose is entirely negative: *so that no human being might boast in His presence*. The selection criteria were designed to rule something out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God chooses to work with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The principle is not confined to people. It runs through the whole Old Testament as a method:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["a **tent peg and a hammer** against nine hundred iron chariots (Judges 4)", "a **nursing lamb** on an altar while a Philistine army closed in (1 Samuel 7)", "a **shepherd's sling** against a man in full armour", "**jars and torches**, three hundred men, no swords drawn (Judges 7)"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in that list looks like a weapon. Every one of them is what God chose, and the choosing is the point — *so that no human being might boast in His presence.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A piece of bread and a small cup belong on that list. It is not that they are adequate despite being small; small is the specification. See the notes on Judges 4:17–24 and 1 Samuel 7:7–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Christ is said to have become"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wisdom from God — and everything that puts us right with God, and everything that makes us holy, and everything that sets us free.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things, and every one of them is the sort of thing a person would otherwise be trying to achieve. Paul does not say Christ helps with them. He says Christ **became** them, for us."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The quotation at the end.** *Let the one who boasts, boast in the Lord* — from Jeremiah, where the context is a warning against boasting in wisdom, strength or riches. The three things Jeremiah names line up almost exactly with the three Paul has just said the Corinthians did not have much of. He is not improvising the argument; he is landing on a text."}]}, "1 Corinthians 10:11–13": {"title": "\"The way of escape\" — and what it is escape from", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the most quoted promises in the New Testament, sitting immediately after a list of Israel's worst failures in the desert."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just run through the golden calf, the immorality at Peor, the grumbling and the snakes. Then: *all these things happened to them as examples, and they were written down as instruction for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for examples is **typoi** — patterns, impressions, the mark a die leaves. Paul reads Israel's history as a shape that repeats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning that comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let the one who thinks he is standing take care that he does not fall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the sentence the famous promise is attached to, and it is a warning against confidence, not an encouragement to it. The order matters: watch yourself, **then** the assurance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised, precisely"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the testing is *common to everyone* — not unique, not a sign of being singled out", "God is faithful, and will not allow testing beyond what you are able to bear", "with the testing He *will also make the way of escape*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last phrase is worth reading closely: the escape is **so that you are able to bear it**. Not a door out of the situation — a way through it that does not break you. Paul's own thorn, which he asked three times to have removed and did not, is the same shape."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"more than you can bear.\"** The verse is often quoted about suffering in general — God will not give you more than you can handle. Paul is talking about *temptation and testing*, and elsewhere he says flatly that he was burdened beyond his strength, so that he would rely on God who raises the dead. The comfort here is real but specific: no one is ever cornered into sin with no way out."}]}, "1 Corinthians 10:31–33": {"title": "\"Whether you eat or drink\" — the end of a long argument about dinner", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famous line is the conclusion of three chapters about meat, and knowing that changes how it reads."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the argument was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth's markets sold meat that had been sacrificed in pagan temples. Some Christians ate it without a thought; others could not, and were distressed by those who did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's answer runs long, and it never resolves into a rule about the meat. It resolves into a question about people: what does eating this do to the person next to me?"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three groups"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give no offence to Jews, or to Greeks, or to the church of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the whole social map — the synagogue, the pagan city, and his own congregation — and asks them to consider all three. Not a private conscience question; a public one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The standard he sets himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not seeking my own profit but the profit of the many, so that they may be saved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *profit* is a plain commercial one — advantage, benefit, what is in it for me. Paul says he runs the calculation for other people rather than for himself, and he says it as a description of how he already lives, not as advice."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"do everything for the glory of God.\"** Lifted out, it becomes a general piety about doing your best. In place it is a tiebreaker for a specific and genuinely difficult question — when two Christians disagree about something permitted, and there is no rule to settle it, decide by what serves the other person. The very next line of the letter is *follow my example, as I follow Christ's*."}]}, "1 Corinthians 11:20–22": {"title": "Not the Lord's Supper", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul says something startling about a meeting that thought it was taking communion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So when you assemble together in one place, **it is not the Lord's Supper that you eat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *you are taking it badly*. It is not that meal at all. Whatever they were doing, the name did not apply to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Each one goes ahead with his own supper, and one is left hungry while another is drunk.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Corinthian gathering was a full meal. Wealthier members arrived earlier — they were not working — and ate what they had brought. Slaves and labourers came when they could get away, to nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *goes ahead* is about taking yours first, before the others."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you not have houses to eat and drink in? Or do you **despise the church of God**, and **shame those who have nothing**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not accuse them of bad theology about the bread. He accuses them of humiliating poor people at the table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *shame* is to put to shame, to disgrace — a public thing done to somebody's face."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In this I do not praise you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has praised them a few lines earlier for holding to what he passed on. Here he withdraws it, in the same formula, so the reversal is unmissable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famous words come next — *the Lord Jesus, on the night He was betrayed, took bread.* Paul recites the institution **as the correction** to what he has just described."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a few verses on, *not discerning the body* — which in this context is a gathering that cannot see the people it is eating with. See the note on 1 Corinthians 11:27–30."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The point of the whole passage.** A meal about a body given for everyone was being eaten in a way that divided the room by income. Paul's remedy at the end is two words long: *wait for one another.*"}]}, "1 Corinthians 11:23–26": {"title": "Received from the Lord", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The oldest written account of the Lord's Supper anywhere — earlier than any of the four Gospels — and Paul says where he got it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I received from the Lord\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I **received from the Lord** what I also delivered to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul was not in the upper room. He was not a disciple then, and he does not say Peter told him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the Lord did. The same man tells the Corinthians in his next letter about being caught up to the third heaven and hearing words no one is permitted to repeat. Whatever else Paul received, he received this — and he passed on this one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Broken for you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take, eat: this is My body, **which is broken for you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither Matthew nor Mark has this phrase. Luke has *given for you*. Paul has **broken**, and he adds the two words that make it personal: *for you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only place in the New Testament where the breaking of the bread is stated as being for the eater's sake."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 53 says *by His stripes we are healed* in the same breath as *He was wounded for our transgressions*. Two provisions in one passage, and two elements on one table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In remembrance of Me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice — once over the bread, once over the cup. Repetition in Paul is never accidental."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **anamnēsis** is stronger than a fond recollection; the Septuagint uses it for the memorial portion of an offering, the part that brought a thing to mind before God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The new covenant in My blood\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a renewal of the old. A different covenant, and Jeremiah 31 had promised it: *I will forgive their iniquity and remember their sin no more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old covenant's terms were *if you obey*. The new one's terms are the blood it is named after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You proclaim the Lord's death until He comes\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For as often as you eat this bread and drink this cup, you **proclaim** the Lord's death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katangellō** — to announce publicly, the word used for preaching. Eating is a form of announcement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the announcement is aimed at more than the people in the room. Something is declared every time the bread is taken."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until He comes.* The instruction has an end date, which is also the only date in it that has not yet arrived."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage is answering.** Paul does not recite the institution as a liturgy lesson. He recites it because the Corinthians were shaming poor people at that table. The most quoted communion passage in Scripture was written as a correction. See the notes on 1 Corinthians 11:20–22 and 11:27–30."}]}, "1 Corinthians 11:27–30": {"title": "Discerning the body", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul gives a reason for weakness and sickness in the Corinthian church, and the reason he gives is not sin in general. It is one specific failure: *not discerning the Lord's body.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Diakrino** — to distinguish, to tell one thing apart from another. Not *deserve*. Not *feel worthy of*. To recognise what is in front of you and see what it is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the failure is a failure of recognition. Something was being handled without being seen for what it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What there was to recognise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just quoted the words of institution, four verses earlier: *this is My body, which is for you.* The bread is named as the body given on their behalf."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what was going unrecognised at Corinth — people eating in a hurry, some drunk, the wealthy shaming the poor, and the bread on the table treated as nothing in particular. Paul's whole complaint is that they were not seeing what they were holding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two things to tell apart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Diakrino** does not only mean to notice. It means to **separate** one thing from another — to draw a line between two items and treat them differently."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There are two things on that table, and Paul has just named what each one is:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the cup** — *the new covenant in My blood*, and Matthew adds *for the forgiveness of sins*", "**the bread** — *My body, which is broken for you*, and Matthew's own Gospel says of that body, *He took our infirmities and bore our diseases*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "To take the cup for forgiveness and swallow the bread as though it were nothing in particular is to separate what He handed over together. The blood is not doubted; the body is passed over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the failure named in the verse, and Paul attaches a consequence to it in the next line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same word, on the road to Jerusalem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus uses **diakrino** in Mark 11, the morning after the fig tree: *whoever says to this mountain, be removed, and **does not doubt** in his heart…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word rendered *doubt* is this word. Doubting, in that sentence, is being divided in yourself — saying one thing and holding another, split down the middle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mountain and the fig tree are not two different kinds of work; a man who divides them will not move either. Neither are the body and the blood. See the note on Mark 11:20–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In an unworthy way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is an adverb. It describes **how** they ate, not who they were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That distinction is the difference between a table and a tribunal. Paul is not saying only worthy people may come — that would empty the room, and it would contradict what the bread means. He is saying there is a way of coming to it that misses the point of it entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remedy he gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not staying away. Two verses later: *wait for one another.* And in the same paragraph, *let a man examine himself, and so let him eat* — the examining leads to eating, not away from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer to eating badly is eating properly, with the body recognised."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line, and what it implies.** *For this reason many among you are weak and sick.* Paul states a link between failing to discern the body and physical weakness — which means the reverse is worth taking seriously too. This is a table where the body broken *for you* is set in front of you, and Paul plainly thought that recognising it mattered for more than the soul."}]}, "1 Corinthians 11:31–34": {"title": "Three judgments in two verses", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*If we judged ourselves, we would not be judged. But when we are judged, we are chastened by the Lord.* Three judgment words in two lines — and in the Greek they are not all the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first one is a different verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If we **judged** ourselves…* — **diakrino**. To distinguish, to separate, to tell one thing apart from another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same verb Paul used two verses earlier in *not **discerning** the Lord's body*, and the same one Jesus used in Mark 11 for the man who *does not doubt* — is not divided — in his heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the first clause is not about interrogating yourself for sin. It is the verb of telling things apart, applied to yourself at that table: judging yourself healed because of a body broken for you, and forgiven because of blood poured out for you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second and third are another verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*…we would not be **judged**. But when we are **judged**, we are chastened by the Lord.* — **krino**, both times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different Greek verbs, rendered by one English word, in a single sentence. The distinction is invisible in translation and it is the whole hinge of the passage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And Paul says what the second word means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not leave it to be guessed. He defines it in the same breath:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **chastened** by the Lord, **so that we would not be condemned along with the world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chastening and the condemning are set against each other, not stacked together. Whatever *krino* is doing here, Paul's own clause rules out its being sentence and condemnation — that is what the world is under, and he says so explicitly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Chastened\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paideuo**, from **pais**, a child. It is the vocabulary of raising a son — training, instruction, correction inside a family."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews 12 uses the same word at length: *the Lord disciplines the one He loves… no discipline seems pleasant at the time, but afterward it yields the peaceable fruit of righteousness.* Correction, and a stated harvest. See the note on Hebrews 12:5–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And condemnation is off the table already"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*…so that we would not be condemned along with the world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John 3 settles who is where: *whoever believes in Him is not condemned… he who does not believe is condemned already.* The condemnation is the world's condition, and it is described as a thing already in place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes to keep the Corinthians clear of that, not to put them under it. See the note on John 3:14–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The practical instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you come together to eat, **wait for one another**. If anyone is hungry, let him eat at home.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After all of it — the warnings, the weakness, the sleeping — the remedy is five words long, and it is about manners at a shared table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is told to stay away."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *And the rest I will set in order when I come.* Paul closes the subject the way a man closes a letter, not the way a judge closes a case."}]}, "1 Corinthians 12:29–31": {"title": "Seven questions expecting no", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks seven questions in a row, and in Greek each is framed to expect the answer *no*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are all apostles? Are all prophets? Are all teachers? Are all workers of miracles? Do all have gifts of healings? Do all speak with tongues? Do all interpret?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The construction is unambiguous — he is not wondering. He is closing off the idea that every believer should exhibit every gift."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole argument of the chapter: a body is made of parts that are not the same, and an eye that resented not being a hand would be misunderstanding what a body is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Earnestly desire the best gifts\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after saying not everyone has everything, he tells them to want the better ones — a plural imperative, addressed to the church as a body rather than to individuals shopping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *best* is defined two chapters later, in his own terms: what builds the church up. In chapter 14 he ranks prophecy above tongues in a gathering for exactly that reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"A still more excellent way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence that ends the chapter, and it is the doorway into chapter 13 — the passage about love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the structure people miss when 1 Corinthians 13 gets read at weddings. It is not a standalone poem; it is Paul's answer to a church quarrelling about which gifts made a person important."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How chapter 13 opens.** *If I speak in the tongues of men and of angels but have not love, I am a noisy gong.* He lists the very gifts they were competing over and prices each one at nothing without the thing he is about to describe. See the note on 1 Corinthians 14:1–5, where he picks the argument back up: *pursue love*, and then desire the gifts."}]}, "1 Corinthians 14:1–5": {"title": "\"Pursue love\" — the verb, and what it is set against", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 13 has just finished — faith, hope and love, and the greatest of these is love. Then, without a break, this: *pursue love.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two different verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses one word for love and a different one for the gifts, and the pair is deliberate:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**pursue** love — *dioko*, to chase, to hunt down, to run after. The word used of persecutors pursuing their target.", "**desire earnestly** the spiritual gifts — a strong word for wanting, but not the same as running someone down."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Love is the thing you chase. The gifts are things you want. Nobody reading this in Corinth could have missed which one Paul put in the hunting verb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure he applies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after this is judged by one question: does it build up the church?"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the one who speaks in a tongue *builds himself up*", "the one who prophesies *builds up the church*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not dismissive — he says he wishes they all spoke in tongues, and elsewhere that he does so more than any of them. His argument is about the gathering: in a room full of people, the thing that everyone can understand does more good than the thing only one person understands."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"prophesy\" means here.** Paul defines it in the passage rather than leaving it to be assumed: speaking *to build them up, to urge them on, and to comfort them*. That is a wider and more ordinary thing than predicting the future, and all three of its stated purposes are aimed at other people."}]}, "1 Corinthians 15:23–28": {"title": "The last enemy", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is arguing for the resurrection against people who had decided it was nonsense, and here he sets out the order of events."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The last enemy that will be destroyed is death\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things in that sentence are easy to miss."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Enemy.** Death is not described as natural, or as a doorway, or as part of the design. It is on a list of hostile powers being put down. The Bible's usual tenderness about death — falling asleep, departing — never softens into calling it a friend."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Last.** It goes, but it goes at the end. Everything else is subdued first. That is a straight answer to the question of why death is still here if it has been defeated: Paul says the defeat is real and the removal is scheduled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ the firstfruits, and afterward those who are Christ's at His coming.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Firstfruits is a harvest word — the first sheaf brought in, which is not a sample but a guarantee. The rest of the field is coming, and it will be the same crop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the argument of the whole chapter compressed into a phrase: what happened to Him is what happens to His."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The strange ending.** Paul says that when everything has been put under Christ, the Son Himself will be subject to the Father, *so that God may be all in all.* It reads oddly against everything else the New Testament says about Christ's glory — and it is Paul's way of saying that the goal of the whole operation is not a rearranged universe with a new authority in charge, but God filling everything, with nothing left out and nothing left over."}]}, "1 Corinthians 15:56–57": {"title": "\"The strength of sin is the law\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two short clauses in the middle of Paul's chapter on resurrection, and the second one is startling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **sting of death is sin**, and the **strength of sin is the law**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things linked backwards: death gets its sting from sin, and sin gets its power from the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Remove the last link and the whole chain goes slack."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Strength\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dunamis** — power, the word behind dynamite. Not *the measure of sin* or *the definition of sin*. Its power source."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same thing at length in Romans 7: *I would not have known covetousness unless the law had said, \"You shall not covet.\" But sin, taking opportunity by the commandment, produced in me all manner of evil desire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *the commandment which was to bring life, I found to bring death.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which does not make the law bad"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul defends it in the same breath: *the law is holy, and the commandment holy and just and good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fault is not in the statute. It is in what a prohibition does when it meets the old nature — a wall to push against, a line to notice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin is not weakened by being told to stop. It is armed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this is in a chapter about resurrection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just quoted Hosea: *O Death, where is your sting? O Hades, where is your victory?* It is a taunt, thrown at a defeated enemy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he explains what made the sting sharp in the first place — and the two clauses are there to show that the whole apparatus has been dismantled at the root, not merely at the grave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **thanks be to God, who gives us the victory** through our Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *who helps us achieve* it. Gives — present tense, continuous in the Greek."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The victory is handed over, and the passage names where it came from and does not name any contribution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, my beloved brethren, be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, knowing that **your labour is not in vain** in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one command in the paragraph comes after the victory has been given, not before it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chain is broken.** *You are not under law but under grace* — Romans 6:14, and the promise attached to it is *sin shall not have dominion over you.* Paul puts the two in one sentence, in that order. See the note on Romans 6:12–14."}]}, "1 Corinthians 3:5–9": {"title": "Two men, one field, and the verb that settles the argument", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Corinthians had split into fan clubs — some for Paul, some for Apollos. Paul answers with farming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tenses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I planted, Apollos watered, but God is the One who made it grow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Greek the first two verbs are aorist — single, finished actions. Somebody turned up, did a job, and left. The third is imperfect: **kept on giving the growth**, continuous, still going."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument is in the grammar. Two men each did a discrete piece of work. One party has been doing the thing that actually matters, the whole time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Neither one is anything\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a hard sentence about himself, and Paul means it as a corrective to people building identities out of which preacher they preferred."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But he does not leave them worthless: *each will receive his own reward according to his own labour*. Not according to results. According to labour — which is the only part a planter or waterer controls."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *We are fellow workers who belong to God; you are God's field, God's building.* Paul flips the metaphor mid-sentence, from farm to construction, and he will run with the building for the next several verses. Both images have the same point: the workers are staff, and the property is somebody else's."}]}, "1 Corinthians 5:1–2": {"title": "A grief not yet felt", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul names a situation in one sentence and then turns, immediately, to the congregation's reaction to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being reported"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immorality of a kind **not found even among the nations around you** — that a man is living with his father's wife.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman law itself forbade this; Cicero calls a comparable case an almost unheard-of crime. Paul's measure is not the synagogue but the pagan city, and Corinth was not a strict place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His father's wife*, not *his mother* — the phrase indicates a stepmother, and Leviticus 18 names it directly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **you** are swollen with pride over it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is the one Paul has been using through the whole letter for the Corinthians' inflation — puffed up, blown out like a bellows. He has used it in chapter 4 twice already."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses in, and the subject has changed from the man to the church."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What should have been there instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Should you not rather have been **broken with grief**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is the one used of mourning the dead. Paul does not ask for outrage, disgust, or a statement. He asks for a funeral."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And notice the order in his sentence: *so that the man… would be taken out from among you.* The mourning comes first, and the action follows from it. A church that had grieved would have known what to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they were proud"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter's whole argument gives the answer. Corinth prized wisdom, freedom, and being beyond the reach of small rules. A man doing this and remaining in fellowship was, in that atmosphere, a demonstration of how free they were."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does not do.** He does not write to the man. The letter is addressed to the church, the pride is the church's, and the correction lands there. See the notes on 1 Corinthians 5:3–5 and 1 Corinthians 5:9–11."}]}, "1 Corinthians 5:3–5": {"title": "\"So that the spirit may be saved\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of Paul's severest paragraphs, and the last clause tells you what the whole thing is for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Present in spirit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though I am absent in body I am **present with you in spirit**, and I have already reached my judgment, as though I were standing there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same thing to the Colossians — *absent in the flesh, with you in spirit* — and there it is warm approval of a church holding its line. Here it is a verdict."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase does both, which suggests it means something more than a fond thought at a distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When you are gathered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, **when you are gathered together** — my spirit with you, and the power of our Lord Jesus Christ with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things are named as present at the meeting: the church, Paul's spirit, and *the power of our Lord Jesus Christ*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a gathering in the name, with the power, and Paul is careful to say so before he says what is to be done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Handed over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Such a one is to be handed over to Satan for the destruction of the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *handed over* is the same verb used of Jesus being delivered up. It means to place into someone's hands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The flesh** here is set against **the spirit** in the next clause, and Paul's usual use of *sarx* is not the body but the whole self as it operates apart from God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*…**so that the spirit may be saved** in the day of the Lord Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the sentence bends toward this. The severity is instrumental. Paul is not describing a punishment with an end in itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What actually happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If 2 Corinthians 2 refers to the same man — and most readers have taken it that way — the sequel is Paul urging the church to *forgive and comfort him*, in case he is *swallowed up with too much sorrow*, and to *reaffirm your love for him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The discipline worked, and Paul's next concern is that it not go too far."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is really about.** Not the offender. Paul's anger in verse 2 is at the congregation: *and you are proud!* A church that boasted of its tolerance is the target, and the sentence in verse 5 is the shock administered to it."}]}, "1 Corinthians 5:9–11": {"title": "The letter they misread", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul had written to Corinth before, and they had taken his instruction and turned it exactly the wrong way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The earlier letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I wrote to you in my letter not to keep company with people who are sexually immoral.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A letter we do not have. This is one of the places where the New Testament refers plainly to correspondence that was not kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did not mean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I did not mean the immoral of this world **at all** — otherwise you would have to leave the world altogether.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had read it as a rule about outsiders. Paul says the opposite was intended, and his reason is practical to the point of dryness: you would have to leave the planet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no instruction anywhere in Paul to withdraw from unbelievers. The separation he asks for runs in the other direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did mean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not to keep company with anyone **who is called a brother** and is…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The qualifier is the hinge of the paragraph. The list that follows applies inside the church, to someone claiming the name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six items, and they are not sorted by how shocking they are:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["sexually immoral", "**greedy**", "an idolater", "**someone who tears people down with his words**", "a drunkard", "a swindler"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "A reviler sits between an idolater and a drunkard. Paul puts the sins of the mouth and the wallet in the same sentence as the sin that started the chapter, without ranking them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Given that chapter 5 began with the church being *swollen with pride*, the inclusion of the reviler is not accidental."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not even to sit down and eat\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The table was where fellowship was expressed. In Corinth it was also where the Lord's Supper was eaten."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Withholding the meal is the specific action named — and read against chapter 11, where the rich shamed the poor at that same table, the instruction is not about keeping the meal pure. It is about not letting the meal say something untrue."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter ends.** *God judges those outside.* Paul draws the line explicitly: the church's business is the church. The verse most often used to police the world is, in its own context, a refusal to do that."}]}, "1 John 2:16–17": {"title": "The three cravings, and where they were first used", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John lists three things and says none of them come from the Father. The list is not arbitrary — it has been used before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the craving of the body**", "**the craving of the eyes**", "**the bragging about what a man has**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now set that beside Genesis 3, where Eve looks at the tree:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the fruit seemed **good to eat*** — the body", "***pleasing to the eye*** — the eyes", "***desirable for the wisdom it promised*** — the standing it would give her"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same three, in the same order, at the first temptation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And in the wilderness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three temptations of Christ run the same way. Turn the stones into bread — the body. All the kingdoms of the world shown to Him — the eyes. Throw Yourself down and let the angels catch You — the display, the proving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One test taken in a garden with everything available, and one in a desert with nothing. The first was failed and the second was not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John says about the shelf life"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The world is on its way out, and everything it makes you crave along with it. But whoever does what God wants stays forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is present continuous — the world **is passing**, in progress, already under way. He is not predicting an end so much as describing something currently draining away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is not between pleasure and misery. It is between what is going and what stays."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why it is not a list of forbidden things.** John does not name any object — no food, no possession, no specific pleasure. Every item on his list is an appetite pointed a particular direction. The problem he identifies is where the craving comes *from*, not what it happens to land on."}]}, "1 John 2:20–21": {"title": "\"There is an anointing on you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John is writing about people who had left the church claiming superior knowledge, and this is his answer to the readers who stayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chrisma** — an anointing, and the noun is built on the same root as **Christos**, the Anointed One. John is making the connection deliberately: the Anointed has anointed them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Israel the oil went on prophets, priests and kings. John says the ordinary believers reading his letter have it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you know all things.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is not a claim to omniscience — the next verse makes that clear: *I have not written to you because you do not know the truth, but because you **do** know it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not adding to their information. He is telling them they already have what the departing teachers claimed to be selling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses just before name the problem: people had gone out from among them, teaching that Jesus was not the Christ. The whole passage is about a congregation being told they were missing something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's response is not a counter-argument. It is: you have the anointing, and you know."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"No lie ever comes out of the truth\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "A short, blunt line, and it is the test he gives them. Truth does not generate lies. If a teaching leads away from what they already knew about Christ, its source is settled by that fact alone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it goes.** A few verses on: *the anointing you received from Him remains in you… as it has taught you, remain in Him.* John does not tell them to seek a fresh experience. He tells them what they have stays, and to stay in the One who gave it."}]}, "1 John 4:16": {"title": "\"We have put our weight on it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs about the love of God, and the second one is what makes the verse usable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Known, and trusted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We have come to know the love God has for us, and we have put our weight on it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are perfect tense — arrived at, and standing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first is knowledge: having come to see it. The second is the ordinary word for believing or trusting, and the Illumination renders it physically, which is what trust means when it is real. Not *we agree with it*. We have leaned on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A person can know something is load-bearing and still stand somewhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Love is what God is\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says it twice in this chapter, and it is the only sentence of its kind in Scripture — no other quality is described this way. Not *God is loving*, which would be an attribute among others."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else John says about God in this letter — light, truth, righteousness — is held together by it rather than competing with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Staying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever stays in love stays in God, and God stays in him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is **meno**, John's great word: abide, remain, stay put. It runs through his Gospel — abide in Me, and I in you — and all through this letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not describe an achievement. It describes not leaving."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it is for.** The next verse says the purpose: *so that we can stand bold on the day of judgment* — and then, *there is no fear in love; perfect love throws fear outside.* The knowing and the leaning are what put the fear out. See the note on 1 John 4:17."}]}, "1 John 4:17": {"title": "\"What He is, that is what we are, in this world\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the boldest sentences in the New Testament, and it is easy to read past because of how quietly John drops it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because what He is, that is what we are, **in this world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what we will be. Not what we are working toward. The tense is present and the location is named — here, now, in this world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not say we are like Him in some respects. He says *as He is, so are we*. The comparison is unqualified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is given as the reason for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that we can stand bold on the day of judgment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **parresia** — freedom of speech, the confidence of someone who has the right to be in the room. John uses it four times in this letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the ground of that boldness is not the reader's record. It is that their standing and Christ's are the same standing. If He is accepted, so are they — because it is the same acceptance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sentence starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is how love comes to full growth with us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole thing is about love being brought to completion. And the very next verse says what that maturity does: *there is no fear in love; perfect love throws fear outside.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sequence runs — love matured, boldness at judgment, fear thrown out. Not by working on the fear, but by knowing what you are in Christ."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why it is such a strong statement.** John has just spent the chapter saying God is love, that He sent His Son, and that we love because He first loved us. This verse is the conclusion of all of it: the love that came down did not stop at rescuing us. It made us, in this world, what He is."}]}, "1 John 4:19": {"title": "\"We love Him because He loved us first\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six words in Greek, and the argument of the whole letter is in the last one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Protos** — first, prior, before. It is doing all the work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John is not saying God loved us more, or better. He is saying He loved us **earlier** — before there was anything to respond to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the order matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read backwards, the verse becomes an instruction: love God, and He will love you. That is the reverse of what John spends the chapter arguing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In this is love, not that we loved God, but that He loved us and sent His Son.* He says it four verses earlier in almost the same words, ruling out the wrong order before he states the right one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it makes love"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A response. Not something worked up, and not a duty discharged — the reaction to something that already happened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why John can say elsewhere that His commands are not burdensome. A command to love, given to someone who has been loved first, is not the same weight as a command given cold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter has just been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is no fear in love; perfect love throws fear outside.* And: *what He is, that is what we are, in this world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This verse is the summary of both. The fear goes because the love was there before we arrived, and it did not wait to see how we would turn out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What John does with it next.** He goes straight to the brother you can see: *if anyone says \"I love God\" and hates his brother, he is a liar.* The love that came first is expected to run through and land on somebody. See the note on 1 John 4:16."}]}, "1 Kings 1:5–10": {"title": "\"His father had never once crossed him\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prince crowns himself, and the narrator stops mid-story to explain why."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His father had **never once crossed him**, never asked, \"**Why do you do this?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *never grieved him at any time by saying*. Not once, in his whole life, had David asked him a question about his conduct."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts it in as the explanation. It is not an aside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Adonijah began to **strut and swell** with ambition. \"**I will be king**,\" he told himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not consult David, who is still alive. He does not consult the prophet. He says it to himself and then acts on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He got himself **chariots and horsemen, and fifty men to run ahead of him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trappings first. He arranges the appearance of a king before there is anything behind it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he learned it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was a handsome man, **born just after Absalom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Absalom did exactly the same thing — *Absalom prepared chariots and horses, and fifty men to run before him* — and it ended with him hanging in an oak tree."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David watched one son do it and did not ask the next one a single question."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Absalom's rebellion also had a history. Amnon violated Tamar, and *when King David heard of all these things, he was very angry* — and did nothing. Two years later Absalom killed Amnon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three sons, one pattern, and the same silence behind each of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who joined him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joab, the commander of the army, and **Abiathar the priest***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The army and the priesthood. Adonijah had the two institutions that mattered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Zadok the priest, Benaiah, **Nathan the prophet**, Shimei, Rei, and David's mighty men.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nathan is the man who came to David after Bathsheba with *you are the man.* He is on the other side, and it is Nathan who goes to Bathsheba and gets Solomon crowned before the day is out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guest list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He invited all his royal brothers and all the king's men of Judah. **But he did not invite Nathan the prophet, or Benaiah, or the mighty men, or his brother Solomon.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He knew exactly who would say no. The omissions are a complete list of everyone with the standing to stop him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage suggests David should have been harsh with him. David was never harsh with anybody in his family, and that is the problem the verse names."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never asked. *Why do you do this?* — one question, and it was never put."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How it ends.** Solomon lets him live: *if he proves himself a worthy man, not one hair of his shall fall to the earth.* Adonijah then asks for Abishag, the girl who had nursed David — a claim on the throne by another route — and Solomon has him executed. The strutting outlives the reprieve."}]}, "1 Kings 12:6–11": {"title": "Two sets of advisers", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A new king asks the same question twice and gets two answers. The kingdom splits over which one he takes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you will be a servant to them today — if you answer them kindly — they will be your servants forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old men who had stood before Solomon give him a piece of counsel that is almost a paradox: serve them today and they are yours for life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It costs one conversation. The return they promise is permanent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My little finger is thicker than my father's waist… My father disciplined you with whips? I'll use whips with iron barbs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men he grew up with tell him to escalate. Their advice is not strategy; it is posture — a way of appearing strong to people who had asked for relief."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read what the people actually wanted. Solomon's building programme had been paid for in forced labour, and the request was *lighten the load your father put on us, and we will serve you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were not rebelling. They were offering loyalty in exchange for relief. Both sets of advisers understood that; they simply disagreed about whether to take the offer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rehoboam takes the harsher counsel and ten tribes walk away that day. The kingdom never reunites. Every king after this, north and south, lives inside the consequences of one afternoon's advice."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The detail about who he asked.** He went to the old men first — which was right — and then went and asked again. Getting counsel and taking counsel are two different acts, and a man who keeps asking until he hears what he wanted is not being advised. See the note on Proverbs 11:8–14 on many counsellors, and what the surrounding verses assume about their quality."}]}, "1 Kings 17:1": {"title": "\"The One I stand before\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elijah arrives in the narrative with no introduction — no father named, no calling scene, no commission recorded. One sentence and he is in front of the king."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As surely as the LORD lives — the God of Israel, **the One I stand before and serve**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his whole credential. Not *the prophet of Israel*, not a title or an office. A description of where he stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew *to stand before* is the language of a servant attending a king in his throne room — the phrase used of courtiers waiting on Solomon. Elijah is saying he is in attendance somewhere else, and speaking from there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about the weather"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There will be no dew and no rain in these years, not a drop, **until I say the word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things a farming nation could not live without, and the shutting of both is put on his word. Baal was the storm god — the one whose whole portfolio was rain and fertility."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the drought is not a general judgment. It is aimed precisely at the thing Baal was supposed to supply, in a country that had gone over to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the authority came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James is the one who tells us what happened before the sentence: *he prayed hard that the rain would stop, and it stopped.* The declaration in front of Ahab is the outcome of praying, not a substitute for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He prayed it first, then announced it, and then lived on a brook and ate what ravens brought him for the duration."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"Elijah\" means \"my God is the LORD.\"** In a kingdom that had spent a generation deciding otherwise, the man's own name is the argument. And on Carmel the nation shouts it back — *the LORD, He is God* — without necessarily noticing whose name they are saying."}]}, "1 Kings 17:8–16": {"title": "\"Make a little loaf for me first\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A starving widow with one meal left is asked to give it away before she eats it, and what happens next is one of the plainest pictures of provision in the Bible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A handful of flour in a jar and a little oil in a jug.* She is gathering sticks to bake it, and she says out loud what the plan is after that: *we'll eat it, and then we'll die.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not being dramatic. That is arithmetic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Make a little loaf for me **first**, and then make something for yourself and your son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is doing all the work. Not instead — first. The prophet is not asking her to go without; he is asking her to change the order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the request comes with *don't be afraid* in front of it, and a promise behind it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The jar of flour will not run out, and the jug of oil will not run dry, until the day the LORD sends rain.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a barn filled overnight. A jar that does not empty. The supply is daily, and it lasts as long as the drought does — provision measured to the length of the need."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So she went and did what Elijah said.* No argument recorded. A foreign widow in Sidon — Jezebel's own country, outside the covenant — takes an Israelite prophet's word and bakes the last of her flour for him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there was food every day.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who Jesus mentions.** Preaching in Nazareth, He says there were many widows in Israel in Elijah's day, and the prophet was sent to none of them — only to this one, in Sidon. It is one of the sentences that made His hometown want to throw Him off a cliff. See the note on Luke 4:16–21."}]}, "1 Kings 18:17–24": {"title": "\"How long will you go limping between two opinions?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three and a half years of drought end with a confrontation, and it opens with an accusation and a correction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who troubled Israel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So it's you — the man who's brought all this trouble on Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I haven't troubled Israel. You have.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahab blames the prophet who announced the drought. Elijah puts it back on the man whose house abandoned the commands and went after the Baals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word Ahab uses is a strong one — the same root used of Achan, whose theft brought disaster on the whole camp. Elijah does not soften it; he redirects it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question to the people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How long will you go limping between two opinions?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is the one used a few verses later of the prophets of Baal **dancing** around their altar. Israel is doing the same hop from foot to foot, and the word is chosen deliberately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had not rejected the LORD outright. They were keeping both, and Elijah's charge is that this is not a position at all — it is a limp."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people said nothing — not a word.* Nobody answers him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The terms of the test"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two bulls, two altars, no fire lit by anyone. *The God who answers by fire — He is God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a fair test and the crowd says so. Baal was the god of storm and lightning; fire from the sky was precisely his advertised speciality. Elijah lets them choose their bull first and gives them the whole morning."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"I am the only prophet of the LORD left.\"** It is how he sees it, and it is not quite true — Obadiah has a hundred hidden in caves, and the same chapter has just said so. God corrects the same thought a chapter later with the seven thousand who never bowed. Elijah's courage is real, and so is his sense of being alone in it."}]}, "1 Kings 18:30–39": {"title": "Twelve stones, four jars three times, and one prayer", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The contest on Carmel, and every detail Elijah arranges is aimed at leaving no other explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The twelve stones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took twelve stones, one for each of the tribes descended from Jacob — the man to whom the word of the LORD had come, \"Israel shall be your name.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The northern kingdom had been split from Judah for generations. Elijah rebuilds the altar as though the nation were whole, and the writer stops the narrative to mention the renaming at the Jabbok."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not addressing ten tribes. He is addressing Israel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four jars, poured over the offering and the wood. Then *do it again*. Then *a third time*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve jars in total, in the middle of a drought, on a mountain — water that must have been carried up. It fills the trench."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every drop is there to remove an excuse. No hidden coals, no dry tinder, no sleight of hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After the prophets of Baal had spent a day shouting, dancing and cutting themselves, Elijah prays about sixty words:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*Let it be known today that You are God in Israel, that I am Your servant, and that I have done all these things at Your word. Answer me, LORD, answer me, so these people will know that You, O LORD, are God — and that You are turning their hearts back to You.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three requests, and all three are about **them**: that they would know who God is, and that their hearts are being turned back. Nothing is asked for the prophet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the fire of the LORD fell. It consumed the offering, the wood, the stones, the very dust — and licked up all the water in the trench.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not just the sacrifice. The stones and the dust and the water — the fire takes everything, including the things fire does not normally take."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the people say.** *The LORD — He is God! The LORD — He is God!* In Hebrew that is *YHWH hu ha-Elohim*, twice. And it is the meaning of the prophet's own name — Eliyahu, *my God is the LORD*. The nation ends the day shouting his name back at him without knowing it."}]}, "1 Kings 18:41–46": {"title": "\"I hear the sound of a heavy rain\" — before there is a cloud", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elijah announces rain to the king before anything has changed in the sky, and then goes up the mountain to pray it in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go on up — eat and drink, for **I hear the sound of a heavy rain coming**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no rain. There has been none for three and a half years, and the sky is clear. He says he hears it anyway, tells the king to go and eat, and only then climbs Carmel to pray."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The announcement comes before the answer, and the praying comes after the announcement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He bent down to the ground and put his face between his knees.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a dignified stance. The man who had just called fire out of heaven in front of a nation folds himself onto the ground and stays there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Seven times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go look toward the sea\"… \"There's nothing\"… Seven times Elijah said, \"Go back and look again.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six times the report comes back empty. He does not adjust the announcement or conclude he misheard. He sends the servant again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what finally arrives is *a cloud as small as a man's hand* — the least impressive possible confirmation, and enough. He immediately sends word to Ahab to get down the mountain before the rain stops him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to him afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The power of the LORD came on Elijah — he tucked his cloak into his belt and ran ahead of Ahab's chariot all the way to Jezreel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roughly seventeen miles, on foot, ahead of a horse-drawn chariot, by a man who had been awake since before dawn and had fasted through a contest and a slaughter. The chapter records it in one sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was praying for.** God had already told him, at the start of the chapter, *I will send rain on the land.* The promise was given first; Elijah announced it, then prayed it down, then sent seven times for the evidence. The praying was not to persuade God of something. It was to bring in what had already been said."}]}, "1 Kings 3:5–14": {"title": "A listening heart, and everything he did not ask for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God offers a blank cheque, and the interest of the story is entirely in what Solomon does with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he actually asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The English usually says *an understanding heart*. The Hebrew is **lev shomea** — a **listening** heart. A heart that hears."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a striking thing for a king to request. Not brilliance, not judgement in the abstract, but the capacity to take in what is being said to him — by God and by the people he is about to govern."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he gets there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the run-up. He talks about David, then says two things about himself:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*I am only a child* — he was a grown man with a throne", "*I do not know how to go out or come in*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request comes out of an admission, and the admission comes before the ask. Then the reason: *who on his own could ever shepherd this great people of Yours?* He is not asking for wisdom to be impressive. He is asking because he has looked at the job and knows he cannot do it."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because you asked for this — not long life for yourself, not riches, not the death of your enemies…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God lists the things Solomon did not ask for, which tells you what the offer was understood to include. And then: *what you did not even ask for, I give you too.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The riches and honour arrive as an unrequested extra. They are not the reward for a clever request; they come because of what the request revealed about the man making it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conditional at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if you walk in My ways… I will give you long life as well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Worth reading precisely. The wisdom is given outright, no condition. The riches and honour are given outright. Only the long life carries an *if* — and Solomon's own story, which turns badly in his later years, is the reason that detail is in the text."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What this passage is not.** It is not a formula for getting more by asking for the right thing. God's pleasure is in what the request showed about Solomon's estimate of himself and the size of the task. The story records a man who knew he was out of his depth, and the whole answer follows from that."}]}, "1 Peter 1:13": {"title": "Where Peter tells them to put their hope", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This verse is the hinge of Peter's letter. Everything before it is what God has done; everything after it is how to live. And the joint between the two is a single instruction that most readings skip over to get to the commands that follow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three pictures, and only one of them is the command"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**get your mind stripped down and ready to move** — the Greek is one word, literally *gird up the loins of your mind*. A man in a long robe pulled it through his belt to run or work. Nobody had to explain it.", "**keep a clear head** — the word for not being drunk, used of staying alert.", "**pin your hope … on the kindness that arrives the day Jesus is revealed**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first two are participles. The third is the actual imperative. In Greek that distinction is unmistakable: the sentence is *hope*, and the other two describe the posture of someone doing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the command in this verse is not \"try harder\" or \"stay sober.\" It is **hope**, and hope aimed at one specific thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he tells them to hope in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in an improved version of themselves. Not in surviving the pressure they were under — and they were under real pressure; that is why the letter exists."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them to put their hope on *the kindness that arrives* — grace, being brought to them, at the revealing of Jesus Christ. It is spoken of as something in transit, on its way, arriving."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All of it, nothing held back\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is an adverb in the sentence — *teleios*, completely, without reserve. It can attach to either the clear head or the hoping, and translators split over it. Either way the force is the same: nothing partial. Peter is not asking for hope with a fallback plan behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a hard instruction for people being pressed, which is exactly who he is writing to. The alternative he is ruling out is not despair. It is hedging — hoping in grace while quietly keeping a second account open in your own effort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it comes exactly here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at what precedes it: an inheritance kept in heaven, a salvation ready to be revealed, prophets and angels leaning in to look at it. Peter spends twelve verses on what has been done and is coming. *So* — get your mind ready, and put your hope there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is not decorative. The commands in the rest of the letter are addressed to people who have already been told where to look."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the holiness that follows.** Three verses later comes *be holy, for I am holy*, and it is often quoted on its own as the letter's demand. In Peter's sentence it stands downstream of this one: hope fixed on grace first, and holiness described as what children of that Father look like."}]}, "1 Peter 1:4–5": {"title": "A fortune with your name on it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three Greek adjectives, all built the same way, describing what cannot happen to what is being kept for you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nothing can rot it, nothing can dirty it, nothing can drain the colour out of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each word in Greek begins with an *a-* prefix — the negative, like our *un-*:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**aphthartos** — not perishable. It cannot decay from inside.", "**amiantos** — not defiled. Nothing can spoil it from outside.", "**amarantos** — not fading. It will not lose its colour with time."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter stacks three negatives because he is ruling out every way anything has ever been lost: rot, contamination, and the slow bleaching of age."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third word is where we get *amaranth*, the flower the ancients said never withered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is put away in heaven, waiting for you.* The verb is perfect passive — already reserved, and standing reserved. Someone else did the putting away, and it is still there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And who is kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God's power stands guard over you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the second half, and it is the one people miss. The inheritance is guarded in heaven; the heir is guarded on earth. Peter uses a military word — garrisoned, protected as a city is protected."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both ends of the arrangement are held by someone other than the person inheriting."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who he was writing to.** Scattered believers about to face real persecution — the letter goes on at length about suffering. This is what he opens with: everything you were born into is out of reach of anything that is about to happen to you."}]}, "1 Peter 2:24": {"title": "\"He hauled our sins up onto the wood\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes Isaiah 53 and changes the tense, and the change is the whole point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He hauled our sins up onto the wood Himself, in His own body.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anaphero** — to carry up, to bear upward. It is the word used in the Greek Old Testament of a priest carrying a sacrifice **up** onto the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter, writing about a man nailed to a raised beam, chooses the altar word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In His own body\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is precise about the location of the transaction. Not in principle, not representatively — in a body, on a piece of wood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word he uses for the cross is *xylon*, timber, the same word the Greek Old Testament uses in the line about anyone hung on a tree being under a curse. Paul quotes that verse in Galatians for the same reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Isaiah, in the past tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You were healed by his wounds.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah wrote *we are healed* — a prophet describing something ahead of him. Peter writes **were**, aorist: done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the difference the cross makes to the sentence. Isaiah is looking forward; Peter is looking back at an accomplished thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that we would be dead to sin and alive for what is right.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The purpose clause is worth keeping attached. Peter does not present the bearing of sins as an end in itself but as the ground of a life lived differently — and the two verbs are both about a state, not an effort."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who he was writing to.** The paragraph is addressed to household slaves being treated unjustly, and the verses just before hold up Christ's response to suffering as the pattern: He did not retaliate, but *entrusted Himself to Him who judges justly.* The healing sentence arrives in a passage about people with no power, and Peter tells them what was carried for them."}]}, "1 Peter 2:4–5": {"title": "A house made out of people", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter, whose name means a stone, writes about stones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Keep coming to Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a present participle — continuous. Not *come once*; keep coming."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same posture the whole passage assumes: something already true, being returned to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stone that is alive"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is a stone, and **He is alive**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two words do not belong together. A stone is the standard image for what does not move, does not grow, and is not alive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter puts them side by side without explaining, and then does it again to his readers: *and you are stones too, **alive the way He is**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Rejected and chosen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People took one look and wanted nothing to do with Him — but God chose Him, and to God He is worth everything.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verdicts on the same stone, and Peter reports both. The builders' assessment was not a mistake about the stone's quality; it was simply theirs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is quoting Psalm 118 — *the stone the builders rejected has become the cornerstone* — the psalm sung at the close of Passover, on the night of the bread and the cup."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God is doing with the stones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God is **stacking you up** into a house He lives in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passive. The stones are not building anything; they are being built."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the house is not a place people visit. It is where He lives, and it is made out of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Priests carrying the Spirit's own sacrifices"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are **the priests in it**, set apart, and the sacrifices you carry in are the Spirit's own — and through Jesus Christ God is glad to take them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things worth slowing down on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Priests.** Not a class within the house. The stones and the priests are the same people. Under the old covenant one tribe served and everybody else waited outside a curtain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The Spirit's own.** The offerings are called *spiritual sacrifices* — they originate on the other side. The priest carries in what he was given to carry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through Jesus Christ God is glad to take them.* The acceptability is located in the phrase *through Jesus Christ*, and not in the quality of the offering."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The building materials.** No stone in this house was quarried square. Peter's readers were *scattered strangers*, refugees in five provinces, and he tells them they are load-bearing."}]}, "1 Peter 4:15": {"title": "The kind of suffering that is your own fault", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter is writing to Christians under real persecution, and he stops to draw one distinction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just make sure none of you is suffering for being **a murderer, a thief, a crook, or someone who cannot stay out of other people's business**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three crimes and then a fourth item that is not a crime at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind the last one is **allotriepiskopos** — a word that appears nowhere else in Greek literature, and Peter may have coined it. It is built from *belonging to another* and *overseer*: a person who supervises what is not theirs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is on that list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts a busybody beside a murderer and a thief. It is startling, and it is not a slip — the sentence is short and every item in it was chosen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Interfering in another person's affairs produces suffering, and Peter classes the suffering it produces with the suffering a thief brings on himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is separating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *if you are insulted for the name of Christ, you are blessed.* The verse after: *but if anyone suffers as a Christian, let him not be ashamed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So there are two kinds of trouble a believer can be in, and Peter will not let them be confused. One is because of Christ. The other is because of you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And a third kind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither category covers Job."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not suffering for the name, and he was not suffering for anything he had done — chapter 1 says so and God says so out loud. His friends spent twenty-five chapters insisting it had to be the second kind, because their system had no room for anything else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's distinction is real and it does not exhaust the possibilities. A man can be in the ashes for neither reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter tells them to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let those who suffer according to the will of God **commit their souls to Him in doing good, as to a faithful Creator**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *commit* is a banking word — to deposit something with someone for safekeeping. It is the verb Jesus used on the cross: *into Your hands I commit My spirit.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The name he gives God in that sentence.** *A faithful Creator* — the only place in the New Testament God is called that. Peter reaches for the word for the One who made the thing, writing to people whose lives are being taken apart."}]}, "1 Samuel 15:7–9": {"title": "What Saul kept", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Saul wins the battle and loses the kingdom, and the difference is a list of what he decided to keep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sorting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They kept the best of the sheep and cattle, the fat calves and lambs — everything that was good. These they were unwilling to destroy. But whatever was weak or worthless, they devoted to destruction.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He obeyed the command exactly, on everything he did not want."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator lays it out as a sorting exercise: good things kept, poor things destroyed. Obedience applied to the portion where obedience cost nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Agag"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took Agag, king of the Amalekites, alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king is the other thing kept. And that detail runs a long way — Haman, in the book of Esther, is called an Agagite, and he comes within a decree of destroying the Jewish people centuries later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What Saul kept alive turned up again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read on and it is the excuses that undo him. He tells Samuel he *has* performed the LORD's command; when the bleating of sheep gives him away, he says the people took them, and then that it was to sacrifice to the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel's answer is the line the chapter is remembered for: *to obey is better than sacrifice.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for the sparing.** *They were unwilling* — the Hebrew is about not being willing, refusing. It is not that Saul lacked the opportunity or the means. Everything in the sentence turns on what he wanted to keep."}]}, "1 Samuel 16:1–3": {"title": "\"I'll show you what to do\" — guidance given one step at a time", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Samuel is sent on the most consequential errand of his life and is not told how it ends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is given"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["go to Bethlehem", "to a man named Jesse", "I have already picked one of his sons"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, when Samuel raises a genuine danger — Saul will kill him if he hears — God does not rebuke the fear. He gives him something practical to do about it: take a heifer, say you have come to sacrifice, invite Jesse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is not given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A name. Samuel arrives knowing the family and not the son, which is why the scene that follows works the way it does — seven brothers paraded past, each one looking more like a king than the last, and the answer is a boy nobody thought to call in from the field."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I'll show you what to do.* The instruction is deliberately incomplete, and the missing piece is promised for when he gets there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that opens it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How long are you going to grieve over Saul? I have set him aside.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sounds harsh until you notice what it is not. Samuel's grief is real and God does not call it foolish — He calls time on it, and the reason is that a replacement has already been chosen. *I've already picked one of his sons.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the shape of the whole scene: the old arrangement has failed, the mourning is genuine, and the next thing is already decided before the prophet has filled his horn. Samuel is not being told to feel less. He is being told to fill the horn and go, because there is somewhere to go."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line that gets quoted from later in the chapter.** *People look at the outside; the LORD looks at the heart* — it arrives while Samuel is standing in front of the eldest brother, impressed. Even the prophet on the errand needed telling. The correction is not aimed at a crowd of shallow people; it is aimed at the man who had been sent, and who had already made up his mind."}]}, "1 Samuel 17:34–37": {"title": "The lion and the bear", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David's argument for being allowed to fight Goliath is not about courage. It is a work history."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he offers as his credentials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When a lion or a bear came and carried off a sheep from the flock, I went after it, struck it down, and rescued the sheep from its mouth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody saw this. There was no army watching, no king, no reward. A boy alone with sheep, going after a predator to recover one animal that belonged to his father."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the entire résumé, and it is enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he went after them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to prove anything. Because the sheep was his father's, and a shepherd who lets a lion keep it is not a shepherd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The detail is precise — *rescued the sheep from its mouth*, and when the animal turned on him, he took it by the fur and killed it. Whoever preserved this account preserved the specifics."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion he draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD who rescued me from the paw of the lion and the paw of the bear will rescue me from the hand of this Philistine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reasoning runs backwards from what has already happened. He is not claiming to be strong; he is naming who did the rescuing last time and expecting the same person to do it again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the change of word: paw, paw, and then **hand**. Goliath is put in the same category as the animals."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Saul says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go, and the LORD be with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king with no better plan sends a shepherd. And the thing that persuaded him was not a display of strength — it was an account of what God had already done where nobody was looking."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the hidden work goes.** Everything David uses in the valley he learned on the hills: the sling he had used on predators, the refusal of armour he had never worn, and the conviction that the LORD rescues. The lion and the bear are not preparation for the giant in some general sense. They are the reason he knows what he knows."}]}, "1 Samuel 17:45–47": {"title": "\"The battle is the LORD's\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A boy with a sling makes a speech before he makes a shot, and the speech is longer than the fight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two inventories"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You come at me with **sword and spear and javelin**, but I come at you **in the name of the LORD Almighty**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Goliath's equipment has been listed in detail earlier in the chapter — a bronze helmet, a coat of mail weighing five thousand shekels, bronze greaves, a bronze javelin, a spear whose shaft was like a weaver's beam, and a shield-bearer walking ahead of him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's list has one item on it, and it is a name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he calls God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD Almighty, **the God of the armies of Israel** — the very One you have defied.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**LORD of hosts** — Yahweh Sabaoth, the God of armies. The title is chosen for the occasion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the offence is relocated. Forty days of Goliath shouting at Israel, and David identifies who was actually being insulted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says before it happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This very day** the LORD will hand you over to me. **I'll strike you down and cut off your head.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past-tense certainty about a future event, spoken in the open, to the man's face, before a stone has left the pouch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes further — *the bodies of the whole Philistine army I'll give to the birds* — announcing not just a duel won but an army routed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So the whole earth will know there is a God in Israel.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated purpose is not David's survival or Israel's border. It is what everybody watching will conclude."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD does not save with sword or spear.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said by a man who is about to use a sling and a stone — which are also not nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not despising the weapon. He is naming what the weapon is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**For the battle is the LORD's**, and He will give all of you into our hands.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the battle belongs to the LORD*. It is a statement of ownership."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jehoshaphat is told the same thing in almost the same words centuries later: *the battle is not yours, but God's* — and he sends singers out in front of the army. See the note on 2 Chronicles 20:16–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had already told Saul"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lion and the bear. *Your servant has killed both lion and bear, and this uncircumcised Philistine will be like one of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had a record nobody had seen, built in a field with nobody watching, and it is what he was standing on."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David ran toward.** *When the Philistine arose and came and drew near to meet David, **David hurried and ran toward the army** to meet the Philistine.* He did not wait to be approached, and the text notes that he ran."}]}, "1 Samuel 17:48–51": {"title": "\"David broke into a run\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The whole fight takes one verse, and the detail worth noticing is the direction David is moving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He ran at him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As Goliath moved in to attack, David **broke into a run toward** the battle line to meet him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty days the army had stood still while the giant shouted. The first thing that changes is a shepherd running the wrong way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He closes the distance himself. A sling needs room, and he gives up room to shorten it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weapons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So David won with a sling and a stone. Without a sword in his hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator says it twice, which is how Hebrew underlines. He had refused Saul's armour a few verses earlier — it was untested and he could not walk in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sling was not a toy. Judges mentions seven hundred left-handed Benjaminites who could sling a stone at a hair and not miss. It was the ranged weapon of a man with no armour, and David had used it on lions and bears where nobody was watching. See the note on 1 Samuel 17:34–37."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forehead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He struck the Philistine on the forehead. The stone sank in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one place the champion was not covered. Verse 5 describes the bronze helmet, the coat of scale armour weighing about fifty kilos, greaves, a javelin — and a gap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sword"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then David ran and stood over him, drew Goliath's own sword from its sheath, and cut off his head with it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He finishes with the giant's own weapon. The thing everyone had been terrified of becomes the instrument of the ending."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David had already said.** *The battle is the LORD's, and He will give all of you into our hands.* He announced the outcome to Goliath's face before he picked up a stone, and the account records the running, the sling and the sword as the working out of a sentence already spoken."}]}, "1 Samuel 18:1–4": {"title": "The covenant Jonathan made, and what he took off to make it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The heir to the throne meets the man who will take it from him, and gives him his clothes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When David finished speaking with Saul.* The conversation immediately after Goliath — David is holding the giant's head, and he is a shepherd nobody had heard of that morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jonathan is the crown prince, a seasoned soldier who had won his own famous victory a few chapters earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he gave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took off the robe he was wearing and gave it to David, along with his tunic, and even his sword, his bow, and his belt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five items, and they are not gifts of affection. They are the marks of his position:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the robe** — the outer garment of a king's son", "**the tunic** — what was under it", "**sword, bow, belt** — his weapons and his rank"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He hands over the visible evidence of being next in line, to the man who is replacing him, on the day they meet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covenant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jonathan made a covenant with David, because he loved him as himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is Jonathan who initiates it. David is not recorded as asking, and has nothing to offer in return."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That covenant is the reason David goes looking for a survivor of Saul's house years later, and the reason Mephibosheth gets a seat at the table. See the note on 2 Samuel 9:5–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jonathan knew where this went. Later he says it plainly to David: *you will be king over Israel, and I will be second to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was never second to anyone. He died at Gilboa beside his father, and David wrote the lament."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for the knitting.** *Something knit Jonathan's soul to David's.* The Hebrew verb is used of tying or binding fast — the same root used later of Jonathan's love for him. Whatever else is in this friendship, the text describes it as a bond made rather than a feeling that arrived."}]}, "1 Samuel 6:19–21": {"title": "\"Who can stand before the LORD, this holy God?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The ark comes home after seven months among the Philistines, and the welcome ends badly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God struck down some of the men of Beth-shemesh because they looked into the ark of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They opened it. The lid — the mercy seat, the place God had said He would speak from — was lifted, and what was under it was the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers had laid down that even the Levites who carried the holy things were not to look at them, on pain of death. These were farmers in a wheat field who took the covering off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew reads oddly — seventy men and fifty thousand — and the figure has long been discussed, since Beth-shemesh was a small town. Some manuscripts and early witnesses have only the seventy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text as it stands records both, and the mourning that followed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who can stand before the LORD, this holy God? Where can the ark go from here?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the right question, asked too late. And their answer is to send it somewhere else — the ark goes on to Kiriath-jearim, where it stays twenty years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the chapter asks how a person might come near safely. They ask who will take it off their hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage is actually about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The uncovered law kills. That is the plainest reading of it, and it is the same point the New Testament makes in different words — *the letter kills, but the Spirit gives life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What made the ark approachable on the Day of Atonement was blood on the lid and smoke over it. Take the covering away and what is left is holiness with nothing between."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read with Exodus 25:17–22.** God had said He would meet them *above the mercy seat*. Not at the tablets — above them, over the covering. Beth-shemesh is what happens when the covering comes off, and it is why the arrangement was given in the first place."}]}, "1 Samuel 7:7–11": {"title": "A nursing lamb and the thunder", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An army is coming, and the one thing Israel does about it is kill a very small lamb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Samuel offered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Samuel took a **nursing lamb** and offered it **whole** as a burnt offering to the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a bull, not a ram, not the seven bullocks that a national emergency might seem to call for. A lamb still on its mother's milk — the smallest offering a man could bring."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *whole* — the Hebrew is the word for entire, offered complete, nothing kept back and nothing eaten."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While Samuel was offering the sacrifice, the Philistines drew near to attack.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator puts the two clauses in the same sentence on purpose. The enemy is closing while the lamb is on the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is armed. Nobody has taken a position. Israel is at worship with an army walking toward them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who fought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But that day the **LORD thundered** with a mighty voice against the Philistines and threw them into such confusion that they were routed before Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *confusion* is the panic-word — the same one used at the Red Sea and against the Canaanite kings under Joshua. It is a specific act of God recorded in a specific verb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel's part comes second: *the men of Israel rushed out… and chased them.* They pursue a battle that has already been won over an altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The small thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The size of the lamb and the size of the deliverance are not proportioned to each other, and the chapter does not apologise for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What the offering did was proclaim a death. Everything after the thunder is Israel walking into ground the sacrifice had already taken. See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:26–31 on what God chooses to confound the strong with."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Samuel set up afterwards.** A stone, named **Ebenezer** — *thus far the LORD has helped us.* A rock in a field, with no power in it at all, put there so that people walking past would have to remember what happened. The chapter ends with a memorial as small as the lamb it commemorates."}]}, "1 Samuel 8:1–3": {"title": "\"They were nothing like their father\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses that set up everything that follows — the demand for a king, the warning about what a king will do, and the whole monarchy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The decision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Samuel had grown old, and so he handed the reins of Israel to his sons.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the book of Judges established succession by inheritance. Judges were raised up individually; the office did not pass from father to son. Samuel makes his sons judges anyway."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text does not editorialise. It records the appointment and then records the result."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They chased after money, took bribes, and bent the truth to line their own pockets.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bribery and perverted justice are exactly what the law forbids of judges, in almost these words — Deuteronomy warns that a bribe blinds the eyes of the wise. Samuel's sons are doing the specific thing the office exists to prevent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The echo nobody in the story mentions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel had grown up in the house of Eli, whose own sons were corrupt, and whose failure to restrain them was the reason his house was cut off. Samuel himself delivered that message as a boy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he appoints his own sons, and they turn out the same way. The book puts the two households in parallel and never comments on it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it triggers.** The elders come to Samuel and ask for a king *like all the other nations*, and they cite his sons as the reason. Their complaint is legitimate. What they ask for as the remedy is the thing God says amounts to rejecting Him — and He tells Samuel to give it to them anyway, with a warning attached."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 4:13–15": {"title": "Grief with something left in it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The earliest surviving Christian letter, written to people who had a specific worry: friends of theirs had died before the Lord came back, and they did not know what that meant for them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not tell them to stop grieving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that your sorrow is not the sorrow of people who have nothing left to hope for.* The comparison is not between grief and no grief. It is between two kinds of grief — one with a floor under it, one without."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That distinction has held up better than any attempt to talk Christians out of mourning. Paul assumes they will weep. He is dealing with what they weep into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Fallen asleep\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses it three times in three verses. It was already a common euphemism, but in Christian use it acquired a specific edge, because sleep is the one state everybody expects to end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not being delicate about death — the next paragraph is trumpets and shouting and the dead rising. He is choosing a word whose whole force depends on waking up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument in one line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We believe that Jesus died and rose again; and in the same way, God will bring with Him those who have fallen asleep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything rests on the parallel. Not on a general hope of the soul's survival — on one event that happened, and the claim that the same thing happens to those who belong to Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The anxiety he is answering.** *Those of us who are still alive… will not go on ahead of the ones who have fallen asleep.* They seem to have feared their dead would miss it, or come second. Paul's correction is almost blunt: the dead in Christ are not at a disadvantage. If anything, they go first."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 4:16–18": {"title": "What the passage is actually for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the most argued-over paragraph about the Lord's return, and Paul ends it with an instruction that tells you what he wrote it for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three sounds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With a shout, with the voice of the archangel, with the trumpet of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing hidden or quiet. The word for shout is a **command** shouted aloud — used of an officer to troops, or a captain to rowers. Whatever else this is, it is not secret."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Caught up\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **harpazo** means to seize, to snatch away by force. It is used of the Spirit carrying Philip off, and of a child snatched up to God in Revelation. The Latin translation of it, *rapturo*, is where the English word rapture comes from — which is why that word is not in most English Bibles: it came in through Latin, not from a missing Greek term."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is not a gentle ascent. It is being grabbed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order Paul gives"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the Lord comes down", "the dead in Christ rise **first**", "then the living are caught up **together with them**", "and so we will be with the Lord always"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is still answering the question from the verses before: their dead are not left behind. The sequence exists to settle that worry, and *together* is the word carrying it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line is the point.** *Comfort one another with these words.* Not \"chart these events,\" not \"work out the timing.\" Paul wrote this to be said to grieving people, and the destination of the whole passage is the plainest thing in it: *we will be with the Lord always.* Everything else in the paragraph is the transport."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 5:9–11": {"title": "\"God never marked us out for wrath\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent a chapter and a half on the Lord's return, and this is the conclusion he draws from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appointment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God never marked us out for wrath. He marked us out to come into salvation through our Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is about setting or appointing — placing someone in a category. Paul denies one appointment and states another, and both are God's doing rather than the reader's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Thessalonians had been anxious. The letter has already reassured them about their dead; now it settles the other question, which is what is coming for the living."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Awake or asleep\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that awake or asleep, we are alive with Him, and alive together.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has used *asleep* for the Christian dead all through chapter 4. Here he uses both words in one clause and says the outcome is identical."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether the Lord finds a believer alive or already buried makes no difference to the result — *alive with Him, and alive together.* It is the same reassurance he gave earlier, compressed to a phrase."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he tells them to do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So keep encouraging one another, and keep building one another up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The practical instruction attached to the second coming is not preparation, not calculation, and not vigilance about dates. It is to speak to each other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said the same at the end of chapter 4 — *comfort one another with these words*. Twice, and both times the teaching about the return is handed over as material for encouraging somebody else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last clause.** *Which is exactly what you are already doing.* Paul adds it almost in passing, and it is characteristic — he tells a church to do the thing and then notes that they are doing it. The instruction arrives as confirmation rather than correction."}]}, "1 Timothy 4:14–16": {"title": "What \"meditate\" meant, and what \"saved\" covers", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Take these things to heart, our text says — and that is the right feel for it. Underneath sits a Greek word with more packed into it than any one English phrase can carry, so here is what is in there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "It is the Psalm 1 word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word is meletao. It is the word the Greek Old Testament uses in Psalm 1 — the man whose delight is in the law, and who meditates in it day and night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And look at what Paul said two verses earlier: give your attention to the reading of the Scriptures aloud. Then he says this. He is telling a young minister to do with these things what the blessed man of Psalm 1 does with the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "It is not emptying your mind. It is filling it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Behind the Greek stands a Hebrew word, hagah. It does not mean sitting still and going blank. It means muttering, murmuring, going over something under your breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same Hebrew word gets used for a lion growling over its food, and for a dove moaning. It is a noise word. Biblical meditation is closer to chewing something over out loud than it is to silence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And it also means practice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In ordinary Greek, meletao is what a public speaker does when he drills a speech — going over it until it is in him and he no longer has to reach for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So both are in the one word: turn it over, and let it become yours. Say it until it lives in you."}, {"k": "note", "t": "One thing not to push too far. The Greek word for meditate does cover quiet reflection as well — the noisy, muttering sense comes from the Hebrew word behind the Psalm, not from the Greek on its own. The claim that holds is this: biblical meditation means filling your mind with something and going over it, not emptying your mind. That much is solid, and it is enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why \"profiting\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is prokope. It comes from a verb meaning to cut forward — the way someone hacks a path through undergrowth ahead of everyone else. It is not money quietly accumulating. It is ground gained."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is saying: stay in this, and people will be able to see the path opening up in front of you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And now the word this block ends on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at how it finishes: you will be the saving of yourself and of everyone who listens to you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is sozo."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sozo means to save, to rescue, to heal, to make whole, to preserve, to bring safely through. Complete salvation and complete deliverance — in any and every sense a person could need it.** That is not a devotional stretch. That is the size of the word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In English, saved has narrowed until it means one thing: getting out of hell. The Greek word never narrowed like that, and it never had to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "A picture of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Think of a man pulled out of the sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The English word stops at the moment he stops sinking. He did not drown. That is saved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But the rescue is not finished there, and no one who has actually been rescued thinks it is. He is hauled over the side. The water is got out of him. Somebody wraps him in something dry, puts hot food in his hands, and sits with him until he stops shaking. He is carried to shore. He is taken home. In the morning he wakes up in his own bed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sozo is the whole of that. Not the instant he stopped going under — everything from the water to the front door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same word does all of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word the disciples shout when the boat is going down in the storm and they wake Jesus: save us, we are perishing. Nobody in that boat is thinking about the afterlife. They mean get us out of this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word used when a blind beggar receives his sight. It is the word used of the woman who had been ill for twelve years, when she touches Him and the bleeding stops. It is the word James uses for a sick man raised up when the church prays over him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is the word for the rescue of a life, finally and for ever."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word, wide enough to hold all of it — because what God does with a person was never divided into compartments in the first place."}]}, "2 Chronicles 18:1–3": {"title": "\"I am as you are\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A good king ties himself to a bad one, and the chapter opens by telling you exactly how it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now Jehoshaphat had great riches and honour, **and he joined himself to Ahab by marriage**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prosperity and the alliance are in the same breath. Jehoshaphat was one of Judah's better kings — the previous chapter has him removing idols and sending teachers out with the law through every town."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The marriage was almost certainly a sensible piece of statecraft: his son to Ahab's daughter, two kingdoms at peace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The visit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some years later he went down to visit Ahab in Samaria. Ahab slaughtered many sheep and cattle for him… and he urged him to march up against Ramoth Gilead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *urged* is a strong word — enticed, persuaded, worked on him. The banquet came first and the request came after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am as you are, and my people as your people; we will join you in the war.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He agrees before asking a single question. Only afterwards does he think to say *first seek the counsel of the LORD* — and when four hundred prophets say go, and one says otherwise, and Ahab admits he hates that one for never prophesying anything good, Jehoshaphat still goes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had already given his word at the dinner."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How it ends for him.** He survives, but a prophet meets him on the way home: *should you help the wicked and love those who hate the LORD?* And years later the marriage he made brings Ahab's daughter Athaliah into Judah's royal house, where she murders almost the entire line of David. The alliance outlived the battle by a generation."}]}, "2 Chronicles 18:28–34": {"title": "\"Jehoshaphat cried out, and the LORD helped him\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two kings go into a battle they were warned about. One of them has a plan, and it involves the other one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Ahab's arrangement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will disguise myself and go into battle, but you wear your royal robes.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks his ally to stay identifiable while he becomes invisible. And the reader already knows what Ahab does not: the king of Aram has ordered his chariot commanders to fight nobody *except the king of Israel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the disguise sends the entire enemy strike force at the man in the robes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to the man in the robes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the chariot commanders saw Jehoshaphat, they thought, \"This is the king of Israel,\" and they turned to attack him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is surrounded, in the wrong battle, wearing the wrong clothes, because he agreed to an alliance at a banquet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Jehoshaphat cried out, and the LORD helped him. God drew them away from him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three short clauses. He was in a mess entirely of his own making, in a war he had been told not to enter, and when he shouted, God moved the army off him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No rebuke recorded at that moment. No conditions. He cried out and was helped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But someone drew his bow at random and struck the king of Israel between the sections of his armour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shot fired at nobody in particular, finding the one gap in a disguised man's armour. Everything Ahab had arranged to make himself safe was undone by an arrow with no aim."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chronicles reports it without comment: *at random*, and then a king propped up in his chariot until sunset, dying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Two men, one battle.** Both were where they should not have been. One called out and was pulled clear; the other had a plan and did not survive the afternoon. The chapter puts the two outcomes side by side and leaves them there."}]}, "2 Chronicles 20:16–19": {"title": "Singers in front of the army", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three armies are coming and Judah is told the battle is not theirs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moab, Ammon and Mount Seir, marching on Jehoshaphat together. His own prayer sums it up: *we have no power against this great multitude that is coming against us, nor do we know what to do — but our eyes are on You.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He admits all three: no strength, no plan, no idea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will not even need to fight this battle.** Take your positions, stand still, and watch the salvation of the LORD on your behalf.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same instruction Moses gave at the Red Sea, word for word in places: *stand still and see the salvation of the LORD.* See the note on Exodus 14:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three commands, and all three are about stopping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And yet they go out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Tomorrow, **go out to face them**, and the LORD will be with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not told to stay home. They have to march to the battlefield of a battle they will not fight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jehoshaphat bowed with his face to the ground, and all Judah and the people of Jerusalem fell down before the LORD to worship Him. Then the Levites… **stood up to praise the LORD with a very loud voice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole nation on the floor, and then a choir at full volume."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of march"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next morning Jehoshaphat did something no commander does. *He appointed those who should sing to the LORD, and who should praise the beauty of holiness, **as they went out before the army**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Singers in front. Unarmed men, out ahead of the soldiers, in the tribe whose name means praise. See the note on Genesis 49:8–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they sang"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Praise the LORD, for His mercy endures forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six words. It is the refrain of Psalm 136, repeated twenty-six times in that psalm, and it is what Judah took into a valley with three armies in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the enemy. Nothing about the danger. One line about His mercy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **when they began to sing and to praise**, the LORD set ambushes against the people of Ammon, Moab and Mount Seir, and they were defeated.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "When they began. The timing is in the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the time Judah reached the lookout point there was nothing standing. It took them three days to carry away what was left."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the valley was renamed.** *Beracah* — blessing. They assembled there on the fourth day to bless the LORD, and the name stuck. A place named for what happened in it, by people who had not raised a weapon."}]}, "2 Chronicles 26:1–5": {"title": "\"As long as he sought the LORD\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Fifty-two years on the throne, and the chronicler puts the whole reign under one condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sixteen years old"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a boy when they make him king. His father Amaziah had been murdered by conspirators, and the people take the son and put him on the throne."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in his position was earned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man beside him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He set himself to seek God **in the days of Zechariah, who taught him to fear God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the prophet who wrote the book — an otherwise unknown man, named here only, and named because Uzziah's seeking is tied to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A young king with someone teaching him. The chronicler thought that worth recording."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **as long as he sought the LORD**, God gave him success.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *gave him success* is to make prosper, to cause to advance — the same verb used of Joseph in Potiphar's house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter goes on to list what that looked like: victories over the Philistines, tribute from the Ammonites, towers built in the desert, cisterns dug, vineyards in the hills, an army of over three hundred thousand, and engines of war on the walls of Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And his fame spread far, for he was marvellously helped, till he was strong.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Till he was strong\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "That clause is the pivot of the whole chapter, and it is doing two jobs. It marks how far the help went — and it marks where the trouble starts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *but when he had grown strong, his heart became proud, and it led to his ruin.* See the note on 2 Chronicles 26:16–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What lifted him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the east, not the west, not the desert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is God who judges. He brings one down and lifts another up.* The psalm says promotion has one source, and this chapter is a fifty-two-year illustration of it — including the end. See the note on Psalms 75:6–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter's phrase lands elsewhere in Scripture.** *In the year that King Uzziah died, I saw the Lord.* Isaiah dates his vision of the throne by the death of this man — the king who went into the sanctuary he had no business in, and the prophet who saw the true throne the year that king was buried."}]}, "2 Chronicles 26:16–18": {"title": "Strength turned to pride", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sentence that ends a fifty-two-year reign is eleven words long."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hinge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **when he had grown strong**, his heart became proud, and it led to his ruin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The strength was God's doing. The chapter has just said so twice — *God gave him success*, *he was marvellously helped, till he was strong.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the strength is what he stumbled over. The Hebrew for *led to his ruin* is to corrupt, to spoil — the same root used of the earth being corrupt before the flood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not stop being helped and then get proud. He got proud because of how far the help had gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What pride made him do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went into the temple of the LORD **to burn incense** on the altar of incense.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not idolatry. Not a foreign god. He walks into the house of the LORD to worship the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offence is not what he did. It is who he was when he did it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line he crossed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is **not for you**, Uzziah, to burn incense to the LORD, but **for the priests, the sons of Aaron**, who are consecrated to do so.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two offices, and God had kept them apart the whole way through Israel's history. Saul lost the kingdom for sacrificing; the sons of Korah died for claiming the censer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Uzziah had been given a throne, an army, towers, cisterns and international fame. What he had not been given was a censer, and that is the one thing he reached for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eighty men"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Azariah the priest went in after him, and with him **eighty priests of the LORD, courageous men**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler calls them courageous, and they needed to be. They walked into the sanctuary to stand in front of a king at the height of his power and tell him to leave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they told him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Leave the sanctuary, for you have been unfaithful, and **there will be no honour for you from the LORD God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had come in to add to his honour. He is told, standing there with the censer in his hand, that this will bring him none."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Only one man ever held both offices.** A king on a throne who is also a priest — Melchizedek in Genesis, and then the promise in Zechariah, *He shall be a priest upon His throne.* Uzziah reached for it and was struck. It belonged to Somebody. See the note on 2 Chronicles 26:19–21."}]}, "2 Chronicles 26:19–21": {"title": "The leprosy at the altar", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The censer is still in his hand when it happens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Uzziah **flew into a rage**. The censer was in his hand… and **while he raged at the priests**, leprosy broke out on his forehead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is corrected and his answer is fury. The chronicler puts the anger and the leprosy in the same sentence, in that order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it appeared"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Leprosy **on his forehead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The forehead is where the high priest wore the gold plate engraved *HOLINESS TO THE LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man standing where only the priest could stand, and the mark comes out on the one place the priest's holiness was displayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he was standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There before the priests, in the house of the LORD, **beside the altar of incense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The location is spelled out. Not later, not at home, not on the way out. At the altar, in the middle of the act."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two directions at once"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **rushed him out**, and **he himself hurried to leave**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priests were pushing and he was already going. A leper could not remain in that house and he knew it before anyone had to explain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because the LORD had struck him.* The chronicler names who did it and does not soften it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So King Uzziah was a leper **until the day he died**. He lived in a separate house, a leper, **cut off from the house of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifty-two years on the throne and the last of them spent in isolation, with his son running the kingdom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cut off from the house of the LORD* — the one place he had forced his way into is the one place he can never enter again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of the reign"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*as long as he sought the LORD, God gave him success*", "*he was marvellously helped, till he was strong*", "*when he had grown strong, his heart became proud*", "*the LORD had struck him*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sought, helped, strong, proud, struck. The chronicler lays it out in order so nobody can miss which step led to which."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Isaiah saw the year this man died.** *In the year that King Uzziah died, I saw the Lord, high and lifted up, and His train filled the temple.* A king who forced his way into the sanctuary is buried, and a prophet is shown the One whose throne it actually is — with a live coal from the altar carried to his mouth by a seraph, because no one takes it for himself."}]}, "2 Corinthians 1:15–20": {"title": "Where the Amen actually goes", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the verse this app opens on, and it is usually quoted from halfway through. The half that gets left off is the reason Paul wrote it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He is defending a change of travel plans"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is genuinely what starts it. Paul said he would visit, then did not, and somebody in Corinth used it to suggest he was unreliable — a man whose yes meant nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His answer runs: *do I make my plans the way anyone makes them, so that with me Yes, yes and No, no come out of the same mouth in the same breath?* Then, instead of defending his diary, he moves straight to the character of the message he preached. My travel arrangements are not the point, he says. What we brought you was never *Yes and No at once*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after this — the most quoted sentence in the letter — is Paul arguing from God's consistency, not his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"However many promises God has made\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is deliberately open-ended. Not *some*. Not *the important ones*. However many there are, they all find their Yes in one place: **in Him.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which locates them. The promises are not free-floating guarantees attached to circumstances, and they are not conditional on the reader's steadiness — the whole argument began with Paul's own unsteadiness. They are held in Christ, and that is why they can be described with a word as flat as *always*."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the Amen ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here is the part almost always dropped:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*and in Him the Amen, spoken through us to the glory of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amen is a Hebrew word meaning firm, settled, so be it. It is what a congregation says back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's picture has both halves: God says Yes in Christ, and the Amen comes back *through us*. The promises are answered by God, and then answered again by the people who trust them. Nobody has to generate the Yes. What is left for a person to do is agree with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a very different act from working to secure the promise, and it is why the sentence ends where it does — *to the glory of God*. The praise goes to the one who said Yes, not to the one who managed to believe it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it matters that this began with a broken plan"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's point lands harder because of where it started. A man whose word had just been questioned uses the moment to say: whatever you think of my reliability, God's Yes does not run on the same terms."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"a second helping of grace.\"** Paul's phrase for the visit he had planned. He thought of turning up in person as another portion of grace for them. It is a small thing, but it is the vocabulary of a man who thought of grace as something you get more of, not something you use up."}]}, "2 Corinthians 10:3–6": {"title": "The fortresses are arguments", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Military language from start to finish, and Paul names the target so there is no guessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is defending himself against critics at Corinth who said his letters were weighty but his presence was unimpressive. The whole chapter is about that quarrel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the warfare he describes is not abstract spiritual combat. It is what he is doing in the letter — dealing with what people were saying and thinking about the gospel and about him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What gets pulled down"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We pull down **arguments**, and every high thing that lifts itself up against the knowledge of God.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**arguments** — reasonings, calculations, the conclusions people reach", "**every high thing** — the word for a rampart or a proud elevation, something raised up"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fortresses in this passage are made of ideas. That is what the weapons are for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Take every thought prisoner\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image completes the siege: the walls come down, and then the occupants are led out as captives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And bring it into the obedience of Christ.* The destination is not destruction — it is submission. A captured thought is brought somewhere and put to work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weapons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not the weapons of the flesh at all; through God they have power to pull down fortresses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not list them here, and the omission is deliberate; the point is what they are not. In Ephesians he does list them, and the offensive one is the word of God."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse people skip.** *We are holding ourselves ready to punish every act of disobedience, once your own obedience has been brought to the full.* Paul deals with his own house first. The order matters — the readiness to address others waits on the readiness of the people he is writing to."}]}, "2 Corinthians 12:1–4": {"title": "Visions and revelations", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is being forced into boasting by people in Corinth who boast well, and he tells the greatest thing that ever happened to him in the third person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I know a man in Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is talking about himself. He says so a few verses later — *lest I should be exalted above measure by the abundance of the revelations* — but he will not put it in the first person while he is describing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The distance in the grammar is the point of the paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Fourteen years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fourteen years ago.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had not mentioned it. Not in Galatians, not in Romans, not in either Thessalonian letter. Fourteen years of preaching and letter-writing and never once bringing it up, and it comes out now only because the Corinthians have pushed him into a boasting contest he did not want."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"God knows\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whether he was in the body I do not know, or out of the body I do not know; **God knows**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice. The most remarkable experience in his life, and the thing he insists on is what he is not able to tell you about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third heaven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Paradise** in the second sentence appears to be the same place named twice — Paul restates the vision rather than describing a second stage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is Persian in origin: a walled garden, a royal park. The Greek Old Testament uses it for Eden, and Jesus uses it to the thief on the cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Words beyond all speaking, words that no human being is permitted to say aloud.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **arrheta** — unspoken, unspeakable — and then a second clause: it is *not permitted*. Both that they cannot be put into words and that he is not allowed to try."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the content is withheld. Whatever Paul was shown, the church did not get it here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did pass on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He kept that to himself for fourteen years. What he did hand over, he introduces in the other letter with the same claim to have got it directly: *I received from the Lord what I also delivered to you* — the bread and the cup."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of everything he was given, that is the piece he made sure everybody had. See the note on 1 Corinthians 11:23–26."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes immediately after.** The thorn. *Lest I should be exalted above measure.* Paul places the highest experience and the standing affliction in the same paragraph, and the sentence that ties them together is *My grace is sufficient for you.*"}]}, "2 Corinthians 3:16–18": {"title": "From glory to glory", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of Paul's long comparison between the two covenants, and it finishes with a mirror."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The veil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But whenever anyone **turns to the Lord, the veil is taken away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent the chapter on Moses coming down the mountain with his face shining, and putting a veil over it — and Paul says why, which Exodus does not: *so that the children of Israel could not look steadily at the end of what was passing away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The glory was fading, and the veil hid the fading."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The removal is not something the reader performs. *Whenever anyone turns.* The veil comes off in the turning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Freedom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now the Lord is the Spirit, and where the Spirit of the Lord is, **there is freedom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eleutheria** — liberty, the state of a free person as against a slave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mirror"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of us, **with faces unveiled, gazing at the glory of the Lord as though into a mirror**, are being changed into that same likeness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things at once. Looking at Him, and seeing it in a mirror."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had to veil his face because of what looking at God had done to it. Paul says every face is unveiled now, and what you see when you look is being reproduced in you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are **being changed** into that same likeness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metamorphoo** — the word behind metamorphosis, and the word Matthew and Mark use of the transfiguration."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is passive, and it is present continuous. Something is being done to you, and it is not finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the change comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **all of it comes from the Spirit of the Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul closes the sentence by removing the reader from the mechanism entirely. Not by effort, not by discipline, not by resolve."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You look, and you are changed, and the changing is His work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From glory to glory\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from failure to glory. From one degree of it to the next."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *from glory into glory* — a movement between two of the same thing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the beholding is of.** Not yourself in the mirror. *The glory of the Lord.* The chapter after this says where that glory is located: *the light of the knowledge of the glory of God **in the face of Jesus Christ**.*"}]}, "2 Corinthians 3:4–6": {"title": "\"The letter kills\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul disqualifies himself twice before saying what he is qualified for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he denies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not that we are **equal to anything in ourselves**, as though we could claim any of it as coming from us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hikanos** — sufficient, adequate, up to the job."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing to a church that has been comparing him unfavourably with more impressive teachers, and his answer is to concede the point entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what he affirms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever we are equal to **comes from God**, who has made us equal to the work of serving a new covenant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same word, three times in two verses. Not sufficient — sufficiency is from God — made sufficient."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two covenants"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not of the letter but of the Spirit. For **the letter kills, and the Spirit gives life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The letter* is **gramma** — the written character, the thing engraved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What follows in the chapter identifies exactly what he means: *the ministry of death, **written and engraved on stones**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only one thing in Scripture was written and engraved on stones. Ten commandments, by the finger of God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two ministries, compared"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does the comparison himself over the next few verses, and he is not shy about it:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the ministry of **death**… was glorious* — glorious, and he calls it that", "*the ministry of the **Spirit** is more glorious*", "*the ministry of **condemnation** had glory*", "*the ministry of **righteousness** exceeds much more in glory*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never says the first was bad. He says it had glory — Moses' face shone from it. And then he says the glory was *passing away*, and that Moses veiled his face so nobody would watch it fade."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day it was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses came down the mountain with the tablets and three thousand men died at the hands of the Levites. See the note on Exodus 32:25–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at Pentecost, when the Spirit came, Peter preached and *about three thousand souls were added to them.* See the note on Acts 2:40–41."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two mountains, two givings, and the same number counted on each day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not permit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not arguing that behaviour stops mattering. He is arguing about what produces it, and Galatians answers the question directly: *walk in the Spirit, and you shall not fulfil the lust of the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fruit list ends with *against such there is no law* — nothing on it needs legislating. See the note on Galatians 5:22–24."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is engraved now.** *You are our epistle, written in our hearts, known and read by all men… written not with ink but by the Spirit of the living God, not on tablets of stone but on tablets of flesh, that is, of the heart.* Paul says it two verses before this one. Same finger, different surface."}]}, "2 Corinthians 4:13–15": {"title": "The same spirit of faith", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul names something he shares with a psalmist who lived a thousand years before him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We have **the same spirit of faith** as the one who wrote, \"**I believed, and so I spoke**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is quoting Psalm 116. And he does not say *the same faith* — he says the same **spirit** of faith, as though it were a disposition, a way of operating, that both men were working out of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Believed**, then **spoke**. The speaking comes out of the believing, not the other way round."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We too believe, and so we speak.* Paul repeats the pattern about himself in the same sentence, which is the whole point of the quotation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalmist was in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 116 is not a victory song written afterwards. Read the line Paul is quoting in its own setting:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I believed, even when I said, \"**I am badly shaken**.\" In my alarm I said, \"Every person is a liar.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He believed and he said he was shaken, in one breath. The psalm has him with *the pains of death* around him and *the sorrows of Sheol*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the spirit of faith Paul is claiming is not one that denies the state it is in. The psalmist says both things — I am badly shaken, and I believed. See the note on Psalms 116:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul was in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same chapter, a few verses earlier: *hard pressed on every side… perplexed… persecuted… struck down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And his own list later in the letter: beaten five times with thirty-nine lashes, three times with rods, once stoned, three shipwrecks, a night and a day in the open sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the man saying *we too believe, and so we speak.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he believes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that **the One who raised up the Lord Jesus will raise us up as well**, and stand us in His presence together with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The content of the faith is a resurrection — one that has happened, and one that will."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's confidence is not about the next few weeks. It reaches past the outcome of whatever is currently pressing on him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of it is **for your sake**, so that the grace reaching more and more people may make **thanksgiving overflow**, to the glory of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He turns even his own beatings outward. The chapter has already said it: *death is working in us, but life in you.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verses on either side.** Before: *we have this treasure in earthen vessels, that the excellence of the power may be of God and not of us.* After: *though our outward man is perishing, yet the inward man is being renewed day by day.* The spirit of faith sits between a clay pot and a body wearing out, and speaks anyway."}]}, "2 Corinthians 4:16–18": {"title": "The scales Paul is using", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*This trouble of ours is light, and it lasts a moment.* Coming from most people that sentence would be worthless. Two chapters later Paul lists what he means by trouble: beatings, imprisonments, shipwreck, a night and a day in open water, hunger, cold, and being let down a wall in a basket to escape assassination."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has earned the sentence, which is the only reason it can be read."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two pairs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage runs on opposites, and they are stacked:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**outer person** wearing away — **inner person** being made new, *day after day*", "**light** and **a moment** — **weight** and **endless**", "**what can be seen** — **what cannot**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The middle pair is a deliberate joke in Hebrew terms. The word for glory, *kavod*, comes from a root meaning heavy. Paul sets a light affliction against a weight of glory: the very thing that feels heaviest is the lighter side of the scale."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Day after day\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The renewal is not described as a single experience but as something happening on a daily basis, in parallel with the wearing away. Both processes run at once, and Paul does not pretend the first one stops."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That honesty is what makes the paragraph usable by someone in a hospital chair. Nothing here says the outer wearing away is imaginary or will reverse."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"We keep our eyes not on what can be seen.\"** The verb is about aiming attention deliberately — the word behind our *scope*. It is not denial of the visible; Paul has just catalogued it. It is a decision about which set of facts gets to set the terms."}]}, "2 Corinthians 5:16–17": {"title": "A whole new creation", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul stops assessing people by what can be seen, and gives the reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The new measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From this moment on we **do not size anyone up by what the flesh can see**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *according to the flesh* — by outward appearance, by natural reckoning: background, record, credentials, family, what a man looks like he is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Even about Christ"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even if we did once size up Christ that way, **we do not know Him that way any longer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had. He had assessed Jesus of Nazareth by the visible facts — a carpenter's son from a bad village, executed as a criminal — and drawn the obvious conclusion, and gone to Damascus with letters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is admitting his own worst misjudgement as the example."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So if anyone is in Christ — **a whole new creation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no verb in the Greek. It reads *if anyone in Christ, new creation* — the words land without a connective, which is why translators supply one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ktisis** is the creation word. Paul does not say improved, reformed, forgiven, or repaired. New — **kainos**, new in kind rather than merely recent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The old things **are gone**; look, all things have **become new**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are gone* is a completed action, standing done. *Have become new* likewise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is in progress and neither is future."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that reaches"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old things being gone is stated without a list of exceptions — which is Paul's habit whenever he describes what happened at the cross. In Colossians the record against us was *wiped out… nailed to the cross*; in Romans, *there is therefore now no condemnation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever came down the line before — a family's history, a father's diagnosis, a past that had a claim — the sentence puts it under *the old things are gone.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole thing is located there. Not *if anyone tries hard* — *if anyone is in Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is Paul's most-used phrase, some two hundred occurrences across his letters, and it is where every benefit in his theology sits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now all things are of God, who has reconciled us to Himself through Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the sentence the chapter is famous for: *He made Him who knew no sin to be sin for us, that we might become the righteousness of God in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The new creation is not a self-improvement claim. It is the result of an exchange described eleven verses later."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul had been.** *A blasphemer, a persecutor, and an insolent man* — his own description, in his own letter. The man who wrote *the old things are gone* had more old things than most."}]}, "2 Corinthians 5:18–19": {"title": "\"Refusing to hold their sins against them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul states what God did, and then what He has put into our hands, and the two are the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did the reconciling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of it comes from God, who has brought us back to Himself through Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The direction is fixed and it is worth noticing. In ordinary usage the offended party is the one who needs to be appeased — you reconcile the person you have wronged."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul never says God was reconciled to us. He says God reconciled **us** to Himself. The move came from the side that had been sinned against."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"God was in Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not God sending an agent at a distance. Paul puts Him in the person doing the work — which is why the next clause can be so absolute."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bookkeeping term"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Refusing to hold their sins against them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is **logizomai** — to count, to reckon, to enter in a ledger. It is the same word Paul uses in Romans 4 for righteousness being credited to Abraham."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the claim is accounting language: the sins are not being entered. Not overlooked in a vague sense — not posted to the account."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the scope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bringing **the world** back to Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a narrow group. Paul then says the message of that homecoming has been placed in us, and two verses later he is pleading with people to be reconciled — so the reconciling accomplished and the appeal to receive it are both in the passage, without contradiction."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"the ministry of reconciliation\" actually is.** Paul defines it in the same breath: God has *placed in us the message of that homecoming.* The work handed over is not to reconcile anybody — that is done. It is to carry the news that it has been."}]}, "2 Corinthians 5:20–21": {"title": "The great exchange", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and it is built as a swap with nothing left over on either side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He made Him who never knew sin **to be sin** for us, so that in Him we might become **the righteousness of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *treated as a sinner*, and not *made righteous-ish*. Paul writes both sides at full strength:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "| Him | us | |---|---| | never knew sin | had nothing else | | **made to be sin** | **made the righteousness of God** |"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever weight the first half carries, the second carries the same. If He was genuinely made sin, then we are genuinely made righteousness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Never knew sin\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is about knowing by experience. It is not that He avoided it successfully — He had no acquaintance with it at all. That is what made the transfer possible: there was nothing of His own on His side of the ledger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The location matters and Paul is exact about it. The righteousness is not manufactured in us and inspected. It is **in Him** — held in the same place the blessings of Ephesians 1 are held."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means it is as secure as He is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence before it is the appeal: *we come as ambassadors on behalf of Christ… come back to God.* The exchange is the reason the appeal can be made at all. An ambassador carries terms he did not set, and these are the terms."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word \"become.\"** Not *have* righteousness, as a possession that could be mislaid. Paul says we **become** it — the noun, not the adjective. It is the strongest way the sentence could have been put."}]}, "2 Corinthians 8:9": {"title": "The exchange, in one sentence", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is in the middle of organising a collection for the poor in Jerusalem, and he stops to give the reason. It turns out to be the whole gospel in a line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The structure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though He was rich, for your sakes He became poor, so that you, through His poverty, might become rich.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four movements, and they cross over:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "| Him | you | |---|---| | was rich | were poor | | **became poor** | **become rich** |"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is left in the middle. What was His went one way; what was ours went the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You know the grace\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul opens with *for you know* — he is not teaching them something new. He is reminding a church that had gone cold on a promised collection what grace had already cost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **charis**, and here it is applied to an act rather than an attitude: the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ *is* the exchange he then describes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never orders them to give. The next verse says he is not commanding, and a few lines on comes the sentence about God loving a cheerful giver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Instead he sets one act beside another: Christ emptied Himself for you, so finish what you started for them. Generosity is argued from generosity received, not from obligation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the riches are.** Paul does not spell it out here, which is why the verse travels so well. Read alongside Ephesians 1 — every blessing, already given, in the heavenly places — and the wealth being described is not narrow. What He gave up was real; what came back the other way is described in the same unqualified terms."}]}, "2 Kings 4:32–37": {"title": "A son raised", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elisha raises the Shunammite's boy, and every step of it is worth counting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it took"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["he **shut the door** on the two of them", "he **prayed** to the LORD", "he **climbed onto the bed and lay over the boy** — mouth to mouth, eyes to eyes, hands to hands", "the child's body **grew warm**", "he **got up and walked back and forth** across the room", "he **bent over him once more**", "the boy **sneezed seven times** and opened his eyes"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven movements. A closed door, a prayer, physical contact along the whole length of the body, a pause, and a second attempt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet has to give himself to it — literally lie on the boy and cover him, limb for limb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Take your son\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elisha said, \"**Take your son**.\" She came in, fell at his feet, and bowed to the ground. Then she took her son and went out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has to come in and collect him. And she goes to the floor first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shunem, in the Jezreel valley. The name means double resting place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A few miles away over the ridge of Mount Moreh — whose name means teacher — is Nain, which means pleasant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two towns in the same valley, either side of one hill, and a boy raised from the dead in each of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Nain there is no closed door, no prayer, no stretching over the body, no pacing, no second attempt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Young man, I say to you, get up.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, spoken in the open air, in front of two crowds. He does not shut anybody out and He does not ask God for anything — He addresses the boy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the end: *Jesus **gave him back to his mother**.* Elisha said *take your son.* Jesus carried him over. See the note on Luke 7:11–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elisha had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had asked for a double portion of Elijah's spirit and had received it. He is the greatest of the prophets by the count of his miracles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And this is what the best of them had to do to raise one boy."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the woman had said on the way.** Her son had died on her lap that morning, and she saddled a donkey, rode to Carmel, and when Gehazi asked if all was well she answered *it is well.* She said it to Gehazi and she said it to her husband before she left. She had not told anybody the boy was dead."}]}, "2 Kings 5:14": {"title": "Flesh like a little child's", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A general with an army behind him ends up standing in a muddy river doing something undignified seven times, and comes out with the skin of a child."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had brought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naaman arrived with horses, chariots, ten talents of silver, six thousand shekels of gold and ten changes of clothes — a fortune, and a letter from his king."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha did not even come outside. He sent a servant with a message: go and wash in the Jordan seven times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naaman's fury is recorded in detail. He had expected a ceremony. He had better rivers at home — and he names them, Abana and Pharpar, clean water against Jordan's brown."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What actually healed him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination puts it plainly: *it was not his greatness that healed him, nor his gold, nor the rivers he preferred. It was the goodness of God, freely given, and received at last like a child.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he brought was useless. The one thing required was to go down into the water he thought beneath him, the full seven times, and come up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Clean and fresh as the flesh of a little child\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is exactly that — his flesh returned like the flesh of a small boy. Not merely cured. Made new."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is a nice detail in who talked him into it. His own servants — the people with no standing at all — reason with him: if the prophet had asked something great, you would have done it. Why not this?"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The gift he tried to give afterwards.** Naaman goes back and offers the whole treasure, and Elisha refuses every coin of it. The healing was free at both ends: nothing bought it, and nothing was accepted for it. Gehazi chasing after the money in the next paragraph, and catching the leprosy, is the chapter's own comment on that."}]}, "2 Peter 1:19": {"title": "A lamp in a pitch-black room", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter has just described the mountain — the voice, the brightness, the *this is My beloved Son*. He was standing there. And then he says something surprising about what is more reliable than that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Firmer now than ever\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the word the prophets spoke **stands firmer now than ever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a comparative: *more sure*, more confirmed. Peter is comparing the prophetic word to the transfiguration he has just recounted as an eyewitness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not doubt what he saw. He says the written word is the steadier of the two things to stand on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Picture a lamp lit in a pitch-black room — that is what it is.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word behind *pitch-black* is **auchmēros**: literally dry, parched, squalid, and so murky. It appears nowhere else in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamp is not lighting a pleasant evening. It is the one light in a dry dark place, and the passage takes for granted that the place is dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Until"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You watch it until the morning breaks through and the star that carries the day comes up **inside you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things happen at the end of the sentence, and they are different in kind:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the day dawns** — outside, the whole sky", "**the morning star rises in your hearts** — inside, personal"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Phōsphoros* is the light-bearer, the planet that rises just before sunrise. In Revelation, Jesus calls Himself *the bright morning star*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamp is for the meantime. The star is the thing the lamp was pointing at all along."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse that follows.** Peter's next sentence is his reason: no prophecy of Scripture came about by the prophet's own working out of it. The lamp is trustworthy in the dark because of where it was lit."}]}, "2 Peter 3:3–4": {"title": "\"Where is the promise of His coming?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter tells his readers what they will hear, and then gives the argument in the mockers' own words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prediction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the last days scoffers will come, scoffing and following their own evil desires.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not treat the objection as a surprise to be defended against. He treats its arrival as evidence — the mocking is itself one of the things that was said would happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is the promise of His coming? Ever since our ancestors died, everything goes on exactly as it has since the beginning of creation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a decent argument, and it is still made. Two parts:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the delay** — the people who first heard the promise have died", "**the uniformity** — nothing ever changes; the world runs on as it always has"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is disputing the facts. Peter's answer is that the second point is false as history."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he answers it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He points at the flood. The world *did* change once, catastrophically, and the people who assumed continuity were wrong then too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the two answers that follow: God's reckoning of time is not ours, and the delay is deliberate patience. See the note on 2 Peter 3:9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says they are doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They deliberately forget.* The Greek is about it escaping their notice on purpose — a chosen blindness rather than an oversight."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Peter's diagnosis of the argument. Not that the scoffers lack information. That the information is available and being stepped around."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The scale he sets it in.** The chapter runs from creation, through the flood, to the elements melting and a new heavens and new earth. The mockers' claim that everything continues unchanged is answered by a history in which it has already not, and a future in which it will not again."}]}, "2 Peter 3:9": {"title": "What looks like slowness", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter is answering scoffers who have noticed that the promised return has not happened, and he reframes the delay completely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation he is answering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before it have people asking where the promise of His coming is, since everything carries on as it always has."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter does not argue about the timetable. He changes the category the delay belongs to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lord is not dragging His feet over what He promised, whatever some people call it. He is being patient with us. He does not want anyone destroyed. He wants every last one to turn.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word behind *patient* is **makrothumeo** — long-tempered, slow to anger, the quality of someone who could act and does not yet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is being read as inability or indifference is a decision being held open."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He wants"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two statements, one negative and one positive, and both are unrestricted:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**not wanting** anyone to be destroyed", "**wanting** every last one to come to repentance"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter puts God's desire on record in the plainest possible terms, in a chapter otherwise full of fire and dissolving elements."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the delay is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the answer to the scoffers. The time that looks like absence is the time being given — and Peter says so again five verses on: *consider the patience of our Lord as salvation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The waiting is not neutral. It is rescue in progress."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse before it.** *With the Lord a day is like a thousand years, and a thousand years like a day.* Peter answers the timing question twice — once by saying God's reckoning of time is not ours, and once by saying the delay is deliberate mercy. He does not pick between them."}]}, "2 Samuel 1:5–10": {"title": "The Amalekite who came to collect a reward", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A young man arrives at David's camp with Saul's crown in his hands and a story he has clearly rehearsed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he says he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*An Amalekite.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the detail the chapter is built on. Saul had been commanded to destroy the Amalekites and had spared what he chose to spare. Now, at the end of his life, it is an Amalekite standing over him — and an Amalekite who arrives to profit from his death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing kept back came back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The story he tells"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says Saul was leaning on his spear, called him over, and asked to be finished off, and that he did it and took the crown and the armlet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The account does not match what the previous chapter recorded, where Saul fell on his own sword after his armour-bearer refused. Most readers conclude the Amalekite found the body and invented a role for himself, expecting a reward from the man Saul had hunted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he misjudged"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He read David the way the world reads: an enemy is dead, the throne is open, and the man who brings news of it will be paid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's response is to tear his clothes, mourn until evening, and then execute him for laying a hand on the LORD's anointed. Then he writes a lament for the man who had spent years trying to kill him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The pattern the chapter draws.** What Saul was told to deal with and did not, stood over him at the end and took the crown off his head. The narrative makes no comment. It simply gives the man's nationality in the fifth verse and lets the reader remember chapter 15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Amalek was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy describes the nation by its method: it came at Israel's back and cut down the stragglers *when you were weary and worn out*. It hunts what is already tired."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul was told to blot it out. He kept the king and the best of the livestock — what looked useful — and destroyed the rest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is spared because it still seems worth something does not stay where it was put. It stands over you at the end and takes the crown off your head, and four hundred years later it signs a decree in Persia with a king's ring on its hand. See the notes on Deuteronomy 25:17–19 and Esther 3:1–4."}]}, "2 Samuel 19:31–40": {"title": "Barzillai turns it down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old man who had kept a king alive is offered a place at his table, and lists reasons why not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was he who had **kept the king supplied** while David stayed at Mahanaim.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David was in flight from Absalom, with an army and no supply line. Chapter 17 gives the inventory Barzillai brought out: beds, basins, wheat, barley, flour, parched grain, beans, lentils, honey, curds, sheep and cheese."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For they said, \"The people are hungry and weary and thirsty in the wilderness.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He backed the true king when the throne was in doubt and it was dangerous to do so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Cross over with me. Come to Jerusalem, and I will care for you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Open-ended, from a restored king, to the man who had fed him. There is no condition on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"How many years do I have left…? **I am eighty now.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then a run of questions, all in the negative:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"Can I still **tell good from bad**?\"*", "*\"Can I **taste** what I eat and drink?\"*", "*\"Can I still **hear** the voices of singers, men and women?\"*", "*\"Why should your servant be **a burden** to my lord the king?\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judgement, taste, hearing — he inventories what he has lost, and concludes from the list that the invitation is not for him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he wants to go instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let me go back home, and let me die in my own town, near the graves of my father and mother.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks to go back and be buried where his parents are. That is his stated ambition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he sends instead of himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But here is **your servant Kimham** — let him cross over with my lord the king.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most readers take Kimham for his son. The place at the table gets filled; Barzillai is not in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He crosses *a little way over the Jordan*, takes the king's kiss and blessing, and goes home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Set beside Caleb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caleb, at eighty-five, is five years older than Barzillai here, and this is what he says:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am as strong this day as on the day Moses sent me; just as my strength was then, so now is my strength for war… **give me this mountain**\" — the one with the giants and the fortified cities on it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two old men, five years apart. One counts what he can no longer taste; the other asks for the hill country. See the note on Joshua 14:6–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue. *The king kissed Barzillai and blessed him, and Barzillai went home.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is taken away from him, and nothing is forced on him. The offer stood and he declined it, and the record is kind to him throughout — David remembers him on his deathbed and tells Solomon to show kindness to his sons."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he does not say.** He never doubts David or the kingdom. His reasons are all about himself — his age, his senses, his usefulness. The invitation was not conditional on any of them."}]}, "2 Samuel 9:5–8": {"title": "Mephibosheth at the king's table", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A king sends for the last surviving heir of the family he replaced, and the man arrives certain he is going to die."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jonathan's son, Saul's grandson. Crippled in both feet since he was five, when his nurse dropped him fleeing the news that his father and grandfather had been killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the political logic of that world he was a dead man — a rival claimant living on borrowed time. That is why he had been living in **Lo Debar**, a name that means roughly *no pasture*, or *nothing there*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who is your servant, that you would even notice a dead dog like me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is trembling, face to the ground. He does not ask for anything. He does not argue that he is harmless or that he deserves better."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls himself a dead dog."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David gives him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things, and none of them are requested:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**don't be afraid**", "**every piece of land that belonged to your grandfather Saul**", "**you yourself will always eat at my table**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land is restored, and the seat is permanent. The chapter ends by saying he ate at the king's table like one of the king's sons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am going to show you kindness **for the sake of your father Jonathan**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason has nothing to do with Mephibosheth. It is a covenant made years earlier between David and Jonathan — see 1 Samuel 18:1–4 — and the kindness runs to him on the strength of somebody else's name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not being rewarded. He is being kept."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last verse of the chapter.** *And he was lame in both feet.* Second Samuel mentions it three times, and the final time is the very last line — after the land, the table, and the sons. The condition never changed. His place at the table was not conditional on it."}]}, "2 Thessalonians 3:10–12": {"title": "\"Unwilling\" is the whole word", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This verse gets quoted at people in hard circumstances more often than almost any other, and usually with one word quietly dropped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word that is always dropped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anyone who is **unwilling** to work should not eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek does not say *does not work*. It says *does not want to*. Paul is describing a decision, not a situation. Everyone who cannot find work, cannot work, or has run out of options is outside the sentence entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was actually writing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next line names them: people in that church who were *drifting, doing no work at all, and yet somehow finding the hours to busy themselves in everyone else's affairs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The likeliest cause is in the letter itself. Some of them had concluded the Lord's return was imminent and had downed tools to wait. Paul is not scolding the poor. He is dealing with a specific enthusiasm that had turned into idleness — and, as he notes drily, into meddling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he tells them to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Settle down, do your own work without all the noise, and eat food you have earned.* Then, immediately after, he tells the rest of the church not to grow weary in doing good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both instructions in the same breath: work quietly, and keep being generous."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How Paul argues it.** He appeals to his own habits first — he paid his way in Thessalonica rather than take support, though he says outright he had the right to. He is asking of them something he had already done in front of them."}]}, "2 Timothy 1:6–7": {"title": "Fan the gift into flame", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's last surviving letter, written from a Roman prison to a younger man who appears to be losing his nerve."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is already there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God **placed a gift inside you** the day I laid my hands on you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past tense, and settled. Nothing in the passage asks Timothy to obtain anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole instruction assumes the gift is present and has been present for years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Fan it back up into open flame\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anazopureo** — *ana* (again) + *zoos* (alive) + *pur* (fire). To rekindle; to stir a fire back up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You do not fan an ashtray. The verb takes for granted that there are coals in there, banked and covered, still hot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timothy is not told to light something. He is told to stir something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Was never a small, cowering thing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What God breathed into us was never a small, cowering thing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek noun is **deilia** — cowardice, timidity. It appears nowhere else in the New Testament, and it is not a neutral word; the Greeks used it as the opposite of courage, a fault rather than a temperament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say Timothy is not afraid. He says where the fear did not come from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three things it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gave us **power**, and He gave us **love**, and He gave us **a mind that stays clear and steady**.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**power** — *dunamis*, the plain word for ability and force", "**love** — *agape*", "**a clear and steady mind** — **sophronismos**, a word about soundness of mind, self-command, a head that is in one piece"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third is the one that gets flattened in translation. It is built from the words for *safe* and *mind*: a mind that is intact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul lists it as something given, alongside power and love — not as a disposition Timothy has to manufacture under pressure."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul asks for at the end of the letter.** His cloak, the books, and especially the parchments. A man writing about power, love and a sound mind from a cell, and asking for a coat because winter is coming."}]}, "2 Timothy 4:6–8": {"title": "Three finished things, and a crown that is not only his", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last words we have from Paul, written from a Roman cell with no expectation of release."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Poured out like an offering\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is a drink offering — wine poured out beside a sacrifice, the last act of a ritual and the smallest part of it. Paul does not compare himself to the sacrifice. He is the libation poured alongside one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The hour of my going is here* — and the word for going is **analusis**, the noun of the verb he used in Philippians for casting off. Same picture, years later, with the ship now actually leaving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three perfects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have fought the good fight. I have finished the race. I have kept the faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three are in the perfect tense — done, and the result stands. Note what he does not claim: he does not say he won, or that he did it well. He says he fought, he finished, he kept."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The middle one is not about coming first. The Greek is simply completing the course."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crown"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Laid aside and kept for me* — a verb meaning reserved, put away for someone. It is already in storage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the line that stops it being about Paul:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "and not to me only, but to every last one who has loved His appearing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The condition for the crown, stated by a man about to be executed, is not achievement or office or martyrdom. It is having loved the fact that He is coming."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read on to verse 9.** Immediately after this — the crown, the finished race, the settled peace — Paul writes *do your best to come to me quickly*, and later asks for his cloak and his books. The grandeur and the cold cell are three sentences apart, and both are in the letter because both were true."}]}, "3 John 1:2": {"title": "\"The way your soul is already doing well\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A single verse from a letter of fourteen, written by an old man to a friend named Gaius."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he prays"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*More than anything I hope things go well for you and that your health holds up, the way your soul is already doing well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things in one sentence: that it goes well with him generally, that his body holds up, and that both would keep pace with a soul that is already thriving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb behind *go well* is **euodoo** — literally to have a good road, a prosperous journey. It is used of travelling without obstruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure John uses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The way your soul is already doing well.* The soul is the benchmark, and it is in good order — that much John states as fact rather than wish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the prayer is not for prosperity in the abstract. It is that the outward life would come up level with the inward one, which John already knows is flourishing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he was confident about the soul"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse says why: travellers had come and reported that Gaius was walking in the truth, and John says nothing gives him greater joy. Then the letter commends him for how he treated strangers and missionaries passing through — at his own cost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gaius was a generous man, and John prays for his health and his road in the same breath as praising it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What kind of sentence it is.** It sits where a first-century letter always put a wish for the reader's health — the standard opening courtesy of the era. John takes that ordinary form and fills it with something else: a prayer that the body and the circumstances would match the state of the soul. He evidently thought the three belonged together."}]}, "Acts 1:9–11": {"title": "\"This same Jesus\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They watched Him go, and two men had to tell them to stop looking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While they were **looking straight at Him**, He was lifted up, and a cloud took Him out of their sight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is careful about this — they were watching. It happened in daylight, in front of eleven men, on a hillside outside Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cloud"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not weather. The cloud in Scripture is the visible sign of the presence — the pillar in the wilderness, the cloud that filled the tabernacle and then the temple, the bright cloud at the transfiguration."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Standing there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were **still standing there staring into the sky** as He went.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it twice, and the two men in white say it back to them as a question: *why are you standing here looking into the sky?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody rebukes them for grief. They are simply moved along — there is a room in Jerusalem to go to and ten days to wait."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This same Jesus**, who has been taken from you into heaven, will come back **in the same way** you have watched Him go.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves matter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**This same Jesus.** Not a different one, not an altered one. The Man they had walked with for three years — the one who wept at a grave, cooked breakfast on a beach, touched a leper, and would not break a bruised reed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is who went up, and that is who is coming back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In the same way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bodily, visibly, and to the same place. Zechariah says it: *in that day His feet will stand on the Mount of Olives* — which is precisely the hill they were standing on when they were told this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was doing as He left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's Gospel gives the detail Acts leaves out: *He lifted up His hands and blessed them. And it came to pass, while He blessed them, He was parted from them and carried up into heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His hands were up and He was in the middle of a blessing. The blessing was never finished — He went up mid-sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who He is now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has taken His seat at the right hand of God's throne.* Every priest under the old covenant stood, because the work was never done. He sat down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He *always lives to make intercession for them* — the same Man, doing that, now."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last thing recorded of the disciples in that chapter.** *They returned to Jerusalem with great joy.* He had just left, and Luke says joy. They had been told He was coming back exactly as He went."}]}, "Acts 10:34–43": {"title": "\"He went about doing good\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter's sermon in a Roman officer's house, and the summary of Jesus' ministry in it is the shortest in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I see now, truly, that God shows no favouritism.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says *I see now* — he had not seen it before that week. It took a vision on a roof, three men at the gate, and a walk to Caesarea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is vivid: God is *no taker of faces*, no respecter of appearances."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How God anointed Jesus of Nazareth with the Holy Spirit and with power, and how He went about **doing good** and **healing everyone under the oppression of the devil**, because God was with Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sentence for three years. Two verbs: doing good, and healing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is speaking to people who had heard the reports — *you know what has happened all through Judea.* He does not need to persuade them it occurred."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the healing is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone under the oppression of the devil.* The word means being tyrannised, held down by force."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter characterises sickness there as an oppression being lifted, and healing as release rather than reward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Because God was with Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given for the whole ministry. Peter puts the anointing, the power, and the presence together and hangs everything on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every one of the prophets bears witness to Him, that through His name **everyone who believes** in Him receives forgiveness of sins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the last sentence before the Spirit interrupts him. The word *everyone* is what he is saying when it happens — in a Gentile house, to people he would not have entered a room with a week earlier. See the note on Acts 10:44–48."}]}, "Acts 10:44–48": {"title": "\"Peter was still speaking\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The moment the church stopped being a Jewish movement, and it happens before the sermon finishes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter was still speaking when the Holy Spirit fell on everyone who was listening.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is cut off mid-sentence. Nothing has been required of these people — no baptism, no circumcision, no probation, no instruction on keeping the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke tells us what Peter had just said: *everyone who believes in Him receives forgiveness of sins through His name.* The Spirit falls on that sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was astounded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The believers of the circumcision who had come with Peter were astounded that the gift of the Holy Spirit had been poured out on the Gentiles as well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the Gentiles. The Jewish Christians who had travelled with him — men who had needed a vision on a rooftop and a direct instruction to walk into that house at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They believed in Jesus and were still astonished at this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Can anyone refuse water to keep these people from being baptized, when they have received the Holy Spirit just as we did?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The logic runs backwards from what God has already done. The evidence came first; the church's practice has to catch up with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the argument Peter will use again in Jerusalem when he is called to account for eating with Gentiles — *if God gave them the same gift, who was I to think I could stand in God's way?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Afterward they asked him to stay on with them a few days.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jewish apostle stays in a Roman officer's house. In the culture of the time that sentence is nearly as significant as the Spirit falling."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter opens.** Cornelius is described as devout, God-fearing, generous and prayerful — and none of it saved him; an angel still had to send for Peter. Luke gives him every religious credential available and then has the gospel arrive anyway, unearned."}]}, "Acts 13:34–37": {"title": "\"Served God's purpose in his own generation\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is preaching in a synagogue in Antioch, and he uses David to make an argument that turns on a single observable fact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm says *You will not let Your Holy One see decay*. Paul's point is simple and almost forensic:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["David said it", "David died, was buried, and **did see decay** — his tomb was a known location", "therefore he was not talking about himself"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the One God raised up saw no decay at all.* The psalm had to be about somebody else, and Paul says who."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line about David"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After he had served God's purpose in his own generation, [he] fell asleep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a remarkable summary of a life that filled several books. Not \"after he had won his wars\" or \"after he had built the kingdom.\" He served God's purpose **in his own generation** — the phrase locates the whole thing inside a lifetime, with a start and a finish, and treats the finish as unremarkable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David is not diminished by it. He is placed: a man who did what was his to do, in the years he had, and then slept."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two halves do together.** Paul's whole case rests on the difference — David's work ended and his body decayed; the other did not. But the way he says it about David is not dismissive. Serving God's purpose in your own generation is offered as a good and sufficient description of a life, by a man arguing that only one person ever escaped the ending."}]}, "Acts 16:14–15": {"title": "\"The Lord opened her heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first convert in Europe is a businesswoman at a prayer meeting by a river."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who she was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A woman named **Lydia**, a **seller of purple cloth** from the city of Thyatira, and she **worshipped God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Purple dye was the most expensive commodity in the ancient world — extracted a drop at a time from a shellfish, and reserved by law in some places for royalty. A dealer in it was a wealthy woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is from Thyatira in Asia Minor, and she is in Philippi, running a business abroad."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they were at a river"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philippi had no synagogue. Ten Jewish men were required to form one, and the city evidently did not have them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So Paul goes down to the riverside on the Sabbath *where prayer was customarily made*, and finds a group of women."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gospel enters Europe at an informal women's prayer meeting outside the city gate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She was listening, and **the Lord opened her heart** to pay attention to what Paul was saying.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things at once, and Luke puts them side by side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She was listening — that is her. The Lord opened her heart — that is Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *opened* is the word used of opening a door, and elsewhere in Luke of opening the Scriptures on the Emmaus road and of the disciples' eyes being opened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul preached. Somebody else did the opening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her household"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **she and her household** had been baptised.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not just her. The Greek is her house — family, servants, dependents. The whole establishment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase turns up at the end of the same chapter, over the jailer: *believe on the Lord Jesus Christ, and you will be saved — you and your household.* See the note on Acts 16:29–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two households in one chapter, at opposite ends of Philippi's social order: a purple merchant and a prison officer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she did with her house"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you have judged me faithful** to the Lord, come into my house and stay.\" And **she prevailed on us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She makes it a question of whether they think she is genuine, which is not a request anyone can easily refuse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *prevailed* is to compel, to force — the same verb the Emmaus two used on Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What her house became"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The church at Philippi. Paul's warmest letter is addressed to it, and the last lines of this chapter have him and Silas going back to *the house of Lydia* after the earthquake, to encourage the brethren, before leaving the city."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the money went.** Philippi is the church that sent Paul support again and again — *even in Thessalonica you sent aid once and again for my necessities* — and the one he thanks in Philippians for a gift carried by Epaphroditus. The church that funded him started in a wealthy woman's front room."}]}, "Acts 16:29–31": {"title": "\"You and your household\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prison officer at midnight, with a sword already in his hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had done that day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was the man who took Paul and Silas after the beating, *put them into the inner prison, and fastened their feet in the stocks.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The inner prison is the deepest cell. The stocks were a punishment device as much as a restraint — the legs forced apart and held."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did that to two men whose backs had just been laid open with rods, and then went to bed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What woke him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At midnight Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns to God, and the prisoners were listening to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the earthquake, the doors open, everyone's chains loose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He drew his sword to kill himself, because under Roman practice a jailer answered with his own life for escaped prisoners. Paul stopped him: *do yourself no harm, for we are all here.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody ran. That is the detail that brings him in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Trembling, he **fell down before Paul and Silas**… \"Sirs, **what must I do to be saved?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is on the floor in front of two men he had put in the stocks a few hours earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Believe on the Lord Jesus Christ, and you will be saved** — you and your household.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight words to the question. No conditions, no probation, no list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *make restitution*. Not *release us first*. Not *stop being a jailer*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And your household\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase used over Lydia at the start of the chapter. See the note on Acts 16:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse says they *spoke the word of the Lord to him and to all who were in his house* — the household hears it too, and then *he and all his family were baptised immediately*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did before the night was over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took them the same hour of the night and **washed their stripes**… and he set food before them, and **rejoiced, believing in God with all his household**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He washed the wounds he had caused, in the dark, and then fed them at his own table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke puts the washing and the baptism in the same sentence: *he washed their stripes, and immediately he and all his family were baptised.* Water both ways, in the same hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two households, one chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A wealthy purple merchant at a riverside prayer meeting, and a Roman jailer at midnight. The top and the bottom of that city, in one chapter, and both times Luke says *and her household*, *and his household*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Philippi cost them.** Beaten with rods, illegally — they were Roman citizens, and Paul says so the next morning. The magistrates who ordered it come down in person to ask them to leave quietly. Paul goes back to Lydia's house first."}]}, "Acts 16:6–8": {"title": "Guidance that arrives as a closed door", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three sentences, and almost nothing happens in them. Paul tries to go one way and cannot. He tries another and cannot. He ends up somewhere he was not aiming for. It is one of the most useful passages in the New Testament about being led, precisely because so little of it looks like leading."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Spirit actually does here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the verbs:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**kept** from speaking the word in Asia", "**would not allow** them into Bithynia", "so they **passed by** Mysia and came down to Troas"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a word of positive instruction. No voice, no vision, no verse. The Spirit's whole contribution in these three sentences is to stop them, twice, and the narrative does not say how — Luke, who elsewhere reports angels and trances and voices in detail, does not tell us."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were being stopped from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the part worth sitting with. They were not being stopped from sin. They were being stopped from **preaching the gospel in Asia**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was nothing wrong with the plan. It was a good plan, made by good men, for the best possible reason. It was simply not the one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That should adjust how a closed door gets read. A blocked way is not proof that the intention was bad, and the passage never suggests Paul had made a mistake in wanting to go."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Troas is not a destination in this story; it is where you end up when you have run out of directions. And it is there — after two refusals and a journey that looks like wandering — that the vision of the man from Macedonia comes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order matters. The clear guidance arrives *last*, and it arrives to men who had kept moving while they only had the unclear kind. If they had stopped at the first refusal and waited for something positive, they would not have been at Troas to receive it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not license"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It would be easy to turn this into a rule: every obstacle is God redirecting you. Acts will not support that. In the same book Paul is stoned, jailed, shipwrecked and opposed at length, and none of those are read as the Spirit's redirection — he goes back to several of the cities he was driven out of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The difference is not in the size of the obstacle. Luke simply tells us, in these two cases, whose hand it was. Most of the time nobody gets told, which is why the next verse matters as much as these: they were still walking when the answer came."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the word \"kept.\"** *Koluo* is an ordinary word for hindering or preventing — used of stopping someone crossing a street or forbidding an action. Luke does not dress it up. Whatever the experience was, he reports it as plainly as a road being shut."}]}, "Acts 2:1–4": {"title": "Wind, fire, and languages", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three images in four verses, and every one of them had been used before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A sound from heaven like a great rushing wind, and it filled the whole house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is careful: a **sound like** wind. Nothing is described as blowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In both Hebrew and Greek the word for spirit is also the word for wind and breath. Ezekiel's valley of dry bones is raised when the breath comes; Jesus told Nicodemus the wind blows where it wishes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What looked like tongues of fire, separating and coming to rest on each one of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fire had marked God's presence before — the bush, the pillar, the mountain, the altar on Carmel. Always one fire, in one place, that the people stood back from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it divides, and a piece of it settles on every individual in the room. Nobody is standing at a distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The languages"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Began to speak in other languages, as the Spirit gave them the words.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd outside is the point: fifteen nations are listed in the next paragraph, and each hears its own tongue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babel scattered people by confusing their language. This gathers people by overcoming it. Luke does not draw the comparison; he just lists the nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sermon goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter stands up and explains it from Joel — *in the last days I will pour out my Spirit on all people* — and adds the detail from Psalm 16 about the Holy One not seeing decay."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This Jesus God has raised up, and we are all witnesses of it… He has poured out what you are seeing and hearing.* The wind and the fire are presented as evidence of an ascension. See the note on Acts 2:32–33."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The day it happened.** Pentecost was the feast of firstfruits of the wheat harvest, fifty days after Passover — and by tradition, the day the law was given at Sinai. Three thousand died the day the law came down at Sinai. Three thousand were added the day the Spirit came down here."}]}, "Acts 2:32–33": {"title": "\"He has poured out what you are seeing and hearing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter is explaining a phenomenon, and his explanation runs backwards from the noise in the street to a throne nobody present can see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*This Jesus God has raised up, and we are all witnesses of it*", "*lifted up to the right hand of God*", "*having received from the Father the promised Holy Spirit*", "*He has poured out what you are seeing and hearing*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four links: resurrection, ascension, receiving, pouring. Peter builds the whole argument to explain a sound and a sight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crowd can check"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What you are seeing and hearing.* They are not being asked to accept an invisible claim. Something audible and visible is happening in front of them, and Peter says: here is what it proves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The evidence for the ascension, in his argument, is the Spirit's arrival. Nobody watched Jesus sit down at the right hand of God — but this is what it looks like from below."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Having received… He has poured out\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Son receives from the Father and gives to the people. Peter puts all three in one sentence without explanation, and the church spent centuries working out what he had said in passing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it mattered that day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd's first suggestion was that these people were drunk at nine in the morning. Peter's answer is not a defence of behaviour. It is a claim about location: the man you crucified is at God's right hand, and this is Him acting from there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the hearers do next.** *They were cut to the heart* and asked what to do — and Peter's answer includes *the promise is for you and your children and all who are far off.* The pouring out described here is presented as available to the crowd, not just to the hundred and twenty in the room."}]}, "Acts 2:40–41": {"title": "Three thousand added", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first day of the church, and Luke gives the number."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter kept saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said much more than this, and he kept urging them, \"Be rescued from this crooked generation.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke tells us the sermon we have is a summary — there was much more. And the plea he records is passive: *be rescued*, be saved. Not save yourselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd's own question had been *what shall we do?*, and the answer they were given was to turn and be baptised, and to receive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That day about three thousand people were added to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The figure is not chosen at random by history. Exodus 32 records three thousand falling on the day the law was given at Sinai — and Pentecost was, by long tradition, the anniversary of that giving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same feast, same number, and the verb reversed. Three thousand fell there; three thousand were **added** here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not comment. He was writing for people who knew the story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Added\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is the ordinary one for putting something to an existing total, and Luke will use it repeatedly through the early chapters — the Lord added to their number daily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in Acts is described as joining. They are added, and the one doing the adding is named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the day cost them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is said about qualifications. They had, seven weeks earlier, been part of the city that called for a crucifixion — Peter says so to their faces two paragraphs before this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The response to that sermon was three thousand baptisms."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows immediately.** The famous four: the apostles' teaching, the fellowship, the breaking of bread, and the prayers. The number is not left as a headline; Luke goes straight on to what those three thousand then did every day."}]}, "Acts 2:46–47": {"title": "\"From house to house\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke's picture of what the first weeks actually looked like, and it is domestic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two places"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Day after day they were together **in the temple courts**… and they broke bread **from house to house**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both, and they are different activities. The temple was public, Jewish, and enormous — the courts held tens of thousands. The houses were small."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The teaching and the praise happened in the open; the meals happened where people lived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Day after day\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not weekly. Luke says it twice in two verses — daily in the temple, and the Lord adding to their number daily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the shape of the early church was, it was not a Sunday event."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Breaking bread"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase covers an ordinary meal and carries the memory of the Last Supper, and Luke has just used it a few verses earlier as one of the four things they devoted themselves to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not separate the two. They *ate their meals with glad and generous hearts* — the eating and the remembering are described in one sentence, at somebody's table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words for how"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**glad** — the Greek is a strong word for rejoicing, exultation", "**generous** — literally *simplicity* of heart, the same root as the word behind God giving *with both hands* in James 1:5. Single-minded, without a hidden second motive."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how Luke describes the atmosphere: glad and unguarded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Praising God and finding favour with all the people. And the Lord added to the church daily.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The favour is with outsiders, and the adding is God's doing. Neither is presented as the goal of the meals — both are reported as what followed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the picture.** No building, no programme, no name for the movement yet. A temple courtyard, other people's houses, and a shared table every day."}]}, "Acts 20:7–12": {"title": "The young man in the window", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke was there for this one — the *we* in the passage is his — and he tells it with a doctor's eye and a slight glint of humour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the first day of the week, when the disciples came together to break bread.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the earliest indications of Christians meeting on Sunday, and of what they met for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There were many lamps burning in the upper room.* Luke mentions it because it matters: many oil lamps in a crowded upper room, at night, burning the air. A young man in a window is looking for cooler air."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Paul talking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was leaving the next day, and he kept on talking until midnight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not defend him. The word he uses for the sinking sleep is a medical term — being borne down, overcome by degrees. Eutychus does not doze off carelessly; he is gradually overwhelmed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He fell from the third floor, and **they picked him up dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke, a physician, states it flatly. Not *as though dead*. Dead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Paul went down, bent over him, and took him in his arms.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The posture is Elijah's over the widow's son, and Elisha's over the Shunammite's boy — stretching himself on the child. Paul repeats an old gesture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not be alarmed. His life is in him.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he went back up, broke bread and ate, and talked with them a long while, until daybreak, and then he left.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He resumes. The meeting continues, the bread is broken, and the preaching runs till dawn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boy is mentioned once more, in the last line: *they brought the young man home alive, and were greatly comforted.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Luke leaves out.** Any comment. No sermon on the meaning of it, no rebuke of the boy, no reflection on the length of the sermon. A death, a raising, and then breakfast — reported by a man who was in the room."}]}, "Acts 23:1–3": {"title": "\"You whitewashed wall\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul in front of the council, and the exchange goes badly within two sentences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brothers, I have lived my life before God with a completely clear conscience up to this day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He addresses the Sanhedrin as equals — *brothers*, not *rulers* — and claims a clear conscience, which is not a modest thing to say to a court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is struck on the mouth for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God is going to strike you, you whitewashed wall. You sit there to judge me according to the law, and you break the law by ordering me to be struck?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The legal point is exact. Striking an unconvicted defendant was illegal, and Paul names the contradiction precisely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The insult is also exact. **Whitewashed wall** — clean on the outside, structurally unsound; the same image Jesus used of whitewashed tombs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bystanders tell him he is reviling God's high priest, and Paul answers: *I did not know, brothers, that he was the high priest* — and then quotes Exodus about not speaking evil of a ruler of your people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not defend the outburst. He retracts it on the spot, on the basis of a text."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Luke includes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not have to. This is Paul at his least impressive — losing his temper, and then discovering he had insulted the wrong man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports his hero's failures the way the Gospels report Peter's."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Whether he really did not know.** Readers have wondered for centuries: poor eyesight, a long absence from Jerusalem, or an irregular seating arrangement. The text does not say. What it does record is a man who spoke first, was corrected, and immediately took it back — which is the more useful half of the story."}]}, "Acts 26:4–8": {"title": "The wolf in the evening", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's last recorded defence, before a king, and he turns it into something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is in the room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Agrippa, Bernice, the governor Festus, the commanders and the prominent men of Caesarea, *with great pomp*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul, in chains, given permission to speak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about his past"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They could testify, if they were willing, that **I lived as a Pharisee, by the strictest party of our religion**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not soften it. Elsewhere he puts it harder: *I persecuted this Way to the death, binding and delivering into prisons both men and women.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Benjamin's blessing was one line: *in the morning he devours the prey, and in the evening he divides the spoil.* Paul names his own tribe twice in his letters, and the morning is what Luke describes with the word used of an animal mangling a carcass. See the note on Genesis 49:27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says he is on trial for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And now I stand here on trial **for the hope of the promise God made to our fathers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for a crime, a riot, or a sect. For a hope, and he says it is the hope *our twelve tribes* are serving God night and day to see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts himself on the same side as his accusers and says the disagreement is about whether it has happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks the king"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why should it be judged something incredible by any of you that God raises the dead?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A prisoner asking the court a question. It is not a defence — nothing in it is about his innocence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with the hearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them about the road to Damascus: the light above the brightness of the sun, the voice, and the commission — *to open their eyes, to turn them from darkness to light… that they may receive forgiveness of sins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Festus interrupts him: *Paul, you are beside yourself! Much learning is driving you mad.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then Agrippa: *you almost persuade me to become a Christian.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "His answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I would to God that not only you, but also all who hear me today, might become both almost and altogether such as I am — **except for these chains**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is in irons, and he wishes the whole room were as he is. The only thing he excludes is the chains."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the evening dividing the spoil. Everything he took in the morning he is handing out, from a prisoner's dock, to the men who are trying him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Agrippa concluded.** *This man might have been set free if he had not appealed to Caesar.* He appealed, and it took him to Rome — which is where he wrote the letters. Philippians, Ephesians, Colossians and Philemon come out of that appeal."}]}, "Acts 27:9–12": {"title": "The prisoner who was right, and the vote that went the other way", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A ship's council, a prisoner's opinion, and a decision made the sensible way that turns out to be wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was in the room"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the pilot** — the professional navigator", "**the ship's owner** — the money", "**the centurion** — the authority, and the man who had to decide", "**Paul** — a prisoner in chains, with no standing whatsoever"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke notes that the centurion listened to the first two rather than the last, and that *most of them were in favour* of sailing. Expertise, ownership and the majority all lined up on one side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the timing mattered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sailing had now become dangerous because the Fast was already over* — the Day of Atonement, which puts this in late September or October. Ancient sailing in that sea effectively shut down for the winter, and everyone aboard knew it. The question was not whether it was risky but whether they could make one short hop to a better harbour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a reasonable call. It was also the wrong one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage does not do.** It never says God told Paul. It says *Paul warned them* and reports what he could see. Later in the chapter an angel does speak to him, and Luke says so plainly — he distinguishes the two. This part of the story is a man's judgement being right while the experts were wrong, and Luke lets that stand without dressing it up as prophecy."}]}, "Acts 4:32–35": {"title": "Everything held in common", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke's second summary of the young church, and every clause in it is worth slowing down over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "One heart and soul"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The whole company of believers was **one in heart and soul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is literally *of one heart and one soul* — a phrase used in Greek literature of the closest friendships, applied here to thousands of people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said about property"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not one of them said that anything he owned was **his alone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is precise. He does not say they had no possessions. He says nobody used the word *mine* about what he had."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The change is in the pronoun before it is in the ledger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Great power, great grace"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And with **great power** the apostles kept giving their witness to the resurrection of the Lord Jesus, and **great grace** was on them all.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two adjectives are the same Greek word, and Luke uses it twice in one sentence — great power on the preaching, great grace on everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The power was on the apostles. The grace was on all of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not a needy person among them\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is an echo. Deuteronomy 15 promised Israel *there shall be no poor among you* if they kept the covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports it as an accomplished fact in Jerusalem, and the mechanism he describes is not a law. It is people selling things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "As he had need"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was distributed to each one as he had need.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not equally. According to need — which requires somebody to know each person's situation. The distribution assumes a community small enough to be known in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What this paragraph is doing where it is.** It follows the prayer of chapter 4, where the place was shaken and they spoke the word with boldness. Luke's order is: they pray, the room shakes, and then nobody owns anything alone. He puts the economics downstream of the prayer meeting."}]}, "Acts 5:1–6": {"title": "A gift and a lie", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hardest story in Acts, and Luke reports it without a word of explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was not required"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Before you sold it, was it not your own? And once it was sold, was the money not yours to do with as you pleased?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter states it twice, plainly. The land was theirs. The money was theirs. Nobody had commanded them to sell anything, and no percentage was owed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That removes the obvious reading. This is not about how much they gave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why has Satan filled your heart, so that you would **lie to the Holy Spirit** and keep back part of the price?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *keep back* is **nosphizomai** — to embezzle, to set aside for yourself out of something already dedicated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word the Greek Old Testament uses for Achan, who kept back devoted things from Jericho and hid them under his tent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was lied to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have not lied to men. You have lied to God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says *the Holy Spirit* in one line and *God* in the next, about the same act. It is one of the clearest statements in Scripture of who the Spirit is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pretence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 4 has just described Barnabas doing exactly this — selling a field, bringing the money, laying it at the apostles' feet. Luke names him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ananias performs the same scene with a portion, and presents it as the whole. The sin is not the withholding. It is the performance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke records afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A deep fear came over everyone who heard of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **phobos**, and Luke repeats it a few verses later after Sapphira, extending it to *the whole church*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He offers no commentary, no softening, and no moral. The young men wrap him and carry him out, and the narrative moves on to signs and wonders in the next paragraph."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this sits in Acts.** Immediately before it, a community where *there was not a needy person among them*. Immediately after it, crowds bringing the sick into the streets. The story is set between two descriptions of extraordinary grace, and Luke placed it there."}]}, "Acts 5:12–16": {"title": "\"Every one of them was healed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke's summary of what the early weeks looked like, and he does not hedge the numbers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People even carried the sick out into the streets and laid them on cots and mats… Crowds came in from the towns around Jerusalem too, bringing the sick and those troubled by unclean spirits, and **every one of them was healed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses the same phrase Matthew used of the evening at Peter's house: all of them. He is a careful writer — he says elsewhere that he investigated everything closely — and this is what he reports."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shadow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hoping that as Peter came by, at least his shadow might fall across some of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records what the people hoped. He does not say the shadow healed anybody, and no apostle is described as teaching it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What the detail conveys is the expectation in the city at that moment. People were carrying relatives into the street on the chance of being in the right place when Peter walked past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two reactions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one else dared to attach himself to them, though the people held them in high regard. And still more were added to the Lord, crowds of men and women alike.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both at once — a wariness about joining, and steady growth. Luke has just recorded the deaths of Ananias and Sapphira, and *great fear came on the whole church*. He puts the fear and the crowds in the same paragraph without resolving the tension."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they met"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Solomon's Colonnade* — the covered walkway on the east side of the temple courts. They were meeting in public, at the temple, in the open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two paragraphs later the apostles are arrested there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Luke says was happening among them.** *The believers all gathered with one heart.* The healings, the growth and the unity are reported in one sentence, and Luke keeps making that connection through the early chapters — see also Acts 2:40–41."}]}, "Acts 7:54–60": {"title": "The one time He is seen standing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports a detail here that he does not report anywhere else, and the whole scene turns on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sitting, everywhere but here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The New Testament describes the risen Christ at the right hand of God more than a dozen times, and almost always **seated** — Hebrews makes a point of it, because a seated priest is one whose work is finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stephen, with the court screaming at him, looks up and sees Jesus **standing**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it twice in two verses, once as narration and once in Stephen's own words. He is not being careless with a posture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Stephen says as he dies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sentences, and both of them are Jesus' own words from the cross with one word changed:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Lord Jesus, receive my spirit* — Jesus had said *Father, into Your hands I commit my spirit*", "*Lord, do not hold this sin against them* — Jesus had said *Father, forgive them*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stephen prays to Jesus what Jesus prayed to the Father. For the first Christian to die, that is a considerable thing to have on record, and Luke simply lets it stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He fell asleep\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has just described a stoning in unflinching detail, and he ends it with the gentlest verb available. It became the ordinary Christian word for a believer's death — the same one Paul uses in Thessalonians — and it did not mean the death was mild. It meant it was not final."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The coats at the young man's feet.** Luke names him: Saul. He will not mention it again for chapters, but it is here, in the first paragraph about a Christian being killed, that Paul enters the story — holding the coats."}]}, "Colossians 1:15–20": {"title": "\"In Him all of it holds together\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Probably an early hymn, quoted by Paul, and it is the highest sustained statement about Christ in his letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Firstborn of all creation\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase has been misread for centuries as making Him part of what was created — and the next sentence rules that out: *in Him everything was created.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Prototokos** in the Old Testament is a title of rank, not chronology. God calls Israel His firstborn, and David — the youngest of eight sons — is called firstborn in Psalm 89. It means the one with the rights of the heir."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prepositions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hymn turns on four small words, and they cover everything:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["created **in** Him", "created **through** Him", "created **for** Him", "and He is **before** all things"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the line that holds the passage together: *in Him all of it holds together.* The verb means to stand together, to cohere. The universe is described as continuously held rather than wound up and released."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two firstborns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is *firstborn of all creation* and *firstborn from among the dead*. The hymn puts the two in parallel — first place in the old creation and in the new one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the stated purpose: *so that in everything He might hold the first place.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through Him He should bring everything back into peace with Himself, having made that peace by the blood of His cross.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After thrones, lordships, rulers and authorities, after all things visible and invisible held together — the hymn ends at a cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blood is what the cosmic language was building toward, which is what keeps the passage from being an abstraction."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it lands next.** *And you, who once were alienated and enemies in your mind… He has now reconciled.* Having described the whole universe being brought back, Paul turns immediately to the readers and applies it to them personally. See the note on 2 Corinthians 5:18–19."}]}, "Colossians 1:9–12": {"title": "The chain of a prayer", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of Paul's two great prayers, and it runs as a chain — each link producing the next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The links"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**filled with the knowledge of what He wants**, with wisdom and spiritual understanding", "*so that* you **walk in a way worthy of the Lord**, pleasing Him through and through", "**bearing fruit in every good work** and **growing in the knowledge of God**", "**strengthened with every strength** His glorious power supplies", "ready for **endurance and patience** — *and all of it with joy*", "**giving thanks to the Father**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "It begins with knowing and ends with thanking. Everything in between follows from the first link."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not strength. **Knowledge of what God wants** — and the word for knowledge here is the intensified form, full or thorough knowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's prayers consistently start here. He treats seeing rightly as the thing that produces walking rightly, rather than the other way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strength, and what it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Strengthened with every strength that His own glorious power supplies, ready for endurance of every kind and for patience that does not run out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Enormous language — glorious power, every strength — and then the purpose lands somewhere unexpected. Not for miracles or conquest. For **endurance and patience**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the last three words change the tone completely: *and all of it with joy.* Not grim survival. Staying power that is glad."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last link"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Giving thanks to the Father, who has made us fit to take our share of what belongs to His holy people in the light.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is about qualifying someone, making them sufficient. It is past tense and God is the subject: the fitness was conferred, not developed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the prayer goes next.** Straight into one of the highest passages about Christ in the New Testament — the image of the invisible God, in whom all things hold together. Paul prays for knowledge and then immediately supplies some."}]}, "Colossians 2:4–7": {"title": "Keep on walking in Him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing to a church he has never met, and the thing he is guarding them against is not immorality. It is persuasive speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Talk you around\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I say this so that no one may **talk you around** with words that sound persuasive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is a reckoning word — to miscount, to defraud someone by faulty arithmetic, and so to beguile with plausible reasoning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The danger named is not error that sounds wrong. It is error that adds up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Good order and firm faith"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am glad to see the good order among you and how firm your faith in Christ stands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both nouns are military. **Taxis** is a rank drawn up in formation; **stereōma** is a solid front, a bulwark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, writing from prison to people he has never seen, describes them as a line that is holding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as you **received** Christ Jesus the Lord, **keep on walking** in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and the second is present tense — continuous. However you got in is how you go on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You received Him — you did not achieve Him, negotiate Him, or qualify for Him. Walk the same way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four participles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then four pictures stacked in one sentence, and they do not come from one world:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**rooted in Him** — agricultural, and the tense is a completed act; the root went down once", "**being built up in Him** — architectural, and this one is ongoing", "**made firm in the faith exactly as you were taught** — legal, the word used of a contract being confirmed", "**overflowing with thanksgiving** — a river over its banks"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rooted is done. Built up is happening. That order is deliberate."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter goes on to name.** Philosophy and empty deceit, human tradition, food rules, festivals, new moons, sabbaths, angel worship. All of it answered by one sentence: *in Him all the fullness of the Deity lives in bodily form, and you have been made full in Him.*"}]}, "Colossians 2:8–10": {"title": "\"In Him you have been made complete\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul warns against something and then, instead of arguing point by point, states a fact that makes the alternative unnecessary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take care that no one carries you off as his prize.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is used of kidnapping — carrying someone off as plunder. Not persuading; taking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what does the carrying: *philosophy and hollow deceit… traditions handed down by men… the elementary lessons the world teaches.* Colossae was being sold a package of rules, visions and observances as an upgrade."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fullness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For in Him all the fullness of what God is lives in bodily form.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pleroma** — fullness, the whole content. The word was in use among the teachers Paul is opposing, and he takes it off them: the fullness is not spread across a hierarchy of spiritual beings. It is in Christ, and it is *bodily*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that ends the argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And in Him **you have been made complete**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek uses the same root. The fullness is in Him; you have been filled full in Him. Perfect tense — done, and standing done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why the warning works. Anyone offering an addition is offering to add to something already complete. Nothing on the market can be a next step from here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who is the head over every ruler and every authority.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The powers the false teaching was anxious about — angelic hierarchies, elemental spirits — are put under the same head the reader is joined to."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does not do.** He never refutes the philosophy in detail. He states what Christ is and what the reader has in Him, and lets the offer collapse under the comparison. It is the same move as Ephesians 1: prayer that they would see what they already have, rather than instruction on getting more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before this one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning does not stand on its own. Paul has just written *just as you received Christ Jesus the Lord, keep on walking in Him* — rooted, built up, made firm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what makes the kidnapping possible to resist. A man who is rooted can be approached by a persuasive teacher and stay where he is. See the note on Colossians 2:4–7."}]}, "Colossians 3:15–17": {"title": "Peace as the umpire", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*Let the peace of God have the deciding voice in your hearts.* The English has to spread out to say what the Greek says in one word, and that word is where this verse gets interesting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word is from the stadium"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Brabeuo.** A *brabeus* was the judge at the games — the official who watched the race and said who had won, whose call settled the matter. The verb means to act as that official: to arbitrate, to decide, to rule on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So Paul is not saying *feel peaceful*. He is saying: give peace the umpire's whistle. Let it be the thing that makes the call inside you when two courses of action are competing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a far more practical instruction than it usually gets treated as. An umpire is not an atmosphere. An umpire is a decision-maker."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being umpired"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The context is a chapter about how believers live together — putting off anger and malice, putting on compassion, kindness, humility, patience, bearing with one another and forgiving. Then this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it was into that peace you were called, in one body.* The peace is not first a private calm; it is the peace they were called into *together*. So a good deal of what this umpire is ruling on is how a person deals with other people, and the verse right after it is about the word of Christ living in them richly enough to teach and correct one another."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Reading it for guidance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This verse is used constantly as a way of finding God's will, and that use is not foreign to it. Paul does put peace in the position of the one who decides."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things keep it honest. First, it says *the peace of God*, not the absence of nerves — the same peace Jesus described as not the kind the world hands out. Someone can feel nervous and still be at peace, and can feel enthusiastic and have no peace at all. Second, it sits in a sentence about the body, and Paul immediately surrounds it with the word of Christ dwelling richly and with people teaching one another. Peace umpires; it does not umpire alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And be thankful people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul attaches this to the end of the sentence, and it is easy to read as a pleasantry. In the paragraph it is structural: he says it here, again two lines later with the singing, and a third time at the close — *giving thanks through Him to God the Father*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in three verses. Whatever else the umpired life looks like, Paul seems to think it is audibly grateful."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"whatever you do, in word or in work.\"** The paragraph ends by collapsing the distinction between religious activity and ordinary work — everything, in the name of the Lord Jesus. That is where a chapter about how to live together ends up: not with a special category of holy tasks, but with the whole of a life brought under one name."}]}, "Colossians 3:22–25": {"title": "The wages nobody expected", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing to household slaves, which is worth saying plainly rather than softening into \"servants.\" These were people with no legal standing and no prospect of wages at all — and the sentence he lands on would have been startling in that room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase that does the work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that the Lord Himself will hand you the inheritance as your wages.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words are being deliberately jammed together. An **inheritance** is what a son receives because of who he is. **Wages** are what a worker earns. A slave could expect neither."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says they will be handed the first *as* the second. The thing they could never earn is given to them in the form of the thing they never got."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Work at it from the soul\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is literally *out of the soul*, and it is set against work that only happens while someone is watching — the phrase behind it means eye-service, work done for the eye."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is not to work harder. It is to change who the work is aimed at: *as for the Lord and not for people*. That transfer is what makes a hidden task and a visible one worth the same."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line at the end.** *There is no playing of favorites* — and the very next verse turns to the masters and tells them they have a Master in heaven. In a world that ran on rank, Paul puts both parties under the same eye and tells the ones on the bottom that their unwitnessed work is seen."}]}, "Daniel 2:31–33": {"title": "The great statue", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A pagan king dreams the whole future of the world, and a Jewish captive tells him what he dreamed before interpreting it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Daniel had to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nebuchadnezzar demanded that his wise men tell him the dream itself, not just its meaning — on pain of death for all of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the description in these verses is Daniel telling the king what was in the king's own head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The metals, top to bottom"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**head** — fine gold", "**chest and arms** — silver", "**belly and thighs** — bronze", "**legs** — iron", "**feet** — part iron, part clay"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel names the first outright: *you are that head of gold.* Babylon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, in his own words, *another kingdom inferior to yours*, and another, and a fourth as strong as iron that breaks everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction of travel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each metal down the statue is **less valuable** and **harder**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gold to silver to bronze to iron to iron mixed with baked clay. Worth decreasing, strength increasing, and then the last one is strong and brittle at the same time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Empires get tougher and cheaper as the statue goes down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is standing on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Its **feet** part iron and part clay.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything above rests on the weakest joint in it. Iron and fired clay do not bind — Daniel says so a few verses later: *they will not hold together, just as iron does not mix with clay.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole weight of world history, in this dream, is balanced on a mixture that cannot adhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the king saw it as"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Huge it was, **blazing with a bright and fearsome light**, and the look of it was **terrifying**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how the kingdoms of men look from a throne — enormous, blazing, and frightening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel's God shows the same thing to Daniel as a beast-vision in chapter 7. The king saw a magnificent statue. The prophet saw animals."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the dream yet.** A stone, cut without hands, which strikes the feet and grinds the whole statue to chaff, and then becomes a mountain filling the earth. That is the next part of what Nebuchadnezzar saw, and it is the part the dream was given for. See the note on Daniel 2:44–45."}]}, "Daniel 2:44–45": {"title": "The stone cut without hands", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The statue is enormous, blazing and terrifying, and what destroys it is a rock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not by human hands\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the stone you saw, **cut from the mountain but not by human hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one detail Daniel repeats. It is what distinguishes the stone from everything else in the dream — every empire in the statue was quarried, shaped and raised by men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody made this one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it hits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stone strikes the **feet** — the iron and clay, the weakest joint, the part everything else is standing on. And the whole statue comes down at once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Broke to pieces the iron, the bronze, the clay, the silver, and the gold.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the order Daniel lists them in: from the feet up to the head. It collapses from the bottom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to the pieces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before this describe it: the fragments become *like chaff on a summer threshing floor*, and the wind carries them off so that no trace of them is found."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not defeated and occupied. Gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The kingdom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the days of those kings, **the God of heaven will set up a kingdom that will never be destroyed**. It will not be left to another people… and it will stand forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things said about it:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**never destroyed** — unlike every kingdom in the statue", "**not left to another people** — no succession, no handover; the whole statue is a chain of handovers", "**stand forever**"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the stone becomes a mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The stone that struck the statue became a great mountain and filled the whole earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It arrives small enough to be called a stone and ends up filling everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who this was said to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Babylonian king, by a captive, about the end of Babylon and everything that would follow it — and Nebuchadnezzar's response is to fall on his face."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Truly your God is the God of gods and the Lord of kings.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The great God has made known to the king **what is yet to come**. The dream is true, and its meaning is sure.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel signs off on it. Two clauses, both about reliability."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the stone is called elsewhere.** *The stone the builders rejected has become the chief cornerstone* — Psalm 118, and quoted by Jesus of Himself the week He died, and by Peter in Acts 4. A stone the builders looked at and discarded; a stone no builder cut."}]}, "Daniel 9:24": {"title": "Seventy sevens", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Daniel had been reading Jeremiah, counting the seventy years of the exile. The answer he gets back is about seventy of something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The unit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seventy **sevens** are decreed for your people and your holy city.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shabuim* — sevens, weeks. Seventy sevens is four hundred and ninety."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel had been counting years; the answer comes back in the same currency, and Israel already reckoned in sevens — a sabbath year every seventh, a jubilee after seven sevens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose clock it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Decreed for **your people** and **your holy city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel and Jerusalem. The measure is not running on the world in general."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six things it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "1. *to **finish the transgression*** 2. *to **put an end to sin*** 3. *to **make atonement** for wickedness* 4. *to **bring in everlasting righteousness*** 5. *to **seal up vision and prophecy*** 6. *to **anoint the Most Holy***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them is something Israel does. All six are things done for and to them, and the third one names the mechanism: atonement — the Hebrew is **kaphar**, to cover, the word behind the Day of Atonement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everlasting* righteousness. Not a righteousness that has to be maintained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The division"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses that follow split the four hundred and ninety: seven sevens, then sixty-two sevens, then one seven left over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the going forth of the command to restore and build Jerusalem until Messiah the Prince* — sixty-nine sevens. Four hundred and eighty-three years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, in verse 26, something happens that is not in the arithmetic: *the Anointed One will be cut off and will have nothing.* See the note on Daniel 9:26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sixty-nine sevens run out at the cutting off. The seventieth is described in verse 27 and has not been described as beginning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the sixty-ninth and the seventieth there is a space, and the church age is in it. The clock in this prophecy is Israel's, and it stopped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Daniel was praying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's first half is one of the great prayers of Scripture, and it is confession — *we have sinned, we have done wickedly* — for a nation, by a man with nothing personally to confess."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel arrives *at the time of the evening sacrifice*, in a city with no temple and no sacrifice for seventy years, and tells him he is *greatly beloved*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the answer is bigger than the question.** Daniel asked about seventy years. He was given seventy sevens, and the six things listed are not about getting a people home from Babylon. They are about finishing sin and bringing in a righteousness that does not end."}]}, "Daniel 9:26": {"title": "\"Cut off, and will have nothing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most extraordinary sentence in the prophets, and it is put in five words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it falls in the count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After the sixty-two sevens…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven sevens and sixty-two sevens make sixty-nine — four hundred and eighty-three years from the command to restore and rebuild Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Anointed One"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Anointed One** will be cut off.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Mashiach** — Messiah. Daniel names Him by title, and the previous verse has already used it: *until Messiah the Prince.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Cut off\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **karath** is the ordinary word for executing a covenant-breaker, and it is also the verb used for making a covenant — *to cut* a covenant, from the animals divided in Genesis 15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And will have nothing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clause everything turns on. The older rendering is *but not for Himself*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is cut off, and gets nothing out of it. There is no throne at the end of the sixty-nine sevens, no kingdom, no crown, no gain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah had said the same thing in his own words: *He was cut off from the land of the living; **for the transgressions of My people** He was stricken.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people of **the prince who is to come** will destroy the city and the sanctuary.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written in Babylon, in the sixth century BC, about a city that at that moment lay in ruins and had not yet been rebuilt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Titus and the Roman legions did it in AD 70 — the city and the temple, and not one stone left on another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus said the same, weeping, on the same hill: *they will not leave one stone standing on another in you, because you did not recognise the time when God came to you.* See the note on Luke 19:41–44."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in the verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Messiah is cut off **first**. The city and the sanctuary are destroyed **after**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel's sequence requires the Messiah to come and be killed before AD 70. The temple He had to come to was gone within forty years of the crucifixion, and the genealogies that would prove anyone's descent from David burned with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Its end will come like a flood\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is an overwhelming torrent. And then: *war will continue to the end, and desolations are decreed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence does not stop at AD 70. It runs on."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in this verse.** The seventieth seven. Verse 27 describes it — a covenant made and broken in the middle of the week — and verse 26 stands between the sixty-ninth and it, describing a cutting off, a destroyed city, and a long stretch of war and desolation. The gap is where the sentence puts it. See the note on Daniel 9:24."}]}, "Deuteronomy 11:10–12": {"title": "Not like Egypt", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses describes the land they are entering by telling them what it is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Egypt's agriculture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where you planted your seed and watered it by foot, like a garden of vegetables.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt had no rainfall to speak of. Farming there meant the Nile, irrigation channels, and lifting water — either by foot-driven wheels or by opening and closing channels with your feet in the mud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was reliable and it was relentless. The water was there, and getting it onto the field was entirely your own labour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The land ahead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A land of hills and valleys, drinking in the rain from heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hills cannot be irrigated from a river. Everything depends on rain, which nobody can dig for, schedule, or manufacture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From a farmer's point of view this is a downgrade in security. Moses presents it as an upgrade — and the reason follows immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A land the LORD your God cares for. The eyes of the LORD your God are on it all the time, from the first of the year to the last.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *cares for* is a verb about seeking out and attending to. And *the eyes of the LORD are on it* — continuously, the whole year, without a season off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two descriptions belong together: a land whose water comes from above is a land where attention has to come from above too. What looks like less control is described as more care."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this lands in the New Testament.** Egypt's watering by foot is effort applied to a channel you dug yourself; the hill country is a place you cannot irrigate and must receive. It is the same distinction Paul keeps drawing between working for a wage and receiving a promise — see the note on Romans 4:13–15."}]}, "Deuteronomy 11:13–15": {"title": "The early rain and the late", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two rains, named separately, because in that country they did two different things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the farmer's year looked like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land had a long dry summer with no rainfall at all. Everything depended on two seasons of rain at opposite ends of the growing year:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the early rain** — autumn, October to November. It softened ground baked hard all summer so a plough could break it and seed could go in.", "**the late rain** — spring, March to April. It came just before harvest and filled out the grain in the ear."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Without the first there was no planting. Without the second the crop came up thin and the harvest was poor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why both are promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will send rain on your land in its season, the early rain and the late rain, so you may gather your grain, your new wine, and your oil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise covers the whole cycle, beginning to end. God undertakes to start the year and to finish it — and the three crops named are exactly the three the land was known for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So you may gather\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rain is God's; the gathering is theirs. Nobody in this passage is promised a harvest that appears without a plough."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the shape of it: He supplies what no one can manufacture, and they do the work that rain makes possible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you truly listen… loving the LORD your God and serving Him with all your heart and all your soul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Covenant terms, stated plainly. And notice what the listening is described as — loving and serving, not merely complying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the whole image matters.** These verses come three lines after Moses says the land is not like Egypt, where you watered by foot from a river. See the note on Deuteronomy 11:10–12. The two rains are the reason: a country you cannot irrigate is a country where the harvest is asked for rather than engineered."}]}, "Deuteronomy 11:18–21": {"title": "\"As many as the days the heavens are above the earth\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Where the words are to be kept, and the promise attached to keeping them there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four places"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let these words settle into your **heart** and your **soul**. Tie them on your **hand**… bind them on your **forehead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Inward first, then outward. Hebrew thought does not separate them: what is on the hand governs what you do, what is between the eyes governs what you look at, and both are downstream of the heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Observant Jews still take the outer instruction literally — the small boxes worn on the arm and the head, and the case on the doorpost, come from this passage and its twin in chapter 6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Talk of them when you sit at home and when you walk on the road, when you lie down and when you rise up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two locations and two moments — indoors and out, last thing and first thing. Between them they cover the day, which is the point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here describes a lesson. It describes conversation, in the ordinary places, at the ordinary hours."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being taught"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Teach them to your children.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction about parents' hearts arrives first and the children second. What gets passed on is what has already settled in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that your days and your children's days may be many in the land… as many as the days the heavens are above the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is a measurement that cannot be measured. As long as there is a sky over the ground — that is the span being described."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it is attached to.** These verses come immediately after the promise of the early and late rain, and immediately before *every place where you set your foot will be yours.* The words in the heart, the rain in its season, and the ground underfoot are one passage."}]}, "Deuteronomy 11:26–28": {"title": "\"Today I am setting before you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses lays two options in front of a nation and tells them to choose. It is the clearest statement of the covenant's terms in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The terms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look — today I am setting before you a blessing and a curse. The blessing, if you obey… the curse, if you will not obey.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no ambiguity in it. Blessing is attached to obedience; curse to disobedience. The whole arrangement runs on the word **if**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the Sinai covenant was: a conditional agreement, freely entered into, with both outcomes stated in advance and in detail. Deuteronomy 28 spells out both lists at length."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it assumes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer is genuine and the choice is real. Moses is not describing a trap — he is putting the actual terms in front of people about to enter the land, and he tells them to write them on their doorframes and teach them to their children."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is unfair. Everything depends on a performance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the New Testament takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes this covenant structure and then says something startling about it: *Christ redeemed us from the curse of the law, having become a curse for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two columns did not disappear. He took one of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why a Christian reads this passage from a different position than the crowd standing at the Jordan. The if-clause has been answered by somebody else's obedience — which is the argument of Romans 5:18–19."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How Moses puts it later.** In chapter 30 he sets out the same choice and adds two words that are not here: *therefore choose life.* He does not leave it balanced. Having laid out both, he tells them which one to take."}]}, "Deuteronomy 25:17–19": {"title": "Remember Amalek", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses about one nation, and the instruction opens and closes with the same command about memory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How they fought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How they **met you on the road** and **cut down all the stragglers at the rear**, when you were **weary and worn out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a battle line. Amalek came at the back, where the exhausted were — the old, the sick, the ones carrying children, whoever could not keep the pace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the method of a predator, not an army. You attack what is already struggling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When they came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had just come out of Egypt. They had no land, no army, no experience, and they were *weary and worn out* from the road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek picked the moment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"They had no fear of God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing said about their character, and it is the explanation for the rest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Amalek is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is Esau's grandson — the flesh's line, born of the man who sold a birthright for a bowl of stew and wanted the blessing without the covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek is self-effort: the constant, wearying pressure to get it done yourself, to strive, to keep up, to secure your own position because if you do not do it nobody will. It comes at you hardest when you are already tired, and it takes the stragglers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why it has to be blotted out and not managed. Something that hunts the weary cannot be kept on a leash."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was commanded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You shall **blot out the memory of Amalek** from under heaven. **Do not forget**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two halves are held together on purpose: erase the memory of them, and do not forget to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was to be done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the LORD your God gives you **rest** from all your enemies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not while fighting. From rest. The command was given for the moment of settled security — which is exactly when a man decides the old enemy is no longer worth dealing with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul was sent to do it and spared Agag, the Amalekite king, and the best of the livestock — *but everything despised and worthless, that they utterly destroyed.* He kept what looked useful."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It cost him the kingdom. An Amalekite is the man standing over him claiming to have finished him on Gilboa. And four hundred years later *Haman the Agagite* has a signet ring and a decree."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is spared because it seems useful comes back with authority. See the notes on 2 Samuel 1:5–10 and Esther 3:1–4."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first battle and the raised hands.** Exodus 17 records the fight, and Israel prevailed only while Moses' hands were held up — by Aaron and Hur, because his own arms gave out. Then the altar built and named *The LORD is my banner*, and the words *the LORD will have war with Amalek from generation to generation.* The one enemy that is never beaten by effort was beaten under raised hands somebody else was holding."}]}, "Deuteronomy 28:7–14": {"title": "\"The head and never the tail\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The blessing list of the covenant, and it is as detailed and generous as anything in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["enemies who come at you one way and scatter seven", "blessing on the barns and on everything you set your hand to", "established as His own holy people, so that the nations see it", "overflow of children, livestock, harvest", "*the heavens, His rich storehouse* opened for rain in season", "lending to many nations and borrowing from none", "**the head and never the tail; always on top and never underneath**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The images are agricultural and national — a farming people who wanted rain, children, animals and safety, being told they would have all four."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you listen to His commands and hold to them, never turning aside to run after other gods.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is stated at the end of the passage and it governs everything before it. This is the covenant made at Sinai: a real offer, with real terms, and the curses in the same chapter are the other half of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the chapter whole and the proportions are sobering — fourteen verses of blessing, and over fifty of curse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How a Christian stands to this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul deals with this covenant directly. *Christ redeemed us from the curse of the law, having become a curse for us* — and he says why: *so that the blessing of Abraham might come to the Gentiles.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say the blessings were withdrawn. He says the curse was taken by somebody else, and the blessing let out to people who were never under Sinai at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the difference between reading this list as terms to meet and reading it as a description of what a covenant-keeping God is like toward His people."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"The head and not the tail.\"** The Hebrew image is about position in a line — leading rather than trailing. In context it belongs with lending rather than borrowing: a nation that others come to, rather than one that depends on them."}]}, "Deuteronomy 32:1–4": {"title": "\"Let my teaching fall like rain\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opening of Moses' last song, and he asks for his words to behave like weather."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four kinds of falling water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let my teaching fall like **rain**, let my words settle like the **dew**, like gentle **showers** on new grass, like **soft rain** on the tender green.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four terms, and every one is gentle. Nothing here is a downpour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice he specifies what it lands on: *new grass* and *the tender green* — the most fragile stage of a plant's life, when heavy rain does damage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Teaching, in this image, is measured to what can take it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen, you heavens… hear, O earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses calls sky and ground as witnesses, which is the formal language of a covenant lawsuit. The song that follows is not gentle at all — it is a long account of a people who will forget, and the consequences."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the soft rain of the opening is the manner, not the content."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he proclaims"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will proclaim the name of the LORD. Oh, tell of the greatness of our God!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before a word of the history, he states the subject. The song is about Israel's failure and it is announced as being about God's name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Rock"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is the Rock — His works are perfect, and all His ways are just. A faithful God who does no wrong, upright and true is He.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first time God is called the Rock, and Moses uses it repeatedly through the song. Five statements in one verse, and each is about consistency: perfect, just, faithful, without wrong, upright."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is establishing the standard the rest of the song will measure the people against."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When it was sung.** Moses is about to climb Nebo and die. This song was given to be taught to the children so that when everything he predicted came true, the words would already be in their mouths — a lesson planted before it was needed."}]}, "Deuteronomy 32:19–25": {"title": "\"I will hide My face\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Song of Moses, taught to Israel before they entered the land, and it tells them in advance what will happen and what it will look like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is about to die. He has not yet let them cross the Jordan, and God tells him to write a song and teach it to the people so that *when many troubles come upon them, this song will testify against them, for it will not be forgotten.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a song about the future, given to be memorised before any of it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will hide My face** from them,\" He said, \"and see what their end will be.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence, and it is the sentence the whole exile is described by."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *I will destroy them*. Not *I will leave them*. **Hide My face** — the Hebrew is to conceal, to cover over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What hiding is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of the song is the proof. Read on past this passage and it goes to *the LORD will vindicate His people and have compassion on His servants… He will avenge the blood of His servants… and make atonement for His land and His people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The face is hidden and He is still working. That is the season Hosea named *Lo-Ammi*, and it is the season the book of Esther is set inside — no name of God in ten chapters, and His hand on every one of them. See the notes on Hosea 1:8–9 and Esther 1:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mirror"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They made Me jealous with **what is no god**… so I will make them jealous with **what is no people**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two halves are built to match. *No-god* answers *no-people*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul picks up exactly this line in Romans 10 and applies it to the Gentiles being brought in — the *no-people* who become *sons of the living God*. See the note on Romans 9:25–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Children who have no faith\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is children in whom there is no **emun** — no faithfulness, no steadiness, from the root behind *amen*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not children who did wrong. Children with nothing dependable in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the song is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD saw it and turned away, **grieved by His sons and daughters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is grieved and they are still called sons and daughters. The relationship is named in the same verse as the turning away."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the song ends.** Not with the arrows. *Rejoice, O nations, with His people, for He will avenge the blood of His servants… and provide atonement for His land and His people.* Moses taught them the whole thing, and the last note in it is atonement."}]}, "Deuteronomy 33:24–25": {"title": "\"May your strength match all your days\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses' last words, blessing the tribes one by one. Asher's blessing ends with a line that has outlived its context by a long way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let your gates be barred with iron and bronze, and may your strength match all your days.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is famously compressed — four words, roughly *as your days, your strength*. There is no verb in it, which is why translations differ: *as thy days, so shall thy strength be*, or *your strength will equal your days*, or *may your strength match all your days*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sense everyone agrees on is a ratio. Whatever the days require, the strength is measured to match."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why iron and bronze"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asher's territory was the northern coastal strip — fertile, rich in olive oil, and exposed. *Let him bathe his feet in oil* is about the olive presses; the barred gates are about the border."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blessing pairs prosperity with security, and then adds strength for the days themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How to read it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a blessing spoken over a tribe by a dying man, not a promise of physical vigour to individuals. Read as it stands, it says the supply is matched to the demand — not stockpiled in advance, and not exhausted before the days are."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verses after it.** Moses ends the whole blessing with *the eternal God is your refuge, and underneath are the everlasting arms* — and then he climbs Nebo and dies. The last recorded thing he does is bless people who are going somewhere he is not."}]}, "Deuteronomy 34:5–8": {"title": "A hundred and twenty, and a grave nobody found", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last paragraph about Moses, and it records three things nobody else in the Bible gets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He was buried by God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He buried him in the valley in Moab… but to this day no one knows where his grave is.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The subject of that verb is the LORD. Moses climbs a mountain alone, dies alone, and is buried by God — no funeral party, no family plot, no marker."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason is not given. Jude preserves a strange fragment about a dispute over the body, and the plainest guess is the simplest: a known grave of Moses would have become a shrine within a generation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The physical detail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses was a hundred and twenty years old when he died, yet his eyes were not weak and his strength had not left him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer goes out of his way to say Moses was not failing. He did not die of old age in the ordinary sense; he died because his part was finished and the river was somebody else's to cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That detail sits in scripture alongside Psalm 90 — Moses' own psalm, with its seventy years, eighty if we are strong. The man who wrote that line lived to 120 undimmed. The Bible keeps both on the page."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Thirty days.** Israel wept for Moses for a month, the same span they later gave Aaron. Then the book ends by saying no prophet has risen since like him, whom the LORD knew face to face — and the next book opens with God telling Joshua that Moses is dead and it is time to cross. Both the grief and the moving on are given their room."}]}, "Deuteronomy 8:6–9": {"title": "\"A land where you will lack nothing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses describes the country ahead in the concrete terms of somebody who has been living on manna for forty years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Streams and springs, water welling up from valleys and hills; wheat and barley, vines and fig trees and pomegranates; olive oil and honey; bread without going without; stones of iron, and hills you can dig copper from.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven crops — the same seven the rabbis later called the species of the land — plus water, bread, and metal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a poetic abundance. It is an inventory: food, drink, and the raw material for tools and weapons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two lines that frame it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before: *keep the commands of the LORD your God — walk in His ways and hold Him in awe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After, a few verses on: *when you have eaten and are satisfied, praise the LORD your God for the good land He has given you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The abundance sits between an instruction and a thanksgiving. Moses is not worried about their poverty. He is worried about what full barns do to memory — the very next warning is *be careful that you do not forget.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The danger he names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You may say to yourself, \"My power and the strength of my hands have produced this wealth for me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the risk this chapter exists to address, and it comes with the remedy: *remember the LORD your God, for it is He who gives you the ability to produce wealth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that the ability is unreal. That its source is elsewhere."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the manna comes first.** The chapter opens with forty years of being humbled, hungry, and fed with something they had never seen — *to teach you that man does not live on bread alone.* The lesson in the desert is what makes the land safe to enter. Jesus quotes that line in His own wilderness. See the note on Matthew 4:1–4."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 2:24–26": {"title": "\"The task of gathering and piling up\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first place in Ecclesiastes where the Preacher stops complaining and says something plain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he has been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters of trying everything. Wisdom, pleasure, wine, houses, vineyards, gardens, pools, servants, herds, silver and gold, singers, every delight — *whatever my eyes desired I did not keep from them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *I hated life… I hated all my labour, because I must leave it to the man who comes after me, and who knows whether he will be wise or a fool?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is nothing better for a person than to **eat and drink and find joy in his work**. And this, I saw, is **from the hand of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread, a drink, and satisfaction in the day's work. That is the whole list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second sentence is what makes it more than resignation — he names where it comes from. Ordinary contentment is described as a gift handed over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Apart from Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **apart from Him, who can eat, who can find any real enjoyment?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not who can afford to eat. Who can eat and get anything out of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just proved by experiment that a man can own everything in that list and enjoy none of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To the one who **pleases Him**, God gives **wisdom and knowledge and joy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of the three is money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But to the one who misses the mark, He gives **the task of gathering and piling up, only to hand it over** to the one who pleases God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to gather and to heap up*. It is described as an assignment — a **task** given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the collecting. Somebody else does the keeping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs says the same thing twice: *the wealth of the sinner is stored up for the righteous*, and *whoever swells his wealth by interest gathers it for one who is kind to the poor.* See the note on Proverbs 28:7–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"This too is vapour\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This too is vapour, a chasing after the wind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **hebel** — breath, vapour, what you can see on a cold morning and cannot hold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He applies it to the gathering, not to the gift. The eating and drinking and joy are *from the hand of God*; the heaping up is wind."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the book ends.** *Fear God and keep His commandments, for this is the whole duty of man.* Twelve chapters of a man with unlimited means testing every avenue, and the conclusion is two clauses long."}]}, "Ephesians 1:15–19": {"title": "What Paul prays for people who already have everything", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just spent twelve verses listing what these believers possess. Then he prays — and he does not ask for a single additional thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit of wisdom and of unveiling, in coming to know Him — that the eyes of your understanding would be flooded with light.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word behind *unveiling* is **apokalypsis**, the uncovering of something already there. Not new information. The removal of whatever is in the way of seeing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *the eyes of your understanding* is literally *the eyes of your heart* — the seeing organ Paul is worried about is not the intellect alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things he wants them to see"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**what the hope is that He called you into**", "**how glorious and rich a share He has among His holy people**", "**how far past measuring His power is toward us who believe**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second one is worth reading twice. The Greek most naturally means **His** inheritance in the saints — God's own share, and the people are it. Paul has already said in verse 11 that we have obtained an inheritance; now he prays they would grasp that they *are* one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer is for perception, not supply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole shape of it. Everything in chapter 1 has already been given — chosen, adopted, redeemed, forgiven, sealed. Paul's response is to pray that they would know it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the same thing in chapter 3, where the prayer is that they would have power to grasp the dimensions of a love that surpasses knowledge."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The measure of the power.** *Power in step with the working of His mighty strength* — and then the next verse says what strength that was: the force that raised Christ and seated Him above every authority. Paul's answer to \"how much power is available\" is to point at the resurrection. See the note on Ephesians 1:20–23."}]}, "Ephesians 1:20–23": {"title": "How far above, and what it has to do with you", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is praying that they would know the power at work in them, and to describe its size he points at one event."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure he uses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That was the strength He put to work in Christ when He raised Him from the dead and seated Him at His own right hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The resurrection is offered as the unit of measurement. Not a general statement that God is powerful — the specific force applied on one morning, named as the same force now at work toward believers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The height"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*High above every ruler and every authority and power and lordship, and above every name that can be named, not only in this present age but in the age still to come.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul empties the vocabulary. Four categories of authority, then *every name that can be named*, then both ages — this one and the next."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is deliberately leaving nothing outside the sentence. Whatever it is, whatever it is called, whenever it exists, it is under."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything so far is about Christ's exaltation. Then:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*And He put everything under His feet, and gave Him, over all things, **as head to the church, which is His body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The elevation is not held privately. He is given as head *to the church* — and a head and a body are not in two locations. Paul will say it outright two chapters on: seated together with Him in the heavenly places."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Where the head is seated is where the body is."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"The fullness of the One who fills everything.\"** Paul calls the church that, which is an extraordinary thing to call a scattered collection of first-century congregations with the problems his letters keep addressing. He does it anyway."}]}, "Ephesians 1:3–6": {"title": "Everything already given, and where it is kept", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opening of the longest sentence in the New Testament, and it begins with a claim in the past tense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Has blessed us\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *will bless*. The verb is aorist — done, complete, a settled fact by the time Paul writes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the extent is total: *with every blessing the Spirit has to give*. Nothing is being held in reserve pending performance. Whatever the Christian life turns out to involve, Paul's starting position is that the giving has already happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Up where heaven is* — in the heavenly places. Twice more in this letter Paul uses the phrase, and it is not a description of somewhere distant. It is where Christ is seated, and where he later says believers are seated with Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the blessings are located in the same place as the person they are held in. That is the force of the phrase Paul repeats through this whole passage: **in Him**, in Christ, in the One He loves. Eleven times in twelve verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Before the world was ever laid down\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The choosing is dated earlier than creation, and the purpose is stated: *so that we would stand before Him holy, without a single flaw in us, in love.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things about that. It is the goal of the choosing, not its condition. And the last two words attach either to the standing or to the choosing itself — Greek allows both, and either way love is in the sentence rather than obligation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The reason given.** *This was simply what pleased Him, and what He wanted.* Paul offers no cause outside God's own good pleasure, and then says the point of the whole thing is *the praise of the shining beauty of His grace*. When he comes to explain why any of it happened, he does not point at anything in us."}]}, "Ephesians 1:7–10": {"title": "Bought out, and the word for it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is one long sentence into this letter and shows no sign of stopping. In Greek, verses 3 to 14 are a single unbroken sentence — the longest in the New Testament — and this is the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word behind \"bought out of our captivity\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Apolutrosis.** The root is *lutron*, a ransom — the price paid to free a prisoner of war or to buy a slave out of ownership. The prefix makes it a release accomplished by that payment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a commercial word being used about people, and it carries a specific picture: someone was held, a price was named, the price was paid, and the person walked out. Paul says where the price came from in the same breath — *by His blood*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Poured over us without measure\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase for grace here is extravagant even by Paul's standards: the *sheer wealth* of His grace, and a verb meaning to make something overflow, to have more than enough of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not describing a sufficiency. He is describing a surplus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the surplus is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It brought with it every kind of wisdom and clear-eyed good sense.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is an unexpected turn. The overflow of grace does not arrive as feeling — it arrives as understanding, and specifically as being let in on something: *He let us in on the secret of what He wants.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the sentence is going.** The purpose Paul names is enormous: that everything, in heaven and on earth, would be gathered up under one head in Christ. The Greek verb is from arithmetic — adding a column of figures and writing the total at the top. All of it, summed up, in Him. That is the end the ransom was paid toward, and Paul reaches it without once pausing for a full stop."}]}, "Ephesians 2:4–7": {"title": "\"But God\" — and three verbs with \"together\" in them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two of the most consequential words in the New Testament, and then three verbs that all say the same thing about location."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"But God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence before this is a description of the dead: following the course of this world, by nature children of wrath. Then the turn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say *but they tried harder*, or *but God saw potential*. He says *But God — so rich in mercy, and loving us with a love so great*, and the reason given for everything that follows is entirely on His side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each one in Greek is a compound built with *syn* — together with:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**made us alive together** with Christ", "**raised us up together** with Him", "**seated us together** with Him"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not alongside as spectators. The same three things that happened to Christ — resurrection, ascension, enthronement — described as having happened to us with Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And all three are aorist: completed. Not a process under way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Even while we were dead\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The timing is stated twice for emphasis. The making-alive happened while the condition was death, not after any improvement. Dead men do not cooperate with their own resurrection, which is Paul's point — hence the parenthesis he cannot help inserting: *by grace you have been saved.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that in the ages still to come He could show the overflowing wealth of His grace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The purpose is exhibition. We are the evidence God intends to display, and the thing being displayed is the size of His kindness — with the ages to come as the audience."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where \"up where heaven is\" leads.** Paul has already put Christ there in chapter 1, seated far above every ruler and authority. Now the same location is given to the church, because a head and a body are not in two places. See the note on Ephesians 1:20–23."}]}, "Ephesians 4:1–3": {"title": "\"Guard the oneness the Spirit gives you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hinge of the letter. Three chapters of what God has done, and then the first instruction — and it is about how to treat each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not being asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Working hard to **guard** the oneness the Spirit gives you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to create it. Not to achieve it. The unity already exists — it is the Spirit's, and it is given. The job is to keep it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That distinction changes the work entirely. Nobody is being asked to manufacture agreement. They are being asked not to break something that is already there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four qualities"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Never above anyone, gentle in your handling of people, patient, putting up with one another because you love one another.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**never above anyone** — the Greek word for lowliness of mind. In classical Greek it was an insult, a term for a grovelling nature. The New Testament picks it up and makes it a virtue.", "**gentle** — the word for strength under control, used of a broken horse", "**patient** — long-tempered, the same word used of God in 2 Peter 3:9", "**putting up with one another** — bearing with, enduring"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is a quality you only need when someone is being difficult. This is not a description of people who naturally get along."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The tie of peace\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a bond, a ligament — the thing that holds a joint together. Paul uses the same image later in the chapter for the body being knit together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peace is not the absence of friction here. It is the connective tissue."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"I, the Lord's prisoner.\"** Paul opens the practical half of his letter by naming where he is writing from. The man asking them to be patient with each other is in chains, and he mentions it once, without complaint, as his credential for asking."}]}, "Ephesians 5:22–24": {"title": "\"He Himself is the Saviour of the body\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses that get quoted on their own, and almost everything about them depends on the sentence before and the twenty-five verses after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb is not in the Greek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wives, submit to your own husbands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no verb in verse 22 in the oldest manuscripts. It is carried over from the previous sentence — *submitting to one another in the fear of God* — which is addressed to everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The paragraph begins with mutual submission and then works out what it looks like in a household."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As to the Lord\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The measure is set immediately. Not *because he has earned it*, and not *however he asks for it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Head"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the husband is **head** of the wife, as Christ also is **head of the church**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is what fills the word. Whatever headship means here, it means what Christ's headship means, and Paul spends the next twelve verses saying what that is:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Husbands, love your wives, **just as Christ also loved the church and gave Himself for her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern set for the husband is a man on a cross. Nothing in the passage authorises anything else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*— and **He Himself is the Saviour of the body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul breaks his own comparison to say it. He has just lined up husband/wife with Christ/church, and then he stops and notes the point at which the parallel does not run: Christ is the Saviour of the body, and no husband is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The body.** Not the soul, not the spirit. Paul is talking about the church as a body and he says outright that Christ saves it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same thing a few verses later: *no one ever hated his own flesh, but nourishes and cherishes it, **just as the Lord does the church** — for we are members of His body.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nourishes and cherishes.** The second Greek word is used of a bird warming her young under her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole passage is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is a great mystery, but I speak concerning **Christ and the church**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Paul's own summary, and it comes at the end. The marriage was never the subject. It is the picture, and he says which way the picture points."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the husband is not given.** No authority to command, correct, or require anything. Every instruction in the passage addressed to him is about what he gives: love her, give yourself for her, nourish her, cherish her, love her as your own body. The list is one-directional."}]}, "Ephesians 5:28–30": {"title": "\"Of His flesh and of His bones\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing about marriage and says something about the church that goes further than the illustration needed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one ever hated his own flesh; **he feeds it and tends it warmly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *tends it warmly* is **thalpo** — to keep warm, and it is used of a bird brooding over her young in the nest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's case to husbands is not built on duty. It is built on what a man already does for his own body without being asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then the turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*— exactly as **the Lord does the church**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two verbs, applied to Him. He feeds it and He broods over it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause that goes furthest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **we are parts of His body, of His flesh and of His bones**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul could have stopped at *His body*. He does not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is Adam's, word for word, when he first saw Eve: *this is now **bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adam said it about a woman taken out of his side while he slept. Paul says it about the church, and two verses later he quotes the rest of that sentence — *for this reason a man shall leave his father and mother and be joined to his wife, and the two shall become one flesh* — and adds: *this is a great mystery, but I speak concerning Christ and the church.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the union is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an arrangement, an alliance, or a membership. Paul uses the language of one body."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who is joined to the Lord is one spirit with Him*, he says in the other letter. And in this one: *God raised us up together, and made us sit together in the heavenly places in Christ Jesus* — the tense is past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that means about the body"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The head is in one place and the body is not sick in a way the head is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever is true of Him is what the passage says is true of the parts. *As He is, so are we in this world* — 1 John says it about now, not later. See the note on 1 John 4:17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the instruction lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Husbands ought to love their own wives as their own bodies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything addressed to the husband in this passage is about what he gives: love her, give yourself for her, feed her, keep her warm."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was taken from His side.** Adam's bride came out of an opened side while he slept. On the cross, a soldier *pierced His side with a spear, and immediately blood and water came out.* John records it and then insists on it: *he who has seen it has borne witness, and his testimony is true.*"}]}, "Ephesians 6:1–4": {"title": "The first commandment with a promise attached", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes the fifth commandment and adds a note about it — *which is the first commandment with a promise attached to it* — and the aside is worth as much as the quotation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is pointing out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of the ten, this is the first that comes with an outcome named: *so that it may go well with you and you may live a long life on the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not inventing the promise; he is drawing attention to the fact that it is there. In a list mostly of prohibitions, one command arrives with a stated benefit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How to read the promise honestly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a promise in the shape a promise takes in the Old Testament — a general truth about how a society and a life tend to run, not a personal guarantee. The same Bible records godly people who honoured their parents and died young."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read as it was written, it is saying that a people who hold the generations together last, and a people who do not, do not. Everyone who has watched a family either work or come apart knows the observation is sound."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line to fathers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not stir your children up to anger.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul turns and addresses the party with all the power, which in a first-century household was a genuinely radical thing to do. The word describes provoking someone into exasperation — the slow kind, built up over time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the alternative he gives is not passivity: *raise them in the training and correction of the Lord*. Not less involvement. Involvement of a different sort."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"obey\" and \"honour.\"** Paul uses both, and they are not the same. Obeying belongs to children in a household. Honouring is what the commandment asks, and it has no expiry date — it is what an adult still owes an elderly parent long after obedience has stopped being the relevant word."}]}, "Ephesians 6:10–13": {"title": "\"Having done everything, to stand\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's last section, and the verb he keeps returning to is not what people expect from a passage about warfare."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word he repeats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Stand.** Four times in four verses: stand against the schemes, stand your ground, and — having done everything — *stand*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not advance, not conquer, not take. The posture being described is holding a position that has already been won, which is consistent with everything in the first three chapters: seated with Christ, above every ruler and authority."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You defend ground you already hold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Be strong in the Lord\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is passive — *be strengthened*. It is not an exhortation to summon resolve. And the strength named is *the strength of His might*, the same phrase Paul used in chapter 1 of the power that raised Christ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the fight is with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not against flesh and blood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul rules out people first. Whatever the Ephesians were facing — and the city was famous for its magic and its temple of Artemis — he tells them the opposition is not the human beings in front of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four terms follow: ruling powers, authorities, world-rulers of this present darkness, spiritual armies of wickedness in the heavenly places. He does not define them, and no one since has been able to sort them into a tidy hierarchy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The whole armour of God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice. It is **God's own** armour — Isaiah describes the LORD putting on righteousness as a breastplate and a helmet of salvation, and Paul is handing the same equipment to the church."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing on the list is manufactured by the wearer."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is in the list.** Truth, righteousness, the gospel of peace, faith, salvation, the word of God — and then prayer. Every item is something already given in the first half of the letter. Chapter 6 is chapters 1 to 3, put on."}]}, "Esther 1:1–4": {"title": "The king who ruled the world", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book opens on the most powerful man on earth, and God is not mentioned. He will not be mentioned anywhere in the ten chapters that follow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is missing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no name of God in Esther. No prayer is recorded, no temple, no sacrifice, no prophet, no miracle, no angel, no law quoted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That absence is the book. It is the story of a people God had told, through Hosea, *you are not My people* — and it is set in exactly that season, when His face was hidden and His hand was not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not absent from the story. He is not named in it. There is a difference, and the whole of Esther is written to show it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The empire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From India all the way to Cush, a hundred and twenty-seven provinces.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahasuerus is Xerxes. The reach described is the largest empire the world had seen to that point, and the writer measures it in provinces rather than miles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Jews are somewhere inside it, scattered, with no land, no king and no temple to go to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "A hundred and eighty days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For a long while — a full **hundred and eighty days** — he laid out before them the wealth of his glorious kingdom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six months of display. The verb is to show, to make visible; the whole of it is about being seen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then a further seven-day feast for everybody in Susa, which the next verses describe down to the marble pillars and the silver couches."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the world looks like at the start"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every visible thing belongs to Ahasuerus. The wealth is his, the law is his, the army is his, and by the end of chapter 3 the life of every Jew in the empire is his to sign away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where the book starts, and it starts there deliberately. The reader is meant to look at all of it and ask the question the whole book answers."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The name hidden in the text.** Four times in the Hebrew of Esther the letters of the name **YHWH** stand at the start of four consecutive words, spelled out forwards twice and backwards twice. And once, in Esther 7:5, the letters of **I AM** — *ehyeh* — are formed the same way, in the sentence where the king demands to know who has dared to do this thing. The name is in the book. It is written the way He was working: present, and not announced."}]}, "Esther 1:16–20": {"title": "A small refusal, a great fear", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most powerful court in the world panics over one woman not coming to dinner, and legislates."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escalation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Queen Vashti has not only wronged the king, but **every official and every people in all the provinces**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Memucan takes a private embarrassment and makes it an imperial crisis in one sentence. A hundred and twenty-seven provinces are now involved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were afraid of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Word of what the queen has done will get out to all the women, and they will look down on their own husbands.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fear is of a story travelling. Not of Vashti — of what people will say about her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There will be no end of contempt and quarreling.\"* The men who ruled from India to Cush are worried about arguments at home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remedy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let him send out a royal decree, written into the laws of Persia and Media **so it cannot be revoked**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The irrevocability of Persian law is introduced here, casually, in chapter 1. It will matter enormously in chapter 8, when a death sentence on a whole people cannot be cancelled either."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer plants it early, in a trivial context, so that the reader already knows the rule when everything depends on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that makes room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let the king give her royal place **to another woman better than she**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Memucan does not know what he has just said. He is drafting a decree about household order, and the clause he adds at the end opens the throne of Persia."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four chapters later a Jewish girl sits in that place, and the empire's entire Jewish population is alive because of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody prays. Nobody consults a prophet. Nobody mentions God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A drunk king, a proud queen, and a nervous adviser between them arrange the one thing that will be needed. See the note on Esther 1:9–12."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line of the decree.** *Then every wife will honor her husband, from the greatest to the least.* An empire-wide law was passed to make women respectful, and the historical result of it, so far as the book records, is that the Jews were not exterminated."}]}, "Esther 1:9–12": {"title": "Queen Vashti's own feast", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A queen says no, and a vacancy opens that will save a nation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seventh day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the seventh day, when the king's heart was merry with wine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer tells us the state he was in before he tells us what he ordered. Six days of drinking are behind the command."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he wanted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To bring Queen Vashti before him wearing her royal crown. He wanted to **display her beauty** to the people and the officials.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb as the six months of showing off the kingdom's wealth. She is the last item in the display — the treasury, the army, the palace, and then his wife."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Seven names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mehuman, Biztha, Harbona, Bigtha, Abagtha, Zethar, and Carkas.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer lists all seven eunuchs, and no reader has ever needed to know their names. He does the same with the seven princes a few verses later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book keeps its eye on detail like this, on court procedure and paperwork and who was standing where. That is the level at which the whole rescue is going to happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Would not come\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in the Hebrew, and the empire tilts on them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the text explains her. No reason is given, no speech recorded. She simply does not come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the refusal produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The king was furious, and the anger smoldered in him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Out of that anger comes the decree, out of the decree comes the vacancy, and out of the vacancy comes a Jewish orphan on the throne of Persia four years before Haman writes his letters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in chapter 1 is trying to save anybody. A queen refuses a drunk man's summons and a place is made ready that will be needed later."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the book is doing here.** God is not named and nothing miraculous happens. A woman's pride, a king's temper and a courtier's advice do all the work — and every one of them is moving a piece into position. He is ruling through it while none of them know His name."}]}, "Esther 2:15–16": {"title": "The girl who asked for nothing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence in this passage is doing quiet work, and it is easy to read past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What every other woman did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has already explained the arrangement: each young woman went in to the king once, and could take with her whatever she asked for from the harem. One night, one chance, and an open request."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You can imagine what most of them took."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Esther took"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She asked for nothing except what Hegai, the king's eunuch and keeper of the women, suggested.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hegai had been running that household for years. He knew the king. Esther, with one opportunity and permission to ask for anything, asked the man who actually knew what he was talking about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not passivity — she is a woman in an impossible position playing the one card available with unusual coolness. It is judgement."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**A book that never mentions God.** Esther is the only book in the Bible in which God's name does not appear, and moments like this are why readers have always felt Him in it anyway: nothing miraculous happens, and yet the details keep landing right. The story is told entirely at ground level — a favour here, a sleepless night there — and the shape only becomes visible from the far end of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word the chapter keeps using"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Esther **obtained favour** in the sight of all who looked at her.* The Hebrew is **chen** — grace, unearned favour, the same word used of Noah finding grace in the eyes of the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter uses it about her again and again. She wins the position without qualifications: no family, no wealth, no nation to speak for her, an orphan and a captive. What she has is favour, and the writer says so every time she advances."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is what carries her in chapter 2 and it is what saves her life in chapter 5, when a golden sceptre is held out to a woman who came in unsummoned. See the note on Esther 5:1–2."}]}, "Esther 2:5–7": {"title": "A Jew in the fortress, a girl in his care", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two people are introduced, and everything the book needs is in their description."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The genealogy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mordecai son of Jair, son of Shimei, son of **Kish**, a **Benjamite**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Kish, a Benjamite, is the name of Saul's father. The writer is pointing at something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul was the Benjamite king commanded to destroy Amalek, and he did not. Haman is introduced two chapters later as *the Agagite* — Agag being the Amalekite king Saul spared."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A descendant of Kish and a descendant of Agag, in the same court, four hundred years later. The unfinished business of 1 Samuel 15 is the plot of this book. See the note on Deuteronomy 25:17–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Carried away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His family had been carried away from Jerusalem among the captives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mordecai is in Susa because his people lost their land. He is not there by ambition. He is there because of the exile, in the middle of the season Hosea called *not My people*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mordecai was raising **Hadassah, that is, Esther**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hadassah** is Hebrew — the myrtle. **Esther** is the Persian name she is known by in the palace, and it is the name on the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The myrtle is a fragrant shrub that gives off its scent most strongly when it is crushed, and Isaiah 55 puts it in a promise: *instead of the brier shall come up the myrtle tree.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her Hebrew name is hidden under a foreign one, in a book where God's name is hidden under an ordinary story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"She had neither father nor mother\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in three lines. The writer wants it registered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The girl who will stand between a nation and its extermination is an orphan in a foreign city, with no standing, no family, and no claim on anybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mordecai had taken her as his own daughter.* The Hebrew is literally *took her for a daughter*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is already in place.** Before Haman is promoted, before the decree is written, before anyone knows there is a danger — a Benjamite of Kish's line is in the fortress and the girl in his house is beautiful. The pieces are set out before the game starts."}]}, "Esther 3:1–4": {"title": "Haman lifted high", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The villain is introduced with a family name, and the family name is the whole argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The Agagite\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Haman son of Hammedatha **the Agagite**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Agag was the king of Amalek whom Saul was ordered to destroy and chose to spare — 1 Samuel 15, the disobedience that cost Saul the kingdom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek was the nation that attacked Israel from behind in the wilderness, picking off the tired and the stragglers, and God said He would have war with them from generation to generation. See the note on Deuteronomy 25:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four centuries after Saul let Agag live, Agag's descendant is second in the Persian empire and signing a death warrant for every Jew alive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is not put to death does not stay small. It comes back with an office and a signet ring."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promotion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*King Ahasuerus promoted Haman… raising him higher than all the other officials.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No reason is given. Haman has done nothing in the book. Mordecai, by contrast, has just saved the king's life in the previous four verses — and got nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts those two facts side by side without comment. It looks like the wrong man rising, and for three chapters it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Mordecai would not bow\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole court bows. It has been commanded. Mordecai stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives one reason, and it is the only reason in the book: *he had told them he was a Jew.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue, campaign, or explain further. He is asked *day after day* and *he would not listen.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the servants were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they told Haman, **to see whether Mordecai's reason would stand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They report him in order to watch what happens. That is the sentence that lights the fuse — a small piece of court malice, and an empire-wide massacre is drafted because of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**A Benjamite and an Amalekite.** Mordecai has already been introduced as *son of Kish, a Benjamite* — Saul's line. The old war Saul left unfinished is standing in a Persian gateway, and this time the Benjamite does not bow. See the note on Esther 2:5–7."}]}, "Esther 3:12–15": {"title": "Letters sealed with the king's ring", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The genocide is not a mob. It is paperwork, properly filed, in every language of the empire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The machinery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The king's scribes were called… to each province in **its own script** and to each people in **its own language**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Translated for every people so that nobody could plead they had not understood. The empire's whole administrative apparatus is turned on a single ethnic group."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sealed with the king's ring.* Under Persian law that made it irrevocable — the rule the reader was taught back in chapter 1, when it was only about a queen not coming to dinner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ordering that all Jews be **destroyed, killed, and wiped out**, young and old, little children and women, all in a single day — and their goods taken as plunder.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The piling-up is deliberate. And the phrase *young and old, little children and women* is Amalek's signature: the people at the back, the ones who cannot run."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plunder clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their goods taken as plunder.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haman is buying the empire's cooperation. Anyone who kills a Jew keeps the house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hold this clause in mind — in chapter 9, when the Jews defend themselves and win, the writer says three separate times that *they did not lay a hand on the plunder.* They were entitled to it under the counter-decree and they left it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eleven months"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lot had fallen on the thirteenth of Adar. The letters go out in the first month, Nisan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every Jew in the world now has a date on which they are legally to be killed, and eleven months to look at it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the king and Haman sat down to drink**, while the city of Susa reeled in confusion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men with a glass, and a city outside the window not knowing what has hit it. The writer sets the two clauses in one line and lets them stand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the low point of the book. There is no God named, no prophet, no army, no appeal — a sealed law that cannot be revoked, and the men who wrote it are drinking."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the reader knows and Susa does not.** Nisan is the month of Passover. The decree ordering the destruction of the Jews was written on the day before the feast that commemorates their deliverance."}]}, "Esther 3:5–6": {"title": "A rage that would not stop at one man", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and a private insult becomes a policy of extermination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Too small a thing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It seemed **too small a thing** to lay hands on Mordecai alone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Killing the man who offended him is beneath the scale of Haman's anger. He wants proportion, and the proportion he settles on is a nation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is contemptuous — it despised in his eyes. Murder was not enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What made it possible"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They had told him **who Mordecai's people were**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servants' report in the verses before did this. Mordecai's answer had been *I am a Jew*, and that single fact converts one man's stubbornness into a target the size of an empire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Amalek doing what Amalek does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haman is *the Agagite*, and this is Amalek's method exactly as Deuteronomy describes it: not a declared war on soldiers, but an attack on a whole people including *young and old, little children and women* — the phrase in the decree nine verses later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek attacked the stragglers at the back. Haman writes them into the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To wipe out **all the Jews, every one of them**, throughout the whole kingdom of Ahasuerus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred and twenty-seven provinces. There was nowhere in the known world a Jew could go that this decree would not reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had no land, no king, no army and no temple. There was no earthly protection anywhere in the picture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God is still not mentioned. Nobody prays in this chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reader who knows the end of the book knows what is already true while Haman is planning: a Jewish girl has been queen of Persia for four years, and a king's insomnia is coming."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The date they cast lots for.** Haman throws the *pur* — the lot — to pick the day, and it falls on the thirteenth of Adar, eleven months away. The one thing he leaves to chance gives his enemies a year to live. Proverbs says the lot is cast into the lap, but its every decision is from the LORD."}]}, "Esther 4:12–14": {"title": "For such a time as this", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mordecai's answer is the theological centre of a book that never mentions God, and he manages to say everything without using the name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Relief and deliverance will arise from another place\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you stay silent now, **relief and deliverance for the Jews will arise from another place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is completely certain the rescue is coming. That is not in doubt anywhere in his sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *perhaps we will be saved*. He says the deliverance will arrive, with or without her — and he does not name where from. *Another place* is as close as this book comes to saying the name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question on the table is never whether the Jews will be delivered. It is whether Esther will be in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You and your father's house will perish\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one who stands to lose is the one who stays out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had just sent word that going in unsummoned carries a death sentence unless the golden sceptre is held out. Mordecai answers that the danger she is weighing is not the real danger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Who knows whether you have come to the palace for such a time as this?\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every step that put her in that room was somebody else's doing. A queen's refusal, a drunk king's temper, an adviser's decree, a eunuch's advice, a beauty contest she did not enter herself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mordecai looks at that chain and reads it correctly: she did not arrive there by accident, and the position was given for a reason that is only now becoming visible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who knows* — he does not claim a revelation. There is no prophet in this book. He is reading providence from the shape of it, which is what the whole book asks its reader to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her answer is three verses later: gather the Jews, fast three days, *and if I perish, I perish.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She goes in. And the position that had been prepared for her turns out to be the place the whole nation is saved from."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Mordecai is not saying.** He is not telling her the outcome depends on her. He has already ruled that out — the deliverance is coming from another place. He is telling her there is a part with her name on it, and that she can miss it. See the note on Esther 5:1–2."}]}, "Esther 5:1–2": {"title": "The golden sceptre", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and they carry the weight of the whole book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the **third day** Esther put on her royal robes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days of fasting are behind this sentence. She had asked for them, and she comes out of them on the third day and walks in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she wore"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her **royal robes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not sackcloth. She had been in mourning clothes at the end of chapter 4 — the Hebrew here is literally *she put on royalty*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She approaches him as a queen, in what belongs to her position, not as a petitioner in rags."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The law she is breaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had spelled it out herself two chapters earlier: anyone who goes into the inner court unsummoned is put to death, *unless the king holds out the golden sceptre.* She had not been called for thirty days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has no right to be standing there. That is the fact the scene begins with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the king saw Queen Esther standing in the court, **she won his favour**, and he **held out to her** the golden sceptre.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *she obtained grace in his eyes* — **chen**, the same word the book has used over her from the beginning, unearned favour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She did not earn the sceptre by three days of fasting and she did not argue her way to it. It was extended."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who moves first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sceptre goes out before she says anything. She has not stated her business, made her case, or explained why she came."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *so Esther came near and **touched the top of the sceptre**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She approaches because it was held out, and what she touches is the thing that spared her. The order in those two verses is the order the whole book runs on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was on the other side of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What is your request? It shall be given you, **up to half the kingdom**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the next sentence. The law said death; the sceptre said half an empire."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the throne came from.** She is standing in a room she only occupies because Vashti refused to come when she was summoned. One woman would not come when she was called; another came when she was not called, and lived. See the note on Esther 1:9–12."}]}, "Esther 5:9–13": {"title": "One man at the gate spoils everything", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Haman has everything, and the writer lets him list it out loud so the last sentence can land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inventory"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He boasted to them of his **glorious riches**, his **many sons**, and **every way the king had promoted him**… \"And besides all that, **Queen Esther invited no one but me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wealth, sons, rank, and a private banquet with the king and queen. In that world there was nothing else to want."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He counts it in front of an audience he has assembled for the purpose — his wife and his friends, called in to hear it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that undoes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet all of this means nothing to me** as long as I see Mordecai the Jew sitting at the king's gate.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is stronger than the English can quite carry: all of it is worth nothing to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One man, sitting down, not moving. That is the entire offence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mordecai did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mordecai **did not rise or so much as tremble** before him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not insult Haman. He did not speak. He sat still."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer adds *or so much as tremble* — Haman is not even given the compliment of fear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Still, he held himself in\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haman restrains himself in public and goes home to say it. The rage is not managed; it is stored."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the end of the same conversation his wife and friends have proposed the gallows, and *the thing pleased Haman, and he had the gallows made.* Fifty cubits — seventy-five feet, higher than anything needed for the job, built to be seen across the city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is standing when he says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just come from Esther's first banquet, and he has been invited back tomorrow. He thinks the invitation is an honour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a trap that has already closed. The reader can see it; Haman is at home counting his sons."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the boast cost him.** He builds a gallows that night for a man he will be leading through the streets on a horse by tomorrow afternoon, and he will hang on it himself before the second banquet is over. See the notes on Esther 6:10–11 and Esther 7:7–10."}]}, "Esther 6:1–3": {"title": "The night the king could not sleep", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The turning point of the book is a man who cannot get to sleep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hinge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That night **sleep would not come to the king**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is it. No angel, no dream, no prophet, no plague. The most powerful man in the world lies awake, and from that one ordinary thing everything reverses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is impersonal — sleep fled from the king. The writer does not say who took it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Count what has to line up on this one night:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["Haman is at that moment building a gallows for Mordecai", "the king cannot sleep", "of everything he could call for, he calls for the **chronicles**", "of the whole reign's records, they read the page about **Mordecai**", "the reader is told *nothing has been done for him at all*", "and Haman walks into the court at daybreak to ask for Mordecai's death"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of those is unremarkable on its own. Any one of them going differently and the book ends another way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the record was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mordecai had uncovered the plot of Bigthana and Teresh back in chapter 2, four years earlier, and it was written down and forgotten."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That failure to reward him looked at the time like an injustice. It was a debt being held until the night it would do the most work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Nothing has been done for him at all\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servants' answer, and it is the sentence the king cannot leave alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not send for a treasurer. His next question is *who is in the court?* — and Haman has just arrived, early, to ask permission to hang the man the king is about to honour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is showing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no miracle in Esther 6. There is insomnia, an administrative record, and bad timing for one man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how the whole book works: nothing supernatural on the surface, and every detail landing exactly where it has to. He is not named in the chapter. He is all over it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Haman was doing while the king lay awake.** Standing in his own courtyard watching a seventy-five-foot gallows go up. Two men in one city that night — one building, one unable to sleep — and only one of them thought he was in control of the morning."}]}, "Esther 6:10–11": {"title": "The parade he never imagined", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Haman describes, in loving detail, the honours he wants for himself, and then has to carry every one of them out for the man he came to hang."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had just designed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king had asked what should be done for the man he delights to honour, and Haman — *whom would the king delight to honour more than me?* — designed it himself: the king's own robe, the king's own horse, the crown on its head, and a noble to lead it through the city square shouting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was writing his own coronation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"For Mordecai the Jew\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do all of this for **Mordecai the Jew**, who sits at the king's gate.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king names him with the two things Haman hates most: his name, and his nation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who sits at the king's gate* — the very posture that started the whole rage. See the note on Esther 5:9–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Leave out nothing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Leave out nothing** of everything you have described.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item. The king will not let him trim it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haman had built the list for maximum glory, and now the length of it is the length of his own humiliation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had come to ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is standing in that court at daybreak for one reason: to get permission to hang Mordecai on a gallows already built and waiting outside his house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never gets the sentence out. The king speaks first, and by the time Haman is allowed to answer he is holding a robe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Haman **took** the robe and the horse, and he **dressed** Mordecai, and he **led** him through the city square… **calling out** before him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs, all his, all performed on the man he meant to kill. He dressed him with his own hands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is what is done for the man the king delights to honour!\"* — shouted, by Haman, in the street, all afternoon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No one has intervened. No prophet has spoken, no plague has fallen, nobody has prayed on the page."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sleepless night, an old record, and a man's own vanity handing him the exact opposite of what he came for."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for it.** *Whoever digs a pit will fall into it, and a stone will come back on the one who starts it rolling.* Haman's own gallows, his own robe, his own parade. Every instrument in this book turns around in the hand that made it."}]}, "Esther 6:12–14": {"title": "Home in grief", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men go home from the same parade, and the writer records both journeys in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Mordecai goes back to work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Afterward **Mordecai returned to the king's gate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Robe off, horse gone, back to his seat. Nothing is recorded of what he said or felt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had not asked for the parade, had not campaigned for it, and does not appear to have adjusted his life because of it. He goes back to the gate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Haman goes home"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Haman hurried home, **his head covered**, mourning and shaken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Covering the head was the posture of grief and shame. In 2 Samuel it is what David does going up the Mount of Olives, and what the mourners do for the dead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was leading a parade an hour ago."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The advisers change their tune"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same wife and the same friends who suggested the gallows the previous evening now say this:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If Mordecai, before whom you have **begun to fall**, is **one of the Jewish people**, you will not overcome him. You will surely fall before him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They give a reason, and the reason is his nation. Pagan advisers in a Persian house state the principle the book never states outright — that there is something about this people you cannot win against."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They cannot name what they are describing. They can see it working."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Begun to fall\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has the verb twice: you have *begun to fall* before him, you will *surely fall*. Once it starts, they say, it does not stop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has happened yet except a robe and a horse. There has been no trial, no accusation, and Haman is still the second man in the empire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are reading the direction of things off one afternoon, and they are right."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were still speaking when the king's messengers arrived, **hurrying Haman away** to the banquet Esther had prepared.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not get to finish the conversation. He is taken, mid-sentence, to the room where he will be exposed and sentenced within the hour."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The pace of the chapter.** Everything in Esther 6 happens in a single day, and the verbs are all urgent — *hurry*, *hastened*, *hurrying away*. Eleven months of waiting under a death decree, and then one day in which the whole thing turns over."}]}, "Esther 7:3–4": {"title": "The night she finally asked", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two banquets, an invitation held back for a day, and then the request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king had asked her twice what she wanted, and twice offered up to half his kingdom. Both times she asked only that he and Haman come to dinner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In between the two banquets: a sleepless night for the king, the royal records read aloud, Mordecai's unrewarded loyalty discovered, and Haman paraded through the streets leading the horse of the man he meant to hang."}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of that happens if she asks on the first night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How she asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If I have found favour in your eyes, my king, and if it pleases the king, let my own life be given me as my petition, and my people as my request.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She asks for her own life first. Until this moment the king does not know she is Jewish — the decree he sealed includes her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She puts herself inside the sentence before she mentions anybody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line about slavery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Had we only been sold as slaves, men and women, I would have kept silent, for such a trouble would not be worth disturbing the king.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A deliberate piece of restraint. She tells a Persian king that she would not have troubled him over slavery — which makes clear that what she is troubling him about is worse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To be destroyed, to be killed, to be wiped away.* Three, and they are the exact three from Haman's decree, quoted back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not describing the danger in her own words. She is reading his own paperwork to him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The book that never names God.** Nothing miraculous happens in Esther. A queen waits a day, a king cannot sleep, a record is read at the right hour. Only from the far end does the shape become visible — which is the whole method of the book. See the note on Esther 2:15–16."}]}, "Esther 7:5–6": {"title": "Who would dare", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The king's question, and the answer that ends Haman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who is he? Where is the man** who has dared in his heart to do such a thing?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king does not know. He signed the decree himself — sealed it with his own ring — and never asked which people it was for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is furious at a crime he authorised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name written into the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the Hebrew of this verse, the first letters of four consecutive words spell out **EHYEH** — *I AM*. The name God gave Moses at the bush: *I AM WHO I AM.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It stands in the king's demand to know who has dared to do such a thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the fourth and last of the hidden names in Esther. The letters of **YHWH** are formed the same way four times elsewhere in the book — twice reading forwards and twice reading backwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name is never printed in this book. It is built into the text, in the places where the story turns, and it is written in the one sentence where the question being asked out loud is *who is behind this?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The man, the enemy, this wicked Haman.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three phrases, and no verb between them. In Hebrew it lands like three blows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She names him in front of the king, at the king's table, with Haman sitting at it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had waited for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two banquets, two days. She had the sceptre and the offer of half the kingdom on the first day and asked only for another dinner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The delay put Haman in the room on the night after his humiliation, at the exact hour the king's mind had already turned against him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Haman stood frozen with terror\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that he was terrified before the king *and the queen*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had not known she was a Jew. Nobody in the palace had — Mordecai had told her not to say. The decree he wrote and sealed included the woman sitting opposite him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the king still does not know.** He never learns that God is in this. He goes to his grave believing he lost his temper twice and settled a matter of court intrigue. The book lets him. The name is in the text and not in his mouth."}]}, "Esther 7:7–10": {"title": "The gallows he built", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Everything Haman made turns around and is used on him, item by item, in the space of four verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Pleading with the woman he sentenced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Haman stayed behind to **plead with Queen Esther for his life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who wrote a decree to kill every Jew in the empire is on his knees to a Jewish woman asking her to spare him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not know that is what he is doing until the sentence before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The couch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As the king came back from the garden… there was Haman, **fallen across the couch** where Esther reclined.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is begging. The king walks in and reads it as an assault."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Will he even assault the queen while I am in the house?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing Haman did not do is the thing he is accused of, and it is the accusation that finishes him. He had built his whole career on the reading people put on appearances."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"They covered Haman's face\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And as the words left the king's mouth, they covered Haman's face.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Covering the face was what was done to a condemned man. No trial, no verdict pronounced — the servants read the room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had gone home the day before with *his head covered* in shame. Now other hands do it, and it means execution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Harbona"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Harbona**, one of the king's servants, said, \"Look — the gallows Haman made for Mordecai… stands beside Haman's own house, fifty cubits high.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Harbona is named back in chapter 1, one of the seven eunuchs who were sent to fetch Vashti. The writer listed all seven names then, and it looked like nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks once in the whole book, and it is this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gallows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy-five feet, built the night before, standing beside his own house so the whole city could see it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Hang him on it.\" So they hanged Haman on the gallows he had built for Mordecai.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He chose the height. He chose the location. He supplied the instrument, and the only thing he got wrong was who would be on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it costs him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next chapter opens with his estate given to Esther, his signet ring given to Mordecai, and his household put under the man he meant to kill."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he had listed to his friends — the riches, the rank, the sons — goes across to the other side of the ledger."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last clause.** *And the king's fury was stilled.* The same phrase closes chapter 2 after his rage at Vashti. Twice in this book a king's temper decides everything, and both times it lands where it had to for the Jews to live."}]}, "Esther 8:1–2": {"title": "\"Esther's house, Mordecai's ring\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The transfer happens in a single day, and it is total."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"That same day\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a process. The morning Haman was still second in the empire; by evening his estate belongs to the woman he sentenced to death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The house"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*King Ahasuerus gave Queen Esther **the household of Haman, the enemy of the Jews**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer will not let his name stand alone. He is *the enemy of the Jews* here and in five other places — the title follows him to the end of the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he boasted about to his friends — the glorious riches, the great house — is signed over to a Jewish orphan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"For Esther had told him what he was to her\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The secret comes out at last. Mordecai had told her at the beginning not to reveal her people or her family, and she had kept it for years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She tells the king now, and the man at the gate walks into the royal presence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The king took off his signet ring, **the one he had taken back from Haman**, and gave it to Mordecai.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same ring. Not a new one — the writer says where it came from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That ring sealed the decree ordering the death of every Jew in the empire. It is now on the hand of a Jew, and the next thing it will seal is the decree that lets them live."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instrument of the sentence becomes the instrument of the rescue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Esther sets him over it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Esther set Mordecai over the household of Haman.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She receives it and immediately puts him in charge of it. Two verses, and the whole apparatus of the enemy is in the hands of the two people it was built against."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What has not changed.** The first decree still stands. Persian law cannot be revoked, and the thirteenth of Adar is still in the calendar — Haman is dead and the sentence he wrote is not. That is the problem chapter 8 has to solve, and it is not solved by cancelling anything. See the note on Esther 8:9–14."}]}, "Esther 8:15–17": {"title": "The city rejoiced", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The man who sat at the gate in sackcloth comes out of the palace in blue and white, and a city that had been reeling shouts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is wearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Royal robes of **blue and white**, a great **crown of gold**, and a mantle of fine **linen and purple**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Blue and white are the colours of the Persian royal house, and they are also the colours of the tabernacle hangings. Fine linen and purple are priestly cloth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four chapters earlier the same man was in sackcloth and ashes in the middle of the city, crying out with a loud and bitter cry, and could not pass the gate because no one in sackcloth was allowed in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The city"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the city of Susa shouted and was glad**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last time Susa was described, in chapter 3, it *reeled in confusion* while the king and Haman sat down to drink."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same city, same writer, same word placement — and the reversal is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four words for it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the Jews there was **light and gladness, joy and honour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Light** first. It is not the obvious word in a list about rejoicing, and it is the word chosen to lead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had had no prophet, no temple, no king, and no name of God spoken over them for a generation. What arrives is light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Everywhere the decree reached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In every province and every city, wherever the king's word and his decree arrived, there was gladness and joy among the Jews, feasting and a holiday.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gladness travels at the speed of the couriers. It is the arrival of the letter that produces it — not the thirteenth of Adar, which is still eight months off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has happened yet. They are celebrating a word that has been sent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **many of the peoples of the land became Jews**, for the fear of the Jews had fallen upon them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Outsiders join. The Hebrew is a rare verb — they *judaised*, made themselves Jews."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people who were *not My people*, hidden and scattered, are suddenly the thing the empire wants to belong to."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**From the gate to the palace.** Mordecai never asked for any of it. He would not bow, he sat still, and he told his cousin the deliverance would come from another place whatever she did. The robe found him at the gate. See the notes on Esther 6:10–11 and Esther 4:12–14."}]}, "Esther 8:9–14": {"title": "The second decree", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first decree cannot be revoked. So a second one is written, and it does not cancel the first — it answers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Persian law, once sealed with the king's ring, stands. The king says so himself in the verse before: *what is written in the king's name and sealed with the king's ring, no one can revoke.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order to destroy every Jew on the thirteenth of Adar is still law. Haman is dead; his decree is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By these letters the king granted the Jews in every city **the right to gather and defend their lives**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an amnesty. A counter-decree, of equal force, issued through the same machinery."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Written the same way, deliberately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set the two side by side and the writer's care is obvious. Every element of Haman's decree in chapter 3 is repeated here:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the king's scribes summoned", "to a hundred and twenty-seven provinces, India to Cush", "each province in its own script, each people in its own language", "written in the name of the king, sealed with the king's signet ring", "sent by couriers, hurried", "for the same single day — the thirteenth of Adar", "with the same clause about *their little ones and their women*", "and the same right to take *the goods of their enemies as plunder*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Word for word, structure for structure. The same ring, the same road, the same date."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one difference is who is holding the pen. See the note on Esther 3:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The right to stand\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to stand for their life — to make a stand. It is defensive language throughout."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not told to go on the offensive on any other day. One day, the day already fixed against them, and on that day they may stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eight months' notice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letters go out in Sivan, the third month. The day is in Adar, the twelfth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haman's lot fell on a date eleven months away, and that delay is what gives the second decree time to reach India."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plunder they did not take"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The right to take the enemy's goods is granted here in so many words. Chapter 9 then reports, three separate times, that *they did not lay a hand on the plunder.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was theirs by law and they left it. Whatever the day was about, it was not about that."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What one signet ring did twice.** The same ring sealed the sentence and the deliverance. Nothing about the empire changed, nothing about the law changed, and the king never learned whose hand was on any of it."}]}, "Exodus 1:15–22": {"title": "The midwives who feared God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two women are named in this passage. The king of Egypt is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who gets a name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Hebrew midwives, **Shiphrah and Puah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most powerful man in the world is *the king of Egypt* and *Pharaoh*, a title, never a name. Two midwives are recorded by name and have been read out loud for three thousand years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's judgement about who mattered is built into the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were to **put the newborn Hebrew boys to death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boys only. It is the same shape as Haman's decree — an administrative solution to a people who are increasing, carried out through the ordinary machinery of the state."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek at the back of the column; Pharaoh at the birthstool. The attack is always on what is weakest and cannot resist."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the midwives **feared God**, and they refused to do it; they let the boys live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is not compassion, courage or ethics. It is fear of God, and the writer names it as the cause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice what Deuteronomy says about Amalek: *they had no fear of God.* The same phrase, on the opposite side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the king demanded why, they answered that the Hebrew mothers were strong and gave birth before they could arrive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They lie to Pharaoh's face. The text records it without a word of criticism, and the next sentence is about God being kind to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **God was kind to the midwives**, and because they honoured Him, **He gave them families of their own**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two women who spent their lives delivering other women's babies were given households of their own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reward matches the work exactly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escalation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At last Pharaoh ordered **all his people** that every newborn Hebrew boy was to be cast into the Nile.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The private instruction failed, so it becomes public policy. Every Egyptian is now deputised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the Nile — the river Egypt worshipped, the source of everything they had — is made the instrument."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes of it.** *Still the people kept increasing.* Said in the middle of the chapter and true at the end of it. And the very next paragraph has a Levite woman putting her son into the Nile in a basket, into the hands of Pharaoh's own daughter. See the note on Exodus 2:1–10."}]}, "Exodus 10:21–29": {"title": "Darkness you could feel, and light where Israel lived", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The ninth plague is the strangest of them, and the last one before the death of the firstborn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Darkness that could be touched"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew phrase is unusual — a darkness that may be *felt*, groped at. Not night. Something with substance to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For three days *no one could see anyone else, and no one moved from where he was*. Egypt stops. Nobody travels, nobody works, nobody visits. A whole civilisation sits still in the dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The separation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But all the Israelites had light where they lived.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plagues have been dividing Egypt from Goshen for several chapters now — the flies, the livestock, the hail — and the text keeps saying so plainly. Here the division is at its most visual: one people cannot see their hand, and a few miles away the lamps are on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this plague, and why now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt's chief deity was the sun. The plague before this stripped the land bare; this one puts out the god. And it comes immediately before the night when the firstborn dies and Israel walks out — a rehearsal in the dark for what is coming."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last exchange.** Pharaoh tells Moses to get out and never show his face again, and Moses agrees: *you have spoken well; I will never see your face again.* It is the end of the negotiations that ran through the whole cycle — the compromises about who could go and what they could take are over, and the next thing in the book is the Passover."}]}, "Exodus 10:7–11": {"title": "The negotiation over the children", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh has stopped saying no. That is what makes this passage worth reading slowly — the refusals have become offers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go, worship the LORD your God. But tell me — who exactly is going?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not blocking the worship any more. He is negotiating the guest list, which is a different and more effective tactic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Moses' answer leaves nothing out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We will go with our young and our old, our sons and daughters, our flocks and herds.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four categories, and he names them deliberately: both ends of the generations, both sexes of the children, and the livelihood. There is nothing left in Egypt to come back for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Pharaoh draws the line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Only the men may go.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The children are the sticking point, and Pharaoh knows exactly what he is doing. A generation of men can go and worship in the desert and still leave everything behind that would make the departure permanent. Keep the children and you keep the future."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The rest of the pattern.** This is the third of four attempted compromises across the chapters: worship inside Egypt; do not go far; leave the children; and finally, leave the livestock. Each one concedes more than the last, and each keeps hold of something. Moses' answer to the fourth is the one that ends it — *not a hoof will be left behind.*"}]}, "Exodus 11:1–3": {"title": "The wages paid on the way out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses of instruction between the darkness and the last plague, and the instruction is about asking the neighbours for silver."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word that is usually mistranslated"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Older versions have Israel *borrow* from the Egyptians, which made generations of readers uneasy — it sounds like taking things under false pretences."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is simply *ask*. There is no promise of return in it. What follows is a request made openly, and granted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is not theft"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years of unpaid labour stand behind it. The law God gives later requires that a freed slave not be sent away empty-handed but supplied generously from the flock and the threshing floor — and that is exactly the shape of what happens here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel leaves as freed labourers being paid off, not as looters. Psalm 105 says it plainly: *He brought His people out with silver and gold.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The LORD had already softened the Egyptians\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The favour is not attributed to Israel's charm or to Egyptian generosity. The text says God had gone ahead of the request and changed how these people saw their slaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then a line about Moses himself: *a great man in that land, respected by Pharaoh's officials and by the people alike*. The man who had fled Egypt as a wanted murderer is, by the end, the most respected person in it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the silver was for.** Some of it ends up in the tabernacle a few chapters later — gold, silver, bronze, blue and purple yarn, brought as freewill offerings. And some of it ends up in the golden calf. The same wealth, out of the same pockets, going two directions."}]}, "Exodus 12:1–13": {"title": "\"When I see the blood\" — who the sign was for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most important sentence in the passage is the one that says who the mark was for, and it is not the person inside the house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"When I see the blood\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *when you see it*. Not *when the destroyer sees it*. The seeing is God's, and the text says it twice, once at the start of the promise and once at the end — the second time as a plain repetition, as though the point were too important to risk being missed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the protection never depended on the confidence of the family behind the door. A household could have spent that night terrified and the blood would have done what blood does. The sign was addressed outward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is ruled out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage states the negative itself: *you are not spared by your goodness or your strength. You are spared because the blood is there, and I see it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had done nothing to deserve exemption; the plagues had fallen on Egypt and their own record over the following forty years is not flattering. Nothing about the household's quality is in view. Only whether the lamb had died and the blood was where it could be seen."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The details worth noticing"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**a lamb for a household** — and if a household is too small, share with the neighbour. It is reckoned by how many can eat, not by how much sin needs covering", "**without flaw**, and kept four days before it is killed — long enough to be looked over", "**eat it dressed to travel** — sandals on, staff in hand. They were to eat the meal in the posture of people already leaving", "**leave nothing until morning** — the whole lamb is consumed that night"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the tenth day of this month they shall take to them every man a lamb… and you shall keep it up **until the fourteenth day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four days in the house before it is killed. Long enough for a family to look it over, and for any blemish to show."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus rode into Jerusalem on the tenth of Nisan, went into the temple, and turned over the tables. From that morning to the Passover He is examined by one delegation after another — the chief priests and elders, the Pharisees and Herodians, the Sadducees, a lawyer — and each goes away with nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Pilate: *I find no fault in Him.* Herod finds none. See the note on Matthew 21:23–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The month starts here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This month is now the first of months for you, the very start of your year.* Before a single Israelite has walked out of Egypt, God resets the calendar. The year now begins at the lamb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a strange administrative detail to include in a rescue narrative, and it tells you how the event was to be reckoned: not one night among others, but the point everything is counted from."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word Passover.** *Pesach* — to pass over, and possibly also to hover or to shield. Both senses have been argued from the same root, and Isaiah uses the word of the LORD protecting Jerusalem like a bird over its nest. Either way the action belongs to God at the door, not to the people behind it."}]}, "Exodus 12:33–36": {"title": "The wealth of Egypt", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They went in as guests, spent four centuries as slaves, and walked out carrying the treasury."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was doing the hurrying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Egyptians urged the people to hurry out** of the land, for they said, \"We will all die.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt is pushing them toward the door. Nobody has to negotiate a release any more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dough"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people took their dough **before the yeast was added**, wrapping their kneading bowls in their cloaks and carrying them on their shoulders.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where unleavened bread comes from. Not a symbol first and a practicality second — they left before it could rise, and the feast keeps the memory of the speed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Just as Moses had told them\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **just as Moses had told them**, the Israelites asked the Egyptians for articles of silver and gold and for clothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was instructed in advance, in chapter 11, and before that it was promised to Abram four hundred years earlier: *afterward your people will come out with great wealth.* See the note on Genesis 15:12–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody improvised this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *asked* is the ordinary verb for making a request. And the Egyptians *gave them whatever they asked.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the settlement of an account. Four hundred years of building store-cities — Pithom and Raamses — with no wages paid, and on one morning the books are balanced."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose doing it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **LORD had made the Egyptians so favourable** toward His people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *gave the people favour in the eyes of the Egyptians* — **chen**, grace, the same word used over Esther in a Persian palace and over Noah before the flood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Israelites did not persuade anybody. They asked, and the disposition of a nation had already been changed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some of it went into a golden calf a few chapters later. Most of it went into the tabernacle — the gold of the ark, the lampstand, the mercy seat, the boards overlaid, the silver sockets, the linen and the blue and purple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt's wages built the tent God met them in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the psalm sums it up.** *He brought His people out with silver and gold, and not one of their tribes stumbled.* Wealthy and well, in one line — the morning after the lamb. See the note on Psalms 105:37–38."}]}, "Exodus 14:10–14": {"title": "Stand still and see", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Water in front, an army behind, and Moses tells them to stop moving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God had brought them there. Two chapters back He had told Moses to have them turn and camp by the sea — deliberately, in a place with no exit, *for Pharaoh will say, they are wandering in confusion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trap was arranged. They were exactly where they had been led."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Were there no graves in Egypt**, that you brought us to the wilderness to die?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is bitter and it is sarcastic. And then: *\"Better to serve them than to die in the desert.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Within days of coming out, they are asking to go back in. The mind had not left Egypt when the body did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had just done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were terrified **and cried out to the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text records both. The terror and the crying out are in the same sentence, and neither is criticised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four instructions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not be afraid. **Stand still**, and **watch** the salvation the LORD will work for you today… The **LORD will fight for you**. You need only to **be still**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is a command to stop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *stand still* is to take a position and hold it — plant yourself. And *be still* is the verb for silence: hold your peace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are given nothing to do. Not a weapon, not a formation, not a plan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"These Egyptians you see today, **you will never see again**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rescue arrives at the last possible hour, with the chariots visible on the horizon. It could have come at any point in the previous three days. It comes when they can see the dust."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sea does not open until they are standing on the shore of it with nowhere else to be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line the Illumination adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The battle is not yours to win. God will fight, and you may rest.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is what Moses has just said, put plainly. And it is the whole difference between this passage and every strategy a frightened person reaches for."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says to Moses next.** *Why are you crying out to Me? Tell the Israelites to go forward.* The stillness is not passivity — it is that the fighting is His. When the word comes to move, they move into a road that was not there a minute earlier."}]}, "Exodus 14:15–18": {"title": "\"Why are you crying out to Me?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Israel is trapped between an army and a sea, Moses is praying, and God tells him to stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Why are you crying out to Me? Tell the Israelites to move forward.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the chapter suggests Moses was praying wrongly. He had just told the people to stand still and see the salvation of the LORD, which is a fine thing to say."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But there was a word already given. Back at the burning bush, God had said He was bringing them out. The situation had not changed the instruction — it had only made it look impossible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is told to do instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lift up your staff, stretch out your hand over the sea, and divide it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The staff is the one that had already turned into a snake, already struck the Nile, already brought hail. He is not being handed something new."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer is not rebuked so much as redirected: there is a time to ask, and a time to pick up what you were already given and use it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the dividing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction says *divide it* — spoken to Moses. Two verses on, the account says the LORD drove the sea back with a strong east wind all night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are true, and neither cancels the other. The man stretches out his hand; God moves the water. That is the pattern the whole book runs on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why God says He is doing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will gain honour through Pharaoh and all his army… Then the Egyptians will know that I am the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is not only Israel's rescue. God names His own honour and Egypt's knowledge as the point — the rescue is real, and it is also a demonstration aimed at people watching."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line just before it.** *The LORD will fight for you; you need only to be still.* Moses says it, and God's next words are *tell them to move forward*. Both are in the same passage, four verses apart. Stillness and forward movement are not opposites here — the stillness is about who does the fighting."}]}, "Exodus 2:1–10": {"title": "Drawn from the water", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The decree said put him in the Nile. She put him in the Nile."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Obedience of a kind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh's order was that every Hebrew boy be cast into the river. His mother hides the child three months, and when she cannot hide him any longer she does exactly what the law required — and seals him first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The basket"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A basket of **papyrus**, sealed with **tar and pitch**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word for the basket is **tebah**. It occurs in only one other place in the whole Bible: Noah's ark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the ordinary word for a box or a chest. The word for the vessel that carried one family through the water while everything else went under, used again for a container the length of a baby."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *sealed with tar and pitch* is what Noah was told to do to his — *cover it inside and out with pitch.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she put it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Among the reeds along the bank of the Nile.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for reeds is **suph** — the same word in *Yam Suph*, the Sea of Reeds, which is the sea that will open for this child eighty years later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is drawn out of the reeds, and he brings a nation out through the reeds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who comes down to the river"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Pharaoh's daughter** came down to bathe at the river.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of everyone in Egypt who could have found that basket, it is found by the daughter of the man who signed the order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her heart went out to him.* And she names the problem out loud: *\"This is one of the Hebrew children.\"* She knows precisely what she is looking at and takes him anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sister"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The baby's sister **stepped forward**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Miriam has been standing at a distance watching. She waits for the right moment and speaks to a princess."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Shall I go and find a Hebrew woman to nurse the child for you?\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the mother got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take this child and nurse him for me, **and I will pay you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is handed back her own son, with the protection of the palace over him, and Egypt pays her wages to raise him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had put him in the river because the law said so. She gets him back, legally, salaried, and safe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She named him **Moses**, saying, \"I drew him out of the water.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Egyptian princess gives him a Hebrew name, or one that works in both languages. And it becomes the name of a man whose whole life is drawing people out of water."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the deliverance arrived.** No angel, no plague, no army. A basket, a princess taking a bath, a girl brave enough to speak, and a river the empire worshipped. God is named in this chapter only at the very end, when He hears the groaning — and by then He has already had the deliverer raised in Pharaoh's own house for forty years."}]}, "Exodus 25:10–16": {"title": "Wood and gold, and poles that never come out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first thing God tells Moses to build is not the tent. It is the box that goes inside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Start with the ark.* Before the table, the lampstand, the curtains, the courtyard — the instructions begin at the innermost point and work outward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The building starts where God will sit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two materials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Acacia wood** — the hard, durable timber of the desert, the only usable wood they had out there. And **pure gold**, inside and out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wood from the wilderness where they were, and gold, which in Scripture is the metal of what is divine and imperishable. The wood is not replaced by the gold or hidden by it. Both are in the finished object, inseparably."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Readers have long seen the two natures of Christ in that combination, and the instruction to overlay *inside and out* is part of why: gold on the visible surface and gold on the surface nobody would ever see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The poles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Slide them through the rings on the sides so the ark can be lifted and carried. Leave the poles in the rings — **they never come out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An odd, specific rule. The poles are not to be removed even when the ark is at rest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The practical effect is that the ark is permanently ready to move. Israel was about to spend forty years going where the cloud went, and the piece of furniture God sits over is built to travel at any hour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had to prepare it. It was always packed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What went inside.** The Testimony — the tablets. Hebrews adds two more items that ended up in there: the jar of manna and Aaron's staff that budded. The bread He supplied, the priesthood He chose, and the law they broke, in one box, under a lid of mercy."}]}, "Exodus 25:17–22": {"title": "The mercy seat, and the place He chose to speak from", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God gives the design for the ark and then tells Moses where He will be found."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the lid was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kapporet** — from the verb to cover, to atone. It was not a decorative top. The lid *was* the place of atonement, which is why English has long called it the mercy seat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pure gold, hammered from one piece with the cherubim — not assembled. The angels and the covering are the same object."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was underneath it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Inside put the Testimony I'll give you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stone tablets. The commandments Israel had already broken before Moses got down the mountain lay inside the box, and the mercy seat lay over them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the arrangement: the law underneath, the covering above, and the blood sprinkled on the covering once a year."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the cherubim are looking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Facing each other, their eyes turned down toward the cover.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last time cherubim appeared they were at the gate of Eden with a flaming sword, guarding the way back. Here they are bent over a lid, looking down at the place where blood is applied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same creatures, different posture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that is the point of the whole passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And this is where I'll meet you. From above that mercy seat, from the space between the two cherubim… Right there — that's where I choose to draw near and be heard.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from the altar where the sacrifices burned, and not from the tablets. From the space above the covering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God names the address at which He will speak, and it is the one place in the tabernacle where mercy sits on top of the broken law."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul calls Jesus.** In Romans 3 he uses the same word — *hilasterion*, the term the Greek Old Testament uses for this lid. God set Him forth publicly as the mercy seat. What was hidden behind a curtain and visited once a year is the thing Paul says was displayed in the open."}]}, "Exodus 28:36–38": {"title": "The gold plate on the forehead", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last item in the high priest's clothing, and it exists to solve a problem nobody usually thinks about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Make a plate of pure gold and engrave on it, as on a seal: HOLY TO THE LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Worn on the forehead, tied with a blue cord to the front of the turban. The first thing anyone saw when the high priest faced them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem it addresses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That way Aaron carries the weight of any guilt tied to the sacred gifts the Israelites set apart, whatever their holy offerings may be.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read that again. There was guilt attached to **the holy offerings themselves** — the gifts people brought, the worship they gave. Even the best thing Israel brought God needed something to cover it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a startling admission built into the system. Not only their sins, but their sacrifices, required a bearer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who carried it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron. On his forehead. Continuously — *keep it always on his forehead*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest wore the guilt of the nation's worship and the words HOLY TO THE LORD in the same place, at the same time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the people and their gifts are welcomed before the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is about acceptance — being received with favour. The plate was not decoration. It was the mechanism by which imperfect worship from imperfect people was made welcome."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why it matters for prayer.** Hebrews argues at length that we now have a great high priest who has gone into heaven itself and appears before God on our account. What Aaron wore on a gold plate, the letter says Christ *is* — which means the acceptance of what we bring never depended on the quality of the bringing. See the notes on Hebrews 9:23–24 and Hebrews 4:14–16."}]}, "Exodus 3:13–15": {"title": "\"I AM\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses asks for a name and gets one that is a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They ask me, 'What is his name?' **What shall I tell them?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A reasonable question in that world. Every nation had gods with names, and a name was what you had to know in order to call on one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I AM WHO I AM.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ehyeh asher ehyeh** — the verb *to be*, first person, twice, with *who* between them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a description of anything. It is not tied to a place, a function, a mountain, a season or an element, which is what every other name in the ancient world was tied to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the short form"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Say to the Israelites: **'I AM has sent me to you.'**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes His own sentence and gives Moses one word out of it to carry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then the third form"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Say to the Israelites, '**the LORD**, the God of your fathers — the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob — has sent me to you.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The LORD**, in capitals, is **YHWH**, and it is built from the same verb. The name and the sentence are the same root."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He attaches it immediately to three men: Abraham, Isaac, Jacob. The name that is pure existence is given to Moses fastened to a family."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"This is My name forever\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The name you are to remember Me by **from generation to generation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew for *remember* here is the memorial word — the name is given as something to be kept in mind, generation after generation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the name turns up hidden"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the Hebrew of Esther 7:5 — the verse where the king demands *who is he, where is the man who has dared in his heart to do such a thing?* — the letters of **EHYEH**, *I AM*, stand at the start of four consecutive words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name given at a burning bush is written into a Persian banquet hall, in the sentence asking who is behind all this, in a book that never says God once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **YHWH** is formed the same way four times elsewhere in that book, twice forwards and twice backwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said it was His name forever. It is in the one book where nobody speaks it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jesus does with it.** *Before Abraham was, I AM.* They picked up stones, because they understood exactly which sentence He had just used. And in the garden, when the soldiers say they are looking for Jesus of Nazareth and He answers *I am* — they drew back and fell to the ground. See the note on Esther 7:5–6."}]}, "Exodus 3:7–10": {"title": "\"I have seen. I have heard. I know. I have come down.\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years of silence, and then four verbs in a row."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have seen it — I have truly seen the misery of my people… I have heard them crying out… **and I know their pain**. So I have come down to rescue them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seen, heard, known, come down. The Hebrew doubles the first — *seeing I have seen* — which is how that language says *thoroughly*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third is the one to sit with. **I know their pain.** Not *I am aware of the situation*. The verb is the ordinary word for knowing by experience, the same one used of intimacy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was doing during the silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the chapter suggests He had been absent. The claim is the opposite: He had been watching the whole time, and the seeing is recounted before any action is described."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That matters for a people who had been in Egypt four centuries and would reasonably have concluded otherwise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is bringing them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of that land into a good land, a wide-open land, a land flowing with milk and honey.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *wide-open* is spacious, broad — a word used elsewhere of being brought out into a large place after being hemmed in. Milk and honey is the shorthand for a land that produces without being forced: pasture rich enough for herds, and wildflowers enough for bees."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not describe it as adequate. He describes it as generous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now go. I am sending you to Pharaoh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs of divine action, and then the fifth verb is Moses'. The rescue God has just described in the first person arrives through a reluctant eighty-year-old with a stick."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not offered.** Nowhere is Egypt made comfortable. The answer to the misery is not improved conditions in the place they were in — it is out, and then up into somewhere else entirely."}]}, "Exodus 32:25–29": {"title": "Three thousand, at the giving of the law", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The day the tablets came down the mountain, and what happened at the bottom of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses saw that the people were running wild — Aaron had let them get out of control.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been at Sinai for weeks. Moses was on the mountain receiving the law; the camp had made a calf and was holding a festival for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *running wild* suggests being let loose, unrestrained — and the text puts the responsibility on Aaron for permitting it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line drawn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever is for the LORD, come to me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A call to choose sides, in the open, in front of everyone. The Levites come."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What follows is grim and the text does not soften it: swords through the camp, gate to gate, and about three thousand dead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three thousand, on the day the law was given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That figure appears once more in Scripture, and the setting is deliberate: Pentecost — by tradition the anniversary of Sinai — when Peter preaches and *about three thousand people were added* to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same festival, same number, opposite direction. Luke was writing for readers who knew Exodus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses says afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Today you have been set apart to the LORD… And so a blessing has come to you this day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tribe that answered the call becomes the priestly tribe. It is the origin of Levi's role in Israel, and the passage records both the cost and the appointment in the same sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How to hold this passage.** It is a hard chapter and it should not be smoothed over. What can be said is what the New Testament says: the law came through Moses, and grace and truth came through Jesus Christ — and the two three-thousands are the plainest illustration in Scripture of what each of them produced on its opening day."}]}, "Exodus 34:5–7": {"title": "\"He called out His own name\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses had asked to see His glory. This is what he got."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asked for and what came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Show me Your glory* — chapter 33. And the answer was *I will make all My goodness pass before you, and I will proclaim the name of the LORD before you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The glory turns out to be a name spoken out loud."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the proclaiming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD came down in the cloud and stood there with Moses, and **He called out His own name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He introduces Himself. This is God's own description of God, and it is the passage the rest of the Old Testament quotes back to Him — David, Nehemiah, Joel, Jonah and the psalmists all cite it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD, the LORD — a God **tender and kind, gracious and full of compassion, slow to anger, overflowing with steadfast love and faithfulness**. He keeps His mercy for thousands upon thousands, **forgiving wrongdoing and rebellion and sin**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six or seven adjectives before anything else, and every one of them is mercy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Overflowing with steadfast love* is **rav chesed** — abundant in covenant loyalty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Forgiving wrongdoing and rebellion and sin* — three Hebrew words covering three categories: crookedness, deliberate revolt, and missing the mark. All three are named as forgiven."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then the clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet He does not sweep guilt aside** as if it were nothing, and the weight of a father's sin can fall upon children and grandchildren, to the third and the fourth generation.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is emphatic — *by no means clearing the guilty*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is merciful and He does not pretend the debt is not there. Both are in one self-description, and the old covenant had no way to hold them together. A holy God cannot simply overlook it, and this verse says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the new covenant says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will be merciful to their unrighteousness, and **their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews 8, quoting Jeremiah 31. See the note on Hebrews 8:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not overlooked, and not swept aside. Remembered no more — which is a different thing again, and it required somebody to satisfy the clause in Exodus 34 rather than delete it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the cross did. *Whom God set forth as a propitiation… to demonstrate His righteousness, **that He might be just and the justifier** of the one who has faith in Jesus.* Paul writes that sentence to answer exactly this verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was spoken"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is holding two blank stone tablets, cut by himself, to replace the ones he smashed. The golden calf was six weeks ago."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God proclaims His mercy standing over the rubble of the first covenant."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses does next.** *He made haste and bowed his head toward the earth and worshipped.* No question, no discussion. He heard the name and went down."}]}, "Exodus 4:10–12": {"title": "\"Who gave man his mouth?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses' fourth objection, and the one that is usually read as modesty. It is not quite that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have never been a man of words, not before and not since you have spoken to your servant. I am slow of speech and slow of tongue.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *heavy of mouth and heavy of tongue*. Whatever the difficulty was — a speech impediment, a lost fluency in Egyptian after forty years with sheep — he presents it as a fixed fact about himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the aside: *not since you have spoken to your servant*. He is pointing out that the burning bush has not changed anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "God's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who gave man his mouth? Who makes him deaf or mute? Who gives him sight or makes him blind? Is it not I, the LORD?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four questions, and none of them deny the disability. God does not say Moses is mistaken about his speech; He asks who made the mouth in question."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a large and difficult claim, and the passage does not soften it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now go. I myself will be with your mouth, and I will teach you what to say.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, both first person. Not *your speech will improve* — I will be with it, and I will teach you."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How it ends.** Moses objects a fifth time anyway — *please send someone else* — and this time God's anger is recorded. Aaron is appointed as the spokesman. The concession is real, and so is the displeasure, and Exodus reports both without resolving them. Moses, notably, does most of the talking from chapter 5 onward."}]}, "Ezekiel 18:19–20": {"title": "\"The soul who sins is the one who will die\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel is answering a proverb the exiles kept repeating, and the answer is one of the clearest statements of individual responsibility in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proverb he is dismantling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the chapter: *the parents eat sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a complaint. The exiles were saying they were suffering for their ancestors' sins — which had the convenient effect of making their own conduct beside the point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God tells them to stop using it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ruling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The child will not share the guilt of the parent, nor the parent the guilt of the child. The righteousness of the righteous will be credited to him, and the wickedness of the wicked will be charged against him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two accounting words — **credited** and **charged**. Each account stands alone; nothing transfers between them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the principle, and Paul later uses the same vocabulary when he says righteousness is credited to Abraham through faith."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The soul who sins is the one who will die\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated twice in the chapter. It is a sentence about death being earned rather than inherited from a parent's record."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read alongside Romans, where death entered through one man and life comes through one Man, the two are addressing different questions: Ezekiel is answering people blaming their fathers, Paul is describing what Adam and Christ each transferred to those in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole point of this argument is the invitation at the end of it: *turn and live… why will you die, house of Israel?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The individual accounting is not stated to frighten. It is stated so that nobody can say their outcome was fixed by somebody else's file — see the note on Ezekiel 33:10–11, where the same chapter's conclusion comes with an oath."}]}, "Ezekiel 33:10–11": {"title": "\"I take no pleasure in the death of the wicked\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God answers a sentence His people are saying to each other, and the answer comes with an oath attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our sins and failures weigh us down. We are wasting away because of them. How then can we live?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is not defiance. It is despair — people who have concluded the account is too far gone to be worth addressing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel is prophesying to exiles. The city has fallen; the worst has already happened. Their question is whether there is any point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As surely as I live, declares the Lord GOD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God swears by Himself, which He does rarely. Whatever follows is being underlined as hard as language allows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I take no pleasure in the death of the wicked. My joy is that the wicked turn from their ways and live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two halves, negative and positive. The Hebrew for *pleasure* is about delight or desire — He is not describing reluctance to punish. He is denying any appetite for it at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the positive is stronger than mere permission: His *joy* is in the turning and the living."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turn back, turn from your evil ways. Why will you die, house of Israel?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It ends on a question, and the question is almost bewildered. Given that the door is open and He has just sworn He wants them through it — why?"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it turns up again.** Peter says God *does not want anyone destroyed; He wants every last one to turn*, and Paul says God desires all people to be saved. The New Testament keeps making the same claim, and this is the oath underneath it. See the note on 2 Peter 3:9."}]}, "Ezekiel 34:25–27": {"title": "\"There will be showers of blessing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The phrase everybody knows, in a chapter about shepherds who fed themselves instead of the sheep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel 34 is God's charge against Israel's leaders: you eat the fat, you clothe yourselves with the wool, you slaughter the fattened animals, and you do not feed the flock. The weak were not strengthened, the sick not healed, the lost not sought."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *I Myself will search for My sheep.* God takes the job over personally, and these verses describe the country afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covenant of peace"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will make with them a covenant of peace and rid the land of wild beasts, so that they may live safely in the wilderness and sleep in the forests.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Safety in the two places nobody was safe. The wilderness and the forest were where you were most exposed — and the promise is that they will sleep there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The showers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will send down showers in their season. There will be showers of blessing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is literally *showers of blessing*, and Ezekiel keeps the two rains framework of Deuteronomy — *in their season*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note where the blessing is aimed: *I will make them and the places around My hill a blessing.* The people are made into one before the rain is mentioned. The showers land on ground that has already been described as a blessing to its neighbours."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose stated at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they will know that I am the LORD, when I break the bars of their yoke and rescue them from the hands of those who enslaved them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not primarily so the crops come in. So that they know who did it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the shepherd turns out to be.** The chapter goes on: *I will place over them one shepherd, My servant David, and he will tend them.* Written centuries after David died. Jesus picks the whole chapter up in John 10 — the good shepherd, the sheep who were scattered, the hirelings who ran. See the note on John 10:7–10."}]}, "Ezekiel 36:33–36": {"title": "\"Like the garden of Eden\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God describes what a ruined country is going to look like, and the order of events is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the day I cleanse you from all your sins, I will settle people in your towns again, and the ruins will be rebuilt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cleansing is stated before the rebuilding, and the rebuilding is dated from it: **on the day** I cleanse you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has already said how — *I will sprinkle clean water on you… I will give you a new heart and put a new spirit in you.* The restoration of the land follows the restoration of the people, not the other way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison the neighbours make"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People will say, \"This land that was ruined has become like the garden of Eden.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The observation is put in the mouths of outsiders. God does not describe the country that way Himself; He says that is what will be said about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eden is the highest comparison available in Hebrew — the garden before anything went wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the towns were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rubble, deserted and torn down*, and now *walled and full of life*. Ezekiel is writing to people who had watched Jerusalem fall and had walked away from the smoke."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To them, this is not poetry about an abstract future. It is their own streets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why God says He does it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the nations left around you will know that I, the LORD, have rebuilt what was torn down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated audience is the surrounding nations. Earlier in the chapter He says it plainly: this is not for Israel's sake but for the sake of His name, which they had profaned among the nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I, the LORD, have spoken, and I will do it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and the second guarantees the first. Ezekiel ends the promise the way Isaiah ends his — with the fact that it was said out loud by someone who finishes what He says. See the note on Isaiah 55:10–11."}]}, "Ezra 2:1–2": {"title": "The long road back", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A list of names, and one of them is worth stopping on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the list is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These are the people of the province who came up out of captivity… now returning to Jerusalem and Judah, **each one to his own town**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy years after Nebuchadnezzar took them, Cyrus the Persian issues a decree and they are allowed to go home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not everybody went. That is the fact behind the whole book of Esther. Many Jews had built lives in Babylon and Persia across three generations and stayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Each one to his own town\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The towns had been empty for seventy years. Most of the returners had never seen them; they are going to places they know only from their grandparents."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They came with Zerubbabel, together with Jeshua, Nehemiah, Seraiah, Reelaiah, **Mordecai**, Bilshan…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether or not this is the man in the palace at Susa, the name is here in the list of those who went up — and it tells you the road home was open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Jews of Esther's book are in Persia by choice. Susa is where they had settled when Jerusalem was available."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that matters for Esther"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esther is the story of the people who did not go back, in the season Hosea called *not My people* — no temple, no land, no prophet, and God's name never spoken in the whole of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He is at work in every chapter of it anyway. That is the thing the book was written to show: they were out of the land, out of the covenant blessings, out of His visible dealings — and not out of His hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tally"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the tally of the men of the people of Israel, **counted by their families**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that follows is fifty-eight verses of numbers, family by family, village by village. Down to the singers, the gatekeepers, and the temple servants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody counted, and wrote it down, and it was kept."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they came back to.** Rubble, and a foundation. When the older men who had seen the first temple saw the new foundation laid, Ezra says they wept aloud while everyone else shouted for joy, and nobody could tell the weeping from the shouting."}]}, "Galatians 1:15–16": {"title": "\"Set me apart from my mother's womb\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul dates his calling to before he had done anything at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God, who had **set me apart from my mother's womb** and called me **through His grace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from the Damascus road. From the womb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *set apart* is **aphorizo** — to mark off, to separate for a purpose. It is the same root as *Pharisee*, which is what he had been, and he uses it here of what God had done long before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is excluded by the dating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he had listed a few verses earlier: *I advanced in Judaism beyond many of my contemporaries, being more exceedingly zealous for the traditions of my fathers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And everything on the other side of it — *I persecuted the church of God beyond measure and tried to destroy it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither the achievements nor the atrocities are the reason. He was set apart before either existed, and the mechanism named is **grace**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else was told this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah: *before I formed you in the womb I knew you; before you were born I sanctified you; I ordained you a prophet to the nations.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah: *the LORD has called Me from the womb; from the body of My mother He has named Me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Was pleased\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Was **pleased** to reveal His Son in me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eudokeo** — to take good pleasure in, to think it good. It is the word the Father uses at the baptism: *in whom I am well pleased.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The revealing was not a rescue operation forced on Him. It pleased Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *to me*. Paul says the Son was revealed **in** him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that I might **announce Him among the nations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The calling and the assignment arrive together. He is not given a revelation and then left to find a use for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the moment that happened, **I did not sit down and talk it over with flesh and blood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not go up to Jerusalem to the apostles. He went to Arabia, and came back to Damascus, and it was three years before he saw Peter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole point of the passage is where his gospel came from: *I neither received it from man, nor was I taught it, but it came through the revelation of Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he had been on the way to do.** Carrying letters to Damascus to arrest people. The commission he was actually given that morning was *to open their eyes, to turn them from darkness to light* — and he had been set apart for it before he was born."}]}, "Galatians 2:19–20": {"title": "The faith of the Son of God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*The life I now live in the flesh, I live by the faith **of** the Son of God, who loved me and gave Himself up for me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how the older English versions render it, and it is the more literal reading of the Greek. It is worth knowing why, because it changes what the verse asks of you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Greek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pistis Christou** — *faith of Christ*. The genitive most naturally reads as belonging to Him: His faith, His faithfulness. Rendering it *faith in the Son of God* requires taking the genitive as an object instead, which is possible but is the less obvious reading of the words as they stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it fits the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at what Paul has just said, one clause earlier:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*It is no longer I who live — Christ lives in me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has already removed himself as the subject. To then say that the life is sustained by the strength of his own believing would put him straight back at the centre of a sentence he has just moved himself out of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read as *the faith of the Son of God*, the whole thing runs one direction: I died, He lives in me, and the faith I live by is His."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that does to a struggling reader"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It relocates the pressure. If the Christian life runs on the quality of my trust, then every flat morning is a threat. If it runs on His faithfulness, then my believing is a response to something already holding, not the thing doing the holding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's language elsewhere runs the same way — *faithful is He who calls you, who also will do it*, and *if we are faithless, He remains faithful, for He cannot deny Himself.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The four words at the end.** *Who loved me and gave Himself up for me.* Paul writes it singular, which he almost never does. In the middle of the most combative letter he wrote, the argument narrows to one man and the love aimed at him — which is the same move the whole verse makes."}]}, "Galatians 3:10–14": {"title": "\"By becoming a curse for us\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes the law against itself and then says what was done about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is under a curse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who **rests on the works of the law** is under a curse.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not everyone who breaks it. Everyone who rests on it — who stands on their own keeping of it as their position before God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Cursed is everyone who does not continue in ALL the things** written in the book of the law, to do them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 27, the words Israel shouted *Amen* to from Mount Ebal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The killing word is *all*. Not most, not the important ones, not on balance. Everything, continuously, without a gap."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James puts the same thing another way: *whoever keeps the whole law and yet stumbles in one point is guilty of all.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two systems, side by side"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul sets the operating principles next to each other:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the one who is right with God **will live by faith*** — Habakkuk", "*the law is not of faith; its word is rather, **the person who does these things will live by them*** — Leviticus 18"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "One says believe. The other says do. They are not two routes to the same place; they run on incompatible currency."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Christ did with the curse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ has **bought us out from under the curse** of the law **by becoming a curse for us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *bought us out* is **exagorazo** — to purchase out of the market, the word used of buying a slave in order to free him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He did not lift the curse off. He took the position: *becoming a curse for us.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proof text"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 21. The body of an executed criminal hung on a tree was under God's curse and had to be taken down before nightfall so the land would not be defiled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reaches for the one verse in the law that puts a curse on a hanged man, and says that is the transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two purpose clauses, and both are given:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **the blessing of Abraham** might come to the nations in Christ Jesus, and so that through faith we might **receive the promise of the Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The curse was taken so the blessing could go somewhere it had never been — *to the nations.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the blessing of Abraham is the one credited in Genesis 15:6, before there was any law to keep. See the note on Genesis 15:6."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul asks them at the top of the chapter.** *Did you receive the Spirit by the works of the law, or by the hearing of faith?* He does not argue the theology first. He asks them to remember how it started."}]}, "Galatians 3:26–29": {"title": "\"Then you are Abraham's seed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent three chapters arguing that the promise to Abraham came before the law and does not run through it. Here is where the argument lands on the reader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three divisions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is neither Jew nor Greek, there is neither slave nor free, there is neither male nor female.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Those were the three great dividing lines of the ancient world — race, status, sex. A common Jewish morning prayer of the period thanked God for not making the speaker a Gentile, a slave, or a woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the same three categories and says that in Christ they do not determine standing. He is not abolishing the differences; he is saying they no longer decide who inherits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Clothed yourselves with Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is putting on a garment. In the Roman world a boy coming of age exchanged his child's toga for the *toga virilis* — the moment he became a legal adult with rights of inheritance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just used exactly that picture in the verses before: an heir who is a child is no different from a slave until the appointed time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if you belong to Christ, then you are Abraham's seed, and heirs according to the promise.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *like* Abraham's seed. Paul has already argued that the promise was made to Abraham and to his *seed*, singular, and that the seed is Christ. So the route runs: Christ is the heir; you belong to Christ; therefore the promise is yours."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The inheritance arrives by belonging, not by descent and not by performance."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is inherited.** In Genesis it was land, descendants and blessing for all nations. Paul has already named the piece he is most interested in three verses earlier — *so that the blessing of Abraham might come to the Gentiles*, and the promise of the Spirit received through faith. The inheritance he is arguing for is not smaller than Genesis. It is wider."}]}, "Galatians 4:4–7": {"title": "\"Born under the law\" — and the four steps out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul compresses the whole gospel into two sentences, and the logic runs in a chain where each link depends on the one before it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**God sent forth His Son** — born of a woman, born under the law", "**to redeem those who were under the law**", "**so that we might receive the adoption as sons**", "**and because you are sons, God has sent forth the Spirit of His Son**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sendings, and the second follows the first. The Spirit is not sent to make sons; He is sent *because* they are sons. Paul is careful about the order, and it is the opposite of how the sequence is often felt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Born under the law\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Christ is described as entering the exact position the people He came for were in. Not visiting it — born into it, under the same obligation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how the redemption works in Paul's argument: someone had to be genuinely inside the arrangement to buy anybody out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit of His Son into your hearts, crying out, Abba, Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice who is doing the crying here. In Romans 8 it is *we* who cry Abba; here it is the Spirit. Paul says it both ways within two letters, which suggests he did not think of them as different events."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word *Abba* is Aramaic, left untranslated in a Greek letter to Galatia. See the note on Romans 8:15–17 for what it carries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are no longer a slave but a son; and if a son, then an heir of God through Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Slave, son, heir — three words, and the movement between them is the whole letter in miniature. Paul is writing to people being persuaded to go back under law, and his argument is not that it is wrong so much as that it is *backwards*: it is walking out of the family and asking for a job."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"The fullness of the time.\"** Paul's phrase for the timing. Not that the world was at its best, but that a particular moment had filled up — a Roman peace, Greek as a common language, roads, and a scattered Jewish diaspora with synagogues in every port. He treats the arrangement of an era as deliberate."}]}, "Galatians 5:16–18": {"title": "\"If the Spirit is leading you\" — what Paul concludes from it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses that get quoted for the fight and almost never for the conclusion. Paul describes a genuine conflict, and then draws from it a conclusion nobody expects."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Walk by the Spirit, and you will not carry out what the flesh craves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is worth noticing. The instruction is *walk by the Spirit* — a positive, forward thing. The not-carrying-out is presented as the **result**, not as a second command to be worked at."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say fight the craving and then walk. He says walk, and the other follows. In Greek the second half is emphatic — a strong negative, closer to *you certainly will not*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fight is real"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The flesh sets its desire against the Spirit, and the Spirit against the flesh; the two of them stand opposed to each other, so that you do not do the things you want to do.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No spiritual life is being sold here as effortless. Paul describes two settled opposing pulls, and he says plainly that they get in the way of what a person means to do. Anyone who has tried to live this knows the sentence is accurate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But notice which two things are opposed. Not the believer against sin. The Spirit against the flesh. The believer is the field this happens in, not one of the two combatants — which is a large part of why the instruction is *walk*, not *win*."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion he draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here is the sentence that gets left off:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "But if the Spirit is leading you, **you are not under the law.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Given everything before it, you would expect the conclusion to be about victory, or about proving something. Instead Paul uses the reality of the Spirit's leading to settle a question about **jurisdiction**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole letter is about people being pushed back under law-keeping. So Paul's argument runs: you are being led by the Spirit, therefore that is not where you live. He treats being led as evidence of a legal standing, not as a performance to be assessed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that matters for guidance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "People bring this verse to decisions — am I being led? Paul brings it to identity. He does not use it to make anyone anxious about whether their leading is good enough; he uses it to tell them what their being led already proves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the same move he makes in Romans 8, where *led by the Spirit* is immediately followed by adoption, Abba, and inheritance. Both times, leading is raised as evidence of sonship rather than as a test of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"the things you want to do.\"** The line is often read as defeat — I cannot do what I want. In context Paul is describing why a person cannot simply execute their good intentions by willpower, which is exactly the argument for walking by the Spirit rather than trying harder. It is a diagnosis, and the prescription is in the first line of the paragraph."}]}, "Galatians 5:2–4": {"title": "What \"fallen away from grace\" is actually describing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Few verses have frightened more people than this one, and nearly always for the opposite of the reason Paul wrote it. Read quickly, \"you have fallen away from grace\" sounds like a warning that grace can run out — that a person can sin past the edge of it and be dropped. Read in the sentence Paul actually built, it says something closer to the reverse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Look at who he is describing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul names them in the same breath: *you who are being made right by the law*. That is the person in view. Not someone who has sinned badly — someone who has decided to earn their standing with God by keeping rules. The whole letter aims at that one move."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is addressed to the person who has failed. It is addressed to the person who has started keeping score."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two pictures underneath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words carry the verse, and both are stranger in Greek than they sound in English."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**cut off** — the word means to be left with nothing to do, put out of work, made idle. Not cut in half.", "**fallen away** — a word used of a ship blown off its course, and of a flower dropping off its stem."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Put together, the picture is not of grace pulling back from a person. It is of a person stepping off the ground they were standing on. Christ has not run out of power; He has been *given nothing to do* by someone who decided to handle their standing themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says two lines later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes straight on: *we, through the Spirit, wait with longing for the righteousness we hope for* — and he says **we**. Having just written the hardest sentence in the letter, he puts himself back in the same boat as the people he wrote it to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not telling them what they have permanently become. He is telling them what their position costs them while they hold it, which is a different thing. That is why the same chapter opens with *Christ has set us free*. A person cannot stand on two grounds at once, and the ground they were reaching for cannot hold anyone up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this matters practically"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If grace could be forfeited by sinning, the remedy would be to sin less. Paul's remedy is the opposite: stop trying to be made right by what you do. The fall he describes is not downward, into failure. It is sideways, out of the only thing that was holding anyone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "A word about *grace* itself. In Greek it is not leniency, and it is not a mood God happens to be in. It runs from favour freely given through to the working of that favour in a person's life. So \"fallen away from grace\" is not \"fallen out of God's good books.\" It is nearer to having stepped out of the current that was doing the work."}]}, "Galatians 5:22–24": {"title": "The fruit the Spirit grows", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nine words, and the noun in front of them is singular."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Fruit, not fruits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **fruit** the Spirit grows is…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One fruit with nine characteristics, not nine items to be collected separately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And set it against the phrase Paul used eight verses earlier: *the **works** of the flesh are evident.* Works, plural, and manufactured. Fruit, singular, and grown."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody grows fruit. A tree does, and what the farmer does is stay attached to the soil and the season."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose fruit it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The fruit **the Spirit** grows.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The genitive is His. It is not the believer's fruit produced with the Spirit's assistance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus said the same in the vine passage: *apart from Me you can do nothing*, and the branch's whole job is to remain. See the note on John 15:5–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The nine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Love, joy, peace** — Godward, and the first three."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Patience, kindness, goodness** — toward other people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Faithfulness, gentleness, self-control** — the inward three, and it is worth noticing where self-control lands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is last, and it is fruit. Not the root of the list but the ninth thing grown out of it. The one quality most people try to start with is the one Paul puts at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **no law standing against things like these**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent five chapters arguing against putting believers under law, and here is his answer to the obvious objection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing on that list needs legislating. No statute was ever passed against joy. Produce this and the law has no business with you — not because it was lowered, but because it has been outgrown."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it another way in Romans: *love is the fulfilment of the law.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the flesh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those who belong to Christ **have crucified** the flesh with its passions and its cravings.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perfect tense — done, and standing done. Not *should crucify*, and not *are crucifying daily*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reports it as an accomplished fact about everyone who belongs to Christ, the same way he reports it in Romans 6: *our old man was crucified with Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is only one, and it is four verses earlier: *walk in the Spirit, and you shall not fulfil the lust of the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A promise attached to a direction of travel. Nothing in the paragraph tells anybody to work on the list."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul feared for them.** *You have become estranged from Christ, you who attempt to be justified by law; you have fallen from grace.* That is the letter's charge — not immorality. The Galatians' problem was that they had started trying, and Paul's remedy is a fruit that grows."}]}, "Genesis 1:26–31": {"title": "\"Let us make human beings in our own image\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sixth day, and the account changes register for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Let us\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything before this is *let there be*. Here God speaks about the work rather than commanding it into existence — a deliberation, in the plural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Christian readers have long heard the Trinity in it, and the New Testament supports the direction: all things were made through the Son, and the Spirit was hovering over the waters in verse 2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the image of God He created them; male and female He created them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said three times in one verse, which is how Hebrew emphasises. And the second clause is part of the definition, not an afterthought — the image is carried by humanity as male and female together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for image is used elsewhere of a statue set up to represent a ruler in a province he could not visit in person. That is the job description: a representation, placed where the king is not visibly present."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have children, and multiply, and fill the earth, and tend it. I am placing it all in your care.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older translations say *subdue* and *have dominion*, and the words are strong. But they are attached to the image — this is rule exercised on behalf of someone else, by people made to look like Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Very good\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then God looked over everything He had made — and it was not merely good, it was very good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refrain through the chapter has been *good*, five times. Only at the end, with humanity in it, does the assessment change."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter never says.** Nothing here is earned and nothing is conditional. The blessing, the food, the care of the earth and the image itself are all given before a single human being has done anything at all."}]}, "Genesis 1:3–5": {"title": "\"Let there be light\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first recorded action of God toward a dark world is that He spoke."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was looking at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *the earth was without form and void, and darkness was on the face of the deep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the situation. Formless, empty, dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then God **spoke**. \"Let there be light.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not repair the darkness, examine it, or comment on how dark it was. He said what He wanted to be there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is three words: *yehi or* — and it is answered by two: *vayehi or.* Light was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"At once, effortlessly, simply because He said so\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no interval in the sentence. The saying and the being are the same moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews puts it this way: *the worlds were framed by the word of God, so that the things which are seen were not made of things which are visible.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Light before the sun"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sun, moon and stars are not made until the fourth day. There is light on the first day and no light source in the sky for three more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever this light is, it does not come from a lamp."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"God looked at the light and loved what He saw\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing called **good** in the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He *drew a line between the light and the darkness*. He separated them rather than mixing them or managing the balance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the pattern is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God is made in no one's image; man is made in His. And the first thing recorded of Him is that He faced a dark, empty, formless situation and said what He wanted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what He could see. What He wanted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs puts the same thing about us: *death and life are in the power of the tongue.* See the note on Proverbs 18:17–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Evening, then morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Evening came, then morning — the first day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew day runs from dark to light, not light to dark. Every one of the six days is counted that way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The direction is always toward the morning."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the New Testament puts this verse.** *For it is the God who commanded light to shine out of darkness who has shone in our hearts, to give the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ.* Paul quotes day one and says the same command was spoken over a person."}]}, "Genesis 12:10–20": {"title": "Abram goes down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with the greatest promise in the Old Testament and closes with the man who received it lying to a foreign king about his wife."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Went down\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abram **went down** to Egypt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is geographical — Egypt is lower ground — and Genesis uses it that way consistently. But the direction is the whole shape of the story."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set it beside Isaiah 2: *Come, let us **go up** to the mountain of the LORD.* Up is toward the house of God. Down is toward Egypt, and Abram was not told to go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no word from the LORD anywhere in this paragraph. That is the loudest thing in it. The chapter before is *the LORD had said to Abram*; here, nothing — he sees a famine and he moves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plan"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Say you are my sister, **so that I will be treated well for your sake** and my life will be spared because of you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He works out the arithmetic before he crosses the border. His wife is the price and his safety is the return."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is half telling the truth — Sarai was his half-sister — which is what makes it usable. It is the kind of lie that can be defended."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he gained"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pharaoh treated Abram well for her sake, and Abram acquired sheep and cattle, donkeys, male and female servants, and **camels**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plan worked. He came out of Egypt richer than he went in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Male and female servants.** The next chapter has Lot's herdsmen quarrelling with Abram's because their possessions were too great for the land to hold them. And a few chapters later a woman appears in the household with a name and a country: *Hagar, her Egyptian maidservant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he picked up in Egypt he took home with him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who protected the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the LORD struck Pharaoh and his household with serious diseases because of Sarai, Abram's wife.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram does not fix this. He does not pray, confess, or intervene. God moves directly on Pharaoh's house to get Sarai back out of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise of a son through her was already given, and the promise is defended by the One who made it while the man who received it is silent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rebuke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What have you done to me? Why didn't you tell me she was your wife?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pagan king rebukes the friend of God, and every word of the rebuke is just. Abram has no reply recorded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abram went **up** from Egypt… to the place where his tent had been at the beginning… to the place of the altar he had made there at first, and there Abram called on the name of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the first line of chapter 13. The way back is up, to the old tent site, to the altar he had already built."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not said.** Not one sentence of condemnation from God. The next time He speaks to Abram, in chapter 13, it is to enlarge the promise: *lift up your eyes and look… all the land you see I will give to you.* The detour is recorded in full, and it does not cost him the covenant."}]}, "Genesis 15:1": {"title": "\"I Myself am your shield\" — what Abram had just turned down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*After all this* — three words that make the verse. To feel it you have to know what just happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abram had just done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 14: he rescues Lot by attacking a coalition of kings at night with 318 men. On the way back he meets Melchizedek, gives him a tenth of everything, and then the king of Sodom offers him all the recovered goods."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram refuses. He will not take *a thread or a sandal strap*, so that no one can ever say the king of Sodom made him rich."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So he has just fought a war, given away a tenth, and declined a fortune. Then night falls."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Don't be afraid, Abram. I Myself am your shield, your protector. And your reward will be very great.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves answer something specific:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**shield** — he has just made permanent enemies of four kings", "**reward** — he has just refused payment"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for reward is a plain one for wages or spoils. God uses the vocabulary of the thing Abram walked away from and applies it to Himself."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Abram says next.** He does not thank God. He says: what can You give me, since I remain childless? The great promise lands on a man whose actual complaint is elsewhere entirely — and God takes him outside to look at the stars rather than rebuking him for it."}]}, "Genesis 15:12–16": {"title": "\"Afterward they will come out with great wealth\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Abram is asleep, and God tells him four hundred years of his family's history before any of it exists."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The darkness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A deep sleep fell over Abram, and **a thick, terrifying darkness** settled on him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *deep sleep* is **tardemah** — the same word used of Adam when the woman was taken out of his side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is unconscious for the covenant. What follows in the chapter is a smoking firepot and a burning torch passing between the divided animals, and only one party walks the path. Abram is asleep for the whole thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four hundred years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your descendants will live as strangers in a land not their own. **They will be enslaved and mistreated for four hundred years**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told in advance. The slavery is not a surprise God responds to later; it is announced to Abram while Isaac is not yet born."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the sentence after it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But I will judge the nation that enslaves them, and afterward your people will come out with great wealth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Great wealth** — the Hebrew is a large substance, heavy possessions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years of unpaid work, and God says at the start how the account will be settled. What happened on the night of the exodus was not a windfall; it was the back wages, promised centuries before the first brick was laid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus records it plainly: *the LORD had made the Egyptians so favourable toward His people that they gave them whatever they asked. And so Israel walked out carrying the wealth of Egypt.* See the note on Exodus 12:33–36."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits in Abram's life"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters earlier, Abram had come back from rescuing Lot, met Melchizedek, and *gave him a tenth of everything.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He refused a penny of the king of Sodom's — *I will not take a thread or a sandal-strap, lest you should say, \"I have made Abram rich.\"* He tithed to the priest and turned down the king."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, in chapter 15, God says *I am your shield, your **exceedingly great reward*** — and lays out the four hundred years and the great wealth on the far side of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You will go to your ancestors in peace\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the middle of a prophecy about slavery, one line addressed to the man himself: *you will be buried in a good old age.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not going to see any of it. He is told anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Amorites"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In the fourth generation your descendants will return here, **for the wrongdoing of the Amorites is not yet complete**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason for the delay is not about Israel at all. Another people is being given four centuries."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who walked between the pieces.** In that culture both parties to a covenant passed between the divided animals, invoking the same fate on themselves if they broke it. Abram was asleep. Only the fire went through, which means the covenant was cut on one signature."}]}, "Genesis 15:4–5": {"title": "Count the stars", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God takes a childless old man outside at night and gives him something to look at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No. That man will not be your heir.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram had proposed Eliezer of Damascus, his steward. It was the sensible legal arrangement of that world for a man with no son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God refuses the workaround. *A son from your own body — he will be your heir.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Outside"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And God **led him outside into the night**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is taken out of the tent. Whatever conversation was happening indoors, it continues under the sky."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look up. **Count the stars**, if you can even number them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is **saphar** — and it carries both senses at once. To count, and to recount: to tell, to declare, to narrate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the root behind **sepher**, a book or scroll, and **sopher**, a scribe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the instruction has two readings sitting in one word: number them, and read what they tell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the heavens are said to be doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 19 puts it plainly: *the heavens **declare** the glory of God, and the firmament **shows His handiwork**. Day unto day utters speech, and night unto night **reveals knowledge**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *there is no speech nor language where their voice is not heard. Their line has gone out through all the earth, and their words to the end of the world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sky is described as speech going out to every nation, with no language barrier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The story told there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The account runs from the virgin to the lion — from **Virgo**, the woman with the seed, to **Leo**, the lion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 3:15, spoken in the garden: *He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise His heel* — the seed of the woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation 5:5: *the **Lion** out of Judah's tribe, the Root of David, has won.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first promise and the final victory, and the whole of it written overhead where Abram was told to look. See the notes on Genesis 3:14–15 and Revelation 5:5–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he believed the LORD, and He counted it to him as righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the very next verse — the sentence Romans and Galatians are built on. It follows a night sky and nothing else. See the note on Genesis 15:6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sand, later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Genesis 22, after Isaac on the mountain, God adds the second image: *as the stars of the heaven and as the sand which is on the seashore.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two innumerable things, and both given as a picture before there was one child."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How long he had to hold it.** Isaac is born in chapter 21. Between this night and that morning is roughly a quarter of a century, and a stretch of it he spent being called *father of a multitude* with no son by his wife. See the note on Genesis 17:3–8."}]}, "Genesis 15:6": {"title": "Credited as righteousness", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nine words in English, and three New Testament books are built on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Abram **believed** God, and God **counted it to him as righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *believed* is **aman** — to lean on, to trust, to find something firm enough to put weight on. It is the root of **amen**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was believing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before it: God takes him outside at night and says *look now toward heaven, and count the stars if you are able to number them. So shall your descendants be.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram is seventy-five, childless, with a barren wife. There is nothing to inspect except the sky and the promise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Counted\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chashab** — to reckon, to impute, to enter into an account. Paul's Greek for it is **logizomai**, the bookkeeping word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness was **credited** to him. Not produced by him, not earned, not demonstrated. Entered on his account by the One keeping the books."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abram had not done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was no law. Sinai is four hundred and thirty years away — no commandments, no tabernacle, no priesthood, no sacrifice system, no circumcision yet either. Paul makes a great deal of that last one: the declaration came *while he was still uncircumcised*, so that he could be the father of everyone who believes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had not built anything, kept anything, or performed anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He believed, and the entry was made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abram did afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Went down to Egypt and told a king his wife was his sister. Took Hagar because the promise seemed slow. Laughed on his face when God said Sarah would have a son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The entry stood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three books that quote it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul in Romans 4, to prove righteousness is credited rather than earned. Paul again in Galatians 3, to prove the blessing of Abraham comes by faith. And James 2, from the other side, on what a living faith looks like."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three arguments, one verse, and all three treat it as the founding precedent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God calls him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The offspring of **Abraham My friend**.* Isaiah says it, and 2 Chronicles and James say it too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only man in Scripture God calls His friend, and the friendship is dated from a night when he had nothing but a sky full of stars and a sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter does next.** The covenant is cut — animals divided, and Abram put into a deep sleep while a smoking firepot and a burning torch pass between the pieces alone. He was asleep for the signing. See the note on Genesis 15:12–16."}]}, "Genesis 17:15–16": {"title": "Sarai becomes Sarah", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The wife gets a new name too, and hers is the more improbable of the two."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As for Sarai your wife, **you are no longer to call her Sarai; her name will be Sarah**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The difference in Hebrew is one letter. **Sarai** to **Sarah** — and the letter added is *hey*, which is also the letter added into Abram to make Abraham."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same letter, out of God's own name, put into both of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sarah** is princess, noblewoman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was true of her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ninety years old. Barren her entire life — the very first thing Genesis records about her, back in chapter 11, is *Sarai was barren; she had no child.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is her introduction in Scripture, and it stood for sixty-five years of narrative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says over her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will bless her**, and I will give you a son by her. **I will bless her so that she will become nations; kings of peoples will come from her.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will bless her* — twice in one verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nations.** **Kings of peoples.** Said of a woman who had never carried a child."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who has to say the name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham does. *You are no longer to call her Sarai.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So for the year before Isaac, a man of ninety-nine called *father of a multitude* was addressing his ninety-year-old barren wife as *princess*, and the whole household heard both names every day. See the note on Genesis 17:3–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham did in the next verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Abraham fell on his face **and laughed**, and said in his heart, \"Shall a child be born to a man who is a hundred years old? And shall Sarah, who is ninety years old, bear a child?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He laughs, face down, doing the arithmetic."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sarah laughs too, a chapter later, behind the tent door. And the boy is named **Isaac** — *he laughs*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament says about her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By faith Sarah herself also received strength to conceive… because she judged Him faithful who had promised.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The laughing is not mentioned. The reckoning is. See the note on Hebrews 11:8–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Peter holds her up as an example to wives, calling her a woman of *a gentle and quiet spirit* — which is not the phrase most readers would have chosen for the woman who threw Hagar out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The name in the name.** The letter added to both of them, *hey*, appears twice in the covenant name YHWH. Whatever else the renaming did, it put something of His own name into theirs before either promise came true."}]}, "Genesis 17:3–8": {"title": "Abram becomes Abraham", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God changes a man's name to a claim that is not yet true, and makes him answer to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ninety-nine years old. Twenty-four years since the promise was first made. One son, Ishmael, by a servant girl, and none by Sarah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abram fell facedown.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Abram** — exalted father. That name was already a stretch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Abraham** — *ab hamon*, father of a multitude."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"No longer will you be called Abram; your name will be Abraham, **for I have made you the father of many nations**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **have made** you.* Past tense, completed, spoken to a childless man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *I will make you*, though the promises around it are future — *nations will come from you, kings will be among your descendants*. This one clause is put in the past, and it is the clause the name is built on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes exactly this line in Romans 4 as his example of *the God who calls things that do not exist as though they already do.* See the note on Romans 4:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him to carry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every person in that camp — servants, herdsmen, his wife, the neighbours he traded with — now had to call him *father of a multitude*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For a year. Isaac is not born until chapter 21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man of ninety-nine, with no child by his wife, introducing himself by that name for a full year. Every use of it was either a joke or an act of faith, and Genesis records no complaint from him about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sarai too"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A few verses on: *As for Sarai your wife, you shall not call her name Sarai, but **Sarah** shall be her name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sarah** means princess. *And I will bless her, and she shall be a mother of nations; kings of peoples shall be from her.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ninety years old, barren her whole life, and renamed princess and mother of nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"everlasting\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "It appears three times in these six verses — an **everlasting** covenant, an **everlasting** possession, and *I will be their God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **olam** — perpetual, without a stated end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abraham fell facedown **and laughed**.* That is verse 17, and Genesis reports it without softening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He laughs, Sarah laughs in the next chapter, and the boy is named **Isaac** — *he laughs*. God took the laughter and made it the child's name."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not asked of him here.** Nothing. The whole passage is *I will* — I will make, I will keep, I will give, I will be their God. Circumcision comes in the next paragraph as the sign of a covenant already made, not a condition of getting one."}]}, "Genesis 2:15–17": {"title": "How much of the garden was permitted", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first command God gives a human being is usually remembered as a prohibition. Read the sentence and the proportions are the other way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Count the words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Eat freely from any tree in the garden — all of it is yours to enjoy. Only one tree is not for you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Hebrew the permission is emphatic — the verb is doubled, a construction that intensifies: *eating you shall eat*. Every tree. Then one exception."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination's phrase for it is *a generous word*, and that is a fair reading of the grammar. The restriction exists inside an abundance, and it is a fraction of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is at stake in the one tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The tree of the knowledge of good and evil.* Not a magic fruit. The name describes what taking it would mean: deciding for yourself what counts as good and what counts as evil, rather than receiving that from God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is why the serpent's pitch in the next chapter is exactly that — *you will be like God, knowing good and evil.* He is not offering something the tree does not offer. He is recommending it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"On the day you do, you will surely die.\"** They ate, and did not drop dead that afternoon, and readers have puzzled over it ever since. The Hebrew phrase can carry the sense of a sentence passed rather than an execution carried out — and the chapter that follows describes exactly that: exile, hard ground, a guarded gate, and a life now running toward dust. Something did die that day, and the rest of the Bible is about what it cost to undo."}]}, "Genesis 20:17–18": {"title": "The first prayer for healing in the Bible", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*So Abraham prayed to God, and God healed Abimelech.* It is the first time anyone in scripture prays for someone else's healing, and the circumstances are uncomfortable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is praying for whom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham had just told a foreign king that Sarah was his sister — the same lie he had told in Egypt — and Abimelech had taken her into his household on the strength of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the man praying is the man whose deception caused the crisis. The man being prayed for is the injured party who behaved better throughout: God Himself says Abimelech acted *with a clear conscience and clean hands*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says to do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the chapter God tells Abimelech to return the woman, and adds: *he is a prophet, and he will pray for you, and you will live*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is not that Abraham deserves to be heard. It is simply what is to happen. God assigns the intercession to the person in the wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD had closed up every womb in Abimelech's household.* The reason for the healing is only explained after it is granted — the writer holds it back to the last line of the chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Abraham, who has been childless for decades and is one chapter away from Isaac, prays for another household's fertility and is answered."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage will not support.** It is not a model of a righteous man whose prayers were effective because of his conduct. Genesis records the lie in full, without excusing it, and then records the prayer being answered. Both things happened, and the text is not embarrassed by either."}]}, "Genesis 21:1–2": {"title": "\"Right on time, at the very season God had spoken of\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-five years after the promise, two verses record its arrival, and they are almost entirely made of repetition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repeated phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD came to Sarah, just as He had said He would. He did for her exactly what He had promised… right on time, at the very season God had spoken of.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in two verses: as He said, as He promised, at the appointed time. The Hebrew is even more insistent than the English."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer is not being redundant. He is making the only point that matters about a promise delayed by a quarter of a century — it did not arrive early, and it did not fail."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What had happened in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The intervening chapters are not a story of patient waiting. Abraham laughed at the promise. Sarah laughed at it. They tried to arrange it themselves through Hagar, with consequences that run for the rest of Genesis. Twice Abraham passed his wife off as his sister to save himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of that is edited out, and none of it changes the timetable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac means *he laughs*. The child is named after the reaction the promise provoked, and Sarah says so herself in the verses following: *God has brought me laughter, and everyone who hears about this will laugh with me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The joke told at God's expense becomes the boy's name."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line the Illumination adds.** *Nothing was too late. Nothing was forgotten.* It is a translator's summary rather than a separate clause in the Hebrew, and it is a fair rendering of what the triple repetition is doing. The passage exists to say that the delay was not neglect."}]}, "Genesis 24:10–11": {"title": "Ten camels, loaded", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*The servant took ten of his master's camels and set out, with all kinds of good things from his master in his hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The number is not incidental. Genesis counts things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Ten, and what it carries"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten camels, each loaded, standing for the whole of what Abraham had to send. The servant is not carrying a sample. He is carrying his master's substance out on an errand, and every good thing in that caravan came from the man who sent him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham is the man who had already given a tenth to Melchizedek and refused to take a thread from the king of Sodom. The wealth on those camels is what a man has when he has handled his blessing that way — held loosely, given first, received back with interest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the wealth was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not display. Every item on those animals was going to be given away — to Rebekah, to her mother, to her brother, before the servant would even eat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the shape of the whole chapter. The blessing goes out ahead of the servant, arrives before he explains himself, and secures the bride for the son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The picture the chapter draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Christian readers have long seen the outline here and it is hard to miss: a father sends a trusted servant to find a bride for his son, and the servant goes loaded with the father's own riches to give away on the way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The camels are how the errand is funded. Ten of them, fully laden, is Genesis saying the sending was not done by halves."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Rebekah does with them.** She waters all ten — somewhere between fifty and a hundred litres, hauled up by hand for animals belonging to a stranger. The chapter establishes her character by what she was willing to do for a caravan she had nothing to gain from, and then puts her at the head of the promise."}]}, "Genesis 25:29–34": {"title": "\"So Esau treated his birthright as though it were nothing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The narrator waits until the last line to say what the story was about, and then says it flatly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Esau treated his birthright as though it were nothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *Jacob cheated him*. Genesis is perfectly willing to record Jacob's manipulation elsewhere and does not spare him — but the summary of this scene lands on Esau, and on his estimate of what he was holding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not condemned for being hungry. He is condemned for the exchange rate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a birthright was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The firstborn's portion: a double share of the inheritance, the headship of the family, and in this family the covenant line — the promise made to Abraham and passed to Isaac."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what was on one side of the trade. On the other side: lentils."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speed of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Esau ate and drank, then got up and left.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs in a row, with no pause between them. The Hebrew reads as a rapid sequence — he did not linger, did not reflect, did not appear to think anything significant had occurred."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The transaction cost him nothing at the moment he made it. That is precisely what the writer wants you to see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I'm about to die\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look, I'm about to die. What good is a birthright to me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not about to die. He had come in from hunting, tired and hungry, and talked himself into an emergency — and used the emergency to justify letting go of the long thing for the immediate one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the New Testament does with him.** Hebrews 12 calls him godless and ties him to the warning about missing God's grace — *who traded away what was his by birth for a single meal.* The word there is about a person who treats sacred things as common. Esau's failure is presented as a failure of valuation. See the note on Hebrews 12:12–17."}]}, "Genesis 26:12–14": {"title": "A hundredfold in a famine year", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, and the timing is everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When he planted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with a famine — *besides the one in Abraham's time* — and God telling Isaac not to go down to Egypt but to stay put."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sowing happens in a year when the land was not producing, in a country belonging to somebody else, by a man who had just been told not to go where the food was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The harvest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Isaac planted crops in that land, and that same year he reaped a hundredfold, because the LORD blessed him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundredfold was extraordinary in ordinary conditions — Jesus uses it as the top end of the good-soil yield in the parable of the sower. In a famine it is absurd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the writer gives the reason in the same sentence, without elaboration: *because the LORD blessed him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"sowed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is plain agriculture: he put seed in the ground. There is no ritual described, no vow, no negotiation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What preceded it is in the verses just before — God's promise to be with him and bless him if he stayed. Isaac stayed, and then he planted, which is what a man does with land he intends to remain on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What followed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His wealth kept increasing until he became very wealthy… so many flocks and herds and servants that the Philistines grew envious of him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter does not stop there, and it is worth reading on: the envy leads to his wells being stopped up, and to Abimelech asking him to leave. The blessing brought friction with the neighbours almost immediately."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Isaac does about the wells.** He digs them again, is driven off, digs again, is driven off again, and digs a third — and names it Rehoboth, *room*, saying the LORD has made room for us. The prosperity in this chapter is not frictionless. It is a man who keeps digging."}]}, "Genesis 26:3–5": {"title": "The promise repeated to the second generation", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only chapter in Genesis where God speaks to Isaac at length, and what He says is almost entirely a repetition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stay in this land, and I will be with you and bless you… I will make your descendants as many as the stars in the sky, and I will give them all these lands, and through your offspring all nations on earth will be blessed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item was said to Abraham first. Isaac receives no new terms — he receives his father's covenant restated in his own hearing, with his own name on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because Abraham obeyed me and kept my charge, my commands, my decrees, and my instructions.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the striking part. The reason offered to Isaac is his father's obedience, not his own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac is at this point a middle-aged man whose chief recorded acts are being nearly sacrificed, receiving a wife arranged for him, and — in the very next paragraph — lying about that wife exactly as his father had twice done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He inherits a promise anchored in somebody else's faithfulness. That is the shape of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stay in this land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One command, and it runs against the obvious response to famine. Abraham had gone down to Egypt in a famine and it went badly. Isaac is told to stay in the place of scarcity, with a promise attached."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hundredfold harvest a few verses later happens in the land he was told not to leave."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the New Testament puts the reader.** *If you belong to Christ, then you are Abraham's seed, and heirs according to the promise.* Paul makes the same move Genesis does here — an inheritance received on the strength of somebody else's faithfulness. See the note on Galatians 3:26–29."}]}, "Genesis 27:28–29": {"title": "The blessing spoken over the wrong son", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Isaac blesses the son in front of him, believing he is blessing the other one, and the words cannot be taken back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May God give you the dew of heaven and the richness of the earth — an abundance of grain and new wine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dew** first. In a land with no summer rainfall, dew was what kept vines and crops alive through the dry months — moisture arriving overnight from nowhere anyone could arrange."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then grain and wine, the staples of the covenant blessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May nations serve you… be lord over your brothers… may those who curse you be cursed, and those who bless you be blessed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last line is the promise made to Abraham, handed down. Isaac is passing on the covenant, not merely wishing his son well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The deception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob is wearing Esau's clothes with goatskins on his hands and neck, and he lies twice — *I am Esau your firstborn*, and then *the LORD your God gave me success*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis does not hide any of it, and Jacob spends twenty years in exile as a consequence, deceived in turn by Laban with a substituted older sibling. See the note on Genesis 29:21–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens when Isaac finds out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Isaac trembled violently* — and then says something remarkable: *I blessed him, and indeed he will be blessed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not revoke it. Not because he could not have tried, but because he recognises he has been overruled. The blessing had already been assigned before either boy was born: *the older will serve the younger.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Esau gets.** A blessing of a kind, and it is mostly an absence: away from the richness of the earth, living by the sword, serving his brother. The chapter that opened with a father's plan ends with the plan overturned and nobody in the family happy — and the covenant intact."}]}, "Genesis 29:21–25": {"title": "\"When morning came — there was Leah\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four words in this passage do more work than the rest of the chapter, and in Hebrew they land as bluntly as they do here: *when morning came — there was Leah.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jacob had already done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To feel this you have to remember the last time a father, a firstborn and a disguise appeared together in this family. Jacob had gone in to his blind father wearing his brother's clothes with goat's hair on his hands, and when Isaac asked who he was, he said: *I am Esau your firstborn.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now Jacob is the one in the dark, and it is a firstborn substituted for a younger sibling that undoes him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Laban even says it out loud a few verses later — *it is not done here to give the younger before the firstborn*. It is the exact thing Jacob did, spoken back to him as local custom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What have you done to me? Didn't I serve you for Rachel? Why have you deceived me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word for *deceived* is the one used of Jacob in the earlier story. He is asking Laban the question his brother had every right to ask him. The narrator does not comment. He does not need to."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the story is not saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a lesson that God pays people back — the chapter never says so, and what follows is not punishment but a long, tangled family in which God keeps working anyway. Leah, married by trickery and unloved, becomes the mother of Judah, and the line runs through her to David and to Christ. Not through Rachel, the one Jacob chose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some things are only visible from the other side of them. Jacob had a name for what his brother did to him and no name for what he had done himself, until he woke up on the receiving end of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the use of this passage: it puts a man in the position he once put someone else in, and it lets him hear his own words come back in another man's mouth. Nothing is explained to him. He simply sees it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On Leah.** She is the person this is being done *to*, and the story rarely gets told from where she is standing. Handed over, unwanted the next morning, and told for years that she was not the one. The chapter that follows names her sons and you can hear it in every name. And it is her son Judah, not Rachel's, whose line ends up carrying the promise. The Bible keeps doing this, and it is worth noticing every time."}]}, "Genesis 3:14–15": {"title": "The first promise, spoken to the serpent", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first announcement of a rescue in the Bible is not addressed to the people being rescued. It is spoken to the enemy, over their heads."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being talked to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God curses the serpent, and in the middle of the sentence describes a future conflict — and its outcome. Adam and Eve are standing there listening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not consulted, promised anything directly, or asked to do anything. They overhear their own hope while being sentenced."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Her seed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Between your offspring and hers. Her seed will crush your head.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is unusual. Descent in Hebrew genealogy runs through the man, and Scripture speaks of the seed of Abraham, of David, of Israel. The seed **of a woman** is not how anyone talked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Christian readers have seen a virgin birth signalled from the first pages because of it, and Paul's phrasing in Galatians follows the same track: *God sent forth His Son, born of a woman.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two wounds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her seed will crush your head, and you will strike his heel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both parties are injured, and the injuries are not equal. A crushed head ends the fight. A struck heel is real damage that heals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything from here to the cross is the working out of that asymmetry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the promise sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even here, in the wreckage, God spoke a promise.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before the curse on the ground, before the exile from the garden, before the garments of skin. The first thing God says after the fall is that it will not stand."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the wound cost.** *You will strike his heel* is not a small thing. Whatever the crushing of the head would take, the promise made in Genesis 3 already includes the injury to the one doing it. See the note on Isaiah 53:4–6."}]}, "Genesis 3:20–24": {"title": "Garments of skin, and the sword at the gate", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three things happen in these five verses, and they do not obviously belong together: a name, a set of clothes, and an expulsion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man named his wife Eve, because she would become the mother of all who live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does it immediately after being told he will return to dust. Standing under a death sentence, Adam names his wife **Life**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only thing he has to go on is the promise about her seed, three verses earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clothing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD God, tender even in judgment, made garments of animal skin and clothed them Himself — covering what they could not cover on their own.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had made coverings out of fig leaves. Those are not mentioned again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God's replacement required an animal to die — the first death in Scripture, and it happens so that two guilty people can be dressed. He does not hand them the skins. He clothes them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The expulsion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He drove the man out, and east of the garden He stationed cherubim and a flaming sword.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated reason is the tree of life: *he must not reach out and take also from the tree of life and live forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read as pure punishment that is bleak. Read as it stands, it is a mercy — immortality in a fallen condition would have locked the ruin in permanently. The gate is shut so that the situation stays fixable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cherubim, with a sword, guarding the way back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next time cherubim appear in Scripture they are hammered out of gold at either end of the mercy seat, wings over it, looking down at the place where the blood goes. Same creatures. The sword is gone, and the way is open."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line of the chapter.** *To guard the way to the tree of life.* Not to abolish it — to guard the road. Revelation ends with that tree in the middle of the city, its leaves for the healing of the nations, and no sword mentioned anywhere."}]}, "Genesis 3:6–7": {"title": "\"Their eyes were opened — but not the way the serpent said\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The serpent had promised that their eyes would be opened and they would be like God, knowing good and evil. The eyes did open. Everything else was a lie."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things she saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The fruit seemed good to eat, pleasing to the eye, and desirable for the wisdom it promised.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three appraisals, made in order, before a hand moves. The tree had been there the whole time; what changed was the looking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's list of what the world offers runs the same three ways — the craving of the body, the craving of the eyes, the bragging about what a man has. See the note on 1 John 2:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Who was with her\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She took some and ate, and gave some to her husband, **who was with her**, and he ate too.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words that are easy to skim. Adam was not away in another part of the garden. He was standing there through the entire conversation, and said nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the opened eyes actually showed them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Suddenly they saw their own nakedness, and they felt exposed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise was knowledge of good and evil. The delivery was self-consciousness — the first thing the new sight produced was shame about themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been naked in the previous chapter and it is specifically noted that they felt no shame. Nothing about their bodies changed. What changed was what they could see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fig leaves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they sewed fig leaves together and made coverings for themselves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first human response to guilt is a covering they make. It does not last — before the chapter ends God makes them garments of skin, which required a death they did not perform."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was actually lost.** Not innocence in the abstract. Read on: the sound of God walking in the garden, and two people hiding from it. The tree cost them the walk."}]}, "Genesis 3:8–13": {"title": "\"Where are you?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God's first recorded words after the fall are a question, and He already knows the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sound"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They heard the sound of the LORD God walking in the garden in the cool of the day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The detail implies a habit. This was not the first evening He had walked there — what is new is that nobody comes out to meet Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where are you?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an inquiry. It is the opening of a conversation with people who have hidden, and it invites them to say where they have got to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every question in this passage works the same way: *who told you that you were naked?* and *what have you done?* God asks; He does not announce."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Adam actually admits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I heard You in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked, so I hid.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice what is in the answer and what is missing. Fear, nakedness, hiding — all true. The eating is not mentioned until God names it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, when it is named: *the woman You gave me — she gave me the fruit, and I ate.* Two people blamed in one sentence, and one of them is God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The woman's answer is shorter and does the same: *the serpent deceived me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody says *I did it*. That is the whole exchange, and it is the first conversation between God and guilty people in the Bible."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does anyway.** The curse, then the promise about the woman's seed, and then — before the exile from the garden — *the LORD God made garments of skin for Adam and his wife, and clothed them.* They had made coverings out of leaves. He replaced them with something that required a death, and dressed them Himself."}]}, "Genesis 32:22–26": {"title": "\"I will not let You go unless You bless me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jacob sends everyone he loves across a river and stays behind, and something happens in the dark that changes his name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Alone, on purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jacob was left alone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just sent his wives, his children and everything he owns across the ford. Esau is coming with four hundred men and Jacob has spent the evening arranging gifts to go ahead of him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he empties the camp of everybody and stays on the wrong side of the water by himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wrestling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man wrestled with him until the breaking of day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text calls him a man. Hosea, looking back, calls him the angel; Jacob will say he has seen God face to face and names the place accordingly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the man saw that he could not overpower Jacob* — which is a strange sentence about God, until you notice how it is resolved. One touch to the hip socket and the match is over. The strength was never the question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grip"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let me go, for the day is breaking.\" But Jacob said, \"I will not let You go unless You bless me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is now injured and cannot win, and he holds on anyway. The wrestling turns into clinging at the exact moment he stops being able to fight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asked his name — the same question his father asked him in the dark years earlier, when he answered *I am Esau your firstborn*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This time he says Jacob: heel-grabber, supplanter. He tells the truth about himself, and is immediately given a new name."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he took away.** A blessing, a name, and a limp. Genesis says the sun rose on him as he passed Penuel, *and he was limping because of his hip*. The injury does not get healed in the story. He walks into the meeting with Esau newly named and permanently marked."}]}, "Genesis 4:8–12": {"title": "Blood that cries out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first death in the Bible is a murder, and God's response introduces an image the rest of scripture keeps using."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Your brother's blood is crying out to me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew noun is plural — *bloods*, which the rabbis noticed and commented on for centuries: not one life only but every life that would have come from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the verb is the ordinary word for a shout, a cry for help, the noise a wronged person makes. Blood that has been spilled is pictured as a voice, and what it says is *this was wrong and nothing has answered for it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what innocent blood does. It does not go quiet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question and the dodge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is your brother Abel?* — God asks a question He does not need answered, exactly as He did in the garden."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Am I my brother's keeper?* is Cain's answer, and it is a good deal more brazen than Adam's. He does not hide; he throws the question back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to Cain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ground he worked will no longer yield to him, and he becomes a wanderer. But read on four verses: when Cain says the punishment is more than he can bear and that anyone finding him will kill him, God puts a mark on him to protect him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first murderer in scripture is not executed. He is marked so that nobody touches him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this comes back.** Hebrews says the blood of Jesus *speaks better than the blood of Abel* — and the comparison only works because of this passage. Abel's blood cried out for the guilty to answer. The other blood is described as saying something better, which does not mean louder. It means it speaks in the opposite direction. See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}]}, "Genesis 43:26–34": {"title": "Five times as much, and a man who had to leave the room", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Joseph gives a dinner for the brothers who sold him, and two things happen at it that they do not understand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room he went into"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph hurried out, for his heart was overflowing at the sight of his brother, and he was about to weep. He went into his private room and wept there. Then he washed his face, came out, controlled himself, and said, \"Serve the meal.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been the second most powerful man in Egypt for years. He sees his full brother for the first time since he was seventeen and has to leave his own house to cry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis records the washing and the composure. It is one of the most human paragraphs in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The brothers were seated in front of him in order, from the oldest to the youngest, and they looked at one another in amazement.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had told the steward their ages. Ten men are placed in birth order by a household they have never visited, and they cannot account for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator gives no explanation either. He simply reports them staring at each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Benjamin's portion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Benjamin's portion was five times larger than anyone else's.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph does it deliberately, and it is a test as much as a gift. These are the men who once could not stand their father's favouritism toward one son and threw him in a pit for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now, in front of them, the youngest is singled out again — and this time nobody objects. *So they drank and were merry together with him.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it is going.** The next chapter is the silver cup planted in Benjamin's sack, and Judah — the brother who proposed selling Joseph — offering himself as a slave in the boy's place. The dinner is the setup. Joseph is finding out whether they are the same men, and they are not."}]}, "Genesis 45:21–28": {"title": "\"Don't quarrel on the way\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Joseph sends his brothers home loaded with gifts, and the last thing he says to them is oddly practical."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What each of them got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To each of them he gave a new set of clothing, but to Benjamin he gave three hundred pieces of silver and five sets of clothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just revealed himself and forgiven them outright. Then he hands out clothes — and gives Benjamin five times what anyone else received, exactly as he did at the dinner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not being careless. He knows what favouritism did to this family; he grew up in the middle of it. He does it anyway, in the open, having already told them the whole thing was God's doing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parting instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Don't quarrel on the way.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew can mean do not be agitated, or do not fall out with one another. Both fit the situation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have a three-week journey ahead with a great deal to talk about — chiefly who suggested what, twenty-two years earlier, at a pit. Joseph, who has just told them not to be distressed or angry with themselves, adds one more sentence for the road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He forgave them and then anticipated the argument they were going to have about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What convinced Jacob"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jacob's heart went numb; he could not believe them.* Words did not do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when he saw the carts Joseph had sent to carry him, the spirit of their father Jacob revived.* The carts did — physical evidence that somebody in Egypt had made arrangements for an old man to travel."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says.** *It is enough. My son Joseph is still alive. I will go and see him before I die.* The Hebrew for *it is enough* is one word — rav, plenty, abundance. He has just been told he owns Egypt's best, and what he says is enough, and then names the only thing he actually wants."}]}, "Genesis 49:1–2": {"title": "\"What will happen in the days to come\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old man on a bed calls twelve grown sons around him, and what follows is the longest piece of poetry in Genesis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The days to come\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will tell you what will happen to you **in the days to come**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **acharit ha-yamim** — the latter days, the end of the days. It is the standard prophetic phrase; Isaiah, Hosea, Micah and Daniel all use it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not handing out farewell advice. He announces prophecy and then delivers twenty-five verses of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Come together and listen, sons of **Jacob**. Listen to **Israel** your father.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both names in one sentence, and Genesis never uses them carelessly. Jacob is the supplanter, the man who grabbed a heel; Israel is the name he was given at Peniel, after the wrestling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The father speaking is both of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of blessings these are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the twelve and they are not uniformly pleasant. Reuben loses his birthright in three lines. Simeon and Levi are told their anger is cursed and they will be scattered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not flattering anybody. Whatever is in these verses, he is saying what he sees."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the weight falls"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of the twelve get far more than the rest — Judah, and Joseph. Judah gets the sceptre and the line: *the sceptre shall not depart from Judah… until Shiloh comes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what is said over the others is short. A single line each, in most cases — Gad, Asher, Naphtali, Issachar, Dan, Zebulun. Two dozen words apiece, and there is a great deal packed into them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this chapter is worth going slowly through"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the road to Emmaus, *beginning at Moses and all the Prophets, He expounded to them in all the Scriptures the things concerning Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Beginning at Moses. Genesis is Moses, and this is the last chapter of it but one. The blessings over the twelve sons were in that sermon. See the note on Luke 24:13–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each of these sons became a tribe, and each tribe was given a piece of ground — and Jesus walked that ground, town by town."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter ends.** *He drew his feet up into the bed, breathed his last, and was gathered to his people.* Twenty-five verses of prophecy and then a man pulling his feet up onto a bed. Genesis notices things like that."}]}, "Genesis 49:13": {"title": "Zebulun — a harbour for ships", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One line, about a coastline, and it was said two hundred years before the land was divided."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Zebulun will **live by the seashore**. He will be **a harbour for ships**, and his border will reach toward Sidon.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob is in Egypt, dying, with twelve sons and no territory anywhere. Nobody had surveyed anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two centuries later Joshua divides the land by lot, and Zebulun's portion runs up into the north country toward the coast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Zebulun** means dwelling. Leah named him: *now my husband will dwell with me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He lived"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He left Nazareth and went to live in **Capernaum**, a town beside the sea in **the territory of Zebulun and Naphtali**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew says it deliberately, and then quotes Isaiah over it:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Land of Zebulun and land of Naphtali… **the people sitting in darkness have seen a great light**, and on those living in the land where death casts its shadow, light has dawned.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nazareth is in Zebulun. Capernaum is on the border of Zebulun and Naphtali. He grew up in one and made His home in the other, and both are in the tribe whose name means dwelling. See the note on Matthew 4:12–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The harbour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zebulun is *a harbour for ships*, and the lake it sits on is where the boats were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon's boat, pushed out a little from the land, used as a pulpit and then filled until it settled low in the water. The storm crossing. The walking on the sea. Levi's tax booth at the harbour of Capernaum."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And after the resurrection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Simon Peter said, \"I am going fishing\"… and all that night they caught nothing at all.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Back on the same water, in the same trade, after everything. And He is on the beach at daybreak with a charcoal fire, bread and fish already on it, asking whether they have any food."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He fills their net, feeds them breakfast, and then restores Peter three times over a charcoal fire — the only other charcoal fire in John's Gospel is the one Peter warmed himself at while he denied Him. See the note on John 21:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Livelihood, a meal, and the thing Peter could not say. All three, in the tribe called dwelling."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses added over Zebulun.** *Rejoice, Zebulun, in your going out… they shall call the peoples to the mountain; there they shall offer sacrifices of righteousness, for they shall partake of the abundance of the seas.* A tribe on the trade routes, calling the nations in."}]}, "Genesis 49:14–15": {"title": "Issachar — the donkey between the burdens", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The saddest of the twelve, and it describes a man who could have had rest and chose a load instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Issachar is a **strong donkey**, resting among the sheepfolds. When he sees how good his resting place is and how pleasant the land, **he will bend his shoulder to the load and submit to hard labour**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the logic of it. He sees the rest is good. He sees the land is pleasant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And that is the reason he takes up the burden — the Hebrew is that he becomes a servant to forced labour, a slave gang."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had the rest in front of him and put his shoulder under a load."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Issachar** means *there is hire*, or *he brings wages*. Leah named him that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man whose name means wages, ending up in forced labour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Issachar's portion was the Jezreel valley — some of the best farmland in the whole country, which is what makes the *pleasant land* line so exact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in that valley sits a town called **Nain**, which means pleasant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened at that gate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As He came near the town gate, a dead man was being carried out — the only son his mother had, and she was a widow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A funeral procession, in the tribe whose blessing is a donkey between two burdens. Two dead men in one town — the son on the frame, and the mother's whole future with him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He touched the frame, the bearers stood still, and He said *young man, I say to you, get up.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did not ask for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jesus **gave him back to his mother**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is requested. No follow, no serve, no condition. He raises the boy and hands him over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Issachar submitted to hard labour to keep a pleasant land. In the pleasant town in Issachar's own valley, a man is given his life back and is asked for nothing at all. See the note on Luke 7:11–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two burdens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older rendering has him *couching down between two burdens* — the two packs a donkey carries, one on each side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the Hebrew word is exactly, the picture is a strong animal lying down under weight it did not have to carry."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the tribe was later known for.** *The sons of Issachar, who had understanding of the times, to know what Israel ought to do.* Two hundred chiefs of them came to David at Hebron. The tribe that bowed its shoulder to a load became the tribe that could read a season."}]}, "Genesis 49:19": {"title": "Gad — the troop and the last word", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eleven words in English, and in Hebrew it is a piece of wordplay Jacob almost certainly meant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name inside the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Gad** means a troop, a raiding band. And the Hebrew line runs *gad gedud yegudennu, ve-hu yagud aqeb* — the same consonants hammered five times over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A troop shall troop upon him, but he shall troop upon their heel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Gad will be **attacked by raiders**, but he will **turn and strike at their heels**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trouble is not denied. He gets raided, and the raiders are described as a troop — more than he can meet head-on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the last clause is the last event. Whoever hits first, Gad has the final word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground Gad was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "East of the Jordan, on the frontier — the most exposed territory any tribe held, open to Ammon, Moab and every raiding party out of the desert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob's line was not decorative. It described the border they would live on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus walked into it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Across that lake, on the eastern shore, He met a man who could not be bound with chains, living among the tombs, cutting himself with stones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What is your name?\" \"**Legion**,\" he answered, because a great many demons had gone into him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Roman legion was some thousands of men. It is the largest concentration of the enemy named anywhere in the Gospels, and it is standing on Gad's side of the water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks, and it comes out. One word against a troop, and the man is found sitting, dressed and in his right mind. See the note on Luke 8:30–33."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will strike at their heels.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heel is where Genesis started. Chapter 3: *He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise His heel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob's fourteenth son-blessing uses the word from the promise made in the garden, and the man who grabbed his brother's heel at birth is the one saying it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shortest blessings carry the most.** Gad, Asher and Naphtali get one line each. That is the whole inheritance in words — a troop turned back, food fit for a king, and a doe set free — and every one of them turns up as ground Jesus stood on."}]}, "Genesis 49:20": {"title": "Asher — bread fit for a king", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One line, and it is about food."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Asher's food will be **rich**; he will provide **delicacies fit for a king**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *rich* is **shamen** — fat, the choicest part. And the second phrase is *dainties of a king*, the food set on a royal table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Asher** means happy, blessed. Leah named him: *happy am I, for the daughters will call me blessed.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asher's portion was the coastal strip in the far north-west, running up toward Tyre and Sidon — the richest agricultural land in Israel, olive country."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses says it in Deuteronomy 33: *let Asher dip his foot in oil.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus went into it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus went out from there and departed to the region of **Tyre and Sidon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Asher's border, and it is the only recorded time He left Israel's territory altogether."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There a Canaanite woman came after Him about her daughter, and what she got from Him was a sentence about bread:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is not right to take **the children's bread** and toss it to the little dogs.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children's bread"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls it bread, and He calls it the children's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not argue with the arrangement. She takes the picture and finishes it: *\"Yes, Lord — and even the little dogs eat **the crumbs** that fall from their master's table.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A crumb off that table broke a demonic hold on a child in another house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asher's line said *rich food, delicacies fit for a king*, and the meeting that happens on Asher's ground is a conversation about whose table the bread comes from. See the note on Matthew 15:21–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Woman, your faith is great. Let it be done for you just as you desire.\"* And her daughter was healed from that hour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She asked for a crumb and it was enough."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses added over Asher.** *As your days, so shall your strength be.* Set beside Jacob's line about rich food, the two blessings on this tribe are about being fed and being strong for the length of the days you get. See the note on Deuteronomy 33:24–25."}]}, "Genesis 49:21": {"title": "Naphtali — a doe set free", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The gentlest line in the chapter, and it is about something being let go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Naphtali is **a doe set free**, that bears **beautiful fawns**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a hind loosed, released. Something that was tied is not tied any more, and what comes out of it is beautiful."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second half can be read as beautiful fawns or as beautiful words — the Hebrew carries both, and older translations render it *he gives goodly words*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Naphtali** means my wrestling. Rachel named him after the struggle with her sister."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naphtali's territory was the north-west shore of the Sea of Galilee — Capernaum, Bethsaida, Chorazin, the Mount of Beatitudes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where Jesus based Himself. *Leaving Nazareth, He came and dwelt in Capernaum, which is by the sea, in the regions of Zebulun and **Naphtali**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew quotes Isaiah over it: *the land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali… the people who sat in darkness have seen a great light.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most of the miracles happened on that shoreline, and the Sermon on the Mount was preached on that hillside. Beautiful words, on Naphtali's ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The loosing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a synagogue in that country there was a woman bent double for eighteen years, who could not straighten up at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Woman, you are **loosed** from your infirmity.\"* And He laid His hands on her, and immediately she was made straight."}, {"k": "p", "t": "When the ruler of the synagogue objected about the Sabbath, He used the word again — *ought not this woman, being a daughter of Abraham, whom Satan has bound for eighteen years, be **loosed** from this bond on the Sabbath?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Loosed, twice, over a woman who had been tied for eighteen years, in the tribe whose blessing is a doe set free. See the note on Luke 13:10–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did the binding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it plainly in that synagogue: *whom Satan has bound.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a discipline, not a lesson. A binding, and He unties it in a sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses said over Naphtali.** *O Naphtali, satisfied with favour, and full of the blessing of the LORD.* The word is *ratson* — good pleasure, acceptance. Satisfied with favour, on the shore where He did most of His work."}]}, "Genesis 49:22–26": {"title": "Joseph — branches over the wall", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The longest of the twelve blessings, and Jacob spends most of it on titles for God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Joseph is a fruitful vine, a fruitful vine **beside a spring**, whose branches **climb over a wall**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things: fruitful, next to water, and not staying inside the boundary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *climb over* is to march over, to stride across. The wall does not stop it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The archers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"With bitterness archers attacked him; they shot at him and pressed him hard. **But his bow stayed steady**, and his arms were made strong **by the hands of the Mighty One of Jacob**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything true of Joseph's life is in that line — the pit, the slave sale, Potiphar's wife, the prison, the forgotten cupbearer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the strength is credited: *by the hands of the Mighty One.* His arms were made strong by somebody else's hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names for God, stacked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four in two lines, and Jacob is running out of ways to say it:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the Mighty One of Jacob**", "**the Shepherd** — the first time in Scripture God is called this", "**the Rock of Israel**", "**the Almighty** who blesses you"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old man who wrestled at Peniel calls Him a Shepherd, and it is the first occurrence of the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Blessings of the skies above, blessings of the deep that lies below, blessings of the breast and womb… **greater than the blessings of the ancient mountains**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Above, below, and the family line. And then a comparison — greater than the mountains, which is Hebrew for greater than the oldest and most permanent things anyone can point at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the branches went over the wall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph's portion was Samaria. And Jacob's well was on that ground — *near the piece of ground Jacob had given to his son Joseph.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A fruitful vine beside a spring.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was a wall between Jew and Samaritan, and it was absolute: *Jews have no dealings with Samaritans.* His disciples marvelled that He was talking with her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He went through Samaria, sat down at that well worn out from the journey, and asked a woman for a drink. See the note on John 4:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The branches ran over the wall on Joseph's own land."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *Let all these rest on the head of Joseph, on the brow of **the prince among his brothers**.* The Hebrew is the one separated from his brothers — the same word used of a Nazirite, one set apart. It is exactly what they did to him, turned into a title."}]}, "Genesis 49:27": {"title": "Benjamin — the morning and the evening", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last of the twelve, and it is one line about a wolf and two halves of a day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Benjamin is a **ravenous wolf**; **in the morning** he devours the prey, and **in the evening** he divides the spoil.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same animal, two different activities, split by the hours of one day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Morning: tearing. Evening: distributing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rachel died bearing him and called him **Ben-Oni**, son of my sorrow. His father renamed him **Benjamin**, son of the right hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only son of Jacob born in the land, and the only one whose name his father overruled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The most famous Benjamite"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Circumcised the eighth day, of the stock of Israel, **of the tribe of Benjamin**, a Hebrew of Hebrews.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it about himself twice — in Philippians and in Romans."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Saul was consenting to his death… **he made havoc of the church**, entering every house, and dragging off men and women, committing them to prison.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *made havoc* is the word used of a wild animal mangling a body."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *breathing threats and murder against the disciples of the Lord*, going to Damascus with letters, to bring back any he found *bound to Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the wolf in the morning, and Luke's vocabulary for it is not accidental."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same man, later, writing from a cell: *I have learned, in whatever state I am, to be content.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen letters distributed to churches across the empire. Collections carried from Macedonia and Achaia to the poor saints at Jerusalem. Timothy, Titus, Luke, Onesimus, Philemon, Priscilla and Aquila."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the evening he divides the spoil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what he divides is what he took: *what things were gain to me, these I have counted loss for Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Standing before Agrippa"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And now I stand here on trial for **the hope of the promise God made to our fathers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who dragged people to prison is in chains himself, on trial, and calls it standing for a hope. He asks the king a question rather than mounting a defence: *why should it be judged something incredible by any of you that God raises the dead?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the end of that hearing he has told Agrippa he wishes every man in the room were *as I am, except for these chains.* See the note on Acts 26:4–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it took"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One morning on the Damascus road, and a light above the brightness of the sun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in Jacob's line says the wolf became a different animal. It says what the same animal did with the second half of the day."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other famous Benjamite.** King Saul — the first king of Israel, from this tribe, who hunted David and died on Gilboa. And then Saul of Tarsus, from the same tribe, who hunted the Church and was renamed. Two men called Saul, one tribe, and only one of them let go of the sword."}]}, "Genesis 49:3–4": {"title": "Reuben — unstable as water", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The firstborn gets the longest list of titles in the chapter and loses everything in the next line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Reuben, you are **my firstborn, my might, the first sign of my strength** — first in rank and first in power.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four titles stacked, and every one of them is genuine. The double portion, the headship of the family, the priesthood of the household — all of it was his by right of birth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob lists it in full before taking it away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet** you are as unruly as the churning waters, and you will no longer hold first place.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older rendering is *unstable as water*, and it is the phrase the tribe is remembered by."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water takes whatever shape the container gives it. It has no form of its own and no way of holding a line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For you **climbed into your father's bed** and defiled it — you went up onto my couch.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 35 records it in one sentence and moves on: *Reuben went and lay with Bilhah his father's concubine, and Israel heard about it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is all. No confrontation, no punishment, no conversation — *Israel heard about it*, and then thirty-five chapters of silence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The silence ends here, on a deathbed, in front of all eleven brothers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the flesh at its best produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reuben is not a villain. He is the one who tried to save Joseph from the pit — *let us not kill him… throw him into this pit*, intending to come back for him. He is the one who offered his own two sons as security for Benjamin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He means well every time, and nothing holds. Good instincts, no strength to carry them through — which is the definition of the word Jacob uses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What became of the tribe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No judge came out of Reuben. No prophet, no king, no leader of any note."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deborah's song asks about them: *among the divisions of Reuben there were great searchings of heart… why did you sit among the sheepfolds?* They debated it at length and did not come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reuben first, and he loses the birthright. Simeon and Levi next, and their anger is cursed. Then Judah, and the sceptre."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three sons pass before the blessing lands anywhere, and the first three are what the natural man is: unstable, and then violent."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the birthright went.** *The birthright was given to the sons of Joseph* — 1 Chronicles 5 says so outright, and explains why: *because he defiled his father's bed.* The double portion went to Joseph, the kingship went to Judah, and the priesthood went to Levi. What Reuben held alone was split three ways and given to other men."}]}, "Genesis 49:5–7": {"title": "Simeon and Levi — the anger, not the men", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only curse in the chapter, and it is aimed carefully."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 34. Their sister Dinah was violated by Shechem, and the two brothers proposed a treaty: intermarriage, on condition that every male in the town be circumcised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it came to pass on the third day, when they were sore, that two of the sons of Jacob took each man his sword and came upon the city boldly, and slew all the males.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They waited until the men could not defend themselves, and killed the town."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In their fury they crippled oxen\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In their anger they killed men, and in their fury they **crippled oxen**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to hamstring — cut the tendon so the animal can never work again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the detail Jacob picks out. Not just the killing. Destruction beyond any purpose, on animals that had done nothing, because the rage had to go somewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is cursed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Cursed be their anger, so fierce, and their fury, so cruel.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read it exactly. Not *cursed be Simeon and Levi*. Their anger is cursed, and their fury."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob separates the men from the thing in them. It is the only curse he pronounces in the whole chapter and it is laid on a temper rather than a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will **scatter** them throughout Jacob and **disperse** them across Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had used their unity to do it — two brothers acting together. So they are separated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simeon's inheritance ended up as scattered towns inside Judah's territory, and the tribe faded; it is missing from the blessing of Moses in Deuteronomy 33 altogether."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to the other one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Levi was scattered too — forty-eight cities among the tribes, with no land of their own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the scattering became their office. *The LORD is their inheritance*, and the tribe with no territory is the tribe that carried the tabernacle, taught the law and served at the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Levi at Sinai was the tribe that rallied to Moses when the calf was made. The sword that had been drawn on Shechem was put at God's disposal, and a curse ended up a priesthood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three at the start"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reuben, unstable. Simeon and Levi, violent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first three sons in the chapter, and the whole natural man is in them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Judah."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jacob says he will not do.** *\"Let me not enter their council; let me not join their gathering.\"* He will not be associated with the decision. He had said nothing at the time except that they had made him stink among the inhabitants of the land — and he says the rest of it here, twenty chapters later, dying."}]}, "Genesis 49:8–12": {"title": "Judah — the sceptre and the vine", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fourth son, and the chapter changes register completely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Judah, your brothers will **praise** you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yehudah** means praise. Leah named him: *now I will praise the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first three sons produced instability and violence. The fourth is called praise and gets the throne."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What praise does in that tribe's history"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jehoshaphat, king of Judah, faced three armies with no plan, and was told: *you will not even need to fight this battle. Take your positions, stand still, and watch the salvation of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He appointed singers to go out **in front of the army**, and *when they began to sing and to praise, the LORD set ambushes* — and it was over before Judah reached the battlefield. See the note on 2 Chronicles 20:16–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Judah is a **young lion**… like a lion he crouches and lies down — who would dare rouse him?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three stages of one animal in two lines: the young lion that has come up from the prey, the mature lion, and the lioness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation names Him from this verse: *the Lion of the tribe of Judah has prevailed.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sceptre"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The sceptre will not depart from Judah, nor the ruler's staff from between his feet, **until he to whom it belongs shall come**, and the obedience of the peoples will be his.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of *he to whom it belongs* is **Shiloh** — a word that occurs nowhere else, and the oldest Jewish readings take it as a name of Messiah. Its root carries the sense of rest, tranquility."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the obedience of the peoples* — not Israel. The peoples, plural, the nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The donkey and the vine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will **tie his donkey to the vine**, his colt to the choicest branch.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody ties livestock to a vine. A donkey would strip it in an hour, and a vine is the most carefully tended plant a man owns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a picture of abundance so complete that the loss does not matter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a colt, tied, waiting for a king — *tell the daughter of Zion, behold, your King is coming to you, lowly, and sitting on a donkey, a colt, the foal of a donkey.* They found it *tied* at a doorway, and He rode it into Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will **wash his garments in wine**, his robes in **the blood of grapes**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not spilled on. Washed in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 63 takes the same image the other way: *why is Your apparel red, like one who treads in the winepress? I have trodden the winepress alone.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vine, again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am the vine; you are the branches… **apart from Me you can do nothing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it in an upper room, on the night of the cup, and the branch's whole job in that passage is to stay attached. The fruit is not the branch's work. See the note on John 15:5–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How long the sceptre stayed.** Judah held the right to try capital cases until Rome took it away, and the old accounts record the rabbis lamenting that the sceptre had departed and Shiloh had not come. He was in Nazareth at the time."}]}, "Genesis 5:21–24": {"title": "The man who is missing from the list", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 5 is a genealogy with a refrain. Every entry ends the same way, and one does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lived so many years, he had sons and daughters, and then he died.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight times in the chapter, like a drumbeat. Even Methuselah, who outlives everyone, ends with those two words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Enoch: *Enoch walked with God, and then he was no more, because God took him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence everybody else gets is missing from his."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Walked with God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in four verses. The Hebrew form suggests a continuing, habitual walking — not a journey with a destination, but a way of going about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only one other person in Genesis is described this way: Noah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the detail worth noticing is when it started. *When Enoch was 65, he fathered Methuselah. And Enoch walked with God — closely, faithfully — for 300 years after that.* The walking begins when the child arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shortest life in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "365 years, in a list where the others run to eight and nine hundred. By the standards of the genealogy he barely got started."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter measures everyone in years. Enoch is the one it does not need to."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Hebrews adds.** *By faith Enoch was taken from this life, so that he did not experience death… for before he was taken he was commended as one who pleased God.* The commendation came first, and the taking followed. Whatever the walking was, God enjoyed it enough to end it by simply keeping him."}]}, "Genesis 6:1–4": {"title": "The hundred and twenty years, and what they probably count", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four of the strangest verses in the Bible, and the honest thing to say about them first is that nobody is certain what they describe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Their days will be a hundred and twenty years\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two readings have been argued for a very long time, and both are serious:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**a new limit on human lifespan** — from now on, people live at most 120 years", "**a countdown to the flood** — 120 years of reprieve before the waters come"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second fits the immediate context better. The sentence begins *My Spirit will not contend with humanity forever*, which sounds like a clock running out on a situation rather than a change to biology. And the flood is the next thing that happens in the narrative."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first reading has a real problem: several people after this live far longer — Noah, Shem, Abraham at 175. If it were a hard limit, it did not take."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sons of God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sons of God saw that these daughters were beautiful, and they took wives from among them, whomever they chose.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three explanations have long competed: angelic beings, the line of Seth intermarrying with the line of Cain, or dynastic rulers taking women by right. The phrase *sons of God* elsewhere in the Old Testament usually means heavenly beings, which is why the first reading is the oldest — but nothing here settles it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage is doing.** Whatever the details, the direction is clear: the phrase *took… whomever they chose* is the language of power used without restraint, and it sits immediately before God looking at the earth and seeing that every inclination of the human heart was bent. Genesis is describing a world coming apart, and it does not stop to explain the mechanics."}]}, "Genesis 6:9–12": {"title": "\"Noah walked with God\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three things are said about Noah, and then three about the world he lived in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said about him"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**righteous** — right, in the sense of standing straight with God", "**blameless among the people of his time** — the word is used of sacrificial animals without defect", "**he walked with God**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third phrase is used of only one other man in Genesis: Enoch, four chapters earlier. Both are described the same way, and both stand out from a genealogy of people who simply lived and died."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"blameless\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not mean sinless. Genesis will show Noah drunk and shamed within a chapter of leaving the ark, and it does not edit that out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blameless among the people of his time* is a comparison. In a world the next verses call corrupt and violent, he was the one who was not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said about the world"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The earth was corrupt in God's sight and full of violence. God looked at the earth and saw how ruined it was.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word for *corrupt* and the word for *ruined* are the same root — and it is the same root used a few verses later when God says He will destroy them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The judgement matches the diagnosis exactly. They had ruined it; it would be ruined. God is described as confirming a state of affairs rather than inventing a penalty for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What kept Noah afloat.** Hebrews says he built the ark *by faith*, moved with godly fear, having been warned about things not yet seen. And Genesis, before any of the building starts, says the thing the whole account rests on: *Noah found favour in the eyes of the LORD.* The favour comes first, in the verse before this paragraph."}]}, "Genesis 7:1–5": {"title": "\"You and all your household\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and the two halves of it do not obviously match."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mismatch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go into the ark, **you and all your household**, for I have seen that **you alone** are righteous in this generation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is about one man. The invitation covers eight people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is said about Noah's wife, his sons, or his sons' wives — not a word about their character in either direction. They go in on the strength of the verdict passed on him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern this belongs to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It happens repeatedly. A household is spared for the sake of one man's standing: Noah's family here, Rahab's family in Jericho, Lot's family in Sodom, the Israelite houses at Passover reckoned by household rather than by person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Bible keeps counting people in units of *house*, and it keeps doing it at the moments of rescue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Come into the ark\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is *come*, not *go*. It is the word you use to invite someone toward where you already are — which means God is not sending Noah into the boat from outside. He is calling him in from somewhere He already is."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *And Noah did everything the LORD commanded him.* It is the fourth time Genesis says it in two chapters. The obedience is recorded flatly, without commentary, and then the rain starts seven days later — which is how long Noah had to sit in a finished boat under a clear sky."}]}, "Hebrews 1:1–3": {"title": "\"He sat down\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opening sentence of the letter, and it ends on a posture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How God used to speak"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In piece after piece, and in one way after another, and through the prophets.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in Greek, both beginning with the same sound — in many portions and in many manners. Fragments, over centuries, by dreams and visions and voices and signs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about that is criticised. It is simply described as partial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How He speaks now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has spoken to us **in a Son**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No article in the Greek — not *through the Son* as one more prophet in the line, but in the manner of a Son. The mode of communication has changed, not just the messenger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said about Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God's own glory, blazing out. He is exactly what God is, pressed into view.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second image is from a seal pressed into wax — the exact impression, every feature reproduced. The word is **charakter**, which is where our word comes from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He carries the whole of it along on one powerful word.* Not holding the universe up like a pillar. Carrying it forward, by speaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He had cleansed our sins away Himself, **He sat down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Priests in the tabernacle did not sit. There was no chair in it — the work never finished, because the sacrifices never finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This priest sat down. The letter comes back to it again and again, and it is the whole argument in one verb: the sitting proves the work is over."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where He sat.** *At the right hand of Majesty, in the heights.* Not merely resting — the position of authority beside the throne. Finished, and seated in the place of rule."}]}, "Hebrews 10:19–21": {"title": "\"A road that is alive\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The letter's argument turns here from explanation to invitation, and it opens with a place nobody was allowed to enter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the way leads"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The way into the holiest place is open to us now, and we can walk in without a flinch.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The holiest place is behind the curtain — one man, one day a year, with blood and smoke. See the note on Leviticus 16:11–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word behind *without a flinch* is **parrhesia**, boldness, the freedom of somebody with the right to speak in the room. The same word the letter used at 4:16 about the throne."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What makes it open"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because of the blood of Jesus.* Not because of improvement, and not because the holiness of the place has been reduced."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"A road that is alive\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He opened up a road for us that no one had ever walked before, a road that is alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two claims. It is **new** — the Greek word originally meant freshly slaughtered, and came to mean recent, unused. Nobody has been down it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is **living**. A road is not usually alive. The writer is stretching the image on purpose, because the way is a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Through the curtain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It runs straight through the curtain, which is His own body.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records the temple curtain torn in two from top to bottom at the moment Jesus died. Hebrews puts the two together: the torn veil and the torn body are one event, and what they produce is a road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Matthew 27:51–53."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The priest at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we have a great Priest set over God's household.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things are now in place — an open way, a road that lives, and a priest already inside. The next verse begins *let us draw near*, and the rest of the chapter is what to do about it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why \"flinch\" is the right word.** Everything in the old arrangement taught people to hesitate: the curtain, the once-a-year, the incense so the priest would not die. The letter is not asking readers to feel casual. It is telling them the reason for the hesitation has been dealt with."}]}, "Hebrews 11:23–29": {"title": "Moses, and the accounting he did", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six sentences about Moses, each beginning the same way — *faith is why* — and the middle one is a calculation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the parents did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Moses, newly born, was hidden by his parents for three months: they could see he was a beautiful child, and the king's order did not frighten them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter begins its Moses section with somebody else's faith. Two slaves defy an infanticide order and are credited with it before Moses does anything at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weighing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He weighed the disgrace of Christ against everything stored up in Egypt and found the disgrace worth more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is a commercial one — reckoning, estimating value. This is not described as a sacrifice made against his own judgement. It is a man doing the arithmetic and concluding that one column was larger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the thing he weighed against a kingdom's treasury is called *the disgrace of Christ*, written of a man who lived over a thousand years before the crucifixion. The writer is claiming the same reproach runs through both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He could see the One nobody can see\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line is a deliberate contradiction, and it explains how the arithmetic came out that way. Moses walked away from Egypt *without a thought for how furious the king would be* because the invisible was, to him, the more solid of the two."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The list ends in water.** *Faith is why they walked through the Red Sea as though it were dry ground — and when the Egyptians tried the same thing, the water swallowed them.* The same act, performed by two parties, with opposite outcomes. The writer's point across the whole chapter is that the difference was never in the walking."}]}, "Hebrews 11:30–31": {"title": "Rahab in the roll of honour", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two sentences, side by side, and the pairing is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The company she is in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is a list: Abel, Enoch, Noah, Abraham, Sarah, Isaac, Jacob, Joseph, Moses. Patriarchs and prophets. Then the walls of Jericho — and then Rahab."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is one of only two women named in the chapter, and the writer does not soften her description. *Rahab the prostitute.* He could have written \"Rahab of Jericho.\" He did not, and neither did James."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What her faith is said to consist of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since she had welcomed the spies in peace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a confession, not a sacrifice, not a long obedience. She let two men in and did not turn them over. The writer treats a single act of hospitality toward God's people as the visible form of faith in her case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Did not die with those who refused to believe.* The Greek word is about disobedience through unbelief — refusing to be persuaded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone in Jericho had the same evidence, as she says herself on the roof. The line the chapter draws is not between the informed and the ignorant. It is between those who were persuaded and those who were not."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**James, on the same woman.** *Was she not shown to be right by that?* James uses Rahab as his second example of faith proved by action — the first being Abraham. Between the two writers she is cited as an example of faith and as an example of works, and it is the same episode both times. The two arguments are not in conflict; see the note on James 2:17."}]}, "Hebrews 11:32–35": {"title": "The sentence that turns halfway through", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the passage people quote for triumph, and it is the passage that makes the chapter honest — because of where it stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brought down kingdoms, dealt out justice, got hold of promises, shut lions' mouths, put fires out, got clear of the sword, were made strong where they had been weak, turned fierce in battle, sent foreign armies running. Women got their dead back alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item is a deliverance. Faith that worked, visibly, in this life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And others were tortured and would not take the way out, so that they could rise to something better.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word — *others* — and the same faith produces the opposite outcome. The writer does not change vocabulary, does not explain, does not suggest the second group had less of it. He puts both under the same heading."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read on past this block and it gets starker: mocked, flogged, chained, stoned, sawn in two, destitute, wandering in deserts. And then the verdict on all of them: *the world was not worthy of them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any teaching that treats deliverance as the proof of faith has to account for the back half of this sentence, where people with the same faith were not delivered and the writer says so approvingly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Would not take the way out* is the detail that settles it. They were offered release and refused it — which means their situation was not a failure of faith but an exercise of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The end of the chapter.** *None of them received what was promised.* All of them — the delivered and the destroyed alike — are described as still waiting. Whatever this chapter is doing, it is not sorting people by outcome."}]}, "Hebrews 11:8–12": {"title": "\"He went out without knowing where he was going\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three times in five verses the writer says *faith is why*, and each one is attached to something that looks like a disadvantage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Faith is why he went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Abraham obeyed when he was called… **he went out without knowing where he was going**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 12 records the instruction: *get out of your country… to a land that I will show you.* Not a land I have shown you. Will show."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had a direction and no destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Faith is why he camped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why he **lived as a foreigner in the very land he had been promised**, camping in tents.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the strange one. The land was his by promise, and he spent his life in it as a resident alien in a tent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only piece of it he ever owned outright was a burial cave he paid four hundred shekels for, to bury Sarah in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With Isaac and Jacob, who were promised the same thing he was* — three generations in tents, in a country deeded to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was actually waiting for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **waiting for the city with real foundations, the one God designed and built**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is between a tent and a city, and between pegs and foundations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's explanation is that Abraham was not disappointed by the tent because he was not aiming at the land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Faith is why Sarah conceived"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why **Sarah herself was given the strength to conceive** and bore a child when her body was long past it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that she received power for the laying down of seed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reason given: *because she **counted the One who promised as good for it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is a reckoning word — she assessed His credit and judged it sound. It is the same kind of accounting language Paul uses of Abraham."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Sarah had done at the tent door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She laughed. *Therefore Sarah laughed within herself, saying, \"After I have grown old, shall I have pleasure, my lord being old also?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then denied it: *I did not laugh* — *for she was afraid.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The New Testament puts her in the roll of faith anyway, and says nothing about the laughing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So from **one man, and one whose body was finished**, came descendants past counting — as many as the **stars** overhead, as many as the **grains of sand** along the shore.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two images God used with Abraham himself — the stars in Genesis 15, the sand in Genesis 22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One dead body at the start of the sentence and an uncountable multitude at the end of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter says a few verses later.** *These all died in faith, **not having received the promises**, but having seen them afar off, and were assured of them, and embraced them.* Every one of them died still waiting, and the chapter calls it faith rather than failure."}]}, "Hebrews 12:1–4": {"title": "\"Do not take your eyes off Jesus\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The whole of chapter 11 was a list of names. Chapter 12 begins with *therefore*, and turns the list into a stadium."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look at the size of the crowd around us, **every one of them a witness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *so great a cloud of witnesses*. Abel, Enoch, Noah, Abraham, Sarah, Moses, Rahab, Gideon, Samson, David — and the ones at the end of the chapter who were sawn in two and wandered in sheepskins."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **martus** is one who testifies. They are not spectators in a gallery; they are the ones whose evidence has already been given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two things to drop"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Strip off **whatever weighs us down**, and **the sin that keeps winding itself around our legs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two separate items. The first is a weight — not necessarily sinful, just heavy. The second is the Greek **euperistatos**, a word found nowhere else, built from *well* and *standing around*: something that surrounds you closely, like a long robe tangling your stride."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The track"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Run without quitting, all the way down **the track that has been staked out in front of us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The course is laid out. Nobody in the passage is choosing a route."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not take your eyes off Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is **aphorao** — it means to look away *from* one thing in order to look *at* another. Both halves are in the single word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You cannot simply add Him to what you are already staring at. Something has to be looked away from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is where our faith **starts** and He is the One who brings it to the **finish**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Archegos** — the originator, the pioneer, the one who goes first. And **teleiotes** — the completer, the one who finishes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both ends of it belong to Him. It is His faith from the starting line to the tape, and the instruction is simply to keep looking at the One holding both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How He ran"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A joy was waiting for Him, and to get to it **He went through the cross and counted the shame as nothing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The joy was in front of Him, not behind. He was looking at something too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And now He has **taken His seat** at the right hand of God's throne.* Every other priest stood. He sat down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In your fight with sin **you have not yet had to bleed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written to people under real pressure who thought they had reached their limit. He puts their situation beside His and leaves it there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter goes next.** Straight into the passage about being brought up as sons — *paideia*, upbringing. Fix your eyes on Him, and then: you are being raised, not managed. See the note on Hebrews 12:5–11."}]}, "Hebrews 12:12–17": {"title": "The root of bitterness, and what it actually grows out of", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This passage gets quoted in two directions at once. One crowd reads *chase holiness; without it nobody is going to see the Lord* and hears a threshold to clear. Another reads *nothing bitter takes root* and hears a warning about holding grudges. Both are picking up something real, and both are reading past the sentence that ties them together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Read the order of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning is one sentence with two halves, and the halves are joined:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Watch that nobody misses out on God's grace**", "**and that nothing bitter takes root**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *misses out* is a word for falling short of something, coming up behind it, being left out — the same word Paul uses in *all have sinned and fall short*. And the bitterness grows out of that. Not out of what somebody did to you. Out of somebody missing the grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole diagnosis. Bitterness is what grows in a person who is trying to get by on something other than grace. It is the fruit, not the root."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The root is a quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is not the writer's own image. He is reaching back to Deuteronomy, where Moses warns against a root among the people producing poison — someone who hears the covenant and decides privately he will be fine on his own terms. That is the person in the picture. Not a wounded person. A self-sufficient one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is why the next line lands where it does: *nobody sexually immoral or godless like Esau*. Esau is not introduced as an example of lust. He is introduced as a man who traded what was already his for something immediate, because he did not value it. Missing the grace comes first. The visible failure comes after."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other half: what \"holiness\" is doing here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Chase peace with everybody, and chase holiness; without it nobody is going to see the Lord.* Read alone, that reads like a bar to clear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But look where it sits. Seven lines earlier, the same writer says God disciplines us *so that we come to share His holiness* — His, given, shared. The word is the same one. Holiness here is not a standard the reader manufactures and presents; it is something being handed to them, that they are told to go after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image in the surrounding verses is medical, not judicial: hands gone limp, knees giving way, a limb about to be thrown out of joint — *healed instead*. The writer is treating an injury, not setting an entrance exam."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it looks like in practice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage never says \"watch that nobody sins.\" It says watch that nobody **misses the grace**, because that is where the trouble starts and everything else follows. It puts the attention one step upstream of where we usually put it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On Esau and \"no room for repentance.\"** This line frightens people badly. In the Greek the sentence is about *Isaac's* decision, not God's: Esau found no way to reverse the blessing his father had already spoken over Jacob, though he begged for it with tears. It is a story about a transaction that could not be undone, not a statement that a person can want God and be turned away."}]}, "Hebrews 12:18–24": {"title": "Two mountains, and which one you are standing on", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is one of the most carefully built passages in the New Testament, and its whole argument is carried by two words that are easy to read straight past: **what you have come to**. He says it twice, and the second time reverses the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What you have come to is not a mountain a hand can touch* — fire, gloom, dark, howling storm, a trumpet, a voice. This is Sinai, where the law was given, and the writer describes it entirely in terms of what it did to the people standing at the bottom of it:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["they begged for the voice to stop", "they could not bear the command that was given", "even Moses said he was terrified and shaking"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the description is about the law being wrong. Everything in it is about what happens to people who come to God that way. It is unbearable — and the one man in the story who had God's own attention is the one who says he is shaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What you have come to is Mount Zion* — and then a list that does not stop:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the city of the living God, the Jerusalem above", "angels in numbers nobody can count", "the great gathering, every name on record in heaven", "God the Judge of all", "righteous people brought to completion", "Jesus, standing between God and us", "blood that says something better than Abel's"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice the tense. Not *will come to*. **Have come to.** The writer is not describing a destination for later; he is telling people where they are already standing while they read."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail everyone misses: Abel's blood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage ends by comparing two kinds of blood, and it expects you to know the story. Abel's blood cried out from the ground — it called for justice against the man who spilled it. That is what innocent blood does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So when the writer says this blood *says something better*, he is not saying it speaks more loudly. He is saying it speaks in the opposite direction. Abel's blood asked for the guilty to answer. This blood answers for the guilty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole argument of the letter in one image, and it is placed at the end of the mountain comparison on purpose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two details from Genesis sharpen it. God says to Cain *your brother's blood is crying out to me from the ground* — the verb is the ordinary word for a shout for help, and the noun is plural, *bloods*, which readers have long taken to mean not one life but every life that would have come from it. Spilled blood is pictured as a voice that will not go quiet until something answers it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then notice what God actually does with Cain. He does not execute him. When Cain says the sentence is more than he can bear, God puts a mark on him so that nobody will touch him. Even in the passage that gives us blood crying for justice, the first murderer walks away marked for protection. See the note on Genesis 4:8–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it matters where you are standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two mountains are not two eras that a reader chooses between as a matter of taste. They are two ways of approaching God, and the passage says plainly that one of them terrified everybody who tried it, including Moses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A person can be at Zion by fact and still be living at Sinai by habit — still approaching on the basis of how the week went. The passage is written to move them, and it does it not by pressure but by telling them where they already are."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the fire at the end.** The next lines say our God is a fire that burns things up, and it is often read as a threat bolted onto the comfort. Look at what the fire is doing in context: it takes away *everything that can be shaken* so that *what cannot be shaken is left standing*. It is not aimed at the person. It is aimed at what will not hold them."}]}, "Hebrews 12:25–29": {"title": "What the shaking is for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Read on its own, this sounds like the letter ending on a threat: do not turn Him down, everything is going to be shaken, our God is a fire that burns things up. Read as the finish of the argument that has been building since the two mountains, it is doing almost the opposite."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose clause is the whole thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence about shaking has a *so that* in it, and everything depends on where it points:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "the taking away of everything that can be shaken … **so that what cannot be shaken is left standing**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shaking is not the goal. It is the method. The goal is what is still there afterwards. And the writer tells you immediately what that is: *the kingdom we are being given can never be shaken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek makes it plainer than English does. The things that go are described with an ordinary participle — things being shaken. What remains is *asaleutos*, a single word meaning unshakable, immovable. It is not that the kingdom survives the shaking. It is that it is not the kind of thing shaking touches."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Being given, not built"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The kingdom we are being given.* Not earned, not held onto by strength, not kept by performance. Receiving is the only verb in the sentence, and it is passive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That matters for how the next line reads. *Let us hold on to grace, and by it worship God.* A person who thinks the shaking is aimed at them will hold on to their own record. The writer says hold on to grace — and then says it is *by* that grace that acceptable worship happens at all."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fire at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our God is a fire that burns things up.* This is the last line of the chapter and it is often read as the sting in the tail."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But it is a quotation from Deuteronomy, and it is the same image the passage has just spent four verses explaining. Fire is what removes what will not last. Set next to the sentence before it — a kingdom that cannot be shaken — the fire is not aimed at the person holding the kingdom. It is aimed at everything that could not have held them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this is for, practically"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone is standing on a mix of things: what God has said, and a quieter set of supports we would rather not test — how the year went, how disciplined we have been lately, what other people think. This passage says a day comes when everything of that second kind goes, and the point of saying so is not to frighten anybody. It is so that a reader knows now which of the things they are standing on will still be there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"how much less will we get away with turning our backs.\"** The warning is real and should not be softened. But notice its shape: it is about *refusing to hear the One who is speaking* — turning away from the voice. It is not a warning about failing. It is a warning about not listening, addressed to people the writer has just told they have already arrived at Zion."}]}, "Hebrews 12:5–11": {"title": "The word behind \"discipline,\" and what it is not", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the passage people reach for when something has gone wrong and they want to know whether God did it to them. That is a heavy question to bring to a text, and this one is usually read with the wrong word in mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word actually is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word running through the whole passage — five times in seven verses — is *paideia*. It is built on the word for a child, and in ordinary Greek it means the whole business of bringing a child up: training, teaching, forming. It is the word a Greek would use for an education."}, {"k": "p", "t": "English \"discipline\" has drifted toward punishment, and once that is the word in your head, the passage reads as God arranging misfortunes. Put *upbringing* where discipline stands and read it again. The subject is not what God sends. It is that He is raising sons rather than managing strangers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument the writer is making"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Follow the logic and notice what it is arguing *for*:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["being brought up this way is how you know you are handled as a son", "being left out of it would mean you were not one", "our own fathers did it and we thought better of them for it, not worse"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point being pressed is not \"expect suffering.\" It is **you belong to Him.** The writer is comforting people who were beginning to wonder, and the evidence he offers them is that they are being brought up at all."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it says about the pain, exactly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No discipline feels like a good thing while it is happening — it feels miserable.* The passage does not spiritualise that, and neither should any reading of it. It concedes the misery outright."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then it says the outcome is *a harvest of peace and righteousness* — and notice that both of those are given, not achieved. Two verses earlier the same writer says God does it *so that we come to share His holiness*. Share. His. Whatever this upbringing produces, the passage never describes it as the reader manufacturing something to present back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not say every hard thing that happens is sent by God to teach a lesson. The passage never makes that claim, and reading it in is how this text ends up being used to explain a diagnosis or a bereavement to somebody who is already on the floor."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What it says is narrower and kinder: God is bringing you up as a son, and a son is not left to raise himself. That is a claim about your standing with Him, made to people who were losing their nerve, and it is meant to steady them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"He lays the lash on every son.\"** The line quotes Proverbs, and it is the hardest sentence here. In its own context the image is a father's correction, not a judge's sentence — and the writer's use of it lands on the word *son*, which is the noun the whole passage keeps returning to. The emphasis is on who is being addressed, not on the instrument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul uses the same word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **chastened** by the Lord, so that we would not be condemned along with the world* — 1 Corinthians 11:32, and the verb there is **paideuo**, from this same root."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul puts the chastening and the condemning on opposite sides of the sentence. Whatever the upbringing is, it is what keeps a son out of the world's verdict, not a lighter version of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the same argument this passage is making at length. See the note on 1 Corinthians 11:31–34."}]}, "Hebrews 13:1–6": {"title": "\"I will never let go of you\" — and what it is attached to", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the most quoted promises in the Bible, and it is quoted almost entirely out of its sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the promise is given as a reason for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep your way of life free of the love of money, and be satisfied with what you have, **because** He has said it Himself: I will never let go of you, and I will never walk away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the argument for contentment. The reasoning runs: you can stop grasping, because the one thing you cannot lose is Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a more useful promise than the free-floating version. It is not offered as general comfort — it is offered to a specific anxiety about money and security, as the ground for letting go of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strength of the Greek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence in Hebrews stacks negatives in a way English cannot reproduce — something like *I will never, no never, leave you; I will never, no never, forsake you*. Five negative particles in one line."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer is quoting God's promise to Joshua, and he piles the language up because he wants it to be unmissable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So we can say it right out: The Lord is the One helping me — I will not be afraid of anything anyone can do to me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives the promise and then supplies the answer, quoting Psalm 118. The believer's confidence is not manufactured; it is a line handed to them to say back."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The company it keeps.** The list before it is entirely practical: love each other, take in strangers, remember prisoners as though chained with them, honour marriage. Then contentment, then the promise. Whatever else this chapter is, it is not abstract — and the promise about God never letting go sits among instructions about hospitality and prison visits."}]}, "Hebrews 13:15–19": {"title": "A sacrifice made of words", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The letter has spent twelve chapters explaining that the sacrifices are finished. Then it names three that are not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through Him let us keep bringing God an offering of praise, words out of our mouths thanking His name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is from Hosea — *the fruit of our lips*. Under the old system you brought an animal; here the offering is speech."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two details keep it from being sentimental. It is **through Him** — the same mediation the whole letter has been arguing for. And it is *continually*, a present-tense keeping on rather than an occasion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second and third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not forget to do good and to share what you have; those are the offerings God takes pleasure in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Praise, then generosity — and the writer uses sacrificial vocabulary for both. The word for *takes pleasure* is the one used of a sacrifice being accepted as a pleasing aroma."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the offerings that remain are: what you say about God, and what you do for people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction about leaders"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They stay awake over your souls, and they will have to give an account, so let it be a glad job for them and not a groaning one — groaning would do you no good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An unusually practical note. The reason given for cooperating is not the leaders' authority but the readers' own benefit: a discouraged shepherd is no use to the sheep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pray for us… I ask it all the more urgently, so that I can be given back to you sooner.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer of the most theologically dense letter in the New Testament ends by asking for prayer and admitting he wants to see them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why \"through Him\" carries the weight.** Praise offered by people who know their own record can only be brought if somebody else makes it acceptable — which is precisely what the gold plate on Aaron's forehead was for, and what the letter says Christ now is. See the note on Exodus 28:36–38."}]}, "Hebrews 2:14–16": {"title": "Slaves to a fear, and what was actually broken", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is one of the plainest statements in the New Testament about why the incarnation happened, and it gives a reason that has nothing to do with teaching or example."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since the children are made of flesh and blood, He took on the same flesh and blood Himself* — and then a purpose clause, which is the point of the sentence:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**so that by dying He could break the one who held the power of death**", "**and set free the people who had spent their whole lives as slaves because they were afraid to die**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He became human in order to die. Not primarily to teach, not to demonstrate. The argument is that only someone with a body could do this particular thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Break\" is not \"destroy\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *katargeo* — the same word behind *cut off* in Galatians 5, and it means to make idle, to put out of work, to leave with nothing to do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That precision matters, because the devil is plainly still active in the rest of the New Testament. The claim is not that he was annihilated at the cross. It is that the specific thing he held — *the power of death* — was taken out of his hands and he was left with nothing to hold over anybody."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The slavery is a fear, not a fate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look closely at what the passage says people were enslaved by. Not death. **Fear of death** — and *their whole lives*, which is the phrase that gives it away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The slavery being described is not what happens at the end. It is what fear of the end does to the middle: the way a person lives who is quietly managing an outcome they cannot control. The letter treats that as a life in chains, and treats the cross as the thing that unlocks it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He takes hold of\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last line uses a vivid verb — reaching out and seizing, taking a grip on. It is used elsewhere of catching hold of someone who is sinking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is not angels He takes hold of; it is Abraham's children.* The writer is answering an unspoken question about why the rescue took the form it did. It took that form because of who was being grabbed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What this does not promise.** The passage does not say a believer will not die; the same letter goes on to list people who died in faith without receiving what was promised. What it says was broken is death's *hold* — the fear that ran a person's whole life. That is a real freedom, and it is not the same claim as exemption."}]}, "Hebrews 3:1–2": {"title": "\"The High Priest of the faith we own out loud\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the title given to Jesus in them is unusual."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the readers are called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brothers **set apart as holy**, sharing in **a call that came down from heaven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before anything is asked of them, two things are said about them. They are holy, and their calling originated somewhere other than in themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *sharing* is **metochoi** — partners, partakers. The same word is used later of *partakers of Christ*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Think hard about **Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katanoeo** — to fix the mind on, to observe closely, to consider attentively. It is the word used of Peter looking into the tomb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole command in these two verses. Not do, not try. Look at Him carefully."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two titles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One God sent* — **apostolos**, the sent one. The only time in the New Testament the word is applied to Jesus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **High Priest** of the faith we own out loud.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An apostle comes out from God to men. A high priest goes in from men to God. Both directions, one Person, in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Of our confession\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **homologia** — from *homo* (same) and *lego* (say). To say the same thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the High Priest **of that**. Of what is said out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not of our feelings, and not of our private opinions. The word means speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter keeps saying about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word turns up twice more, and both times as something to keep hold of:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since we have a great High Priest who has passed through the heavens, Jesus the Son of God, **let us hold fast our confession**.* — 4:14"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us **hold fast the confession of our hope** without wavering, for He who promised is faithful.* — 10:23"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hold fast, and hold it without wavering, and the reason given is not about the one holding. It is *He who promised is faithful.* See the note on Hebrews 4:14–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Faithful as Moses was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was faithful to the God who appointed Him, **just as Moses was faithful in all God's house**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is not disparaged. He is called faithful in the same clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the argument turns on a distinction: *this One has been counted worthy of more glory than Moses, inasmuch as He who built the house has more honour than the house.* Moses was faithful **in** the house, as a servant. Christ is faithful **over** it, as a Son."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who \"the house\" is.** *Whose house we are, if we hold fast the confidence and the rejoicing of the hope firm to the end.* The letter has just called them holy brothers, and now it calls them the building."}]}, "Hebrews 4:14–16": {"title": "Walk right up", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The letter has spent four chapters on rest and on the word that cuts. Then it turns to prayer, and the instruction is startlingly forward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Walk right up\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is about coming to, approaching — and the adverb attached is **parrhesia**: boldness, freedom of speech, the frankness of someone entitled to be there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not creep in. Not petition from a distance. Approach with the freedom of a citizen speaking in his own assembly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For readers who knew the tabernacle, this is a considerable sentence. The place where God sat was behind a curtain, entered once a year, by one man, with blood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it can be said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is not a high priest who cannot feel it when we are weak. He was put to the test in every way we are, and He never sinned.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things at once. He has been through it — the verb for *feel it* is about suffering along with, sympathising from the inside. And He came through clean."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both matter. A priest who had not been tested could not sympathise; one who had failed could not represent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The throne"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The throne where grace sits.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a throne — the seat of a ruler with every right to judge. And what the writer says is on it is grace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is waiting there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mercy, and grace arriving exactly when we need help.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase for well-timed help is one word, about being in season, at the right moment. Not grace stockpiled in advance. Grace that shows up on time."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse before it.** *We have a great high priest who has gone right up through the heavens.* He went up; we walk up. The whole basis of the boldness is that somebody is already inside — see the note on Hebrews 9:23–24."}]}, "Hebrews 7:8": {"title": "The one receiving it now is alive", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*Here, the men who collect the tenth are men who die. There, the one who collects it is the one the testimony says is alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and the tense in it is the thing to see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two priesthoods, two conditions"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**here** — the Levites, receiving tithes and dying, generation after generation. Aaron's robes taken off one man and put on his son, over and over.", "**there** — Melchizedek, of whom the record says only that he lives. No genealogy, no death notice, no successor."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's argument is that Christ is a priest *in the order of Melchizedek*, and that this order does not run out of men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The present tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for receiving is present. The writer does not say tithes *were* received; he says they are, and that the one receiving them is testified to be living."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a striking thing to leave standing in a letter written decades after the cross. Whatever a reader concludes about how much or how often, the picture the sentence gives is of a living Priest still receiving what is brought to Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the argument needed Abraham"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham gave the tenth to Melchizedek before Levi was born — and the writer says Levi, still in his ancestor, paid it through him. The greater blesses the lesser; the lesser pays the tenth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the tenth in scripture is first an act of honour toward a priest-king who blesses, and only later a Mosaic arrangement for feeding a tribe. The order runs blessing first, response after, from the very first time it appears."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter lands.** *He is able to save completely those who come to God through Him, because He always lives to intercede for them.* The same word — He **lives** — carries both halves: the one who receives, and the one who never stops praying for the people who come."}]}, "Hebrews 7:9–10": {"title": "Levi paid it through Abraham", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer makes an argument about a man who was not born yet, and he knows how it sounds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You could even put it this way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He flags it himself — *so to speak*, in most translations. He is aware he is pressing something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then he presses it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Levi, **who collects the tenth, paid it himself** — paid it through Abraham. **He was still inside his forefather's body** when Melchizedek came out to meet him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Levi is Abraham's great-grandson. He is four generations away and centuries from existing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer says he was there, in Abraham, and that what Abraham did that afternoon counted for him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the argument is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole of Hebrews 7 is establishing that Melchizedek's priesthood outranks Levi's, and therefore that Christ's does — *You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The logic: the lesser is blessed by the greater. Abraham gave a tenth to Melchizedek and received a blessing from him. So Melchizedek is the greater — and Levi, still in Abraham's body, was on the paying end of that transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle underneath it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man's act reached forward into descendants who did not exist."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same structure for Adam — *by one man's disobedience many were made sinners* — and for Christ: *by one Man's obedience many will be made righteous.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What one man did, a whole line was counted in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Melchizedek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*King of Salem, priest of God Most High* — Genesis 14, and he appears in three verses and vanishes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He brings out **bread and wine** to Abraham coming back from the slaughter of the kings, and blesses him. Then Abraham gives him a tenth of everything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews notes what the record leaves out: no father, no mother, no genealogy, no beginning of days, no end of life — *made like the Son of God, he remains a priest continually.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the tithe stands in the argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham gave it before the law existed. Sinai was four hundred years off; there was no commandment, no tabernacle and no Levitical system to fund."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gave it to a priest-king who came out with bread and wine, and the writer of Hebrews uses that afternoon to prove a priesthood that has no end."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Abraham refused the same day.** The king of Sodom offered him the goods and he would not take a thread or a sandal-strap — *lest you should say, \"I have made Abram rich.\"* He gave a tenth to the priest and turned down the king, on one evening."}]}, "Hebrews 8:10–12": {"title": "\"I will remember no more\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The longest Old Testament quotation in the New Testament, and the writer of Hebrews reproduces it in full because of how it ends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four promises, all first person"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every clause is God's own undertaking. Nothing in the new covenant is stated as the people's contribution:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**I will set My laws inside their thinking and carve them into their hearts**", "**I will be their God, and they will be My own people**", "**all of them will know Me already**", "**I will show mercy… and their sins I will remember no more**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old covenant was an agreement with terms on both sides. Read this list and notice there is only one signatory doing anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Inside, not on stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will set My laws inside their thinking and carve them into their hearts.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the one used of engraving. The law is not abolished in this promise — it is relocated. Out of a box, off tablets, into the person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah wrote this while watching the temple system collapse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From the lowest of them to the highest\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the arrangement needs an intermediary to tell them who God is. In a world of priests and Levites and scribes, that is a considerable claim, and it is made about *all of them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not overlooked, not filed. God undertakes to not remember — which is not forgetfulness in a being who knows everything, but a decision about what will be brought up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer of Hebrews quotes the whole passage twice, and both times he is building to that sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he concludes from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where these have been forgiven, there is no longer any offering for sin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his argument in a line. If the sins are not remembered, there is nothing left for a sacrifice to do — which is why the priest in this letter is sitting down. See the note on Hebrews 1:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word \"new.\"** *By calling this covenant new, He has made the first one obsolete.* The writer says it plainly one verse before this quotation, and it is the whole reason he is quoting Jeremiah at all."}]}, "Hebrews 9:23–24": {"title": "\"There He is now, standing before God's face on our account\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer has spent a chapter on the tabernacle, and here he says what all of it was a model of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Copies and the real thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ did not walk into a holy place that hands had built, a stand-in for the real one. He walked into heaven itself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *stand-in* is **antitypos** — a corresponding figure, a copy made to match a pattern. Moses had been shown the pattern on the mountain and told to build to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the tent in the desert was never the original. It was a scale model of something that already existed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not into the model. *Into heaven itself* — the place the tent was copied from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the argument of the whole letter compressed: everything Israel did once a year in a tent, He did once, in the real one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The present tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there He is now, standing before God's face on our account.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *went and returned*. He is there, presently, in front of God — and the phrase is *on our behalf*, in our interest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for appearing is the one used of being made visible, presented. What is presented before God is Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that does to prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gold plate on Aaron's forehead made Israel's gifts acceptable while he stood in the tent. Here the writer says the priest is inside the true holy place, permanently, and that He is there for us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is why the letter can tell its readers to walk right up to the throne. The boldness is not confidence in ourselves; it is knowledge of who is already standing there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line just after.** He did not go in *to offer Himself again and again*, as the high priest entered yearly with blood that was not his own. Once, at the end of the ages, to put away sin by the sacrifice of Himself. The visit is not repeated because it does not need to be."}]}, "Hosea 1:8–9": {"title": "Lo-Ammi", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prophet is told to name his son a sentence, and the child carries it around for the rest of his life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hosea's wife bears three, and each name is a message:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Jezreel** — God scatters", "**Lo-Ruhamah** — Not-Pitied, not shown mercy", "**Lo-Ammi** — Not-My-People"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet's household is the sermon. Every time he calls his son in for dinner he says it again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For you are **not My people**, and **I am not your God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the second clause is closer to *I am not I-AM to you* — the name from the burning bush, withdrawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it describes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the season of the exile and everything after it. No temple that mattered, no king on David's throne, no prophet after Malachi, no ark, and the visible dealings of God with that nation suspended."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book of Esther sits inside this. Ten chapters, a Persian empire, a death decree, and the name of God never once written down. That is what *Lo-Ammi* looks like from inside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not mean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not gone. Esther is the proof of it — a sleepless king, an old record read at the right hour, a gallows turned round, and a nation alive at the end of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hidden face, and not one hand withdrawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same chapter is already going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read what stands immediately before verse 9 and immediately after it. Hosea 1:10:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet the number of the children of Israel shall be as the sand of the sea, which cannot be measured or numbered; and in the place where it was said to them, \"You are not My people,\" there it shall be said to them, \"You are the sons of the living God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reversal is in the same chapter as the sentence. It is not a later correction; the promise is written on the other side of the same page."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In the very place\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not somewhere else, and not to a different people. In the place where the words *not My people* were spoken."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes exactly that line in Romans 9 and applies it — and he applies it to Gentiles being brought in. See the note on Romans 9:25–26."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Hosea is told to do in chapter 3.** Go and love her again. He buys his own wife back for fifteen shekels and a homer and a half of barley — the price of a slave. The names of the children are what the book says; the purchase is what the book is about."}]}, "Isaiah 1:18": {"title": "\"Come now, let us talk this through\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most generous sentence in the first chapter of Isaiah, and the chapter around it is one of the harshest in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A nation described as sick from head to foot, a country burned, worship God calls a burden He is weary of carrying, and hands full of blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *come now, let us talk this through.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The invitation arrives in the middle of the indictment, not after it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a legal term — to argue a case, to reason together, to settle a dispute. It is the language of a courtroom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God summons them to the argument, and then does not argue. He offers terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two colours"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though your sins are scarlet, they can turn white as snow. Though they are red as crimson, they can become soft and clean as wool.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both dyes named were the fastest, most permanent colours in the ancient world. Scarlet and crimson came from an insect and soaked into the fibre — cloth dyed that way did not fade, and could not be washed out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the point of choosing them. The stain being described is the kind that does not come out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they become"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not merely clean — **snow** and **wool**. Wool is what the cloth was before it was dyed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is not of a stain removed but of the material restored to its original state, as though it had never been in the vat."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows immediately.** *If you are willing and obedient, you will eat the good of the land.* The offer has a shape, and it belongs to the covenant Isaiah is prosecuting. What Christians have always heard in the verse is the colour chemistry: God naming the most permanent stain available and saying it can go."}]}, "Isaiah 2:2–3": {"title": "Nations streaming uphill", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah's second chapter opens with a picture that requires water to do something water does not do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Streaming upward"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the nations will **stream toward it** like rivers running home.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb **nahar** is built from the noun for a river. Rivers run down. These are running up a mountain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is deliberately impossible, and it is the first thing said about the nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The highest of the mountains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The mountain of the LORD's house will stand as the highest of the mountains, lifted above the hills.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mount Zion is not a large hill. It is overtopped by the Mount of Olives across the valley, and by most of the ranges around it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is not measuring altitude."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the inviting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Many peoples will come and say, **Come, let us go up**…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is sent to fetch them in these two verses. The invitation is on the lips of the peoples themselves, and they say it to each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they want when they get there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will teach us His ways, and we will walk in His paths.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not protection, not prosperity, not a verdict on their enemies. Teaching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second clause commits them before the first is answered — *we will walk*. They ask to be taught by somebody they have already decided to obey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the teaching goes out from Zion, the word of the LORD from Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Teaching** is **torah**, which is instruction before it is legislation — the word a parent's guidance is called in Proverbs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes next in the chapter.** Swords into ploughshares, spears into pruning hooks, and nations that *will not learn war any more*. The disarmament is placed after the teaching, not before it — in Isaiah's order, they stop fighting because they have been to the mountain."}]}, "Isaiah 38:1–3": {"title": "A word from God that did not turn out to be the last word", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prophet arrives with a message that leaves no room in it — *you are going to die, you will not recover* — and by the end of the chapter it has not happened. This is one of the most theologically awkward passages in the Old Testament, and it is worth reading rather than smoothing over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah actually said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two statements, both flat, both introduced with *this is what the LORD says*:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["put your house in order", "you are going to die, you will not recover"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no *unless* in it. Hezekiah is not offered terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hezekiah does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not accept it and he does not argue with Isaiah. He turns his face to the wall — away from the prophet, away from the room — and prays. Then he weeps bitterly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His prayer is short and it contains no request. He reminds God how he has walked, and he cries. What he asks for is never stated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before Isaiah has left the palace grounds, he is sent back with fifteen more years."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this passage is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a technique. Hezekiah does not do anything a reader could copy — there is no formula, no confession, no claim. And the same book is full of prayers that did not turn out this way; the psalms are half made of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nor does it make Isaiah a false prophet. The word was true of the situation as it stood. What changed was not God's honesty but the situation, once a dying man prayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a record that a word about the future is not always a locked door, and that God is not offended by a person who takes bad news to Him and weeps."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth holding carefully in both directions. It is real permission to pray about an outcome that has been declared. It is not a promise that everyone who prays gets fifteen years — the same chapter has Hezekiah writing afterwards about how the reprieve felt, and the rest of his story is not a simple triumph."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The part people forget.** The extra fifteen years are not presented as an unmixed good. Manasseh, the worst king in Judah's history, is born in them, and the chapter after this has Hezekiah showing the treasury to Babylonian envoys — the visit Isaiah tells him will end in exile. The Bible gives him the years and then tells the truth about what he did with them."}]}, "Isaiah 40:21–24": {"title": "The circle of the earth", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah asks four questions and then says something about the shape of the world."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He sits enthroned above **the circle of the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chug** — a circle, a sphere, a compass, the shape drawn by swinging a fixed radius."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah wrote it around 700 BC. Every culture around him thought the earth was flat, and it stayed that way in the popular mind for two thousand years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you not know? Have you not heard? Was it not told to you from the beginning? Have you not understood since the earth was founded?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks them the way you ask someone who ought to know already."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grasshoppers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And its people are **like grasshoppers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The view described is from above the circle, looking down, and from there the people are insects."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a change of altitude in the middle of a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He stretches out the heavens **like a curtain**, spreads them **like a tent** to live in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is used elsewhere in Isaiah of the heavens being stretched out, and it is the word for pitching a tent — pulling fabric taut."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The princes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He reduces princes to nothing… **No sooner are they planted, no sooner are they sown, no sooner do they take root** — than He breathes on them and they wither.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses to say how briefly. The whole career of a ruler is compressed into the time between sowing and the first root."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He *breathes* on them. It takes nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 40 opens with *comfort, comfort My people*, and this passage is inside that comfort."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point of the circle, the tent and the grasshoppers is not astronomy. It is that the people frightening you are seed that has not rooted yet, and the One above the circle knows your name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's last lines are the famous ones: *those who wait on the LORD shall renew their strength; they shall mount up with wings like eagles.* See the note on Isaiah 40:29–31."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where else the same word turns up.** Proverbs 8, the passage about Wisdom present at creation: *when He drew a **circle** on the face of the deep, I was there.* And Job says the same thing in his own words — *He drew a circle on the surface of the waters.* See the note on Job 26:5–10."}]}, "Isaiah 40:29–31": {"title": "The order the verbs come in", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*They will soar on wings like eagles. They will run and not grow weary. They will walk and not faint.* The list descends, and readers have wondered about that for centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the sequence goes down"}, {"k": "p", "t": "You would expect a crescendo: walk, then run, then fly. Isaiah puts them the other way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The likeliest reason is that it ends where life mostly is. Soaring is the picture; running is the effort; walking is the long ordinary stretch — and *not fainting* on that stretch is the hardest of the three. The promise is arranged to finish on the least dramatic thing, because that is where it is needed longest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is spoken to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even the young grow tired and weary, even strong young men stumble and fall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah names the strongest people available and says they run out. That is the setup: the promise is not that some people are naturally tireless. It is that strength comes from somewhere other than youth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for \"wait\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **qavah** carries the sense of a cord being twisted — strands drawn together under tension. From that comes waiting, expecting, hoping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not passive. It is closer to being wound tight around something. And the promise attached is not that strength returns but that it is **exchanged** — the verb means to change one thing for another, as you would change clothes."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter starts.** *Comfort, comfort my people.* This is the opening of the section of Isaiah written to exiles, and the whole chapter builds toward these three verses: God measures the oceans in His hand, weighs mountains on scales, brings princes to nothing — and then gives strength to the one with nothing left. The size of Him is the argument for the promise."}]}, "Isaiah 40:3–5": {"title": "Every valley lifted, every hill brought down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Road-building language, used of a coming that has not happened yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the image is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the ancient world, when a king travelled, work parties went ahead to make the route passable — filling in the dips, cutting down the high ground, straightening what wandered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah applies that to God arriving. The road is not being built *to* Him. It is being made ready *for* Him to come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two directions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let every valley be lifted up, every mountain and hill made low.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both movements aim at the same result — level ground — and both are stated in the passive. The valley does not raise itself. The hill does not step down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That levelling shows up all through Scripture as the way God deals with people: the low lifted, the high brought down. Mary sings it in the Magnificat; James says the humble are exalted. Here it is described as the preparation for glory being seen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is spoken to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the opening of the second half of Isaiah, addressed to a people in exile, and it begins two verses earlier with *comfort, comfort my people*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The road being described leads home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All four Gospels quote these verses of John the Baptist. Luke quotes the longest stretch, and ends where Isaiah does — *all people together will see it.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *The mouth of the LORD has spoken.* Isaiah adds it as the guarantee. The chapter goes on to say that grass withers and flowers fall, but the word of our God stands forever — and this is one of the things that word has said."}]}, "Isaiah 41:11–13": {"title": "\"You will look for your enemies and not find them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A promise about opposition disappearing, and it ends with a hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to the opposition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All who rage against you will be ashamed and disgraced. Those who oppose you will be as nothing and perish. **You will look for your enemies and not find them.***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The middle line is the striking one. Not that the enemies are defeated in front of you — that you go looking and they are not there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice the phrase *as nothing* appears. The Hebrew is a word for non-existence, nothingness. What was threatening is described as having no substance left to locate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I am the LORD your God, who takes hold of your right hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise is not grounded in Israel's strength or in the enemies' weakness. It is grounded in a grip."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is specific: the **right** hand — the working hand, the sword hand. God is described as holding the hand a fighter would need free."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says while holding it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not fear. I Myself will help you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The emphatic pronoun is in the Hebrew. Not *help will come* — I, Myself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah uses this construction repeatedly in these chapters, and each time the comfort is anchored in who is speaking rather than in the circumstances improving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it was said to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exiles, or people about to be. The chapter opens with God summoning the nations to a courtroom and asking who has directed history, and it addresses Israel as *My servant, whom I have chosen, the offspring of Abraham My friend.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the standing from which the promise is made — a relationship named before a single enemy is mentioned."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse just before this passage.** *Do not fear, for I am with you; do not be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you and help you; I will uphold you with My righteous right hand.* His right hand holding theirs. The image runs through the whole section."}]}, "Isaiah 41:8–10": {"title": "\"Abraham My friend\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four titles handed out in three lines, and then five promises in a row."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He calls him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The offspring of **Abraham My friend**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *Abraham who loved Me* or *My beloved* — the participle works both ways, and both readings have been held."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is said of nobody else. 2 Chronicles repeats it and James repeats it: *he was called the friend of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What earned it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing that Abraham did. The verse Scripture attaches to the title is the one about believing: *Abraham believed God, and it was accounted to him for righteousness — and he was called the friend of God.* James puts the two clauses in one sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had no law to keep. He lied about his wife twice, took Hagar, and laughed at the promise. And he is the friend. See the note on Genesis 15:6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He calls Israel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My **servant**… whom I have **chosen**… the **offspring** of Abraham.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chosen, and then the negative that goes with it: *I have chosen you **and not cast you away**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He found them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **took you from the ends of the earth**, I **called you from its farthest corners**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The initiative is entirely His in both verbs. Nobody in the passage came looking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five promises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **do not fear**, for I am with you. **Do not be dismayed**, for I am your God. I will **strengthen** you. I will **help** you. I will **uphold** you with My righteous right hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two commands and three undertakings, and each command has a reason attached rather than an exhortation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Do not fear — *for I am with you*. Not because the situation is manageable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The right hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **My righteous right hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the right hand of My righteousness. What holds a man up in this passage is God's own righteousness, not his."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the same thing Isaiah 54 says: *you will be established in righteousness… their righteousness is from Me.* See the note on Isaiah 54:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He calls them a few verses later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Fear not, **you worm Jacob**, you men of Israel! I will help you,\" says the LORD and your Redeemer, the Holy One of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's own title. And in the next breath: *I will make you into a new threshing sledge with sharp teeth; you shall thresh the mountains and beat them small.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A worm, told it will thresh mountains. The transformation is announced in the same sentence as the insult, and the only thing between them is *I will help you.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is arguing.** It is a courtroom scene — God challenging the idols to predict anything at all. *Show the things that are to come hereafter, that we may know that you are gods.* And in the middle of the trial He turns aside to reassure a frightened people that He has hold of their hand."}]}, "Isaiah 45:22–23": {"title": "\"By My own self I have sworn\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An invitation shouted at the edges of the world, and then an oath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turn to Me and be saved, **all the ends of the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *turn* is to look, to face toward. The older rendering is *look unto Me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same posture as the bronze serpent in the wilderness — a nation dying of snakebite, told to look at something lifted up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole instruction. Not climb, not fetch, not achieve. Turn your face this way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is addressed to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the ends of the earth.* Written to Israel, in exile, and aimed past them at everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I am God, and there is no other.* The chapter says it repeatedly — Isaiah 45 is the most concentrated statement of monotheism in the Old Testament, and it is addressed partly to Cyrus, a Persian king named a century and a half before he was born."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By My own self I have sworn.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is nobody higher to swear by. Hebrews makes the point: *because He could swear by no one greater, He swore by Himself.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was sworn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My mouth has spoken **righteousness**, a word that **will not come back empty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that it will not return — the same idea as chapter 55: *my word shall not return to Me void, but it shall accomplish what I please.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness is what the sworn word is about, and it is spoken out of His own mouth rather than required from anybody's hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before Me **every knee will bow**, by Me **every tongue will swear**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it twice — in Romans 14, of the judgement seat, and in Philippians 2, where it is the climax of the passage about Christ emptying Himself:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That at the name of Jesus every knee should bow… and every tongue should confess that Jesus Christ is Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes a verse where God swears by Himself and applies it, without comment, to Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The knee and the tongue"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the second clause is that every tongue will *swear* — will make its own declaration."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two go together in the verse: a knee bent and a mouth open. And the next verse in Isaiah is what the mouth says: *surely in the LORD I have righteousness and strength.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in myself. In Him, and said out loud. See the note on Romans 10:5–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes right after.** *In the LORD all the descendants of Israel shall be justified, and shall glory.* Justified — the same word Paul builds Romans on, in a prophet writing seven centuries earlier."}]}, "Isaiah 48:17–18": {"title": "\"Teaches you what is best for you\" — and the sigh that follows", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the second one changes the tone of the first completely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God calls Himself here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your Redeemer, the Holy One of Israel* — and then two working descriptions:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**who teaches you what is best for you** — the Hebrew is the verb for profiting or benefiting. Teaching aimed at the pupil's advantage.", "**who leads you in the way you should go**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are stated as ongoing character, not as offers. This is what He is like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the sigh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If only you had listened to My commands, your peace would have flowed like a river, your righteousness like the waves of the sea.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not a threat. It is regret — the kind of sentence a parent says looking at a situation that did not have to happen. And it is spoken to a people already in exile, already living with the consequences."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The images are worth noticing: a river, and sea waves. Both are pictures of something that keeps coming. Not a reservoir that could run out — a supply with a source behind it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read on.** The very next verses do not end in the sigh. God goes straight to bringing them out, telling them to leave Babylon with a shout, and reminding them He led people through the desert without letting them go thirsty. The regret is real and it does not get the last word."}]}, "Isaiah 49:22–26": {"title": "\"I Myself will save your children\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The passage answers a question that is put in the middle of it, and the question is a good one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection, raised inside the text"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Can plunder be taken from a warrior? Can captives be rescued from the grip of the strong?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The expected answer is no. That is the point of asking — it is the objection of someone looking at a situation and concluding that whatever has hold of it is stronger than anything likely to intervene."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah puts the doubt in the text rather than talking past it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yes, even the captives of the warrior will be taken back… I Myself will fight those who fight you, and I Myself will save your children.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The emphatic pronoun appears twice. Not *your children will be saved* — **I Myself**. The rescue is claimed personally, by name, against a stronger captor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The imagery at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will make your oppressors eat their own flesh. They will be drunk on their own blood as if it were sweet wine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is violent, and it should not be trimmed. Isaiah is writing about empires that took children into captivity, and the language of judgement in the prophets is deliberately unbearable — it is meant to sound like what it describes."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The setting.** This whole chapter is addressed to people who had concluded God had forgotten them; a few verses earlier is the famous *can a mother forget the child at her breast?* The promise about children is the answer to a complaint about abandonment, which is why it is stated in the first person twice over."}]}, "Isaiah 52:13–15": {"title": "\"Disfigured beyond that of any man\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fourth Servant Song begins here, three verses before the chapter break, and it opens with the ending."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will be raised up and lifted high and greatly exalted.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs of exaltation, stacked, **before** a word about suffering. Isaiah gives away the outcome first and then tells you what it cost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three-fold lifting language is used elsewhere in Isaiah of God Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disfigurement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His appearance was disfigured beyond that of any man, his form beyond human likeness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is stronger than most translations manage — a marring so severe that what people were looking at no longer registered as a man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah wrote this centuries before crucifixion existed as a Roman practice. What he describes is a body damaged past recognition, in front of appalled onlookers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So he will startle many nations\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb has been debated for a long time. It can mean to sprinkle — the word used of a priest sprinkling blood — or to startle, to make them recoil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parallel in the sentence favours the second: *just as many were appalled at him… so he will startle many nations.* One reaction answering another. But the sprinkling sense sits underneath, and readers have heard both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Kings with nothing to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Kings will shut their mouths because of him. For what they were never told, they will see, and what they never heard, they will understand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The silence is not respect. It is people with authority having no reply."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reason given is that something arrives outside the report — not passed down, not heard about. Seen."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it goes next.** *Who has believed our message?* — the opening line of chapter 53. The song moves straight from kings shutting their mouths to the complaint that nobody believed it. Both are true of the same figure, and Paul quotes the second line in Romans 10."}]}, "Isaiah 53:4–6": {"title": "The verbs of exchange", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six lines, and almost every one of them is a transfer: something that was ours, moved onto him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What moves, and where it goes"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**took up** our pain — the Hebrew *nasa*, to lift and carry away", "**carried** our sorrows — *sabal*, to bear a load on the back", "pierced **for** our transgressions", "crushed **for** our iniquities", "the punishment that brought **us** peace was laid **upon him**", "the LORD has laid on him the guilt of **us all**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage is described as being fixed in place. Everything is moved from one party to another. That is the grammar of substitution, and it is why this chapter is quoted more often in the New Testament than almost any other in the prophets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet we thought him stricken by God, struck down by Him, and afflicted.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the confession of the people speaking. They saw a man suffering and drew the ordinary conclusion — that God must be against him. They were exactly wrong, and the chapter is written from the far side of finding out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is worth remembering the next time suffering is read as a verdict."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"By his wounds we are healed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew *rapha* is the ordinary word for healing, used of bodies and of nations and of God healing a people's turning away. It is broad, and Isaiah does not narrow it here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What the sentence does specify is the mechanism: the healing is *by his wounds*. Whatever is being healed, it is being healed at his cost and not at ours — which is the argument of the whole chapter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *We all, like sheep, have wandered off. Each of us has turned to his own way.* Note the two halves: a herd going astray together, and every single one going a different direction. Both are true at once, and the chapter puts the collective and the individual in the same sentence before laying all of it on one man."}]}, "Isaiah 54:1–3": {"title": "\"Make the tent wider\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens by telling a barren woman to sing, and then to build for children she does not have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sing, **you who never held a child**. Break into song and shout for joy, **you who never knew the pains of birth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that world this was the worst thing that could be said about a woman, and the chapter opens by naming it twice and then commanding singing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The singing comes first. Before anything has changed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 54 follows Isaiah 53 — *wounded for our transgressions… by His stripes we are healed… He shall see His seed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The barren woman sings because of what happened in the chapter before. The Servant's suffering is what produces her children."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **make the tent wider. Stretch out the curtains. Do not hold back. Lengthen your ropes and drive your stakes down deep.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five commands, and they are all construction work on an empty tent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is told to prepare accommodation before there is anyone to accommodate — to buy the cot, in effect, while still childless."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lengthen your ropes* — the guy lines go further out, because the tent is getting bigger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Drive your stakes down deep* — and the Hebrew is *strengthen your stakes*. A wider tent takes more wind; the anchoring has to go deeper as the span increases."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both at once. Wider and better anchored, not one or the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will spread out to the right and to the left.** Your children will take over the nations and settle the emptied cities.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *spread out* is to break forth, to burst through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage tells her to produce a child, or to try. The enlargement is what she does; the children are what God does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the woman left alone will have more children than the woman with a husband, says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Further down: *you will be established in righteousness… their righteousness is from Me, says the LORD.* See the note on Isaiah 54:13–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the guarantee He gives for the whole thing is a rainbow: *as I have sworn that the waters of Noah would no longer cover the earth, so have I sworn that I would not be angry with you.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who Paul says this is about.** He quotes verse 1 in Galatians 4, of *the Jerusalem above, which is the mother of us all* — the covenant of promise rather than the covenant of law. The barren woman with more children than the married one is, in his reading, grace outproducing the law."}]}, "Isaiah 54:13–14": {"title": "\"Established in righteousness\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and everything in the second one hangs off a single condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All your children will be **taught by the LORD**, and great will be their peace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *taught of the LORD* — not taught about Him. He is the teacher."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus quotes it in John 6: *it is written in the prophets, \"And they shall all be taught by God.\" Therefore everyone who has heard and learned from the Father comes to Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *great will be their peace* — **shalom**, which is not the absence of noise. Wholeness, soundness, nothing missing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be **established in righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *established* is to be set firm, fixed, made to stand — the word used of a foundation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what a person is established in is named: righteousness. Not effort, not vigilance, not track record."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has already said whose righteousness it is — verse 17: *this is the heritage of the servants of the LORD, and **their righteousness is from Me**, says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things, all of them consequences of the establishing:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Cruelty will be far from you*", "*you will have nothing to fear*", "*Terror will be far from you, and it will not come near*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *far* is distance — put away, removed to a distance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fear is not managed here. It is relocated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 54 is the chapter immediately after Isaiah 53 — the suffering servant, wounded for our transgressions, by whose stripes we are healed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And 54 opens *Sing, O barren, you who have not borne!* The whole chapter is the result of chapter 53, and it is addressed to somebody who had nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For the mountains shall depart and the hills be removed, but **My kindness shall not depart from you**, nor shall My covenant of peace be removed,\" says the LORD, who has mercy on you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He names the guarantee: *this is like the waters of Noah to Me; for as I have sworn that the waters of Noah would no longer cover the earth, so have I sworn that I would not be angry with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He points at a rainbow and says the two promises are the same kind."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last verse.** *No weapon formed against you shall prosper, and every tongue which rises against you in judgement you shall condemn.* It is the most quoted line in the chapter, and the sentence it ends on is *their righteousness is from Me.* The verse is anchored to whose righteousness it is."}]}, "Isaiah 54:9–10": {"title": "The oath God swears about His own anger", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God does not often swear oaths. When He does, it is worth stopping to see what He binds Himself to — and here He binds Himself twice, on the same terms, about two different things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison is doing the work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is like the days of Noah to Me. I swore then that the waters would never again cover the earth. So now I swear that I will not be angry with you or rebuke you again.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument runs through the flood. After it, God gave a sign in the sky and a promise that took the form of a permanent renunciation: never again by water. Everyone reading Isaiah knew that promise had held for centuries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the second oath is measured against the first. *That* is how settled this is. Whatever you believe about the reliability of the rainbow, believe this on the same footing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being sworn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that nothing hard will happen. The oath is specifically about **God's disposition**: I will not be angry with you or rebuke you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a strange thing for God to promise a people whose failures the same book catalogues at length. Isaiah is not saying they became blameless. He is saying something changed on God's side about how He will deal with them — and the chapter before this one is the reason, which is why 53 and 54 sit next to each other."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mountains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though the mountains walk away and the hills come loose and fall, My love for you will never walk away, and My covenant of peace will never come loose.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is deliberately absurd. Mountains are the standing metaphor for permanence in Hebrew poetry — the oldest, heaviest, least movable things anyone could point at."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah says: suppose those went. Suppose the most fixed thing you can see got up and left. Even then. He picks the strongest available comparison and then says this outlasts it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is spoken to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens by addressing a barren woman, a widow, someone abandoned and ashamed. That is the audience being sworn to. Not a people at their best — a people at their least deserving of a promise about God's permanent good will."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"covenant of peace.\"** *Shalom* is wider than the absence of conflict; it is wholeness, everything in its right place. The word God attaches to His oath is not \"truce.\" It is a settled state of things that He is committing Himself to maintain."}]}, "Isaiah 55:10–11": {"title": "\"It will not come back to Me empty\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A comparison between rain and speech, and the comparison does more work than it first appears."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What rain does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rain and snow come down from the sky and do not return there until they have soaked the earth, making it bud and bloom, giving seed to the sower and bread to the eater.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah traces the whole cycle: down, into the ground, up as a plant, out as seed and as bread. Nothing about it is instant, and nothing about it is wasted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rain does not look like bread on the day it falls."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So it is with the word that goes out from My mouth. It will not come back to Me empty. It will do what I intend and finish the work I sent it to do.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two claims: it will not return empty, and it will complete its errand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *empty* means without result, having achieved nothing — the same word used of coming back from somewhere with nothing in your hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The part the image supplies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rain that soaks into ground and disappears has not failed. It is at the beginning of its work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the comparison is for. A word that has been spoken and appears to have vanished is described here as being in the middle of doing something, on a timescale the passage has just spelled out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before this are the famous ones — *my thoughts are not your thoughts, neither are your ways my ways.* And the invitation at the top of the chapter: *come, all who are thirsty… without money and without cost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the passage about a word that finishes what it was sent for sits inside an offer of something free."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The chapter's last image.** *You will go out in joy and be led forth in peace; the mountains and hills will burst into song, and all the trees of the field will clap their hands.* Isaiah keeps the agriculture running to the end — what the rain produces, in his telling, is a harvest that celebrates."}]}, "Isaiah 55:12–13": {"title": "\"Where the thornbush grew, the cypress will rise\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last two verses of the chapter, and they are the harvest of everything in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two exchanges"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where the thornbush grew, the cypress will rise. Where the briar grew, the myrtle will stand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thorns and briars are the first curse. When the ground was cursed in Genesis 3, what it produced was *thorns and thistles* — and they have stood for that ruin ever since."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cypress and myrtle are the opposite kind of plant: tall evergreen timber, and a fragrant shrub used at festivals. Neither is a crop. Both are things you would plant because they last and because they are good to be near."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exchange is not thorn-for-wheat. It is thorn-for-something-permanent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they grow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where the thornbush grew.* The same ground. Nothing is relocated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the specific promise: the site of the curse becomes the site of the cypress."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The going out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will go out in joy and be led home in peace. The mountains and the hills will burst into song before you, and all the trees of the field will clap their hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written to exiles, so *go out* means leaving Babylon. The landscape is described as celebrating the road home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This will be a name for the LORD, a sign that lasts forever and is never cut off.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trees are a monument. What the exchange is *for* is a name — a permanent, visible marker of who did it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *cut off* is the word used of felling a tree, which is a deliberate touch in a sentence about trees."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the crown of thorns fits.** The Gospels record it without comment. Readers have long noticed that the first curse on the ground ends up pressed onto a head — and Isaiah, four chapters after his Servant is pierced, ends with thorn ground growing cypress."}]}, "Isaiah 55:8–9": {"title": "\"Higher than your ways\" — what the comparison is about", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The verse gets quoted at funerals and after disappointments, as a way of saying God's reasons are beyond us. Read in its own paragraph it is doing something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let the wicked forsake his way, and the unrighteous man his thoughts; let him return to the LORD, and He will have mercy on him, and to our God, for **He will abundantly pardon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *for My thoughts are not your thoughts, and your ways are not My ways.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The *for* connects them. The subject of the comparison is the pardoning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Human beings forgive on a scale — a bit, grudgingly, up to a point, with conditions. God's forgiving is described as operating at a different altitude altogether."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *abundantly pardon* is emphatic: He will multiply to pardon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sentence is not primarily about God's mysterious purposes in suffering. It is about how far past our instincts His mercy runs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As high as the heavens are above the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The largest distance available to an ancient writer, and Isaiah uses it twice elsewhere for God's steadfast love and for the removal of sins — *as far as the east is from the west.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this reading matters pastorally"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quoted as a general statement about inscrutability, the verse can be used to shut down a grieving person's questions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read as it stands, it says something warmer: whatever you would do with someone who had wronged you, His response is higher than that — and the gap is the size of the sky."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter opens.** *Come, all you who are thirsty… come, buy and eat, without money and without cost.* The whole chapter is an invitation to receive something free, and the verse about higher ways sits in the middle of it. See also the note on Isaiah 55:10–11."}]}, "Isaiah 58:8–9a": {"title": "\"Your healing will come quickly\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four promises in a row, and the chapter is careful about what they are attached to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Do this\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promises begin with a condition, and the chapter has just spelled it out at length. Israel had been fasting and complaining that God took no notice. His answer describes the fast He actually wants:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "loose the chains of injustice, share your bread with the hungry, bring the homeless poor into your house, clothe the naked, do not hide from your own flesh and blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do this*, and then these four."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**your light will break forth like the dawn** — sudden, and unstoppable once begun", "**your healing will come quickly** — the Hebrew word is about new flesh sprouting over a wound; it is a recovery word, used of a nation's restoration and of a body's", "**your goodness will walk ahead of you**", "**the glory of the LORD will be your rear guard**"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The formation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last two are military. Something goes in front and something covers the back — the same picture as the exodus, where the pillar of cloud moved from before Israel to behind them, between the camp and Egypt's army."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A person in that formation is covered at both ends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then you will call, and the LORD will answer. You will cry out, and He will say, \"Here I am.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last phrase is the same one Abraham and Moses and Samuel say to God — *hinneni*, here I am. Here it comes back the other way, from God to the person calling."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the condition matters.** The chapter is not describing a technique for getting light and healing. It is God telling a religious people that the observance they were proud of had left the hungry hungry — and that the breakthrough they wanted was on the other side of feeding them."}]}, "Isaiah 60:1–3": {"title": "Arise and shine — with a light that is not yours", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two imperatives, and then the reason for both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Arise. Shine. **Your light has come**, and the glory of the LORD rises upon you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commands come first and the reason second, and the reason is not an instruction to generate anything. The light *has come*. The glory *rises upon* you — the verb is the one used of the sun coming up over a horizon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is being told to produce light. They are being told to get up, because something has risen on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The darkness is not denied"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look, darkness covers the earth, thick darkness over the peoples.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second word is heavier than the first — a deep, enveloping dark. Isaiah puts it right between the command to shine and the promise of nations coming, and does not soften it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is the point: *but the LORD rises upon you*. Both things are true at once, and the light is not described as ending the darkness elsewhere. It is described as landing here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who ends up looking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nations will come to your light, and kings to the brightness of your dawn.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The light attracts. That is the whole mechanism given — no campaign, no persuasion. Something is lit in a dark landscape, and people walk toward it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it lands in the New Testament.** Jesus tells a hillside crowd *you are the light of the world* and *a town built on a hill cannot be hidden* — the same picture of light in a dark place drawing eyes to it. Both passages put the attention that follows on someone else: nations coming to *your* light, and people giving glory to *your Father*. See the note on Matthew 5:13–16."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What arrives once the light is up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter does not stop at the dawn. The next two verses tell the city to look around at who is walking toward it — sons from far away, daughters carried close and safe, and then the wealth of the sea and the riches of nations streaming in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is worth holding on to. The light comes first and it is not the city's own; then people come to it; and only then does anything arrive that could be counted. Isaiah never presents the traffic as the point — it is the consequence of something having risen. See the note on Isaiah 60:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the darkness is not said to lift everywhere. *Darkness covers the earth, thick darkness over the peoples* stays true in the same breath as *the LORD rises upon you*. Whatever this chapter is promising, it is not a world with the lights back on. It is one lit place in a dark landscape, and a road toward it."}]}, "Isaiah 60:4–5": {"title": "\"Your heart will race and swell\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The light has risen in the verses before this. Now Isaiah tells the city to look up and see who is walking toward it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your sons come from far away, and your daughters are carried close and safe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before any mention of wealth, the returning crowd is family. Isaiah is writing to a people who had watched their children marched off into exile, and the first thing he describes coming back is them — the daughters carried, which is the detail a parent notices."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the wealth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wealth of the sea will be brought to you, and the riches of the nations will come streaming in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for wealth is a broad word — strength, force, resources, army. It can mean a nation's substance in every sense. And *the sea* means overseas trade, which for Judah meant the whole Mediterranean world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is of a city that had nothing becoming the destination everything arrives at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will see it, and your face will glow. Your heart will race and swell with joy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is physical: the heart *trembles and grows wide*. It is the body's response to news too large to take in — the same verb used elsewhere of fear, here turned to joy."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On reading this as a promise of transfer.** The passage has a long history of being applied to personal finances. What it actually describes is a restored city at the centre of a world that had scattered it, and Revelation picks it up that way — the kings of the earth bringing their glory into the new Jerusalem, with the gates never shut. It is worth reading first as the thing it says before it is applied to anything smaller."}]}, "Isaiah 7:14–16": {"title": "\"The virgin will conceive\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A sign given to a frightened king, and a name that carries more than the moment it was spoken into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahaz, king of Judah, is facing two enemy kings and is about to buy Assyrian protection instead of trusting God. Isaiah offers him a sign — *ask for anything, as deep as the grave or as high as heaven* — and Ahaz refuses, in a show of false piety."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sign is given anyway, over his refusal. *Therefore the Lord Himself will give you a sign.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **almah** — a young woman of marriageable age. It is used a handful of times in the Old Testament and never of a married woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two centuries before Christ, Jewish scholars translating into Greek chose **parthenos**, which means virgin specifically. That is the reading Matthew quotes, and it was made by translators with no Christian argument to advance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She will call his name Immanuel* — with us, God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew quotes the verse and then translates the name for his readers, which is how he tells you what he thinks it means."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the Gospel closes on the same note: *I am with you always, to the very end of the age.* The name at the beginning and the promise at the end are the same claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The near horizon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before the boy knows enough to reject the wrong and choose the right, the land whose two kings you dread will be left empty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sign had an immediate function — a timescale for Ahaz, measured in a child's early years. Prophecy in Isaiah often works on two horizons at once, and the near one does not cancel the far one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Ahaz did with it.** He went to Assyria anyway, sent the temple silver as tribute, and got the protection he wanted along with everything that came after. The sign of Immanuel was given to a man who had already decided not to need it."}]}, "James 1:17": {"title": "\"He never swings around to throw a shadow\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James reaches for astronomy to say something about God's character, and the metaphor is doing precise work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim about gifts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anything good that ever came into your hands, anything that reached you whole and unspoiled, came down from up there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in the Greek — every good *giving* and every perfect *gift*. The act and the thing given. Both are traced to one source."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The direction is stated: **down from above**, from the Father of lights."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why lights"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the One who made every light burning in the sky.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heavenly bodies were the ancient world's picture of reliability — and also of variation. The sun moves through the year, the moon waxes and wanes, constellations rise and set. Everything in the sky changes by turning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James names God as the maker of all of them and then says the thing that separates Him from what He made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He never dims, never shifts, never swings around to throw a shadow He was not throwing before.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**no variation** — the astronomical word for change or alteration", "**no shadow cast by turning** — the word is *trope*, the root of *tropic*, used of the solstice, the point where the sun turns back"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything that gives light in the sky produces shadow as it moves. God does not turn, so there is no side of Him that is dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why James says it here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at the verse before: *do not be deceived, my beloved brothers.* He has just been saying that God tempts nobody, and that people are drawn away by their own desire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the correction to a suspicion — that God might be behind the trouble, or might have a colder side that shows sometimes. James says the light is unvarying, and everything good that ever reached you came from it."}]}, "James 1:21": {"title": "What \"saved\" actually covers", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Our text says the message has power to save your souls. Both of those words — saved and souls — are wider in Greek than they have become in English."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same word, in this same letter, means healed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is sozo. James uses it again at the end of his letter: the prayer offered in faith will sozo the sick man. Same verb. There it plainly means made well — a man getting up off his bed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So inside one short letter James uses this one word for the rescue of a person's whole life, and for a sick man being restored. That is not a stretch or a clever reading. It is simply how he uses it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "It is a rescue word, not a religious word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sozo means to bring someone safely through. It is used of a drowning man being pulled out, of a ship that survives a storm, of someone brought out of danger in one piece. Whatever the threat is, sozo is coming through it intact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "English saved has narrowed to mean one thing: not going to hell. The Greek word never narrowed like that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And \"souls\" is wider too"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is psyche. In English, soul makes us think only of the invisible part that outlives the body. In Greek it means the life, the self, the whole person — you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "When Jesus asked what it profits a man to gain the whole world and lose his psyche, He was not talking about one compartment of a person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So save your souls is not keep the spiritual part of you out of hell. It is nearer to: rescue the whole of you, and bring you through in one piece."}, {"k": "note", "t": "Do not overreach with this. Sozo genuinely does include eternal salvation — that is not being denied here, and this is not a case of everyone having had the word wrong. The claim that holds is that saved is WIDER than we usually hear it, not that it means something other than we thought. And the width of a word does not by itself promise a particular outcome in a particular case. What it tells you is the size of what God is dealing with: not a compartment of you, but you."}]}, "James 1:4": {"title": "\"No piece of you left unfinished\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and the two words at the end of it are not about moral perfection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will come out grown up and whole, with no piece of you left unfinished.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**teleios** — brought to its end, complete, mature. Used of a full-grown adult as against a child, and of a thing that has reached what it was for.", "**holokleros** — literally *whole-lot*, having all its parts. It was used of a body with nothing missing, and of a stone fit for building because it had no defect."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither word means sinless. Both mean **nothing lacking**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James then says it a third way in case it was missed: *lacking in nothing*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What produces it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So let that endurance run all the way to the finish.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before names the mechanism — testing produces endurance — and this one says: now let the endurance finish its work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is not to generate anything. It is to let something already underway complete rather than cutting it short."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a person who has suffered and become tougher. A person with no piece missing — the parts that only get built under pressure now present."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why James opens the letter by telling scattered, harassed believers to count trials as joy. Not because the trial is good, but because of what it is in the middle of making."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The next verse.** *If any of you is short on wisdom, ask God.* It follows directly, and the connection is easy to miss: a person in the middle of an unfinished process, told to let it run, is exactly a person who will not understand what is happening. So the immediate provision is wisdom, given generously and without being made to feel small for asking. See the note on James 1:5."}]}, "James 1:5": {"title": "The two words that take the sting out of asking", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One short verse, and two Greek words in it are doing something the English has to work hard to carry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"With both hands\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **haplos**. It means singly, simply, without folds — and by extension, without a hidden second motive. It is the opposite of giving with one hand while keeping an account with the other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the promise is not merely that God gives generously in quantity. It is that He gives *straightforwardly*: no strings running back, no ledger being kept, no part of Him withholding while another part hands something over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He never once makes you feel small for asking\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second phrase is **me oneidizontos** — not reproaching. *Oneidizo* is the word for upbraiding somebody, throwing something in their face, shaming them with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James puts it in the sentence because he knows exactly what stops people asking. It is not doubt that God can. It is the suspicion that the asking will be remembered — that the answer will arrive with a comment attached about why you needed it, or how often you have needed it lately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He rules that out in four words."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits in his letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes immediately after James has told them to count it joy when they fall into various trials, and that endurance has a work to finish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the wisdom on offer is not academic. It is for people in the middle of something they do not understand, who need to know what to do next. That is when a person is least confident of a welcome, and it is precisely there that James puts the promise about not being shamed for asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no qualification about the asker's state. Not *if any of you is worthy*, not *if you have been faithful*. The condition is **short on wisdom** — the lack itself is the qualification."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the outcome is stated with equal plainness: *it will be put right into your hands*. Not considered. Given."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the verses that follow.** James goes on to talk about asking in faith and not being double-minded, and that is sometimes used to load the promise back up with conditions. Read what he actually describes: a person in two minds about whether to ask God at all, pulled one way and then the other. He is not setting a standard of confidence to reach. He is saying: decide who you are asking, and then ask him."}]}, "James 2:17": {"title": "Does James contradict Paul?", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the verse people set against Paul. Paul says a person is put right with God through faith and not by works. James says faith without works is dead. Here are seven reasons they are not fighting each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "1. They are answering two different questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is answering: how does a guilty person get right with God? James is answering: how can you tell a living faith from a dead one? Same words, different question. Neither of them has to lose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "2. They are arguing with two different people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's opponent thinks doing good earns you standing with God. James's opponent thinks saying you believe is enough and nothing needs to follow. Neither man is attacking what the other one taught."}, {"k": "h", "t": "3. James tells you exactly which faith he is rejecting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verses back, the man SAYS he has faith. It is a claim, not a life. And two verses on, the belief being tested is simply believing God exists — which, as James points out, the demons manage too, and it makes them shake."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not attacking trust in God. He is attacking a counterfeit that he carefully describes first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "4. The little word \"that\" — the strongest point of all"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Greek there is a small word, the, which James attaches to faith every single time he points at the counterfeit: 2:14, this verse, 2:20, 2:22 and 2:26. Your text shows it — that faith, this faith. He means the one he has just described."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he drops that little word in exactly one place: 2:24, where he stops describing the fake and states the general rule."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So every time he means the counterfeit, the word is there. The one time he states the principle, it is gone. That is grammar, not opinion — it sits in the text whether anyone likes it or not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "5. Abraham's dates settle the order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham believed God and was counted righteous in Genesis 15. He put Isaac on the altar in Genesis 22 — chapters later, years later. James quotes the earlier moment and says the altar filled it full. The standing came first; the deed filled it out. There is more on this at 2:21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "6. Chapter 1 had already settled it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before this argument even begins James has said four things in order: God acted first and nobody talked Him into it (1:18); the message is already planted in you, and it is the message that saves you (1:21); now carry it out (1:22); and you are blessed IN the doing, not paid for it (1:25)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gift comes before the work — twenty verses before anyone mentions works."}, {"k": "h", "t": "7. The whole passage is about showing, not paying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Show me. I will show you. Do you need showing. Look at what happened. You see it now. Over and over. James is asking for evidence that the faith is alive, not for payment to make it count."}, {"k": "note", "t": "One thing not to claim: do not say Paul and James use different words. They use the same ones — faith, works, justified, righteousness, the same Abraham, and the same Old Testament verse about him. That is precisely why this passage has been argued over for centuries. The claim that holds up is not that they used different words, but that they used the same words to answer different questions."}]}, "James 2:21": {"title": "Shown to be right, or made righteous?", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This verse has been argued over for five hundred years. Here is why our text reads shown to be right rather than justified or made righteous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word itself means both"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word is dikaioo. It carries two senses, and both are ordinary:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["made right — a verdict, the way a judge declares it", "shown to be right — proof of something that was already true"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither side can win this by pointing at the word. It will not choose between them. So the question has to be settled from the passage, not the dictionary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "James answers it with dates"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things happened in Abraham's life, a long way apart. In Genesis 15 he believed God, and God counted him righteous. In Genesis 22 he put Isaac on the altar. Those are chapters apart, and years apart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now the key move: in the next verse but one, James quotes the earlier moment — the believing — and says the altar filled it full."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So James himself puts the righteousness first and the altar second. If Abraham had been made righteous at the altar, the verse James quotes would be describing something that had not happened yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "A picture for it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man passes his exams and is licensed as a doctor. Years later he saves a life in an emergency. Did the emergency make him a doctor? No. He already was one. The emergency showed it, in public, where everyone could see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Abraham. Righteous by believing, back in Genesis 15. The altar showed it, in Genesis 22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three reasons this rendering stands"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["Shown to be right is a real, ordinary meaning of the word — nothing invented.", "James never says in front of God or in front of people. Those words are simply not in the chapter. Paul does add that kind of phrase when he writes about Abraham; James does not, and our text stays as quiet as he does.", "The whole passage runs on showing: show me, I will show you, do you need showing, look at what happened, you see it now."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "What should not be claimed"}, {"k": "note", "t": "It would be too strong to say the Greek means shown and not made. The word genuinely carries both, and that claim can be knocked down in a moment — taking the good arguments with it. The honest statement is this: the word can mean either; we read it as shown because James's argument runs on showing, because he names no courtroom, and above all because he dates the righteousness to Genesis 15 and calls the altar its filling-full. That is a reading of the passage, not a fact about the word."}]}, "James 2:25": {"title": "Why James picks Rahab second", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James has just used Abraham. Then he reaches for the least likely person in the Old Testament to put beside him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pairing is deliberate"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Abraham** — the father of the nation, the man the whole argument about faith is usually conducted through", "**Rahab** — a Canaanite, a prostitute, an outsider with no covenant standing at all"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "If his point were that respectable people prove their faith by respectable behaviour, the second example destroys it. He chooses the two most distant points on the map and says the same thing happened in both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What her \"work\" was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She took the messengers in and sent them out another road.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the entire deed. She hid two men and pointed them at the hills. It is not charitable giving or moral improvement — it is one decision, made at real risk, that showed which side she had joined."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Shown to be right\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the same one under discussion throughout the chapter, and its range matters: it can mean *made* right, and it can mean *shown* to be right. Read the question James is asking — how can you tell a living faith from a dead one — and the second sense is the one carrying the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody made Rahab righteous by hiding spies. What she did made visible where she already stood."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**See also.** The note on James 2:17 works through why this is not a contradiction of Paul, and the note on Hebrews 11:30–31 shows the same episode being used as an example of *faith* by a different writer. Two arguments, one woman, one night."}]}, "James 3:17–18": {"title": "How to tell which wisdom you are hearing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James has just described a wisdom that is earthly and produces disorder. Here is the other kind, and he gives it a checklist."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wisdom that comes down from above is **clean, first of all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *first* deliberately — this one leads. The word is about being pure, unmixed, without a hidden agenda."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else on the list is relational, and the purity has to come first because the rest can be counterfeited by someone who simply wants to be liked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the list"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**it makes peace**", "**it goes easy on people** — gentle, considerate, the word for not insisting on the letter of your rights", "**it will hear you out and give ground** — willing to be persuaded, open to reason", "**packed with mercy and with good things that actually grow**", "**it does not play favourites and it never puts on a face**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read it as a test and it becomes very practical. Not *is this clever*, but: does it make peace, does it go easy, will it listen, does it produce anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wisdom that is technically correct and leaves wreckage behind it fails the test James sets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The harvest line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People who make peace plant their seed in peace, and what comes up out of that ground is a life put right.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is agricultural and the sequence matters — the peace is both the soil and the seed. Righteousness is the crop, not the method."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he is contrasting it with.** The verses just above describe wisdom that is earthly, unspiritual, and demonic, marked by bitter envy and selfish ambition — and James says where it shows: *disorder and every evil practice.* Both wisdoms are recognised by what grows out of them, which is the only test he offers for either."}]}, "James 3:6–8": {"title": "\"Nobody has ever broken in the tongue\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James has just used two pictures — a bit in a horse's mouth, a rudder on a ship — about small things steering large ones. Then he stops being gentle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the tongue is that fire… touching off the whole running wheel of your life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase behind *running wheel* is odd in Greek and has been discussed for centuries — something like the wheel of existence, the whole course of a life turning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is of a spark at the axle. Not damage in one place; damage that travels with the wheel wherever it goes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim about taming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People have broken in every sort of creature there is… but nobody has ever broken in the tongue.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James surveys the whole animal kingdom — the four categories from Genesis — and says humanity has managed all of them and not this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It will not be held still.* That is a statement about human capacity, and it is absolute. Nobody. Ever."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he says something so bleak"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because the chapter opens by warning that not many should become teachers, since they will be judged more strictly. James is writing to people who talk for a living, and he is refusing to pretend it can be mastered by effort."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The honest reading is that this is a diagnosis with no cure offered in the paragraph — which is deliberate. If nobody can tame it, self-improvement is not the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the letter goes for the answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wisdom that comes down from above is clean, first of all. Then: it makes peace, it goes easy on people, it will hear you out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight verses later, and the solution is something that comes **down from above** rather than something worked up. See the note on James 3:17–18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul says about the same problem.** *If by the Spirit you go on putting the body's doings to death, you will live* — continuous, and by the Spirit, not by resolve. The two writers agree about the diagnosis and about where the remedy comes from. See the note on Romans 8:12–14."}]}, "James 5:1–3": {"title": "\"The last days are what you piled it up for\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The bluntest paragraph in the New Testament, and it is addressed to a specific group."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being spoken to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now you. **The rich.***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the church. James is addressing a class of people from outside it — the next verse names what they did: *you kept back by fraud the wages of the labourers who mowed your fields.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Unpaid wages. That is the charge, and it is the same charge Egypt had four hundred years of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three corrosions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your **wealth has gone rotten**. Your good clothes are **full of moth holes**. Your gold and your silver have **gone green with corrosion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three kinds of stored wealth in that world — grain, garments, and metal — and each is described decaying in its own way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gold does not corrode. James says it anyway; the point is not metallurgy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The corrosion as a witness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That corrosion is going to **stand up and testify against you**, and eat its way through your flesh like fire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rust becomes a witness in court. The evidence against them is produced by the hoard itself, sitting in the dark, doing what unused things do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You piled the stuff up — and **the last days are what you piled it up for**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *you have heaped up treasure in the last days*, and it carries both senses: they did the heaping in the last days, and the last days are what the heap is for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pile has a destination that is not theirs. Ecclesiastes said the same: *to the one who misses the mark, He gives the task of gathering and piling up, only to hand it over to the one who pleases God.* See the note on Ecclesiastes 2:24–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What James tells the believers to do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing aggressive. Four verses later:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be patient, brethren, until the coming of the Lord. See how the farmer waits for the precious fruit of the earth, **waiting patiently for it until it receives the early and latter rain**. You also be patient. **Establish your hearts**, for the coming of the Lord is at hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A farmer, two rains, and a heart established. No scheming, no grasping, no hurry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs: *the stingy man hurries after wealth and never knows that want is coming for him.* See the note on Proverbs 28:21–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wages that cried out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The cries of the reapers have reached the ears of the Lord of Sabaoth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sabaoth** — the LORD of armies. James reaches for the military title in a paragraph about farm wages."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same thing happened in Egypt: *their cry came up to God because of the bondage, and God heard their groaning.* And on the way out, the back pay was settled. See the note on Exodus 12:33–36."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who James was.** The brother of Jesus, and the leader of the Jerusalem church — writing to Jewish believers scattered by persecution, most of them poor. He does not tell them to organise. He tells them to wait for the rain."}]}, "James 5:10–11": {"title": "\"What the Lord finally made of it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only mention of Job in the New Testament, and it tells you what the whole book was for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who James points at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Think about **the prophets**… take them as your pattern: what they had to take, and how long they took it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not offer them as examples of success. He offers what they had to take, and the length of time they took it for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The ones who hold out\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupomeno** — to remain under. Not to grit the teeth; to stay put under a weight rather than get out from under it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ones who hold out under it are the ones we call fortunate.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict on Job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have heard how Job held out, and you have seen **what the Lord finally made of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James assumes his readers know the story. What he tells them is where to look in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not at the boils. Not at the ashes, or the arguments, or the thirty-five chapters of speeches. At **the end** — the Greek is *the end of the Lord*, the outcome the Lord brought it to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the outcome shows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*— that **He feels for people deeply, and He is kind all the way through**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in the Greek, and the first is one James appears to have coined: **polusplagchnos**, literally *many-boweled*, from the word for the inward parts, where compassion was felt. Very compassionate. It occurs nowhere else in Greek literature before him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is **oiktirmon** — merciful, full of pity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the New Testament's one-sentence summary of the book of Job, and it is not about patience, endurance, or the mystery of suffering. It is a statement about what God is like, drawn from how the story ended."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What James is telling his readers to conclude"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If you want to know what the Lord is, read to the end of that book. He is deeply compassionate and kind all the way through — and the whole forty-two chapters are offered as evidence for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the middle of it looked like, the end of it is what tells you His character."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The famous phrase.** *The patience of Job* comes from the older English of this verse. The Greek word is not patience but staying under — and James's point is not really about Job at all. It is about what the Lord was, the whole time, and what He turned out to be at the end."}]}, "James 5:16–18": {"title": "\"Made of exactly the same stuff we are\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James picks the most spectacular prayer in Israel's history and then goes out of his way to say the man who prayed it was nobody special."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elijah was made of exactly the same stuff we are.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is one word — **homoiopathes**, of like feelings, subject to the same things. Paul and Barnabas use it at Lystra when a crowd tries to sacrifice to them: *we are men of the same nature as you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James is heading off the obvious objection before anyone makes it. Yes, but he was Elijah. No — he was not a different kind of creature."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the man James chose is one whose recorded low point is asking God to let him die under a broom tree."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He prayed hard that the rain would stop… then he prayed again, and the sky let the rain down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *prayed hard* is a Hebrew-flavoured construction: he prayed with prayer. Not a technique — earnestness, the same thing James means when he says a righteous man's prayer works *when he means it all the way down*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What makes a prayer effective"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When a man who stands right with God prays… it puts real power to work.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *stands right* is the righteousness word. In the New Testament that standing is a gift, received rather than achieved — which is what makes this verse usable by ordinary people rather than only by the very good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James has just spent a letter on practical faith, and he lands here: confession to one another, prayer for one another, healing, and an ordinary man's prayer shutting the sky."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the detail comes from.** The three and a half years is not stated in 1 Kings — James supplies it, and Jesus gives the same figure in Luke 4. The length of that drought was evidently well known, and both of them count it the same way."}]}, "Jeremiah 16:5–7": {"title": "A prophet told not to mourn", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah is forbidden three ordinary human things, and each prohibition is a sermon he has to live rather than preach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not enter a house of mourning.** Do not go to grieve or to comfort them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that culture, staying away from a funeral was unthinkable. Mourning with the bereaved was a duty everybody owed everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told not to go, and his absence at every graveside in Jerusalem is the message."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter forbids him three things in a row: do not marry or have children here, do not go to a funeral, do not go to a feast."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No wife, no mourning, no celebration. He is cut off from the beginning, the end and the middle of ordinary life, and the isolation is deliberate — it is what the nation is about to experience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being withdrawn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have taken My peace away from this people**, says the LORD, My steadfast love, My mercy.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three nouns: *shalom*, **chesed** — the covenant love — and mercy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same season Hosea named *Lo-Ammi*, not My people. See the note on Hosea 1:8–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cup of consolation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"No one will **lift the cup of consolation** for a father or a mother.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was a customary cup handed to mourners after a burial. The chapter names it and says it will not be offered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody will break bread with the grieving. Nobody will cut himself or shave his head. Every rite that made a death bearable is stripped out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the other side of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Keep reading. Nine verses later:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Behold, the days are coming, says the LORD, that it shall no more be said, 'The LORD lives who brought up the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt,' but 'The LORD lives **who brought up the children of Israel from the land of the north and from all the lands where He had driven them**.' For I will bring them back into their land which I gave to their fathers.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the return will be a bigger event than the exodus — so much bigger that the nation will change the way it swears its oaths."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That was written around 600 BC, to people about to be deported, and the scattering that eventually followed lasted nineteen centuries."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for the sending.** *I will send for many fishermen, and they shall fish them… and afterward I will send for many hunters, and they shall hunt them from every mountain and every hill and out of the holes of the rocks.* Fishermen first, then hunters. Jeremiah says the gathering will reach the ones who do not come when they are called."}]}, "Jeremiah 22:28–30": {"title": "The curse on Jeconiah, and the line that had to get past it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the strangest problems in the Bible is set up here, and it is not solved until Matthew's first chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Write this man down as childless, a man who will not prosper in his days. For none of his offspring will prosper, none will sit on the throne of David or rule again in Judah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Coniah — also called Jeconiah and Jehoiachin — was king of Judah for three months before Babylon carried him off. The curse is not that he has no children; he had seven. It is that **none of his descendants will ever sit on David's throne.**"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that is a problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God had also sworn to David that his throne would be established forever, and that the Messiah would come from his line."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the promise runs through David, and the royal line runs through Jeconiah, and the royal line has just been declared permanently disqualified. Both statements are in the same Bible and neither is withdrawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How Matthew resolves it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's genealogy runs straight through Jeconiah — he names him — and traces the line down to *Joseph, the husband of Mary*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the wording changes. Every other link in that chapter says *was the father of*. This one does not:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*Jacob was the father of Joseph, the husband of Mary, and it was **from Mary** that Jesus was born.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph gives Jesus the legal claim to David's throne, as his adoptive father — which He needs. But the blood does not come through Joseph, so the curse on Jeconiah's descendants does not touch Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Luke's genealogy takes a different route entirely, through David's son Nathan rather than Solomon, bypassing the cursed line altogether."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What that means about the virgin birth.** It is usually argued from prophecy and from purity. This is a third reason, and it is legal: a Messiah descended physically from Jeconiah could not have taken the throne. The line had to arrive by another road, and Matthew's careful change of wording is where he shows you it did."}]}, "Jeremiah 29:10–14": {"title": "\"Plans to give you a future and a hope\" — and the seventy years in front of it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted verse in Jeremiah, and it begins with a number that never gets quoted with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence it belongs to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When seventy years are completed for Babylon, I will come to you and make good on My gracious promise to bring you back to this place.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy years. Most of the people reading the letter would die in Babylon. The promise is real, and its fulfilment is beyond the lifetime of nearly everyone being addressed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the setting for *I know the plans I have for you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who received it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exiles — and Jeremiah's letter tells them what to do in the meantime: build houses, plant gardens, marry, have children, and *seek the peace of the city where I have sent you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not endure it. Settle in it, and pray for the place holding you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the promise says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Plans for peace and not for harm, to give you a future and a hope.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *future* is literally an **end**, an outcome — the far side of something. Paired with hope, it means: there is a later, and it is good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Spoken to people whose visible situation was going to last seventy years, that is not a small thing. It is precisely the promise a person needs when the near view holds nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it matters that we read it whole"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quoted alone it can sound like a guarantee that plans will go well soon. In place it is stronger and harder: God's intentions are good, the timescale may be longer than your life, and you are told to plant gardens while you wait."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows.** *You will call on Me and come and pray to Me, and I will listen. You will seek Me and find Me, when you seek Me with all your heart.* The promise about the future is immediately followed by an invitation to talk to Him in the middle of the seventy years, not only at the end of them."}]}, "Jeremiah 33:6–9": {"title": "\"They will tremble in awe at all the good\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A promise of restoration made to a city under siege, by a prophet in prison."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jeremiah was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens by noting he was *still confined in the courtyard of the guard*. Babylonian siege ramps were against the walls. The city fell shortly after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything promised here is spoken into that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Health and healing… abundant peace and security… bring them back from exile… rebuild them as they were before… cleanse them of every sin… forgive all their sins of rebellion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six undertakings, and the last two are about guilt rather than circumstances. The rebuilding of walls and the cleansing of sin are in the same sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *abundant peace* is a phrase about an overflow of shalom and truth — wholeness, and reliability with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the nations do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then this city will bring Me joy and honour and praise before all the nations of the earth, when they hear of all the good I do for it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The restoration is public. The audience is everybody else, and what they hear about is not Judah's recovery but *the good I do*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line worth stopping on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will tremble in awe at all the good and all the peace I pour out on it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Trembling is normally the response to judgement. Here the nations shake at **goodness**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is an unusual sentence, and Jeremiah means it: the sheer scale of the mercy is what unsettles the onlookers."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is promised three verses later.** *In this place, of which you say, \"It is a waste\"… there will be heard again the voice of joy and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom and the voice of the bride.* Jeremiah quotes the city's own despairing description back and reverses it."}]}, "Jeremiah 7:30–31": {"title": "\"It never even entered My mind\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the most disturbing passages in the prophets, and one of the most revealing sentences about God in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They have built the high places of Topheth in the Valley of Ben Hinnom, and there they burn their own sons and daughters in the fire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Child sacrifice, in a valley outside Jerusalem, by people who also kept the temple running. Jeremiah is not describing a foreign nation; this is Judah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The valley's name — Ge-Hinnom — comes through Greek into the New Testament as **Gehenna**, the word Jesus uses for hell. The place became the image."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three denials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God says three things about it in a row, and the third is the one that stops you:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["I never commanded it", "I never spoke of it", "**it never even entered My mind**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *it did not come up on My heart*. Not merely that He forbade it — that the notion had never crossed His thoughts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it needed saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because they were doing it in His name, in a valley within sight of His temple, alongside sacrifices they believed He wanted. The whole passage is about worship that had become something God did not recognise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has to state that He never conceived of it, because the people doing it thought it was religion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He wanted instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the same chapter: *obey My voice, and I will be your God and you will be My people, and walk in all the way that I command you, that it may be well with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The alternative offered is not more sacrifice. It is listening."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the valley ends up.** Josiah later desecrated Topheth precisely to stop this, and the place became a refuse dump — which is how a name for a valley became a name for the fire. The worst thing people did to their children gave its name to the worst thing there is."}]}, "Jeremiah 9:23–24": {"title": "The three things not to boast in, and the one thing to", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three prohibitions and one permission, and the permission is the surprise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let not the wise boast in their wisdom. Let not the strong boast in their strength. Let not the rich boast in their riches.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wisdom, strength, wealth — the three things every ancient society ranked people by, and ours has not moved on much."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note that none of the three is condemned. Solomon was given wisdom and wealth by God directly. The prohibition is on boasting in them, which is a different thing from having them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if anyone would boast, let them boast in this alone, that they have the understanding to know Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Boasting is not forbidden. It is redirected. There is something a person is permitted to be loud about, and it is knowing God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *understanding* here is about insight gained — a knowing that has been worked at and arrived at, not merely information held."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He wants known about Himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That I am the LORD, and that I show steadfast love, justice, and righteousness on the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He specifies. Of everything that could be said about God, three things are named: **hesed** — the covenant-keeping love — along with justice and righteousness. And all three are described as being done *on the earth*, not in the abstract."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *for in these I take delight.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes the last line twice — in 1 Corinthians 1 after saying God chose the foolish and weak things, and again in 2 Corinthians. Both times as *let the one who boasts, boast in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He treats it as settling the question of what a Christian may be proud of."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When Jeremiah said it.** In a book largely about a nation about to lose its wise men, its soldiers and its wealth to Babylon. The three things he tells them not to boast in were all about to be taken away, and the one thing he leaves them was the one thing exile could not touch."}]}, "Job 1:1–5": {"title": "A blameless life", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book opens with a man nobody has anything on, and the very first habit it records about him is worth noticing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was a good man, **honest and whole all the way through**. He walked with God and turned away from evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things, and the writer says them flatly. There is no irony in the sentence and no qualification after it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *honest and whole* is **tam** — complete, entire, without a crack running through it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seven thousand sheep, three thousand camels, five hundred yoke of oxen, five hundred donkeys… **the greatest man in all the East**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The numbers are given so the reader can measure the losses later, and so the doubling at the end of the book can be checked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one habit recorded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Job would gather his children and set them right before God. **Early in the morning** he would rise and offer a burnt offering for each one of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for himself. For them. Seven sons and three daughters, one offering each, after every round of feasting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he said to himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Perhaps** my children have sinned and cursed God in their hearts.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the reason he gives, and he gives it to himself — the text says he said it in his heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had happened. Nobody had done anything. There is a *perhaps*, and there is an early morning and ten burnt offerings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"This is what Job always did\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *thus did Job all the days*. A continuous, unbroken practice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book's very first picture of its hero is a man rising before dawn, week after week, because of something his children *might* have thought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had no way of knowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether the offerings were needed. Whether they worked. Whether God was pleased."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no priest in this book, no tabernacle, no Scripture, no covenant, and no word from God to Job about any of it. He is doing what he can think of, on his own, with a *perhaps*, and repeating it every time the feasting comes round."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the man the rest of the book is about."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the New Testament says about that arrangement.** *Every priest stands daily ministering and offering repeatedly the same sacrifices, which can never take away sins. But this Man, after He had offered one sacrifice for sins forever, sat down.* Job stood up early. There is Somebody who sat down."}]}, "Job 1:6–8": {"title": "The adversary comes before God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The scene shifts to a place Job never sees and never learns about, and the whole book turns on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He got in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sons of God came to present themselves before the LORD, and **the adversary came among them too, the accuser**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ha-satan**, with the article — *the* adversary, *the* accuser. It is a job description before it is a name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the thing to see is that he is standing there. He has access. He walks into the throne room and is asked a question and answers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"From **wandering the earth, roaming back and forth** across it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the same picture: *your adversary the devil walks about like a roaring lion, seeking whom he may devour.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who brings up Job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD said to him, \"**Have you noticed My servant Job?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The accuser did not name him. God did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what God says about him is the same four things the writer said in verse 1 — *blameless and upright, a man who walks with Me and turns away from evil* — with one addition: *there is no one on earth like him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is God's own verdict on Job, spoken out loud, before any of the trouble starts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job never knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not told about this conversation. Not in chapter 1, not in chapter 42, not anywhere in the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty-two chapters of arguing about why it happened, and the reader has the answer from page one while Job never gets it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has changed since"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing Job did not have is the one thing this scene depends on: nobody was standing between him and that accuser."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone sins, we have an Advocate with the Father, Jesus Christ the righteous.* *He always lives to make intercession for them.* And in Revelation the accuser of the brethren is *cast down* — the one who *accused them before our God day and night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job had no mediator in that room. He says so himself in chapter 9: *there is no umpire between us, who might lay his hand on us both.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole difference between his position and ours, and it is not a small one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the accuser wanted.** *Does Job fear God for nothing?* His entire case is that the man is bought. Take the hedge down, he says, and he will curse You to Your face. The wager was never really about Job's suffering. It was about whether anyone loves God for who He is."}]}, "Job 11:13–15": {"title": "\"Zophar, and the formula\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third friend, and he brings a procedure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If** you would only set your heart right… **if** there is sin in your grip, put it far away… **then** you could lift up your face without a stain.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two conditions and a result. It is a formula, and it is the oldest one there is: do this, and God will do that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The steps"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*set your heart right*", "*stretch out your hands to Him*", "*if there is sin in your grip, put it far away*", "*let no wrong live in your tent*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four items on a checklist, handed to a man covered in boils scraping himself with a piece of broken pot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then you could **lift up your face without a stain**. You would stand firm and unafraid.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a few verses on: *your life would be brighter than noonday; its darkness would become like the morning.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The words are beautiful. The offer is genuine. And everything in it is conditional on Job's performance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is the wrong word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every clause depends on something Job has to produce, and the whole premise is that his darkness is being caused by sin he is holding on to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader knows it is not. Chapter 1 said so, and God Himself said so out loud in the throne room: *there is no one on earth like him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zophar is telling a man to clean up something that is not there, and then promising him light on the other side of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it sounds like now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your darkness is because of sin — deal with it, and the brightness will come back.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is still preached. And it still lands on people who are already on the floor, and it still asks them to produce a righteousness of their own before God will lift their face."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The face is lifted at Calvary, and the sin was put far away by Somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Zophar had said just before this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Know then that God exacts from you **less than your iniquity deserves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his opening position. He begins by telling Job he is getting off lightly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does not have"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of the three friends has a word from God. They have observation, tradition, and a formula, and between them they cover every way of speaking to a sufferer that leaves him to sort himself out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verdict.** All three are named in chapter 42, and the same sentence is spoken over all three: *you have not spoken of Me what is right.* And what they have to do about it is bring seven bulls and seven rams and stand there while Job prays for them. See the note on Job 42:7–8."}]}, "Job 16:1–6": {"title": "Miserable comforters", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job tells his three friends exactly what they are, and then tells them what he would have done in their place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name he gives them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Comforters? You are a misery, every one of you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a play on words — *comforters of trouble*, comforters who are themselves the trouble. It is the phrase that gave English the expression *Job's comforters*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They came to comfort him. Chapter 2 says so: they *made an appointment together to come and mourn with him and comfort him.* That was the intention."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I have heard all this before\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have heard all this before, and heard it many times. **Is there no end to your windy words?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *windy* is words of wind — air."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And there is a question after it: *what is it that stirs you up to keep answering?* He is asking what is driving them. Nobody has asked them that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he could do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I could talk the way you talk, **if it were you sitting where I sit**. I could pile up fine speeches against you and shake my head over you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument collapses into that clause. If the positions were reversed, he could produce the same material."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their theology costs them nothing to hold. It costs him everything to be under."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says he would do instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But no. I would use my mouth to steady you**, and the comfort of my lips would ease your pain.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence. A man scraping himself with a piece of broken pot, having buried ten children, describing what he would have said to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *steady* is to strengthen, to make firm — and *ease your pain* is the verb for restraining, holding something back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is telling them the thing they are for, and none of the three does it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then the honest line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yet whether I speak, my grief is not eased, and if I stay silent, it does not leave me either.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no position he can take that helps. Speaking does not relieve it; silence does not either."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where he is, and the book leaves it on the page without softening it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they had already done right.** Seven days on the ground with him and not a word, *for they saw that his suffering was very great.* The best thing the three of them managed was the week before any of them opened their mouths."}]}, "Job 19:1–6": {"title": "\"How long will you crush me?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job counts, and then draws a line his friends have been crossing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What words do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"How long will you keep **tormenting me and grinding me to pieces with words**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *grinding to pieces* is to crush, to break something small. It is used of pounding grain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is naming the weapon. Not the boils, not the losses — the talking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Ten times\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Ten times now** you have insulted me, and you are not ashamed to wound me so.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been keeping count. Ten is a round number in Hebrew for a full tally, the whole of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are not ashamed.* Not that they are wrong — that they feel nothing about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line he draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Even if I have gone wrong, **that is mine to carry, not yours**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a complete answer to all three of them. Suppose he is guilty. It is between him and God, and none of it is their business to prosecute."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my error remains with me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were really doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you really mean to **lift yourselves above me** and **use my disgrace as proof against me**…\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things he names, and both are about them rather than him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are using his condition as evidence, and they are standing on it. A man on the ground is useful to a theology that says the upright are never on the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he concludes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then understand this. **It is God who has bent me down** and drawn His net tight around me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's settled conviction, and he holds it for the whole book: God did this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader knows better. Chapter 2 said in plain words that *the adversary went out from the presence of the LORD and struck Job* — and Job is never told. See the notes on Job 1:6–8 and Job 2:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone in the argument is working from the same wrong premise. The three friends and Job all agree God sent it; they only differ on whether He was right to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirteen verses further on, out of the middle of all this, comes the most famous sentence in the book:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I know that my Redeemer lives, and He shall stand at last on the earth… and in my flesh I shall see God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Redeemer** is **go'el** — the kinsman-redeemer, the relative whose job was to buy back what a man had lost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is crushed, convinced God has netted him, and he says it anyway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he asks for two verses before that.** *Oh, that my words were written… engraved on a rock forever.* They were."}]}, "Job 2:7–8": {"title": "Struck from head to foot", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the shortest of them names who did it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who struck him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the adversary** went out from the presence of the LORD and **struck Job** with painful boils.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence names the agent. It was not God who put the boils on him — the text says the accuser went out and did it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth holding on to, because Job spends the next thirty-five chapters convinced it was God, and his friends spend the same thirty-five chapters agreeing with him about that and only arguing over why."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader knows something the whole cast does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From the soles of his feet to the crown of his head\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is a merism — the two ends stated to mean everything in between. No part of him was untouched."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy uses the identical phrase for a specific curse: *the LORD will strike you with painful boils… from the sole of your foot to the top of your head.* Any reader who knew that verse would draw the conclusion Job's friends draw."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book puts that conclusion in the reader's hand and then spends forty chapters showing it is wrong in this case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pottery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Job took a **piece of broken pottery** to scrape himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The greatest man in all the East, sitting with a shard of a broken pot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had seven thousand sheep a chapter ago. What he has now is a fragment of something that was already thrown away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ashes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he **sat down among the ashes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ash heap outside a town was the rubbish dump, where the sweepings and the burnt refuse went. It is where the destitute sat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he still had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His wife tells him to curse God and die. His three friends arrive, do not recognise him from a distance, and sit with him on the ground for seven days without a word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And no one spoke a word to him, for they saw that his suffering was very great.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seven silent days are the best thing the three of them do in the entire book."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says at the end of chapter 2.** *Shall we accept good from God, and not trouble?* And the writer adds: *in all this Job did not sin with his lips.* He is holding, and he is holding without knowing one thing about why."}]}, "Job 22:21–30": {"title": "\"Whatever you decide will hold firm\" — and who says it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Some of the loveliest promises in the Old Testament are in this speech, and the speaker is one of Job's three friends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is talking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eliphaz the Temanite, in his third and harshest speech. A few verses earlier he has accused Job of stripping the clothes off the poor and sending widows away empty — charges the book has already told the reader are false."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the passage is a true thing said by a man who is wrong about the person he is saying it to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Make peace with Him, Job, and be at rest. Then good things will find their way back to you… You will pray, and He will hear you… Whatever you decide will hold firm, and light will shine on every path you take.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Taken as statements about God, these are sound and the rest of Scripture backs them. Taken as Eliphaz means them — repent of the sins we have decided you committed and your fortunes will return — they are a cruelty, and God says so at the end of the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the book settles it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God's verdict on the three friends is unusually blunt: *you have not spoken of Me what is right, as My servant Job has.* They are told to bring sacrifices and have Job pray for them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which makes the last line here almost unbearable in hindsight. Eliphaz tells Job that God *will rescue even the guilty, and they will be delivered through the cleanness of your hands* — and that is precisely what happens, with Eliphaz as the guilty party and Job praying for him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How to use a passage like this.** The sentences are true of God; that is why they read so well on their own. What the book adds is the warning that true statements can be aimed at a suffering person as a weapon. Job's friends were at their best in the seven days they sat with him and said nothing."}]}, "Job 26:5–10": {"title": "\"He hangs the earth on nothing at all\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job's answer to Bildad, and in the middle of it he says something no one else in the ancient world said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He stretches the northern skies over **empty space** and **hangs the earth on nothing at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *upon nothing* — **beli-mah**, without anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job is probably the oldest book in the Bible. Every culture around it had the earth resting on something: on a turtle, on elephants, on pillars, on the shoulders of a giant, on the back of a great fish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it is hanging on nothing, and nobody could confirm it for three and a half thousand years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The circle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **drew a circle on the surface of the waters**, a boundary line where light meets darkness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same Hebrew word Isaiah uses — **chug**, a circle drawn with a fixed radius."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second clause is exact. The boundary where light meets darkness on the surface of a sphere is a circle, and it moves. It is called the terminator, and it is what you are looking at in every photograph of the earth from space."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clouds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **wraps up the waters in His thick clouds**, and the clouds do not burst beneath their weight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *wraps up* is to bind in a bundle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An average cumulus cloud carries hundreds of tonnes of water and does not fall. Job noticed that it does not, and said the not-falling was the remarkable part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is in the argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is a man on an ash heap, covered in boils, whose three friends have been telling him for twenty chapters that he brought it on himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His reply is a description of the universe. Bildad had just said *how then can man be righteous before God?* — and Job's answer is four verses of cosmology."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And these are but the outer fringes of His ways. How faint the whisper we hear of Him! Who then can understand the thunder of His power?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *outer fringes* is the ends, the edges. He has just described empty space, a hanging earth, bundled oceans and a drawn circle, and calls it the hem of the garment."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who says the same thing later.** *In Him all things hold together* — Colossians 1. Job says the earth hangs on nothing; Paul names what nothing is."}]}, "Job 29:14–17": {"title": "\"I wrapped myself in righteousness\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job's longest speech, remembering what he used to be, and it is worth counting the pronouns."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The garment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I wrapped myself** in righteousness, and it clothed me; justice was my robe and my crown.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He put it on himself. That is what the verb says, and the whole passage is built the same way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah describes a different arrangement: *He has clothed me with the garments of salvation, He has covered me with the robe of righteousness.* Somebody else does the dressing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's robe is one he wove and wrapped around himself, and every word of it is true — he really was that man. Chapter 1 said so and God said so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I was **eyes to the blind** and **feet to the lame**. I was **a father to the needy**, and when a stranger came with a case I did not know, **I searched it out for him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The record is extraordinary. He was not boasting about property or influence — he is listing the blind, the lame, the poor and the stranger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I broke the jaws of the wicked and pulled the prey from between his teeth.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has happened to him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the chapter as a whole and count. In chapter 29, Job refers to himself well over forty times and to God about five."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not how he began. Chapter 1: *the LORD gave and the LORD has taken away; blessed be the name of the LORD.* Chapter 2: *shall we accept good from God and not trouble?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between there and here, three men have spent twenty-five chapters telling him his suffering is his own fault. He has had to defend himself for so long that he has become his own subject."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what preaching from experience, tradition and a formula does to the man underneath it. It does not produce repentance. It produces a case for the defence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what it turns into"}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the next chapter he is looking down on men whose fathers he *would not have trusted even with my sheepdogs.* See the note on Job 30:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bitterness and the pride arrive together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is still in it at the end of chapter 31: *let the Almighty answer me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the Almighty does, out of a whirlwind, and Job's answer is *I have heard of You by the hearing of the ear, but now my eye sees You.* The robe he wrapped himself in comes off the moment he actually sees Him. See the note on Job 42:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the chapter is here.** It is not a villain's speech. Every claim in it is true, and God will publicly call him *My servant Job* four times over in chapter 42. It is what a genuinely good man sounds like after twenty-five chapters of being told to justify himself."}]}, "Job 29:21–25": {"title": "\"They waited for me as for the rain\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job remembering what his life used to be, and the memory is about how people listened to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They waited for me as for the rain, and opened their mouths wide as for the spring showers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **spring** showers — the latter rain, the one that filled out the grain just before harvest. See the note on Deuteronomy 11:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mouths open, upturned, waiting for something that only comes from above and cannot be summoned. That is how he says people used to receive what he said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What his words did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My words fell gently on them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the dripping or distilling of dew — the same picture Moses uses in Deuteronomy 32 of his own teaching. Not a downpour. Something that settles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the effect: *after I had spoken they had nothing to add.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did with the standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I chose the road for them and sat at their head. I lived like a king among his troops, **like one who comforts those who mourn**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last phrase turns the whole passage. He has been describing authority — men falling silent, princes deferring, a seat at the head — and he ends it by comparing himself to someone consoling the grieving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his own account of what the position was for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the chapter exists"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job is speaking in the past tense throughout. The next chapter begins *but now they mock me* — men younger than him, whose fathers he would not have put with his sheepdogs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is a man remembering, out loud, in the middle of losing everything."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was actually like.** The verses just above this passage are his own account: he rescued the poor, was eyes to the blind and feet to the lame, took up the case of the stranger, and broke the jaws of the wicked. The book's assessment of him at the start — blameless and upright — is not contradicted by anything he says here."}]}, "Job 30:1–4": {"title": "Laughed at by the lowest", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chapter turns on two words, and then Job says something he would not have said in chapter 1."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"But now\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 29 was *oh, that I were as in months past*. Chapter 30 begins *but now*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two chapters are a hinge — everything he was, and everything he is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Those younger than I make sport of me, men whose fathers **I would not have trusted even with my sheepdogs**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the line. He is not describing what they did to him; he is placing them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their fathers were beneath the dogs that watched his flock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they actually are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the next three verses, because Job describes them himself:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Gaunt from want and hunger, they gnawed the dry ground… They picked salt herbs among the bushes, and the roots of the broom tree were their bread.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Starving people. Eating roots and weeds in waste ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who was *eyes to the blind and feet to the lame*, *a father to the needy*, is looking at the destitute and measuring them against his sheepdogs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has been done to him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is chapter 30 of a book in which three men have spent twenty-five chapters telling him his ruin is his own fault."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man forced to defend himself for that long ends up with a case, and the case needs someone to be beneath him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bitterness in chapter 30 and the self-justification in chapter 29 are the same thing looked at from two sides, and both of them arrived after his comforters started talking. He did not sound like this in chapter 1 or 2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The honesty of the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is tidied up. The writer records the greatest man in the East sneering at starving men and does not annotate it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job has to hear from God before this comes off him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the whole speech is going.** By the end of the chapter: *I cry out to You, and You do not answer me… I am a brother of jackals and a companion of ostriches… my harp is turned to mourning.* He is not enjoying the contempt he is handing out. He is a man in pieces, and the pieces cut in every direction."}]}, "Job 32:1–5": {"title": "The three fall quiet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-nine chapters of argument end because everyone has run out of words, and a young man who has been sitting there the whole time stands up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the three stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was nothing left to say, because **in Job's own eyes he was in the right**, and no argument of theirs could move him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the writer's own summary, not a friend's accusation. After everything they threw at him, Job's settled position is that he is right."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it himself in chapter 29 — *I wrapped myself in righteousness, and it clothed me* — and in that speech he refers to himself well over forty times and to God about five. See the note on Job 29:14–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three men's preaching did not humble him. It hardened him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Elihu"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But there was a young man sitting by, **listening to it all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been present since chapter 3 and this is the first the reader hears of him. Nine chapters of speeches, and he was in the room for every one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elihu, son of Barachel the Buzite, from the family of Ram.* The fullest genealogy given to anyone in the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His name means *He is my God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is angry at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things, and they are stated separately:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*angry at **Job** for making himself out to be more in the right than God*", "*angry at **the three friends** too, because they had found no real answer, yet they had gone right on condemning Job anyway*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the only person in the book who is against both sides of the argument. And his charge against the three is precise: they had no answer, and they kept condemning anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he waited"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All this time Elihu had **held his tongue, waiting**, because the others were older than he was and it seemed only fitting.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nine chapters of silence out of respect."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu speaks for six chapters. Nobody interrupts him. Job — who answered every one of the other three, at length, ten times over — does not say a word back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And when Elihu finishes, the next verse is *then the LORD answered Job out of the whirlwind.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says he is not doing.** *I will not show partiality to anyone, nor flatter any man, for I do not know how to flatter — my Maker would soon take me away.* The three came to defend a system. He says at the outset that he has no side."}]}, "Job 33:19–22": {"title": "The man on the bed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elihu describes a man at the end of himself, and the passage exists to set up what comes next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it looks like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"An ache that never leaves his bones. Now **the sight of food turns his stomach**; even the finest dish is nothing to him. His flesh wastes away until you can hardly see it, and **the bones that were hidden now jut out**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a clinical description, and it is unsparing. Constant pain, no appetite, visible wasting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whoever wrote this had watched somebody go through it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is heading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"His soul drifts toward the grave. His life inches toward **the messengers of death**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the destroyers, the ones who bring death. He is not dying quickly. He is being carried there by degrees."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word the whole passage waits for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse begins: *\"**Yet even then**, if there is a messenger at his side…\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in these four verses is the *before*. It is written to be the darkest possible position, so that the *yet even then* has something to stand against."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which messenger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice the two in the passage, and that they are the same Hebrew word."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["verse 22 — the **messengers** of death, carrying him toward the pit", "verse 23 — the **messenger** at his side, one in a thousand, who speaks for him"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same noun, and they are pulling in opposite directions over one man on a bed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that messenger does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not tell the man what he did wrong. He does not prescribe a remedy or take a history."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He shows him God's righteousness, and God answers: *I have found the price for his release.* See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then his flesh grows fresh again, softer than a child's.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "From bones jutting out to skin like a child's, and the thing that stands between the two descriptions is a ransom found by God. See the note on Job 33:25–28."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why Elihu is describing this at all.** He is talking to a man scraping boils with a piece of broken pot. The three friends looked at Job and produced an accusation. Elihu looks at the same man and produces a mediator."}]}, "Job 33:23–24": {"title": "\"I have found the price for his release\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The turning point of the whole book, spoken by a young man nobody had asked, and it is the answer to everything the three friends could not solve."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu has just described a man on a sickbed — flesh wasting, bones jutting out, his soul drifting toward the grave, his life inching toward the messengers of death. See the note on Job 33:19–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *Yet even then, **if there is a messenger at his side**…*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one in a thousand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One who **speaks for him**, one in a thousand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a **melitz** — an interpreter, a spokesman, a go-between. It is the word used of the interpreter standing between Joseph and his brothers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *one in a thousand* — there is one such person, and He is not common."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is exactly what Job had been crying out for. In chapter 9: *He is not a man, as I am, that I should answer Him… **there is no umpire between us**, who might lay his hand on us both.* In chapter 16: *my witness is in heaven, my advocate is on high.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job knew what was missing. Elihu names it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the messenger does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **remind this person of what is right and true**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to examine him. Not to search out his sin, prescribe steps, or diagnose the cause — which is what all three friends spent twenty-five chapters doing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to declare to the man His **uprightness** — God's righteousness, shown to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The messenger's job is to show a suffering man what God is like. That is the whole commission."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then God speaks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **God is gracious to him** and says, \"Spare him. Do not let him go down into the pit. **I have found the price for his release**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *gracious* is **chanan** — the verb behind *chen*, grace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ransom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have found the price for his release.* The Hebrew is **kopher** — a ransom, a covering, the price paid to redeem a life that was forfeit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same root as **kippur**, the covering of the Day of Atonement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read who does the finding. Not the sick man. Not the friends. **I have found it.** God locates the ransom Himself, and announces it as the reason the man is spared."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no condition attached to it in Elihu's sentence. A messenger shows him God's righteousness; God says a price has been found; the man does not go down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the other side of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *then his flesh grows fresh again, softer than a child's. He returns to the strength of his young days.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healing follows the ransom. It does not produce it. See the note on Job 33:25–28."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the messenger is.** *There is one God and one Mediator between God and men, the Man Christ Jesus, who gave Himself a ransom for all.* One God, one Mediator, one ransom — and Elihu says it in a book that has no priest, no temple and no covenant in it anywhere."}]}, "Job 33:25–28": {"title": "\"Softer than a child's\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elihu is speaking — the young man who waited for the three friends to finish — and this is the best passage in his long speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restoration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then his flesh grows fresh again, softer than a child's. He returns to the strength of his young days.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same picture as Naaman coming up out of the Jordan with skin like a little boy's. Not repaired. Made new."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second line adds the strength back — not merely healed, but restored to what he could once do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word \"then\" is attached to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then* is the first word of the passage, and it is pointing back at something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu has described a man brought to the edge — laid low on his bed, his flesh wasting until you can hardly see it, his life inching toward the messengers of death. Then a messenger comes and shows him God's righteousness, and God says: *I have found the price for his release.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Then** his flesh grows fresh again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is fixed and it does not run the other way. Ransom found, and the renewal follows it. Nothing in these four verses is produced by the sick man's effort — he is on a bed, unable to eat, and a price is found for him by Somebody else. See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *ransom* is **kopher**, a covering, the price paid to buy back a forfeited life — and it is what the New Testament is built on: *the Son of Man came to give His life a ransom for many.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the man then does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He prays to God, and God welcomes him with delight. He looks on God's face and shouts for joy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things: prayer, a welcome, and then his face lifted. The Hebrew for *welcomes him with delight* is the word for accepting a sacrifice as pleasing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confession he makes afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I sinned. I twisted what was straight, and it gained me nothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes **after** the rescue, not before it — he is singing it to everyone as testimony. The order in Elihu's account is: ransom found, life restored, and then the honest account of what he had done."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who Elihu was.** God's verdict at the end names Eliphaz, Bildad and Zophar and tells them they have not spoken what is right. Elihu is never rebuked, never corrected, and never answered by Job — who argued with all three of the others and has nothing to say to this one. He is the only voice in the book that speaks of a mediator and a ransom, and the storm that brings God's own voice comes immediately after he has finished."}]}, "Job 38:1–3": {"title": "God begins to speak", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Thirty-five chapters of silence, and then He speaks — and what He says is not what anyone in the book was expecting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Out of the storm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He spoke **out of the storm**, out of the wind and the wild weather.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the whirlwind, the tempest. It is how He came for Elijah too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not answer a single one of Job's questions. Not one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not explain the boils. He does not mention the throne room, the accuser, or the wager. He does not defend Himself against the charge Job has been laying for thirty chapters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He does not confirm the friends' diagnosis either. There is no list of Job's sins, because there is no list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have questions for you**, and it is your turn to answer Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks. Four chapters of questions — seventy-odd of them — and almost every one is about something Job has never had anything to do with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Where were you when I laid the earth's foundations. Have you entered the springs of the sea. Have you commanded the morning. Who provides food for the raven. Do you know when the mountain goats give birth. Can you bind the Pleiades."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them is about Job's conduct."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Brace yourself like a strong man\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *gird up your loins* — pull your robe through your belt so you can move."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not gentle and it is not a rebuke either. He is telling Job to stand up. Job has been on an ash heap for thirty-five chapters and God's first instruction is to get on his feet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Words that carry no knowledge\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Who is this who **muddies My counsel** with words that carry no knowledge?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *muddies* is to darken, to obscure — the same picture as silt stirred up in clear water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's answer, when it comes, is to agree: *it was me.* See the note on Job 42:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the questions are actually doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole speech is about the ostrich and the wild donkey and the storehouses of snow and the horse that laughs at fear. It is a tour of things being cared for that no one is watching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The raven's chicks are fed. The mountain goats calve without a midwife. The rain falls *on a land where no one lives, on a desert with no one in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers a man who thinks he has been abandoned by showing him how much is being looked after where nobody can see."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Job says he gets out of it.** *I have heard of You by the hearing of the ear, but now my eye sees You.* Not an explanation. He never gets one. He gets the One he had been asking to see."}]}, "Job 4:7–11": {"title": "\"Eliphaz, and what he had seen\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first friend to speak, and the ground he stands on is his own observation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where his authority comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What I have seen** is this: those who plough trouble and plant sorrow harvest exactly that.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his source. Not a word from God, not a Scripture — what he has watched happen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same thing again a few verses later: *a word was secretly brought to me… a spirit passed before my face, and the hair of my flesh stood up.* A vision in the night, a voice he could not identify."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Experience is not the mother of wisdom. The Word of God is. A man who preaches from what he has personally seen will build a rule out of a sample, and then apply the rule to a man he does not understand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Name one honest person who was ever destroyed for nothing.** Where were the upright ever cut down?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sounds unanswerable. It is a rhetorical question with an obvious answer, put to a man sitting in ashes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer is sitting in front of him. Job is the one honest person destroyed for nothing, and Eliphaz cannot see it because his rule will not let him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rule does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It converts the suffering into evidence. Once you hold *the upright are never cut down*, then a man who is cut down cannot be upright, and every day he stays in the ashes is another day of proof."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the machine Job is now inside for twenty-five chapters, and all three friends are feeding it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sowing and reaping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Those who plough trouble and plant sorrow harvest exactly that.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a true principle. Paul states it — *whatever a man sows, that he will also reap.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "True, and applied to the wrong man, in the wrong situation, at the worst possible hour. The problem with Eliphaz is not that what he says is false in general. It is that it is false about Job, and he says it anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four lines about lions with their teeth broken and their cubs scattered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is describing the fate of the wicked, in poetry, to a man whose ten children were killed a fortnight ago."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says about him at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"My wrath is aroused against you and your two friends, for **you have not spoken of Me what is right**, as My servant Job has.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said directly to Eliphaz, by name, in chapter 42. He is the first of the three to speak and the one God addresses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else the three of them got wrong, God's own summary of the charge is that they misrepresented **Him**."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the friends thought they were defending.** God's justice. They spent twenty-five chapters protecting His reputation, and the verdict at the end is that they had not said one right thing about Him. See the note on Job 42:7–8."}]}, "Job 40:6–8": {"title": "\"Would you condemn Me so that you could be in the right?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second speech from the whirlwind, and it contains the only direct charge God lays against Job in the entire book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Would you really cancel My justice? Would you condemn Me so that you could be in the right?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is it. Forty-two chapters, and this is the whole indictment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the boils. Not any sin the friends dug for. Not one item off Eliphaz's, Bildad's or Zophar's lists — because none of them was there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is that Job put God in the wrong in order to be in the right himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"cancel My justice\" means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to break, to annul, to make void — the word used of breaking a covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's defence had required a verdict on God. If Job is innocent and God sent the suffering, then God is unjust. That is the only place the argument could go, and Job followed it there: *it is God who has bent me down and drawn His net tight around me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The choice in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There are two positions and only one of them can be occupied. Either God is right, or I am."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job had spent thirty chapters taking the second one — and every man does, by instinct. Being wrong is the harder of the two to say out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David took the other one: *against You, You only, have I sinned… **so You are right when You speak, You are clear when You judge**.* He wrote it after the worst thing he ever did. See the note on Psalms 51:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole difference. One man justifies himself and God ends up in the dock; the other agrees God is right and finds himself in the clear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the self-justifying came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job did not start here. Chapter 1: *the LORD gave and the LORD has taken away; blessed be the name of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It came out of him after twenty-five chapters of men telling him to account for himself. A man who is made to defend himself long enough ends up with a case, and a case needs a defendant on the other side. See the note on Job 29:14–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Brace yourself again\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same words as chapter 38 — *stand up now, brace yourself like a strong man.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is being stood up a second time, not knocked down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job says back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I know that You can do all things… I spoke of things I did not understand. **It was me.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes the other position, and the book turns over in that sentence. See the note on Job 42:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where Paul lands in the same place.** *For I know that nothing good lives in me — that is, in the old nature.* It is not despair; it is the end of an argument. A man who has settled that stops trying to build a defence, and can take a righteousness that was given to him. See the note on Romans 7:18–20."}]}, "Job 42:1–3": {"title": "\"It was me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job's last speech, and it is six verses long. He has been talking for thirty chapters and this is what he has left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He quotes the question back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You asked, '**Who is this who hides My counsel without knowledge?**'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God had put it as a question in chapter 38 and Job repeats it word for word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It was me.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words. There is no negotiation, no *but*, no explanation of the circumstances."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not plead the ash heap, the ten graves, the boils or the friends. He answers the question that was asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he admits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I spoke of things I did not understand, wonders far too great for me, things I did not know.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that he sinned in a way that caused the disaster — that was the friends' charge and it was never true, and God will say so in five verses' time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he concedes is that he talked about something he could not see, and formed a verdict on the One running it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he stops doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last thing he says in the passage that follows is *I abhor myself, and repent in dust and ashes*, and the Hebrew of that first phrase is difficult — it can be read *I retract*, or *I melt away*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way the case is withdrawn. Chapter 29 was a man wrapping himself in his own righteousness; here he takes it off. See the note on Job 29:14–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now I know that **You can do all things**, and that **no purpose of Yours can ever be stopped**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has agreed God is right, and the first thing he says after it is about God's ability rather than his own failure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never learns why. Not one word about the accuser, the throne room, or the wager, then or ever."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What actually changed him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have heard of You by the hearing of the ear, **but now my eye sees You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an argument. Four chapters of ostriches and rainstorms and wild goats calving, and a Person on the other side of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three friends had reasoned at him for twenty-five chapters and hardened him. He sees Him once and the whole defence drops."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes immediately after.** Not a rebuke. God turns to Eliphaz and calls Job *My servant* four times in two verses, and tells the three friends that Job is the one who spoke rightly of Him. See the note on Job 42:7–8."}]}, "Job 42:10": {"title": "\"When Job prayed for his friends\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and the turn of it is in the first four words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trigger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **when Job prayed for his friends**, the LORD restored everything he had lost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when he repented — that was four verses earlier and nothing was returned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when he understood — he never did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "When he prayed for **them**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eliphaz, who told him no innocent man was ever destroyed. Bildad, who said his children got what they deserved. Zophar, who told him he was getting off lightly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-five chapters of it. He had called them *miserable comforters* and told them they were grinding him to pieces with words, and he was right about all of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Those are the three he is told to pray for, and God will not forgive them without it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He still had nothing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the order of the chapter. Job prays while he is still on the ash heap. The sheep are still dead, the children are still buried, the boils are still on him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not praying out of an abundance. He has nothing to give but the prayer, and he gives it to the men who hurt him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **restored** everything he had lost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *turned the captivity of Job* — the same phrase used of God bringing Israel back from exile."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is release language. He had been held."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And gave him **twice as much** as he had before.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not restored to level. Doubled, and the numbers in verse 12 confirm it item by item against chapter 1: seven thousand sheep become fourteen thousand, three thousand camels become six thousand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What did not wait"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the verse says Job's own situation had to improve before he could pray for theirs. It says the reverse — the praying came first, and the restoring followed it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He interceded for men who did not deserve it, out of a position where he had every reason not to, and that is the sentence the whole book turns on."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one thing that is not doubled.** He had seven sons and three daughters, and he ends with seven sons and three daughters. The livestock doubles; the children do not. Readers have always noticed it, and the usual reading is the kind one — the first ten were not lost to him."}]}, "Job 42:12–15": {"title": "Twice as much as before", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last picture in the book, and the writer takes his time over the details."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Checked against chapter 1"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["7,000 sheep → **14,000**", "3,000 camels → **6,000**", "500 yoke of oxen → **1,000**", "500 donkeys → **1,000**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every figure exactly doubled. The numbers in chapter 1 were given so this could be counted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD blessed **the last part of Job's life even more than the first**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The daughters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He named the first daughter **Jemimah**, the second **Keziah**, and the third **Keren-happuch**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sons are counted; the daughters are named. In the whole of Job the only people given personal names besides Job, his friends and Elihu are these three girls."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The names are all about beauty and scent — **Jemimah** is dove, **Keziah** is cassia, the fragrant bark, and **Keren-happuch** is *horn of eye-paint*, the flask of dark cosmetic a woman kept."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man came out of the ashes and named his daughters after perfume and a dove."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"No women as lovely as Job's daughters\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In all the land there were **no women as lovely** as Job's daughters.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer states it as fact, in the same breath as the livestock count. It is part of the blessing being listed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Beauty is something God gives, and it is written into the ledger here alongside the sheep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inheritance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And their father gave them **an inheritance among their brothers, right alongside the sons**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not how it was done. Daughters inherited only where there were no sons — the case of Zelophehad's daughters in Numbers 27 had to be brought to Moses specially."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job had seven sons. He gave the girls a share anyway, as equals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who has lost everything and been given it back twice over is not careful about the rules for dividing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Job died, old and full of days.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *satisfied with days*. He had enough."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He saw four generations — *his children and their children to the fourth generation* — which is one hundred and forty more years after the ash heap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is still missing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is never told why. Not in these verses, not anywhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets his life back doubled, four generations of grandchildren, three beautiful daughters with an inheritance, and no explanation at all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What James says the end of it proves.** *You have heard how Job held out, and you have seen what the Lord finally made of it — that He feels for people deeply, and He is kind all the way through.* The New Testament's verdict on this chapter is not about endurance. It is about what God turned out to be. See the note on James 5:10–11."}]}, "Job 42:7–8": {"title": "\"You have not spoken of Me what is right\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The verdict, and it goes the opposite way from everything the previous forty chapters seemed to be building toward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is in trouble"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My anger burns against you and your two friends.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three men who spent twenty-five chapters defending God's justice are the ones He is angry with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eliphaz is named. He spoke first and he is addressed first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **you have not spoken of Me what is right**, as My servant Job has.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice, once at the start and once at the end, framing the whole instruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offence is not how they treated Job — though they crushed him with words for twenty-five chapters. It is what they said about **God**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had made Him the kind of God who breaks a man to punish hidden sin, who operates a formula, who has to be defended by accusation. Every sermon they preached misrepresented Him, and that is the thing He answers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As My servant Job has\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job, who said God had netted him, bent him down, and would not answer — Job is the one who spoke rightly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said hard things and he said them to God's face. They said correct-sounding things about Him behind His back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *My servant Job* is used four times in these two verses. God keeps repeating the title over the man his friends had been treating as a defendant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they have to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take **seven bulls and seven rams**, and go to My servant Job, and offer them up as a burnt offering for yourselves.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven and seven — the largest sacrifice named anywhere in the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they have to carry it to Job. Three older men driving fourteen animals across to the ash heap of the man they had been lecturing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And who has to pray"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And My servant Job will pray for you. I will accept his prayer** and not deal with you as your foolishness deserves.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sacrifice is not enough on its own. God makes their forgiveness run through the man they wronged."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job has to intercede for Eliphaz, Bildad and Zophar. They cannot approach without him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that makes him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man standing between God and three guilty people, praying, with a burnt offering on the fire — which is precisely the office Elihu had described back in chapter 33: *a messenger at his side, one who speaks for him, one in a thousand.* See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what it does to Job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very next verse: *when Job prayed for his friends, the LORD restored everything he had lost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when he repented. Not when he understood. When he prayed for them. See the note on Job 42:10."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the verdict.** Any answer. God rebukes the friends, vindicates Job, and never once explains the boils to anybody. The book ends with the question unanswered and the man restored."}]}, "Job 8:8–19": {"title": "\"Bildad, and the generations before\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second friend, and he stands on something older than himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where his authority comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Just ask the generations who came before**; consider what their ancestors learned.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his case. Not observation, like Eliphaz — inheritance. What has always been taught."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We were born only yesterday and know nothing.\"* He disqualifies the present in favour of the past, and then speaks with total confidence about a man in front of him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tradition does not mean truth. Something can be handed down for four hundred years and still be the wrong word for the man in the ashes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The papyrus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Can papyrus grow tall where there is no marsh?** Can reeds flourish without water?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A proverb, and a good one. Cut the reed off from the water and it withers faster than grass that was never green."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That is the way of everyone who forgets God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just called Job a man who forgot God, in a metaphor, without saying his name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The spider's web"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What he leans on is fragile; what he trusts is **a spider's web**. He leans on his house, and it will not hold. He grabs it, and it will not last.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The house that will not hold. Job's ten children died when a house came down on them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bildad had opened his speech in this chapter with *if your children have sinned against Him, He has cast them away for their transgression.* He is not being careless. He means it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The uprooted plant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He is like a well-watered plant in the sunlight, spreading its shoots… gripping the rocks. But **tear it from its place**, and that place disowns it: 'I never saw you.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most accurate description of Job's situation in the chapter, and it is delivered as an accusation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The greatest man in the East, spread out over the hills, torn out. Bildad has the picture exactly right and the diagnosis exactly wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the traditional wisdom actually says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bildad's inherited system is simple: God blesses the righteous and destroys the wicked, visibly, in this life, every time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is neat, and much of the Old Testament reads that way at a distance. The book of Job exists because it is not the whole story, and because a man was sitting in ashes for whom it was not true at all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verdict on him.** *You have not spoken of Me what is right.* He quoted the fathers, he used the proverbs correctly, and God's summary of his theology was that it misrepresented Him. See the notes on Job 4:7–11 and Job 42:7–8."}]}, "Joel 2:21–24": {"title": "\"Just as before\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Joel has spent a chapter and a half describing a locust invasion that stripped the country bare. This is the turn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is told not to be afraid"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times, and the first two are addressed to things that cannot hear:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Do not be afraid, **land***", "*Do not be afraid, **you wild animals**, for the pastures of the wilderness are turning green again*", "*Be glad, **you children of Zion***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ground and the animals are addressed before the people. The devastation was total, so the reversal is announced to everything it touched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gives you the early rain in right measure, and He sends the showers down for you, the early rain and the late, **just as before**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both rains, in their seasons, restored to what they used to be. The Hebrew for *in right measure* carries the sense of according to what is right — the proper amount, neither withheld nor destructive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not more than before. **As** before — which, to a country that had watched everything eaten, is the whole promise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The overflow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The threshing floors will be full of grain, and the vats will overflow with new wine and oil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three crops of Deuteronomy 11, named again: grain, wine, oil. The promise is being restated in the vocabulary of the original covenant blessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The famous line just after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will repay you for the years the locusts have eaten.* It comes two verses on, and this passage is the run-up to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note that Joel does not say the years were not lost. He says they will be repaid — which assumes the loss was real."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter ends.** *And afterward, I will pour out My Spirit on all people.* The same verb — pour — that has been used of rain the whole way through. Peter quotes it at Pentecost, and by then the harvest language has become something else. See the note on Acts 2:1–4."}]}, "John 1:14–18": {"title": "\"Moved in among us\" — and grace on top of grace", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three phrases in this passage are doing more than the English can easily carry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Moved in among us\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is built on the word for a **tent**. Literally: He pitched His tent among us, He tabernacled with us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every Jewish reader knew which tent. The tabernacle in the wilderness was where the glory settled — and John's next words are *we saw His glory with our own eyes*. He is saying the presence that filled the tent has taken a body and moved into the neighbourhood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Grace on top of grace\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is odd in Greek too — literally *grace instead of grace*, or grace succeeding grace. The picture is of one wave arriving as the last recedes: nothing is used up, and what replaces it is more of the same."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not grace once, and then the reader's effort. Grace, replaced by grace, out of a fullness John says we have all been drawing from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The law was given through Moses; grace and truth came through Jesus Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different verbs, and the difference is deliberate. The law was *given* — handed over, delivered as a deposit. Grace and truth **came into being** through Jesus Christ: the verb John uses of things happening, coming to pass."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say the law was bad. He says one was delivered and the other arrived as an event, in a person."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *No one has ever seen God. The only Son… He is the One who has made Him known.* The verb behind *made known* is where the word *exegesis* comes from — to lead out, to explain, to set forth in full. Jesus is described as God's own exposition of Himself. John has spent seventeen verses building to that, and then the Gospel begins."}]}, "John 1:29–34": {"title": "\"Look — the Lamb of God\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John the Baptist gives Jesus a title nobody had used before, and it carries a thousand years of Israel's worship in three words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which lamb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every lamb in Israel's memory is standing behind the phrase:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the ram caught in the thicket in place of Isaac", "the Passover lamb whose blood went on the doorposts", "the two lambs offered every morning and evening in the temple", "the lamb Isaiah described, led to the slaughter and silent"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not specify. He says *the Lamb of God*, and lets the whole system answer to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Takes away\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb means to lift and carry off. Not covers, not compensates for — removes, by carrying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the difference between the old arrangement and this one. The sacrifices covered sin year after year; this one is described as taking it away and going off with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sin offering looked like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus 4 sets out the procedure, and the order of it is worth walking through:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the man brings the animal — **the animal is examined**, not the man", "he **lays his hand on its head**, and the Hebrew is to lean, to press down", "the animal is killed in his place", "the blood is applied by the priest", "*and it shall be forgiven him*", "**and the man goes home**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about his sincerity, his record, or his resolve enters the ceremony. The inspection falls on the lamb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why the four days of examination in Passion Week matter, and why every delegation that came at Him went away with nothing, and why Pilate's verdict is *I find no fault in Him.* The lamb was looked over in public and passed. See the note on Matthew 21:23–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Of the world\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not of Israel. John the Baptist, a Jewish prophet in the Judean wilderness, extends it to the whole world in his first recorded description of Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I did not know myself who He was\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice. They were relatives — Luke has their mothers meeting before either was born — so he knew the man. What he did not know was the identity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The confirmation came by a sign he had been told to watch for: the Spirit coming down and **staying**. The verb is the one John uses all through his Gospel for abiding."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he calls himself.** Nothing. Asked earlier who he is, he says he is a voice. Here he gives his whole testimony and it is entirely about somebody else — including the line *He was there long before I ever was*, said by a man six months older."}]}, "John 10:11–15": {"title": "The shepherd and the hired hand", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says *I am the good shepherd* twice in five verses, and between the two He describes the alternative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"good\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kalos** — not merely morally good but fine, admirable, the right one for the job. It is the word used of a good tree, a beautiful thing, a work well done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What makes him good"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The good shepherd lays down His life for the sheep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not his skill or his knowledge of the country. The definition offered is a willingness to die, and it is stated before anything else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ordinary shepherds risked their lives — David fought lions and bears. But risking and *laying down* are different, and John's Gospel keeps the distinction: no one takes it from Me, I lay it down of My own accord."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hired hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sheep are not his own, so when he sees the wolf coming he abandons them and runs… he is only hired, and the sheep mean nothing to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is said about him being wicked. He is not accused of stealing — that was the thief, two verses earlier. His failure is ownership: they are not his, so at the moment it costs something he leaves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The knowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I know My own, and My own know Me — just as the Father knows Me, and I know the Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is astonishing and easy to read past. The mutual knowledge between Christ and His people is set on the same scale as the knowledge between the Son and the Father."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *I lay down My life for the sheep* twice in the passage — once as the definition and once as the conclusion, with the knowing in between."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who was listening.** This follows the healing of the man born blind, whom the religious leaders had thrown out of the synagogue for defending Jesus. The hirelings who scatter and abandon are described with those men present. See the note on John 10:7–10."}]}, "John 10:27–30": {"title": "Two hands, and a double negative", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the passage people are quietly reaching for when they ask whether they can be lost. It answers, and the way it answers is worth seeing in detail, because Jesus stacks the assurance four times over in three sentences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four layers"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**I give them life that lasts forever** — given, not lent", "**they will never be lost** — the Greek uses a double negative, the strongest form of denial the language has: not ever, under any circumstance", "**no one will ever tear them out of My hand**", "**no one is able to tear them out of My Father's hand**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, having named two hands, He closes with *I and the Father are one*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The security is not asserted once and left. It is stated, strengthened, transferred to a second pair of hands, and then sealed by saying the two are one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"no one\" covers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is unrestricted. It is not *no enemy* or *no other person*. The grammar does not carve out an exception for the sheep itself, and it would be strange to read one in, since the point of the sentence is the strength of the hand rather than the grip of the sheep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And notice the word Jesus uses for what cannot happen: *tear out*, snatch, seize by force. It is the language of a predator — the same word John uses earlier in the chapter for the wolf."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Read the first line again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My sheep listen to My voice. I know them, and they follow Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs. Only one of them is the sheep's, and it is *listen* — and even that is described as a recognition rather than an achievement. Sheep are not clever animals. Their whole competence in this picture is knowing which voice is theirs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has already said why they can: the shepherd calls them by name and goes ahead of them. Recognition is possible because He speaks first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the passage is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I and the Father are one* is not an aside tacked onto a comfort. It is the conclusion of the argument, and the people listening understood it perfectly — the next verse has them picking up stones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the assurance and the claim about His identity are the same statement. Whether you can be held depends on who is holding you, and Jesus answers that question by saying whose hand it is."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the sheep who wander.** Nothing here says a sheep never strays; the chapter next door in Luke is about one that does. What this passage rules out is the sheep being *taken*, and it rules it out with the strongest negative Greek can build. Straying and being snatched are not the same thing, and only one of them is addressed here."}]}, "John 10:7–10": {"title": "The gate, and the thief", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus calls Himself two things in this chapter — the gate and the shepherd — and the first one is the stranger claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I am the gate\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sheepfolds in the hill country were stone enclosures with a single gap in the wall. There was no door. At night the shepherd lay down across the opening and slept there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shepherd *was* the gate. Nothing came in or went out except over him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the gate is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever comes in through Me will be saved, and will go in and go out and find pasture.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two directions. A gate that only let you in would be a cage; this one is for coming and going. *Going in and out* is a Hebrew idiom for living freely and safely — the phrase used of Israel's leaders moving among the people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Safety and freedom in one sentence, and the pasture is outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thief's three verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The thief comes for one reason only — to steal, to kill, to destroy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, and they escalate. Take what you have, end what you are, ruin what is left. Jesus gives no other motive: *for one reason only.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have come so that they may have life, and have it overflowing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *abundantly* — beyond the necessary measure, over and above. Not merely enough life to go on with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set beside three verbs of destruction, the answer is one verb of giving, with an adverb attached that means more than required."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the thieves were.** The chapter follows the healing of a blind man whom the religious authorities threw out of the synagogue for saying who healed him. Jesus' words about hirelings who abandon the sheep and thieves who come to destroy are said with those men in the room."}]}, "John 11:23–27": {"title": "\"I am the resurrection and the life\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Martha has correct doctrine and a dead brother, and Jesus moves the thing she believes from a date to a Person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your brother will rise again.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deliberately ambiguous. He means in about twenty minutes. She hears something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I know he will rise again, **in the resurrection on the last day**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not wrong. That was standard Jewish belief and Jesus taught it Himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But she has taken a promise about *now* and filed it under a date far enough away to be no comfort at all. Her brother has been dead four days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am** the resurrection and the life.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *there will be* a resurrection. **I am** it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes an event she was waiting for and identifies it as Himself, standing in front of her, in a village outside Jerusalem, on a Thursday."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the fifth of the seven *I am* sayings in John, and the only one spoken at a graveside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever believes in Me **will live, even if he dies**. And everyone who is alive and believes in Me **will never die at all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different people are described."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first has died — and lives anyway. Physical death happens and does not terminate anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is alive and believing, and of him the Greek is emphatic: *ou me apothane eis ton aiona* — a double negative, shall never ever die, unto the age."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever death is for a believer in that sentence, it is not the thing the word normally means. The New Testament's habit of calling it **sleep** — *our friend Lazarus sleeps*, four verses of this chapter earlier — follows from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you believe this?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts it directly, to a grieving woman, before doing anything about the grave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yes, Lord. I believe that You are the Messiah, the Son of God, the One who was to come into the world.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not answer the question He asked. She answers a bigger one — not *do you believe this claim* but *I believe who You are*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her confession is nearly word for word Peter's at Caesarea Philippi, and John puts it in the mouth of a woman in a village, mid-funeral."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weeps. Then *Lazarus, come forth.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why He waited.** John records it plainly and it is uncomfortable: *when He heard that he was sick, **He stayed two more days** in the place where He was.* And to the disciples: *Lazarus is dead. And I am glad for your sakes that I was not there, that you may believe.*"}]}, "John 11:33–37": {"title": "\"Jesus wept\" — and the word before it that is stronger", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The shortest verse in the Bible, and the sentence before it is the one that carries the weight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Something moved deep in His spirit, and He was shaken\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two Greek verbs, and neither is gentle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first, **embrimaomai**, is used in classical Greek of a horse snorting. Where it appears elsewhere in the Gospels it describes a stern, forceful reaction. It is not sorrow — it is closer to indignation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second, **tarasso**, means to stir up, agitate, throw into turmoil. It is what John uses of water being churned, and of Jesus' own soul later in the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So before the tears there is anger, and it is anger at something. He is standing in front of death with the man's sisters weeping beside Him, and He is about to undo it in ten minutes' time. The reaction is not grief at a loss He cannot fix. It is fury at the thing itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the tears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus wept.* The verb is different from the one used of the crowd — theirs is loud, ritual wailing; His is quiet weeping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He cries at a funeral He is about to interrupt. Nothing about the outcome required it. The tears are simply what He does in the presence of what death does to people He loves."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two reactions in the crowd.** *Look how He loved him* — and, from others, *could He not have kept this man from dying?* John reports both without picking one. The second is the sharper question and it is left standing in the text, as it usually is in life. The answer, when it comes four verses later, is not an explanation. It is Lazarus walking out."}]}, "John 14:25–27": {"title": "The Helper's job description, and a peace unlike the world's", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is hours from arrest, and He spends the time telling frightened men what will happen after He is gone. Two promises, and both are narrower and more practical than they are usually quoted as."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Helper is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Parakletos** — from *para*, alongside, and *kaleo*, to call. One called to your side. In ordinary Greek it was used of an advocate: the person who stands with you when you are in trouble, and speaks for you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "English has never found a single word for it. *Comforter* is too soft, *Counsellor* too clinical, *Advocate* too legal. The picture underneath all of them is someone summoned to stand next to you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things He is said to do here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is unusually specific, and both jobs point in the same direction:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**teach you everything**", "**bring back to your mind everything I have said to you**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are about the words of Jesus. Not new material of the Spirit's own — He teaches what Jesus taught, and He reminds them of what Jesus said. Two chapters later Jesus makes it explicit: the Spirit will not speak on His own, and He will glorify Christ rather than Himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a useful measure. Something that pulls attention away from Christ and onto itself is not doing the work described here."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "A promise to men who were about to forget"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is something quietly kind in the second job. These are the men who would scatter that night. Peter is hours from denying he knows Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not say *remember everything I told you*. He says Someone will bring it back to their minds. He is making arrangements for their forgetfulness before it happens, without mentioning it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not the way the world gives\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peace I leave with you; My peace I give to you. I do not give to you the way the world gives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The world's peace is conditional and it is mostly the absence of something — quiet while nothing is going wrong, safety while the money holds. It is also given in a particular way: with expectation attached, and withdrawn when circumstances change."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus distinguishes His gift on *both* counts. It is a different peace, and it is handed over differently. And He says it in a room where nothing outward was about to improve for any of them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"do not let your hearts be troubled.\"** It sounds like an instruction to manage a feeling, which nobody can do on command. In the sentence it comes attached to a gift just handed over — peace given, then the instruction. He is not asking them to generate calm. He is telling them what they now have to settle themselves with."}]}, "John 14:8–11": {"title": "\"Show us the Father\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Philip asks for one thing that would settle everything, and the answer is that he has been looking at it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, show us the Father, and that will be enough for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a foolish thing to want. Moses asked to see God's glory. Philip is asking for the same and saying it would be sufficient."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pathos is in *that will be enough* — a man who thinks one more thing would do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have I been with you all this time, Philip, and you still do not know Me? Anyone who has seen Me has seen the Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three years of walking, eating and watching. Jesus is not surprised at the question so much as at who is asking it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the claim is unqualified: seeing Him **is** seeing the Father. Not a resemblance, not a good representation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two grounds He offers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The words I speak to you I do not speak on My own; it is the Father, living in Me, who is doing His work.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *believe Me when I tell you… or else believe because of the works themselves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives them two ways in — take His word for it, or reason back from what they have watched Him do. He does not insist on the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this settles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything a person guesses about God's character has to be checked against this face. If Jesus is what God looks like, then the disposition on display in the Gospels — toward lepers, tax collectors, the bent-over woman, the thief on the cross — is the Father's disposition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is nothing behind Jesus that is different from Jesus."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter opens.** *Do not let your hearts be troubled… I am going to prepare a place for you.* Philip's question comes minutes later, on the same night, from a man who has just been told the way and asked where it goes. The whole conversation is Jesus explaining Himself to people about to lose Him."}]}, "John 15:5–8": {"title": "\"I am the vine; you are the branches\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last of the seven *I am* sayings in John, spoken on the way to Gethsemane."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a branch does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am the vine; **you are the branches**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A branch does not manufacture fruit. It does not decide to produce grapes, work at producing grapes, or know how a grape is made."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is attached, and the sap does the rest. Everything the fruit is made of comes up through the vine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whoever remains in Me**, and I in him, is the one who bears much fruit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meno** — to remain, to stay, to abide. It occurs eleven times in this chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not work, not strive, not produce. Stay where you are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **apart from Me you can do nothing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *very little*. Nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is an absolute, and He states it as the reason the previous sentence is true."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which vine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had been called a vine before. Isaiah 5: God planted a vineyard, cleared the stones, put in the choicest vine — *and He looked that it should bring forth grapes, and it brought forth wild grapes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 80: *You brought a vine out of Egypt.* Jeremiah: *I planted you a noble vine… how then have you turned into the degenerate plant of an alien vine?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time, the vine fails."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, in an upper room: *I am the **true** vine.* The Greek is genuine, real, the authentic one — the vine the others were pictures of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says about the asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you remain in Me, and **My words remain in you**, you may ask whatever you want, and it will be done for you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The condition is not a formula. It is remaining, and His words remaining in you — and out of that, the asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Jacob's line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will tie his donkey to the vine, his colt to the choicest branch. He will wash his garments in wine.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob said it over Judah on a deathbed in Egypt. Sixteen hundred years later a Man of Judah's tribe sat in an upper room in Jerusalem, on the night of a cup, and said *I am the vine.* See the note on Genesis 49:8–12."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says the fruit is for.** *This is how **My Father** is glorified: that you bear much fruit.* The branch is not producing to prove anything about itself. The whole crop goes to the vinedresser."}]}, "John 16:12–15": {"title": "A Spirit who does not speak about Himself", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The clearest thing Jesus ever says about how the Spirit works, and it is almost entirely about restraint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus withholds, and why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is much more I have to say to you, but you are not able to carry it now.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is about bearing a weight. He is not keeping anything back for the sake of mystery; He is not loading men who are already at their limit. It is the night before the arrest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That sets up everything after it. The Spirit's coming is described first as the means by which the rest arrives — later, when they can carry it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things He does not do"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**He will not speak on His own** — He speaks what He hears", "**He will glorify Me** — not Himself", "**He will take what is Mine and make it known**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says the second one outright: the Spirit's work is to make Christ look like what He is. That is a usable test. Something that draws attention to itself, or to the person carrying it, is not doing what is described here."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"Everything the Father has is Mine.\"** Jesus adds this as the explanation for why the Spirit taking what is His counts as taking from the Father too. In four lines all three are named and their work is shown to be one work — the Father's possession, the Son's to give, the Spirit's to deliver. It is one of the plainest trinitarian sentences in the Gospels, and it arrives as an aside."}]}, "John 16:23–24": {"title": "\"Until now you have not asked for anything in My name\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus tells the disciples that something is about to change in how they pray, and He is precise about what."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What changes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On that day you will not ask Me anything.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "For three years they had asked Him directly — He was standing there. After the cross and the ascension the arrangement alters: *whatever you ask the Father in My name, He will give you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a downgrade. The request now goes to the Father, and it carries the Son's name on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In My name\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a formula appended to the end of a prayer. A name in that world meant authority to act on someone's behalf — the way a servant buying at market in his master's name was doing the master's business with the master's credit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So praying in His name is asking for what He would ask for, backed by who He is rather than by who we are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that stings a little"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until now you have not asked for anything in My name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been with Him the whole time and had never once done this. It was not possible yet — the door He is describing opens at the resurrection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it without reproach. It is a statement about timing, and the next words are an invitation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ask, and you will receive, **so that your joy may be full**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated purpose of answered prayer here is not efficiency or provision. It is joy — filled up, brought to the brim."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it sits.** This is in the middle of the last conversation before Gethsemane, in a passage about sorrow turning to joy like a woman after childbirth. He is preparing men for the worst night of their lives by telling them how they will pray afterwards."}]}, "John 17:1–5": {"title": "\"This is eternal life\" — the definition Jesus gives", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "In the middle of the longest prayer of His recorded, Jesus stops to define a term, and the definition is not what most people would guess."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The definition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And this is eternal life: that they know You, the only true God, and Jesus Christ, whom You sent.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not endless duration. Not a destination. **Knowing a person** — and the Greek verb is the ordinary one for knowing by acquaintance and experience, not by information."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Duration is real and John says so elsewhere. But when Jesus supplies the definition Himself, He puts the emphasis on relationship rather than length."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The hour has come\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "John has been counting down to this since chapter 2, where Jesus tells His mother His hour has *not* come. It is said again in chapter 7, and again in chapter 8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it arrives, and what it means is the cross. The prayer that follows is spoken by a man who knows what the next eighteen hours contain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I have finished the work\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said before the arrest. The verb is the same root as *it is finished* on the cross — completed, brought to its end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks of the work as done while the hardest part is still ahead, which is consistent with everything else in John: the crucifixion is described throughout as a lifting up and a glorifying, not as a defeat that gets reversed later."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The request.** *Glorify Me in Your own presence with the glory I had with You before the world began.* He does not ask for something new. He asks for the return of something He had and set down — which is what Paul describes in Philippians 2 as emptying Himself. The prayer assumes a life before Bethlehem."}]}, "John 17:9–12": {"title": "\"Not one of them is lost\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus prays for a specific group of people, and the way He describes them is worth reading slowly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ones You have given Me, because they are Yours… Everything that is Mine is Yours, and everything Yours is Mine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are described as a gift from the Father to the Son, and the ownership is mutual. Nothing in the passage describes them as having attached themselves to Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four times in these verses they are *the ones You have given Me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Holy Father, keep them safe by Your own name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not by their vigilance. By the Father's name — the same name Jesus says He had been keeping them safe by while He was with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request is for continuity: I am leaving, You keep doing what I have been doing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those You gave Me I guarded, and not one of them is lost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is hours from the cross, giving an account of the men He was given — men who are about to scatter, one of whom will deny Him three times before morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He counts Peter among the not-lost while knowing exactly what Peter is going to do that night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Except the one doomed to be lost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not soften it and neither should any reading of it. Judas is named as an exception, and the sentence attaches it to Scripture being fulfilled rather than to a failure of keeping."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why it matters that He prayed it out loud.** The disciples were in the room. Jesus could have prayed this privately; John records it because it was said in front of them. Men who were about to fail badly heard themselves described as given, guarded, and kept — before the failing happened."}]}, "John 19:28–30": {"title": "\"It is finished\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three words in English, one in Greek, and it was a word people used every day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Tetelestai"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is **teleo** — to complete, to bring to its end. In the perfect tense: finished, and the result stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It turns up on business documents of the period stamped across a bill: **paid in full**. It was used of a task discharged, a debt settled, an obligation met."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *I am finished*. **It** is finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John says was finished"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that everything had now been brought to completion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it before the word is spoken, and he uses the same root. The completion is stated as an accomplished fact, and the shout is Jesus announcing it rather than discovering it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hyssop"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They soaked a sponge in it, put it on a stalk of hyssop, and lifted it to His mouth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only John mentions hyssop, and it is a strange plant for the job — a small bushy herb, not an obvious tool for reaching a man on a cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the plant Israel was told to use to brush the lamb's blood on the doorposts at Passover, and the plant used in the cleansing rituals of Leviticus. John is writing his Gospel around a Passover, and he puts hyssop at the mouth of the Lamb of God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Gave up His spirit\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is active. John does not say He died; he says He handed it over — the same claim Jesus made in chapter 10: *no one takes it from Me. I lay it down of My own accord.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He was thirsty for.** John notes it was said *so that the Scripture would come true* — Psalm 69, in a psalm of suffering. The last thing recorded before the shout is a prophecy being deliberately completed. Even the thirst is part of what was being finished."}]}, "John 2:1–5": {"title": "\"They have no more wine\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first sign in John's Gospel happens at a party, and it starts with an embarrassment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What running out meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jewish wedding feast could last a week, and providing for the guests was the family's responsibility. Running out of wine was not a catering inconvenience — it was a public humiliation the village would remember for years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody was in danger. Nobody was ill. The need was social, and Mary brings it to Him anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They have no more wine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the entire request. No instruction, no suggestion, no argument about why He should act. She states the situation and stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Dear woman, what does this have to do with you and Me? My hour has not come yet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It reads sharply in English. The address is not harsh in Greek — it is the same word He uses to her from the cross. The idiom behind *what does this have to do with you and Me* is a way of saying the matter is not ours to be involved in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *My hour*, in John, always means the cross. He is saying the time for public disclosure has not arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His mother turned to the servants and said, \"Whatever He tells you, do it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not argue with Him and she does not withdraw the request. She hands the matter over and instructs the staff to obey whatever comes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the last recorded words of Mary in Scripture, and they are about doing what He says."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He then makes.** Water in six stone jars kept for ceremonial washing — the vessels of purification rites — turned into the best wine of the feast. The master of the banquet says the good wine has been saved till last, and has no idea where it came from. Only the servants who filled the jars knew."}]}, "John 21:1–3": {"title": "A night with nothing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "After the resurrection, seven men go back to work, and it is the same lake and the same result as the first time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By the **Sea of Tiberias*** — the Sea of Galilee, in the territory of Zebulun and Naphtali."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zebulun's blessing was *a harbour for ships*, and Zebulun's name means dwelling. He had lived there, taught there, and done most of His work on that shoreline. See the note on Genesis 49:13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Simon Peter, Thomas, Nathanael, the sons of Zebedee, and two others.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven of them. Peter first, and Thomas next — the one who had said he would not believe unless he put his hand in the side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I am going fishing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words in the Greek, and John gives no explanation for them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the trade Peter left when he pulled two boats onto the shore, full of fish, and walked away from the catch of his life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They said to him, \"We will come with you.\"* Nobody argues."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All that night they caught nothing at all\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same lake, the same trade, the same night hours, the same empty nets."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is exactly what Luke 5 recorded before any of them followed Him: *we worked hard all night long and came up with nothing at all.* See the note on Luke 5:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three years of walking with Him, and the fishing goes the same way it always did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is waiting on the beach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the morning had now come, Jesus stood on the shore; yet the disciples did not know that it was Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks whether they have any food, tells them which side to cast on, and the net comes up with a hundred and fifty-three fish and does not break."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when they had come to land, they saw a **fire of coals** there, and **fish laid on it, and bread**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had breakfast cooking before they got there. He did not need their catch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charcoal fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word for it, **anthrakia**, occurs twice in the whole New Testament, both in John."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other one is in the high priest's courtyard, where *the servants and officers had made a fire of coals… and Peter stood with them and warmed himself*, and denied Him three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's restoration happens over the same kind of fire, and he is asked three questions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He handled that morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The work — a net so full they could not haul it in. The body — bread and fish already on the coals. And the thing Peter could not say out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three, and He did not raise the third until the man had eaten."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does not mention.** The denial. Not once. He asks *do you love Me?* three times and never says why He is asking three times."}]}, "John 21:20–23": {"title": "\"What is that to you?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter has just been restored — three questions, three answers, and a commission — and then he immediately looks over his shoulder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, and what about this man?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus has just told Peter how he will die. Peter's response is to point at somebody else and ask what their arrangement is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is such an ordinary reaction that John, who was the man being pointed at, has no interest in softening it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If I want him to remain until I come, what is that to you? You follow Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last two words are emphatic in Greek — *you*, as opposed to him. Jesus does not tell Peter what will happen to John, and He does not rebuke the curiosity at length. He redirects it in four words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction Peter is given is the same one he was given at the beginning, on the same shore, three years earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction John adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the word went around that this disciple would not die. But Jesus had not said that.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John is writing decades later, presumably as the last of them alive, and he stops to correct a rumour that had grown up around a conditional sentence. *If I want* had become *he will*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a small, careful act of honesty, and it tells you something about the writer: given the chance to leave a flattering story standing, he prints the exact words instead."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it leaves the reader.** Each of them gets a different road — one martyred young, one apparently growing old — and neither is told the other's. The only instruction that fits both is the one Jesus gives: *you follow Me.*"}]}, "John 3:14–18": {"title": "The snake on the pole, and why it is attached to the famous verse", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John 3:16 almost never gets quoted with the sentence before it, and that sentence is the reason it says what it says."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison Jesus chose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as Moses lifted up the snake in the wilderness, so the Son of Man must be lifted up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The story is in Numbers 21. The people are dying of snakebite, and God's remedy is not to remove the snakes. It is a bronze snake on a pole — an image of the very thing killing them, raised up where everyone can see it. Anyone who looked at it lived."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They did not have to understand it, deserve it, or do anything but look."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the picture Jesus reaches for to describe His own death, and the verse everybody knows follows immediately from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Lifted up\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **hypsoo** means to raise up, and also to exalt. John uses it three times in this Gospel of the crucifixion, and each time both senses are live at once: lifted onto a cross, and lifted to glory. The word will not let you keep the two apart."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the famous verse actually claims"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For God loved the world so much that He gave His only Son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The love comes first and the giving follows from it. That order matters, because the sentence is often heard the other way round — as though the cross persuaded a reluctant God to love. John says the love was the reason for the gift."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the scope is *the world* — the same word John uses elsewhere for the world that does not know Him. Not the deserving part of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verses nobody quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God did not send His Son into the world to condemn the world, but so that the world might be rescued through Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is verse 17, and it exists because verse 16 raises the question. Jesus states the purpose negatively and positively in the same breath: not to condemn; to rescue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then verse 18 draws the line: whoever believes is not condemned; whoever does not *stands condemned already*. Not *will be* — already. The condemnation is the situation being rescued from, not a punishment for refusing the rescue."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the snake, once more.** Israel kept that bronze snake for centuries and eventually started burning incense to it, until Hezekiah broke it up. Even the right symbol becomes a problem when people look at the object instead of what it points to."}]}, "John 4:1–6": {"title": "Through Samaria", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six verses of travel arrangements, and every detail in them is load-bearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"His road took Him through Samaria\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *He had to pass through Samaria* — it was necessary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Geographically it was the direct route, and Jews going north routinely crossed the Jordan to avoid it rather than set foot in that country. The necessity in John's sentence is not about mileage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jews have no dealings with Samaritans*, John says a few verses later, and his disciples *marvelled that He talked with a woman.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two walls in one conversation, and He goes through both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul names it in Ephesians: *He has broken down the middle wall of partition.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose ground it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Near **the piece of ground Jacob had given to his son Joseph**, and **Jacob's well** was there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John names the owner. This is Joseph's portion, and Jacob's blessing over Joseph was:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph is a fruitful vine, **a fruitful vine beside a spring**, whose branches **climb over a wall**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A spring, and branches over a wall. He is sitting at the well on the land, and the wall He crosses is the one He is about to cross with a Samaritan woman. See the note on Genesis 49:22–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Worn out\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus was **worn out from the journey**, and He sat down **just as He was** beside the well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *worn out* is to labour to the point of exhaustion. John, who opens his Gospel with *the Word was God*, writes this six verses into chapter 4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as He was* — no ceremony, nothing arranged. He sat down the way a tired man sits down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sixth hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Noon. The hottest part of the day, and the wrong time to draw water — the women came at evening, together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She comes alone at midday because of what her life is, and He is there, tired, at exactly that hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks her for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Give Me a drink.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing He says to her is a request. He has no bucket and the well is deep, and He opens the conversation from a position of need."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the end of the chapter she has left her water jar, gone into the town, and brought it out to meet Him. See the note on John 4:10–15."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He tells the disciples when they come back.** They had gone to buy food and He would not eat. *My food is to do the will of Him who sent Me.* Then: *lift up your eyes and look at the fields, for they are already white for harvest.* He is looking at a Samaritan town walking toward Him."}]}, "John 4:10–15": {"title": "\"If you knew the gift\" — a conversation He starts by asking for help", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus opens by asking a Samaritan woman for a drink, which is the least likely sentence in the chapter. He is thirsty and has nothing to draw with. The whole exchange runs from a position of need."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"If you knew the gift God is holding out\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things she does not know: *what* is being offered and *who* is doing the offering. He names both as missing, then says what she would do if she had them — *you would have asked Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not qualified, not prepared. Asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Living water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In ordinary speech *living water* meant running water — a spring or a stream, as opposed to still water in a cistern. Her reply takes it that way, which is why she points out He has no bucket and the well is deep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not being obtuse. She is answering the plain sense of the words, and John lets the misunderstanding stand for four verses because the second meaning arrives better after the first has been exhausted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He promises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who drinks this water will be thirsty again.* True of the well, and true of everything else that has to be gone back to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The water I give will become a spring inside him.* The location changes. Not a better well to keep returning to — a source relocated inside the person, welling up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Her answer.** *Give me this water, so that I will not be thirsty and will not have to keep coming all this way to draw.* She is still thinking about the walk, and He does not correct her or make her understand first. The next thing He says is about her husband, and the conversation goes somewhere else entirely. He answers people mid-misunderstanding."}]}, "John 4:46–50": {"title": "\"The man took Jesus at His word and started for home\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A court official walks about twenty miles uphill to ask Jesus to come back down with him, and goes home alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He begged Him to come down and heal his son, who was at the point of death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A specific request with a method attached: come, and heal. The verb for *begged* is continuous — he kept asking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Capernaum is on the lake shore; Cana is up in the hills. He had made a hard journey with a dying child behind him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Unless you people see signs and wonders, you simply will not believe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is plural — *you people* — so it is not aimed only at this father. Jesus is saying something about the crowd's appetite for spectacle before He does anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man does not defend himself. He says: *Sir, please — come down before my little boy dies.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is given instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go on home. Your son lives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what he asked for. He wanted a journey; he gets a sentence. And the tense of *lives* is present — not *will live*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man took Jesus at His word and started for home.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the hinge of the story. He had come for a person and left with a sentence, and he turned around and walked back down the hill on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verses have his servants meeting him on the road with the news, and the fever leaving at the exact hour Jesus spoke. John notes that the man worked it out and then *he himself believed, and his whole household.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Twice at Cana.** John points out that this is where the water became wine. Both signs are at the same village, and in both the person who acts on His word before seeing anything — the servants filling jars, and this father walking home — is the one who knows what happened."}]}, "John 5:24–27": {"title": "\"Has crossed over\" — the tense that settles it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three statements, stacked, and each one is stronger than the last. Jesus is not building toward a promise here; He is closing a question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Read the three parts"}, {"k": "note", "t": "whoever hears My word and trusts the One who sent Me **has** eternal life. He **does not come up** for condemnation. He **has already crossed over** out of death and into life."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*has* — present tense. Not will have.", "*does not come up for condemnation* — the word is the courtroom one. He is not saying the verdict will be favourable. He is saying the person does not appear on that docket.", "*has already crossed over* — and this is the one that carries the weight."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense in that last phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is in the perfect. English does not have a good single word for it: it means a thing was done at a point in the past and the state it produced is still the case. Not \"crossed over and we shall see,\" but crossed, and standing on the other bank now."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same tense as *it is finished* on the cross. A completed action whose result stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sentence is not describing something a person is working toward or could slip back out of. It describes a border already behind them."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is conditioned on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Worth being exact, because the condition is stated and it is short: *hears My word and trusts the One who sent Me.* Hearing and trusting. There is no third item, and nothing about sustaining it by effort."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole entry requirement in a sentence that then hands over eternal life, immunity from the courtroom, and a completed crossing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strange middle of the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Jesus says a time is coming *and it is here now* when the dead hear His voice and live. Both at once — future and present in the same breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He means it in both senses, and John's Gospel goes on to show it: Lazarus called out of a tomb, and people all through the book crossing from death to life while still walking around. The resurrection at the end is not a different event from what happens when someone hears Him now. It is the same voice."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why this passage gets used the way it does.** People reach for it when they are afraid of losing what they have. It answers that fear, but notice how — not by promising to hold on tightly enough, but by pointing out that the crossing is in the past tense. The question the verse settles is not *will I make it*. It is *where am I standing*, and it answers: on this side already."}]}, "John 6:10–13": {"title": "Twelve baskets of leftovers", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only miracle in all four Gospels, and John keeps a detail the others share and nobody quite explains."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As much as they wanted\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a ration. John says the fish were handed out *as much as they wanted*, and that everyone *ate their fill*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for eating their fill is a strong one — it was used of feeding animals to satisfaction. Five thousand men, plus the women and children the other accounts mention, all eating until they stopped wanting more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gather up the pieces that are left over, so that nothing is wasted.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the sentence worth stopping on. The man who has just produced an unlimited supply out of five loaves tells His disciples to pick up the crumbs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abundance and waste are treated as different things. The miracle does not make the fragments unimportant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Twelve baskets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They end with more bread than they started with, and the number is exact — twelve, one for each disciple. The men who had said *what are five loaves among so many* each end the day carrying a full basket."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not comment on that either. He just records the number."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What happens next.** The crowd tries to make Him king by force, and He withdraws to a mountain alone. The following morning He tells them they are looking for Him because they ate the loaves, and then calls Himself the bread of life — a claim so hard that most of them stop following Him by the end of the chapter. The feeding is the beginning of a much longer argument."}]}, "John 6:35–40": {"title": "\"I will never turn away\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of the seven great *I am* sayings in John, and what follows it is a run of promises with nothing conditional in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am the bread of life. Whoever comes to Me will never be hungry, and whoever believes in Me will never be thirsty again.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread and thirst in one sentence — He answers a need He was not asked about. The crowd had come looking for another meal after the five thousand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The double negative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who comes to Me I will never turn away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek stacks two negatives, which in that language intensifies rather than cancels: I will certainly not, under any circumstances, cast out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the verb is *ekballo* — to throw out, put outside. It is the word used of driving out demons and of expelling someone from a synagogue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else is uncertain, the person who comes is not going to be shown the door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Twice, the same will"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus states the Father's will twice in three verses, and both times it is about keeping:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*that of everything He has given Me I should **lose none of it**, but raise it up on the last day*", "*that everyone who looks to the Son and believes in Him should have eternal life, and I will raise him up*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for *lose* is the one used of a lost sheep and a lost coin. He describes His own commission as not losing anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone the Father gives Me will come to Me, and the one who comes to Me I will never turn away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are in the sentence and John does not resolve the tension. The giving comes first; the coming is real; and the welcome is unconditional."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The repeated line.** *I will raise him up on the last day* appears four times in this chapter. Whatever the bread of life turns out to mean, Jesus keeps attaching it to resurrection rather than to sustenance."}]}, "John 6:47–51": {"title": "\"The bread I will give is My flesh\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus compares Himself to manna and then says the comparison does not go far enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manna argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your ancestors ate manna in the wilderness, and **they died**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd had raised the manna themselves, as a credential — Moses gave our fathers bread from heaven. Jesus accepts the fact and points at the graves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wilderness generation ate the miracle bread every morning for forty years and is buried in the desert. Whatever the manna did, it did not deal with death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the bread that comes down from heaven, so that a person may eat it and not die. I am the living bread.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The manna came down and was gathered. This bread is described as **living** — alive in itself, not merely supplied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the tense at the end is future: *the bread I **will** give is My flesh, and I will give it for the life of the world.* He is pointing forward to the cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the crowd could not take it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verses have them arguing among themselves — *how can this man give us his flesh to eat?* — and many disciples turn back before the chapter ends."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not soften it or explain it away. He repeats it, harder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb He uses in the following verses is a blunt one — the ordinary word for chewing. Whatever else the language does, it insists on something taken in personally rather than admired from outside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Manna had to be gathered by each household, every morning, and eaten. Nobody could gather it for you."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter ends.** *The words I have spoken to you are spirit and they are life.* Jesus Himself supplies the interpretive key after the hard sayings — and then asks the twelve whether they want to leave too. Peter's answer is about having nowhere else to go."}]}, "John 7:37–39": {"title": "Rivers of living water", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He waits until the last day of the feast, and then He shouts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day He chose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the **last day of the festival, the great day**, Jesus **stood up and called out in a loud voice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Feast of Tabernacles ran seven days, and every morning a priest carried a golden pitcher down to the pool of Siloam, filled it, and poured it out at the base of the altar while the people sang from Isaiah 12 — *with joy you will draw water from the wells of salvation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "By most accounts the pouring did not happen on the eighth day. The pitcher stayed empty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into that silence, a man stands up in the temple court and says: if anyone is thirsty, come to Me."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Let him come to Me and drink\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and both are simple. Not qualify, not prepare. Come, and drink."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The invitation is addressed to a condition — *if anyone is **thirsty*** — and thirst is not an achievement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the rivers come out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of his **innermost being** will flow **rivers** of living water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a trickle and not a cistern. **Rivers**, plural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *out of his belly* — the deepest inside of a person, the part nothing reaches."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And note the direction. He drinks, and what comes out of him is more than he took in, and it comes out for other people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As the Scripture has said\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not tell us which Scripture, and no single verse matches word for word. The picture runs through the prophets — water from the rock in the wilderness, Ezekiel's river out from under the temple threshold, Zechariah's living waters going out from Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is drawing on a whole current, not quoting a line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "John's own footnote"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was speaking about the Spirit, whom those who believed in Him were going to receive; for the Holy Spirit had not yet been given, **because Jesus had not yet been glorified**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John, writing decades later, explains it for us. The Spirit waits on the cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the sequence is stated plainly: glorified first, then given. What was announced in a temple courtyard at a feast arrived in an upper room at Pentecost."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the water had been coming from.** The pool of Siloam — the same pool Jesus sends the blind man to wash in two chapters later. John keeps his images and comes back to them."}]}, "John 8:31–36": {"title": "\"We have never been anyone's slaves\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted line in this passage — *the truth will set you free* — is attached to a condition on one side and an argument on the other, and both get cut off when it is quoted alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you hold to My word, then you really are My disciples. And you will know the truth…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb means to remain, to stay put, to keep living somewhere. Freedom is promised to people who stay in what He said, not to people who encounter it once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim that gives the game away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are Abraham's descendants, and we have never been anyone's slaves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were speaking in a country under Roman occupation, as descendants of people who had been slaves in Egypt, and after Babylon. The sentence is historically false three times over, and they say it without blinking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole diagnosis. A person can be quite certain they are free while standing inside the thing that holds them, and the certainty is part of the condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says the slavery is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who commits sin is a slave of sin.* Not \"does wrong things occasionally\" — the construction is about a settled practice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the family image: *a slave does not stay in the house forever, but a son belongs there forever*. Slaves in a household could be sold; sons could not be un-sonned. He is contrasting two kinds of belonging, and only one of them is permanent."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"Free indeed.\"** The word is *ontos* — really, actually, in fact. Jesus adds it because He has just been told by the people in front of Him that they were free already. The adverb is aimed at that claim: not free in the way you think you are. Free in fact."}]}, "John 8:57–59": {"title": "\"Before Abraham was, I AM\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The shortest sentence He ever said about Himself, and the crowd reached for stones before He finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are not yet fifty years old**, and You have seen Abraham?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had just said *Abraham rejoiced to see My day, and he saw it and was glad.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their objection is about arithmetic. Two thousand years and one lifetime do not overlap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I tell you the truth, **before Abraham was, I am**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tenses do not agree, and that is the whole sentence. Not *I was*. Not *I existed*. **I am.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before Abraham came into being — the Greek verb is one of becoming, of coming to exist — I **am**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ego eimi.* It is the Greek of Exodus 3, where God tells Moses to say *I AM has sent me to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name given at the burning bush, in the present tense, applied to Himself in the temple courts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Exodus 3:13–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At that they **picked up stones** to throw at Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was no misunderstanding. Stoning was the penalty for blasphemy, and nobody in that court had to have it explained."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's earlier claims — that He was sent, that He does the Father's will, that they would die in their sins — had not produced this. This did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven \"I am\"s in John"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread of life. Light of the world. The door. The good shepherd. The resurrection and the life. The way, the truth and the life. The true vine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven with something after them, and this one, with nothing after it at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He walked into"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Jesus **hid Himself and went out of the temple, passing right through the middle of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Through the crowd holding the stones. John does not explain it and does not comment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the same in Luke 4, when Nazareth takes Him to the brow of the hill to throw Him off — *passing through the midst of them, He went His way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They could not lay a hand on Him until the night He let them, and then in the garden, when the soldiers said they were looking for Jesus of Nazareth and He answered *I am*, they *drew back and fell to the ground.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The name and what it does not specify.** *I AM WHO I AM* — the sentence has no predicate. It is not *I am strong* or *I am merciful*. It is the verb standing alone, and whatever the need in front of it, the name is large enough."}]}, "John 9:24–27": {"title": "\"One thing I do know\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man who has been blind from birth is interrogated twice by the religious authorities, and he wins."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they want him to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Give the glory to God.** We know this man is a sinner.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a legal formula — an oath, roughly *tell the truth before God*. And they attach the verdict they want in the same breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not asking him a question. They are telling him what the answer is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he will not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whether He is a sinner, I do not know.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He declines to go past what he has. He is not a theologian and does not pretend to be one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does have"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**One thing I do know: I was blind, and now I see.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no argument against that sentence. They can dispute the doctrine; they cannot get at the fact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was blind on that street every day of his life — the neighbours in this chapter argue about whether it is even the same man. And he is standing there looking at them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second interrogation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What did He do to you? How did He open your eyes?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask him to go through it again. They had already heard it — verse 15 records the first telling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are looking for a discrepancy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have already told you, **and you would not listen**. Why do you want to hear it again? **Do you want to become His disciples too?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A beggar, unschooled, being examined by the Sanhedrin, and he asks them if they are signing up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the funniest line in John's Gospel and it is delivered by a man with everything to lose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they threw him out.* Excommunicated — put out of the synagogue, which in that world meant out of the community."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His own parents had already backed away: *he is of age, ask him*, because *they feared the Jews.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who came looking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus heard that they had cast him out, and **when He had found him**, He said, \"Do you believe in the Son of God?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes and finds him. The man had never seen His face — he had been sent to the pool to wash while still blind, and had not laid eyes on Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, I believe.\" And he worshipped Him.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says about the whole thing.** *For judgement I have come into this world, that those who do not see may see, and that those who see may be made blind.* The Pharisees ask whether He means them, and He answers: *if you were blind, you would have no sin. But now you say, \"We see.\" Therefore your sin remains.*"}]}, "Joshua 1:6–9": {"title": "What \"meditate day and night\" meant to a soldier", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famous line here is about turning God's words over in your mind day and night. It is worth knowing who was told to do it and what he was about to do, because it was not a quiet man in a quiet room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Hebrew word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hagah.* It is not silent reflection. Its ordinary uses are physical and audible — a lion growling over its prey, a dove moaning, a person muttering under their breath. The same word is in Psalm 1, and both times it is paired with *day and night*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is why the instruction here begins with the mouth: *keep the words of this book always on your lips*, and then turn them over. It is closer to chewing than to studying — going over something aloud until it works into you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua, on the day he takes over from Moses, facing a river and a walled city. The book is about a military campaign, and the first thing he is given is not a strategy. It is a way of handling words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in four verses he is told to be strong and courageous, which is the tell: this is a man being steadied, and the muttering of the text is part of how he is steadied."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The promise attached.** *Then your path will open before you, and you will do well.* The Hebrew word behind it means to act with insight and so to succeed — it covers both the wisdom and the outcome. It is not a guarantee of ease; the book that follows has failures in it. It is the promise that a man soaked in what God said will know how to act."}]}, "Joshua 11:21–23": {"title": "\"The land had rest from war\" — and the three cities left standing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The summary at the end of the campaign, and it contains a detail the writer did not have to include."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joshua took the entire land, exactly as the LORD had promised Moses… And the land had rest from war.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is as complete as the book gets. The giants are cut off from the hill country — Hebron, Debir, Anab, the highlands — and *not one of the Anakim was left in the land of Israel*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception, stated in the same breath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Only in Gaza, Gath, and Ashdod did any remain.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three Philistine cities on the coastal plain. The writer records the total victory and the surviving pocket in consecutive sentences, without appearing to notice a problem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It matters later. Gath is where Goliath comes from, four centuries on — a descendant of the people this chapter says were cleared out, living in one of the three cities this chapter says they were not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of claim it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Bible's histories are willing to say *the whole land* and then name what was left, and to do it on the same page. The summary is describing the campaign's outcome — the field armies broken, the hill country taken — not a census."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters later God says outright: *there is still so much land left to possess*. The book contains its own qualification."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The phrase that closes it.** *The land had rest from war* appears twice in Joshua, here and at the end of Caleb's chapter. It is the note the conquest narrative keeps landing on — not conquest as the goal, but rest as the point of it."}]}, "Joshua 13:1–7": {"title": "\"You've grown old, and there is still so much land left\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the bluntest sentences God says to anyone in the Old Testament, and He says it to the man who had just finished the conquest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You've grown old, and there is still so much land left to possess.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are true and neither cancels the other. Two chapters earlier the book said Joshua *took the entire land, exactly as the LORD had promised* — and it meant it, at the level of the campaign. Now God lists what is still out there by name: the Philistine cities, the Geshurites, the Sidonians, Lebanon up to Hermon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The victory was real. The occupation was not finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not rebuke Joshua. There is no suggestion the old man failed, and no call for one more campaign. The instruction that follows is administrative: *divide this land among the nine and a half tribes as their inheritance*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua's job changes. He is to allocate what has been promised, and the taking of it will belong to the tribes and to the years after him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the driving out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I myself will drive them out before my people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is stated in the middle of the list of what remains. The unfinished part is not handed over as a burden — it is handed over with the promise that the work is God's, and the tribes' part is to go and live in what is theirs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The tension the book leaves standing.** Joshua says the land was taken; Judges opens with a long account of what was not. Both are in the canon and neither is edited out. A promise can be entirely given and still not entirely entered, and this chapter is where the Bible says so out loud."}]}, "Joshua 14:6–15": {"title": "Caleb at eighty-five, and the hill he asked for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Forty-five years after the report that cost his generation the land, Caleb walks up and asks for the hardest piece of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic he does out loud"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["forty years old when he was sent to scout", "forty-five years of waiting since", "eighty-five now, *as strong now as the day Moses sent me out*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He counts it publicly. He is not claiming exemption from time; he is saying the promise held through it, and so did he."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a quiet valley. **Hebron** — and he names the problem himself: *You heard yourself that the Anakim are there, giants in great fortified cities.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The giants are the exact reason ten of the twelve scouts had said no, all those years earlier. Caleb asks for the place his generation refused, by name, and tells Joshua the giants are still in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase the chapter keeps repeating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He followed the LORD my God completely* — with a whole heart. It appears three times in these verses, twice from Caleb and once from the narrator, and it is what Moses' oath was hung on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *completely* means to fill up, to make full. Not flawlessly. Fully — nothing held back."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *And the land had rest from war.* The chapter about an eighty-five-year-old asking for a fight ends on rest. Both things are in the same paragraph, and the order matters: the rest comes after the hill country is taken, not instead of it."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Caleb was asking against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read this chapter beside the two around it and the request gets sharper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua 11 says the Anakim were cut off from the hill country and *not one was left in the land of Israel* — with three coastal cities named as the exception. Joshua 13 has God telling Joshua, now old, that *there is still so much land left to possess*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caleb is standing in that gap. The campaign is over, the summary has been written, and there is still a fortified hill country full of giants that nobody has moved into. He does not ask for something new. He asks for the part of the promise that was made to him personally and has been sitting unoccupied for forty-five years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what he did with it after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 15 records the follow-through in one line: *from there Caleb drove out the three sons of Anak* — named, individually — and then went on against Debir."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The asking and the taking are two different chapters, and the second one is short. See the notes on Joshua 11:21–23, Joshua 13:1–7, and Joshua 15:13–19, where his daughter asks him for springs in exactly the same spirit."}]}, "Joshua 15:13–19": {"title": "Achsah, who asked for the springs", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A short domestic scene dropped into the middle of a long list of boundary lines, and it is there because of what a young woman says."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had been given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Land in the Negev — the dry south. It is a real inheritance and it is genuinely hers, and without water it is close to useless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has just described her father taking Hebron off three giants, so nobody in this family is short of nerve. Achsah is her father's daughter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give me a blessing. You've given me land in the dry Negev — now give me springs of water too.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She names the gap precisely. Not more land, and not a complaint about what she received. The specific thing that would make what she already has work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the detail the narrator keeps: *as she climbed down from her donkey*. She dismounts to ask — the posture of someone making a formal request, and the sort of small physical detail the Bible records when someone was actually there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she gets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Caleb gave her the upper springs and the lower springs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both. She asked for springs and he gave her the whole watershed, top and bottom. No hesitation is recorded, and no negotiation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the story is in the book at all.** Judges tells it a second time, almost word for word — which means it was remembered and repeated. In a chapter that is otherwise surveyors' work, someone thought this was worth keeping: a woman who looked at a good gift, saw what it lacked, and asked her father for the rest."}]}, "Joshua 2:12–14": {"title": "\"Kindness and truth\" — the two words she is given", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Rahab asks for a specific thing, in specific words, and the answer comes back in the same vocabulary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have shown you kindness — promise me you will show kindness to my family.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word is **hesed**: the loyal, covenanted, will-not-let-go kind of love that is Israel's central word for how God treats His own. She uses it of what she did, and asks for it back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *give me a sure sign*. She is not content with a promise; she asks for something she can point to. That is what the scarlet cord becomes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We will treat you with kindness and truth* — **hesed and emet**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That pairing runs right through the Old Testament as a description of God's own character: loyal love and dependability, the two together. It is what Moses hears when God passes by him on the mountain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two spies, in a compromised house, in a doomed city, hand a Canaanite prostitute the pair of words that describe what God is like."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the pair turns up again.** John writes that the Word became flesh, *full of grace and truth* — and that grace and truth *came through Jesus Christ*. Greek this time, but the same pairing, and John's readers would have heard the Hebrew behind it. What Rahab was promised on a rooftop is what John says arrived in a person. See the note on John 1:14–18."}]}, "Joshua 2:15–21": {"title": "The scarlet cord, and the terms she agreed to", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman in a doomed city is given a set of conditions, and the conditions are worth reading closely, because every one of them turns up again in how the Bible talks about rescue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three terms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "1. **the cord in the window** — the same rope she let them down by, tied where it can be seen 2. **everyone must be inside her house** — *if anyone steps outside your doors into the street, their blood is on their own head* 3. **say nothing about the arrangement**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The safety is not spread across the city or attached to her personally wherever she goes. It is located: this house, this mark, everyone in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where you have read this before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A household. A doorway. A red mark on the outside. Everyone who stays in is safe, everyone who goes out is not — and the destruction passes through the city regardless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 12 is the same shape, and Rahab's night is the only other place in scripture where the pattern appears whole. Whether she knew that is not the point; the text is built so that the reader does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Rahab's own words, three verses earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I know that the LORD has given you this land.* Everyone in Jericho had heard the same reports about the Red Sea and the two Amorite kings, and the whole city was melting with fear. She is not better informed than her neighbours."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is the only one who acts on it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she was.** The text never softens her trade, and neither do the two New Testament writers who name her — Hebrews lists her among the faithful, James as an example of faith that did something. Matthew puts her in the genealogy of Jesus. Nobody in scripture is embarrassed about Rahab, which is itself worth noticing."}]}, "Joshua 2:8–11": {"title": "What the whole city knew, and what one person did about it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Rahab's speech is the most theologically loaded thing anyone says in the book of Joshua, and it comes from a Canaanite prostitute on a rooftop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "She names the evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We've heard how the LORD dried up the waters of the Red Sea… We've heard what you did to Sihon and Og.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Red Sea was forty years earlier. The two Amorite kings were recent. She has been following the news for a long time, and she has drawn a conclusion from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the city knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Terror of you has fallen on us. Everyone living here is melting with fear… there was no courage left in anyone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the crucial detail. Rahab is not the only person in Jericho with the information. The whole city has it, and the whole city is terrified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She differs from her neighbours in exactly one respect: she acts. The rest of Jericho locks the gates."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line she ends on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD your God — He is God, in the heavens above and on the earth below.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not \"your God is strong.\" It is a statement about which God is God, in both realms, and it is close to the wording Moses uses in Deuteronomy. A woman outside the covenant, in a condemned city, states the central claim of Israel's faith more plainly than most Israelites in the previous forty years managed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Two verses on.** *I know that the LORD has given you this land* — she says *has given*, past tense, before a single stone of the wall has moved. Then she asks for her family. She is not gambling on an outcome; she is negotiating on the far side of one she has already accepted."}]}, "Joshua 6:15–19": {"title": "The one house left standing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The city falls, and the exception is named out loud in the middle of the instruction to destroy it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the exception is placed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The city and everything in it are set apart to the LORD for destruction. Only Rahab and everyone in her house will live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an afterthought once the dust settled. Joshua announces it as part of the command, before the shout, before the wall goes down. The rescue was arranged in advance and spoken publicly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her house was in the wall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 2 mentions it almost in passing: *her house was built into the city wall, so that she lived within the wall itself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means when the wall came down, the one structure that had to stand was the one built into the thing that fell. The text does not comment on it. It simply records the wall collapsing and Rahab surviving, and leaves the reader to put the two facts side by side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because she hid the messengers we sent.* Not because she was Israelite — she was not. Not because she had reformed — the narrative never says so, and both New Testament writers who mention her still call her by her trade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had made a covenant with the people of God, marked her house, and gathered her family inside it. That is the whole of her qualification."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows immediately.** The next paragraph is Achan taking what was devoted to destruction, and the trouble it brings on the whole camp. The chapter puts them together on purpose: an outsider who is spared because she was inside the terms, and an insider who is ruined because he ignored them."}]}, "Judges 1:1–7": {"title": "\"Who should go up first?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book opens with a leaderless nation asking a question, and getting an answer that is already in the past tense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question and the answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joshua was gone now, and the people were unsure where to turn. So they asked the LORD, \"Who should go up first?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer: *Judah will go. I have already handed the land to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the tense. Not *I will hand it over*. The giving is spoken of as done, and the going up is what remains. That order — given first, taken afterwards — runs through the whole conquest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Judah's first move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah does not go alone. He asks Simeon to come along, and offers the same in return: *come up with us into the land that's ours, and we'll fight side by side. Then we'll do the same for you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simeon's allotted territory lay inside Judah's, so the two tribes had a natural reason to work together. Whatever else it is, it is a leader's first act being an invitation rather than a command."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grim episode"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They capture Adoni-Bezek and cut off his thumbs and big toes — mutilations that made a man unable to hold a weapon or run."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he says it himself: *seventy kings with their thumbs and big toes cut off once gathered scraps under my table. Now God has paid me back for what I did.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verdict is in the mouth of the man it falls on. Judges is a violent book and it rarely comments; here it lets the victim of the reprisal describe it as exactly what he had done to others."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the name matters.** *Judah* means praise, and the tribe is sent up first. Genesis had already said the sceptre would not depart from Judah, and the line runs from here to David and to Christ. The book's first move puts that tribe at the front."}]}, "Judges 1:16–21": {"title": "\"The LORD was with Judah\" — and what stopped them anyway", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD was with Judah, and they took the hill country. But they could not drive out those in the valley, for they had iron chariots.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two halves, and the second one does not follow from the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Presence and the chariots"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse says God was with them. Then it explains a failure by pointing at enemy equipment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Those two things cannot both be doing the work. If the LORD was with Judah, then iron was never the deciding factor — and the book itself proves it a few chapters later, when Deborah and Barak face Sisera's **nine hundred** iron chariots and the LORD routs every one of them in a river valley."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same terrain. Same technology. Opposite outcome. What changed was not the metal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Caleb was doing in the same paragraph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As Moses had promised, Hebron was given to Caleb, and he drove out the three sons of Anak from it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An eighty-five-year-old walks into a fortified hill town and clears out giants by name, in the same six verses where a whole tribe stops at flat ground because of chariots."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts them side by side without comment. He does not need to say anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is beginning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read on and the word *but* keeps returning: this tribe did not drive them out, and neither did that one, and neither did the next. Chapter 2 opens with the consequence spelled out — the peoples left behind become a snare."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land had been given. God had said so in the first paragraph of the book: *I have already handed the land to them.* What is being described here is not a promise falling short. It is a promise not fully entered."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the eyes went.** Judah looked at the valley and saw iron. Caleb looked at Hebron and said *if the LORD is with me, I will drive them out, exactly as He said.* One of them was reckoning with the chariots and one with the promise, and the chapter records what each of them ended up with."}]}, "Judges 11:30–31": {"title": "Jephthah's vow — a bargain nobody asked him to make", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the darkest passages in the Old Testament, and the first thing to notice is that God never speaks in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has already happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit of the LORD came upon Jephthah.* That is the verse immediately before this one. He has already been equipped, already commissioned. The outcome is not in doubt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then he makes a vow anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If You will truly give the Ammonites into my hand… then whatever comes out of the door of my house to meet me when I return in peace — it will be the LORD's, and I will offer it up as a burnt offering.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is negotiating for something he has already been given. That is the shape of the tragedy: not too little faith in God's power, but a bargain offered to someone who had already said yes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law he lived under said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Torah is unambiguous. Human sacrifice is named repeatedly as the thing the nations did that God detests, and Leviticus provides a way to redeem a rash vow by payment. A priest could have released him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the text presents the vow as pious or the outcome as required. Judges records it; it does not commend it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the book frames it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges is a downward spiral, and it ends with the line *everyone did what was right in his own eyes*. Jephthah's story sits inside that. The book is describing what happens to a people, not modelling behaviour."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On what became of his daughter.** The Hebrew is genuinely ambiguous, and readers have divided for centuries between a literal sacrifice and a life dedicated in perpetual virginity — the text dwells oddly on her never marrying. The honest position is that we do not know. What the passage does say plainly is that a man bound himself needlessly, and that a child paid for it."}]}, "Judges 14:5–7": {"title": "\"He told no one, not even his parents\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first recorded act of Samson's strength, and it happens where nobody can see it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The anointing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit of the LORD came powerfully upon him, and he tore the lion apart with his bare hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is about rushing or advancing on someone — it is used of an army charging. It comes on Samson four times in his story, and every time something physical follows immediately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note what it is not: not a mood, not an inward sense. Judges reports it as a force arriving and a thing being done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As easily as one might tear a young goat\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is deliberately domestic. Tearing a kid was something a farmhand did without thinking about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the strength was, the narrator describes it as effortless rather than strained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he told no one, not even his parents, what he had done.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence worth stopping on. Samson's whole story is otherwise a catalogue of showing off — riddles, boasts, a secret eventually talked out of him by a woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here, at the start, he says nothing. His parents were walking a little way ahead and never knew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went on and spoke with the woman, and she was right in his eyes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is not presenting Samson as a model. He is on his way to a marriage his parents objected to, in Philistine territory, and the phrase *right in his eyes* is the book's own shorthand for what was wrong with that whole era — *everyone did what was right in his own eyes.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the lion becomes.** He goes back that way later, finds honey in the carcass, and builds a riddle out of it that starts the feud with the Philistines. The private victory turns into the public trouble. Judges keeps reporting both without comment."}]}, "Judges 15:18–20": {"title": "The spring called \"the one who cried out\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Samson has just won a battle single-handed, and the next thing recorded is that he nearly died of thirst."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have given Your servant this great victory. Must I now die of thirst and fall into the hands of these uncircumcised men?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things worth noticing. He credits the victory to God without hesitation — which, for Samson, is not guaranteed. And then he complains, bluntly, about the state he has been left in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a polite prayer. It is a man at the end of his strength saying so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So God split open the hollow place at Lehi, and water came pouring out. Samson drank, his strength returned, and he revived.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No rebuke. No lesson about attitude. Water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *revived* is the word for a spirit returning — the same idea used of someone coming round after a faint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is why the spring is called En-hakkore, the Spring of the One Who Cried Out — and it is there in Lehi to this day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The place is named after the cry, not the victory. Not after the thousand Philistines or the jawbone, both of which the chapter has just described at length. After a man shouting for help because he was thirsty."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who Samson was.** This is not a model believer. His story is a catalogue of appetite and broken vows, and the book knows it. The passage is worth reading precisely because of that: God answers a complaint from a man in the middle of a badly lived life, and the memorial left behind is of the asking."}]}, "Judges 4:17–24": {"title": "Jael and the tent peg", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The commander of nine hundred iron chariots is killed with two objects that belong to housework."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Sisera was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with him: twenty years of harsh oppression, nine hundred chariots of iron, an army Israel had no answer to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He ends the day on foot, in a woman's tent, asking for a drink."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jael took up a **tent peg** and a **hammer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pitching and striking the tents was women's work among the nomads. The peg and the mallet were the tools of her trade, in her hand every time the camp moved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not go looking for a weapon. She kills him with what she already had."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The milk"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He asked for a little water, and she opened a skin of milk and gave him a drink.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked for less than she gave him. The milk is what puts him into the *exhausted sleep* the next sentence describes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges 5, Deborah's song, dwells on this: *he asked for water, she gave him milk; she brought him curds in a lordly bowl.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"At the hand of a woman\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the great commander died at the hand of a woman.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator says it as a verdict, and it had been announced ten verses earlier. Deborah had told Barak that because of the way he was going about it, *the LORD will sell Sisera into the hand of a woman.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barak arrives after it is over. *Come, and I'll show you the man you're looking for.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the story is told this way"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Iron chariots against a hammer and a tent peg. The book of Judges keeps doing this — an ox goad, a jawbone, three hundred men with jars and torches, a millstone dropped from a wall."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same principle Paul states outright: *God chose the weak things of the world to shame the strong.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:26–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The small ordinary thing is not a lesser weapon. It is the one God keeps choosing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter closes.** *Israel's hand pressed harder and harder against Jabin, until at last they destroyed him.* One peg in one tent, and then twenty years of oppression unwinding."}]}, "Judges 4:4–10": {"title": "Deborah, Barak, and the honour that went elsewhere", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman is running the country, a general will not move without her, and the victory ends up credited to a third person nobody has met yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Deborah's position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A prophet was leading Israel — Deborah, a woman of fire and wisdom.* She judges under a palm tree, and Israel comes up to her for judgment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text states it without commentary or apology. She is the only judge in the book described as holding court, and the only one who is also a prophet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Barak's condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you go with me, I'll go. But if you won't go with me, I won't go.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just been given a direct command from God through a prophet, complete with the battle plan and the promised outcome. His answer attaches a condition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges is a book of increasingly compromised leaders, and this is early in the slide — not a refusal, but not a straight yes either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it costs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because of the road you are taking, the honour will not be yours — the LORD will hand Sisera over to a woman.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She goes anyway. The mission is not withdrawn and the victory is not smaller; what changes is whose name is on it. And the woman in question turns out not to be Deborah but Jael, who nobody in this conversation has mentioned."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Hebrews does with him.** Barak is named in the roll of faith in Hebrews 11 — Deborah is not. The New Testament lists the man who hesitated among those commended, without mentioning the hesitation. Both facts sit in the canon: the honour redirected here, and the name kept there."}]}, "Leviticus 16:11–14": {"title": "Blood on the mercy seat, once a year", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Day of Atonement, and the only occasion in the year when anyone went behind the curtain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He deals with himself first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Aaron slaughters the bull that is his own sin offering, making atonement for himself and his household.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before he can carry anything in for the nation, the high priest has to be covered himself. The man doing the mediating needs a mediator."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews makes a great deal of this: *He has no need, like those high priests, to offer sacrifices daily, first for his own sins and then for the people's.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The incense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A cloud of sweet smoke rises and covers the mercy seat — **so that he may live and not die**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The smoke is not decoration or atmosphere. Leviticus says plainly what it is for: it veils the mercy seat so the priest is not destroyed by what is above it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even on the one day access is permitted, and even after the bull has died, there is a screen between the man and the glory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He sprinkles it with his finger on the front of the mercy seat, and seven times before it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Once on the lid itself, seven times on the ground in front. Seven is the number of completeness — a full measure, not a token."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blood is applied to the exact place God had said He would speak from. What was underneath the lid was the broken law; what went on top of it was blood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The frequency"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Once a year, one man, with blood, behind a curtain, in smoke, having first atoned for himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set that beside *let us walk right up to the throne where grace sits* and the distance the New Testament is describing becomes measurable."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The curtain.** Matthew records that at the moment Jesus died, the curtain of the temple was torn from top to bottom. Not opened. Torn — and from the top, which is the end no man could reach. See the notes on Hebrews 9:23–24 and Hebrews 4:14–16."}]}, "Leviticus 17:13–14": {"title": "\"The life of every creature is its blood\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A rule about hunting, and inside it a statement about biology that nobody on earth knew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the life of every creature is its blood** — its blood is its very life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said three times in two verses. Leviticus does not usually repeat itself like that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nephesh** — life, soul, the living self. The life of the flesh is in the blood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Around fifteen centuries before Christ."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Physicians did not understand what blood does until William Harvey worked out the circulation in 1628, and bleeding remained standard medical practice for another two hundred years after that. George Washington was bled to death by his doctors in 1799 — several pints, for a throat infection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Book said where the life was, and it was ignored for three thousand years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He must **pour out its blood and cover it with earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pour it out. Not consume it, not use it, not keep it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For any animal taken in hunting, the blood goes into the ground and dirt goes over it. The life goes back where it came from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the rule exists"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says it a few verses earlier: *I have given it to you upon the altar to make atonement for your souls, for it is the blood that makes atonement for the soul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the reason blood is not food. It has been assigned to something else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Life, poured out, for a life. The whole sacrificial system runs on the sentence in verse 14, and the sentence in verse 14 happens also to be true anatomy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it points to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Without the shedding of blood there is no remission.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reason the sacrifices had to be repeated is in the same passage: *the blood of bulls and goats* could not take sins away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One life was worth the exchange, and Leviticus is holding the place for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one exception.** The blood of every creature is to be poured out and never taken in. And then, at a table in an upper room: *drink from it, all of you. This is My blood of the new covenant.* The one command Leviticus never bends, He bends over a cup."}]}, "Leviticus 2:1–3": {"title": "Fine flour, oil, and frankincense", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The one offering in Leviticus with no blood in it, and every element is specified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What goes in"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**fine flour** — the Hebrew is *solet*, the finest grade, sifted until there is no coarseness left anywhere in it. Nothing is uneven.", "**oil** — poured over it and mixed through it", "**frankincense** — the fragrant resin, laid on top"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the two things that are excluded, a few verses later: no yeast, and no honey. Nothing that ferments, and nothing that sweetens artificially."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The memorial portion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The priest takes a handful of the flour and oil, together with **all** the frankincense, and burns this memorial portion on the altar.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A handful of the flour goes into the fire — and all of the incense. The fragrant part is entirely God's; none of it is kept back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest is food"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The rest of the grain offering belongs to Aaron and his sons.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the shape of this offering: a portion given, and the remainder becoming a meal for the priests. It is the only sacrifice in which the bulk of the gift feeds people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A grain offering was the produce of ordinary work — a farmer's crop, ground and brought. There is no death in it and no atonement language attached."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set among the sin offering and the burnt offering, it stands for something different: a life given over, sifted fine, with oil through it and a fragrance that rises."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the details are worth reading.** Christian readers have long seen a portrait here — the flour with no unevenness in it, the oil mixed all the way through, no leaven and no artificial sweetness, and the whole of the fragrance going up to God. The chapter never says so. It simply specifies the recipe, and the recipe is exact."}]}, "Leviticus 2:4–10": {"title": "Oven, griddle, pan", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The same offering, three ways of cooking it, and the oil is in all three."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three methods"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**baked in an oven** — loaves or thin wafers, the oil mixed in or spread on", "**cooked on a griddle** — an open plate over the fire, the hottest and most exposed method. This one is *broken into pieces with oil poured over it.*", "**prepared in a pan** — a covered vessel, the gentlest of the three"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Household kitchens had all three. Whatever equipment a family owned, there was a version of the offering they could bring."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one that is broken"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The griddle offering is the only one with the breaking specified: **broken into pieces**, and then oil poured on again afterwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fiercest heat, the most exposure, and the oil applied a second time — after the breaking rather than only before it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is constant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fine flour in every case. Oil in every case. No yeast in any of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only the fire changes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The memorial portion again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lifts out the memorial portion and burns it on the altar — a fragrance that pleases the LORD. The rest belongs to Aaron and his sons.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same pattern as the uncooked version: a portion into the fire, the remainder feeding the priests. However it was prepared, it ended up as somebody's meal."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the oil.** In Scripture oil is the standing figure for the Spirit — priests and kings were anointed with it, and the word *Messiah* means the anointed one. In this chapter the oil is mixed in, spread on, and poured over, and it is never optional. Whatever the fire does to the offering, the oil is already through it."}]}, "Luke 1:26–29": {"title": "The greeting that unsettled her", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An angel arrives in an unimportant town and says something that frightens a young woman — not because it is a threat, but because of what it implies."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Gabriel actually says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rejoice, you who have been so greatly favoured! The Lord is with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *greatly favoured* is one word, **kecharitomene** — a perfect passive participle built on *charis*, grace. Something like: you who have been graced, and stand in that state."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is passive. Nothing is said about what Mary has done. The word describes something done **to** her, already complete."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The words unsettled her all the way through, and she turned them over, trying to understand what kind of greeting this could possibly be.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says the greeting disturbed her, not the angel. She is troubled by being addressed that way — and then she does what Luke will say she does twice more in two chapters: she thinks about it rather than answering quickly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the story is set"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A town in Galilee called Nazareth* — a village of a few hundred people, never mentioned once in the Old Testament. Luke has just come from the temple in Jerusalem, where the other annunciation in this chapter happened, to a priest, at the altar, at the hour of incense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is deliberate and Luke does not comment on it. He simply reports where the second angel went."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows.** Mary's question is not *why me* but *how* — a practical objection, and Gabriel answers it. Then: *let it be to me according to your word.* Luke gives her more speaking lines in this chapter than anyone else, and the longest of them is a song about God putting the powerful down and lifting the low."}]}, "Luke 11:37–44": {"title": "\"Without letting go of the others\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is at a dinner party and says the hardest thing in Luke. The sentence at the centre of it is a correction of proportion, not a cancellation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He accuses them of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You carefully tithe your mint and your rue and every garden herb you grow, and you step straight over justice and the love of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The precision is the joke. The law asked for a tenth of the crop; these men were counting leaves out of the kitchen garden while walking over the two largest things in the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line He does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *stop tithing your herbs*. He says:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*These are the things you should have been doing, **without letting go of the others**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both. Justice and the love of God taken up, and the giving not put down. Jesus had every opportunity in that sentence to abolish the practice and instead He kept it in place while re-ranking it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a correction of what had gone missing, not of what they were doing with the mint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remedy He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You Pharisees scrub the outside of the cup and the plate, but inside you are full of greed and cruelty… Give away what is inside as a gift to the poor, and see — everything about you is clean.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Having diagnosed greed under a polished exterior, His prescription is not more careful measuring. It is generosity — giving the contents away. He treats open- handedness as the thing that reaches what scrubbing never could."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the meal setting matters.** This is said at a table, to a host, after Jesus declined to perform the ritual handwashing. Everything in the passage is about outsides and insides — clean hands and grasping hearts, polished dishes and unmarked graves. The tithing line belongs to that argument: a right practice, carried out by a heart that had never been dealt with."}]}, "Luke 13:10–17": {"title": "\"Should she not be untied on the Sabbath?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eighteen years bent double, and the argument afterwards is about whether the timing was appropriate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "She did not ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus saw her, He called her over.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every detail is His initiative. He sees her, calls her, speaks, and lays hands on her. Nothing in the account records her asking for anything — she had come to the synagogue, which is where she presumably went every week."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Woman, you are set free from what has been crippling you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is perfect passive — you **have been** released, and you stand released. He announces it as done before He touches her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words for tying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument turns on one image, and Jesus builds it deliberately:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Is there one of you who does not **untie** his ox or his donkey from the stall on the Sabbath and lead it out to water?*", "*And this woman… whom Satan has kept **tied up** for eighteen years — should she not be **untied** on the Sabbath day?*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verb, three times. If a knot can be undone for an animal that has been tied overnight, what about a woman tied for eighteen years?"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The country He was in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus based Himself in Capernaum, *in the regions of Zebulun and **Naphtali***, and most of His work was done along that shore."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob's blessing over Naphtali is one line: *Naphtali is **a doe set free**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A doe let loose, and a woman untied after eighteen years, in the same territory. See the note on Genesis 49:21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He calls her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A daughter of Abraham.* Not a phrase used elsewhere in the Gospels."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives her the standing of an heir of the covenant, in a synagogue, in front of the man complaining about her — and He does it in the same sentence that names what had been holding her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two reactions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who had been opposing Him was put to shame, and the whole crowd was overjoyed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports both, and the contrast is the point of the paragraph: the same event humiliated the officials and delighted everybody else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she did first.** *Right away she stood up straight, and she began to praise God.* Luke gives the standing and the praise in one clause. After eighteen years of looking at the ground, the first thing recorded is her voice."}]}, "Luke 15:17–20": {"title": "\"He ran\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The son rehearses a speech in a pigsty, and his father ruins the plan by running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What brings him home"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How many hired men on my father's place have more bread than they can eat, and here I am starving to death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is honest about the motive. It is hunger, and a comparison — the servants at home eat better than this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is presented as a noble repentance. He *came to his senses*, which is the language of a man waking up rather than a man reforming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speech he prepares"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three parts: I have sinned against heaven, I have sinned against you, **take me on as one of your hired men**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third part is a proposal. He has worked out terms — a job, a wage, a way to be useful and stay fed without claiming anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the father sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While he was still a long way off, his father saw him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The distance is the detail. To see someone that far away you have to have been looking, and to recognise him at that range you have to have been looking for a while."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The running"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His heart went out to the boy, and he ran — he ran down the road to meet him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that culture, an elderly man did not run. It required hitching up the robe and exposing the legs, and it was undignified in a way that would have been noticed by the whole village."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does it anyway, and he does it before a word of the speech is spoken. The embrace and the kiss come first; the confession arrives afterwards, and the third part of it never gets said at all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the speech never reached.** *Take me on as one of your hired men* is cut off by the father calling for the best robe. The bargain the son had worked out is not refused so much as never allowed into the conversation. See the note on Luke 15:21–24."}]}, "Luke 15:21–24": {"title": "The speech he never finished", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The son has a speech ready. He worked it out in the pigsty, and it had three parts: I have sinned against heaven, I have sinned against you, **make me one of your hired men.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Watch what happens to the third part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He gets two-thirds of the way through"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Father, I have sinned against heaven, and I have sinned against you. I am not fit to be called your son —*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the dash is doing real work. He is cut off. The father is already talking to the servants about a robe. The line the son rehearsed hardest — the arrangement he was going to propose, where he earns his keep and everyone's honour is satisfied — never gets said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Confession is welcomed. The bargain is not allowed to be spoken."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the father calls for, and why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things, and none of them are comfort items. Every one is a marker of standing in that household:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the best robe** — literally the first robe, the one belonging to the head of the house", "**a ring** — a signet: authority to act in the family name", "**sandals** — servants went barefoot; sons did not", "**the fattened calf** — the animal kept for the one occasion big enough to need it"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not get a bath and a probationary period. He gets dressed as a son in front of everybody, before he has done a single thing to prove himself, and the household is fed on the best animal on the property."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"This son of mine was dead\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The father gives his reason twice, and it is not that the boy repented well:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "This son of mine was dead, and he is alive again. He was lost, and he is found."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dead and alive. Lost and found. Those are the two pairs the whole chapter has been building — the sheep, the coin, and now this. In all three the thing that was missing does nothing to recover itself. It is carried, swept up, run to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The son's repentance is real and it matters. But the story is careful about what it accomplishes: it gets him home. It does not buy the robe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is doing in this chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is answering a complaint about the company He keeps. The answer is a father who runs, interrupts an apology, and throws a party — and the point of putting the interruption in is that the son's plan to work his way back was never on the table."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the celebration.** In all three stories the finder calls the neighbours together. The joy is not private, and it is never at the recovered thing's expense. Nobody in the chapter is welcomed home quietly and then reminded later what they cost."}]}, "Luke 15:29–32": {"title": "The older brother, and the word he never uses", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The story is usually named after the younger son, but Jesus did not stop where the party started. He told it to people who were annoyed that He ate with the wrong sort, and the last scene is aimed straight at them. It is the only one of the three stories in this chapter that does not end with something found."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Listen to how he talks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four sentences, and every one of them is worth slowing down for:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*all these years I have worked for you* — the word is the one used of a hired hand's labour, not a son's life at home", "*I have never once disobeyed an order of yours* — an order. Not a word from a father; an instruction from a master", "*you never gave me so much as a young goat* — a man who has never asked", "*this son of yours* — not *my brother*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has lived in the house for years and describes it as a job. The tragedy is not that he is wrong about his own record — the father never disputes a word of it — but that a faultless record has left him standing outside his own home, angry, on the one night the place is full of music."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The father's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My child, you are always with me, and everything I have is yours.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read that against the complaint. He asked for a goat. He is told he owns the estate. He has been *working for* what was already in his name. And the word the father uses is not the formal one for a grown son; it is affectionate, the word you would use for your own boy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is being given to him in that sentence. He is being told what has been true the entire time he was earning it."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thing he never says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Track the vocabulary. The younger son comes home and says *father* — twice. The older son opens with *Look*, and never uses the word at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The younger one lost everything and knew what he was coming home to. The older one lost nothing and did not know where he lived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Jesus ends it unfinished"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The story stops. We are not told whether he came in. Every other thread in the chapter closes — the sheep is carried home, the coin is found, the son is embraced — and this one is left open, because the people Jesus was telling it to were standing in exactly that spot while He told it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the elder son's picture of his father.** Notice that his bitterness is not aimed at his brother first — it is aimed at a father he believes has been withholding a goat for years. That is not what the father is like, and the story has already shown us so. A person's grievance is usually built on a picture of God that the facts do not support, and the correction here is not a rebuke but a piece of information: *everything I have is yours*."}]}, "Luke 15:3–7": {"title": "\"Until he finds it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of the three stories in this chapter, told to people who were complaining about the company Jesus kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why He is telling it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with tax collectors and sinners crowding in to hear Him, and the Pharisees muttering: *this man welcomes sinners and eats with them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three parables answer that one sentence. The complaint is the reason the chapter exists."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Keep going until he finds it\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The searching has no stated limit. Not for a while, not until dark — until."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the ninety-nine are left *out in the open country*. Jesus does not pause to justify that. The shepherd's arithmetic is not the point of the story; the finding is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lifts it up onto his shoulders, and the whole way home he is rejoicing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sheep does nothing. It does not find its way back or follow him home — it is carried the entire distance, on the shoulders of the one who came out for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the mood is set on the road, not at the door. He is rejoicing before anyone else knows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The party"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Celebrate with me — I have found my sheep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of the three stories ends with neighbours being called together. The joy is public each time, and the celebration is never at the recovered thing's expense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*More joy over one sinner whose mind is turned around than over ninety-nine upright people who have no need of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last phrase carries an edge. Jesus is describing people who see no need of repentance, and He is looking at some while He says it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the three stories add up to.** A sheep that wandered off, a coin that could not have moved itself, and a son who walked out deliberately. Different ways of being lost — and in every case somebody goes looking, and there is a party afterwards. See the notes on Luke 15:8–10, 15:17–20 and 15:21–24."}]}, "Luke 15:8–10": {"title": "The lamp and the broom", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The middle story of the three, and the shortest — but it changes one thing about the picture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The coin could not stray"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sheep wanders off. A son leaves on purpose. A coin does neither."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was lost in the house, by no fault and no decision of its own, and it could not have found its way anywhere. That is a third kind of lostness, and Jesus puts it deliberately between the other two."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Does she not light a lamp and sweep out the whole house, going over every corner of it until she finds it?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three actions: light, sweep, search. Houses of that period had small high windows and packed earth floors — a coin could sit in the dust for a long time. The lamp is lit because you cannot find anything in that room without one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And again: **until**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was worth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A drachma, roughly a day's wage. Not a fortune — one tenth of what she had."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She turns the entire house over for it, which is the proportion the story wants you to notice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The joy is somewhere specific"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is like that among the angels of God. They are glad over one sinner whose mind is turned around.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first story said *in heaven*; this one says *among the angels*. Jesus is locating the celebration a little more precisely each time."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why a woman.** Jesus tells three stories in a row and gives the middle one to a woman searching her own house — set beside a shepherd and a father. In a chapter arguing about who God is like, He uses all three without comment."}]}, "Luke 16:9–13": {"title": "\"Faithful with what belongs to someone else\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus has just told the strangest parable in Luke — a dishonest manager who cooks the books and gets commended for shrewdness — and this is what He draws out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale He puts money on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read how He describes it:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*a very small thing*", "*something as small as money*", "*what belongs to someone else*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times, and each one demotes it. Money is the small thing, the borrowed thing, the test case — not the prize."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set against it is *what is genuinely valuable* and *anything of your own*. The comparison is not between poverty and wealth. It is between two orders of property, and the one everybody worries about is the lesser."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strange logic of the first line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Use the money of this world to make friends for yourselves, so that when you finally fail, you will be welcomed into a home that lasts.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase for money is literally *unrighteous mammon* — wealth belonging to a crooked world. Jesus does not tell them to avoid it; He tells them to spend it in a direction that outlasts it. The manager in the parable used his master's money to secure a future. Do that, He says, with better aim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line that ends it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You cannot serve God and money both.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is to serve as a slave, and the point is not that money is evil but that it competes. Two owners cannot both have you. Everything before this — the smallness, the borrowed quality, the shrewd spending — is preparing for a sentence about who owns whom."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"faithful in little\" means here.** Not that careful budgeting earns spiritual promotion. The argument is about character being one thing: how a person handles the small borrowed thing is what they will do with the large permanent one, because it is the same person doing it."}]}, "Luke 17:5–6": {"title": "\"Give us more faith\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The apostles ask for an increase, and the answer they get is not more of what they had in mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the four verses before it. Jesus has just said that if your brother sins against you seven times a day and turns back seven times saying he repents, you must forgive him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give us more faith* is what they say next. It is not a request about mountains or miracles. It is a response to being told to forgive somebody seven times before dinner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If your faith were the size of a mustard seed, you could say to this mulberry tree, \"Pull yourself up by the roots and plant yourself in the sea,\" and it would obey you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not give them more. He tells them the quantity was never the issue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A mustard seed was proverbially the smallest thing anyone handled — and the mulberry, with its deep root system, was proverbially among the hardest trees to shift. Smallest possible faith, hardest possible object, and the tree obeys."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction inside it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You could **say** to this tree.* The faith in the illustration is exercised by speaking to the thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth noticing in a passage about forgiveness. The uprooting Jesus describes is not something felt into being; it is addressed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A parable about a servant who comes in from the field and serves dinner before eating — *we are unworthy servants; we have only done our duty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus keeps the two together: faith the size of a seed can uproot a tree, and the people exercising it are not to think of themselves as impressive for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word \"size.\"** The Greek is literally *if you have faith as a grain of mustard* — a comparison of kind as much as quantity. Something alive and small, which is what a seed is. Not a measured amount of a substance."}]}, "Luke 18:24–27": {"title": "\"What is impossible for people is not impossible for God\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A rich man has just walked away sad, and Jesus watches him go before He says anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus saw the sorrow settle over him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comment that follows is not aimed at the man's back as a rebuke. Luke records Jesus watching the grief land, and Mark's account adds that He had looked at him and loved him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The camel and the needle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is easier for a camel to squeeze through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to get into the kingdom of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Attempts have been made to soften this — a small gate in Jerusalem called the Needle's Eye, or a rope rather than a camel. There is no evidence for the gate, and the rope theory rests on a variant reading."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is meant to be impossible. That is why the disciples react the way they do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then who can possibly be saved?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the right response, and it tells you what they had assumed. Wealth was read as evidence of God's blessing; if the obviously blessed cannot get in, nobody can."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not correct the logic. He accepts it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What is impossible for people is not impossible for God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say it is difficult, or that rich people need to try harder. He moves the whole question out of the category of human capacity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the answer covers the disciples too — the ones who had just pointed out that they left everything."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who it turns out to be possible for.** Two chapters later Luke gives us Zacchaeus, a rich tax collector, up a tree, and Jesus inviting Himself to dinner. Luke tells the impossible saying and then tells the story of it happening."}]}, "Luke 19:41–44": {"title": "\"You did not recognise the time\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He is riding into Jerusalem to shouts of *Hosanna*, the crowds are spreading their cloaks on the road, and He is crying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weeping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When He came near enough to see the city, **He wept over it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **klaio** — to wail, to sob out loud. It is not the quiet word John uses at Lazarus's tomb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is at the top of the Mount of Olives, with the whole city and the temple spread out below in the sun, at the height of the only public acclamation He ever received."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody could tell Him anything about Himself from the size of a crowd."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"On this day of all days\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you had only known, **even now, on this day of all days**, what would bring you peace.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a broken sentence. He does not finish it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**This day.** Nisan 10, the day the Passover lamb was taken into the house and set aside — four days before it was killed. Israel was choosing its lamb that morning, and the Lamb was coming down the hill."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Hidden from your eyes\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But it is hidden from your eyes.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The peace was there. The seeing was not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He describes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your enemies will **throw up a wall of earth around you** and close you in and press you from every side… **they will not leave one stone standing on another**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman siege practice, described forty years before it happened. Titus built a wall of earth right round Jerusalem in AD 70 to stop anyone escaping, and Josephus, who was there, describes it — timber stripped from the countryside for miles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple came down and the stones were prised apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"— **because you did not recognise the time when God came to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the time of your visitation* — **episkope**, the moment of being looked in on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because of their sins in general. Because of a specific missed morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Daniel had already said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After the sixty-two sevens, the Anointed One will be cut off… the people of the prince who is to come will destroy the city and the sanctuary.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written in Babylon five hundred years earlier, in that order: the Messiah cut off first, the city destroyed after. See the note on Daniel 9:26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is weeping over a date that was already on paper."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does immediately after.** Rides down the hill, walks into the temple, and turns over the tables. The weeping and the whip are the same afternoon. See the note on Matthew 21:12–17."}]}, "Luke 2:25–28": {"title": "The old man who was told he would not die yet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four sentences, and the Holy Spirit is named in three of them — unusually dense even for Luke, who mentions the Spirit more than the other Gospel writers combined."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three movements"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the Spirit **was on him**", "it had been **shown to him** by the Spirit that he would not see death until he had seen the Messiah", "**led by the Spirit**, he came into the temple"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "A standing presence, a specific word, and then a prompting on a particular morning. All three are needed for the scene to happen, and Luke lists them in order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as the parents carried the child Jesus in.* Simeon walks into the temple courts at the moment one poor family arrives with a baby to do what the law required — one of many families doing the same thing that week."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing marked them out. Luke gives no star, no crowd, no announcement. An old man crosses a courtyard and takes a child out of a stranger's arms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been promised, exactly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that he would be spared death. That he *would not see death until* — a deferral, not an exemption. The promise had an end built into it, and Simeon plainly knew it: the first thing he says after this is that his master can now let him go in peace."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was waiting for.** *The comfort of Israel* — the Greek is *paraklesis*, from the same root as the name Jesus gives the Spirit in John, the one called alongside. Simeon was waiting for the consolation of his people and was carrying it in his arms before he finished the sentence."}]}, "Luke 2:46–50": {"title": "\"My Father's business\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only recorded episode between infancy and the Jordan, and the first recorded words of Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they found Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the temple, sitting right among the teachers, listening to them and asking them questions.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what a student did — the rabbinic method was question and answer, and a learner sat among the teachers rather than in front of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is careful about the posture. He is not lecturing; He is sitting in the circle, listening first, then asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What astonished them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who heard Him was astonished at how much He understood and at the answers He gave.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are named: what He grasped, and what He said back. A twelve-year-old holding His end of a discussion with the temple teachers for three days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Mary's question and His answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Child, why have You treated us like this? Your father and I have been searching everywhere for You, sick with worry.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why were you searching for Me? Did you not know that I had to be taken up with My Father's business?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She says *your father*. He answers *My Father*, and the contrast is unmistakable in the Greek. It is the first thing He is recorded as saying, and it distinguishes between the two."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase can be rendered *in My Father's house* or *about My Father's business* — the Greek is elliptical, and both are defensible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But they did not understand what He was telling them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it plainly about the two people who knew most about who He was — who had both had angelic visits. Neither the annunciation nor the shepherds nor Simeon had prepared them for this sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does next.** He goes home to Nazareth and lives under their care for eighteen more years. The boy who has just said He must be about His Father's business spends the next two decades in a carpenter's household. See the note on Luke 2:51–52."}]}, "Luke 2:51–52": {"title": "Eighteen years in one sentence", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the last thing the Gospels say about Jesus before He is thirty. Everything between the temple at twelve and the Jordan is covered by two verses, and what they choose to record is unexpected."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went back down to Nazareth with them, and He lived under their care and did as they said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had just told Mary and Joseph He had to be about His Father's business. Then He goes home and spends eighteen years under theirs. Luke puts the two right next to each other without comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Went on growing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is imperfect — continuous, over time, not a state He arrived at. And Luke lists four directions at once:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["in wisdom", "in stature", "in favour with God", "in favour with the people around Him"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third is the one that stops people. Whatever it means, Luke was willing to write it: a real childhood, a real adolescence, real development. Not a divine adult in a small body waiting for the years to pass."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Mary does.** *She kept every one of these things treasured up in her heart* — Luke says something similar after the shepherds' visit. He seems to be telling his readers where parts of this account came from, and the answer is a woman who had been storing things up for decades."}]}, "Luke 2:6–7": {"title": "Wrapped, and laid in a feeding trough", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke tells the birth in two sentences and spends most of them on where the baby was put."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Wrapped Him around and around in strips of cloth\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ordinary practice — newborns were bound in bands of cloth, believed to help the limbs grow straight. Every mother in that world did it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is not ordinary is that Luke mentions it three times in this chapter. The angel gives it to the shepherds as the identifying sign: *you will find a baby wrapped in strips of cloth and lying in a feeding trough.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sign by which the Messiah could be recognised was that He looked exactly like any other newborn, in a place no other newborn would be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trough"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phatne** — a manger, the box animals eat from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not describe a stable, and there is a good case that the setting was the lower room of a village house where animals were kept at night, since the word he uses for *inn* is the one he later uses for the upper room at the Last Supper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way, the detail is the same: the first bed was a feeding trough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"No room left for them\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke states it without accusation. Nobody is blamed and no innkeeper appears in the text — that character comes from later tradition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethlehem was crowded because of the census. There was simply no space, and a young couple took what was available."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He is laid in.** A container that food is put in, in a town whose name means *house of bread*, by a mother who would later hear Him say *I am the bread of life.* Luke does not point at any of that. He just records where they put Him."}]}, "Luke 2:8–12": {"title": "Told to shepherds first", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The announcement of the birth goes to the people who were working the night shift."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shepherds were not romantic figures. The work was dirty, the hours were antisocial, and their testimony was not accepted in a Jewish court — a shepherd could not have served as a witness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke, who has just named Caesar Augustus and Quirinius governor of Syria in the opening verses, brings the news to men in a field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"For you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Today, in the town of David, a Saviour has been born **for you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel does not say a Saviour has been born, and you should be pleased about it. The birth is announced as being on their account."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the titles stack: Saviour, Messiah, Lord — three of the heaviest words available, said to men holding sticks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is how you will know Him: you will find a baby wrapped in strips of cloth and lying in a feeding trough.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A strange sign. Every baby in Judea was wrapped in cloth; only one was in a manger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the identifying mark is ordinariness in an impossible place. Nothing about the sign suggests grandeur, and the shepherds are told to look for it in a town, not a palace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"A great joy meant for everyone\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *all the people* — and Luke, who will end his Gospel with the good news going to all nations, starts widening it here in the second chapter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the glory did.** *The glory of the Lord blazed all around them, and they were terrified.* Then the first words: *do not be afraid.* Every angelic announcement in these two chapters begins the same way, because the reaction is always the same."}]}, "Luke 21:20–24": {"title": "The times of the Gentiles", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version of this discourse says something the other Gospels do not: armies, a siege, and a clock that starts running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When you see **armies camped in a ring around Jerusalem**, then you will know that her ruin is close.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew and Mark have *the abomination of desolation standing where it ought not.* Luke, writing for a Gentile readership, gives the plain military fact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let everyone in Judea **run for the hills**, and let those inside the city **get out of it**, and let those out in the countryside **not go in**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three directions, all outward. Nobody is told to defend the city."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The historian Eusebius records that the Christians of Jerusalem left before the siege closed, and crossed the Jordan to Pella. They had been told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In AD 70 Titus surrounded Jerusalem, and the city and the temple were destroyed. Josephus, who was there, describes the famine inside the walls, and *it will be bitter in those days for a woman carrying a child* reads as understatement beside his account."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will fall by the sword, and they will be carried off as captives among all the nations.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scattering that followed lasted nineteen centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clock"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And Jerusalem will be **trampled under Gentile feet** until the times of the Gentiles **have run their course**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Until.** The trampling is given an end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The times of the Gentiles* is a defined period — Luke's phrase, and the only place it appears. The Greek for *run their course* is to be fulfilled, to be filled up, as a measure is filled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Daniel's statue"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same period is what Nebuchadnezzar saw as a statue: gold, silver, bronze, iron, and feet of iron mixed with clay — a succession of Gentile empires running from the Babylon that burned the first temple onward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in Daniel's interpretation it ends the same way, with a stone *cut from the mountain but not by human hands*, which breaks all of it and stands forever. See the note on Daniel 2:44–45."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two passages share"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both put Jerusalem under Gentile rule for a stated stretch. Both put a term on it. Neither describes the arrangement as permanent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's version of the same night ends with *you will not see Me again until you say, \"Blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord.\"* The same word again. See the note on Matthew 23:37–39."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says immediately after.** Signs in the sun and moon and stars, and then *when these things begin to happen, look up and lift up your heads, for your redemption draws near.* The instruction in the siege was to run for the hills. The instruction at the end is to look up."}]}, "Luke 21:25–28": {"title": "The one instruction in the middle of the collapse", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A description of things falling apart, and then a command that runs against every instinct the description produces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage describes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Signs in sun, moon and stars. Nations at their wits' end. The sea roaring. People fainting from fear and dread. *The powers that hold the heavens in place will be shaken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not soften any of it. The reaction he reports is collapse — people literally giving out from fear of what is coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When all of this begins to happen, stand up straight and lift up your heads.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two postures, and both are the opposite of what a frightened crowd does. The first word means to straighten up — used in this same Gospel of a bent woman being made straight. The second is lifting the head that fear drives down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the timing: *when all of this begins*. Not after it resolves. At the start, while it is still getting worse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because the day of your rescue is drawing near.* The word is **apolutrosis** — the ransom-release word, the same one in Ephesians 1:7 for being bought out of captivity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sign that terrifies everyone else is read by these people as the sound of the rescue arriving. Same events, opposite posture, because of a different expectation about what they mean."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"the powers of the heavens shaken.\"** This is the phrase people connect with modern weaponry, and the words behind it are the ordinary Greek for the starry host and for shaking — the same phrase Isaiah 34:4 already used centuries earlier of judgement on a nation. It is a stock prophetic image for the cosmos being disturbed, not a coded reference to anything later."}]}, "Luke 22:54–62": {"title": "\"The Lord turned and looked straight at Peter\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Only Luke records the look, and it is the hinge of the scene."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter followed a long way behind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's phrasing is exact and it is not only geography. He is close enough to be in the courtyard and far enough to deny knowing Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three denials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They escalate, and they get shorter:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Woman, I do not know Him*", "*Man, I am not*", "*Man, I have no idea what you are talking about*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "An hour passes between the second and third. He had time to leave, and stayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And while the words were still in his mouth, the rooster crowed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke puts the two in the same instant. No gap for reflection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The look"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Lord turned and looked straight at Peter.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus was inside the high priest's house being interrogated. For this to happen He was being moved through the courtyard, or was visible across it — bound, already struck, in the middle of His own trial."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb Luke uses is for a direct, deliberate look at someone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is said. No word is recorded, and no rebuke."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the look did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Peter remembered what the Lord had said to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The remembering is what breaks him — not the servant girl, not the rooster. Christ had told him this would happen, and had said in the same breath: *I have prayed for you, that your faith may not fail; and when you have turned back, strengthen your brothers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been told the failure and the recovery in one sentence, before either happened."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it ends.** *He went outside and wept bitterly.* Luke leaves him there and does not resolve it in this chapter. The resolution is on a beach in John 21, over a charcoal fire — the only other charcoal fire in the Gospels is the one in this courtyard."}]}, "Luke 24:13–16": {"title": "A stranger on the road", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men walking away from Jerusalem on the day of the resurrection, and the man they are talking about falls into step beside them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they were going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A village called **Emmaus**, sixty stadia out from Jerusalem — a walk of about seven miles.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name means warm springs, warm baths. There were hot springs in that country and a village took its name from them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are walking away from the city, and they are on the road on the same day the tomb was found empty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were talking the whole way about everything that had happened… **talking it over and turning it around** between them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for the second is to debate, to dispute — they are going over it and getting nowhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have all the facts. Luke's next paragraph shows they know about the crucifixion, the third day, the women at the tomb, the angels, and the men who went and found it empty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Facts, and no sight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Their eyes were held\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus Himself came up and walked along with them. But **their eyes were held**, and they did not recognise Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is passive — something was holding them. Luke does not say what."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is physically present, close enough to walk seven miles with them, and it does nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what you would expect. He does not identify Himself, and He does not show them the marks — that comes later, in the room, with the others."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And beginning at Moses and all the Prophets, **He expounded to them in all the Scriptures the things concerning Himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives them a Bible study. Seven miles of it, from Genesis forward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which He thought was better"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had the option of simply appearing. Instead He walked the whole way concealed and opened the Scriptures — and their own verdict afterwards is about the road, not the moment of recognition:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Were our hearts not burning inside us **while He talked with us on the road, while He was opening the Scriptures to us**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The burning happened before they knew who He was. It happened while the Book was being opened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in that sermon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses and all the Prophets. Whatever else was in it, Genesis was, and Jacob's blessings over the twelve sons are in Genesis."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He calls them.** *Foolish ones, and slow of heart to believe in all that the prophets have spoken.* Not slow to believe the women, or the empty tomb. Slow to believe what was already written down."}]}, "Luke 24:25–27": {"title": "The best Bible study never written down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two people walking to Emmaus get a seven-mile exposition of the Old Testament from its subject, and Luke does not record a word of the content."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He rebukes them for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How slow you are, and how heavy of heart, when it comes to believing everything the prophets said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for failing to recognise Him — Luke says their eyes were kept from doing that. For not believing what was already written."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The material to work it out had been available the whole time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"had to\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Did the Messiah not have to suffer all of this and then come into His glory?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek carries necessity — it was *necessary*. Luke uses this word repeatedly of Jesus' path."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the order is fixed: suffering, then glory. That is the sequence the two on the road could not make fit, because a defeated Messiah made no sense to them. Isaiah 52 has the same two elements in the same relationship."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Beginning with Moses\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And beginning with Moses and going on through all the Prophets, He opened up for them everything in the Scriptures that was about Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole Hebrew Bible, in order, with Himself as the thread. Later in the same chapter He names the third division too — the Psalms."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every reader since has wanted the transcript. Luke gives the method and not the notes, which may be deliberate: the exercise is left for us to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they recognised Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not during the exposition. *When He was at the table with them, He took bread, gave thanks, broke it and began to give it to them. Then their eyes were opened.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Scriptures burned in them on the road. The recognition came at the breaking of bread."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they said afterwards.** *Were not our hearts burning within us while He talked with us on the road and opened the Scriptures to us?* They knew something had happened before they knew who had done it."}]}, "Luke 24:28–32": {"title": "Known in the breaking of bread", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven miles of Scripture, a meal, and then He is gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He made as if to go further on\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not invite Himself in. He keeps walking, and they have to stop Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stay with us.** The day is nearly over.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *urged* is strong — they constrained Him, pressed Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who becomes the host"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They invited Him into their house, and then:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When He had sat down at the table with them, **He took the bread** and gave thanks over it, and broke it, and handed it to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The guest takes the bread. That is the host's part, and He takes it without asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Took… gave thanks… broke… handed it to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same four, in the same order, as the upper room. The same four as the five thousand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What opened their eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **their eyes were opened and they knew Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same passive as verse 16 — *their eyes were held* — now reversed. Something had been holding them; something releases them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is at the breaking of the bread that it happens. Not during the seven miles of exposition, and not when He first appeared on the road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And He was gone from their sight\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instant they recognise Him He vanishes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not stay for the meal He has just begun. They get one look, and then they are alone with an opened Bible and a broken loaf."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they say to each other"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Were our hearts not burning inside us** while He talked with us on the road, while He was opening the Scriptures to us?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence, and note what they point at. Not the moment of recognition. The road. The Scriptures being opened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The burning had already happened, before they knew who He was, while a stranger was walking them through Moses and the Prophets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They rose up that very hour and returned to Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven miles back, in the dark, immediately, after telling Him it was too late in the day to travel."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they tell the eleven.** *How He was known to them in the breaking of bread.* Of everything on that road — the exposition, the walk, the vanishing — that is the phrase they use for it."}]}, "Luke 4:16–21": {"title": "Where He stopped reading", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus opens His public ministry by reading a passage aloud and stopping in the middle of a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He read"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 61, and the list is specific:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["good news to the poor", "healing for the brokenhearted", "release for the prisoners", "sight for the blind", "freedom for everyone being crushed", "**the year of the Lord's favour**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the job description He chose for Himself, out of the whole scroll, on the first day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah's sentence does not end at *the year of the Lord's favour*. It continues: *and the day of vengeance of our God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus reads to the comma, rolls up the scroll, hands it back and sits down. The day of vengeance is not cancelled — it simply is not what He had come to announce that morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Today, in your hearing, this Scripture has come true.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The year of favour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase points at the Jubilee — the fiftieth year in Leviticus 25 when debts were released, slaves went free and land returned to its family. As far as the record shows, Israel never once kept it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus announces it as arriving in Him, on that Sabbath, in that room."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the anointing was for.** *The Spirit of the Lord is on Me, because He has anointed Me* — and every item in the list that follows is something done for somebody else: the poor, the brokenhearted, the prisoners, the blind, the crushed. He states the anointing and then spends the rest of the Gospel spending it."}]}, "Luke 5:1–3": {"title": "Two boats at the shore", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He needs a pulpit and there are two available."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The crowd kept pressing in around Him, **hungry to hear the word of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a strong word — pressing, crushing. He is being backed toward the water by people who want to hear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two boats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He noticed **two boats** pulled up at the water's edge, **empty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both were there. Both were free. Neither owner was in them — the fishermen had climbed out and were washing their nets after a night that produced nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one He got into"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He stepped into **the one that belonged to Simon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke names the boat by its owner. Of the two available, He picks one, and it is the boat of a man who had worked all night and caught nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon is not asked whether he minds. He is asked to *push out a little way from the land* — a small thing, an inconvenience at the end of a bad shift."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Simon gave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The loan of a boat for an hour, and the trouble of pushing it out and holding it steady while somebody else talked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole of his contribution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he got back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next paragraph: *take her out into the deep water* — and the nets tear, and both boats fill until they are settling low in the water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other boat, still empty on the shore, ends up loaded too. Simon's partners are waved over and they share it. See the note on Luke 5:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He handed over an empty boat and it came back sinking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The teaching happens first. He sits down in the boat and *taught the crowds from the water* — and only after He has finished speaking does He say anything about fish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon heard the whole sermon from a few feet away, sitting in his own boat, before he was asked to let the nets down."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He was doing with a fishing boat.** Using the tool of a man's trade, on the water he had worked all night, to preach. And then filling it. Simon's business was not set aside for the ministry — it was pushed out a little way from the land and taught out of."}]}, "Luke 5:17–20": {"title": "\"When Jesus saw their faith\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four men dig through a roof, and the sentence that follows is one of the most remarkable in the Gospels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pharisees and teachers of the law were sitting there, men who had come in from every village of Galilee and from Judea and from Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is describing an inspection. Religious authorities have travelled from three regions to watch this man, and they are sitting down — the posture of people who intend to stay."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the same sentence: *the power of the Lord was present with Him to heal.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The roof"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Houses had flat roofs of beams, brushwood and packed mud, reached by an outside stair. Luke, writing for a Greek audience, says *tiles* — describing it in terms his readers would picture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way, four men take somebody's roof apart with a room full of dignitaries underneath, and lower a man on a mat into the gap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Their faith\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when Jesus saw **their** faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plural. Luke does not say the paralysed man believed anything. Whatever he thought, the faith Jesus responds to is the faith of the men on the roof."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth sitting with. Somebody else's conviction got him in front of Jesus, and it is credited."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Friend, your sins are forgiven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what anybody in the room came for. They had dug through a ceiling for legs, and He addresses something nobody mentioned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is literally *man*, but warm — the Illumination's *friend* catches the tone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows.** The scribes reason that only God can forgive sins, and Jesus agrees with the premise: *so that you may know the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins* — He said to the paralysed man, get up. The healing is offered as evidence for the forgiveness, which tells you which of the two He considered the greater work."}]}, "Luke 5:4–7": {"title": "Out into the deep", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A carpenter tells a professional fisherman where the fish are, at the wrong time of day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Everything about it is wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fishing on that lake was night work, with nets, in the shallows. Jesus says daytime, deep water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Simon has just spent the entire night proving there was nothing there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Simon says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Master, **we worked hard all night long and came up with nothing at all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states his case first, and it is a good one. Experience, effort, and the evidence of the last twelve hours all agree."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But because You say so**, I will let the nets down.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *at Your word*. That is the entire reason given, and it is set against everything he knows about his own trade."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Net, singular"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon says he will let down *the net* — one. Jesus had said *let your **nets** down*, plural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke keeps the difference. And the next sentence is *the **nets** began to tear apart in their hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He went out with less than he was told to take."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Such an **enormous shoal** of fish that the nets began to tear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same water. The same lake he had swept all night. The fish were not brought from somewhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both boats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They waved to their partners in the other boat to come and help them, and they came, and **the two boats were loaded so full** that they began to settle low in the water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other boat — the one He did not get into — is filled as well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The catch outruns the vessel that was lent. James and John, who had nothing to do with the loan, go home with a full boat too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The loan of an empty boat and one more cast of a net he had already washed and put away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon gave the small thing he had at the end of a wasted night, and the return nearly sank two boats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Depart from me, for I am a sinful man, O Lord.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not thank you. The size of the catch tells him who is in the boat with him, and his first instinct is to ask Him to leave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not be afraid. From now on you will catch men.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they did with the haul.** *They brought their boats to land, left everything, and followed Him.* The biggest catch of Simon's life is sitting on the shore, and he walks away from it. It was never the point; it was the proof."}]}, "Luke 7:11–17": {"title": "The widow of Nain", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two crowds meet at a town gate, one coming in and one carrying a body out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Nain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name means pleasant. It sits in the Jezreel valley, in the territory of Issachar — the tribe whose blessing was about seeing that *the resting place was good and the land was pleasant*, and taking up a load anyway. See the note on Genesis 49:14–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who she was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **only son** his mother had, and she was **a widow**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the two facts that decide everything. No husband, and now no son. In that world she has no income, no legal standing and no protection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is burying her boy and her own future in the same procession."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Nobody asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the point at which the story differs from almost every other healing in the Gospels. There is no request."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not know who He is. She has not called out, touched a hem, sent friends, or torn open a roof. She is walking behind a bier, crying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the Lord saw her, **His heart went out to her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **splagchnizomai** — moved in the inward parts, the strongest word the language had for compassion. Luke says the compassion is for **her**, not for the boy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Please, do not cry\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "An impossible thing to say to a widow at her only son's funeral, unless you are about to do something about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The touch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He **stepped up and laid His hand on the frame** they were carrying, and the men bearing it **stood still**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Touching the bier made a man ceremonially unclean. He does it anyway, in public, in front of a great many people from the town."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bearers stop. Nobody has said anything yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Young man, I say to you, get up.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five words to a corpse. Not a prayer — He does not ask God for anything. He speaks to the boy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The dead man sat up and began to speak.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The handing over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jesus **gave him back to his mother**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the last act, and it is the whole point of the story. He does not enlist him, instruct him, or ask anything of either of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boy is put back into his mother's hands, and Jesus walks on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elisha needed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other raising in that valley is Elisha and the Shunammite's son, a few miles away over the hill."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That one took a shut door, a prayer, lying over the boy mouth to mouth, getting up and pacing the room, and stretching over him a second time. Then *call the Shunammite*, and *take your son* — she had to come and get him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here: one sentence, and He carries the boy across Himself. See the note on 2 Kings 4:32–37."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the crowd said.** *\"A great prophet has risen among us! God has come to help His people!\"* The Greek for *has come to help* is God visiting His people — the same word Zechariah used at the start of Luke, when *the Dayspring from on high has visited us.*"}]}, "Luke 7:40–43": {"title": "Two debts, one lender, and the question Simon answers correctly", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is at a Pharisee's dinner. A woman with a reputation has just come in and wept on His feet, and Simon has said nothing out loud."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the story is told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon's objection was internal: *if this man were a prophet, He would know what kind of woman this is.* Luke tells us he said it to himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus answers the thought. *Simon, there is something I want to tell you* — which is itself the reply to the doubt about whether He knew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two figures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five hundred denarii and fifty. A denarius was a day's wage, so roughly two years of work against two months."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gap is real and Jesus does not flatten it. One debt is genuinely ten times the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail that decides it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Neither of them had anything to pay with.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the equaliser, and it is easy to read past. The difference between the two men is the size of the debt, not their ability to clear it. Both are at zero."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten times nothing and one times nothing come to the same thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which of the two will love him more?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon answers correctly, and slightly grudgingly — *I suppose*. Jesus says he has judged rightly, and then applies it: you gave Me no water, no kiss, no oil; she has not stopped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her many sins have been forgiven — for she loved much.* Read alone that can sound like love earning the forgiveness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parable Jesus has just told settles the order. In the story, the cancelling comes first and the loving follows from it. The love is the evidence of the debt released, not the price of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last thing He says to her.** *Your faith has saved you. Go in peace.* Not your love. Luke has Him say the same words to the woman in the crowd who touched His clothes. See the note on Mark 5:30–34."}]}, "Luke 8:30–33": {"title": "Legion and the herd", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The largest concentration of the enemy named anywhere in the Gospels, and it takes one word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The eastern shore of the lake, the country of the Gerasenes — the old territory of Gad, out beyond the Jordan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gad's blessing was one line: *Gad will be attacked by raiders, but he will turn and strike at their heels.* The Hebrew hammers the word for *troop* five times over. See the note on Genesis 49:19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He crossed the water into the tribe of the troop, and the first thing that met Him on the beach was a legion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What is your name?\" \"**Legion**,\" he answered, because a great many demons had gone into him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Roman legion was some thousands of soldiers at full strength. The man does not give a name — he gives a headcount."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds *for we are many*, and the count of the pigs: about two thousand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is asking whom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **begged** Him not to order them away into the abyss… the demons **begged** Him to let them go into the pigs instead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in three verses. Whatever they are, they cannot move without permission, and the whole exchange is them asking and Him deciding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The abyss** is the bottomless place, the same word John uses in Revelation. It is what they are afraid of, and they name it themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing. No struggle, no formula, no repetition. *He gave them permission*, and they came out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has already told us what the man was before: no clothes, no house, living among the tombs, breaking chains and shackles, driven into the wilderness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after: *sitting at the feet of Jesus, clothed and in his right mind.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the town wanted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not more of it. *They asked Him to depart from them, for they were seized with great fear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He got into the boat and went."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one who wanted to come"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man begged to go with Him — a third begging in the chapter — and this is the one request Jesus refuses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Return to your own house, and tell what great things God has done for you.\" And he went his way and proclaimed throughout the whole city.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who could not be bound is sent home to talk about it, and he is the first person commissioned to preach in Gentile territory."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Gad's line, and where it lands.** *He will turn and strike at their heels.* The heel is the word from Genesis 3 — *He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise His heel.* Jacob used it over the son whose name means troop, and a legion went into the lake on that tribe's ground."}]}, "Luke 9:51–56": {"title": "\"You have no idea what kind of spirit you are speaking out of\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two disciples propose burning a village down, and they have a Bible verse for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the village refused Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The village would not receive Him, because He was clearly heading for Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samaritans worshipped on Mount Gerizim and regarded Jerusalem's temple as illegitimate. A pilgrim on the road there was a walking insult."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refusal was religious, and it was rude."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proposal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, do You want us to call fire down out of heaven and burn them up, **the way Elijah did**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James and John are not inventing this. Elijah had called fire down on soldiers sent to arrest him, in that same region, and they knew the passage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have precedent, provocation, and permission-seeking manners. Everything is in order except the request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rebuke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have no idea what kind of spirit you are speaking out of. The Son of Man did not come to destroy people's lives. He came to save them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue about Elijah. He tells them they have misread which era they are standing in and what He came to do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the diagnosis is about the **source** of the suggestion, not its logic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who these two were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pair who a chapter later ask for the seats on His right and left. Jesus nicknamed them Sons of Thunder, and Luke keeps giving us evidence for why."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John, who wanted to incinerate a village, is the one who ends up writing *God is love* and *we love Him because He loved us first.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they simply went on to another village.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No confrontation, no curse, no shaking of dust recorded. The response to being turned away is to keep walking."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He had just done.** *He set His face to go to Jerusalem, and nothing would turn Him from it.* Luke puts that sentence three verses before this — the road they were refused passage on was the road to the cross. He was on His way to die for Samaritans, and would not let anyone burn one."}]}, "Malachi 3:1–4": {"title": "\"Who can endure the day of His coming?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last book of the Old Testament announces a messenger, and then asks a question nobody wants to answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two figures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am sending My messenger, and he will prepare the way before Me. Then suddenly the Lord you are seeking will come to His temple.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospels take the first as John the Baptist — all four quote or allude to this verse — and the second as Christ. *Suddenly* is the word Malachi chooses, and four hundred silent years followed before it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who can endure the day of His coming? Who can stand when He appears?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people of Malachi's day had been complaining that God was absent and that serving Him was pointless. The prophet's answer is to ask whether they have thought through what His arrival would mean."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same reversal the prophets keep performing — Amos asking why anyone would long for the day of the LORD, since it is darkness and not light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two images"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are about cleaning, and both hurt:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**a refiner's fire** — the silversmith holds the metal in the heat until the impurities rise and can be lifted off", "**a launderer's soap** — a harsh alkaline lye, the strongest cleaning agent of the ancient world"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point of both is stated: *until they bring offerings to the LORD in righteousness*. The refining has an object, and it is worship that is actually worth something."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who gets refined.** *He will purify the Levites* — the priests, the professionals, the people running the temple. The chapter that goes on to charge a nation with robbing God opens by putting the clergy in the fire first."}]}, "Malachi 3:7–12": {"title": "The one place God says \"test Me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God forbids putting Him to the test everywhere else in scripture. Here He invites it, and the subject is giving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the passage begins"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not with money. *Return to Me, and I will return to you.* The tithe question arrives inside an invitation to come back, and it is God's answer to their own question — *how are we to return?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That order matters. This is not a demand for revenue. It is a description of what turning back looks like for a people who had drifted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bring the whole tithe into the storehouse… **Test Me in this**, says the LORD Almighty, and see if I will not throw open the floodgates of heaven and pour out so much blessing that there won't be room enough to store it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three promises follow, and they are specific:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the floodgates opened — the same word used of the windows of heaven at the flood", "**the devourer rebuked** — the locust stopped before it reaches the crop", "the vine not dropping its fruit early"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of the three are about **protection** rather than increase. What is already coming is guarded so it arrives."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tithe is older than the law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the point that decides how a Christian reads it. Abraham gave a tenth to Melchizedek in Genesis 14 — four hundred and thirty years before Sinai, with no commandment anywhere requiring it. Jacob promised a tenth at Bethel before there was a law to obey."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the tenth did not begin as a Mosaic regulation and does not stand or fall with one. It began as something a man did in response to a priest who blessed him, which is the order it still runs in: the blessing first, the tenth after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Hebrews says the priest receiving it now is the living one. See the note on Hebrews 7:8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this passage is not for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a lever for putting a believer in fear. Christ took the curse of the law at the cross, and a Christian giving under threat has misread which covenant they are standing in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read it the way it is written and the fear drops out anyway. *Test Me* is an invitation from someone confident of the outcome, addressed to people who had stopped expecting anything from Him. The passage is trying to get them to find out that He gives."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"All the nations will call you blessed.\"** The promise does not stop at full barns. It ends with a people so evidently cared for that everyone around notices — *yours will be a land of delight*. What is being described is not a transaction but a reputation."}]}, "Malachi 4:1–3": {"title": "Healing in His rays", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last page of the Old Testament, and it ends with a sunrise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sun of righteousness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But for you who fear My name, the **sun of righteousness** will rise with **healing in its rays**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *rays* is **kanaph** — wings. The same word used of the wings in Psalm 36, and the same word for the corner or hem of a garment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word behind the woman who touched the hem of His garment and was healed. The sun rising with healing in its wings, and a woman reaching for the wing of His robe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What rises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *a* sun. **The** sun of righteousness — righteousness itself coming up over the horizon like the morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order in the sentence is worth noticing: righteousness rises first, and healing is what is carried in its wings. The healing is not the sun. It is what the sun brings with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The calves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will go out and frolic like calves let loose from the stall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is to leap or skip. The picture is stall-fed calves — animals kept in and well fed — released into open ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the least solemn image in the prophets, and it is the note the Old Testament goes out on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The furnace"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The day is coming, burning like a furnace… not a root or a branch will be left.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same day is a furnace and a sunrise. Malachi does not soften either half. Which one it is depends on the phrase *for you who fear My name*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes next.** Four hundred years of silence, and then a man in the wilderness. The book's final lines promise Elijah before the great and dreadful day — and the first chapter of Luke says of John that he will go on before Him *in the spirit and power of Elijah*."}]}, "Mark 11:20–26": {"title": "\"Believe that you have received it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter points at a withered tree, and Jesus answers with three sentences about prayer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Have faith in God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first words, and they set the direction. The Greek can be rendered *have the faith of God* — faith that has God as its object and its source."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after this hangs on where the faith is placed, not on how much of it there is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense that carries the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever you ask for in prayer, **believe that you have received it**, and it will be yours.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is aorist — believe you *received*, past. Not that you will receive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sequence is: ask, take it as given, and then it becomes yours in experience. That is an odd order by every ordinary measure, and it is what the sentence says."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone says to this mountain, \"Be lifted up and thrown into the sea.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were walking from Bethany to Jerusalem, over the Mount of Olives, with the Dead Sea visible in the distance. **This** mountain, and that sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And notice the verb: *says to*. Twice. The faith in the illustration is exercised by speaking to the obstacle, not by talking to God about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Does not doubt in his heart\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word rendered *doubt* is **diakrino** — to separate, to divide, to make a distinction between two things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So doubting, in this sentence, is not weak feeling. It is being divided in yourself — one thing held in one hand and its opposite in the other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the two things being divided are named in the passage itself. Peter is astonished at a dried-up fig tree; Jesus answers by talking about a mountain going into the sea. A small withering and a mountain moved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who separates those — who has faith for the tree and none for the mountain — will not see either. That is what the verb rules out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same word, at the table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses **diakrino** twice in 1 Corinthians 11: *not **discerning** the Lord's body*, and *if we **judged** ourselves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things are handed over at that table, the body and the blood, and dividing them is the failure Paul names. It is the same verb, doing the same work: a man divided between two things God gave together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the notes on 1 Corinthians 11:27–30 and 1 Corinthians 11:31–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition He attaches"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And whenever you stand praying, if you are holding something against anyone, forgive it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus attaches one thing to the whole passage, and it is not effort or technique. It is forgiveness — and He makes it the immediate neighbour of the promise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever unanswered prayer is about, He points at the person you have not forgiven before He points anywhere else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the fig tree is there.** The withering brackets Mark's account of Jesus clearing the temple — the tree is cursed on the way in and found dead on the way out, with the temple scene in between. Mark's sandwich construction is deliberate: a tree in leaf with no fruit, and a house of prayer with no prayer in it."}]}, "Mark 14:22–26": {"title": "The bread and the cup", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mark's account of the table is the shortest, and the order of the sentences carries something the other accounts do not put as plainly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in Mark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He took a cup, gave thanks, and passed it to them, **and every one of them drank from it**. \"This is My blood,\" He said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They drank first. The explanation comes after the cup is empty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark is the only one who reports it in that sequence. In Matthew the words come with the cup; here the drinking is a completed act, and then Jesus tells them what they have just done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Until it went into them, it was wine at a Passover table like any other. What made it His blood was not a formula said over the cup. It was His word, and the disciples had already swallowed it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Torn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He picked up the bread. He gave thanks over it, **tore** it, and put it into their hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is to break, and the Illumination renders it *tore* — the tearing of a flat loaf by hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 53 says *He was wounded for our transgressions, He was bruised for our iniquities.* The bread is broken before it is given, and the giving is hand-to-hand: *put it into their hands.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two things named, and what each is for"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the body** — *this is My body*", "**the blood** — *the blood of the new covenant, poured out for many*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blood is given a stated purpose in the text: the covenant, and in Matthew's account, *for the forgiveness of sins.* The body is given without a stated purpose here — and Matthew 8:17 supplies it: *He took our infirmities and carried our diseases.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two elements, two provisions. Paul's warning in 1 Corinthians 11 about *not discerning the body* turns on holding them together. See the note on 1 Corinthians 11:27–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Every one of them drank\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records the participation. Judas is at that table in Mark's account — the betrayal has already been announced in the paragraph above."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last supper is not the last"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will not drink of the fruit of the vine again until the day I drink it new in the kingdom of God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it while the cup is still in His hand. The meal is left unfinished on purpose, with a date set for the rest of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they did next.** *Then they sang together, and went out to the Mount of Olives.* At Passover the closing hymn was the Hallel, Psalms 115–118 — which includes *I shall not die but live, and declare the works of the LORD*, and *the stone the builders rejected has become the cornerstone.* He sang that on the way to Gethsemane."}]}, "Mark 14:27–31": {"title": "\"Even if all of them fall away, I will not\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter's most famous sentence before the courtyard, and what is striking is what he skips over to say it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise he walks past"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says two things. First, *every one of you is going to fall away tonight* — with a quotation from Zechariah about the shepherd struck and the sheep scattered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *but after I am raised, I will go on ahead of you into Galilee.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That second sentence is a plan for after the failure. Before anyone has denied anything, a meeting place has been arranged. Peter answers the first sentence and says nothing about the second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What his answer reveals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even if all of them fall away, I will not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is the tell. Peter's confidence is measured against the other eleven, in front of the other eleven. And when Jesus tells him precisely how far he will go, he says it *again, harder*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark, whose Gospel the early church associated with Peter's own preaching, keeps all of it. Including the last line: *and every one of them said the same thing.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail Mark alone records"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before the rooster has crowed twice.* Only Mark has the two crowings, and only Mark later notes the first one, which Peter did not hear or did not heed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the kind of detail a man remembers about the worst night of his life."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What happened to the arrangement.** At the empty tomb the messenger says: go and tell the disciples *and Peter* that He is going ahead of you into Galilee. The plan made before the denial stands after it, and Peter's name is added to the message."}]}, "Mark 15:42–47": {"title": "Joseph of Arimathea", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The disciples have gone. A member of the council that condemned Him comes forward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **respected member of the council**, a man who was himself **waiting for the kingdom of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The council is the Sanhedrin — the body that had tried Jesus that same morning. Luke adds that he *had not consented to their decision and deed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew says he was rich. John says he was a disciple *secretly, for fear of the Jews*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"It took courage to walk in there\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says it outright, and Mark is the shortest Gospel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes to Pilate — a Roman governor who has just executed a man for sedition — and asks for the body of the condemned. There is no way to do that anonymously."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he had spent a career protecting goes in that request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Pilate's surprise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pilate was **surprised to hear that Jesus was already dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Crucifixion normally took days. Pilate sends for the centurion and checks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark alone records this exchange, and it settles the question of whether He was actually dead: the officer who ran the execution confirmed it to the governor before the body was released."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joseph did with his money"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph **bought** a length of linen, took Him down, wrapped Him in the cloth, and laid Him in a tomb that had been **cut out of the rock**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He paid for the linen. Matthew says the tomb was *his own new tomb, which he had hewn out of the rock*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A wealthy man's grave, bought and cut for himself, given away on a Friday afternoon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they made His grave with the wicked — but with the rich at His death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves. He was executed between two criminals and buried in a rich man's tomb, and Isaiah put the two clauses side by side seven hundred years earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "John's addition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nicodemus came with him — the other secret one, the man who had come by night in chapter 3 — carrying *a mixture of myrrh and aloes, about a hundred pounds*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a royal burial quantity, and an enormous expense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two members of the Sanhedrin who would not declare themselves while He was alive are the two men handling His body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The women watching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mary Magdalene and Mary the mother of Joses were watching, and **they saw where He was laid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark notes it because it matters two days later. They knew which tomb."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was in the tomb by Sunday.** The linen lying there, and the cloth that had been around His head *folded together in a place by itself.* John looked in and reports the arrangement of the cloth before he reports anything else."}]}, "Mark 16:15–18": {"title": "\"Tell the good news to every person alive\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last instruction in Mark, and it has no limits on it anywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go out into **the whole world**, and tell the good news to **every person alive**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *to all creation*. Matthew's version has *all the nations*; Mark's is wider still."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is excluded by geography, nation, or condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who He said it to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven men who had run. The chapter has just recorded Him rebuking them for unbelief and hardness of heart because they would not believe the ones who had seen Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He rebukes them, and in the next sentence hands them the world."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever **believes and is baptised** will be saved; whoever **refuses to believe** stands condemned.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two halves are not symmetrical. Believing and baptism on one side; on the other He does not say *whoever is not baptised* — only *whoever refuses to believe*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dividing line in the sentence is belief."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The signs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And these signs will **go along with** the ones who believe.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to follow after, to accompany. The signs follow the believers; the believers do not chase the signs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five are listed:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*in My name they will **drive out demons***", "*they will **speak in new tongues***", "*if they **pick up snakes** or **drink anything deadly**, it will do them no harm*", "*they will **lay their hands on the sick**, and the sick will get well*"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two that are not commands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here tells anyone to handle a snake or drink poison. Both are described as things that will not harm, not as things to attempt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is bitten by a viper on Malta, shakes it off into the fire, and the islanders wait for him to swell up and die. He had not gone looking for it — he was picking up firewood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In My name\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The authority is stated once and covers the list. Not in their own name, and not by any technique."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the last verse of Mark says happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they went out and preached everywhere, **the Lord working with them** and confirming the word through the accompanying signs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark ends with the two parties named: they preached, and He worked with them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where He is while they are doing it.** *So then, after the Lord had spoken to them, He was received up into heaven, and sat down at the right hand of God.* Mark puts the ascension and the seat in one line, and then says He was working with them. Seated, and working."}]}, "Mark 4:35–41": {"title": "\"Asleep on a cushion\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mark keeps a detail the other Gospels leave out, and it is the kind of thing an eyewitness remembers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cushion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus was in the stern, asleep on a cushion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the* cushion — the definite article. It is the steersman's seat cushion, a fixed part of a fishing boat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Mark records it, and Mark's Gospel is the one the early church associated with Peter's preaching. Peter owned a boat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us cross over to the other side.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence before the storm. Everything that happens next takes place inside that instruction — they are going where He said they were going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question they ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Teacher, do You not care that we are going down?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *save us*. It is an accusation, and it is about His caring rather than His power. Fishermen who have been on that lake all their lives are certain they are sinking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says to the sea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Peace. Be still.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *be still* is the word used for muzzling — put a muzzle on it. It is the same word Mark used a chapter earlier when Jesus silenced an unclean spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks to the storm the way He speaks to a demon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two fears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why are you so frightened? Do you still have no faith?\" And a deep awe came over them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark is precise: they were afraid of the storm, and after it stopped they were more afraid. The word for their reaction afterwards is the great fear reserved for the presence of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask *who is this?* — and the answer, for anyone who knew the Psalms, is that stilling the sea is something God does."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the disciples had already been told.** He had just spent the afternoon teaching in parables from that boat. The storm follows immediately, and Mark places it there deliberately: the men who had heard the word all day are the ones asked whether they still have no faith."}]}, "Mark 5:11–13": {"title": "They had to ask", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, and the striking thing is who is doing the requesting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The begging"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The spirits begged Him.* Mark uses the verb twice in this scene — earlier they had begged not to be sent out of the region."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever they are, they cannot move without permission. The whole exchange is them asking and Him deciding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the request reveals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Send us into the pigs. Let us go into them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They want somewhere to be. The parallel account in Luke has them begging not to be sent into the abyss."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same thing the returning spirit is looking for in Matthew 12 — somewhere to settle, a house to occupy. See the note on Matthew 12:43–45."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He let them go\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words, and Mark does not explain them. The permission is granted without a recorded reason."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Readers have argued about the pigs for centuries — whose they were, what the loss meant, why Jesus allowed it. Mark says nothing about any of that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he does record is the count: *about two thousand of them*. It is the only indication in the passage of the scale of what had been living in one man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in the man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the chapter he gives his name as Legion, *for we are many* — a Roman legion being some thousands of soldiers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody could bind him, not even with chains. He had been breaking irons and cutting himself with stones among the tombs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The pigs stampeded down the steep bank into the lake and were drowned.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The destruction the spirits had been working on the man goes into the herd, and the man is left sitting, dressed, in his right mind."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the region asks for afterwards.** Not more healings. They *begged Jesus to leave*, and He got into the boat. The third begging in the chapter comes from the townspeople, and it is the only one about which He does what is asked without a word. See the note on Mark 5:18–20."}]}, "Mark 5:18–20": {"title": "The one man Jesus told to stay", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man who has just been set free asks to come along, and it is the one request of that kind in the Gospels that gets turned down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As Jesus was getting back into the boat, the man who had been set free asked if he could come along with Him. But Jesus would not let him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone else is called to follow. This man asks to and is sent home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been living among the tombs, cutting himself with stones, so strong that chains would not hold him. Now he is dressed and in his right mind and wants to get in the boat, and Jesus says no."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is sent to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go home, to your own people, and tell them what the Lord has done for you, and how much mercy He had on you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words carry the commission: **what the Lord has done**, and **mercy**. No training, no doctrine, no instruction on what to say beyond his own story."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been an eyewitness for about an hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the man went off and began telling it all through the Decapolis, the ten towns of that region.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was told to go home to his own people. He covered ten cities."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Gentile territory — the region where the pigs were, where the locals had just asked Jesus to leave. The first missionary to the Decapolis is a man with no qualifications except a before and an after."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What happened later.** When Jesus returns to that area in Mark 7 and 8, the reception is completely different — crowds come to Him and He feeds four thousand there. Nobody says why. But somebody had been telling that region what the Lord had done for him for the intervening months."}]}, "Mark 5:30–34": {"title": "He stopped a crowd to find her", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman gets what she came for without permission, and Jesus stops everything to make sure she knows she has it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He felt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus knew at once, in Himself, that power had gone out from Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word is **dunamis** — power, ability, the word behind our *dynamite*. Mark says Jesus registered it going out, immediately, and turned round in the middle of a crush of people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was on His way to a dying child, with a synagogue ruler beside Him. He stopped anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disciples' objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look at this crowd pressing in on You from all sides, and You ask who touched You?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are right about the crowd. Hundreds of people were making contact with Him. Their point is that the question is unanswerable — and Mark's point is that one touch was different from all the others."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But He kept looking around to find her.* He would not move on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it mattered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had been bleeding twelve years — which under the law meant twelve years of being ceremonially unclean, unable to touch or be touched without passing it on. She had come up behind Him deliberately, hoping to take the healing and slip away unnoticed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He would not let her leave anonymous. And when she came forward *shaking and afraid*, He gave her the one word that ended the twelve years socially as well as physically:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Daughter.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only time in the Gospels He calls anyone that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He said next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your faith has made you well. Go in peace, and be free of this trouble for good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He credits her — not the hem, not the accident of proximity. Her faith. And He sends her away with a blessing rather than a rebuke for taking without asking."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The interruption cost something.** While He was talking to her, the message came that Jairus' daughter had died. Jesus had stopped for one woman and a child died in the delay — and then He raised her. Mark tells both stories inside each other on purpose."}]}, "Mark 5:38–43": {"title": "\"Talitha koum\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A house full of professional mourners, and Jesus clears them all out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The noise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus heard the noise of it — the weeping and the wailing and all the commotion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Funeral wailing was hired. Even a poor family was expected to provide mourners and flute players, and the volume was the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says, and what they do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The child is not dead. She is asleep.\" And they laughed in His face.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark keeps the laughter in. The people making the noise switch to mockery instantly, which tells you what the grief was worth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is not denying the death — He uses *asleep* the way the New Testament uses it everywhere, of a state that ends. See the note on 1 Thessalonians 4:13–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is allowed in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He put them all outside.* Then five people: the father, the mother, and Peter, James and John."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parents are in the room. Whatever happens next, they see it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Talitha koum\" — which means, \"Little girl, I am speaking to you. Get up.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark preserves the Aramaic, which he does only a handful of times. This is what He actually said, in the language He said it in — a household phrase, the sort of thing a parent says in the morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No formula, no gesture. He takes her hand and speaks to her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said they should give her something to eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark ends the raising of a dead child with a note about breakfast. The practicality is the tell — the girl is not a vision or a spirit. She is a twelve-year-old who has not eaten."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The detail Mark saves for the end.** *She was twelve years old.* And the woman healed on the way to this house had been bleeding twelve years. Mark deliberately interleaves the two stories, and gives the same number to both. See the note on Mark 5:30–34."}]}, "Mark 8:1–10": {"title": "The second feeding, and the question they should not have asked", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four thousand people, seven loaves, and disciples who have already watched this happen once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What starts it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have compassion for the crowd, because they have already been with Me three days and have nothing to eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He raises it, not them. And the reason given is the length of time they have stayed — three days without provisions, some of them a long way from home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If I send them home hungry, they will collapse on the way.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where could anyone get enough bread to feed these people out here in this lonely place?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been present at the five thousand. Mark has told that story two chapters earlier, with twelve baskets left over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He records the question without comment, which is more damning than an explanation would be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The differences from the first feeding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark keeps the numbers distinct, and they are not the same story told twice:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "| | five thousand | four thousand | |---|---|---| | loaves | five | seven | | baskets left | twelve, *kophinoi* | seven, *spyrides* | | location | Jewish side of the lake | Gentile region, the Decapolis |"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even the word for basket changes. The second is the large hamper — the kind Paul was later lowered over a wall in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the Gentile setting matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the region where the man freed from the legion had been telling people what the Lord had done for him. See the note on Mark 5:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd that stays three days to listen is in the country that once asked Jesus to leave."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jesus says about it later.** In the boat He asks them: when I broke five loaves for five thousand, how many baskets? Twelve. And the seven for four thousand? Seven. *Do you still not understand?* He treats the two feedings as a single lesson they had failed twice."}]}, "Mark 8:34–38": {"title": "\"What could a man possibly offer in exchange for his soul?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus calls the crowd over — not just the disciples — and says the hardest thing in Mark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter has just confessed Him as the Messiah, and then rebuked Him for saying He would be killed. Jesus has answered that sharply."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then He calls the crowd, so that what follows is said to everybody rather than to the twelve."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Pick up his cross\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image was not yet a metaphor. Crucifixion was a Roman execution his hearers had seen, and a condemned man carried the crossbeam to the place through public streets."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone in that crowd knew what a man carrying a cross was: someone already sentenced, walking to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exchange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever tries to hold on to his life will lose it, and whoever gives up his life for My sake and for the sake of the good news will save it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *life* here is **psyche** — life, self, soul. The same word runs through the next two sentences, which is why they belong together:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What good does it do a man to gain the whole world and lose his own soul? What could a man possibly offer in exchange for his soul?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commercial language"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gain*, *lose*, *exchange* — the vocabulary is a merchant's, and the question is about price."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second question is the sharper one. Suppose the trade has already been made and the world is in hand: what is left to buy the soul back with? The answer the question expects is nothing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Whose loss is being described.** *For My sake and for the sake of the good news.* Jesus does not commend self-denial in general — Mark's Gospel is not asking for austerity. The letting go is for a person, and He names Himself."}]}, "Mark 9:14–24": {"title": "\"Help me with the part of me that does not\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most honest sentence anyone says in the Gospels, and Jesus answers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The father's careful wording"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If there is anything You can do, have pity on us and help us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been living with this for years — *since he was small* — and he has just watched the disciples fail. The *if* is not rudeness. It is a man who has stopped expecting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice also *help **us***. He is not only asking for the boy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with the \"if\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you can believe — everything is possible for the one who believes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He picks up the father's own word and hands it back. The man had put the *if* on Jesus' ability; Jesus moves it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is worth reading carefully, because it can be made cruel. Jesus is not saying the healing depends on the father generating enough certainty — the next thirty seconds rule that out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, I do believe. Help me with the part of me that does not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves at once, and the man does not pretend. He has faith and he has unbelief, and he says so, and he asks for help with the second."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus heals the boy immediately. No further requirement, no waiting for the doubt to clear."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What that settles.** Whatever \"believing\" means in this passage, it evidently includes a man who admits mid-sentence that he does not fully believe. The faith that works here is not a quantity achieved. It is a request made to the right person, doubt and all."}]}, "Matthew 1:12–16": {"title": "The one link in the chain that is worded differently", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Thirty-nine times in this chapter Matthew writes the same formula. On the fortieth he changes it, and the change is the point of the whole genealogy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel. Zerubbabel was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Eliakim…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Down through the list, one word doing the work each time — the ordinary verb for begetting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The break"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*Jacob was the father of Joseph, **the husband of Mary**, and it was from Mary that Jesus was born, the One who is called the Messiah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph is not said to be the father of Jesus. He is identified by his marriage, and the birth is attributed to Mary. In Greek the relative pronoun before *from whom* is feminine singular — it can only refer to her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has just spent forty-one verses establishing a legal line and then, at the last link, declines to say the thing the whole form was built to say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the legal line still matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus needed a claim to David's throne, and in that culture it came through the legal father. Joseph, descended from David through Solomon and the kings, gives Him that claim by adoption."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What Joseph does not give Him is blood — which is exactly as well, because that line runs through Jeconiah, and Jeremiah had declared that none of Jeconiah's descendants would ever sit on David's throne. See the note on Jeremiah 22:28–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title comes down the legal line. The life does not."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who else is in the list.** Four women are named before Mary, and Matthew did not have to name any of them: Tamar, who posed as a prostitute; Rahab, who was one; Ruth, a Moabite; and *the wife of Uriah*, whom he pointedly does not call Bathsheba. A genealogy designed to impress would have left all four out. This one puts them in."}]}, "Matthew 10:16–20": {"title": "Snakes and doves", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two animals, chosen for one quality each, and neither is chosen for the obvious reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pairing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be as wise as snakes and as harmless as doves.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the snake** — not for cunning in the sinister sense. The word is *phronimos*, shrewd, practically intelligent, the same word Jesus uses of the wise man who built on rock and the wise servant left in charge.", "**the dove** — the word is *akeraios*, literally *unmixed*. Pure in the sense of nothing added, no hidden second motive."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the instruction is: think clearly about what you are walking into, and have nothing in you that needs concealing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either quality without the other goes wrong quickly. Shrewd without unmixed is just cunning; unmixed without shrewd is walking into a wolf pack blindfolded — which is what He has just said He is sending them into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look at what I am doing: I am sending you out like sheep into the middle of wolves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the danger before He gives the instruction, and He does not pretend the odds are good. Sheep among wolves is not a fair fight, and nothing in the sentence suggests otherwise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He promises instead of safety"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not fret about how to speak or what to say. What you are to say will be given to you at that hour, because it is not you speaking; it is the Spirit of your Father speaking through you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The provision is not protection from the councils and the floggings — He says plainly those are coming. It is words, supplied at the hour they are needed, by Someone else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The turn in the middle.** *That will be your chance to testify.* Being dragged in front of governors and kings is described as an opportunity rather than a disaster. The whole passage assumes the trouble and then repurposes it."}]}, "Matthew 11:20–24": {"title": "The towns that saw the most", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus names three towns and says they are worse off than the cities everyone in His audience would have named as the worst."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Chorazin and Bethsaida** measured against **Tyre and Sidon** — the Phoenician cities the prophets denounced for centuries", "**Capernaum** measured against **Sodom**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in each case the verdict runs the same way: those places would have turned if they had seen what these towns saw."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Capernaum had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was His base of operations. Peter's house was there, and the synagogue. Matthew was called at its tax booth. The centurion's servant, Peter's mother-in-law, Jairus' daughter — the concentration of miracles in that one town is remarkable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You were lifted up to the sky* is a quotation aimed originally at the king of Babylon in Isaiah 14, and the second half of the sentence comes with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had not done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because they had not changed their minds.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because they attacked Him, and not because they drove Him out — Nazareth did that and is not on this list. The charge is that nothing changed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They watched, and stayed the same."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle underneath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "More evidence means more responsibility. Jesus says it elsewhere in plainer terms — to whom much is given, much will be required."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That cuts toward anyone with easy access to Scripture, sermons, and a Bible on their phone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What surrounds this passage.** Immediately before, John the Baptist's question and Jesus' defence of him. Immediately after, *come to Me, all of you who are worn out* — the gentlest invitation in the Gospels. The hardest words Matthew records and the kindest are four verses apart, spoken in the same breath. See the note on Matthew 11:28–30."}]}, "Matthew 11:28–30": {"title": "Whose yoke it is, and what \"easy\" actually says", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted invitation in the Gospels, and the picture in the middle of it is the part most likely to be missed by a reader who has never worked an animal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "A yoke is for two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **zygos** is the wooden beam across the necks of a pair of oxen. A single ox does not wear one. The whole point of the device is that two animals pull together and the load is shared between them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So *take My yoke on your shoulders* is not an offer of a lighter burden to carry alone. It is an offer to be paired with Him. That is why the sentence can hold two things that sound contradictory — come to Me and rest, and take up a yoke — without any strain. The rest is not the absence of work. It is the end of pulling alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"My yoke\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The emphasis in the Greek falls on the possessive, and it matters, because everybody He was speaking to already had a yoke on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rabbis of the period spoke of the *yoke of the law* — the teaching a disciple took on from his teacher. Jesus is not asking people to pick up a burden they had been free of. He is telling them to swap."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is also why the next line is *learn from Me*. It is the language of apprenticeship, and the reason He gives for coming is not that He is powerful: *because I am gentle, and I am humble in heart*. He recommends Himself on His manner, which is not how anyone recruits disciples."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word behind \"fits easily\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chrestos.** It means kind, good, serviceable — and it was used of things that were well made, and of wine that had mellowed. In the Greek Old Testament it is the word in *taste and see that the LORD is good*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Translating it as *easy* has done some damage, because it sounds like a promise that nothing will be demanding. The word is nearer to *this one fits*. A badly made yoke rubs an animal raw over a day's work. A well-made one is shaped to the neck wearing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The claim is not that there is no work. It is that this yoke will not take the skin off you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is invited"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of you who are worn out and carrying too much.* The two words describe being exhausted by labour and being loaded up by someone else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no other entry condition in the sentence. Not repentance first, not understanding first. Tiredness is the qualification, which is a strange basis for an invitation and is exactly what makes this one memorable."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where else this word turns up.** *Rest* here is the same idea running through Hebrews 3 and 4 — a rest already prepared, entered by coming rather than by achieving. Matthew's version puts a person and a yoke at the centre of it: the rest is not a place so much as a partnership."}]}, "Matthew 12:15–21": {"title": "The bruised reed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Pharisees have just decided to destroy Him. Matthew's next sentence is what Jesus does about it, and then the longest Old Testament quotation in his Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He withdrew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing this, Jesus **withdrew** from that place. Large crowds followed Him, and **He healed all of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not confront the plot and He does not stop healing. He moves, and the healing goes with Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of them* — Matthew's habit. He does not report a percentage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the quotation is here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew reaches for Isaiah 42 to explain a man walking away from a fight, and the prophecy he chooses is about a servant who does not raise His voice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the servant does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will not argue or shout. No one will hear His voice raised in the streets.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three negatives before a single positive. The portrait is drawn by what is absent from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reed and the wick"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **bruised reed** He will not break, and a **smoldering wick** He will not put out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A reed grew by the Jordan and was used for measuring, for writing, and cut into a simple pipe. Bruised, it is worthless — you throw it away and cut another, because reeds cost nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A wick with no flame left is smoke and smell. You pinch it out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both images are of something that has stopped doing the one thing it exists for, and is worth less than the effort of keeping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is discarded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Until"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*…**until He carries justice through to victory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gentleness has a destination. It is not resignation or a mild temperament; it is restraint held on purpose, and the sentence says where it is going."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *justice* in Isaiah is **mishpat** — the right ordering of things, what ought to be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And in His name the nations will place their hope.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew, writing to Jewish readers, ends the quotation on the Gentiles — placed here, immediately after Israel's teachers have resolved to kill Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why Matthew put it here.** The chapter's argument is about the Sabbath and a withered hand. A man whose hand had stopped working was, by any practical measure, a bruised reed. He was not broken; he was told to stretch it out. See the note on Matthew 12:9–14."}]}, "Matthew 12:33–37": {"title": "\"The mouth speaks out of whatever fills the heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus explains where words come from, and it is not the mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Make the tree good and its fruit will be good**… because **a tree is known by its fruit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "You do not fix fruit. You cannot improve an apple by working on the apple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fruit is diagnostic — it tells you what the tree is — and the remedy in the sentence is applied to the tree."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The mouth speaks out of whatever fills the heart.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *out of the abundance of the heart* — out of the overflow, what is in excess."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Speech is described as spillage. What comes out under pressure, without editing, is simply the level of what was already in there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The storehouse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"A good person brings good things out of **the good stored up inside**, and an evil person brings evil things out of **the evil stored up inside**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thesauros** — a treasury, a storehouse, the word behind *treasure*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both people are described as bringing out. Nobody manufactures on the spot; they withdraw from a store, and the store was filled earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which is why guarding it matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Above all else, guard your heart, for from it flow the springs of life.* Proverbs puts the heart first and everything else downstream — mouth, eyes, feet, in that order. See the note on Proverbs 4:20–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon's word for *guard* is a sentry's word. It assumes something is trying to get in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Every careless word\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"On the day of judgment people will give an account for **every careless word** they have spoken.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **argos** — idle, inactive, unemployed. Not wicked words. Words that were doing no work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say they carry no weight. He says they will be accounted for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**By your words you will be declared right, and by your words you will be condemned.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb in the first half is the justification word Paul uses all through Romans."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Words are treated here not as a report on the heart but as evidence in a hearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who He is saying it to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Pharisees, who had just said He cast out demons by Beelzebub. He calls them a *brood of snakes* in the middle of this passage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had watched a blind and mute man healed and produced that sentence about it — which is exactly his point. Their words came out of a store, and the store is what he is naming."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other place this principle runs.** Ten men looked at Canaan and said *we are not able*, and two looked at the same country and said *we are well able*. Same forty days, same giants. And God's answer to the first group was *I will do to you the very things you spoke in My hearing.* See the notes on Numbers 13:26–33 and Numbers 14:26–35."}]}, "Matthew 12:43–45": {"title": "\"Empty, swept out and neatly arranged\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A short, strange parable about a house, and the problem with the house is not that it is dirty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When it arrives it finds the place **empty, swept out and neatly arranged**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them sounds like an improvement. The house is clean. Nothing is out of place. Somebody has clearly done work on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And that is exactly the condition in which it gets taken over. The trouble is the first word: **empty**. Nobody moved in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wandering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It wanders through waterless places looking for rest and finds none.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The detail is offered without explanation and Matthew does not develop it. What the parable does say is that the spirit's preference is to return to what it knows: *I will go back to the house I left.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Reform is not the same as occupancy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the argument. A life can be tidied — habits stopped, behaviour corrected, the place genuinely swept — and still be unoccupied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parable's ending is blunt: *that person ends up worse off than before.* A cleaned house with nobody in it is more vulnerable than a lived-in one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Jesus was talking about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is exactly how it will be for this evil generation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He applies it to a generation, not to an individual. The context is a demand for a sign from scribes and Pharisees who had just accused Him of casting out demons by Beelzebul — religious people, morally scrupulous, whose reform had never included receiving Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The alternative in the same chapter.** *Whoever does the will of My Father in heaven is My brother and sister and mother.* Jesus says it moments before this, pointing at His disciples. The empty house and the family are the two options Matthew 12 puts side by side."}]}, "Matthew 12:9–14": {"title": "Stretch out your hand", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man with a withered hand, a room full of men watching to see what Jesus will do, and an instruction to do the one thing he cannot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were there for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Looking for something to accuse Him of, they asked Him, \"Is it permitted to heal on the Sabbath?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew tells us the motive before he tells us the question. Nobody in the room is asking about the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sheep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Suppose one of you has a single sheep, and it falls into a pit on the Sabbath. Will you not take hold of it and lift it out?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers with their own practice. Not a theory about the Sabbath — what they would in fact do, that same day, for one animal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And a human being is worth so much more than a sheep.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is from the lesser to the greater, and the whole weight of it sits on the value of the man standing in front of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Stretch out your hand.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hand is withered. Stretching it out is exactly what it cannot do — that is the definition of the condition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is offered first. No touch is recorded, no word over the hand. He is told to do the impossible thing, and Matthew's next clause is *He stretched it out.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As sound as the other one\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The restoration is measured against something. There was a good hand on the other arm the whole time, and the healed one comes back matching it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the healing produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the Pharisees went out and began making plans against Him, to find a way to destroy Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew puts the plotting immediately after the healing, without a gap. The first recorded plan to kill Jesus in this Gospel follows a restored hand."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He said before the healing.** *So yes, it is permitted to do good on the Sabbath.* He does not argue that the Sabbath is suspended. He rules on what it was for."}]}, "Matthew 15:21–28": {"title": "\"Even the little dogs eat the crumbs\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hardest conversation Jesus has with anyone who came to Him for help, and it ends with the highest compliment He pays anybody in Matthew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus went out from there and departed to the region of **Tyre and Sidon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the far north-west coast — the border of **Asher**, whose whole blessing in Genesis 49 is one line about food:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Asher's food will be rich; he will provide **delicacies fit for a king**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only recorded time He left Israel's territory, and the conversation He has there is about bread and a table. See the note on Genesis 49:20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three obstacles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She meets a wall three times:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*He did not answer her a word*", "*I was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel*", "*It is not right to take the children's bread and toss it to the little dogs*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Silence, then a limit, then what sounds like an insult. And in between, the disciples asking Him to get rid of her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does with each one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She keeps coming. After the silence she comes closer; after the limit she kneels; after the dogs remark she takes the image and finishes it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yes, Lord — and even the little dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their master's table.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not dispute the order He has stated. She accepts it entirely and points out that it does not exclude her: if the children are being fed, there is something under the table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word He used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kunarion** — a diminutive. Not the scavenging street dogs, but the little ones inside a house. It is a household word, and she hears the household in it — *their master's table*, not somebody else's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a crumb did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She asked for a crumb and she got what she asked for. *Let it be done for you just as you desire* — and a demon left her daughter that hour, at a distance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what fell off the edge of the table, unnoticed by the people eating at it: enough to break a demonic hold on a child in another house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The children's bread is what the crumb broke off. Whoever is at that table is not reaching for fragments — they are handed the loaf, torn and put into their hands, and told to eat it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Woman, your faith is great.* He says this to exactly two people in Matthew, and both are Gentiles; the other is the centurion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let it be done for you just as you desire.* And her daughter was healed from that hour, at a distance, with the mother still standing in front of Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she called Him first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have mercy on me, Lord, Son of David.* A Canaanite woman, from the country Israel was told to drive out, addressing Him by His messianic title."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had the doctrine right before the conversation started."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the delay.** Nothing here suggests reluctance in Him — Matthew has already shown Him healing a Roman's servant on request. What the exchange produced was an answer from her that has been quoted ever since, and a public verdict on her faith in front of disciples who had just asked Him to send her away."}]}, "Matthew 16:13–20": {"title": "\"Who do you say I am?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus asks two questions in a place chosen for the asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He asks it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Caesarea Philippi* — as far north as He ever took them, at the foot of Mount Hermon. The town sat beside a cave sacred to the Greek god Pan, with a temple to Caesar Augustus built nearby."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Surrounded by the shrines of other lords and other sons of gods, He asks who they say He is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who do people say the Son of Man is?* Then: *But **you** — who do you say I am?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first is a survey and they answer it easily. The second is emphatic in Greek — *you*, plural, as against everybody else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reported opinions are all complimentary: John the Baptist, Elijah, Jeremiah, one of the prophets. Every one of them is a category He does not fit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are the Messiah, the Son of the living God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves matter. Messiah is the office; Son of the living God is the identity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No human being revealed this to you. My Father in heaven showed it to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not congratulate Peter on his insight. He identifies the source, and it is not Peter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rock"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are Peter, and on this rock I will build My church.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wordplay is deliberate — *Petros*, a stone, and *petra*, rock. Christians have argued for centuries over whether the rock is Peter himself or the confession he has just made."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is not in dispute is the builder and the outcome. *I* will build, and *the gates of the realm of the dead will not hold out against it.* Gates are defensive. The picture is of the church advancing on death's stronghold, not enduring a siege."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What happens four verses later.** The same man, who has just been told the Father revealed this to him, is called Satan for objecting to the cross. Matthew puts the two scenes side by side without softening either. See the note on Matthew 16:21–23."}]}, "Matthew 16:21–23": {"title": "\"A stone in My path\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verses after being told the Father revealed the truth to him, Peter is called Satan. Matthew puts the two scenes back to back on purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From that time on\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From that time on Jesus began to make it plain.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The confession changes what He tells them. Once they know who He is, He starts explaining what that means — and what it means is Jerusalem, suffering, death, and the third day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew notes it as a turning point in the Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter took Him aside and began to correct Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the one used for rebuking — the same word Jesus uses on demons and on a storm. Peter is not raising a question. He is telling Him off, privately, to spare Him the embarrassment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Never, Lord! This will never happen to You.* In Greek it is emphatic: by no means, not ever."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Get behind Me, Satan. You are a stone in My path.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **skandalon** — the trigger of a trap, the thing that makes you stumble. Peter has just been called a rock to build on; now he is a rock to trip over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because you are not thinking the thoughts of God but the thoughts of men.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the charge, and it is worth being precise about. Peter is not accused of malice. He is accused of ordinary human reasoning — a friend who does not want the man he loves to be killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the most natural sentiment in the passage, and Jesus identifies its source as the same one that offered Him the kingdoms of the world without a cross."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The pattern.** Both temptations come from someone who cares. The devil in the wilderness offered a route to the crown with no suffering in it; Peter offers the same route in the voice of a friend. Jesus answers both with the same refusal. See the note on Matthew 4:1–4."}]}, "Matthew 18:15–20": {"title": "\"Where two or three come together\" — the setting of a much-loved verse", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*Where two or three come together in My name, I am there among them* is quoted at small gatherings everywhere, and it is a true and warm thing to say. It is also the last line of a paragraph about something else entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the paragraph is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The subject is a Christian who has wronged another Christian, and the process is laid out in stages:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["go to him **privately** first", "if that fails, take one or two others", "if that fails, tell the church", "if that fails, treat him as an outsider"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every step is designed to keep the circle as small as possible for as long as possible. The aim is stated at the start: *you have won your brother back*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two or three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The number is not chosen for its smallness. It comes from the previous sentence — *every matter may be settled on the word of two or three witnesses* — which is the standard of evidence from Deuteronomy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So when Jesus says *where two or three come together in My name, I am there*, He is promising His presence to the hardest thing a church ever does: the conversation where somebody is confronted about a wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that does to the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not shrink it. It makes it braver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comfort of a handful of believers meeting with Christ among them is real and the verse carries it. But it was given first to people about to sit down in a room and try to win somebody back, and it says that Christ is in the room for that."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On binding and loosing.** The phrase belongs to the same setting — the language is from rabbinic practice, where binding and loosing meant declaring what was forbidden and permitted. In context it is about the church's judgement in exactly the situation being described, not a general formula."}]}, "Matthew 2:1–2": {"title": "The men who came the wrong way", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Foreigners arrive in Jerusalem asking a question that nobody in Jerusalem had thought to ask."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Magi** — the word covers astrologers, court advisers and priests of the eastern kingdoms. Not kings, and Matthew never says how many; the three comes from the number of gifts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were Gentiles, from outside the covenant, practising a discipline Israel's own law forbade. And they are the first people in Matthew's Gospel to worship Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is the child who has been born King of the Jews?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask it in the capital, in the reign of a king who was famously paranoid about his throne — a man who had already killed his wife and three of his own sons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They walk into Herod's city and ask where the real king is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they went wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They went to a palace. It is the obvious place to look for a royal birth, and it is the wrong one — a detour that puts Herod on the scent and leads to the soldiers being sent to Bethlehem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The star had brought them a long way. It did not take them to Jerusalem; their own reasonable assumption did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in Jerusalem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief priests and scribes answer Herod's question immediately: Bethlehem, and they quote Micah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They knew the address. Five miles down the road, and there is no record of any of them going to look."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two groups had.** The scholars had the Scripture and stayed home. The foreigners had a light in the sky, an incomplete map, and enough interest to travel for months. Matthew reports both without comment, in the second chapter of a Gospel written to Jewish readers."}]}, "Matthew 2:16–18": {"title": "Rachel crying for her children", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The part of the Christmas story that does not appear on cards, and Matthew does not soften it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every boy two years old and under was put to death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethlehem was a small town; historians estimate the number of children killed in the low dozens. That is not a comfort, and Matthew does not offer it as one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herod's record makes the account entirely credible — he executed his own sons and had ordered notable men killed at his death so the country would mourn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A voice is heard in Ramah… Rachel crying for her children, and she will not be comforted, because they are no more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew reaches into Jeremiah 31, where Rachel — buried near Bethlehem, mother of the northern tribes — is pictured weeping as her descendants are marched into exile."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note what Matthew does **not** do. He does not say the mothers should be comforted. He quotes a verse in which comfort is explicitly refused."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the quotation comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read on in Jeremiah 31 and the next verse is God's answer: *restrain your voice from weeping and your eyes from tears, for your work will be rewarded — they will come back from the land of the enemy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a few verses further, the same chapter contains the promise of the new covenant — the law written on hearts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew quotes the grief and leaves his readers to know where it is going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is in the Gospel at all"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because it happened, and because Matthew is telling the truth about the world Jesus was born into. A king who kills children is part of the Christmas story."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one detail of mercy.** Joseph is warned in a dream and takes the child to Egypt. Jesus survives — and Matthew, who has already had Him called out of Egypt like Israel, is showing a nation's story replayed in one family: down into Egypt, and out again."}]}, "Matthew 20:24–28": {"title": "\"Not to be served, but to serve\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two brothers have just asked for the best seats, the other ten are furious about it, and Jesus responds by redefining the whole idea of position."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says about the world"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You know how it works among the nations. Their rulers throw their weight around, and the great ones make their authority felt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both verbs in the Greek are compounds with *kata* — lording it **down** over, exercising authority **against**. The preposition does the work: it is power exerted downward onto people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *it must not be that way among you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inversion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever wants to be great among you must be the one who serves you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice He does not say wanting to be great is wrong. He does not rebuke the ambition — He redirects it. The route to greatness is not blocked; it runs the other way from what everyone assumes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for servant is **diakonos**, the ordinary word for someone who waits at a table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give His life as a ransom for many.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern is not offered as a principle but as a biography. He is describing what He is doing, in a chapter where He has just told them for the third time that He is going to Jerusalem to be killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ransom** is *lutron* — the price paid to release a captive or free a slave. It is the noun behind the redemption word in Ephesians 1:7."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the brothers had actually asked for.** Seats at His right and left in His kingdom. When Matthew's Gospel finally shows two people at His right and left, it is at the crucifixion, and they are criminals. The Gospel answers the request without comment, several chapters later."}]}, "Matthew 20:8–12": {"title": "Paid last first", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A landowner hires men all day at different hours and pays every one of them the same. The detail that makes the parable work is the order of the queue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Beginning with the last ones hired\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He could have paid the first group first, sent them home content, and settled with the rest quietly. Nobody would have known."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He deliberately arranges it so the men who worked twelve hours watch the men who worked one receive a full day's wage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not carelessness. He wants them to see it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the first group then expects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the ones hired first came up, they assumed they would get more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had been said to suggest it. They had agreed a denarius at dawn — the standard day's wage, a fair rate, exactly what they asked for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The expectation is generated entirely by watching somebody else be treated generously."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have made them equal to us, who carried the weight of the whole day and the burning heat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The grievance is not that they were underpaid. It is the word **equal**. They object to the last men being brought level with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sharp end of the parable: generosity to somebody else felt like an injury, in men who had received precisely what they were promised."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The landowner's answer.** *Am I not allowed to do what I want with what is mine? Or is your eye evil because I am good?* The Greek idiom is about a grudging, envious eye. And the parable ends where the chapter before it ended — the last will be first, and the first last. Jesus tells it to disciples who had just asked what they would get for having left everything."}]}, "Matthew 21:12–17": {"title": "The temple cleared", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One man clears a courtyard the size of several football pitches, and nobody stops Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He objected to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The money changers and the dove sellers were operating in the Court of the Gentiles — the only part of the temple a non-Jew was permitted to enter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one space in the building where the nations could come and pray had been turned into a livestock market with an exchange rate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is written, '**My house will be called a house of prayer**,' but you have turned it into **a hideout for robbers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first half is Isaiah 56, and Mark keeps the rest of the line: *a house of prayer **for all nations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second half is Jeremiah 7 — the temple sermon, where the people were chanting *the temple of the LORD, the temple of the LORD* while robbing each other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **hideout** is where thieves go after the theft. It is not where the robbery happens; it is where they feel safe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did not damage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He drove out people and overturned furniture. Nobody is reported hurt, and no animal is reported killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He would not allow anyone to carry wares through the temple*, Mark adds. He shut the shortcut down as well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened next in the same courtyard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the blind and the lame came to Him right there in the temple, and He healed them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Matthew records this, and the placement is the point. The tables go over and the room fills with people who could not have got near it before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under the old rule, *the blind and the lame shall not come into the house.* They came in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The children in the temple courts **shouting, \"Hosanna to the Son of David!\"** And they were furious.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief priests object to the children, and He answers them with Psalm 8: *out of the mouths of children and nursing infants You have brought forth praise.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of that psalm says *You have ordained **strength**.* He quotes the Greek, which has praise. Both are in the line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He slept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He left them, went out of the city to **Bethany**, and spent the night there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He cleared the temple and then walked two miles out of the city to sleep at Lazarus's house. Every night that week."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What week this is.** Nisan 10, the day the Passover lamb was brought into the house and kept until the fourteenth. He comes into the temple on the day the lamb comes in, and the next three days are spent being questioned. See the notes on Matthew 21:23–27 and Exodus 12:1–13."}]}, "Matthew 21:23–27": {"title": "By whose authority", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The examination begins, and it runs for three days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening this week"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamb was brought into the house on the tenth of Nisan and kept until the fourteenth — *you shall keep it up until the fourteenth day* — and in those days it was watched for any blemish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus rode in on the tenth. From this point until the Passover, one delegation after another comes at Him with questions."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the chief priests and elders** — by what authority?", "**the Pharisees and Herodians** — is it lawful to pay taxes to Caesar?", "**the Sadducees** — the woman with seven husbands, whose wife is she?", "**a lawyer** — which is the great commandment?"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four inspections in two chapters, from four groups who agreed on almost nothing else, and every one of them goes away with nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pilate's verdict at the end of the week is the standard formula: *I find no fault in Him.* Herod finds none. Pilate's wife, Judas, and the thief on the cross all say the same thing in their own words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamb was examined and passed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question they ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**By what authority** are You doing these things? And **who gave You** this authority?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are asking about the tables. Nobody licensed Him to clear the court."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will ask you one question. If you answer it for Me, I will tell you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not refuse to answer. He puts a condition on it, and the condition is a question they cannot afford to answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question He asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Where did John's baptism come from? From heaven, or from men?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is exactly parallel to theirs. They asked where His authority came from; He asks where John's did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If John was from heaven, then John's testimony about Him settles their question already — *behold, the Lamb of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their working"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records their deliberation, which is the most damning thing in the passage:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If we say, 'From heaven,' He will ask us why we did not believe him. But if we say, 'From men,' **we have the crowd to fear**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one clause about what is true. Both options are weighed entirely by consequence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer they give"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We do not know.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Men whose office was to rule on exactly this kind of question — that was the Sanhedrin's job — say in public that they cannot tell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And His"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Then I will not tell you** by what authority I do these things.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He keeps the terms of the deal precisely."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He tells them instead.** Three parables, straight afterwards, all aimed at them: the two sons, the wicked tenants, and the wedding feast. He gives them no answer about authority and then describes them to their faces for the rest of the chapter."}]}, "Matthew 22:34–40": {"title": "\"Everything hangs on these two\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A lawyer sets a trap and Jesus answers by quoting two verses that were not usually put together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question behind the question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which is the greatest commandment in the law?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a live rabbinic debate — the tradition counted 613 commands, and ranking them was a standing argument. Any answer would offend somebody, which is why it is described as a test."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two He picks"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Deuteronomy 6:5** — love the LORD your God with all your heart, soul and mind. This is the Shema, recited twice daily by every observant Jew. Nobody could object to it.", "**Leviticus 19:18** — love your neighbour as yourself. A single line, buried in a chapter of miscellaneous laws about gleaning, wages and honest weights."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first was the obvious answer. Pulling the second up beside it was not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The second is like it\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word means *of the same kind*. He does not rank them as first and runner-up; He says they are the same sort of thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the move the lawyer would not have expected. Love of God and love of the person next to you are put in one category."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Hangs on\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the one used of hanging something on a peg — and of a body hanging on a cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else, in this image, is suspended from these two. Take them away and the rest has nothing to hold it up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the commanding runs from.** Jesus is answering a question about the law, and the answer is a command. What the New Testament adds is where the capacity comes from: *we love Him because He loved us first.* The order is not a softening of the command — it is the only thing that makes it possible. See the note on 1 John 4:19."}]}, "Matthew 22:41–46": {"title": "Whose Son is He?", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "After three days of being questioned, He asks one of His own, and it ends the week's interrogation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What do you think about the Messiah? Whose son is He?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only question in the Gospels He puts to His enemies about Himself, and it is the one everything hangs on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"David's.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Correct, and it is what every Jew would have said. The Messiah comes from David's line — 2 Samuel 7, Isaiah 11, Jeremiah 23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not wrong. They are short."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then how is it that **David, speaking by the Spirit, calls Him Lord**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes Psalm 110:1 — *the LORD said to my Lord: sit at My right hand until I make Your enemies a footstool.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different words. The first is **YHWH**. The second is **Adonai**, my Lord — and David is the greatest king Israel ever had, with nobody above him but God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls someone else *my Lord*, and that someone is his own descendant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If David calls Him Lord, how can He be David's son?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that culture the line ran one way. A father does not address his descendant as *my Lord*; every honour flows upward through the generations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Unless the descendant existed before the ancestor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other time He said it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Before Abraham was, **I AM**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They picked up stones for that one. See the note on John 8:57–59."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same claim, two forms. Once to a hostile crowd in plain words, and once here, as a question, from their own songbook — and neither time does anybody misunderstand what is being said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Psalm 110 goes on to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has sworn and will not relent: **You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king on David's throne who is also a priest, and not of Aaron's line. Hebrews spends two chapters on that verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one could answer Him a word, and **from that day on no one dared ask Him another question**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The questioning stops. Matthew reports it as the end of it — after this the Sanhedrin stop debating and start planning."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does immediately after.** Turns to the crowds and the disciples and delivers the seven woes, and finishes with *Jerusalem, Jerusalem, how often I have longed to gather your children.* The last public words of His ministry, and they end in tears. See the note on Matthew 23:37–39."}]}, "Matthew 23:13–15": {"title": "Doors shut in people's faces", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of the seven woes, and the charge is about a door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You **shut the door of the kingdom of heaven in people's faces**. You will not go in yourselves, and you will not let anyone else go in either.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is of men standing in a doorway. Not outside it and not inside it — blocking it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version says they *took away the key of knowledge*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who this is said to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Teachers of the law and Pharisees. The most careful, most scrupulous, most observant men in the nation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people who shut the kingdom are not the ones you would expect to. They are the religious experts, and what shuts it is what they preach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Pretenders\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupokrites** — an actor, one who speaks from under a mask. It was the ordinary word for a stage performer before it became an insult."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus uses it seven times in this chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The widows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You **eat up widows' houses**, and to cover it you make long prayers.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things stacked: what they take, and what they use to hide it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *cover* is a pretext, a screen. The praying is the cover story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The convert"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You cross land and sea to win a single convert, and when you have won him, **you make him twice as fit for hell as you are**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The effort is real — land and sea, for one person. Nobody accuses them of laziness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What they hand him at the end of it is the problem. A man taught to stand on his own performance ends up further from the door than the man who never went looking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same thing happens in Job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three men travelled to sit with a friend in ashes, and what they preached to him — experience, tradition, a formula — produced bitterness and a defence rather than rest. And God's verdict on all three was *you have not spoken of Me what is right.* See the notes on Job 4:7–11, Job 8:8–19 and Job 11:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Preaching that puts a man back on his own resources shuts the door on him, whatever it costs the preacher to deliver it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter ends.** After seven woes: *Jerusalem, Jerusalem… how often I have longed to gather your children together.* The harshest chapter in the Gospels finishes in tears over the city, and with the word *until*. See the note on Matthew 23:37–39."}]}, "Matthew 23:37–39": {"title": "\"How often I have longed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the harshest chapter in the Gospels, and it finishes in tears."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Jerusalem, Jerusalem**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doubled name is how you address someone you love. *Abraham, Abraham.* *Moses, Moses.* *Simon, Simon.* *Saul, Saul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven woes have just been pronounced over the scribes and Pharisees. Then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"How often\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How often** I have longed to gather your children together.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's Gospel records perhaps three visits to Jerusalem. *How often* is the speech of somebody who has been reaching for that city for a great deal longer than three years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The One saying it is the One who sent the prophets they killed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The way a **hen** gathers her chicks under her wings.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is not lion, eagle, or shepherd. A hen — an ordinary farmyard bird with no weapons at all, whose one method of protection is to open her wings and let her chicks run under her, and to stay there and take whatever comes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wings language is the Psalms' — *under the shadow of Your wings* — and He puts Himself in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And you were not willing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refusal is theirs and He says so plainly. He does not say they could not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *you did not want to*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Your house is left to you empty\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look, **your house** is left to you empty.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your* house. Two chapters earlier He had called it *My house*, when He turned over the tables — *My house shall be called a house of prayer.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The possessive has changed, and the word *empty* is *desolate*, abandoned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He then walks out of the temple, and the next verse of chapter 24 has the disciples pointing at the buildings and Him saying not one stone will be left on another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For I tell you, **you will not see Me again until** you say, 'Blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until.* It is not a closing of the door; it is a term."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The words He names are from Psalm 118, the crowd's shout at the triumphal entry five days earlier. He is saying the city will say them again, and that when it does, they will see Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last word of the harshest chapter in Matthew is a word about a reunion."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The season this describes.** Hosea's *not My people* — the face hidden, the house empty, the prophets gone. And even then it is *until*, with the words of the return already written out for them. See the notes on Hosea 1:8–9 and Romans 9:25–26."}]}, "Matthew 24:32–35": {"title": "Learn from the fig tree", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A short parable set inside the longest answer Jesus gives about the end, and it is about reading a season rather than fixing a date."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lesson"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As soon as its branch turns soft and puts out its leaves, you know that summer is near.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *turns soft* is about the branch becoming tender — sap moving in wood that was dry all winter. It happens before the leaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is told to calculate. They are told to notice what a farmer notices."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Right at the door\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When you see all these things, you know that He is near, right at the door.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek can be read *it is near* or *He is near* — the verb has no stated subject. The Illumination takes the personal reading, and the phrase *at the door* elsewhere in the New Testament is always used of someone standing there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James uses it: *the Judge is standing at the door.* Revelation uses it: *I stand at the door and knock.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The generation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This generation will certainly not pass away until all these things have happened.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **genea**. It has been read as the people alive then, as the Jewish people as a race, and as the generation that sees the signs begin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is left as He said it, and it has been argued about for two thousand years without being softened by anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Heaven and earth will pass away, but My words will never pass away.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most permanent things a person can point at are named, and then set below His own sentences."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *will never pass away* is a double negative — the strongest form of denial available in the language."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this sits.** The disciples had asked two questions on the Mount of Olives: when will these things be, and what will be the sign. He answers the second at length and, a few verses on, says of the first that no one knows the day or the hour."}]}, "Matthew 26:26–30": {"title": "\"This is My body, this is My blood\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's account adds one phrase the others do not have, and it is the phrase about what the blood is for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Matthew adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is My blood — the blood of the new covenant, poured out for many **for the forgiveness of sins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark and Luke stop at *for many*. Matthew names the purpose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the Gospel that opened with an angel saying *you shall call His name Jesus, for He shall save His people from their sins*. Matthew closes the circle at the table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Every one of you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Every one of you**, drink from it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Matthew it is a command, not just a report. Mark records that they all drank; Matthew records that they were all told to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody at that table had earned it. The betrayer is present and the man who will deny Him three times before morning is holding a cup."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two elements, two things given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cup is given a stated purpose — forgiveness. The bread is given without one: *take this and eat it. This is My body.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has already told us what the body did. In chapter 8 he quotes Isaiah over a night of healings: *He took our infirmities and bore our diseases.* The same writer, the same book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whoever eats the bread and drinks the cup is receiving two things, not one. See the note on 1 Corinthians 11:27–30, where Paul's warning is about failing to distinguish them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Thanks, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **gave thanks** over it… and when He had **given thanks** He passed it to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eucharisteō** — the word the meal has been named after ever since."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gave thanks over bread He was about to call His broken body, hours before the whipping post. The thanks came first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The unfinished cup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will not drink of the fruit of the vine again until the day I drink it new with you in My Father's kingdom.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**With you.** Matthew keeps the pronoun. The next cup has a guest list."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The hymn.** *Then they sang a hymn together and went out to the Mount of Olives.* The Passover ended with the second half of the Hallel — Psalm 118 among them, which says *the LORD is on my side, I will not fear; what can man do to me?* He walked from that hymn into the garden."}]}, "Matthew 27:51–53": {"title": "Torn from the top", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four things happen at the moment Jesus dies, and the first one is reported with a detail that carries the whole meaning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The curtain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The curtain of the temple was torn in two, **from the top all the way down to the bottom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The veil separated the Most Holy Place from everything else. Behind it was the place a single man entered once a year with blood, in smoke, having first atoned for himself. See the note on Leviticus 16:11–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was not opened. It was **torn**, and torn from the end nobody could reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew does not explain it. He records the direction and moves on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The earthquake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ground shook and the rocks split apart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has a pattern of noting the earth's reaction — there is another quake at the resurrection two chapters on, and it is the centurion's response to this one that produces the confession *truly this was the Son of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tombs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tombs broke open, and the bodies of many of God's people who had died were raised to life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The strangest paragraph in the Gospel, and Matthew alone reports it. He is careful about the timing: the tombs open at the death, but the people do not come out until **after** the resurrection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even here, Christ is kept as the firstfruits — nobody precedes Him out of a grave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Matthew does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He offers no interpretation of any of it. Four events, reported flat, in two sentences."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader is left to notice that at the moment the sacrifice was finished, the barrier came down, the ground broke, and death started letting people go."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the letter to the Hebrews takes it.** *We have confidence to enter the holy places by the blood of Jesus, by the new and living way that He opened for us through the curtain, that is, through His flesh.* The torn veil and the torn body are put together, and the conclusion drawn is an invitation to come in. See the note on Hebrews 4:14–16."}]}, "Matthew 3:16–17": {"title": "What was said before He had done anything", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Father speaks aloud twice in Matthew's Gospel. This is the first, and the timing is the whole point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it is said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus has not preached a sermon, healed anybody, called a disciple or told a parable. His public ministry begins in the next chapter, after forty days in the desert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The words come first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is My Son, the One I love. My delight is in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three statements: identity, love, and pleasure. Nothing is conditional and nothing refers to output."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *my delight is in Him* is a verb about being well pleased, taking pleasure in something — and it is in a tense that reads as a settled state rather than a reaction to a recent event."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two texts underneath it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is stitched from two places every listener would have known:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*You are my Son* — Psalm 2, spoken of the anointed king", "*in whom my soul delights* — Isaiah 42, the opening of the first Servant Song"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "King and servant, in one line. Matthew's readers would have heard both, and the second one leads to the servant who is pierced for transgressions."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where else the words turn up.** On the mountain at the transfiguration, the same voice says nearly the same thing and adds three words: *listen to Him.* The Father's public statements about the Son are remarkably consistent, and neither of them is about performance."}]}, "Matthew 4:1–4": {"title": "\"If You are the Son of God\" — the word the tempter starts with", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Read what happened immediately before this. At the end of chapter 3, the Father's voice says *this is My Son, the One I love. My delight is in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the first word out of the tempter's mouth is **if**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The attack"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If You are the Son of God, tell these stones to turn into bread.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not attack the hunger. He attacks the sonship — and he attacks it by suggesting it needs demonstrating. Do something, and the question will be settled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus had nothing to prove. The Father had already said it out loud, in public, before a single miracle had been performed. See the note on Matthew 3:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He answers with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is written: a person does not live on bread alone, but on every word that comes from the mouth of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes Deuteronomy 8 — Moses telling Israel why God let them hunger in the wilderness and fed them with manna."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the deliberate parallel. Israel spent forty years in the desert and failed the tests. Jesus spends forty days in one and does not, and He answers each time with a line from the book about the first forty years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word He lives on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every word that comes from the mouth of God.* He has one in hand already: *this is My Son, the One I love.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temptation is answered by holding onto what has been said rather than producing evidence for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who led Him there.** *Then the Spirit led Jesus up into the wilderness.* The desert is not a detour or a failure of guidance. Matthew puts the Spirit's leading and the testing in the same sentence, immediately after the dove descends."}]}, "Matthew 4:12–17": {"title": "Light in Galilee", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records a change of address and treats it as a fulfilled prophecy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He left **Nazareth** and went to live in **Capernaum**, a town beside the sea in the territory of **Zebulun and Naphtali**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew names both tribes, which is not how anyone in the first century gave directions. The tribal boundaries had not mattered for centuries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names them because Isaiah did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Land of Zebulun and land of Naphtali, along the road to the sea, beyond the Jordan, **Galilee of the nations** — the people sitting in darkness have seen a great light.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 9, seven hundred years earlier. The chapter that goes on to say *for unto us a Child is born… and His name shall be called Wonderful, Counsellor, the Mighty God, the Everlasting Father, the Prince of Peace.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why those two tribes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were the first to go. When Assyria came, 2 Kings records that Tiglath-Pileser took *Gilead and Galilee, all the land of Naphtali*, and carried them away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first territory to fall into the dark is named as the first to see the light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Galilee of the nations\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *Galilee of the Gentiles*. It had been mixed country for centuries, and the Judeans looked down on it — *can anything good come out of Nazareth?* and *search and look, for no prophet has arisen out of Galilee.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where He moved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Zebulun and Naphtali"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their blessings in Genesis 49 are one line each:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Zebulun** — *he will be a harbour for ships.* His name means dwelling, and this is where He dwelt.", "**Naphtali** — *a doe set free.* This is where the woman bound for eighteen years was loosed."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the notes on Genesis 49:13 and Genesis 49:21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Sitting in darkness\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not walking. Sitting — settled in it, no longer trying to get out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *the land where death casts its shadow*. Isaiah's word is the same one in Psalm 23: *the valley of the shadow of death.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He began to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Change your minds and turn to God**, because the kingdom of heaven has come near.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metanoeo** — to change the mind, to think differently afterwards. And the reason given is not a threat. It is proximity: because the kingdom has come near."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on the other side of Isaiah's sentence.** *On those living in the land where death casts its shadow, **light has dawned**.* The verb is a sunrise. Nobody in the dark did anything to produce it; the morning simply came up over them."}]}, "Matthew 4:5–7": {"title": "The temptation that quotes Scripture", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second attempt, and this time the tempter comes with a Bible verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will give His angels orders concerning You, and they will lift You up in their hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is Psalm 91, and it is quoted accurately as far as it goes. The promise is real; the psalm says it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is left out is the line in the middle — *to guard you in all your ways.* The keeping is promised along the road God has for a person, not on a jump the person invents."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The highest point of the temple*, over the Kidron valley — a drop of well over a hundred feet, in full view of the courts below."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The suggestion is a spectacular, public, self-authenticating demonstration. It would have settled the question of who He was in an afternoon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is also written: do not put the Lord your God to the test.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not dispute the psalm. He puts another Scripture beside it, from Deuteronomy 6 — the passage about Massah, where Israel demanded water and asked whether the LORD was among them or not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A promise is not a lever. Testing God is what you do when you are not sure of Him and want proof; and demanding proof is the opposite of the trust the promise was given to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this settles about quoting Scripture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A verse can be quoted correctly and used wrongly. The correction is not less Bible but more of it — Jesus answers all three temptations from Deuteronomy, and here He answers a quotation with a quotation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read alongside Psalms 91:5–8.** The psalm's promises are real and this app takes them as such. What this scene rules out is treating them as a mechanism to be operated, which is exactly what was being suggested."}]}, "Matthew 5:13–16": {"title": "\"You are\" — before anyone has done anything", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two images, and the grammar under both of them is the thing to notice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Not \"become\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You **are** the salt of the earth. You **are** the light of the world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present indicative, stated as fact. Jesus is not setting a target. He is telling a crowd — who at this point in Matthew have done nothing but climb a hill and sit down — what they already are."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything He says afterwards follows from the statement rather than earning it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What can actually go wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two images fail in different ways, and neither failure is about quantity."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**salt** can go flat — lose the thing that made it useful. What is left is not weaker salt; it is something that gets walked on", "**light** can be hidden — nobody in the illustration puts out the lamp. They put a bucket over it"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the warnings are about losing distinctiveness and about concealment. Not about shining harder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the attention goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that when they see the good you do, they will give the glory to your Father in heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The visibility is deliberate — a town on a hill, a lamp on a stand, light filling a house. But the sentence ends somewhere other than where it started: what people see is the good; who they credit is the Father."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**A page later.** In chapter 6 Jesus warns against doing good *to be seen by people*. It looks like a contradiction of this and is not — the difference is in the last clause of each. Let them see the good so they glorify God; do not perform it so they admire you. Same visibility, opposite destination."}]}, "Matthew 5:21–26": {"title": "\"But I say to you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He takes a commandment everyone in the crowd had kept, and moves it somewhere nobody can keep it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The formula"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have heard that it was said**… **But I say to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses it six times in this chapter. Nobody had ever spoken that way about the law — the scribes taught by citing older rabbis, and He cites nobody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commandment was *do not murder*. Almost every man on that hillside had a clean record on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But I say to you, anyone who **nurses anger** against his brother… anyone who says 'You are worthless'… anyone who says 'You fool'.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offence moves from the hand to the temper, and from the temper to a word said under the breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which direction the standard is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Sermon on the Mount does not lower the bar to something manageable. It raises it past the reach of anybody in the audience — and He is only getting started; adultery goes the same way four verses later. See the note on Matthew 5:27–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the passage is for. The law was never a ladder; it is a mirror. Paul says so: *by the law is the knowledge of sin*, and *the law was our tutor to bring us to Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who thinks he has kept it has not read it closely enough. Jesus reads it closely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift at the altar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you are bringing your gift to the altar, and right there you remember that **your brother has something against you**, leave your gift where it is.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the direction. Not that you have something against him — that he has something against you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You are the one who is owed nothing, and you are the one told to leave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Leave your gift where it is, in front of the altar. Go.* The offering waits on the stone while a man walks out of the temple to find somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Settle quickly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Settle it with him quickly, **while the two of you are still on the road together**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is two men walking to court. There is a stretch of road before the judge, and He says use it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this leaves the hearer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last line is *you will not get out of there until you have paid the very last coin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no relief anywhere in the passage. That is deliberate — the Sermon closes with a house falling flat, and the whole of it drives to one place: a man who cannot produce this righteousness, and needs one given."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He said just before starting.** *Unless your righteousness exceeds the righteousness of the scribes and Pharisees, you will by no means enter the kingdom of heaven.* The most meticulous men alive, and He says you need better than theirs. Then six paragraphs showing what better looks like."}]}, "Matthew 5:27–30": {"title": "\"Already, in his heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second of the six, and it does the same thing to adultery that the first did to murder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have heard… 'Do not commit adultery.' **But I say to you**, anyone who looks at a woman with wanting in his eyes **has already committed adultery with her in his heart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seventh commandment concerned an act. He relocates it to a glance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Already.** The Greek is emphatic — it is done, past tense, before anything happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is left standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the question the passage is designed to raise, and the answer is nobody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first paragraph took murder down to a temper. This one takes adultery down to a look. Between them they clear the hillside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anyone who leaves the Sermon on the Mount feeling he has done reasonably well has not been listening. That is the whole point of it: *by the law is the knowledge of sin*, and the law was *our tutor to bring us to Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not making the law harder to keep. He is showing what it always said, and what it always said cannot be kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eye and the hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If your right eye is what trips you up, **tear it out**… if your right hand is what trips you up, **cut it off**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verbs are violent and specific."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the arithmetic is stated: *better to lose one part of you than to have your whole body thrown into hell.* On His own terms that is simply correct — if a hand could be traded for a life, the trade would be worth making."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But a blind man can still want, and a one-handed man can still hate. The surgery does not reach the organ that is actually the problem, and the passage has already named it: *in his heart.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has to be dealt with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing removable is the trouble. That is why the sentence about the heart comes first and the amputations after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel had the only remedy that reaches it: *I will give you a new heart, and put a new spirit within you; I will take the heart of stone out of your flesh and give you a heart of flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An operation nobody can perform on himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Sermon ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two houses, and one of them *fell — and great was its fall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He finishes His most demanding sermon with a collapse, and the crowds are *astonished at His teaching, for He taught them as one having authority.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this leaves a reader.** Exactly where it left the hearers: with a standard that is God's own and no way to reach it, and a Man on the hillside who will go on to keep it entirely and then hand what He earned to people who did not. That is the sequence the Sermon is written into."}]}, "Matthew 6:19–21": {"title": "\"Where your treasure is\" — the direction of the sentence", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*Wherever your treasure is, that is where your heart will be living.* Most people quote it the other way round — put your heart somewhere and your money follows. Jesus says the reverse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Treasure first, heart after. The heart is described as taking up residence wherever the valuables already are."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That makes it a diagnosis and a lever at once. A diagnosis, because a person can find out what they actually treasure by looking at where their resources have gone. A lever, because moving the treasure is presented as the thing that moves the affection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three thieves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moths eat it and rust eats it and thieves break in and carry it off.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three ways to lose it, covering the three forms wealth took: cloth, metal, and anything storable. The Greek behind *rust* is broader than iron corrosion — it is eating away, decay of any kind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is not moralising about wealth here. He is making a practical argument about where things keep."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse after it.** He goes straight on to the eye being the lamp of the body, and then to serving two masters. The passage is a run: where your money is, what your eye is fixed on, who you actually work for. All three ask the same question from a different angle, and Luke's version of the third one is in the note on Luke 16:9–13."}]}, "Matthew 6:25–34": {"title": "Look at the birds", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three times He says do not worry, and in between He points at two things anybody in that field could see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The birds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **do not plant, they do not harvest, they do not store anything away in barns**, and your heavenly Father feeds them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs, all denied. And they are the three stages of agriculture — the whole economy of that hillside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Birds are not idle; a bird works hard all day. What it does not do is the thing the crowd was doing: plan the year, secure the surplus, guarantee the future."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are you not worth far more than they are?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The flowers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look how the wildflowers grow out in the open field. **They do not work at it, they do not spin a thread**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs again, and they are women's work — the crowd is being addressed in halves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not even Solomon in all his splendour was dressed like one of these.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The richest man Israel produced, out-dressed by a weed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If God clothes **the grass of the field** like that — grass that is here today and **tossed into the oven tomorrow** — how much more will He clothe you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dried grass was fuel. He is comparing a person to kindling, and arguing from the lesser to the greater."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You of little faith\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is one word, **oligopistos**, and Matthew uses it five times, always to disciples. It is not a rebuke about doctrine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He attaches it to worry, which tells you what He thinks worry is a shortage of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What worry cannot do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which of you, **by worrying, can add a single hour** to your life?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue that worry is wrong. He argues that it is ineffective, which is harder to dispute."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who chases"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People who **do not know God** chase after all of that, and **your heavenly Father knows perfectly well that you need it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, and the second is the answer to the first. The need is not denied — it is granted, and the point is that it is already known about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seek first the kingdom of God and **His righteousness**, and **all these other things will be given to you as well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note whose righteousness is to be sought. Not one of your own — that is the whole argument of the Sermon, which has just spent a chapter showing nobody has one. See the note on Matthew 5:27–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All these other things* — the food, the drink, the clothes. Not withheld and not despised; put second, and added."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *will be given* is to add on top, to throw in besides. They arrive as an extra."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Today"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not worry about tomorrow. Tomorrow will worry about itself. **Today has enough trouble of its own**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say tomorrow has no trouble in it. He says today's ration is sufficient and there is no provision for carrying a second day's."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse before all this.** *No one can serve two masters… you cannot serve God and mammon.* Then *therefore I say to you, do not worry.* The passage about birds is an answer to a question about who owns you."}]}, "Matthew 6:9–13": {"title": "The prayer, line by line", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "It is the most said prayer in the world, which is exactly why it is worth slowing down over. Jesus gives it as a pattern — *pray like this* — immediately after telling people not to heap up words as though volume helped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Our Father in heaven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in tension, and both matter. **Father** — relationship, nearness, a family word. **In heaven** — and He is not one of us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice it is *our*, not *my*. The whole prayer is plural: our bread, our debts, lead us, deliver us. Jesus taught it to be prayed by someone who has other people in mind, even when they are alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "May Your name be held holy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a request that God become holy. The verb asks that His name be *treated* as what it is — set apart, not used as small change."}, {"k": "h", "t": "May Your kingdom come"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing asked for is not a personal need. And the line that follows tells you what a kingdom coming looks like: *may what You want be done, here on the earth just as it is done in heaven.* Two lines, one request. God's rule arriving is God's will being done."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Give us today the bread we need for today"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek adjective here — **epiousios** — is famous for being almost unknown. It appears in no other surviving Greek text of the period apart from this prayer. Every translation is a considered guess: bread for the coming day, bread that is needed, daily bread."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever it means precisely, the request is small and immediate. Today's bread, today. Nobody is asked to pray for a storehouse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Forgive us our debts, as we ourselves have forgiven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Debts, not sins — Matthew uses the commercial word, something owed. And the second half has already happened: *as we ourselves have forgiven*, past tense. It is not a bargain being struck. It is a person praying who has already let somebody off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the only line Jesus goes back and comments on after the prayer ends, so He evidently expected it to be the sticking point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Do not lead us into the time of testing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word covers both testing and temptation. Given James says plainly that God tempts nobody, the request is best read as it stands: *keep us out of the trial* — a prayer of ordinary self-knowledge, from people who would rather not find out how they would do."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the last line.** *For the kingdom is Yours, and the power, and the glory* — it is absent from the earliest manuscripts of Matthew and was almost certainly added early, from church use. It is not a corruption; it is a congregation's Amen that ended up in the text, and it echoes David's prayer in 1 Chronicles. The app prints it because it is what most readers know."}]}, "Matthew 8:1–4": {"title": "\"If You are willing\" — and the answer that settles it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The leper's question is the one everybody actually asks, and Jesus answers it in two words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the man was sure of, and what he was not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, if You are willing, You can make me clean.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has no doubt about the power. **Can** is not in question — he says it flatly. What he does not know is whether Jesus *wants* to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where most people stand. Not wondering whether God is able. Wondering whether He is willing, in their case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus reached out His hand and touched him. \"I am willing,\" He said. \"Be clean.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes the man's own word and hands it straight back. The uncertainty is not left hanging for a moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He touches him first. Under the law a leper was untouchable, and anyone touching one became unclean until evening. Jesus reaches out before He speaks. The cleanness travels the other way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the touch says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the first healing Matthew records after the Sermon on the Mount, and he places it deliberately. Jesus has just finished teaching with authority; now He puts a hand on the one person in Israel nobody would touch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instantly the leprosy left him.* No process, no waiting."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why He sends him to the priest.** Leviticus 14 sets out a whole ceremony for cleansed lepers that had, so far as anyone knew, never been performed — two birds, cedar, scarlet, hyssop. Jesus tells the man to go and present himself *so they can see it for themselves*. The priests were about to have to open a chapter of the law that had lain unused."}]}, "Matthew 8:10–13": {"title": "\"As you have believed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The one time in Matthew that Jesus is recorded as astonished, and it is by a Roman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He marvelled"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus heard that, **He marvelled**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to wonder, to be amazed. Mark records Him marvelling at unbelief in Nazareth; here it goes the other way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both times it is caused by people's faith — the absence of it, and this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the officer had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: *only speak a word, and my servant will be healed. For I also am a man under authority… I say to this one, \"Go,\" and he goes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a career soldier and he has worked out the whole thing from military structure. Orders travel. The officer does not have to be present for the order to take effect, only to have the authority behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not ask Jesus to come. He asked Him to speak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have **not found faith like this anywhere in Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said out loud, to the crowd following Him, about a Gentile officer of the occupying army."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Many will come **from the east and from the west** and sit down at the table with Abraham and Isaac and Jacob… while **those who thought the kingdom belonged to them by birth** will be put outside.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes one Roman's faith and extends it to a table filled from every direction, and warns the people who assumed the seats were theirs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word to the officer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go home. Let it be done for you just as you have believed.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Matthew's next clause: *the servant was healed at that very hour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At a distance. Jesus never went to the house and never saw the servant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as you have believed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *because you deserved it*, or *because your servant was worth it*, or *because you are a good master* — though the passage shows he was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing named as the measure is what the man believed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records the same formula twice more in his Gospel: to the two blind men, *according to your faith let it be to you*, and to the Canaanite woman, *let it be done for you just as you desire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times, three different people, and every one of them an outsider of some kind."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the officer thought he was.** *Lord, I am not worthy that You should come under my roof.* A commander of a hundred men, in a country he was occupying, saying that to a village carpenter. Jesus never contradicts the self-assessment — He simply speaks the word."}]}, "Matthew 8:14–17": {"title": "Matthew tells you which prophecy the healings were", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An evening of healing, and then Matthew stops the narrative to tell you what he thinks was happening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This was to fill out what the prophet Isaiah had said: \"He took up our weaknesses and carried our diseases away.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line is from Isaiah 53 — the Servant chapter, the one about being pierced for our transgressions and crushed for our iniquities."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew, writing under inspiration and knowing exactly which chapter he is reaching into, applies it to **an evening of physical healings**. Not to the forgiveness of sins, which is what the chapter is usually preached as. To fevers and demons and bodies."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Hebrew he is drawing on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 53:4 in Hebrew reads *surely he has borne our sicknesses and carried our pains*. The words are the ordinary ones for illness and physical suffering; they are used elsewhere in the Old Testament of disease."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So Matthew is not stretching the verse. He is quoting it at its plain sense, and attaching it to what he had just watched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Healed everyone who was sick\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scope is worth reading slowly. Not many. Not those with sufficient faith. *Everyone.* Matthew says the same in several other places — great crowds, and all of them healed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in these scenes is turned away, and no one is told they did not qualify."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The fever \"let go of her.\"** Matthew's verb is the one used of releasing a prisoner or forgiving a debt. He touches her hand and the fever lets go — and the next thing she does is get up and serve. Restored, and immediately useful."}]}, "Matthew 8:5–9": {"title": "The centurion's argument", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A Roman officer stops Jesus in the street and makes an argument about authority that Jesus calls the greatest faith in Israel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A centurion — roughly eighty to a hundred men under him, the backbone of the Roman army, and an occupier in a country that hated the occupation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the person he is asking about is *his servant*. A man with the power to replace a slave is out in the road pleading for one, describing the condition in detail: paralysed, in terrible pain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he refuses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, I am not worthy to have You come under my roof.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus has already said He will come. The centurion turns down the visit — partly because entering a Gentile home would make a Jewish teacher ceremonially unclean, and he knows it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for less access than he is being offered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Only say the word… I am a man under orders myself, and I have soldiers under me. I say to one, \"Go,\" and he goes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here is the reasoning: I know how authority works. I do not need to be present for my orders to be carried out, because the authority is not in my presence. It is in my position."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has understood that Jesus operates the same way — and, crucially, that Jesus is *under* authority too, which is why the word carries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never asks Jesus to do anything except speak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have not found such great faith, not even in Israel.* And then the servant is healed at that moment, at a distance, without a word being spoken over him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he built.** Luke's account adds that the Jewish elders vouched for him: *he loves our nation and he built our synagogue.* An occupying officer paid for the local place of worship. That is the man who says he is not worthy to have Jesus in his house."}]}, "Matthew 9:27–31": {"title": "\"According to your faith\" — and what the question was", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two blind men follow Jesus indoors, and He asks them one question before doing anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you believe that I am able to do this?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *do you have enough faith*, and not *how strong is your faith*. The object of the question is His ability, not their capacity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth holding on to when the next line arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"According to your faith\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let it be done for you according to your faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read alone, it sounds like a measurement — as much healing as you have faith for. Read as the answer to the question He just asked, it means something narrower: according to what you have just said you believe about Me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their faith had exactly one item in it. They believed He could. That was enough to be going on with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they called Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have mercy on us, Son of David.* It is the first time in Matthew anybody uses that title, and it is a messianic one — two blind men see who He is before most of the people watching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew, who is building a case throughout his Gospel that Jesus is the promised son of David, gives the line to them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The instruction they ignore.** *See that nobody hears about this* — and they went out and spread it everywhere. Matthew records the disobedience plainly and without approval. It is the reaction of people who have just received their sight, and the Gospel does not tidy it up."}]}, "Micah 5:2": {"title": "\"But you, Bethlehem\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, seven hundred years early, and it names the village."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which Bethlehem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But you, **Bethlehem Ephrathah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There were two towns of that name. Micah specifies the one in Judah, using its older name, so there is no confusion with the Bethlehem in Zebulun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophecy is precise enough that it could only be fulfilled in one place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Too little to be counted\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Small among the clans of Judah, **too little to be counted**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethlehem did not make the list. When Joshua divided the land and the towns of Judah were enumerated, Bethlehem is not in the Hebrew text of that list at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the village David came from, and even that had not made it a place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes out of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of you will come **one for Me**, a ruler over Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For Me.* The ruler is produced for God's own purposes before He is produced for Israel's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whose beginnings reach back **into the far distance, into ancient days, into times older than memory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *from of old, from everlasting* — **mi-qedem, mi-mei olam**. The same phrase is used in Scripture of God's own eternity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A ruler with a birthplace and no beginning. Micah says both in one sentence and does not explain how."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who read it out loud"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herod asked the chief priests and scribes where the Christ was to be born, and they answered immediately: *in Bethlehem of Judea, for thus it is written by the prophet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They knew the verse, quoted it accurately from memory, and did not go the five miles to look."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it took to fulfil it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph and Mary lived in Nazareth, ninety miles north. A Roman emperor ordered a census of the whole empire, and it required every man to return to his ancestral town."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caesar Augustus moved the population of the Roman world to get one pregnant woman to a village too little to be counted."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is beside it in the chapter.** The verse before speaks of a judge of Israel struck on the cheek with a rod; the verses after describe Him standing and shepherding *in the strength of the LORD*, and *He shall be their peace.* Micah puts the striking and the shepherding on the same page."}]}, "Nehemiah 13:10–14": {"title": "The singers had gone back to their fields", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah comes back from a trip to Persia and finds the temple system quietly collapsed. What he notices first is where people are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail that tells the story"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Levites and singers who did the work had gone back, each to his own field.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody rebelled. Nobody made a decision. The support stopped arriving, and men with families went home to grow food, because that is what a person does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah's question is aimed at the officials, not at the Levites: *why is the house of God neglected?* He puts the failure on the people responsible for the arrangement, not on the men who left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he actually fixes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The interesting part is the administration. He does not merely restart the collection — he appoints four named men over the storerooms, *for they were counted faithful*, whose job is to distribute fairly among their kinsmen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The previous arrangement had been compromised: a few verses earlier, a priest had given a storeroom over to an official's private use. The problem was not only supply. It was that the system had stopped being trustworthy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah's answer to a giving problem is accountable handling of what is given."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The prayer at the end.** *Remember me for this, O my God, and do not wipe away the good I have done.* Nehemiah prays this four or five times in his book, always about specific administrative work — walls, gates, storerooms, rosters. It is the closing note of a man who did unglamorous jobs and wanted them noticed by the only person whose notice he cared about."}]}, "Nehemiah 7:4–5": {"title": "\"My God put it into my heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The wall is finished, and Nehemiah looks at a city that is still mostly empty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The city was **large and spacious, but few people lived in it**, and the houses had not yet been rebuilt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has spent fifty-two days rebuilding the walls against organised opposition, with a trowel in one hand and a weapon in the other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And now there is a defended city with almost nobody inside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **my God put it into my heart** to gather the nobles and the rulers and the people to be registered by families.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my God gave into my heart*. It is Nehemiah's characteristic phrase — he uses it earlier in the book too, of the work on the wall."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not report a vision, a prophet or a voice. He reports a thought arriving, and he says where it came from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it looked like from inside"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Like his own idea. Like an administrative instinct — a governor deciding a census is the sensible next step."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah's habit through the whole book is to name the source of his instincts as he goes. *The good hand of my God was upon me.* *What my God had put in my heart to do for Jerusalem — I told no one.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **I found the record of the genealogy** of those who had come back first.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He set out to organise a registration and found an existing document — the list of the first return under Zerubbabel, ninety years earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that follows is that list, name by name, family by family, down to the singers, the gatekeepers and the temple servants. The same list appears in Ezra 2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the record mattered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The genealogies decided who could serve as a priest — the chapter goes on to describe men who *sought their listing among those who had been registered by genealogy, but it was not found, so they were excluded from the priesthood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A thought in a governor's heart, and a census that recovered a lost document and settled who could stand at an altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 8. *All the people gathered together as one man… and Ezra the priest brought the Law before the assembly*, and read it from morning until midday, and the Levites *gave the sense, and helped them to understand the reading.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The registration led straight to the Book being opened in the square."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was before this.** Cupbearer to Artaxerxes — a servant with a wine cup, in a Persian palace, four months' journey from a ruined city he had never seen. The first thing recorded of him is that he asked how Jerusalem was, and sat down and wept."}]}, "Numbers 13:26–33": {"title": "\"In our own sight, and so we looked to them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Twelve men see the same country for forty days and come back with two reports."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What everybody agreed on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It truly does **flow with milk and honey** — here is its fruit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All twelve say that. The cluster of grapes took two men and a pole to carry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And all twelve agreed on the difficulties too: strong people, vast cities, towering walls, the descendants of Anak, and four named nations holding the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The facts are not in dispute anywhere in this chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word that changes everything"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But** the people who live there are strong.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten men have the same information as Caleb and put a *but* in the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Caleb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Caleb **quieted the people** before Moses and said, \"**Let's go up at once and take it. We can certainly do it.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is emphatic — *we are well able*. It is not bravado about Israel's army; the chapter has already listed the giants and he is not disputing them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other report"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We can't attack those people — they're far stronger than we are.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then it escalates into something else entirely:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The land we explored devours those who live in it.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is no longer a report. Forty days earlier it was a land flowing with milk and honey; now it eats people. The text calls it what it is — *a bad report*, and the Hebrew word is the one for slander."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Next to them we felt like grasshoppers, and that's how we looked to them too.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the two halves in order. First: *in our own sight*. Then: *and so we were in their sight*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They decided what they were, and then reported that the giants had reached the same conclusion. Nobody in Canaan had told them that. It is a guess about somebody else's opinion, made out of their own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The self-assessment came first, and the world's assessment is what they projected out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the giants actually thought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "We find out forty years later. Rahab, in Jericho: *I know that the LORD has given you the land, and that **the terror of you has fallen on us**… our hearts melted, and there remained no courage in anyone because of you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She says it had been that way *when you came out of Egypt* — the whole time the ten spies were feeling like grasshoppers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The giants were terrified. The grasshoppers were the ones who reported otherwise."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it cost.** The next chapter: *as surely as I live, I will do to you the very things you spoke in My hearing.* They said *if only we had died in this wilderness*, and that is precisely what was granted. See the note on Numbers 14:26–35."}]}, "Numbers 14:1–4": {"title": "The night they wept", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A whole nation cries all night over a country they have not seen, on the strength of a report from ten men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weeping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That night the whole assembly broke into loud weeping, and **the sound did not fade until morning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hours of it. Two million people, according to the census figures, weeping in the dark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has happened. No battle has been fought, no one has been hurt. They have received information."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If only we had died in Egypt — or here in this wilderness!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hold that sentence. It is the exact fate God grants them twelve verses later, quoted back word for word: *as surely as I live, I will do to you the very things you spoke in My hearing… in this wilderness your bodies will fall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They asked for it in so many words, in the dark, and it was heard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why is **the LORD bringing us into this land only to fall by the sword**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have redescribed the rescue as a trap. The God of the plagues, the sea and the manna is now a God who brought them out to kill them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Our wives and children will be taken as plunder.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the fear that undoes them, and it is the one God answers most directly: *as for your children — the ones you said would be taken as plunder — **I will bring them in**, and they will know the land you rejected.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very thing they were afraid of losing is the thing that inherits. Their fear was not only wrong; it was inverted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Let's go back to Egypt\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they said to one another, \"**Let's choose a leader and go back to Egypt.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They get as far as a leadership plan. Not a moment of panic — an organised proposal to reverse the exodus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt is where they were slaves, where the boys were thrown in the Nile, where they groaned until God heard. Two years out, it is the safe option."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is at the door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are camped at Kadesh Barnea, on the southern edge of the land. The journey from Sinai had taken about eleven days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are within walking distance of everything promised to Abraham, and they spend the night asking to go back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses and Aaron do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *they fell on their faces before all the assembly.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Joshua and Caleb tear their clothes and plead — *the land is exceedingly good… only do not rebel against the LORD.* The assembly's answer is *stone them with stones.* See the note on Numbers 14:5–10."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The forty years.** *For each of the forty days you explored the land, you will bear your guilt a year.* One year in the wilderness for each day the spies were out. The wandering is measured against the walk they would not take."}]}, "Numbers 14:20–25": {"title": "\"A different spirit\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The forgiveness and the consequence are in the same paragraph, and one man is pulled out of it by name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I have forgiven them\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD said, \"**I have forgiven them, as you asked.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had just interceded — *pardon the iniquity of this people, according to the greatness of Your mercy* — and the answer is granted in five words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not withheld and it is not partial. And the next sentence is still *not one of them will see the land.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ten times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet tested Me these ten times** and would not listen to My voice.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten. The rabbis counted them: at the Red Sea, at Marah, twice over the manna, at Rephidim, the golden calf, Taberah, Kibroth-hattaavah, and now this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He kept a count and states it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**None who treated Me with contempt** will see it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to spurn, to despise. It is a strong word, and it is aimed at what they did with the report, not at their conduct generally."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Caleb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But My servant Caleb has a different spirit in him and has followed Me wholeheartedly.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things said about him, and each is worth its own line."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My servant** — the title used of Moses, Abraham, David, and of the Servant in Isaiah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A different spirit** — the Hebrew is *another spirit*, a spirit other than the one in everybody else. He had walked the same forty days, seen the same walls, and measured himself against the same giants, and something in him was of a different kind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Followed Me wholeheartedly** — literally *filled after Me*, followed fully, all the way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise to him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will bring him into the land he explored, and his descendants will inherit it.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land he explored. The specific ground he walked as a spy is deeded to him personally, forty-five years before he collects it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does collect it, at eighty-five, and he asks for the part with the giants on it. See the note on Joshua 14:6–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then the turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Turn back tomorrow and set out into the wilderness, toward the Red Sea.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Back the way they came. Toward the sea they had walked through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened the next morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They changed their minds — *we have sinned; we will go up.* And Moses told them not to, because the LORD was not among them. They went anyway and were beaten back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A day late, and doing it on their own strength, which is a different thing from faith even when the action looks the same."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two who came out.** Of everyone twenty and over who left Egypt, Joshua and Caleb are the only two who entered. Two out of a nation, and the thing that separated them was not courage in battle — none had been fought. It was what they said about a country after looking at it."}]}, "Numbers 14:26–35": {"title": "\"The very things you spoke in My hearing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God answers the complaint, and the answer is the complaint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have heard every murmur they raise against Me.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *murmur* is the grumbling word used all through the wilderness accounts. It is not shouted; it is muttered among themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He reports having heard all of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As surely as I live, declares the LORD, **I will do to you the very things you spoke in My hearing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then He quotes them: *\"In this wilderness your bodies will fall.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Go back one page. Their words the night before were *if only we had died in Egypt — or here in this wilderness!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives them exactly what they asked for, in their own phrasing, and says so explicitly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is excepted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Except Caleb son of Jephunneh and Joshua son of Nun.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two who said something else. Caleb: *let's go up at once and take it — we can certainly do it.* Joshua, with him, tearing his clothes: *the land is exceedingly good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men out of a nation, and the difference between them and everybody else is what came out of their mouths about the same country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As for your children — **the ones you said would be taken as plunder** — I will bring them in, and they will **know the land you rejected**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He repeats their own fear back to them and reverses it in the same sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The generation that was afraid for their children lost the land; the children got it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**For each of the forty days you explored the land, you will bear your guilt a year** — forty years.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A day for a year. The wandering is measured against the reconnaissance — the walk they took and then would not finish."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Forty years of shepherding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your children will be shepherds here for forty years, bearing the weight of your unfaithfulness **until the last of your bodies lies in the wilderness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The young ones spend their twenties and thirties burying the generation that would not go in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any suggestion the giants got stronger, or that the land became harder to take. It was as takeable the next morning as it had been the day before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua and Caleb walked into it forty years later, and Caleb at eighty-five asked for the hill country with the Anakim on it — *give me this mountain.* See the note on Joshua 14:6–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same giants. A different man's report about them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What God said before all this.** *\"I have forgiven them, as you asked.\"* Moses had interceded and the forgiveness is granted in verse 20, five verses before the sentence. Forgiven, and still not going in — the consequence and the pardon are both in the same paragraph."}]}, "Numbers 14:5–10": {"title": "\"The shade that sheltered them is gone\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men against a whole camp, and the argument they make is not the one you would expect."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joshua and Caleb do not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not say the giants are small, or that the ten scouts exaggerated. The report was accurate — the people were strong and the cities were fortified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What they dispute is the conclusion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strange image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We will swallow them up. The shade that sheltered them is gone, but the LORD is with us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is literally *their shadow has departed from them*. In that world, shade meant protection — the shade of a rock in the desert, the shadow of a king's hand, the shadow of God's wings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the argument is: whatever was covering these people has been withdrawn, and what is covering us has not. It is not a claim about relative strength. It is a claim about who still has cover."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it costs them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The whole assembly talked of stoning them.* That is the immediate response to the most faith-filled speech in the chapter, and the glory of the LORD appears at the tent precisely as the stones are being picked up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word \"bread.\"** Joshua and Caleb's phrase for swallowing the enemy is the ordinary Hebrew for eating — the giants will be *bread* for us. It is a vivid, almost crude reversal of the ten scouts' report, which had said the land devoured its inhabitants. One party says the land eats people. The other says we will eat them. Same country, two readings."}]}, "Numbers 20:25–29": {"title": "The garments taken off one man and put on another", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Aaron's death is unlike any other in the Old Testament. He is told where, told when, and walks up the mountain to it in front of the whole nation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of events"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take off Aaron's priestly garments and put them on Eleazar his son… Aaron will be gathered to his people, and he will die there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The transfer comes **before** the death. Moses strips his brother of the robes on the mountain top, dresses the son in them, and only then does Aaron die."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priesthood is not interrupted for a moment. There is never a point at which Israel has no high priest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it was done in public"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They went up the mountain in the sight of the whole community.* Three men climb; two come down, one of them wearing the vestments everybody recognised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had to be explained afterwards. The nation watched the office change hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it exposes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A priest dies and the robes go to somebody else, over and over, generation after generation. The system depends on replacement because the men wearing it keep running out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The comment the New Testament makes.** Hebrews puts its finger on exactly this: there had to be many priests, *because death kept stopping them from staying in office* — but He continues forever, so His priesthood does not pass to anyone else. The writer had scenes like this mountain in mind. See the note on Hebrews 2:14–16, where the same argument runs the other way: He took a body in order to die."}]}, "Numbers 26:52–56": {"title": "Measured out, and drawn by lot", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The land is about to be divided, and God gives two instructions that look as though they cancel each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two rules"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**by size** — *to a larger group give a larger inheritance, to a smaller group a smaller one, each receiving in proportion to those who were counted*", "**by lot** — *the land itself is to be assigned by lot… whether large or small, every inheritance is to be settled by the drawing of lots*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "How much you get is proportionate. **Where** you get it is drawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why both"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The size rule makes the allocation fair. The lot takes the choosing out of human hands entirely — no tribe lobbying for the fertile valleys, no favouritism, no Moses deciding who gets the coast."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs says it directly: the lot is cast into the lap, but its every decision is from the LORD. The Illumination's line here says the same thing plainly — *God's own hand would set the boundaries.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the second census, taken at the end of forty years. The first generation is gone; these are the people who will actually cross. The counting is done specifically so the land can be divided among them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the whole exercise is forward-looking. They are being numbered for an inheritance, before a single boundary stone has been placed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes next.** The very next paragraph is the daughters of Zelophehad coming forward — because a distribution had just been announced that had no place for them in it. The chapters belong together: the inheritance is described, and then somebody who was left out of the arrangement asks. See the note on Numbers 27:1–4."}]}, "Numbers 27:1–4": {"title": "Five women who asked", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five sisters walk to the front of the camp and ask for something the law had not provided for. It is one of the boldest scenes in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they stood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At the entrance to the tent of meeting, before Moses, before Eleazar the priest, before the leaders and the whole assembly.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a private word. They took it to the most public place in Israel and stood in front of every layer of authority at once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they are named — Mahlah, Noah, Hoglah, Milcah, Tirzah. The chapter records all five, and it records them again three more times in the books that follow. Scripture does not lose their names."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How they argued"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their case is careful, and they clear the ground before they ask:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["our father died in the wilderness", "**he was not among Korah's followers** — they head off the obvious objection before it is raised", "he died for his own sin, and he left no sons"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the question: *why should our father's name disappear from his clan simply because he had no son?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not complain about their treatment. They argue from their father's name and from what the inheritance was *for*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They asked, and they were not afraid to ask.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole scene. The land had not yet been distributed; the promise was still in front of everyone. They looked at what was about to be handed out, noticed they were not in the arrangement, and came forward anyway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where boldness like this is aimed.** They were not asking for something outside the promise. They were asking for their share *of* it — the portion that belonged to their father's name. That is the shape of a good request: not inventing an entitlement, but coming forward for what the promise already contained."}]}, "Numbers 27:5–7": {"title": "\"The daughters of Zelophehad are right\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses does not rule on it. He takes it upstairs, and the answer that comes back is unambiguous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "God's verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The daughters of Zelophehad are right.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words, and they settle it. Not *let them have a portion as an exception*, and not *this is irregular but permitted*. **Right** — their case is correct."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the instruction: *you must surely give them property as an inheritance among their father's relatives. Turn their father's inheritance over to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles the verb for emphasis — *giving you shall give*. It is the same construction used in Genesis 2 for eating freely from every tree. There is nothing grudging in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it changed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is not merely a favourable outcome for one family. Read on: it becomes **statute**. *If a man dies and leaves no son, turn his inheritance over to his daughter* — permanent law in Israel, from that day, because five women asked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request did not bend the rule. It made one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses did right"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He *brought their case before the LORD*. He did not decide it himself, and he did not dismiss it for being unprecedented."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Given what the same book records about Moses' patience running out elsewhere, this is worth noticing: presented with a claim no one had made before, his instinct was to take it to God rather than to defend the existing arrangement."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line the Illumination adds.** *What the overlooked had asked, God Himself upheld.* It is the translator summarising, and it is a fair reading of the scene. The people with no standing in the system got the ruling, and the ruling came from the top."}]}, "Numbers 32:1–5": {"title": "\"Do not make us cross the Jordan\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two tribes look at good grazing land on the wrong side of the river and ask to stop there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the request is framed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every part of it sounds reasonable:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["they genuinely have enormous herds", "Jazer and Gilead genuinely are good cattle country", "they ask politely — *if we have found favour with you*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the sentence is a rebellion. It is a sensible business decision made by men who could see what was in front of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one line that gives it away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not make us cross the Jordan.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Jordan is the whole point. Everything from Egypt onward has been aimed at crossing it — the promise to Abraham, the plagues, the sea, the forty years. The land on the other side is the land God swore to give them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not asking for less than they were promised because they were refused more. They are asking to be excused from receiving it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What made it look wise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The herds were real, and the grass was real. The decision was driven entirely by what suited the livestock — good land, immediately, without a river crossing or a war."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what makes the chapter worth reading slowly. Nothing here is obviously sinful. It is a preference for the visible good over the promised one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Moses' first reaction.** He hears it as the same failure as Kadesh — *are your brothers to go to war while you sit here?* — and reminds them what happened when ten spies discouraged the people forty years earlier. He treats it as serious immediately, and the rest of the chapter is the negotiation. See the note on Numbers 32:20–27."}]}, "Numbers 32:20–27": {"title": "\"Your sin will find you out\" — what it actually refers to", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famous line is quoted as a general warning about secret sin. In its own chapter it means something much more specific."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses agrees to the request, with terms:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["arm yourselves and cross the Jordan with everyone else", "stay until the LORD has driven out His enemies and the land is subdued", "**then** come back, free of obligation, and this land is yours"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The settlement on the east bank is allowed. What is not allowed is settling there while the rest of the nation fights."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the warning is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if you fail to do this, you will be sinning against the LORD, and know for certain your sin will find you out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sin named is **not going**. Leaving your brothers to take the land while you graze cattle in safety. That is the thing that would find them out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a promise-keeping verse, not a hidden-sin verse. Moses is telling men who have just made a commitment that failing to keep it will catch up with them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every one of us who can bear arms will cross over to fight before the LORD, as you have said.* Wives, children, flocks and herds stay; the fighting men go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they kept it. Joshua 22 records Joshua sending them home years later with a blessing, having done exactly what they said — *you have not deserted your brothers over this long time.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it cost them anyway.** Their land, being east of the river, was the first territory Israel lost. Reuben and Gad are the tribes carried off earliest in the Assyrian invasions. They kept the promise and they still ended up living on the exposed side of the border, which is the quiet ending the chapter never spells out."}]}, "Philippians 1:21–24": {"title": "A man weighing up his own death, in a letter about joy", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is in custody, the outcome is genuinely uncertain, and he does something unusual: he thinks out loud about which he would prefer, and admits he cannot decide."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words are both nautical"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I long to cast off and be with Christ* — the verb is **analuo**, used of loosing a ship from its moorings and of striking camp to move on. Not ending. Departing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Staying on in the body* — **epimeno**, to remain at a place, to stay put."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the choice is not between existing and not existing. It is between casting off and staying in port a while longer, which tells you what Paul thought death was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He genuinely cannot pick"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which of the two I would choose, I honestly cannot say. I am caught between them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image behind *caught between* is of pressure from both sides at once, like a man wedged. And the two pressures are not fear and hope — they are two goods:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["being with Christ, which he calls **better by far**", "being useful to people who need him, which he calls **more needed**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is resigned, and nothing is morbid. He is weighing a gain against a gain."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"For me, life means Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famous line is doing structural work. If life means Christ, then living is not a stretch of time to be got through, and if dying means gain, then death is not a loss to be feared. Both halves are needed for the paragraph to make sense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is also why the paragraph does not read as depression. A man for whom living has no content might long to depart; Paul says living is full and departing is better, and that is why he cannot choose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he settles it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He settles it on somebody else's account: *for your sake, staying on in the body is what is needed more.* Then he says he is convinced he will remain, for their progress and joy in the faith."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is worth being careful here. Paul is not claiming a private power over the timing of his own death — he had already written that he was under sentence and had learned not to rely on himself. What he does is weigh the two outcomes as a man who has stopped being afraid of either, and land on the one that serves the people he loves."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this sits in the letter.** Philippians is the joy letter — the word runs through all four chapters — and it was written from prison by a man openly discussing his possible execution. The two facts belong together. It is not joy despite the situation being described; it is joy from a source the situation cannot reach."}]}, "Philippians 2:12–13": {"title": "\"For it is God who is at work in you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses that are almost always quoted apart, and the second is the reason the first is possible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Work out\" is not \"work for\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Work out your own salvation with fear and trembling.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb means to bring to completion, to work something through to its end — what you do with something you already have. It was used of working a mine: extracting what is in there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not telling them to earn it. The letter has already called them saints and said God began a good work in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"for\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is God who is at work in you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything turns on that connective. The reason to work it out is that Someone else is working in you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the verb is **energeo** — the word behind our *energy*. God is the one supplying the power; the working out is what that power produces on the outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both halves of the willing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Both the willing and the doing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not merely the strength to do what you already wanted. The wanting itself is described as His work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence that takes the pressure out of the first verse. If even the desire is being supplied, then *fear and trembling* is not anxiety about performance — it is the seriousness appropriate to God working inside a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Paul says it here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at what precedes it: the hymn about Christ emptying Himself, taking the form of a servant, obedient to death on a cross, and being exalted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so, my beloved friends* — the instruction flows out of that. The working in is by the One who already did the emptying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The purpose clause.** *For the sake of what pleases Him.* The whole operation, willing and doing alike, is aimed at His good pleasure — which is where Paul located the reason for everything back in Ephesians 1. See that note."}]}, "Philippians 3:9": {"title": "Which righteousness a man is wearing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just listed his own credentials — circumcised the eighth day, tribe of Benjamin, a Pharisee, blameless as to the law — and called the whole file rubbish. This verse is what he wants instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two righteousnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not wearing a righteousness of my own that comes from the law, but the one that comes through faith in Christ, the righteousness that comes from God and rests on faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He sets them side by side:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "| his own | the other | |---|---| | from the law | from God | | produced | received | | worn as his own | found in Him |"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say his own record was fake. By the law's own measure he says he was blameless. He says it is the wrong kind of thing to be standing in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Found in Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is about being discovered in a place — where you turn out to be when somebody looks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's ambition is that whenever the inspection comes, he is located inside Christ rather than inside his own record. That is a statement about position, not about effort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for the credentials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The term he used two verses earlier is deliberately coarse — refuse, what is thrown to dogs. He is not saying his advantages were worthless in themselves; he is describing what they became once he had something to compare them with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that I may gain Christ* is the sentence they were traded for."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he wants next.** The verse immediately after: *that I may know Him and the power of His resurrection, and share in His sufferings.* Having settled that the standing is received rather than earned, the thing he then reaches for is not more standing. It is knowing the person."}]}, "Proverbs 10:1–7": {"title": "\"A diligent hand quietly makes him rich\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is where the long run of two-line proverbs begins, and the pattern is fixed from here on: one line, then its opposite. Each verse is a small before-and-after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pair about hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A lazy hand leaves a person poor. A diligent hand quietly makes him rich.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for **diligent** is a sharp word — it is used of a threshing sledge, the tool that cuts through grain. Not busyness; something decisive that gets through work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination's *quietly* is picking up the sense of the verb, which is about steady accumulation rather than a sudden windfall. Nothing dramatic is being promised. Something is being built by a hand that keeps going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one nobody quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wealth won by wrong does no good in the end. Righteousness pulls a soul back from death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It arrives before the industry proverbs and sets the terms for them: the book is not simply in favour of getting more. Two verses later comes gathering in summer versus sleeping through harvest — timing, not just effort."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On reading these as promises.** They are observations about how life generally runs, not contracts. The same book says elsewhere that the poor can be swept away by injustice regardless of how hard they work, and Ecclesiastes exists partly to press on the exceptions. Proverbs tells you where to put your weight; it does not promise the floor never gives."}]}, "Proverbs 10:22–27": {"title": "\"He adds no sorrow along with it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*It is the blessing of the LORD that makes rich, and He adds no sorrow along with it.* One of the most argued-over lines in Proverbs, because the second half can be read two ways in Hebrew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two readings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is compressed, and the disagreement is about what *toil* or *sorrow* attaches to:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**He adds no sorrow with it** — what God gives does not arrive with grief attached", "**and toiling adds nothing to it** — no amount of extra labour increases what the blessing gives"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are defensible from the grammar, and the second has an old pedigree. The first is the reading most English versions take, and the one this translation follows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way the contrast is with the verse a few lines earlier — *wealth won by wrong does no good in the end* — and with the sluggard two verses later. The chapter is not against work. It is against believing work is the source."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What the wicked dreads will fall upon him. What the righteous long for will be given.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pair, set deliberately: one man's fear arrives, the other's longing does. Proverbs states these as the general run of things, in the confident style the book uses throughout."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How to read the run of promises here.** Chapter 10 is a rapid series of contrasts, and it does not pause for exceptions — that is not what this genre does. Job and Ecclesiastes exist partly as the counterweight, and the Bible keeps all three in the same collection without embarrassment. Proverbs tells you which way the world generally leans; it does not promise that it never tips the other way."}]}, "Proverbs 11:8–14": {"title": "\"In many wise counselors there is safety\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famous line closes a run of sayings that are almost all about the mouth, and that is not an accident."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Read what it is sitting with"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*with his mouth the godless would ruin his neighbor*", "*by the blessing of the upright a city is lifted high; by the mouth of the wicked it is torn down*", "*whoever belittles a neighbor has no sense at all*", "*a gossip goes about giving secrets away; a faithful spirit keeps the matter quiet*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *where there is no guidance, a people falls. In many wise counselors there is safety.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the proverb about counsel arrives at the end of a paragraph about how much damage talk can do — which is a caution built into the promise. The counsellors being commended are the sort just described as trustworthy with what they are told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Guidance** is a navigation word — the same root is used of steering a ship, and elsewhere in Proverbs of a person of understanding acquiring *direction*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Safety** is literally deliverance, rescue. Not \"a better outcome.\" Getting out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the proverb assumes.** Not that a crowd is wiser than one person — this book has plenty to say about crowds. It assumes *wise* counsellors, plural, who can keep a confidence. A proverb is a general truth well put, not a mechanism, and this one carries its own quality control in the verses above it."}]}, "Proverbs 12:22–28": {"title": "\"The lazy man never roasts what he caught\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A run of contrasts, and one of them is a picture rather than a principle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hunter who does not cook"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The lazy man never roasts what he caught, but the diligent hold their gain as precious.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is difficult in Hebrew and translators have long divided over it. The sense most take is the one here: he went out, he caught something, and then he never got round to doing anything with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The failure is not in the hunt. It is in what happened after — the thing obtained and not used, the catch left on the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one of the few proverbs about laziness that concerns something already acquired rather than something never attempted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line worth stopping on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Worry weighs a heart down low, but a kind word makes it glad.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb means to bow down or make stoop. Anxiety is described physically — it bends a person over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the remedy offered is not advice or resolution. It is *a good word* from somebody else. In a chapter full of proverbs about hard work and honest speech, this one says that a heart bent by worry is straightened by a kindness spoken to it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *In the way of righteousness there is life, and along that path there is no death.* It is the strongest thing said in the chapter, and it reads oddly among proverbs about lying lips and lazy hands — which is how Proverbs works. The small practical sayings keep being punctuated by something much larger, without transition."}]}, "Proverbs 13:17–25": {"title": "\"Walk with the wise and grow wise\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The proverb everyone knows from this passage is about the company you keep, and the Hebrew behind it is more physical than the English."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb is \"walk\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *admire*, not *listen to*. **Halak** — to walk, to go along with, to travel in someone's company. It is the ordinary word for how you spend your days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is not of picking mentors. It is of who is beside you when nothing special is happening, and the proverb claims that wisdom transfers by proximity rather than by instruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second half is deliberately blunt: *keep fools for friends and come to harm*. Same mechanism, opposite direction. Whatever you walk with, you become."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A longing fulfilled is sweet to the soul. Fools would rather die than leave their evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Odd pairing, until you see it. The verse before speaks of a desire finally satisfied; the verse after describes people who will not let go of what is destroying them. Then: walk with the wise. The proverb about company is set between something worth wanting and an example of wanting the wrong thing badly enough to die of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On reading a proverb.** These are general truths, sharply put — not guarantees. The same chapter says the fields of the poor could yield plenty but injustice sweeps the harvest away, which is the book itself acknowledging that the pattern does not always hold. A proverb tells you which way to lean, not what is certain to happen."}]}, "Proverbs 13:9–16": {"title": "\"Money gathered slowly grows\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eight couplets, and one of them is about how money behaves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two ways it comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Money **grabbed fast slips fast away**. Money **gathered slowly grows**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the first is wealth from vanity, from nothing, from vapour — the same root as Ecclesiastes' *vanity of vanities*. The older rendering is *wealth gotten by vanity shall be diminished.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second is literally *gathered by the hand* — a handful at a time, by labour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb is not against money. It is about the manner of its arrival, and it says the manner determines whether it stays."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes fast, goes fast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb in the first line is to become small, to dwindle. It does not say the money is taken away — it says it shrinks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Hope stretched thin"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hope **stretched thin** makes the heart sick. A longing filled is a **tree of life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *hope deferred*. Something delayed past the point the heart can hold it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer is not a lowered expectation. It is a longing **filled** — and the image chosen for it is the tree in the garden."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lamp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The light of the upright **burns clear and glad**. The lamp of the wicked **flickers out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first verb carries rejoicing. A light that is glad."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the wisdom comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The teaching of the wise is a **spring of life**, turning your feet from the snares of death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A spring, not a cistern. Something with its own source."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one about advice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pride only breeds more quarrels. **Wisdom listens, and takes advice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *with the well-advised is wisdom*. It is not the clever who are wise in Proverbs; it is the ones who let themselves be counselled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the flag"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The clever act on what they know. **The fool spreads his folly like a flag**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that he spreads it out, unfurls it, makes a display."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter says a few verses later.** *A good man leaves an inheritance to his children's children, and the wealth of the sinner is stored up for the righteous.* Slowly gathered, and it outlasts him by two generations. See the note on Proverbs 13:17–25."}]}, "Proverbs 15:1–7": {"title": "\"A gentle answer turns away wrath\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted proverb about speech, and the word behind *gentle* is not what most readers assume."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **rak** — soft, tender. It is used of tender meat, of a delicate child, and of Leah's eyes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not weak, and not evasive. Soft in texture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turns away wrath.* The verb is about causing something to go back the way it came — deflection rather than absorption."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb is not saying a soft answer makes you feel better about the abuse. It says the anger goes back down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second half gives the alternative: *a harsh word stirs the anger up.* The Hebrew for *harsh* means painful, hurtful — a word that causes grief."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves describe an answer. The choice is not between speaking and staying silent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the paragraph sits with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every proverb in the run is about the mouth:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the tongue of the wise makes knowledge sweet*", "*a healing tongue is a tree of life; a twisted tongue breaks the spirit*", "*the lips of the wise scatter knowledge like seed*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tree of life image is the strongest. It appears four times in Proverbs, and one of them is a way of speaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The eyes of the LORD are everywhere, watching the evil and the good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An odd insertion in a run about speech — until you notice what it does to the rest. All these exchanges happen in front of somebody."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where a soft answer is hardest.** The examples in Scripture are unflattering. Peter, told the cross was coming, argued. James and John proposed fire. Paul called a high priest a whitewashed wall and then apologised when told who he was. The proverb is not describing a personality type — it is describing a choice made in the second after being provoked."}]}, "Proverbs 15:16–23": {"title": "\"A word in season, how good it is\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The run ends on a line about timing, and everything before it is about what makes a household bearable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two \"betters\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Better a little with the fear of the LORD than great riches and trouble with it. Better a plate of greens where love is than a fattened calf and hatred at the table.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both compare a small good thing with a large bad one, and both put the difference in the atmosphere rather than the menu."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A fattened calf was the best meal available — the thing killed for the prodigal's homecoming. Proverbs says vegetables beat it if the room is hostile."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The temper proverbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A hot temper stirs up strife. A patient one calms the quarrel down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for patient is *long of nostrils* — slow to flare. The idiom is the same one used of God being slow to anger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is joy in giving the right answer, and **a word in season**, how good it is.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two halves. The right answer, and the right moment — and Proverbs treats them as separate achievements."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A true thing said at the wrong hour is not counted here as a success. The verse just before it makes the same point from the other side: *the one who understands walks straight ahead* rather than blurting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it sits with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Plans fall apart without counsel, but with many advisers they stand.* The season-word proverb comes directly after it — which suggests the good answer is often somebody else's, arriving when you needed it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How to use a run like this.** Proverbs 15 is a chapter of contrasts about speech: a gentle answer turning away wrath at the top, a soothing tongue as a tree of life in the middle, and the word in season at the end. Read the chapter whole and the subject is not wisdom in the abstract. It is what comes out of a person's mouth in a house."}]}, "Proverbs 18:17–24": {"title": "\"Death and life are in the power of the tongue\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the most quoted proverbs about speech, and the two lines it sits between are what keep it in proportion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man eats his fill from the fruit of his mouth; the harvest of his lips is what satisfies him. Death and life are in the power of the tongue, and those who love it will eat its fruit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is agricultural and it runs across both verses: words are a crop, and the person who grew them is the one who has to eat the harvest. What you say comes back to your own table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *power* is literally **hand** — death and life are in the hand of the tongue. It is a small organ given a large grip."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the surrounding verses are about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at the company it keeps in this chapter:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the first to plead his case seems right, until another comes and questions him*", "*a brother offended is harder to win than a fortified city*", "*the poor plead for mercy, but the rich answer with harsh words*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one is about speech between people — testimony, a quarrel that has hardened, an answer given roughly to someone with no power. That is the field this proverb grows in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On how it gets used.** The verse is often taken to mean that spoken words shape outcomes by their own force. What the proverb states is narrower and harder to dismiss: your words do real damage or real good to other people, and you will live with the result. James says the same at length — a small rudder, a small fire — and both writers are talking about the tongue's effect on human beings, not on circumstances."}]}, "Proverbs 2:6–11": {"title": "\"From His mouth come knowledge and understanding\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Wisdom in this chapter is not a skill acquired. It is something given, and the passage says where from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The source"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is the LORD who gives wisdom; from His mouth come knowledge and understanding.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is spoken — out of His mouth. Which puts the search for wisdom in the same place as the search for what He has said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses just before this describe looking for it like silver and digging for it like hidden treasure. Then the reason that search works: the thing being dug for is being handed over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does once you have it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the run of verbs — every one of them is protective:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["He is a **shield** to those who walk with honest hearts", "He **guards** the paths of the just", "He **watches over** the way of those who love Him", "good sense will **keep watch** over you", "understanding will **guard** your steps"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five in six verses. In this chapter wisdom is not primarily about being clever. It is about being kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it settles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wisdom will settle into your heart, and knowledge will be a delight to your soul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *come into* your heart — it takes up residence rather than being consulted. And the result is described as pleasure, not duty: knowledge becoming something a person enjoys."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows immediately.** *Then you will understand what is right and fair, every good path will open before you.* The understanding of what is right comes after the wisdom is given, not before. In Proverbs the discernment is a consequence of the gift, which is why the chapter opens by telling you to go looking for it."}]}, "Proverbs 22:1–7": {"title": "\"Train up a child in the way he should go\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*Train up a child in the way he should go, and when he is old he will not turn from it.* A promise with a long fuse, and the Hebrew has more in it than the English lets through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"According to his way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is **al pi darko** — literally *on the mouth of his way*. There is no word for *should* in the Hebrew at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The natural sense is *according to his way*: this child, his bent, his grain, the particular shape of him. It turns the instruction from a method applied to children in general into an act of attention paid to one child in particular."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You train the child you actually have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chanak** is used elsewhere of dedicating a house or a temple — setting something apart for the purpose it was made for. The related noun gives us Hanukkah, the feast of dedication."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the training is not primarily drilling. It is dedicating: putting a child on the path he was made for, early."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he is old he will not turn from it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The timescale is the striking part. Not next year. When he is old. The promise reaches past the years a parent has any influence at all, and past the stretch where it may look as if nothing took."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what makes it worth having. A promise that only covered the compliant years would be no comfort to anybody."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter opens with.** *Choose a good name over great riches.* The chapter that contains the great parenting promise begins by saying what is worth handing on, and it is not money. Four verses later: *the reward of humility and the fear of the LORD is riches and honour and life.* The inheritance being described runs through character."}]}, "Proverbs 28:21–28": {"title": "\"The stingy man hurries after wealth\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eight couplets about money, and the sharpest of them is about a man in a hurry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hurry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **stingy man hurries after wealth** and never knows that want is coming for him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *stingy* is *a man with an evil eye* — the idiom for a grudging, tight, envious way of looking at things. Jesus uses the same expression: *if your eye is bad, your whole body will be full of darkness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things are said about him: he hurries, and he does not know what is coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is coming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Want.* The Hebrew is lack, poverty — and the verb has it arriving at him, coming upon him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is running toward money with the thing he is running from already on the road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Trust and enrichment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **greedy heart stirs up strife**, but **whoever trusts in the LORD will be enriched**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two halves are the alternative to each other. A grasping heart produces conflict; trust produces increase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *enriched* is to be made fat — the word for prosperity and fullness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift that does not empty"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **gives to the poor will lack nothing**, but whoever shuts his eyes to them gathers many curses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall not lack*. Nothing at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the opposite is not *will be poor* — it is *gathers many curses*. The man who shuts his eyes is still gathering; the chapter simply names what he is collecting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Rebuke and flattery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **rebukes** a man will find more favour at last than the one who **flatters** with his tongue.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At last* — afterward, in the end. The flatterer is preferred now."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set that beside a king who *never once crossed* his son, never asked *why do you do this?* See the note on 1 Kings 1:5–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Trusting your own heart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **trusts his own heart is a fool**, but whoever walks in wisdom will be kept safe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the flat contradiction of the usual advice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last couplet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the wicked rise, people go into hiding, **but when they perish, the righteous increase**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says it twice — the same line appears seven verses earlier."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the same chapter says about interest.** *Whoever swells his wealth by interest and gain **gathers it for one who is kind to the poor**.* He is still gathering. The proverb only disagrees with him about who the pile is for. See the note on Proverbs 28:7–13."}]}, "Proverbs 28:7–13": {"title": "\"Gathers it for one who is kind to the poor\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A proverb about interest that turns out to be about who ends up holding the money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **swells his wealth by interest and gain gathers it for one who is kind to the poor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not stopped, and he is not impoverished. He goes on swelling the pile."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb simply tells him whose pile it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law said about interest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You shall not lend him your money for interest, nor lend him your food at a profit* — Leviticus 25, about a brother who has become poor."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Taking interest from the destitute was the specific practice the law forbade, and it is the practice in view here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who receives it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One who is **kind to the poor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the one who shows favour, who is gracious to the poor — the root of **chen**, grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who squeezed the poor for interest is described as a collector working on behalf of a man who gives to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same idea elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wealth of the sinner is stored up for the righteous* — Proverbs 13:22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To the one who misses the mark, God gives the task of gathering and piling up, only to hand it over to the one who pleases God* — Ecclesiastes 2:26. See the note on Ecclesiastes 2:24–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three places, same claim. The gathering and the keeping are two different jobs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer that offends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a man **turns his ear from hearing the law**, even his prayer becomes an offence.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that his prayer is an abomination. He is still praying — the proverb does not say he stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever leads the upright down an evil road **falls into his own pit**, but the **blameless will inherit good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pit is his. He dug it for somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rich man's eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The rich man is **wise in his own eyes**, but a poor man with sense **sees right through him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that the poor man searches him out. Money buys the appearance of judgement, and it does not fool a discerning man with nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **hides** his sins will not prosper, but whoever **confesses and forsakes** them will find mercy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs on each side, and the outcome named at the end is mercy."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What connects the chapter.** Hiding. The man hides his sins, the wealthy man hides behind his own good opinion, the people hide when the wicked rise. And the one line about not hiding ends in mercy."}]}, "Proverbs 3:1–4": {"title": "\"Tie them around your neck\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A father tells his son to keep two things close, and describes where to keep them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let love and faithfulness leave you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew pair is **hesed and emet** — covenant love and dependability. It is one of the standard descriptions of God's own character, the words He speaks over Himself when He passes by Moses on the mountain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the son is being told to wear what God is like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two places"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tie them around your neck, write them on the tablet of your heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Outside and inside. Around the neck is visible — an ornament, or the cord a seal hung on. On the tablet of the heart is where nobody else sees."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both, and the order matters: the visible one is named first, but the inward one is the permanent one. Jeremiah later promises a covenant written on hearts rather than stone, using the same image."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will add to your days, long years and a peaceful life… you will find favour and good name in the sight of God and everyone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Favour with both — God and people. Luke uses almost exactly this phrasing of Jesus growing up in Nazareth: increasing in favour with God and with people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not forget my teaching, let your heart hold on to my commands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *learn* — do not forget. The instruction assumes it has already been given. Much of Proverbs is a father repeating what a son has heard before, on the understanding that knowing something and keeping hold of it are different jobs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes three verses later.** *Trust in the LORD with all your heart and do not lean on your own understanding.* This paragraph is the run-up to it, and it is worth reading them together: hold on to what you were taught, and do not rely on your own reading of the situation."}]}, "Proverbs 4:20–27": {"title": "\"Health to the whole body\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A father tells his son what to do with words, and then works down the body part by part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the words are called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They are life to those who find them, and **health to the whole body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *health* is **marpe** — healing, a cure, and by extension soundness. It is used of a physician's remedy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the phrase is literally *to all his flesh*. Not to his mind or his character. His flesh."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How to keep them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs in two lines: *pay attention… lean your ear in close… do not let them slip from your sight*, and then the fourth, which is the destination — *keep them deep within your heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ear, eye, and then inside. The route is specified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the body, in order"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the heart** — *above all else, guard it, for from it flow the springs of life*", "**the mouth** — put away twisted speech", "**the eyes** — look straight ahead", "**the feet** — give careful thought to where you step"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Heart first, and everything after it flows from that. The Hebrew for *springs* is the word for the outgoings or sources of a thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Guard your heart\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is a sentry's word — to watch over, to keep under guard. It assumes something is trying to get in, or out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is not sentimental. It is hydraulic: whatever is in there is what will come out everywhere else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the passage ends.** *Do not turn to the right or the left.* The same phrase Joshua was given about the book of the law. The chapter is not describing a mood or an atmosphere — it is describing a road, walked deliberately, by somebody watching where their feet go."}]}, "Proverbs 9:10–12": {"title": "\"Where wisdom begins\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sentence the whole book is built on, stated here for the second time — it opened the collection back in chapter 1."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Begins\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The fear of the LORD is where wisdom begins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word can mean the starting point or the chief part, and both senses are usually intended. It is the first thing and the principal thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What the verse rules out is treating wisdom as a technique that could be acquired by someone starting anywhere. There is a door, and this is it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second line defines the first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And knowing the Holy One is understanding.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew poetry works by pairing lines that illuminate each other. So *fear of the LORD* is set beside *knowing the Holy One* — which keeps the fear from collapsing into terror."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the awe of someone who knows who they are dealing with. You cannot be in awe of someone you do not know at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who carries the result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you are wise, your wisdom rewards you. If you mock, you carry the weight alone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An unusually stark note to end on. Wisdom is described as paying its owner — and the mocker is not threatened with punishment so much as told he will bear it by himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both outcomes land on the person who chose them. Nobody else absorbs either."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it sits in the chapter.** Proverbs 9 stages two dinner invitations — Wisdom has built her house and set her table, and Folly sits at her door calling to passers-by. Both call the same people. This verse is at the hinge, and the chapter is asking which house you eat in."}]}, "Psalms 1:1–3": {"title": "Three verbs going downhill, and a tree that does not", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm that opens the whole book is built on a progression most readers walk straight past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Walk, stand, sit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Won't walk in step with the wicked… won't stop and stand where sinners loiter… won't settle into the seat of the scornful.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three postures, and they descend: moving alongside, then stopping, then sitting down. The company gets worse as the posture gets more settled — wicked, then sinners, then scoffers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a description of how a thing that starts as company ends as identity, and it is arranged so you can see the slope."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for meditating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turning it over softly, day and night.* The Hebrew is **hagah** — the same word in Joshua 1:8, and it is audible: a lion growling, a dove moaning, a person muttering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set against the three verbs above, it is what fills the space they left. The happy person is not defined by avoidance; something else has their attention."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rooted beside flowing streams… offers its fruit right on time, whose leaves never wither.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two details are easy to miss. The tree is **planted** — the Hebrew is passive, transplanted by someone. And the fruit comes *in its season*, which is a real qualification: not constantly, not on demand."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"Whatever that one does comes into bloom.\"** The line has been used to promise success in any undertaking, which the psalm does not support — it is a tree image, and trees do not prosper by trying. What the psalm claims is that a life rooted in the right place bears what it is meant to bear, on time, and does not dry out when the heat comes. Jeremiah 17 uses the same picture and spells that out: it does not fear when heat comes, and in drought it does not stop bearing."}]}, "Psalms 103:3–5": {"title": "\"Your youth is renewed like the eagle's\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five things listed in three verses, and David tells himself not to forget any of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *Bless the LORD, O my soul, and **forget not all His benefits**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is talking to himself, and the list that follows is what he is refusing to forget."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*He **forgives** all your wrongdoing*", "*He **heals** all your sicknesses*", "*He **redeems** your life from the pit*", "*He **crowns** you with steadfast love and mercy*", "*He **fills** your life with good things*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "**All**, twice. All your wrongdoing and all your sicknesses, and the two sit in consecutive lines as a matched pair — the same *all* over both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Forgiveness and healing together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew poetry pairs lines deliberately. These two are not a list of unrelated items; they are set side by side because they belong together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pairing turns up when the paralysed man is let down through the roof: *your sins are forgiven* first, and then *rise, take up your bed.* And in Isaiah 53, *wounded for our transgressions* and *by His stripes we are healed* are two clauses of one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Redeems your life from the pit\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ga'al** — the kinsman-redeemer's word, the relative whose duty was to buy back what a family member had lost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Crowns you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is to encircle, to surround. **Steadfast love** is **chesed**, the covenant word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Crowned with it — worn on the head, visible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The renewal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **fills your life with good things**, so **your youth is renewed like the eagle's**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *fills* is to satisfy, and the older rendering is *satisfies your mouth with good things*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Youth** is **na'ar** — the word for a young man, a lad. The season of natural strength."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb *renewed* is **chadash**, to make new, to restore. It is the same root as the new moon, and the new covenant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eagle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah uses the same bird for the same idea: *those who wait on the LORD shall renew their strength; they shall **mount up with wings like eagles**.* See the note on Isaiah 40:29–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An eagle is long-lived and, in the ancient picture, moults and comes back with new feathers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm says a few lines on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As far as the east is from the west, so far has He removed our transgressions from us. As a father pities his children, so the LORD pities those who fear Him. For He knows our frame; **He remembers that we are dust**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same psalm that promises renewed youth also says He has not forgotten what we are made of."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David says about the source.** *Who satisfies…* — every verb in the list has the same subject, and it is never the reader. Five things, one actor."}]}, "Psalms 105:37–38": {"title": "\"Not one of their tribes stumbled\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm is a retelling of Israel's history, and this is its summary of the exodus in two lines."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it records"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He brought His people out with silver and gold, and not one of their tribes stumbled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two claims. They left with wealth — the payment described in Exodus 11 and 12 — and the whole company got out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *stumbled* means to totter or be feeble. The psalmist is describing a mass departure of perhaps a very large number of people, including the old and the very young, in which nobody fell behind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the whole of Psalm 105 and the pattern is unmistakable: it is a catalogue of things God did, verb after verb, with almost nothing about what Israel did. He sent, He turned, He spoke, He brought out, He spread a cloud, He opened the rock."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's purpose is stated at the start — remember, give thanks, tell what He has done. This verse is one line in a very long list of that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had done the night before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is not describing a crowd that happened to be in good health. It is describing the morning after Passover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every household in that nation had, the previous evening, eaten a lamb — *roasted with fire… his head with his legs and with the appurtenance thereof*, the whole animal, and the instruction was to eat it, not merely to kill it. The blood went on the doorframe and the lamb went inside them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ate the lamb, and they came out the next morning with nobody feeble among them. The psalm puts the two facts side by side because they belong side by side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "However many they were, the number included the elderly, the newborn, and everyone in between — a whole population that had been in hard labour under Egyptian taskmasters for generations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them stumbled. Not one lagged behind. That is what is being recorded, and the psalm records it as a thing God did."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The lamb and the table.** Paul calls Him *Christ our Passover*, sacrificed for us, and the table He left His disciples is the table of that lamb. The bread is His body, broken. What the night of the exodus produced in Israel is what the body was given for. See the notes on 1 Corinthians 11:23–26 and Matthew 8:14–17."}]}, "Psalms 112:1–3": {"title": "\"Their goodness stands forever\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A psalm about a person, and it is deliberately built to echo the one before it, which is about God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 111 describes the LORD: His work is honour and majesty, His righteousness endures forever."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 112 describes the person who fears Him — and uses the same phrases. *Their goodness stands forever* is word for word what the previous psalm said about God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two are twin acrostics in Hebrew, each running through the alphabet. Read together, the second is a portrait of someone taking after the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it begins"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Happy is the one who holds the LORD in awe, who delights, deep down, in what He asks.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *obeys* first. **Delights** — the Hebrew is about taking pleasure in something, wanting it. The commands are described as a pleasure before they are described as a duty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their children will be strong across the land… Their house holds plenty and wealth, and their goodness stands forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three generations of effect: the person, the children, and something that outlasts both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last clause is the one that does not fit the pattern of the other two. Plenty and wealth are in the house; the righteousness stands *forever* — it is the only item on the list that time cannot reach."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read on for the harder verses.** The psalm goes on: *even in darkness light dawns for the upright*, and *they will have no fear of bad news; their heart is steady, trusting in the LORD.* It does not describe a life without darkness or bad news. It describes someone who has light in the first and a steady heart in the second."}]}, "Psalms 116:8–11": {"title": "\"I believed, even when I said, I am badly shaken\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The verse Paul builds *the spirit of faith* on, and it is not spoken from a comfortable place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three rescues"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You rescued **my life from death**, **my eyes from tears**, **my feet from stumbling**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, and they cover three different things: the body, the emotions, the walk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for the middle one is that He delivered his eye from tears. Not that the tears were forbidden — that they were stopped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **walk before the LORD in the land of the living**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in the temple, not in retreat. In the land of the living — ordinary daylight, among people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line Paul quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I believed, even when I said, \"**I am badly shaken**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are in the sentence at once. He believed **and** he said he was in pieces."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I was greatly afflicted*. Nothing is being minimised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes this exact line into 2 Corinthians — *we have the same spirit of faith as the one who wrote, \"I believed, and so I spoke\"* — while listing beatings, shipwrecks and being hard pressed on every side. See the note on 2 Corinthians 4:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So whatever the spirit of faith is, both men exercised it while stating plainly how bad things were."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In my alarm I said, \"**Every person is a liar.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *in my haste* — said hurriedly, in panic. He is quoting himself at his worst and flagging that it was hasty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm keeps his panic on the record rather than editing it out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What surrounds it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the psalm from the top and it opens with *the pains of death surrounded me, and the pangs of Sheol laid hold of me; I found trouble and sorrow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *I love the LORD, because He has heard my voice.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Further down the same psalm: *what shall I render to the LORD for all His benefits toward me? **I will take up the cup of salvation** and call upon the name of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 116 is one of the Hallel psalms, sung at Passover. The cup of salvation is in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus sang this psalm with the disciples in the upper room, on the night He took a cup and said *this is My blood of the new covenant.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse in the middle of it.** *Precious in the sight of the LORD is the death of His saints.* Written by a man who has just said God rescued his life from death — and who still calls the other outcome precious."}]}, "Psalms 119:105–112": {"title": "\"A lamp for the ground beneath my feet\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The best-known verse of the longest psalm, and the two halves describe two different distances."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Lamp and light"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your word is a **lamp for the ground beneath my feet**, a **light laid down along the path** I walk.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An oil lamp of that period was small — a clay dish you carried in one hand or tied at the ankle. It threw enough light for the next step and no further."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second image widens it: light along the path. Near and far, in one verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is floodlighting. You get the next step, and enough sense of the road to keep going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What surrounds it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not use the lamp verse as a comfortable saying. Read the eight lines it belongs to:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*I am worn thin, brought low almost to nothing*", "*my life is always cupped here in my hands* — an idiom for constant danger", "*the wicked set their snare along my way*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamp is being carried by a man in trouble, at night, with traps on the road. That is the setting the verse belongs to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I've sworn an oath, and I intend to keep it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He binds himself twice in the passage — the oath here, and at the end *I've bent my heart to living out Your statutes, forever, faithful, all the way to the end.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *bent my heart* is about inclining or stretching something toward a direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inheritance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your steady words I've taken as my heirloom, my heart's own gladness, treasure without end.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Heirloom** is the word for an inherited portion — the family land that passed down. A man with nothing else says the words are the estate he was left."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The whole psalm.** 176 verses, eight per Hebrew letter, and almost every one mentions God's word under one name or another — law, precepts, statutes, promises, judgments. It is the longest sustained meditation on Scripture inside Scripture, and it was written by somebody being hunted."}]}, "Psalms 119:129–136": {"title": "\"The opening of Your words lets in the light\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The **Pe** stanza — eight lines, every one beginning with the same Hebrew letter, and the middle of it says what happens when the words are opened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The unfolding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **opening** of Your words lets in the light; it gives plain folk the sense they never had.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a noun built from the verb *to open* — an entrance, an unfolding, a doorway. The word itself carries the picture: something shut that is opened, and light comes through the gap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It gives **plain folk** the sense they never had.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **pethi** is the simple one — not the fool, and not the scoffer, but the untaught, the person with no advantage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the one specified as the beneficiary. Not the learned. The opening of the words is what levels the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The panting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I open wide my mouth and pant for breath, so hungry, longing for Your good commands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is an undignified image and the psalmist chose it. The Hebrew is the gasping of someone who has been running, or of an animal in heat and thirst."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The requests in between"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*steady my footsteps by the word You gave*", "*let no wrong thing hold mastery over me*", "*rescue me from the men who crush and cheat*", "*let Your bright face shine warm upon Your servant*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Let Your face shine** is the Aaronic blessing, quoted back to God by a man asking for what was already promised over him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the stanza ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rivers of tears go running from my eyes, because the people will not keep Your teaching.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *channels of water* — the runnels that carry a stream. Not for himself. For everybody else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The stanza's shape.** It opens with the words being wonderful and closes with weeping over people who ignore them. The distance between those two lines is the whole burden of the psalm."}]}, "Psalms 127:1–2": {"title": "\"He gives sleep to His beloved\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A short psalm attributed to Solomon — the man who built the actual house and watched over the actual city — and it begins by saying so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two examples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Unless the LORD builds the house, its builders labour in vain. Unless the LORD watches the city, the watchman keeps awake for nothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Building and guarding: making something, and keeping it. Between them they cover most of what anyone spends a life doing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not say the builders are idle or the watchmen asleep. It says their work is *in vain* without something else — the labour is real and the result is not in their hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The bread of anxious toil\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is no use rising early, sitting up late, eating the bread of anxious toil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is literally the bread of *pains* or *sorrows* — the same root used of the toil laid on the ground in Genesis 3. It is a picture of work eaten as misery: early starts, late nights, and food taken standing up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For to His beloved He gives sleep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew here is compressed and can be read two ways: *He gives sleep to His beloved*, or *He gives to His beloved while they sleep*. Translators have split on it for centuries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way, sleep is the image, and it is offered as the alternative to the anxious bread. The psalm's argument is not that work is pointless — it is that the outcome does not depend on staying awake."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes next.** The rest of the psalm is about children being a heritage from the LORD. The two halves belong together: a house is built and a city is kept, and what actually endures out of a life is described as something received rather than achieved."}]}, "Psalms 128:1–2": {"title": "\"You will eat the fruit of your labour\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the Songs of Ascents — the fifteen psalms pilgrims sang walking up to Jerusalem — and it is about a house rather than a temple."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is happy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Happy is **everyone** who fears the LORD, who walks in His ways.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ashrei** — the word Psalm 1 opens with, and the word behind *blessed are* in the Beatitudes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Everyone.** Not the priest, the Levite, or the notable. The psalm is deliberately ordinary from the first line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eating what you grew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will **eat the fruit of your labour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sounds unremarkable until you set it beside the curse in Deuteronomy 28: *you shall build a house and not dwell in it; you shall plant a vineyard and not eat of it… your ox shall be slaughtered before your eyes and you shall not eat of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the shape of the curse — working and not getting to keep it. The blessing here is the plain reversal: you eat what your own hands produced."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah says the same thing about the world put right: *they shall not build and another inhabit; they shall not plant and another eat.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two things, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be **happy**, and **it will go well with you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles it — good for you, and well with you. Inside and outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rest of the psalm shows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your wife shall be like a fruitful vine in the very heart of your house, your children like olive plants all around your table.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A vine in the middle of the house and young olive trees round a table. Both are slow-growing plants that take years before they yield, and both go on yielding for generations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is a meal. That is the whole vision of blessing in this psalm — a man eating what he grew, at a table, with his family around it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the blessing comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **bless you out of Zion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not out of the field. The blessing on the house is sourced at the sanctuary, and the psalm is sung by people walking toward it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And how far it reaches"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May you see your children's children. Peace be upon Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grandchildren, and then the nation. The psalm starts with one man's fear of God and ends with peace on a whole people."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it sits.** Psalm 127 is the one before it — *unless the LORD builds the house, they labour in vain who build it… He gives His beloved sleep.* The two are a pair: one says the building is His, and then this one describes the house."}]}, "Psalms 129:3–4": {"title": "\"The plowmen plowed across my back\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the shorter Songs of Ascents, and one of its images is unusually brutal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The picture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The plowmen plowed across my back and cut their long furrows there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A plough opening ground, used of a human back. The Hebrew for *furrows* is the agricultural term — the parallel cuts a blade leaves in a field."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel is speaking as one person here, describing centuries of oppression from Egypt onward: *many a time they have afflicted me from my youth, yet they have not prevailed against me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is a harvest image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm is one of the pilgrim songs, sung by farmers walking up to Jerusalem for the festivals. A ploughed field was not a picture of ruin to them — it was what you did before sowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That tension is left standing. A back opened like a field, in a psalm sung on the way to a harvest feast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the LORD is just. He has cut me free from the ropes of the wicked.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs in tension: they cut the back, He cuts the ropes. The same action word answering itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is just* is stated as the reason. Not that Israel endured well — that God is righteous, and therefore the cords did not hold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where readers have taken it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scourging of Jesus is described in the Gospels in a single word and no detail. Christian readers have long heard this verse behind it, and it sits naturally beside Isaiah's servant whose back was given to those who struck him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not say that. It is Israel's own account of survival — and it is worth noticing that the language it reaches for is the language of a body taking what a field takes."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the psalm ends.** With the wicked as grass on a roof — sprouting fast in shallow soil, withered before anyone can gather it. Set against a ploughed field, the contrast is between ground that gets worked and ground nothing grows in."}]}, "Psalms 133:1": {"title": "\"When brothers dwell together as one\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses in the whole psalm, and this is the first. The other two are the pictures."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look how **good**, look how **pleasant**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both, and they are not the same thing. Some good things are not pleasant and some pleasant things are not good. The psalm says unity is both at once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Dwell together\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is about settling, staying, living in one place — not visiting. It is the word used of a family sharing an inheritance rather than dividing it and moving apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is compared to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm goes on to two images, and both are about something running downward:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**oil poured on Aaron's head**, running down his beard, down onto the collar of his robes. The anointing oil of a priest, and the psalm follows it all the way down.", "**the dew of Hermon** falling on the mountains of Zion. Hermon is the great snow peak in the far north; Zion is two hundred kilometres south. The image is of moisture from the highest place arriving somewhere else entirely."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are liquid, both come from above, and both go down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For there the LORD commanded the blessing — life for evermore.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There* — in the place where brothers dwell together. The blessing is located rather than merely described."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What kind of psalm it is.** One of the Songs of Ascents, sung by pilgrims walking up to Jerusalem for the festivals — people from scattered towns arriving in one place three times a year. The unity being celebrated is not theoretical. It is a road full of families converging."}]}, "Psalms 147:1–3": {"title": "\"He heals the brokenhearted\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm puts two things side by side that do not obviously belong together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three actions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **rebuilds Jerusalem**. He **gathers the scattered** of Israel. He **heals the brokenhearted** and **binds up their wounds**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A city, a nation, and a person — and they are in one breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scale drops from a skyline to an individual without any change of tone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Binds up\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chabash** — to bandage, to wrap. It is what you do to a wound with cloth, and it is what a physician does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *wounds* is the word for pain, hurt, a sore place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is metaphorical in the Hebrew. It is field-dressing vocabulary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **counts the number of the stars**; He **calls them all by name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is verse 4, and it is the line the passage is best known for pairing with verse 3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same God binding up one person's wounds is counting a sky nobody has finished counting, and naming every one of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then verse 5: *Great is our Lord, and mighty in power; His understanding is infinite.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stars are there to establish the size of the One doing the bandaging."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stars, and Abraham"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb behind counting the stars is **saphar** — to number, and to recount, to tell."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same word God used when He took Abram outside: *count the stars, if you can even number them.* See the note on Genesis 15:4–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the psalm is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He rebuilds Jerusalem. He gathers the scattered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written for people coming home from exile to a ruined city. Not an untroubled congregation — a returning one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm says God is not impressed by"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Further down: *He does not delight in the strength of the horse; He takes no pleasure in the legs of a man.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two fastest things in that world, and both are dismissed in one verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD takes pleasure in those who fear Him, in those who hope in His mercy.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hallelujah! How **good** it is to sing to our God, how **pleasant**, how **fitting** the praise.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words for it. The psalm argues that praise is good, that it is enjoyable, and that it is appropriate — and then gives four verses of reasons."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm ends on.** *He declares His word to Jacob, His statutes and His judgements to Israel. He has not dealt thus with any nation.* The final boast of the psalm is not the stars or the city. It is that He talked to them."}]}, "Psalms 18:16–24": {"title": "\"He brought me into a wide open place\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David wrote this the day the LORD delivered him from all his enemies and from Saul. It is the longest psalm of rescue in the book, and this is the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Reaching down"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **reached down from on high** and took hold of me. He **drew me up** out of the deep waters.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *drew me up* is the verb in Moses' name — *because I drew him out of the water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David uses the language of a baby lifted out of a river to describe his own rescue from Saul."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From those too strong for me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From my mighty enemy, from those who hated me, **from those too strong for me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He concedes the point without embarrassment. They were stronger. That is the premise of the rescue, not an objection to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They came at me **on my day of ruin**, but the LORD was my support.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the day of my calamity*. He does not say there was no such day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage takes for granted that the trouble happened, that he went under, and that the deep waters were deep. He is not describing an escape from the valley. He is describing being drawn up out of the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wide place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **brought me into a wide open place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Merchab** — a broad place, open ground, room. It is the opposite of the Hebrew word for trouble, which is built on the idea of being pressed and narrow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Distress is being squeezed; deliverance is space. And note the verb: He brought him *into* it — out of one place and into another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He rescued me **because He delighted in me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the reason stated in the text, and it is stated before any of the lines about clean hands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a word of pleasure and desire. He wanted to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Clean hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD rewarded me for my right living. For clean hands He repaid me… I was blameless before Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David says this in a psalm written after Saul had hunted him for years and he had twice refused to put out his hand against the LORD's anointed when he had him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The delight comes first in the passage, and the record follows it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the psalm appears twice.** It is Psalm 18 and it is also 2 Samuel 22 — the same song printed once in the songbook and once in the history. Whoever put the books together thought the day David was drawn out of deep water was worth recording in both."}]}, "Psalms 22:1–5": {"title": "\"My God, my God\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opening line of this psalm is the sentence Jesus cried from the cross, in Aramaic, at the ninth hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My God, my God, **why have You abandoned me?***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not compose it there. He quoted it. And in that culture, naming the first line of a psalm called up the whole psalm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the rest of it and it is a description of a crucifixion written a thousand years before the Romans invented one: *they pierced my hands and my feet… I can count all my bones… they divide my garments among them and cast lots for my clothing… all who see me mock me, they shake their heads.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"My God\" — twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even in the abandonment the possessive holds. He says *my* God, twice, to the One He is saying has left Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is different here from every other psalm of distress"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I cry out **by day, but You do not answer**, by night, but I find no rest.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's psalms of trouble almost always turn on a rescue that comes. This one records the silence and does not explain it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **You are holy**, enthroned on the praises of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word *yet* carries it. Nothing is being accused. In the middle of the forsaking, holiness is affirmed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs about the fathers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In You our fathers **trusted**. They **trusted**, and You **delivered** them. They **cried** to You and were **rescued**. They trusted in You and were **not put to shame**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone before Him trusted and was delivered. That is what makes the first line what it is: the pattern held for all of them, and it did not hold here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the one exception in the history of the psalm. He trusted, and He was not delivered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not stay there. From the middle it turns: *You have answered me.* And it closes on a proclamation to a people not yet born — *they shall come and declare His righteousness to a people who will be born, that **He has done it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three Hebrew words at the end. The Greek of the New Testament renders the same thought in one: *tetelestai*. It is finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three psalms together"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**22** — the cross, forsaken, *He has done it*", "**23** — the shepherd, the valley, the table, goodness and mercy following", "**24** — *lift up your heads, O gates… the King of glory shall come in*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Death, then care, then the King coming in through the gates. They stand in that order in the book. See the notes on Psalms 23:1–3 and Psalms 24:7–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the psalm's opening.** Any answer to the question. He asks *why*, and no reply is recorded. The answer is on the other side of the psalm, in the last line, and it is not an explanation — it is a completed work."}]}, "Psalms 22:16–18": {"title": "\"They have pierced My hands and My feet\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David wrote this around 1000 BC. Crucifixion had not been invented."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When the method appeared"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Persians began impaling people some five centuries after David. The Romans developed crucifixion into what it became, and it was not in use anywhere in David's world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel's own method of execution was stoning. Nothing in David's experience involved piercing a man's hands and feet and leaving him where people could stand and stare."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They have **pierced my hands and my feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words, written by a man describing something he had no category for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What else is in the passage"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Dogs surround me* — the term Jews used for Gentiles, and Romans did the work", "*a band of evildoers has closed in*", "*I can **count all my bones*** — what hanging by the arms does to the ribcage", "*they **stare and gloat** over me* — the crowd standing and watching", "*they **divide my clothes** among them and **cast lots** for my garment*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "That last one is two separate actions, and John records both: the soldiers divided His outer garments four ways, and then came to the tunic, *woven in one piece from the top*, and said *let us not tear it, but cast lots for it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They divided some and gambled for one, because of how the tunic was made. David wrote both halves of it a thousand years earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Not one bone broken"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I can count all my bones.* And in the same psalm's world, Exodus 12 said of the Passover lamb: *nor shall you break one of its bones.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The soldiers came to break the legs of the crucified men to finish them, and found Him already dead, and did not break His."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm opens with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My God, my God, why have You abandoned me?* — the sentence He quoted from the cross at the ninth hour. See the note on Psalms 22:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naming the first line of a psalm called up the whole psalm. He was pointing at this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And how it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has done it.* Three Hebrew words at the close, and the Greek for the same thought is one: **tetelestai**. It is finished."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the psalm.** Any hint that David is describing his own life. Nothing recorded of him matches it. He wrote a first-person account of an execution that would not exist for five hundred years, in a country that never practised it."}]}, "Psalms 23:1–3": {"title": "\"He lays me down\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The best-known psalm in the world, and the verbs in its first three lines are all His."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lays me down… He leads me… He restores… He guides me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs, one subject. The sheep in this psalm is not described as following well, finding pasture, or keeping up. Everything active belongs to the shepherd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing said about the sheep is *I lack nothing* — a state, not an achievement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Lying down"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lays me down in green pastures.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sheep do not lie down when they are hungry, frightened, or bothered by other animals. A resting flock is the sign that every one of those has been dealt with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has the shepherd causing it. He does not merely permit the rest; he brings the conditions about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Still waters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is *waters of rest* — quiet water, water that has stopped moving. Sheep will not drink from a fast stream; the noise and movement frighten them, and a wet fleece will drown them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shepherd in that country dammed a pool or found a still place. The provision is shaped to what the animal can actually use."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He restores my soul\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he brings my soul back* — the verb for returning, turning around. It is used elsewhere of bringing back a wanderer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not merely refreshment. Retrieval."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He guides me along right paths **for the sake of His name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The motive is stated, and it is not the sheep's merit. The shepherd's reputation is bound up with where the flock ends up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm does not skip.** Verse 4 goes straight into the valley of the shadow of death, and the shepherd is still there — *You are with me.* The green pastures and the valley are on the same path, led by the same person."}]}, "Psalms 23:4": {"title": "Through the valley", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and everything in it turns on a single preposition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Through\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even when I walk **through** the valley of the shadow of death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not into it, not down to it, and not around it. Through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word assumes an entrance and an exit. Nobody is promised a route that avoids the valley; the promise is about what the valley is — a passage, not a destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I walk\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not run, not flee. The pace does not change."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The valley"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tsalmaveth** — a compound of *shadow* and *death*, and it is used of deep darkness, the places where light does not reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shadow requires a light source and an object, and it can frighten but it cannot touch. David is a shepherd writing about the ravines he walked his flock through, where the walls are high and the sun does not get in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm changes person"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the psalm from the beginning and watch the pronouns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is **my** shepherd… **He** makes me lie down… **He** leads me… **He** restores my soul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is being talked about. Then the valley:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **You** are with me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The moment the darkness comes, the psalm stops speaking about Him and starts speaking to Him. That is where He stops being the subject of a sentence and becomes the person being addressed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I will fear no evil\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is not that there is no evil in the valley. It is *for You are with me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rod and the staff"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two implements, and they do different jobs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **rod** is the club — a shepherd's weapon, carried against what comes at the flock. The **staff** is the crook, for guiding and lifting a sheep out of a place it has got itself into."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One is used on the enemy, one on the sheep, and both are listed as comfort."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on the other side of the valley.** *You prepare a table before me in the presence of my enemies.* He does not clear the room first. The enemies are still there and the meal is served anyway."}]}, "Psalms 23:6": {"title": "The two that follow", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last verse of the psalm, and the verb in it is not a gentle one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Pursue\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Surely goodness and mercy will **pursue** me all the days of my life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **radaph** — to chase, to hunt, to run after. It is the word used for an army in pursuit, and for Pharaoh's chariots after Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most translations soften it to *follow*, which sounds like something trailing along behind. The verb is a chase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things are hunting him down, and they are goodness and mercy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tov** — goodness. **Chesed** — the covenant word, steadfast love, loyal kindness. It is the word repeated twenty-six times in Psalm 136, once per verse: *His mercy endures forever.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All the days\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one. Not the good ones, not the ones he handled well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The valley is in verse 4 and the enemies are in verse 5, and verse 6 says *all the days of my life* without excluding either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a shepherd's psalm ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm has moved from pasture to water to valley to a table with enemies watching, and then to a house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I will **dwell in the house of the LORD forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to sit, to remain, to settle — the word for taking up residence, not visiting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David wrote this. He was a man who spent his life in tents and caves and on the run, and who was never allowed to build the house he wanted to build."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The whole psalm in its place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set between the cross and the coming King:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Psalm 22** — forsaken, pierced, *He has done it*", "**Psalm 23** — the shepherd, the valley, the table, and the house", "**Psalm 24** — *lift up your heads, O gates, that the King of glory may come in*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the middle one. It is the psalm for now: the walking, the days, the pursuit. See the notes on Psalms 22:1–5 and Psalms 24:7–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first verse and the last.** *The LORD is my shepherd, I shall not want* — and *I will dwell in the house of the LORD forever.* It opens with a want that does not exist and closes with an address that does not change."}]}, "Psalms 24:7–8": {"title": "Lift up your heads, O gates", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A command shouted at architecture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lift up your heads, **O gates**! Be lifted up, **you ancient doors**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the people. The gates."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm tells the doorways to make themselves higher, because what is coming through will not fit under them as they stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Ancient doors\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *doors of old*, everlasting doors. Whatever gates these are, they are older than the city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question and the answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who is this King of glory?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm asks it, and answers it, and then asks again a few lines later and answers again. It is written to be sung antiphonally — one voice at the gate, one voice inside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD, **strong and mighty**, the LORD, **mighty in battle**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer is military. He is identified by a fight He has been in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which battle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm before it is the shepherd. The psalm before that is the cross — *forsaken*, *pierced*, and *He has done it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the time the gates are told to lift their heads, the battle referred to is over. He is not going out to it; He is coming back from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three psalms as one sequence"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**22** — the Saviour dying, forsaken, the work finished", "**23** — the Shepherd, present, walking the days and the valley with His own", "**24** — the King of glory coming in through the gates"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past, present, and what is still ahead. They stand in that order in the book and they have always been read that way. See the notes on Psalms 22:1–5 and Psalms 23:6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the psalm opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The earth is the LORD's, and everything in it, the world and all who live in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The claim is made before the King arrives at the gate. He owns the city He is being asked to be let into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who goes up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The middle of the psalm asks who may ascend the hill of the LORD, and answers: *he who has clean hands and a pure heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then it names the generation that seeks Him, and the last line of that section is *they will receive blessing from the LORD, and righteousness from the God of their salvation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness is listed as something received from Him, in the same breath as the blessing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line of the psalm.** *The LORD of hosts — He is the King of glory.* The title is Yahweh Sabaoth, the LORD of armies. The gates are opened for the commander, coming home."}]}, "Psalms 36:7–9": {"title": "In Your light we see light", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses in the middle of a psalm that opens with a description of wickedness. David turns from that to this without transition, and the change of temperature is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shadow of the wings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People take shelter under the shadow of Your wings.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for shelter is the word for taking refuge — running to a rock or a stronghold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wings are the cherubim wings over the mercy seat, and also a bird's over its young. Both pictures are in the Psalms, and both are about a small thing being covered by something larger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Feasting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **feast on** the rich abundance of Your house, You give them drink from Your river of delights.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the one for being saturated, drinking to the full. The noun behind *rich abundance* is *deshen* — the fat, the richest portion, the part of the offering that was burned as the choice part."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Delights** is **adanim** — the plural of the word Eden is built from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two \"withs\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For with You is the fountain of life, and in Your light we see light.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not near You. **With** You. The source is not somewhere He points to; it is where He is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Fountain** is *maqor* — a spring, the origin of running water, as against a cistern that only holds what was poured in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In Your light we see light\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is circular on purpose. You do not bring a light to God in order to find Him. You see by His."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same logic as the lamp in Psalm 119 and the lamp in 2 Peter 1 — the seeing is done in borrowed light, and the borrowing is not a deficiency."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David was looking at.** The verses immediately before this describe a man who has *no fear of God before his eyes*, who plots on his bed. David does not argue with him. He looks up instead, and the psalm changes register in a single line: *Your unfailing love, O LORD, reaches to the heavens.*"}]}, "Psalms 51:3–4": {"title": "\"You are right when You speak\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Written after the worst thing David ever did, and the astonishing thing is the direction he faces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heading on the psalm says it: *when Nathan the prophet came to him, after he had gone in to Bathsheba.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adultery, and then a man murdered by arrangement to cover it. A year of silence before the prophet arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Never leaves my sight\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I know my rebellion, **my sin never leaves my sight**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that it is *before me continually*. He is not confessing something he had forgotten."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Against You, You only\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Against You, **You only**, have I sinned, and done what is evil in Your sight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Uriah is dead. Bathsheba's life is wrecked. A child will die. And David says *against You only*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not dismissing them. He is naming who the offence is finally against — the psalm is addressed to God and he refuses to spread the account across other defendants."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **You are right when You speak, You are clear when You judge**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the move. He does not argue for a mitigating circumstance. He rules in God's favour before God has said anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that God is *justified* in His speaking and *clear* in His judging."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Set against Job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God's one charge against Job, out of the whirlwind: *would you condemn Me so that you could be in the right?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two positions and only one can be occupied. Job took his own for thirty chapters and God had to come out of a storm about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David takes the other one at the top of the psalm, before anything is asked of him. He agrees God is right and puts himself in the wrong, and everything he receives in the rest of the psalm comes down that channel. See the note on Job 40:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Purge me with hyssop, and I shall be clean. Wash me, and I shall be whiter than snow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hyssop is the little bunch of herb used to put blood on the doorframes at Passover, and to sprinkle a cleansed leper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks to be handled the way a leper is handled — by an application of blood he does not administer himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what he does not offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You do not delight in sacrifice, or I would bring it… **the sacrifices of God are a broken spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king with a treasury and a tabernacle says the one offering that would work is not something he can fund."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he asks not to lose.** *Do not cast me from Your presence, and do not take Your Holy Spirit from me.* Under the old covenant the Spirit came and went — he had watched it happen to Saul. It is a prayer no believer since Pentecost has had to pray."}]}, "Psalms 75:6–8": {"title": "Promotion comes from one direction", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm rules out three directions and leaves one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three that are excluded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For no one is lifted up **from the east or the west, or from the wilderness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "East, west, and the desert to the south. The one direction the psalm does not name is up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever is left after those three is the answer, and the psalmist leaves the reader to say it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to lift up, to raise, to exalt — and it is the word used for promotion, for a head being raised, for a man being set high."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of the compass points can do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **God who judges**. He brings one down and lifts another up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are His. The bringing down and the lifting up are the same hand doing two things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *judges* is not primarily about a courtroom. It is the word for governing, deciding, ruling a matter — the verb the book of Judges is named after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two examples in one sermon's reach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Uzziah** — helped until he was strong, and struck at the altar the day his heart got proud. Fifty-two years, and the last of them in a separate house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Haman** — promoted above every official for no stated reason, and hanged on his own gallows within the day. And **Mordecai**, sitting at a gate, given the ring that had been on Haman's hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither man's rise came from the east or the west. Neither man's fall did either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is a **cup in the hand of the LORD**, foaming with wine, well mixed, and He pours it out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is of a cup passed round — the prophets use it repeatedly, and it is the cup Jesus names in Gethsemane."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Down to the very dregs the wicked of the earth will drink it dry.* The dregs are the sediment at the bottom, the last and worst of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is holding it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage is about whose hand things are in. The cup is in His hand; the lifting is His; the bringing down is His."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the psalm arranges their own position."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the psalm opens.** *We give thanks to You, O God, we give thanks, for Your name is near.* The name is *near* — which is what the book of Esther demonstrates without once writing it down."}]}, "Psalms 76:10–12": {"title": "Even human rage will end up praising You", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One line, and it settles what happens to everything thrown at God's people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even **human rage** will end up **praising You**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not tolerated. Not survived. It ends up as praise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *rage* is the hot word — burning anger, fury. It is what men do at their worst, and the psalm says the finish of it is God's glory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **what rage is left, You put on like a belt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *You gird Yourself with the remainder of wrath*. Whatever anger does not end up as praise is strapped on as a belt — held, restrained, and worn by Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A belt is what you fasten so nothing swings loose. What is left over is not running free; it is buckled to Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Haman"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole of Esther is this verse in narrative."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["his **rage** at one man not bowing produced the decree", "the decree produced the fast and Esther's approach to the throne", "his **rage** built a seventy-five-foot gallows, and it is what he was hanged on", "his **boast** to his wife and friends brought out the advisers' verdict against him", "his **pleading** for his life across a couch is what the king read as assault"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every furious thing Haman did was load-bearing in his own destruction, and the Jews came out of it with the ring, the house and a feast that is still kept."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of those steps required a miracle, and God is not named in any of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm says about kings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **cuts down the pride of rulers**; He is a terror to the kings of the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *cuts down* is to cut off, as of a vine being pruned — the word used of a grape harvest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahasuerus, who ruled from India to Cush, spent the whole book being moved by his own temper and never knew who was moving him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Make your vows to the LORD your God and **keep them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set between the rage that turns to praise and the kings who are cut down, the psalm's one command to the reader is small and domestic: say what you will do, and do it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the psalm begins.** *In Judah God is known; His name is great in Israel.* Known, and named. The book of Esther is the same God at work in a place where He was neither."}]}, "Psalms 78:40–43": {"title": "\"They did not remember His hand\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A long psalm retelling Israel's history, and this is its diagnosis of what went wrong in the desert."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They defied Him… they grieved Him… they tested God… they wounded the Holy One of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third and fourth are the striking ones. **Tested** — they put God through a trial, repeatedly. And the last, often rendered *limited*, has the sense of marking out a boundary or causing pain; the older translation *they limited the Holy One of Israel* is where the familiar phrase comes from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way the psalm attributes something to God that is rarely said aloud: He was grieved. Not inconvenienced. Pained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cause given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They did not remember His hand, the day He set them free.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forgetting is the root, and it is a strange thing to forget — a sea opening, a nation walking out of Egypt in a night. The psalm's charge is not that they disbelieved a promise they had never seen kept. It is that they stopped recollecting one they had."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole psalm is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The opening verses say it: these things were handed down *so that the next generation would know them*, and set their hope in God rather than repeating the pattern."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a psalm written to be taught to children, and its method is to tell the story honestly, failures included."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"limiting\" God.** The phrase has been used to mean that unbelief restricts what God is able to do. The Hebrew is disputed and the psalm's own explanation is simpler and sadder: they forgot what He had already done. Whatever the verb means precisely, the sentence beside it is the one the psalmist chose to explain it."}]}, "Psalms 90:11–12": {"title": "\"Teach us to number our days\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The turn in Moses' psalm. Everything before it is description; this is where it becomes a request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Teach us to count our days.* Not *let us live longer*, and not *explain why*. Teach us to reckon them — the verb is arithmetical, the ordinary word for counting a number of things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The premise is that a person does not naturally do this. Days are spent as though the supply were unlimited, and the psalm asks for the ability to hold the real figure in mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that we may get a heart of wisdom.* The counting is not the goal. It is the instrument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is literally *bring in* a heart of wisdom — like a harvest gathered into a barn. Numbering the days is how the crop gets in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It follows immediately on the verse about God's anger and the fear owed to Him, and it precedes a series of requests that get steadily warmer: *satisfy us in the morning with Your unfailing love… make us glad for as many days as You have afflicted us… establish the work of our hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm moves from lament to petition, and this verse is the hinge. A man watching a generation die in the desert asks first to be taught how to count."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read with the verses above it.** The famous *seventy years, or eighty if we are strong* is four lines earlier, and it is description, not decree — see the note on Psalms 90:7–10. This request is what Moses does with that observation: not resignation, but a prayer to be taught how to spend what there is."}]}, "Psalms 90:7–10": {"title": "\"Seventy or eighty years\" — spoken from inside the wrath", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The best-known number in the Bible about lifespan, and it sits in the middle of a paragraph about being under God's anger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Read the verses it lives in"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Our short lives feel Your fire; **in Your anger** we grow weary and afraid*", "*You set our failings before You, our secret faults lit up in the light of Your face*", "*Our days pass under Your gaze and end like a sigh*", "**then** — *our years may reach seventy, and if we are strong, eighty, yet even the best of them are toil and sorrow*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The number is the last line of a lament about living under displeasure. It is description, not decree — what life measured out to that generation, in those conditions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking, and about whom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heading calls this *a prayer of Moses*, and the wilderness is all over it. He is watching the generation that refused at Kadesh die off across forty years, exactly as had been said they would."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the seventy and eighty. Not a limit God set on human beings in general — the span of a people under judgement, written down by the man watching it happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the same Bible records elsewhere"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Moses himself**, at a hundred and twenty, *his eyes were not weak and his strength had not left him*", "**Caleb**, at eighty-five, asking for a hill country full of giants and saying he was as strong as the day Moses sent him out", "**Hezekiah**, given fifteen years after being told to put his house in order"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who wrote the seventy-year line outlived it by fifty years. That is worth holding in mind before handing anyone the number as though it were an appointment."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses does next.** He does not settle for the description. The psalm turns into a request — *teach us to count our days… satisfy us in the morning with Your unfailing love… make us glad for as many days as You have afflicted us.* He is asking for something better than what he has just described. See the note on Psalms 90:11–12."}]}, "Psalms 91:1–2": {"title": "The secret shelter", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses at the head of the psalm, and they name four things about God in the space of a breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Makes a home\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **makes a home** in the secret shelter of the Most High.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to sit, to remain, to dwell — the word for taking up residence, not visiting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything the rest of the psalm promises runs off this verb. It is not a place you go to in an emergency; it is an address."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The secret place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sether** — a covering, a hiding place, somewhere concealed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is secret because it is hidden, not because it is hard to find. Nobody outside can see you there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shadow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Will **rest in the shadow** of the Almighty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shadow requires being close. You cannot be in the shade of something at a distance from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the Hebrew for *rest* here is to lodge overnight — to spend the night. The first line is about living there; the second is about sleeping there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four names, in order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm stacks them:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Most High** — *Elyon*, the highest, the one above all", "**Almighty** — *Shaddai*, the all-sufficient", "**the LORD** — *YHWH*, the covenant name from the burning bush", "**my God** — *Elohim*, and the possessive is the point"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "It moves from the highest and most distant title down to *my*. The psalm ends the sentence closer than it started it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will say of the LORD, \"**You are** my refuge and my fortress.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first verse is a statement about anybody. The second is spoken, in the first person, and addressed to Him directly — *You are*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm changes from third person to second person inside two lines. The saying of it is part of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Refuge and fortress"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words that are not the same. **Machseh** is a shelter you run into from a storm. **Metsudah** is a stronghold — a fortified place, a mountain hold, the word used of David's strongholds in the wilderness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somewhere to run, and somewhere that holds."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who quotes this psalm.** The devil, in the wilderness, and he quotes verses 11 and 12 accurately — *He shall give His angels charge over you.* He leaves out the line *to keep you in all your ways*. The one place in Scripture where Psalm 91 is cited by name, it is being misused, and the answer is a different verse."}]}, "Psalms 91:14–16": {"title": "The three verses where God speaks for Himself", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm changes speaker at the end, and most readings walk straight past it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is talking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verses 1 to 13 are *about* God — He will cover you, you will not fear. Someone is describing Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From verse 14 the voice switches to the first person and stays there. Seven promises in three verses, every one of them God's own speech:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**I** will set you free", "**I** will lift you high", "**I** will answer", "**I** will be with you in trouble", "**I** will rescue you", "**I** will honour you", "**I** will fill your hands with long life"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not end with a description of God. It ends with God taking over the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because you have held onto Me in love.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb means to cling, to be joined to — the word used of a man joined to his wife, and of Israel told to hold fast to the LORD. It is not achievement and it is not blamelessness. It is attachment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the seven promises are hung on. Someone who has taken hold and will not let go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"With long life I will fill your hands\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *satisfy* — filled up, having enough. Not merely a long span of years but a fullness of them, and then the last line: *I will let you see how deep My rescue runs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm ends on sight. Not only rescued, but shown the rescue — allowed to see how far down it goes."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The trouble is named, not denied.** *I will be with you in trouble* is one of the seven, and it is worth holding on to. This is not a psalm promising a life nothing ever happens in. It promises a Presence in the middle of what happens, and a rescue out the other side, spoken by God about Himself."}]}, "Psalms 91:5–8": {"title": "Around the clock, seen and unseen", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four dangers named in two lines, and the way they are arranged tells you the psalm means all of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The terror of the night, the arrow that flies in the day, the plague that moves in the dark, the ruin that strikes at noon.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two at night, two by day. Two hidden, two out in the open. It is a Hebrew way of saying *everything* by naming the poles — the whole clock, and both kinds of threat, the sort you can see coming and the sort you cannot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is left outside the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"It will not come near you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A thousand may fall at your side, ten thousand at your right hand, but it will not come near you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not pretend the danger is imaginary. People are falling — that is in the verse. What it says is that the thing sweeping the ground around you does not arrive where you are standing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *come near* is the ordinary word for approach. It is a promise about proximity, spoken over someone in the middle of a field where others are going down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is spoken over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 1 sets the terms for everything that follows: *the one who lives in the shelter of the Most High, who stays in the shadow of the Almighty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dwelling is the posture. Not visiting in an emergency — living there. The promises in this psalm are addressed to somebody already at home in that place, and the rest of the psalm reads as a description of what that address is like."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How it ends.** God takes over the sentence at verse 14 and speaks in the first person — *I will set you free… I will answer… I will be with you in trouble… with long life I will fill your hands.* The One making the promises stops being described and starts speaking. See the note on Psalms 91:14–16."}]}, "Psalms 92:10–15": {"title": "\"Even in old age they still bear fruit\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two trees, chosen for two different qualities, and a promise about the far end of a life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The palm and the cedar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The righteous will flourish like the palm tree, they will grow tall like a cedar of Lebanon.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the palm** — grows in the desert, fruits for decades, and its trunk bends in a storm without snapping. It also bears its best dates late in life.", "**the cedar** — the great timber tree of the ancient world, slow, enormous, and used to build the temple itself."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "One thrives where nothing should; the other lasts and towers. Between them they cover fruitfulness and endurance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are planted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Planted in the house of the LORD, they will bloom in the courts of our God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *transplanted* — moved from somewhere else and put here deliberately. Neither tree grew where it happened to land."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the location is the point of the psalm: not planted near the house, or visiting it. In it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise about age"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even in old age they still bear fruit; they stay fresh and green.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two words are *fat* and *fresh* — full of sap, still green. It is a horticultural picture, and it is set against everything people expect of the last stretch of a life: drying out, thinning, stopping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the fruit is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Singing out that the LORD is upright. He is my Rock, and there is no wrong in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm names its own harvest. The old man still bearing fruit is bearing testimony — still able to say what God is like, out of a long life of finding out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The psalm's title.** *A song for the Sabbath day.* This is the rest-day psalm, and it opens with how good it is to give thanks in the morning and at night. The flourishing it describes is the fruit of a life lived at rest, not of a life spent striving."}]}, "Psalms 96:10–13": {"title": "\"Say among the nations, the LORD reigns\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm gives an announcement to make and then hands the singing over to creation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Say among the nations, \"**The LORD reigns.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in Hebrew. It is not a wish or a prayer — it is a statement of what is currently the case, to be said out loud among people who do not know it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The world **stands firm, it cannot be shaken**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is established, fixed, made to stand. The stability of everything is placed downstream of who is reigning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the rejoicing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let the **heavens** be glad and the **earth** rejoice; let the **sea** and all it holds resound. Let the **fields** be joyful, and all that is in them; then all the **trees of the forest** will sing for joy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five subjects, and not one of them is a person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sky, ground, sea, field, forest. The psalm moves outward and downward and the whole of creation is put in the choir."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they are glad"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before the LORD, for **He is coming, He is coming to judge the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice. And the reason creation rejoices is the judgement — which is not how the word usually lands on us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *judge* is the governing word: to put right, to set in order, to rule a matter. Creation is described elsewhere as *groaning*, waiting; here it is told the One who will set it right is on His way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The terms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will judge the world with **righteousness**, and the peoples with **His faithfulness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness and faithfulness — **emunah**, the steady, dependable word, the root of *amen*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first line of it is *sing to the LORD a new song, sing to the LORD, all the earth.* Then *declare His glory among the nations, His marvellous works among all the peoples.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a psalm about telling. The whole thing is instruction on what to say and to whom."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it was first sung.** 1 Chronicles 16 records almost this entire psalm as the song David gave Asaph on the day the ark was brought into Jerusalem. It was written for the day the presence came home."}]}, "Revelation 1:4–6": {"title": "\"He made us kings and priests\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Before a single vision, before the seals and the trumpets, John states what the reader already is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He loved us. With His own blood He washed our sins off us. He made us kings and priests serving God, His own Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three short clauses, all in the past. Loved, washed, made. Nothing here is pending."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the order runs from the inside out: love first, then the cleansing, then the office."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Kings and priests"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Israel those two were kept strictly apart. Priests came from Levi, kings from Judah, and when King Uzziah went into the temple to burn incense he came out a leper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Melchizedek held both, and Hebrews makes much of it. Here both are given to ordinary believers in seven small congregations in Asia, some of which the next two chapters will criticise sharply."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is said to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to spiritual heroes. Read on and these are the churches that have left their first love, tolerated false teaching, and gone lukewarm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The status is stated at the front, before any of that, and it is never withdrawn during the letters that follow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description of Christ"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The witness who never lies, the first to be born out of the dead, the ruler the kings of the earth answer to.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three titles, and they map onto the whole book: the one who tells the truth about what is coming, the one who has already been through death, and the one to whom every earthly power is answerable — which is the thing a persecuted church most needed to hear."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"The One who is here now, who was here before, and who is on His way.\"** John's version of the divine name, stretched across all three tenses, and he puts the present first. Not *was, is, and will be* — but **is**, was, and is coming."}]}, "Revelation 12:10–11": {"title": "The accuser thrown down, and the three things that beat him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A loud voice in heaven announces a defeat, and it names both what the enemy did for a living and what put him out of business."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His job description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who accused our brothers… the one who stood in front of our God accusing them, day and night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what he was doing. Not tempting, in this passage — **accusing**, in front of God, without stopping. The word behind it is a courtroom word, and it is where the title Satan comes from: the accuser, the adversary in a legal sense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job 1 and Zechariah 3 both show him at that work. Zechariah's scene is the clearest: the high priest standing in filthy clothes with Satan at his right hand to accuse him — and God rebuking the accuser and re-clothing the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What beat him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things, and the order is worth keeping:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the Lamb's blood** — the ground of it. Accusation has nothing to work with once the charge has been answered.", "**the message they told** — their testimony, spoken. What was true was said out loud.", "**they would not cling to their own lives even with death in front of them** — people who had stopped being controlled by the last threat available."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first is what was done for them. The second and third are what they did with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Now it has arrived\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Salvation, and strength, and the kingdom of our God, and the power of His Christ.* The announcement of the accuser's fall is made in the same breath as the announcement that the kingdom has come."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two are treated as one event."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the accusing stopped.** Romans asks *who is going to lay a charge against the ones God Himself has chosen?* and answers by pointing at the one at God's right hand — not accusing, but interceding. Revelation shows the same thing from the other side: the seat the accuser used to occupy is empty. See the note on Romans 8:31–34."}]}, "Revelation 2:4–5": {"title": "\"Do again what you did then\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The church at Ephesus is commended for a great deal and then told one thing is wrong, and the one thing outweighs the list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the verses just before: hard work, perseverance, intolerance of evil, they had tested false apostles and found them out, they had endured hardships for His name and not grown weary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By any external measure this is a strong church. Nothing on that list is withdrawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That love you had at the beginning — you have walked away from it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *your first love*, and the verb is to leave or abandon. Not that it cooled. That they walked off from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doctrine intact, effort intact, discernment intact — and the thing that made it all worth doing has been left somewhere behind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remedy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three imperatives, and the middle one is the practical one:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**remember** how high you stood before you fell", "**turn back**", "**do again what you did then** — the first works"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not tell them to work up a feeling. He tells them to do the things they did when the love was fresh. The affection is recovered by returning to the actions that belonged to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *remember* is present continuous — keep remembering, not one act of recollection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will take your lampstand away from where it stands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lampstand is the church itself, as chapter 1 explains. Not a threat to individuals' salvation — a warning that a congregation which keeps everything except the love stops being a light in that place."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being asked for.** The whole letter is written by the One who chapter 1 describes as walking among the lampstands. He is not asking for more output from a church that is already working hard. He is asking to be loved by it."}]}, "Revelation 5:5–6": {"title": "The Lion, and the Lamb", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John is told to look at a Lion and sees a Lamb, and the whole book turns on the substitution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he was crying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: a scroll sealed with seven seals, and *no one in heaven or on the earth or under the earth was able to open it*, and John *wept much*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not weeping over a book. Whatever the scroll is, its unopened state is what undoes him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stop crying. Look — the Lion out of Judah's tribe, the Root of David, has won.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two titles, and both are old promises."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The Lion of Judah** is Jacob's blessing in Genesis 49: *Judah is a young lion… who would dare rouse him?* See the note on Genesis 49:8–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The Root of David** is Isaiah 11: *there shall come forth a Rod from the stem of Jesse, and a Branch shall grow out of his roots.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Has won\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **enikesen** — He conquered, aorist tense. A completed victory, already achieved, reported as news."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John actually sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So I looked, and there in the middle of it all… **stood a Lamb**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told Lion. He sees Lamb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a correction — the same Person. But the conquering was done in the other form."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He had been killed, and He was standing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a lamb *as though slain* — bearing the marks of slaughter, and on its feet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both facts held together in one clause: the wounds are visible and He is standing. It is the only description of the risen Christ in Scripture that keeps the killing in the picture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven horns and seven eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Horns** are the Old Testament image for power, **eyes** for seeing and knowing, and **seven** is completeness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the eyes are the seven Spirits of God, sent out into every part of the earth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the story started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 3:15, in the garden, to the serpent: *He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise His heel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A heel bruised and a head crushed — a wound taken in the winning. That is the Lamb standing with the marks on Him, and it is the first promise in the Bible arriving at its end. See the note on Genesis 3:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The woman's seed at the beginning, the Lion of Judah at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They sing a new song: *You are worthy to take the scroll, for **You were slain**, and have redeemed us to God by Your blood out of every tribe and tongue and people and nation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given for His worthiness is not the Lion. It is the slaying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The scroll.** Nobody in the vision could open it, and John's grief was proportionate to that. Whatever it holds — the title deed of the earth, the unrolling of what is to come — the point of the chapter is that the seals came off in the hands of somebody who had been killed."}]}, "Romans 1:16–17": {"title": "The power of God for everyone", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The thesis of the whole letter, in two sentences, before Paul has argued anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not ashamed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am **not ashamed** of the good news of Christ — not for a moment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing to the capital of the empire, about a Jewish carpenter executed by Rome on a slave's cross. The message had every social reason to be embarrassing, and he opens by naming the embarrassment and dismissing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **the power of God** to rescue everyone who believes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dunamis**. Not that the gospel describes God's power, or points to it, or asks for it. It **is** it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same in 1 Corinthians: *the message of the cross is… the power of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"To rescue\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **soteria** — the standard word for salvation, and wider in that language than the English suggests."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root is used in the Gospels of a bleeding woman made well, a blind man healed, Peter pulled out of the water, and a girl raised from the dead. Rescue, healing, wholeness, preservation, safety."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one word covering all of it, and Paul says the gospel is God's power toward all of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who believes — **the Jew first, and also the Greek**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only qualification in the sentence is believing. Not merit, not nationality, not standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being revealed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In it **the righteousness of God is being revealed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not demanded. Revealed — uncovered, disclosed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the hinge of the letter. The law revealed a righteousness God requires; the gospel reveals one God supplies. Paul returns to it in chapter 3: *the righteousness of God apart from the law is revealed… through faith in Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From faith to faith\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A right standing that comes **from faith and leads on to faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *ek pisteos eis pistin* — out of faith, into faith. It starts there and it goes on there. Nothing switches over to another basis later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The one who is right with God will live by faith.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk 2:4 — five words in Hebrew, quoted three times in the New Testament (here, Galatians 3, Hebrews 10), and the sentence that set Luther off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It can be read *the righteous shall live by faith* or *the one who is righteous by faith shall live*. Paul's argument holds either way, and the letter goes on to make both true."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When Habakkuk said it.** Standing on a watchtower, having complained twice that God was doing nothing about violence and injustice, and being told the answer was coming but not yet. *Though it tarries, wait for it.* The verse the Reformation turned on was written by a man who could not see what God was doing."}]}, "Romans 10:16–17": {"title": "\"Faith comes by hearing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just run a chain of questions — how can they call on someone they have not believed in, believe in someone they have not heard, hear without a preacher — and this is where it lands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So faith comes by hearing, and hearing comes through the word of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two steps, and the direction is fixed. Faith is not summoned up from inside a person; it arrives by a route, and the route starts with something said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in this chain generates their own believing. Something is spoken, it is heard, and faith follows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for \"word\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses **rhema** — a word spoken, an utterance, rather than *logos*. It is the living, said thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why the chain has a preacher in it at all. The gospel in this chapter is something that travels by voice: *how beautiful are the feet of those who bring good news.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The present tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both verbs are present. Faith *comes*, hearing *comes* — ongoing, repeatable, not a single event in the past."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the mechanism does not stop working after conversion. Faith arrives the same way at every stage: something is heard."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse before it.** *But not all of them welcomed the good news.* Paul is honest that the same word is heard and refused. Hearing in the physical sense is not the whole of it — Isaiah's complaint, which Paul quotes, is that the message was announced and not believed. The route is reliable; the response is still a response."}]}, "Romans 10:5–8": {"title": "\"The word is near you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul lets the two righteousnesses speak in their own voices, and they sound nothing alike."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses describes the righteousness that comes from the law like this: \"**The person who does these things will live by them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus 18. One verb, and everything hangs on it: **does**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a harsh statement. It is simply the arrangement, stated plainly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second one speaks differently"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the righteousness that comes from faith **speaks a different way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul personifies it. Faith-righteousness has a voice, and the first thing out of its mouth is a prohibition on two questions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two forbidden questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not say in your heart, 'Who will go up into heaven?'**\" — that is, to bring Christ down — \"**or, 'Who will go down into the deep?'**\" — that is, to bring Christ up from among the dead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are attempts to fetch. One reaches up, one reaches down, and both assume the thing needed is somewhere else and has to be retrieved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul rules out both, and gives the reason in each case as a completed event. Christ has already come down. He has already come up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is nothing to go and get."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it says instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The word is near you, in your mouth and in your heart.**\" That is the word of faith that we preach.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is quoting Deuteronomy 30, where Moses says the commandment *is not too mysterious for you, nor is it far off. It is not in heaven… nor is it beyond the sea… but the word is very near you, in your mouth and in your heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two locations. The mouth first, then the heart — and Paul keeps Moses' order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you **confess with your mouth** the Lord Jesus and **believe in your heart** that God has raised Him from the dead, you will be saved. For with the heart one believes unto righteousness, and **with the mouth confession is made unto salvation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states it twice, mouth and heart both times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *confess* is **homologeo** — to say the same thing, to agree out loud. It is the verb behind *the High Priest of our confession* in Hebrews 3. See the note on Hebrews 3:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"saved\" covers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sozo** and **soteria** are wider than the English. The same words are used in the Gospels of a woman made well, a blind man healed, a sinking man pulled out of water, and a girl raised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rescue, healing, wholeness, preservation. It is one word doing all of that work."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter goes.** *For whoever calls on the name of the LORD shall be saved* — Joel — and then Paul's chain of questions: how shall they call on Him in whom they have not believed, and how shall they believe in Him of whom they have not heard, and how shall they hear without a preacher? The whole passage is about speech, from beginning to end."}]}, "Romans 10:9–13": {"title": "The mouth and the heart", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two organs, named twice, in two different orders — and Paul does that on purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order changes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If your **mouth** confesses the Lord Jesus, and your **heart** believes that God raised Him from the dead, you will be saved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then immediately:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is with the **heart** that a person believes… and it is with the **mouth** that confession is made.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "First mouth then heart; then heart then mouth. He is not being careless. He is tying them together so tightly that neither can be lifted out alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Why the first order at all? Because he is quoting Deuteronomy 30 two verses earlier — *the word is near you, in your mouth and in your heart* — and he keeps the prophet's sequence before explaining it in his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is confessed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lord Jesus.* In a Roman province where *Caesar is Lord* was the civic formula, saying it about a crucified Galilean was not a private sentiment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what is believed is specific: *that God raised Him from the dead.* Not a general trust in God. One historical claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is open to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is no difference here between Jew and Greek, for the same Lord is Lord of all, and He is generous to all who call on Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *generous* is literally *rich toward* — the wealth language Paul uses everywhere. And then the quotation that closes it: *everyone who calls on the name of the Lord will be saved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone. It is from Joel, and Peter used the same line at Pentecost."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the list.** No requirement to clean up first, no probation, no ceremony. A confession and a belief, from anyone, of any nation, calling on a name. Paul spends the next verses insisting that someone therefore has to go and tell them."}]}, "Romans 11:13–16": {"title": "The first handful, and the root", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two images in one verse, and both come from the same place in the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first handful of dough"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the first handful of dough is holy, so is the whole lump.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 15 required the first of the ground meal to be offered as a contribution. Once that portion was given, the rest of the batch was reckoned holy — set apart, usable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's logic runs from the part to the whole: what is true of the first is true of what follows it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The root and the branches"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the root is holy, so are the branches.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same argument, different picture — and this one he will spend the next ten verses on: an olive tree, natural branches broken off, wild branches grafted in, and a warning to the wild ones not to be arrogant about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both images are making his point about Israel: the patriarchs are the root, and what happened at the root is not undone by what happened to some branches."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage is actually about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The subject is not personal blessing. It is whether God has finished with Israel, and Paul's answer is a flat no — *their being received back* would be *life from the dead*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He also says something disarming about his own motives: he makes much of his work among Gentiles partly hoping to provoke his own people to jealousy and save some. An apostle admitting to an ulterior motive is not something the New Testament does often."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On lifting the first line out.** *If the first handful is holy, so is the whole lump* is sometimes applied to giving — set apart the first portion of income and the rest is blessed. The image comes from an offering, so the association is understandable, but Paul's own use of it here is about Israel and the patriarchs, and that is what the sentence is arguing."}]}, "Romans 15:1–4": {"title": "What strength is for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent a chapter on Christians who disagree about food and holy days — the strong who can eat anything, the weak who cannot. This is his conclusion, and it is addressed to the winners of the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those of us who are strong have something to do with that strength, and it is not to please ourselves. It is to carry the weaknesses of those who are not strong.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul counts himself among the strong. He agrees with them on the substance — he has already said no food is unclean in itself. And then he tells them that being right is not the end of the matter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is to *bear* — to pick up and carry. Not tolerate. Carry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The example"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ Himself never lived to please Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the proof he offers is a quotation from Psalm 69 — *the insults of those who insulted You fell on Me*. It is a strange verse to choose until you see the logic: the one with every right to be pleased took what belonged to others instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line about scripture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everything that was written in earlier days was written to teach us, so that through the patience and the comfort the Scriptures give, we might go on holding to hope.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just quoted the Old Testament to settle a first-century church dispute about dinner, and then says why he can: the old writings were for our instruction. Note what he says they produce — patience, comfort, and hope. Not primarily information."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why it sits here.** This is Paul's last word on a quarrel about secondary things, and his solution is not a ruling. He tells the people who are right to use their freedom on behalf of the people who are not, and points at Christ for the pattern. It is the most practical thing in the chapter."}]}, "Romans 2:1–5": {"title": "\"It is God's kindness that is leading you to a change of mind\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent chapter 1 describing a society in freefall, and every reader nodding along is the person chapter 2 is written to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So you have no defence, whoever you are, when you sit in judgment on someone else. In the very act of judging another person you condemn yourself, because you who judge are doing the same things.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first word is *therefore*. Everything in chapter 1 was setting this up. Paul built a list his audience would agree with, and then turned and said: the agreeing is the problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you think so lightly of the riches of His kindness and patience and restraint?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words for God's forbearance, and each is a treasury word — *riches* of them. Kindness, patience, and holding back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is not that the judgmental person has broken more rules. It is that they have **misread what the kindness was for**, treating a delay in judgement as either approval or absence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the kindness is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not realising that it is God's kindness that is leading you to a change of mind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb means to lead by the hand, to conduct somewhere. Kindness is described as the thing doing the leading, and the destination is repentance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth stating plainly, because it is the reverse of how the process is usually imagined. Not: repent, and then God will be kind. His kindness is what brings a person round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The alternative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But with a hard and unchanged heart you are storing up wrath for yourself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same treasury image, turned over. Both the kindness and the wrath are being accumulated; which one a person ends up drawing on depends on what they did with the first."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the argument goes.** Paul is on his way to *all have sinned*, and he gets there by closing every exit — the pagan in chapter 1, the moralist here, the religious in the rest of chapter 2. Nobody is left standing, which is exactly the ground he needs for chapter 3."}]}, "Romans 3:23–24": {"title": "Two sentences, and the word between them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted diagnosis in the Bible, and it is only half of the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it usually stops"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone has sinned. Everyone falls short of the glory of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quoted alone, that is a verdict. But in Greek it is not the end of the sentence — the participle that follows carries straight on into the next clause without a full stop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And everyone is put right with Him as a gift, freely, by His grace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same *everyone* governs both halves. Paul builds the universal charge and then applies the universal remedy in the same breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Falls short\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is about coming up behind, lacking, being left wanting. It is the word used in Hebrews for someone missing out on God's grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *broke a rule*. Failed to reach — which is a description of distance rather than of crimes committed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three words for how it comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul stacks them because one is not enough:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**as a gift** — the word means gratuitously, without cause in the receiver. It is the same word Jesus uses in John for being hated *without reason*.", "**freely**", "**by His grace**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is closing every door through which a contribution might be smuggled back in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ransom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through the ransom paid in Christ Jesus that bought us out of slavery and set us loose.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Apolutrosis** again — the release accomplished by paying a price. See the note on Ephesians 1:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gift is free to the person receiving it. It was not free."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes next.** Paul says God set Christ forth publicly as the mercy seat — the same word the Greek Old Testament uses for the lid of the ark. What was hidden behind a curtain and approached once a year with blood, Paul says was displayed in the open. See the note on Exodus 25:17–22."}]}, "Romans 4:13–15": {"title": "Two ways to be an heir, and why they cannot both be true", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is arguing about Abraham, and his case rests on a point of chronology anyone can check."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise to Abraham came in Genesis 15. The law came at Sinai, four hundred and thirty years later. Circumcision itself came a chapter after the promise, not before it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So whatever made Abraham an heir, it cannot have been law-keeping — the law did not exist yet. Paul makes this argument at length through the whole chapter, and it is unanswerable on its own terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the two cannot be combined"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the people of the law are the heirs, then faith has been drained of meaning and the promise has been cancelled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two strong verbs. Faith is *emptied* — made hollow, void of content. The promise is *abolished*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's point is not that law is bad but that the two systems cannot run at once. A promise received by faith and an inheritance earned by performance are different kinds of thing, and running the second empties the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"What the law brings about is anger\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word is wrath. And Paul gives the mechanism in the same breath: *where there is no law there is no breaking of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law is not accused of causing sin. It is described as the thing that turns wrongdoing into transgression — a crossing of a marked line — and so brings the matter under judgement. He develops this at length three chapters later."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the promise actually was.** *That he would inherit the world.* Genesis promised Abraham a land, descendants, and blessing to all nations. Paul reads the whole thing as one inheritance and states it at its widest — which is the scale he thinks the promise was always working at."}]}, "Romans 4:16–17": {"title": "\"Calls things that do not exist as though they already do\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul explains why God set the whole thing up on faith rather than on performance, and then describes the God being believed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it rests on faith"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is why it rests on faith — **so that it can be a matter of grace**, and so that **the promise stands guaranteed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two reasons, and the second is the practical one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A promise conditional on my keeping is only as certain as my keeping. Move it onto faith and it moves onto grace, and a promise resting on grace has nothing shaky underneath it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *guaranteed* is **bebaios** — firm, valid, the word used of a legally binding document."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it covers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not only for those who have the law, but **for those who share the faith of Abraham**. He is **the father of us all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line is drawn by faith, not by descent. Paul has already pointed out that Abraham was declared righteous while still uncircumcised — before the mark, before the law, before anything that would exclude anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things God does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The God who **gives life to the dead** and **calls things that do not exist as though they already do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul picks these two out of everything he could have said about God, because both are what Abraham's situation required."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Gives life to the dead** — Abraham's body *as good as dead*, Sarah's womb *dead as well*, in Paul's own words four verses later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Calls things that do not exist as though they already do** — the Greek is to call, to name, to summon. God speaks of a thing in the present tense while it is still nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where God did that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have made you a father of many nations**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Genesis 17, and the tense is the point: *I have made* — past, completed, said to a childless man of ninety-nine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the same breath God changed his name. **Abram** means exalted father. **Abraham** means father of a multitude."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had one son by a servant girl and no son by his wife. From that day everybody in the camp had to call him *father of a multitude*, every time they wanted his attention, for a year before Isaac was born. See the note on Genesis 17:3–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God said it first. The name came before the child, the promise before the evidence, and Abraham's part was to answer to the name he had been given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is our father **in the sight of the God he believed**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Genesis 1 sounds like beside this.** *Then God spoke: \"Let there be light.\" And light was.* The same pattern, and Paul reaches for it as a description of who Abraham was dealing with. See the note on Genesis 1:3–5."}]}, "Romans 4:18–19": {"title": "Hoping against hope", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul describes what Abraham did with the facts, and it is not what the facts suggested."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **nothing left to hope for**, Abraham went on **hoping and believing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *against hope, in hope he believed* — **par' elpida ep' elpidi**. Two kinds of hope in one clause, and they are set against each other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was no hope available from the situation. He held a different one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he refused to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **refused to dwell on the facts stacked against him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is **katanoeo** — to fix the mind on, to consider closely, to study. It is the same word used in Hebrews 3 for *think hard about Christ Jesus*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So it is not that Abraham was unaware. Paul is precise about what he did with the information: he would not stare at it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The facts he would not stare at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul lists them, and he does not soften them either:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*his own body **as good as dead**, since he was about a hundred years old*", "*and **Sarah's womb dead as well***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for the first is **nekroo** — to put to death, to make a corpse of. Paul's own vocabulary is blunter than most translations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two dead things, named. That is the raw material."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he looked at instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before tells you: *the God who gives life to the dead and calls things that do not exist as though they already do.* See the note on Romans 4:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had two dead things in his hand and a promise about them, and he attended to the One who made it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So he became the father of many nations\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Exactly as he had been told: \"**That is how many your descendants will be.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Genesis 15 — the night God took him outside and showed him the stars."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the name he had been given, *father of a multitude*, was already true in the telling before it was true in the household. See the note on Genesis 17:3–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not claimed here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul never says Abraham felt confident, or that his circumstances improved, or that he stopped being a hundred years old."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His body stayed as it was until it did not. What changed first was where he was looking."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Hebrews says Sarah did.** *Sarah herself was given the strength to conceive… **because she counted the One who promised as good for it**.* The Greek is a reckoning word — she assessed His credit and found it sound. She had laughed at the door of the tent, and the New Testament puts her in the roll of faith anyway. See the note on Hebrews 11:8–12."}]}, "Romans 4:20–22": {"title": "\"He grew strong in faith\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four things said about Abraham in two verses, and each one is worth its own line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He did not waver"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **did not waver at God's promise in unbelief**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **diakrino** — the same verb behind *discerning the body* in 1 Corinthians 11 and *does not doubt* in Mark 11. It means to be divided, to be in two minds."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not split. He was not holding the promise in one hand and the arithmetic of his own age in the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He grew strong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **grew strong in faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a passive — he was strengthened. Something was done to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And note the direction. Twenty-four years passed between the first promise and this chapter, and his faith did not wear down over them; it was built up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He gave glory to God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **gave glory to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before the son. Before any evidence at all. Glory is given for something not yet received."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He was fully convinced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fully convinced that **what God had promised, God was able to do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **plerophoreo** — carried full, filled to capacity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And look at what he was convinced of. Not that it would be easy, or when it would happen, or how. That God was **able**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is why **it was credited to him as rightness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul comes back to Genesis 15:6, which is the sentence this whole chapter has been unpacking. See the note on Genesis 15:6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness was entered on his account — and the thing entered against it was believing that God could do what God said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was not on the ledger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not his obedience, which was real but patchy. Not his record — he lied about his wife to two kings, and took Hagar because the promise seemed slow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's whole argument is that this is the point: *to him who does not work but believes on Him who justifies the ungodly, his faith is accounted for righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Justifies the ungodly.** Paul's phrase, three verses into the chapter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who else it was written for.** *Now it was not written for his sake alone that it was imputed to him, but for us also.* Paul's next sentence. The ledger entry made in Genesis 15 was recorded as a precedent, and the account it applies to is the reader's. See the note on Romans 4:23–25."}]}, "Romans 4:23–25": {"title": "\"Raised so that we could be put right\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent the chapter on Abraham, and here he says why he bothered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not written down for his sake alone\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The words \"**it was credited to him**\" were not written down for his sake alone. They were written **for ours as well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence Paul is quoting is Genesis 15:6 — *Abraham believed God, and it was accounted to him for righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *credited* is **logizomai**, a bookkeeping word: to reckon into an account, to enter on a ledger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham had nothing to put in. It was entered anyway, and Paul says the entry was recorded in Scripture as a precedent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What we believe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It will be credited **to us who believe in the One who raised Jesus our Lord from the dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham believed God could bring life out of a dead womb — *he did not consider his own body, already dead… or the deadness of Sarah's womb.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The object of faith in both cases is a God who raises the dead. Paul makes them the same faith."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **handed over because of our wrongdoing**, and He was **raised so that we could be put right with God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two events, two prepositions, and both are *because of* in the Greek."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Handed over **because of** our sins — they were the reason He went to the cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Raised **because of** our justification — and that is where the sentence gets interesting. Young's Literal keeps it: *raised up because of our being declared righteous.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which way round"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *raised so that we might one day be justified*. **Because** we were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The debt was settled at the cross, and the resurrection is what happened after the settlement — not the means of it but the proof of it. He went in carrying the sins and came out without them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If anything had been left owing, the tomb would still be shut. The empty tomb is God's receipt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is in this chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 4 is Paul's case that righteousness has always been credited rather than earned — from Abraham, who was declared righteous *while still uncircumcised*, four hundred and thirty years before the law existed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends by extending the same entry to the reader, and then chapter 5 opens: *therefore, having been justified by faith, we have peace with God.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Abraham did not have.** A law, a temple, a priesthood, a Bible, or a synagogue. He had a promise and a night sky, and the ledger entry was made on the spot."}]}, "Romans 5:15–17": {"title": "\"Reign in life\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul compares Adam and Christ and then insists the comparison does not balance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not two of a kind\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The free gift and the trespass are not two of a kind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just drawn the parallel and now he breaks it. The two sides look symmetrical and are not — the second is larger, and he says so three times with *much more*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the asymmetry is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sharpest line is the middle one:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The judgment followed a **single** trespass and brought condemnation, but the free gift followed **many** trespasses and brought a verdict of righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sin was enough to bring the verdict down. The gift had to deal with an accumulation — every trespass since — and it still came out the other side with an acquittal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The switch nobody expects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If by the trespass of the one man **death reigned**… much more will those who receive the overflow of grace and the gift of being put right with God **reign in life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The symmetry would require: death reigned, so life will reign. Paul does not write that. He says **they** will reign — the people, not the abstraction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Death is a monarch in his sentence. Grace does not merely replace it with a better monarch; it puts the formerly condemned on the throne."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Those who receive\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one active verb on the human side of the passage. Not those who achieve or maintain — those who **receive**, and what they receive is described twice as overflowing and as a gift."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"in life\" means.** Not after it. Paul says reign *in life* — the sphere is this one. The whole argument since chapter 3 has been that the standing is given now, which is why he can put the reigning in the present tense of a Christian's existence."}]}, "Romans 5:18–19": {"title": "One man, and then one Man", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's argument here runs on a single comparison, and everything depends on the two sides being the same shape."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parallel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "| through one man | through the One | |---|---| | one trespass | one act of righteousness | | condemnation to everyone | the setting-right that is life | | the many were **made sinners** | the many will be **made righteous** |"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb sits on both sides — *kathistemi*, to appoint, constitute, place into a category. Not treated as. **Made.**"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the symmetry matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody chose to be made a sinner by Adam's act. It arrived by connection, not by decision, and the effects were real long before anyone understood them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul insists the second half works identically. The righteousness comes by the obedience of the One, into a group described in exactly the same terms. If the first transfer was real, so is the second — that is the whole force of the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"One act of righteousness\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is singular and concrete: one righteous deed. Paul does not point at Christ's whole life of obedience in general but at a single act, and in this letter that is the cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Against one trespass, one obedience. The books are balanced by matching pieces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So also through the obedience of the One the many will be made righteous.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The righteousness on offer is not a reward for the reader's obedience. It comes by *His* obedience, credited across the same way the trespass was."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse that follows.** Paul says the law came in so that the trespass would increase — *but where sin increased, grace increased all the more.* He is not trying to make sin look small. He builds it as high as it goes and then says grace went higher."}]}, "Romans 5:20–21": {"title": "\"Grace overflowed all the more\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul says what the law was for, and it is not what most people assume."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The law **came in alongside**, so that **the trespass would increase**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *came in alongside* is **pareiserchomai** — to slip in beside, to enter as a secondary thing. It is not the main line of the argument; it comes in next to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the purpose clause is startling: *so that the trespass would increase.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Paul's own account of what the law was given to do. Not to reduce sin — to make it abound, to bring it out into the open where it could be seen for what it is. He says the same thing in chapter 7: *sin, taking opportunity by the commandment, produced in me all manner of evil desire.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But where sin increased, **grace overflowed all the more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **huperperisseuo** — Paul stacks *huper* (over, beyond) on top of a word that already means to abound. Superabounded. He appears to have built the word for this sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever height sin reached, grace is described as clearing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two reigns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as **sin reigned in death**, **grace might reign through righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both verbs are the same — to reign as a king. Paul is describing two thrones, and only one of them can be occupied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin's reign operated *in death*. Grace's reign operates *through righteousness*, and its outcome is *eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note what grace reigns **through**. Not through leniency, and not by overlooking anything. Through righteousness — which had to be supplied, and was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question this provokes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul knows exactly what the next objection is, and he puts it himself at the top of the very next paragraph:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What shall we say, then? Shall we keep on sinning, so that there will be all the more grace?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody asks that question of a message about trying harder. It is the question grace always draws, and the fact that Paul has to answer it is a sign he had preached grace the way he says he did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His answer is not a rule. It is a funeral: *we died to sin; how can we go on living in it any longer?* See the note on Romans 6:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter has been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole of Romans 5 is two men — *by one man's disobedience many were made sinners, so also by one Man's obedience many will be made righteous.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adam's act reached everyone born after him without their doing anything. Paul's argument is that Christ's act works the same way, and *much more*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The phrase he keeps using.** *Much more.* Five times in this chapter. Paul is not arguing that grace matches what Adam did. He argues at every step that it exceeds it."}]}, "Romans 5:9–11": {"title": "\"How much more\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul builds an argument twice, in the same shape, and the shape is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The form"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If, while we were enemies, we were reconciled to God through the death of His Son, **how much more**, now that we have been reconciled, shall we be saved by His life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is an argument from the harder case to the easier one. If the difficult thing has already been done under the worst conditions, the lesser thing is not in doubt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses it twice in three verses, and it runs all through this chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the harder case was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While we were enemies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already said it two verses earlier — *while we were still sinners, Christ died for us* — and he keeps escalating the description: weak, ungodly, sinners, enemies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reconciliation happened at the worst point of the relationship. That is the foundation the whole argument stands on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Saved by His life\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is deliberate. The reconciling was accomplished by His **death**; the saving that follows is by His **life**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not dead now, and Paul treats that as the guarantee of everything still to come. Hebrews makes the same move — *He always lives to intercede for them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The odd word at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We rejoice in God through our Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the boasting word. Paul has spent two chapters demolishing every ground for human boasting, and then permits exactly one: boasting **in God**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in the salvation, or the security, or the change in circumstances. In Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What has already been said in this chapter.** *Since we have been put right by faith, we have peace with God… and we stand in this grace.* Standing is the posture Paul assigns to a person who has received it — which is why the *how much more* argument can be made at all."}]}, "Romans 6:1–2": {"title": "\"Shall we go on sinning?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks the question his own preaching provokes, and the fact that he has to ask it tells you what he had been preaching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shall we **keep on sinning, so that there will be all the more grace**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just written *where sin increased, grace overflowed all the more.* This is the obvious next move, and Paul makes it himself before anyone else can."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody ever asks this question of a message about behaving better. It only arises if the grace preached was extravagant enough to sound dangerous."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is accused of it elsewhere too: *why not say — as we are slanderously reported and as some claim that we say — \"Let us do evil that good may come\"?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the charge, and Paul reports that people were making it about him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Certainly not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me genoito** — the strongest negative available, roughly *may it never be*. Paul uses it ten times in Romans."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the answer is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *because you will lose your salvation*. He does not say *because God will punish you*. He does not reintroduce a rule, or a threat, or a condition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks a different question back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason he gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We **died to sin**; how can we go on living in it any longer?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *we should die to sin*. Not *we are dying to sin daily*. Past tense, done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is about impossibility of a sort — the absurdity of a dead man continuing to keep house in the place he was carried out of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul spends the rest of the chapter on that one fact: buried with Him, raised with Him, the old man crucified, freed from sin. And only after all of it does an instruction appear — *count yourselves dead indeed to sin, but alive to God.* See the note on Romans 6:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reckoning comes after the reality, and is based on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which way round the chapter runs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every command in Romans 6 arrives after a statement of what is already true. Paul never asks anyone to die to sin in order to become dead to it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says at the end of the chapter.** *For sin shall not have dominion over you, **for you are not under law but under grace**.* The reason given for sin not ruling is the very thing the objector thought would let it rule."}]}, "Romans 6:12–14": {"title": "\"Sin shall not be your master\" — a promise, not a warning", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Almost everyone reads this backwards, and the grammar is what gives it away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb tense nobody points out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For sin shall not be your master, because you are not under law but under grace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Greek that is a **future indicative** — a plain statement of what will be the case. It is not an imperative. Paul is not commanding *do not let sin master you*; he already gave that instruction two verses earlier, and he used a different form to do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sentence is not a rule with a penalty. It is a promise, and it comes with a reason attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason is the whole point"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at what he gives as the ground of the promise:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["not *because you are trying hard*", "not *because you have dealt with that sin*", "**because you are not under law but under grace**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means Paul is claiming something most people find hard to believe: being under grace is what breaks sin's power, and being under law is what leaves it in place. He says it plainly enough that the next verse has him answering the obvious objection — *shall we sin because we are under grace?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That objection only makes sense if he really did say what he seems to have said. Nobody raises it against a preacher of rules."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for \"master\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Kurieuo* — the verb from *kurios*, lord. To have lordship over, to be the one whose word settles things. Paul is not talking about whether a believer ever sins. He is talking about who is in charge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That distinction matters when the verse is read by someone who has just failed. The sentence does not say sin will never touch you. It says sin will not be the one giving orders — and it locates the reason for that entirely outside their performance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it asks of the reader"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction in the two verses before this one is worth reading in the same breath: *hand yourselves over to God as people brought from death to life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *become* people brought from death to life. As people who already are. Everything Paul asks here is asked on the basis of something he says has already happened, and the promise at the end is the reason he can ask it at all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"under grace.\"** It is a position, not a mood — the same construction as *under law*. Paul is describing which of two authorities a person lives under, and the entire argument of chapters 5 to 8 turns on the claim that a Christian has changed jurisdictions. That is why the promise can be stated as fact."}]}, "Romans 6:22–23": {"title": "The wage and the gift", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two nouns, and the whole difference is in which of them you are owed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sin **pays a wage**, and the wage is death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **opsonia** — a soldier's pay, the rations and coin issued at the end of a stint. It is what a man has earned and can demand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is given death. It is drawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God **gives a gift**, and the gift is life that has no end.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **charisma**, from **charis** — grace. A thing handed over free, with no work behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul deliberately does not use the same word twice. He could have written *the wages of sin is death, and the wages of God is life*, and the sentence would have collapsed. The two halves are not the same kind of transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of life"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Zoe aionios** — and it is not primarily about duration. The New Testament's word for endless time is doing something else here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus defines it in John 17: *this is eternal life, that they may know You, the only true God, and Jesus Christ whom You have sent.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is God's own kind of life, given to a man, and the fact that it does not run out is one of its properties rather than its definition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In Christ Jesus\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last three words locate it. The gift is not posted separately; it is in a Person, and it comes with Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Having been **set free from sin** and made slaves of God, you have your fruit, and it leads to being made holy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the state Paul is describing the gift arriving into. Freed first, and then the fruit follows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *made slaves of God* is the strong word — bondservants. Paul's picture is not neutrality; a man is owned either way, and the question is by whom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the chapter exists"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 6 opens with the objection: *shall we continue in sin that grace may abound?* — which is the question grace always provokes, and Paul's answer is not to add rules."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a funeral: *we died to sin; how shall we live any longer in it?* You cannot go back to a house you were carried out of."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The most quoted half.** *The wages of sin is death* is preached far more often than the clause after it, and the clause after it is where the sentence is going. Paul wrote one sentence with a *but* in the middle."}]}, "Romans 6:8–11": {"title": "\"Once for all and never again\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes his case about the believer by first making it about Christ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is true of Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ, having been raised from the dead, **dies no more**; **death no longer has any mastery over Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two negatives, both permanent. Whatever death had a claim on, that claim is finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The death He died"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The death He died was **a death to sin, died once for all and never again**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ephapax** — once, once and for all, decisively. Hebrews uses the same word repeatedly: *once for all He offered up Himself*, *by one offering He has perfected forever*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not die to sin repeatedly, and there is no arrangement by which He does it again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The life He lives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the life He lives is a life lived to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present tense. That is what He is doing now."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In the same way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the same way, **count yourselves** dead indeed to sin, but alive to God through Jesus Christ our Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything said about Him in the three verses before is what the reader is told to reckon about himself. The pattern is not aspirational — it is the same set of facts, applied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Count\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **logizomai** — the bookkeeping word. To reckon, to enter into the account, to regard as so."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not mean to pretend, and it does not manufacture the fact. An accountant reckons what is already in the ledger. Paul has spent seven verses putting the figures there before asking anyone to add them up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the reckoning sits in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's order through Romans 6 is fixed and never reverses:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "1. *we died to sin* — stated 2. *buried with Him… raised* — stated 3. *our old man was crucified with Him* — stated 4. *Christ dies no more* — stated 5. **then**: *count yourselves dead indeed to sin* 6. **then**: *do not let sin reign in your mortal body*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command in verse 12 is the first instruction in the chapter, and it arrives eleven verses after the facts it rests on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Through Jesus Christ our Lord\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last phrase locates it. Not dead to sin in yourself, examined on your own terms — dead to sin *in Him*, which is the position the whole chapter has been describing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"dead to sin\" does not mean.** Not that sin has stopped existing or knocking. Paul says *do not let it reign* in the very next verse, which would be a strange thing to say to someone it could no longer approach. Dead to sin is about jurisdiction — who has a claim — not about whether the old master still calls round."}]}, "Romans 7:1–3": {"title": "A marriage ended by death", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul stops and makes sure of one legal principle before he builds anything on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The law has authority over a person **only for as long as that person is alive**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it flatly, and he says it to people who *know how law works* — Jewish readers in Rome who would grant the point without argument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A law can speak to the living. It has nothing to say to a corpse. No court prosecutes a dead man, and no statute binds one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The illustration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By law she is bound to her husband while he lives; but if her husband dies, she is released from the law that bound her to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law that bound her did not change. It was not repealed, softened, or amended. It is exactly as binding as it was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She simply is no longer inside its jurisdiction, because a death occurred."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"No adulteress at all\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She is **no adulteress at all** if she marries another man.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul presses the same case twice — once with the husband living, once with him dead — so that nobody can mistake which fact does the work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same act is a crime in one paragraph and blameless in the next. Nothing about the woman changed. A death changed everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse applies it: *you were made dead to the law through the body of Christ, so that you could belong to Another.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note who died in Paul's application. Not the law — us. *Through the body of Christ*, which is the phrase he will use again over a table three letters later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law is still the law, holy and just and good, and Paul says so a few verses on. It simply has no claim on somebody who died and was raised."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why he pauses to establish this.** Chapter 6 has just been answering *shall we go on sinning that grace may abound?* Paul's answer is not tighter rules. It is a funeral: you died with Him, and what you are joined to now is a Person, not a code. See the note on Romans 7:4–6."}]}, "Romans 7:18–20": {"title": "The most quoted description of failure in the Bible", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*The good I want to do, I do not do; the evil I have no wish to do is the evil I go on doing.* Anyone who has tried to live a good life recognises the sentence immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence it starts from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I know that **nothing good lives in me — that is, in the old nature**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it about himself, flatly, and he had more to boast about than most: *circumcised the eighth day, of the tribe of Benjamin, a Hebrew of Hebrews, concerning the law a Pharisee… concerning the righteousness which is in the law, blameless.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That man says there is nothing good in his flesh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not despair and it is not fishing for reassurance. It is a settled verdict, and it is the most freeing sentence in the chapter — once it is agreed, there is nothing left to defend and nothing left to produce."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it puts a man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job spent thirty chapters building a case for his own righteousness, and God's one charge out of the whirlwind was *would you condemn Me so that you could be in the right?* Two positions, and only one of them is habitable. See the note on Job 40:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already taken the other one. A man who has stopped expecting good out of his own flesh can be handed a righteousness that is not his — which is the whole argument of the letter: *the gift of righteousness*, *received*, *apart from works*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **wanting** is present and the **doing** is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wanting is right there within reach; the doing of it is not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a claim about willpower. The problem being described is not ignorance of what is right or reluctance to want it. It is the gap between the wish and the act — which is exactly the gap the law cannot close, because a rule can tell you what to do and cannot make you do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends in a cry — *who will rescue me?* — and an answer: *thanks be to God, through Jesus Christ our Lord.* And then chapter 8 opens with *there is now no condemnation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 7 is not a resting place in Paul's argument. It is the wall he builds so that the door in chapter 8 has something to be a door in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is not.** It is not a description of the normal Christian life. It is the sound of a man trying, in his own strength, to keep something holy — *I delight in the law of God according to the inward man*, and losing every time. Paul writes it to bring you to the end of that, so that the first line of the next chapter can land: *there is therefore now no condemnation to those who are in Christ Jesus.*"}]}, "Romans 7:4–6": {"title": "Released to belong to Another", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul applies the marriage illustration, and the application does not go where the illustration seemed to be heading."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who died"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You were made **dead to the law** through the body of Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the picture, the husband died and the wife went free. In the application, the one who dies is **you**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not saying the law was abolished. He says the party under obligation was removed from its reach by death — a death that happened *through the body of Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So that you could belong to Another\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not so that you could belong to nobody. The release is not into independence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him who was raised from the dead* — and the language is marital, carried straight over from the three verses before. The point of the funeral is the wedding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law had been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sinful passions **that the law kept stirring up** were at work in every part of us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Paul's most uncomfortable sentence about the law and he states it without softening: the passions were stirred **by** the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He explains it later in the chapter — *I would not have known what coveting was if the law had not said, do not covet* — and defends the law itself as holy. The fault is not in the statute. But a prohibition, read by the old nature, works like a lit match."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And what they produced was death.* That is the fruit of the first marriage. The second one bears fruit *for God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"That which once held us fast has been put to death\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb behind *held us fast* is to hold down, to confine — the word used for a prisoner in custody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something that had a grip on us was killed, and the release follows from the killing. Nobody wriggled free."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two ways of serving"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that we serve in the **newness of the Spirit** and not in the **old way of the written code**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Serving is in both halves. Paul never offers a release from service; he distinguishes what the serving runs on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *written code* is **gramma**, letter — the same contrast he draws in 2 Corinthians 3: *the letter kills, but the Spirit gives life.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter does next.** It gets worse before it gets better — the whole of *what I want to do I do not do*, and *who will deliver me from this body of death?* Then the answer, and then chapter 8: *there is therefore now no condemnation.* Paul takes the long way round on purpose."}]}, "Romans 8:12–14": {"title": "A debt Paul says you do not owe", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul opens with a strange half-sentence, and the strangeness is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence he does not finish"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are in debt — but not to the old nature, as though we owed it a life lived on its terms.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says who they are **not** indebted to, and then never says who they are. The sentence is left open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read what he has just spent eleven verses on and the missing half is obvious: if anyone is owed, it is the God who did what the law could not. Paul leaves the reader to finish it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"putting to death\" is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If by the Spirit you go on putting the body's doings to death, you will live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things in the grammar keep this from becoming grim self-effort. It is continuous — *go on* doing it, not a single crisis. And it is **by the Spirit**, which names who is doing the work through them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already ruled out the alternative at length: the law could not manage it, and neither could he, as chapter 7 records in painful detail."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line that follows.** *All who are led by the Spirit of God are the sons of God* — and then Paul goes immediately to adoption, Abba and inheritance. He raises the leading in order to prove sonship, not to test it. See the note on Romans 8:15–17, which is the same argument continuing."}]}, "Romans 8:15–17": {"title": "Abba, and the word Paul made up", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two words in this passage are worth stopping on. One is Aramaic, and one Paul appears to have coined himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word Paul built"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit who places you as a son with full standing* — behind that phrase is one Greek word, **huiothesia**. It is not a normal word. Paul takes the word for son and the word for placing and puts them together, and it appears almost nowhere in Greek literature before he uses it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It describes the Roman legal act of adoption, which was not what we mean by the word. A Roman adoption transferred a person into a new family completely: old debts cancelled, old obligations void, full inheritance rights, and it could not be undone. It was more permanent than being born into a family, because a natural son could be disinherited and an adopted one could not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the standing Paul says the Spirit gives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word Jesus used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By Him we cry out, Abba, Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abba* is Aramaic, not Greek — and Paul is writing to Rome, where hardly anyone spoke it. He leaves it untranslated anyway, then puts the Greek beside it. He does the same in Galatians. It is the word Jesus used in Gethsemane, and it survived into Greek-speaking churches as a word people kept saying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not baby-talk, as it is sometimes described. It is family speech — the word a grown son would use of his own father, in the language Jesus prayed in."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is set against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You did not receive a spirit that makes you a slave again, dragging you back into fear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the alternative Paul rules out, and notice what he calls it: fear, and slavery, and *again* — a return to something. He is describing a relationship run on anxiety about standing, and he says flatly that this is not what was given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The evidence he offers is not a feeling. It is the cry itself: the fact that a person calls God Father at all is presented as the Spirit's own testimony, witnessing *together with* our spirit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line about suffering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If indeed we suffer with Him, so that we may also be glorified with Him.* This reads to many people like a condition attached at the end that could void the whole thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But the sentence it belongs to is about inheritance — heirs of God, heirs together with Christ. Paul's point is that being a co-heir means sharing what Christ has, and what Christ has includes both a road that went through suffering and a glory on the far side of it. It is a description of the inheritance, not an entrance fee for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"led by the Spirit\" in the verse before.** The passage just above says all who are led by the Spirit are sons of God — and it is often read as a test of whether someone qualifies. Paul's next sentence is this one, and it goes straight to adoption, Abba and inheritance. He raises leading in order to say what it proves about a person's standing, not to make them anxious about whether they are being led well enough."}]}, "Romans 8:28–30": {"title": "What the \"good\" in \"works together for good\" is", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the most quoted sentence in the letter and the most often quoted half-sentence in the Bible. Almost nobody reads the next line, and the next line is where Paul says what he means by *good*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He defines his own term"}, {"k": "note", "t": "everything, all of it together, is working toward good … **for** those God knew beforehand, He also marked out beforehand **to be shaped into the likeness of His Son**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The *for* is doing the work of a definition. The good being worked toward is not a comfortable outcome, a resolved situation, or a happy ending. It is a person coming to look like Christ."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why the verse holds up in situations where a promise of good outcomes would be an insult. Paul is not saying the thing that happened was good. He is saying nothing is wasted in what God is making."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Shaped into the likeness\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word is *symmorphous* — sharing a form with. It is not imitation from the outside. Paul uses the same root when he speaks of a body being transformed, and of being conformed to Christ's death. The picture is of something taking a shape rather than copying a model."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the purpose given is not the reader's improvement: *so that His Son would be the firstborn among many brothers and sisters.* The goal of the whole process is a family with Christ at the head of it. The reader's transformation is how that family comes to exist, not a self-improvement project with a spiritual label."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five links, and where they land"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul then does something unusual: he runs the whole thing end to end in one breath."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["knew beforehand", "marked out beforehand", "called", "put right with Himself", "**glorified**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every verb is past tense, including the last one. Glorified — a thing no reader has experienced yet — is spoken of as done. Paul is not confused about the timeline. He is describing the chain from God's side, where it is finished, and that is why he can start the paragraph with *we know*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is safe to say with this verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not: everything that happens is good. Paul does not say that, and the chapter around it is full of groaning, weakness and suffering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he says is that all of it together is being worked toward one end, that the end is Christ's likeness in a person, and that the sequence which gets them there is already complete in the mind of the One running it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"those who love God.\"** It reads like a qualifier a person might fail, and anxious readers use it that way. But Paul immediately restates the group by another name — *those who have been called according to His purpose* — and everything after that is God's action, not theirs. The love is how he describes them, not a threshold they maintain."}]}, "Romans 8:31–34": {"title": "Four questions, and nobody answers them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul finishes the argument of eight chapters by asking questions instead of making statements, and the silence where the answers should be is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The questions"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*If God is for us — who is there left to stand against us?*", "*Who is going to lay a charge against the ones God Himself has chosen?*", "*Who is left to condemn?*", "and, a few lines on, *who shall separate us from the love of Christ?*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are courtroom questions — the language of accusation, charge and condemnation. Paul invites anyone who wants to prosecute to step forward, and nobody does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument from the greater to the lesser"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He did not spare His own Son but handed Him over for us all — how will He not, along with Him, freely give us everything else?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the logic the whole paragraph rests on. If the hardest thing has already been given, nothing smaller is in doubt. Paul does not argue that God is generous in general; he argues from one specific act that has already happened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase *did not spare* echoes Genesis 22, where Abraham does not withhold his son. Paul is putting the two scenes beside each other deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is at the right hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is Christ who died — and more than that, who was raised — who is now at God's right hand, and who is pleading there for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only figure the passage places in a position to condemn is the one described as interceding. That is the answer to the questions: the seat the prosecutor would have to occupy is already taken by the defence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word \"freely give.\"** The verb is built on *charis* — grace. Paul does not say God will *provide*; he says He will **grace** us with all things, alongside the Son. The giving of everything else is described in the same vocabulary as the giving of Christ."}]}, "Romans 9:25–26": {"title": "Called My people", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Hosea twice, and both quotations are the reversal of a name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is quoting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hosea's third child was called **Lo-Ammi** — Not-My-People — and God said over Israel *you are not My people, and I am not your God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the two verses that reverse it and sets them side by side. See the note on Hosea 1:8–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will call those who were **not My people** 'My people,' and her who was **not loved** 'beloved.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both of Hosea's negative names, both undone. *Lo-Ammi* becomes *Ammi*. *Lo-Ruhamah* becomes *Ruhamah*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prefix is simply dropped. Nothing else changes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the calling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will call.* The naming is His in both directions — He named them Not-My-People, and He is the one who renames them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in either verse describes them earning the second name. They are called it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**In the very place** where it was said to them, 'You are not My people,' there they will be called **sons of the living God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not somewhere else. The same place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the title is not restored to what it was. They were *My people*; they are now *sons of the living God*. The reversal overshoots the starting point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul is arguing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's subject is God's freedom in mercy — *I will have mercy on whom I have mercy* — and Paul applies Hosea's words about Israel to Gentiles being brought in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is showing from the prophets that a people who were not a people becoming God's own is not a new idea invented by the church. It was written down centuries earlier, about Israel, and God's method has not changed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"called\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "It runs through the whole chapter. *Not of works but of Him who calls… the children of the promise are counted as offspring… even us whom He called.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Calling is what makes the difference in Romans 9, and it belongs to Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the argument ends up.** Two chapters later: *God has not cast away His people whom He foreknew… for the gifts and the calling of God are irrevocable.* Paul spends three chapters on this and does not conclude that anybody was discarded."}]}, "Song of Songs 8:6–7": {"title": "\"Many waters cannot quench love\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The high point of the Song, and the only place in the book where the name of God appears."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set me as a seal upon your heart, as a seal upon your arm.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A seal was a personal signet — pressed into wax to mark what belonged to you, and worn on a cord at the chest or as a ring on the hand. It was identity and ownership in one object."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two placements: the **heart**, where affection is, and the **arm**, where strength acts. She is asking to be stamped on both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The very flame of the LORD\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Its flashes are flashes of fire, the very flame of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shalhevetyah* — a flame with the shortened divine name attached to the end. It is the only occurrence of the name in the book, and it turns up here, in a description of what love is like."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The claim being made is not that love is intense. It is that this fire comes from God's own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things that cannot touch it"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Many waters cannot quench love, neither can the floods drown it* — the deep waters are the Bible's standing image for chaos and death. They cannot put this out.", "*If a man offered all the wealth of his house for love, it would be utterly scorned* — and it cannot be bought either."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Strong as death, unquenchable by the flood, not for sale. Three of the largest forces anyone knows, and love outlasts all three."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the church has read it.** The Song is a love poem and it is not embarrassed about that. It has also been read from the earliest times as describing God's love for His people — and this passage is why: language this absolute, with His own name burning at the centre of it, is doing more than describing an engagement."}]}, "Zechariah 10:1": {"title": "\"Ask the LORD for rain in the springtime\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is an instruction about timing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The season named"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ask the LORD for rain **in the springtime**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word is the one for the **latter** rain — the spring rain that comes just before harvest and fills out the grain. See the note on Deuteronomy 11:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So this is not a general encouragement to pray about weather. It is: when the season for the last rain arrives, ask for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why ask for something already promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy had promised both rains to a people who kept the covenant. Zechariah, centuries later, tells them to ask anyway."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise does not remove the asking. It is what makes the asking sensible — you request what has already been undertaken, in the season it belongs to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is that gives it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is the LORD who makes the storm clouds.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line matters because of what the verse before it says. Zechariah has just described people consulting household idols and diviners, who *speak nonsense and give empty comfort*, which is why the flock wanders like sheep with no shepherd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set against that, this is a redirection: not the teraphim, not the diviner. Ask the One who actually makes the clouds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gives showers of rain **to all**, and green plants in the field to everyone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rain falls across a district, not on one field. What is asked for privately arrives publicly, over everybody's ground."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it sits in the book.** Zechariah 9 and 10 are full of restoration — a king coming humble on a donkey, prisoners released, the house of Judah strengthened. The instruction to ask for rain belongs to that: a people being told to expect a season and to say so out loud."}]}, "Zechariah 13:7": {"title": "\"Strike the shepherd\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and Jesus quotes it about Himself on the last night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the sword is called against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Awake, sword, against My shepherd, against **the man who stands close to Me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for the second phrase is unusual — it means an associate, a companion, someone of the same kind. It is used in Leviticus of a man's neighbour, his fellow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God calls the shepherd His own, and He calls him a companion, and He tells the sword to wake up against him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who wields it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Declares the LORD Almighty.* The command is God's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the difficult centre of the verse, and the New Testament does not soften it — Isaiah 53 says the same: *it pleased the LORD to crush him.* The striking is not something that happened to God's shepherd against His will."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus uses it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the way to Gethsemane: *every one of you is going to fall away tonight. It stands written: I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep will scatter.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes it to explain what His disciples are about to do — and then, in the same breath, tells them where to meet Him afterwards. See the note on Mark 14:27–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in Zechariah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will turn My hand against the little ones.* The Hebrew phrase can carry the sense of turning a hand **to** someone — and the same chapter goes on to describe a remnant refined and brought through fire, who call on God's name and are answered: *they are My people, and they will say, the LORD is my God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scattering is not the end of the sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the shepherd language goes.** Zechariah's struck shepherd stands behind John 10, where Jesus calls Himself the good shepherd who lays down His life for the sheep — *no one takes it from Me.* Both are true at once: the sword is commanded, and the shepherd lays it down."}]}};
